[Event "First moves"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4
{ The King's Pawn takes two central squares at once, d5 and f5, and opens the way for the queen and the light-squared bishop. It is the most played first move at every level: the game tends to open up quickly, with fast development and early tactics. Black's main answers all fight for the center in their own way: [e5] meets the pawn head-on, [c5] (the Sicilian) attacks d4 from the side, while [e6] and [c6] prepare [d5] to challenge e4 directly. [%csl Gd5,Gf5][%cal Ge2e4] }
( 1. d4
{ The Queen's Pawn takes the center with a pawn the queen already protects, so it is harder to attack than one on e4. It controls e5 and c5 and opens the way for the dark-squared bishop. Games after it tend to be more strategic: White often follows with [c4], the Queen's Gambit, or with a set-up such as the London ([Bf4], [e3], [c3]). Black answers with [Nf6], the flexible Indian move, with [d5], the classical reply, or with [e6], which keeps several defenses open. [%csl Ge5,Gc5][%cal Gd2d4,Gc2c4] }
1... Nf6
{ The Indian Defense: Black controls e4 with a piece instead of a pawn, and waits to see White's set-up before committing the center pawns. That flexibility is the point: depending on what follows, Black can choose the King's Indian ([g6]), the Nimzo-Indian or Queen's Indian ([e6]), the Grünfeld or a more classical [d5]. After [c4], White gains space on the queenside; [Nf3] and [Bf4] are quieter, system-like choices. [%cal Gf6e4,Gc2c4,Gg7g6] }
( 1... d5
{ Black mirrors White and plants a pawn in the center, stopping [e4] for now. The position is closed in the middle, so both sides develop calmly before choosing a plan. [c4], the Queen's Gambit, is the main try: it attacks the base of the pawn on d5 from the side. [Nf3] keeps every option open, and [Bf4] heads for the London System, a set-up White can play against almost anything. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd7d5,Gc2c4] }
2. c4 ( 2. Nf3
{ White develops first and keeps the choice of plan for later: [c4] can still turn the game into a Queen's Gambit, while [Bf4] or [e3] build a London or a Colle. The knight also guards e5 and stops a quick [e5] by Black. Black usually answers in kind with [Nf6]; [c6] keeps the Slav in mind, and [e6] the Queen's Gambit Declined. [%cal Gf3e5,Gc2c4,Gc1f4] }
2... Nf6
{ A symmetrical position where both knights guard the key central squares, e4 for Black and e5 for White. White now chooses the character of the game: [c4] leads to the Queen's Gambit, [Bf4] to the London System, [e3] to the Colle, a solid set-up that prepares [e4] later. Black waits with the same flexibility, keeping [e6], [c6] or [Bf5] in reserve. [%cal Gf3e5,Gf6e4,Gc2c4] }
( 2... c6 ) ( 2... e6 ) 3. c4 ( 3. Bf4 ) ( 3. e3 ) ) ( 2. Bf4 ) ) ( 1... e6
{ The Horwitz Defense is a waiting move with a purpose: it frees the dark-squared bishop and keeps Black's options open. After [e4], Black is in a French Defense with [d5]. After [c4], Black can head for the Queen's Gambit Declined, a Dutch with [f5], or check with [Bb4+] at once. White's quiet choices, [Nf3] or [Bf4], usually transpose to familiar set-ups once Black plays [Nf6] and [d5]. The price of [e6] is that the light-squared bishop on c8 is now behind the pawn chain. [%csl Yc8][%cal Ge7e6,Gd7d5] }
2. c4 ( 2. e4 ) ( 2. Nf3 ) ) 2. c4 ( 2. Nf3
{ White develops and keeps the queenside pawns flexible. Played before [c4], the knight move sidesteps some of Black's sharpest replies to an early [c4], such as the Budapest Gambit with [e5], and after [c4] and [e6] it leads to the Queen's Indian rather than the Nimzo-Indian. Black's main answers are [d5], the classical stand in the center, [g6] for a King's Indian or a Grünfeld, and [e6]. [%cal Gf3e5,Gd7d5,Gg7g6] }
2... d5 ( 2... g6 ) ( 2... e6 ) ) ( 2. Bf4 ) ) 1... c5 ( 1... e5
{ The Open Game: Black copies White, claims an equal share of the center and frees the dark-squared bishop and the queen. The pawn on e5 stops White from simply playing [d4] for a big center without a trade. The usual way on is [Nf3], which develops while attacking e5 and leads to the classical openings (Italian, Ruy Lopez, Scotch). [Bc4] aims at f7 straight away, [Nc3] keeps things quiet, and [f4], the King's Gambit, gives a pawn to tear Black's center down. [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge7e5,Gg1f3] }
2. Nf3
{ The knight comes out to its best square, attacks the pawn on e5 and gets White one step closer to castling. Black has to look after that pawn. [Nc6] is the natural way, developing a piece at the same time. [Nf6], the Petrov, counterattacks e4 instead, and [d6], the Philidor, defends with a pawn: solid, but it shuts in the bishop on f8. Defending with [f6] is a known mistake: it takes the knight's best square and weakens the diagonal to the king, and [Nxe5] is already strong. [%cal Gf3e5,Gb8c6,Gg8f6] }
( 2. Bc4 ) ( 2. Nc3 ) 2... Nc6
{ The knight on c6 defends e5 and also watches d4, so both sides have a knight out and the fight is about what White does next. [Bb5], the Ruy Lopez, puts pressure on the knight that guards e5, the deepest and most respected choice. [Bc4], the Italian, aims the bishop at f7, the square only the king defends. [d4], the Scotch, opens the center at once, and [Nc3] leads to the calm Four Knights. [%cal Gc6e5,Gf1b5,Gf1c4,Gd2d4] }
( 2... Nf6 ) ( 2... d6 ) 3. Bb5 ( 3. Bc4 ) ( 3. d4 ) ) ( 1... e6 ) *

[Event "Italian Game (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4
{ The Italian Game, one of the oldest openings on record: Italian masters of the 16th and 17th centuries analysed it. The bishop on c4 aims at f7, the weakest point in Black's camp because only the king guards it, and it stops Black from playing [b:d5] for free. Black has two main answers. [Bc5] mirrors White: the bishop eyes f2, and the queen on d8 still covers g5, so [w:Ng5] is never a worry. [Nf6] hits e4 at once and leads to the sharper Two Knights, where [w:Ng5] becomes possible. [Be7] is solid but passive, and [h6] is slow: White simply answers with [w:d4] and opens the centre. [%cal Gc4f7] }
3... Bc5
{ The Giuoco Piano, the quiet game. Both bishops stare at the enemy's f2 and f7, and White decides the character of the fight. [c3] prepares [w:d4] for a classical pawn centre. [d3] is the slow Giuoco Pianissimo, today's main line at the top, where both sides manoeuvre for a long time. [b4] is the Evans Gambit: a pawn for time and an open centre. [O-O] keeps the choice for later and may lead to the Deutz gambit with [w:d4]. One thing White cannot do here is [w:Ng5]: with the knight still on g8, Black's queen on d8 sees g5 and would take it. [%cal Gc5f2,Gc2c3,Gd2d3,Gb2b4] }
( 3... Nf6 ) ( 3... Be7 ) ( 3... h6
{ A very common club move: [h6] keeps White's knight and bishop off g5 for good. But it develops nothing, and White should use the free tempo to open the centre. [d4]! is the engines' choice: after [b:exd4] [w:Nxd4] White has a Scotch with the bishop already on c4, and the pawn on h6 does little there. [O-O] and [c3], with [w:d4] next, are just as good, since Black has no knight on f6 yet to hit e4. [w:Nc3] and [w:d3] are fine but quieter: they give the tempo back, and Black gets a normal game. [%csl Yh6][%cal Gd2d4,Ge1g1,Gc2c3] }
4. d4
{ Striking with [d4] is the principled way to punish Black's slow 3.[h6]: White opens the centre before Black has developed the kingside. Black should capture [exd4], reaching a Scotch Gambit or Scotch Game after [exd4 Nxd4] where White's bishop already sits actively on c4 and h6 contributes little to the central battle. Holding the point with [d6] is playable but passive after [d6 dxe5], leaving Black cramped on the back two ranks. [%cal Ge5d4,Gd7d6] }
( 4. O-O
{ Castling [O-O] gets White's king safe immediately and keeps [w:d4] or [w:c3] in reserve for the next move, when the f1 rook is ready to step onto e1. Black has to catch up in development now: [Nf6] is the most active reply, attacking e4 and inviting [Nf6 d4 exd4] with counterplay in the centre, whereas [d6] and [Be7] keep the position closed at the cost of ceding space. [%cal Gg8f6,Gd7d6,Gf8e7] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... d6 ) ( 4... Be7 ) ) ( 4. c3
{ Playing [c3] prepares to build a full pawn centre with [w:d4] while keeping b4 covered and giving the c4 bishop a retreat along the b1-h7 diagonal. Because 3.[h6] put no pressure on e4, Black should develop [Nf6] right away to counterattack White's e-pawn as soon as [Nf6 d4] lands. Supporting e5 with [d6] is playable too, though it allows White to establish d4 and e4 side by side. [%cal Gg8f6,Gd7d6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... d6 ) ) ( 4. Nc3
{ Developing [Nc3] is a natural club choice, defending e4 and controlling d5, though blocking the c-pawn gives back the extra tempo that 3.[h6] handed White. Black gets a comfortable Italian setup with [Bc5], eyeing f2 and preparing [b:d6] plus [b:Nf6] without ever having to worry about a pin on g5. [Nf6] is just as solid, transposing into a quiet Four Knights Italian where Black has no problems. [%cal Gf8c5,Gg8f6] }
4... Bc5 ( 4... Nf6
{ Developing [Nf6] reaches a Four Knights Italian structure where Black has spent a tempo on h6 instead of bringing out the f8 bishop. Because Black's dark-squared bishop is still at home, [Nxe4] fork tricks are much less effective here, and White can seize the initiative right away with [d4] ([d4 exd4 Nxd4 Bb4 Nxc6 bxc6]). Quietly playing [d3] or [O-O] keeps a pleasant, risk-free game where h6 stays slightly passive. [%csl Yh6,Yf8][%cal Gd2d4] }
5. d4 ( 5. d3 ) ( 5. O-O ) ) ) ( 4. d3
{ Choosing the quiet [d3] locks in a Giuoco Pianissimo structure where Black's early 3.[h6] is no longer a wasted move, since keeping pieces off g5 fits Black's defensive plan anyway. Black develops smoothly with [Nf6] or [Bc5], aiming for [b:d6] and short castling, or even [d6] followed by a kingside fianchetto with [b:g6] and [b:Bg7]. The game is level, with both sides manoeuvring behind intact pawn chains. [%cal Gg8f6,Gf8c5,Gd7d6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... Bc5 ) ( 4... d6 ) ) 4... exd4
{ Capturing on d4 opens the centre while Black has zero kingside pieces developed and only a pawn on h6 to show for move three. Recapturing [Nxd4] gives White a superior Scotch Game with the bishop already active on c4, leaving Black to untangle carefully via [Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4]. Castling [O-O] in Scotch Gambit fashion ([O-O Nf6 e5]) or offering [c3] for rapid open-file pressure are strong, aggressive alternatives. [%csl Yd4,Yh6,Yc4][%cal Gf3d4] }
( 4... d6 ) 5. Nxd4 ( 5. O-O ) ( 5. c3 ) ) ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... f5
{ The Rousseau Gambit, a King's Gambit idea with colours reversed. Black hits e4 at once, but weakens the diagonal from e8 to h5 and f7. [d4]! is the best answer: White opens the centre while Black's king is exposed, and after [b:exd4] [w:e5] White has a strong space advantage. [d3] is safe and good. The natural capture [w:exf5] lets Black play [b:e4] and equalise: it throws away White's edge. Most club players take, which is why the gambit scores well. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gd2d4,Gd2d3,Rb1c3] }
4. d4
{ White strikes at the centre. After [exd4] White plays [w:e5]! and the pawn on f5 shuts in Black's light bishop; White's space advantage is large, and the knight on g8 has no good square. [fxe4] is riskier: after [w:Nxe5] [b:d5] [w:Bb5] White's pieces swarm the centre. Either way the weakness of the diagonal from e8 to h5 shows, and White is clearly better. [%csl Yh5][%cal Ge5d4,Gf5e4,Ge4e5] }
( 4. d3
{ Declining the Rousseau Gambit with [d3] is a calm, reliable antidote: White holds e4 firmly, keeps the c4 bishop trained on the weakened a2-g8 diagonal, and stops Black from gaining space with an e-pawn push. Black's cleanest continuation is [Nf6], guarding d5 and h5 before choosing where to place the dark-squared bishop; [Bc5] eyes f2 actively, though White stands clearly better because Black's early f-pawn thrust prevents comfortable short castling. [%csl Ye4,Yc4,Ya2,Yg8][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... Bc5 ) ( 4... Be7 ) ) ( 4. Nc3 $4
{ Defending the pawn with the knight looks sound, but after [fxe4 Nxe4 d5] Black wins back the piece with a fork of the bishop and the knight, and takes the centre. White has given Black the game Black wanted. [d4] or [d3] was the move. }
4... fxe4 5. Nxe4 d5 ) ( 4. exf5
{ Accepting the Rousseau Gambit is the most common club choice, yet surrendering the e4 pawn lets Black seize central space with tempo. Pushing [e4] right away is the sharpest point of the gambit, kicking the f3 knight before White can stabilise the e4-h5 diagonal and equalising cleanly after [e4 Nd4 Nf6]. Striking in the middle with [d5] ([d5 Bb5 Bd6]) is playable too, though White keeps a slight pull. [%csl Ye4,Yf3,Yh5][%cal Ge5e4] }
4... e4
{ Black kicks the knight. [Nd4] is the active square, and after [b:Nf6] the game is level. [Ng1] is the safe retreat, but it is passive and Black gets an easy game with [b:d5]. Pinning the pawn with [w:Qe2] is a mistake: after [b:Qe7] the knight still has to go back, and Black has gained a tempo. White has to be careful of traps on the e-file and on h5. White would have done better with [w:d4] on the previous move, and must now play precisely just to keep the balance. [%cal Gf3d4,Gf3g1,Rd1e2] }
( 4... d5 ) 5. Nd4
{ The knight sits in the centre, and Black should develop with [Nf6], covering h5 and defending e4. Then Black equalises easily with [b:d5]. Taking the knight on d4 is a trap: the queen comes to h5 with check, and Black's open kingside falls. In the Rousseau, the square h5 is Black's weak point, and the knight on f6 guards it. [%csl Yh5][%cal Gg8f6,Rc6d4] }
( 5. Ng1 ) ( 5. Qe2 $2
{ Pinning the e-pawn with the queen looks clever, but the knight on f3 is still attacked. After [Qe7 Ng1 Nf6] the knight has gone home anyway, White's queen stands in front of Black's queen on the e-file, and Black develops with gain of time while White has nothing out but a bishop. Black is better. [w:Nd4], centralising the knight, was the move. }
5... Qe7 6. Ng1 Nf6 ) 5... Nf6 ( 5... Nxd4 $2
{ The capture seems to win a piece, since the knight on d4 looks undefended. But [Qh5+] opens Black's king: after [b:g6] [w:fxg6] the pawn threatens h7, and White's queen takes on d4 or e5 next. White regains the material with a winning attack against a king that has no pawns around it. [Nf6] kept h5 covered and the knight on c6 safe. }
6. Qh5+ ) ) 4... exd4 ( 4... fxe4 ) ) ( 3... Nd4
{ The Blackburne Shilling Gambit. The knight moves twice and leaves e5 without a defender, hoping for a trap. White's best is [Nxd4]! [b:exd4] and then [w:O-O] or [w:c3]: White leads in development and Black's pawn on d4 is a target. [O-O] is also good, and [w:c3] a little slower. Taking the e-pawn with the knight is exactly what Black hopes for. The name comes from a story that Blackburne won shillings with it in coffee houses; it is probably a legend. [%cal Gf3d4,Ge1g1,Rf3e5] }
4. Nxd4
{ When Black plays the Blackburne Shilling Gambit 3.[Nd4], capturing [Nxd4] is the simple refutation: instead of falling for the greedy [w:Nxe5] trap ([b:Qg5]), White trades off Black's twice-moved knight and keeps a clean lead in development. Black has to recapture [exd4], since leaving the knight untouched drops a full piece. Afterward White castles [w:O-O], plays [w:c3] or [w:d3], and attacks the overextended d4 pawn with a clear advantage. [%cal Ge5d4] }
( 4. O-O ) ( 4. Nxe5 $2
{ The pawn looks free. But [Qg5]! hits the knight on e5 and White's g-pawn together. White's best is [w:O-O] [b:Qxe5], when Black has the pawn back and a fine game; greedier moves lose more. Simply [Nxd4] would have left White clearly better. }
4... Qg5
{ Black's queen hits e5 and g2. White has to give up something: [Bxf7+] [b:Kd8] or [O-O] [b:Qxe5] are the best tries, and after either Black is better. The capture on f7 with the knight, forking the queen and the rook, is the famous mistake: the queen takes on g2, and the knight on d4 gives a smothered mate on f3. [%cal Gc4f7,Ge1g1,Re5f7,Rg5g2] }
5. Bxf7+ ( 5. O-O ) ( 5. Nxf7 $4
{ The knight forks the queen and rook, and it looks like White wins material. But [Qxg2 Rf1 Qxe4+ Be2 Nf3#] is a smothered mate: White's own pieces block the king. This is the trap that gave the gambit its fame. Castling, or the bishop check on f7, limited the damage. }
5... Qxg2 6. Rf1 Qxe4+ 7. Be2 Nf3# ) ) ( 4. Bxf7+ $2
{ Sacrificing on f7 looks tempting because [Kxf7 Nxe5+] recovers a second pawn with check, yet Black's d4 knight eyes c2 and turns the tables. After [Kxf7 Nxe5+ Ke8 Qh5+ g6 Nxg6 hxg6], snatching on h8 runs into a royal fork on c2, leaving Black ahead in material. Simply capture the loose knight with [Nxd4+]! [%csl Yf7,Yd4,Yc2][%cal Rc4f7,Ge8f7] }
4... Kxf7 5. Nxe5+ Ke8 6. Qh5+ g6 ) 4... exd4 ) 4. c3 ( 4. d3
{ White keeps the pawn on e4 defended and chooses a slow game. [Nf6] is the natural reply, and after [w:c3] the game is a Pianissimo; with [w:O-O] or [w:Nc3] White keeps other plans. [d6] and [h6] are also sound. This move order is popular with players who don't want to learn the Two Knights, since after [w:d3] there are fewer forcing lines. Black need not fear [w:Ng5]: with the knight coming to f6 or h6 and the king ready to castle, f7 is easy to hold, and the jump only loses White time. [%cal Gg8f6,Gd7d6,Gh7h6] }
4... Nf6
{ A quiet position. [c3] leads to the Pianissimo, [O-O] keeps options, and [Nc3] goes for a Four Knights set-up. The attack on f7 with [w:Ng5] is not dangerous now: Black castles, and if White takes on f7, two minor pieces for a rook and pawn favour Black. White's long-term plans are the same as in the Pianissimo: [w:Re1], [w:h3], the knight tour to g3, and [w:d4] when everything is ready. The knight jump is a habit from the Two Knights, where Black has no time to castle; here [b:O-O] answers it calmly. [%cal Gc2c3,Ge1g1,Gb1c3] }
( 4... d6
{ Both sides have played symmetrically into the Giuoco Pianissimo, supporting their e-pawns and opening the c1-h6 and c8-h3 diagonals. White's most purposeful plan starts with [c3], keeping Black's knight out of d4, giving the c4 bishop a retreat via b3 and c2, and preparing either [w:d4] or queenside expansion with [w:b4]. [O-O] and [a4], stopping [b:Na5] from chasing the bishop without a retreat square, are equally sound. [%cal Gc2c3,Ge1g1,Ga2a4] }
5. c3 ( 5. O-O ) ( 5. a4 ) ) ( 4... h6
{ Playing [h6] early stops [w:Bg5] and [w:Ng5] before Black commits the g8 knight or castles, though it delays central development by a tempo. White profits best with [c3], preparing [w:b4] and [w:d4] while Black's kingside knights and rooks are still on their home squares, or [O-O] to keep flexible Pianissimo plans with [w:Re1] and [w:Nbd2]. Challenging the active bishop directly with [Be3] is also completely sound. [%cal Gc2c3,Ge1g1,Gc1e3] }
5. c3 ( 5. O-O ) ( 5. Be3 ) ) ( 4... f5
{ The Lucchini Gambit: Black plays [b:f5] after [w:d3], hoping for quick play on the f-file. [Nc3] is the engines' choice: it develops and holds e4, and after [b:Nf6] or [b:d6] White is clearly better. [Ng5] is the sharpest answer, aiming at f7 and e6: after [b:f4] White's best is [w:h4]!, giving the knight a defender. Taking with [w:exf5] lets Black play [b:d5] and equalise. Black's main danger is the weak diagonal from g8 to a2, where White's bishop sits. [%cal Gb1c3,Gf3g5,Bc4g8] }
5. Nc3 ( 5. Ng5
{ White's knight eyes f7 and e6, and Black must keep them covered. [f4]! is the main line: it gains space and takes e3 from White's bishop, and the knight on g5 is suddenly short of squares: [b:h6] would trap it. White has to answer accurately. Other moves let White's knight and bishop break in on f7. With Black's f-pawn gone from f7, [w:Qh5+] also comes with check. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gf5f4,Rg8f6] }
5... f4
{ The knight on g5 is short of squares, and White must give it a defender: [h4]! is the best move. Black answers [b:h6], and the knight can go back to f3 after [w:Nf3]. The position is sharp but fine for White. Flashy moves on f7 fail here: the king steps to f8, and Black's [b:h6] traps the knight. The pawn on f4, guarded by the one on e5, also shuts in White's bishop on c1. [%csl Yg5][%cal Gh2h4,Rc4f7] }
( 5... Nh6 $4
{ The knight covers f7, which looks like the right idea, and it develops a piece. But it blocks nothing on the diagonal from e8 to h5, which the move [b:f5] opened. [Qh5+ Kf8 Nf7] forks the queen and the rook, and Black's position falls apart. [b:f4] was the move, gaining space and leaving White's knight short of squares. }
6. Qh5+ Kf8 7. Nf7 ) ( 5... Nf6 $2
{ A natural developing move, but it does nothing for f7: a knight on f6 doesn't guard it, and the queen can't reach it. [Nf7] forks the queen and the rook on h8. Black loses the exchange at least, and the king has nowhere to hide. [b:f4] was the only good move. }
6. Nf7 ) ( 5... d5 $2
{ The pawn break looks like the natural way to free Black's game, but it opens the diagonal from a2 to g8 for White's bishop. After [exd5 Na5 Bb5+] Black's king is stuck in the middle, the centre has disappeared, and White is a pawn up with the better pieces. Keep the pawns on e5 and f5 and play [b:f4]. }
6. exd5 Na5 7. Bb5+ ) 6. h4 ( 6. Bf7+ $2
{ The check looks strong, since it drives the king from e8. But [Kf8 h4 h6 Bxg8 hxg5] wins White's knight: the bishop has gone off to g8, and the pawn takes on g5. White has given away material and time, and Black's rook gets the h-file. [h4+] first was the move, giving the knight a defender. }
6... Kf8 7. h4 h6 8. Bxg8 hxg5 ) ( 6. Qh5+ $2
{ The queen check seems to break in on the weakened kingside. But after [g6 Bf7+ Kf8 Bxg6 hxg6] White's queen is attacked by the rook on h8, and White has lost a bishop for two pawns, with no attack left. [h4+] keeps the knight safe and the position balanced. }
6... g6 7. Bf7+ Kf8 8. Bxg6 hxg6 ) ) ) 5. c3 ( 5. O-O ) ( 5. Nc3 ) ( 5. Bg5
{ Pinning the knight with [Bg5] before Black has castled is very popular in club play: White threatens to jump [Nc3] and [Nd5] against the pinned f6 defender. Putting the question to the bishop immediately with [h6] is completely sound here ([h6 Bh4 d6] or [h6 Be3 Bxe3 fxe3]), while [d6] prepares [Na5] or [Be6] to neutralise White's light-squared bishop. Castling [O-O] is playable too, provided Black watches out for [Nc3] and [Nd5]. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gh7h6] }
5... h6 ( 5... d6 ) ( 5... O-O ) ) ( 5. Ng5
{ Jumping to g5 is a reflex from the Two Knights Defence, where Black's f8 bishop is still at home, but once the bishop already stands on c5 the raid misfires. Black simply castles [O-O], defending f7 with the rook while gaining development time. Trading [O-O Nxf7 Rxf7 Bxf7+ Kxf7] gives Black two active pieces against a rook plus one pawn, so White usually ends up retreating the knight after [b:h6] having wasted two moves. [%cal Ge8g8] }
5... O-O ) ) ( 4. O-O
{ White castles first. [Nf6] is the main move: after [w:d3] or [w:c3] it reaches the Pianissimo, and after [w:d4] it is the Deutz gambit, which Black meets with [b:Bxd4]. [d6] is also sound and holds e5. An early [b:Nge7] is passive: it shuts in the queen, and the knight belongs on f6, where it hits e4. White's castled king also means Black can castle without fear of the fishing-pole tricks. [%cal Gg8f6,Gd7d6,Gf1e1] }
4... Nf6
