7. a4 ) ( 7. h3 ) ) ( 4. b4
{ The Evans Gambit. White offers the b-pawn to play [w:c3] and [w:d4] with gain of time on the bishop, and the bishop on c1 may later come to a3 against Black's king. [Bxb4] accepts and is the critical test. [Bb6] declines safely, and then [w:a4] threatens to trap the bishop with [w:a5]. Captain William Evans, a Welsh sailor, first played it in 1827, and Kasparov brought it back in the 1990s, beating Anand with it. [%cal Gb4c5,Gc1a3] }
4... Bxb4
{ Black has taken the pawn, and [c3] chases the bishop with gain of time. White's plan is [w:d4] next, opening the centre while Black's king is still in the middle, and then [w:O-O], [w:Qb3] and [w:Ba3]. Black chooses where the bishop goes: a5 keeps the pin on c3, e7 is the safest, and c5 is the old choice, now known to lose time. White must not delay: without [c3] and [w:d4] the gambit is just a pawn down. [%cal Gc2c3,Gd2d4,Gc1a3] }
