{ The quiet Four Knights' Italian, played in millions of games. [Bg5] pins the knight before Black has castled, and after [b:h6] [w:Bxf6] [b:Qxf6] [w:Nd5] White gains a tempo on the queen. [h3] keeps Black's bishop off g4. [Na4] is the engines' favourite: it goes after the bishop on c5, Black's most active piece, and after [b:Bb6] [w:Nxb6] White has the bishop pair. [O-O] is also fine. Black aims for [b:Be6], [b:h6] and [b:O-O], or [b:Na5] against the bishop on c4. The jump [w:Ng5] is still premature: Black castles, and f7 is safe. [%cal Gc1g5,Gc3a4,Gh2h3,Ge1g1] }
( 5... O-O
{ Castling [b:O-O] before playing [b:d6] or [b:h6] gets Black's king tucked away quickly, but it allows White's favourite pin in the Four Knights Italian: [Bg5]. Because White has not castled yet, [Bg5 h6 Bh4] can even be backed up by an aggressive kingside plan if Black is careless, while [w:Nd5] threatens to pile onto the pinned f6 knight. [h3] to prevent [b:Bg4] first and [O-O] for quiet symmetry are good alternatives. [%cal Gc1g5,Gh2h3,Ge1g1] }
