{ Black's best plan is [Nh5]!, heading for f4, where the knight presses on d3, g2 and h3. It looks loose on h5, but the trick [w:Nxe5] [b:Nxe5] [w:Qxh5] fails to [b:Nxd3], a fork of the rook and the bishop. Black follows with [b:Qf6], and after [w:Nf1] the knight lands on f4. White usually answers with [w:Be3] to trade the bishop on a7. Many top games go this way, and most of them are drawn. [%cal Gf6h5,Gh5f4,Gd8f6] }
10... Nh5 ) ( 6... h6
{ A typical Pianissimo. Black's [b:h6] keeps White's pieces off g5, and White now goes on with its slow plan: [Re1], [Nbd2]-f1-g3, [h3], and [a4] or [w:Bb3] against [b:Na5]. [w:b4] is also played, gaining space against the bishop on c5. Black answers with [b:O-O], [b:a6] and [b:Ba7], and later [b:Be6] or the knight to e7 and g6. Neither side has a weakness, so the game turns on timing: White wants [w:d4] when Black's pieces are badly placed, and Black wants [b:d5] once White's knights have left the centre. [%cal Gf1e1,Gb1d2,Ga2a4,Gh2h3] }
7. Re1
{ The rook protects e4 and prepares [w:d4]. [O-O] is the main move, and [a6] prepares [b:Ba7], out of reach of the d-pawn. [Bg4] pins the knight, but after [w:Nbd2] and [w:h3] the bishop has to decide where it goes. The knight jump [Ng4] is sound here: with the rook gone from f1, the knight and the bishop on c5 both hit f2, and White has to step back with [w:Rf1] or cover it with [w:Re2]. The game stays balanced, and the plans are those of every Pianissimo. [%csl Yf2][%cal Ge8g8,Ga7a6,Gf6g4] }
( 7. Nbd2
{ White keeps building, with [w:Re1] and [w:Nf1] to come. Black can castle now: with White's king already on g1, [O-O] is safe, and the pawn on h6 keeps g5 covered. [a6], preparing [b:Ba7], is just as good, and [Bg4] is playable but lets [w:h3] gain time. Black's usual plan is [b:Be6] to trade White's light bishop, the knight to e7 and g6, and the break [b:d5] once White's knights have left the centre. Neither side is in a hurry here. [%cal Ge8g8,Ga7a6,Gd2f1] }
