7... Bg4 ) ( 7... Ng4 ) ) ( 6... Bb6 ) ( 6... Be6
{ Black offers a trade of bishops. White should not take: [w:Bxe6] [b:fxe6] doubles Black's pawns, but it opens the f-file for Black's rook, and the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5, the very squares White's knights want. [Bb3] keeps the bishop; if Black takes on b3, [w:axb3] opens the a-file for White's rook. [Nbd2] is also good. In the Pianissimo the light bishops are often the most important pieces, so trading them is a decision, not a habit. [%csl Ye6][%cal Gc4b3,Gb1d2] }
7. Bb3 ( 7. Nbd2 ) ) 7. Re1
{ Both kings are castled, and White's rook protects e4. Black has several sound plans: [h6] keeps g5 covered, [a6] gives the bishop a7, and [Ng4] hits f2 now that the rook has left f1. [Bg4] is the most played and also fine, pinning the knight before [w:Nbd2] and [w:h3] come. [b:Be6], offering to trade light bishops, is a fourth good choice. White's plan is [w:Nbd2]-f1-g3 and [w:h3], and in time [w:d4] or a kingside attack. [%csl Yf2][%cal Gh7h6,Ga7a6,Gf6g4] }
( 7. h3 ) ( 7. Bb3 ) ( 7. a4
{ White's [w:a4] has a sharp point: [w:b4] next hits the bishop, and after it retreats to b6, [w:a5] traps it, since the pawn on b4 covers c5 and Black's own pawn sits on a7. Black must make room at once: [a5] stops [w:b4], [a6] gives the bishop a7, and [Bb6] steps back before the pawns arrive. Most other moves, however natural, lose material: after [w:b4] Black has to give a piece for two pawns, as in [w:b4] [b:Nxb4] [w:cxb4] [b:Bxb4]. [%cal Ga7a5,Ga7a6,Gc5b6,Rb2b4] }
7... a5 ( 7... a6 ) ( 7... Bb6 ) ( 7... h6 $2
{ A useful move against [w:Bg5] and [w:Ng5], and one Black plays in almost every Pianissimo. But here [b4] hits the bishop, and it has no square: on b6 it is trapped by [w:a5]. After [b4 Nxb4 cxb4 Bxb4] Black has two pawns for the knight. [b:a5] or [a6] first was needed. }
8. b4 Nxb4 9. cxb4 Bxb4 ) ( 7... Bg4 $2
{ Pinning the knight is a normal plan, but it ignores White's threat. [b4] hits the bishop, which would be trapped on b6 by [w:a5], so Black has to give up a piece: after [b4 Nxb4 cxb4 Bxb4] Black has two pawns for it. [b:a5] or [a6] first was needed. }
8. b4 Nxb4 9. cxb4 Bxb4 ) ( 7... Be6 $2
{ Offering a trade of bishops is normal in the Pianissimo, but here it costs a tempo against the threat. After [Bxe6 fxe6 b4] the bishop on c5 has no good square, and Black loses material; in some lines White's queen even checks on e6. [b:a5] or [a6] first was needed. }
8. Bxe6 fxe6 9. b4 ) ) ( 7. Nbd2
{ White's knight starts its tour. [a6] with [b:Ba7] to follow is the engines' choice, and [h6] and [a5] are just as good. [Be6] offers to trade White's light bishop: after [w:Bxe6] [b:fxe6] the f-file opens for Black's rook and the pawn on e6 covers d5. [b:Bg4] is the most played; after [w:h3] [b:Bh5] the pin lasts, but the bishop is out of play on the edge. In every line White goes on with [w:Re1], [w:Nf1] and [w:Ng3], and the game turns on who breaks in the centre first. [%cal Ga7a6,Gh7h6,Gc8e6,Gd2f1] }
