[Event "Italian Game (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 ( 3... Be7
{ The Hungarian Defence is solid: Black stops every [w:Ng5] idea and keeps the game closed. But it is passive, and [d4]! gives White an easy edge. After [b:d6] White can trade into a better endgame, or push [w:d5] for space. [O-O] and [d3] are fine too, but let Black equalise more easily with [b:Nf6], [b:d6] and [b:O-O]. The opening was played in a correspondence match between Budapest and Paris in the 1840s. [%csl Ye7][%cal Gd2d4,Ge1g1,Gd2d3] }
4. d4
{ Black chooses between holding the centre and giving it up. [d6] keeps a pawn on e5: after [w:dxe5] [b:dxe5] [w:Qxd8+] [b:Bxd8] Black's bishop is passive and White leads in development. [exd4] gives White a free hand in the centre after [w:c3] or [w:Nxd4], though Black's position is solid. Either way White keeps a pleasant edge, which is why the Hungarian is rare at top level. [%cal Gd7d6,Ge5d4,Rg8f6] }
( 4. O-O ) ( 4. d3 ) 4... d6
{ White has two good ways. [dxe5] [b:dxe5] [w:Qxd8+] [b:Bxd8] leaves Black's bishop on a poor square and White ahead in development after [w:Nc3], [w:Be3] and long castling. [d5] gains space and chases the knight; then [w:Bd3], [w:c4] and [w:Nc3] give White a big queenside bind, as in a King's Indian with colours reversed. Both lines keep a small, lasting edge. [%csl Yd8][%cal Gd4e5,Gd4d5] }
( 4... exd4 5. c3 Nf6
{ Black hits e4, and White's best is to kick the knight at once with [e5]: after [b:Ne4] [w:Bd5] the knight on e4 is short of squares, and White keeps a pleasant edge. Taking back with [w:cxd4] is weaker here, as [b:Nxe4] [w:d5] gives Black active play for the centre. Black's king is still on e8 and the bishop on e7 does little, so White wants lines open quickly: [w:O-O] and [w:Re1] come next, and Black must find the right knight move. [%csl Yf6][%cal Ge4e5,Gc4d5] }
6. e5
{ The pawn hits the knight. [Ne4]! is the move: the knight takes a central post, and after [w:Bd5] [b:f5] it stays there, with [b:d6] to follow. Black is fine. The other answers go wrong. [b:Ng4] is the most played, but after [w:cxd4] the knight is out of play and Black is cramped. The central counter [b:d5] is the classical answer to [w:e5], but here it loses a piece, as the bishop on e7 hangs once the knight is taken. [%cal Gf6e4,Rf6g4,Rd7d5] }
6... Ne4
{ The knight sits in the centre. [Bd5] hits it at once, and after [b:f5] [w:O-O] or [w:cxd4] White keeps a small edge, with the pawn on e5 cramping Black. [cxd4] first is the most played and also good, and it invites [b:Bb4+]. Castling straight away gives Black time for [b:d5] or [b:d6]. White should not attack the knight with the queen from e2: Black hits back with [b:d5], takes on d6 en passant and the knight lands on d6 with tempo on the bishop. [%cal Gc4d5,Gc3d4,Rd1e2] }
( 6... Ng4 $6
{ The knight hits e5 and f2 from g4, which looks active, and half the players here go there. But after [cxd4 d5 Bb3] White has a big centre, the knight on g4 has no future, and [w:h3] will chase it again. Black is clearly worse. [b:Ne4] was the move. }
7. cxd4 d5 8. Bb3 ) ( 6... d5 $2
{ Striking at the bishop is the classical answer to the pawn on e5. But here the knight is still hanging: after [exf6 dxc4 fxe7 Qxe7+] White has taken two pieces for one, and Black's extra pawn won't make up for it. Black is a piece down for a pawn. [b:Ne4] was the move. }
7. exf6 dxc4 8. fxe7 Qxe7+ ) ( 6... Ng8 $2
{ Pulling the attacked knight home to g8 looks safe, yet undeveloping Black's only kingside defender leaves f7 fatally weak now that e5 cramps the board. [Qb3] hits f7 and b7, forcing [Qb3 Kf8 Bxf7] with a wrecked king and lost pawn. Jump the knight forward into the middle on e4 instead. [%csl Yg8][%cal Rd1b3] }
7. Qb3 Kf8 8. Bxf7 Na5 9. Qa4 ) 7. Bd5 ( 7. cxd4 ) ( 7. Qe2 $2
{ The queen attacks the knight a second time, which looks natural. But Black hits back in the centre: after [d5 exd6 Nxd6] the knight attacks the bishop on c4, White's e-pawn is gone, and Black is a pawn up with easy development. [Bd5] kept the knight under fire. }
7... d5 8. exd6 Nxd6 ) ) ( 4... Nf6 $4
{ Developing [Nf6] without resolving the tension on e5 drops a clean central pawn with a devastating follow-up! After [dxe5 Ng4 Qd5], White's queen threatens Scholar's Mate on f7, forcing [dxe5 Ng4 Qd5 O-O h3] where the g4 knight is driven to the rim a pawn down. Play [exd4] or [d6] instead. [%csl Ye5,Yf7,Yg4][%cal Rg8f6,Gd4e5] }
5. dxe5 Ng4 6. Qd5 O-O 7. h3 ) 5. dxe5 ( 5. d5 ) ) ( 3... d6
{ The Semi-Italian. Black holds e5 with a pawn but blocks the bishop on f8. White takes the centre with [d4], or with [c3] and [w:d4]. [Nc3] is also good and sets up a famous trap: after [b:Bg4] [w:h3] [b:Bh5] the knight takes on e5, and if Black takes the queen, it is mate. If Black develops with [b:Nf6] at the wrong moment, [w:Ng5] hits f7. Black is solid but passive, and White keeps the easier game. [%csl Yf8][%cal Gd2d4,Gc2c3,Gb1c3] }
4. d4
{ Pushing [d4] challenges Black's passive 3.[d6] directly, threatening to gain space with [w:d5] or trade queens after [w:dxe5] if Black plays carelessly. [exd4] is the clearest reply: after [exd4 Nxd4] Black develops [b:Nf6] and [b:Be7] in a Philidor-like structure where White has freer pieces and more space, but Black's camp has no weaknesses. [Nf6] is playable too, provided Black meets [Nf6 d5] by retreating the knight to b8 or e7. [%cal Ge5d4,Gg8f6] }
( 4. c3
{ Supporting d4 with [c3] prepares to build an ideal two-pawn centre while keeping the d1-b3 diagonal open for the queen to eye f7 and b7. Because Black's bishop on f8 is already hemmed in behind d6, Black must choose carefully: [Be7] develops solidly (though [Be7 Qb3] asks an immediate question of f7), [h6] rules out [Bg5] before [Nf6], and [f5] strikes back in Rousseau style while c3 is occupied. [%csl Yd4,Yd1,Yb3,Yf7][%cal Gf8e7] }
4... Be7 ( 4... h6 ) ( 4... f5 ) ) ( 4. Nc3
{ Black has to be careful with f7. [Bg4] pins the knight, and [Be7] is solid. Developing the knight to f6 looks natural, but it allows [w:Ng5], hitting f7 twice, and the usual defence [b:d5] now costs Black a second tempo, because the pawn already stopped on d6. After [Bg4] the pin is the start of Legal's trap, and Black must know which retreat is safe. [%cal Gc8g4,Gf8e7,Rg8f6] }
4... Bg4
{ The bishop pins the knight to the queen. White's best is [h3], asking the bishop where it goes. If it takes on f3, White recaptures with the queen and has the bishop pair. If it retreats to h5, the pin looks to hold, but then the knight takes on e5 anyway, Legal's trap. [d3] is also good. Taking on e5 at once, before [h3], does not work: the bishop on g4 is still protected. [%cal Gh2h3,Gd2d3,Rf3e5] }
( 4... Be7 ) ( 4... Nf6 $6
{ A natural developing move, but with the knight on f6 the queen no longer guards g5, and the pawn on d6 does nothing for f7. [Ng5] hits it twice, and after [Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+] Black has the Two Knights' main line a tempo down: the d-pawn took two moves to reach d5, and White's knight on c3 is already out. White keeps the pawn with an easy game. Black should cover f7 first with [b:Be7], or meet [w:Nc3] with the fork trick [b:Nxe4]. }
5. Ng5 d5 6. exd5 Na5 7. Bb5+ ) 5. h3
{ The bishop has to decide. [Bxf3] gives White the bishop pair after [w:Qxf3], but it is safe. [Be6] is also playable, offering a trade on e6. Keeping the pin with [b:Bh5] is the trap: [w:Nxe5]! breaks it, because if Black takes the queen, White's minor pieces give mate, and if Black takes the knight, the queen takes the loose bishop on h5. Black loses at least a pawn. [%cal Gg4f3,Gg4e6,Rg4h5] }
( 5. d3 ) ( 5. Nxe5 $4
{ White hopes for [b:Bxd1], which allows mate, and the trap is well known, so the move is tempting. But Black simply plays [Nxe5], and the bishop on g4 is protected by the knight: if [w:Qxg4], [b:Nxg4]. White has lost a piece. The trick only works once the bishop stands on h5, undefended, after [h3]. }
5... Nxe5 ) 5... Bxf3 ( 5... Be6 ) ( 5... Bh5 $2
{ The pin looks like it still holds, but [Nxe5]! ignores it. If Black takes the queen, Legal's mate follows with the bishop and knights. If Black takes the knight, [w:Qxh5] wins the bishop back with a pawn more. Black loses at least a pawn and has a worse position. Play [Bxf3] or [Be6] instead. }
6. Nxe5
{ White has offered the queen. Black must not take it: Legal's mate follows. Black should give back a pawn instead. [dxe5] or [Nxe5] lets White's queen take on h5, and Black is only a pawn down, with chances to fight. This trap is named after Legall de Kermeur, an 18th-century master at the Café de la Régence in Paris, who is said to have played it. Its pattern, a pinned knight that moves anyway, comes up again and again. [%cal Gd6e5,Gc6e5,Rh5d1] }
6... dxe5 ( 6... Nxe5 ) ( 6... Bxd1 $4
{ Black wins the queen, but [Bxf7+ Ke7 Nd5#] is mate: the bishop on f7 and the two knights cover every square around the king, and Black's own pieces block the rest. A queen offered for nothing usually hides a combination. [dxe5] or [Nxe5] loses only a pawn. }
7. Bxf7+ Ke7 8. Nd5# ) ) ) ( 4. d3
{ Answering the Semi-Italian 3.[d6] with [d3] declines the chance to seize the centre with [w:d4] and lets Black solve the cramped f8 bishop comfortably. [Na5] is a clean equaliser, hunting down White's active Italian bishop on c4 before White has created a2 or b3 escape squares with [w:c3] or [w:a3]. [Be7] followed by [b:Nf6] and [b:O-O], or [h6] to rule out [w:Ng5] first, also give Black an easy game. [%cal Gc6a5,Gh7h6,Gf8e7] }
4... Na5 ( 4... h6 ) ( 4... Be7 ) ) ( 4. O-O
{ Castling [O-O] keeps White's central pawns flexible for [w:c3] and [w:d4], while warning Black that careless moves like an unguarded [b:Nf6] would walk straight into [w:Ng5] hitting f7. Black must attend to kingside safety and that diagonal first: [Be7] prepares [b:Nf6] and [b:O-O] cleanly, [Bg4] pins the f3 knight before White plays [w:h3], and [Be6] challenges White's active Italian bishop on c4 right away to ease the defensive burden. [%cal Gf8e7,Gc8g4,Gc8e6] }
4... Be7 ( 4... Bg4 ) ( 4... Be6 ) ) 4... exd4 ( 4... Nf6 ) ) 4. c3
{ White prepares [w:d4], and the pawn on c3 will recapture there to build a full centre. Black must use the tempo. [Nf6] develops and hits e4, forcing White to commit at once. [d6] holds e5 firmly but lets White build the centre unopposed. The old [b:Qe7] keeps the e-pawn too, but the engines find it clearly passive. Waiting moves are not good here: [w:d4] then comes with gain of time against the bishop on c5, or [w:b4] grabs space on the queenside before Black has developed. [%cal Gg8f6,Gf6e4,Gd7d6] }
( 4. O-O Nf6 5. d4
{ The Deutz gambit. White offers a pawn to open lines. [Bxd4]! is the best answer: after [w:Nxd4] [b:Nxd4] [w:f4] [b:d6] Black keeps a sound extra pawn. [exd4] leads, after [w:e5] [b:d5], to the Max Lange Attack, which is fine for Black too. Taking with the knight is a mistake: [w:Nxe5] hits f7 twice, and Black loses the right to castle. White's compensation in all lines is development and the open f-file. [%cal Gc5d4,Ge5d4,Rc6d4] }
5... Bxd4
{ Black has taken with the bishop, and White must recapture: [Nxd4] [b:Nxd4], and then [w:f4] or [w:Bg5] tries for an attack on the f-file. Black is a pawn up and should answer [w:f4] with [b:d6]!. White's compensation is the open f-file and Black's missing dark bishop. Playing for the centre with [w:c3] instead of recapturing just leaves White a piece down for a while, and in the end a pawn down with nothing to show for it. [%cal Gf3d4,Gf2f4,Rc2c3] }
( 5... exd4 ) ( 5... Nxd4 $2
{ The knight takes the pawn and attacks the knight on f3, so it looks like the most active capture. But White answers [Nxe5]!, hitting f7 twice. After [Nxe5 Qe7 Bxf7+ Kf8 Nd3] Black has lost the right to castle and the pawn, and White's pieces are active against Black's king. Take with the bishop instead: [Bxd4], and the knight on f3 has to recapture. }
6. Nxe5 Qe7 7. Bxf7+ Kf8 8. Nd3 ) 6. Nxd4
{ White has taken back on d4. Black recaptures with the knight, [Nxd4], and is a pawn up: the knight stands well in the centre, and after [w:Bg5] or [w:f4] White has only the open f-file and Black's missing dark bishop for the pawn. Taking with the pawn instead lets White push [w:e5] at once: the knight on f6 is kicked, and after [b:d5] [w:exf6] [b:dxc4] [w:fxg7] the extra pawn is gone and Black's king has no safe home. [%cal Gc6d4,Re5d4] }
( 6. c3 $2
{ White plays for a big centre and hopes to win the bishop back later. But after [Bb6] Black keeps the extra pawn and the bishop, White's pawn on d4 is gone, and the knight on f3 faces the pawn on e5 alone. The position offers no compensation. [Nxd4] regains material first. }
6... Bb6 ) 6... Nxd4
{ White is a pawn down after the trades on d4, and has two ways to use the lead in development. [f4] opens the f-file against f7, and Black must answer [b:d6]! [Bg5] pins the knight on f6; after [b:h6] [w:Bh4] the pin stays, and Black chooses between [b:d6] and the sharp [b:g5]. In both lines Black's knight on d4 is strong and Black is better with care, but one natural move can lose at once, and that is White's hope. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc1g5] }
( 6... exd4 $2
{ The pawn takes back, which looks like the natural recapture. But [e5] hits the knight, and after [e5 d5 exf6] and [b:dxc4] [w:fxg7] the extra pawn is gone and Black's king has lost its cover, while White's queen comes to h5. [Nxd4] kept the knight safe and the extra pawn. }
7. e5 d5 8. exf6 ) 7. Bg5
{ The bishop pins the knight on f6 and prepares [w:f4], opening the f-file against it. [h6] asks the bishop at once: after [w:Bh4] Black chooses between [b:d6] and [b:g5]. [d6] first is also good: after [w:f4] [b:Bg4]! the pins cross, and a long series of captures swaps the queens off. [b:Ne6] is a solid third way, trading the knight for the bishop that pins it. Black is a pawn up in all of these lines and only has to stay alert to [w:f4]. [%cal Gh7h6,Gd7d6,Rf2f4] }
( 7. f4
{ White opens the f-file against f7. [d6]! is the only good reply: it holds e5 with a pawn and frees the bishop on c8. After [w:fxe5] [b:dxe5] [w:Bg5] [b:Qe7] Black is a pawn up and safe, and the engines prefer Black. A knight retreat instead allows [w:Bxf7+] and [w:fxe5], when the f-file opens on Black's king and the knight on f6 is pinned. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gd7d6,Rc4f7] }
7... d6 ( 7... Nc6 $2
{ The knight goes back to defend e5, which looks safe and solid. But [Bxf7+]! drags the king out, and after [Bxf7+ Kxf7 fxe5] the knight on f6 is attacked and pinned on the f-file. White wins back the piece with interest. [d6] kept e5 solid with a pawn and left f7 guarded by the king. }
8. Bxf7+ Kxf7 9. fxe5 ) ) 7... h6 ( 7... d6
{ Black holds e5 before kicking the bishop. White goes on with [f4]: after [b:Bg4]! the pins cross, and the captures [w:Bxf6] [b:Bxd1] [w:Bxd8] swap the queens off into an endgame where Black keeps the extra pawn. Black also does well with [b:Be6] or [b:Qe7], keeping the pieces on. The knight on d4 is the best piece on the board, and White can't easily dislodge it, as [w:c3] costs time and the knight hops to e6 with tempo on the bishop. [%csl Bd4][%cal Gf2f4,Bc8g4] }
8. f4
{ The f-file is about to open. [Bg4] is the sharpest answer, pinning back and leading to a queen trade. [Be6] trades off White's bishop on c4, the piece that eyes f7. [Qe7] guards the knight on f6 a second time and puts the queen on the e-file, so that [w:fxe5] [b:dxe5] keeps the centre closed: then White keeps the initiative with [w:f5] or [w:Nc3]. In every line Black's knight stays on d4, and Black is a pawn up with a sound game as long as the f-file stays closed. [%cal Gc8g4,Gc8e6,Gd8e7] }
8... Bg4 ( 8... Be6 ) ( 8... Qe7
{ Black has guarded the knight on f6 twice and put the queen on the e-file. White keeps the initiative with [f5], which cramps the bishop on c8 and takes e6 from the knight, or develops with [Nc3]. Opening the f-file at once lets Black's pawn recapture on e5 and the tension is gone. Chasing the knight on d4 with the c-pawn is slower than it looks: the knight hops to e6, hits the bishop on g5, and trades off White's best attacker. [%cal Gf4f5,Gb1c3,Rc2c3] }
9. f5 ( 9. Nc3 ) ( 9. c3 $2
{ The pawn chases the knight on d4, which looks logical. But the knight has a fine retreat: after [Ne6 Bxe6 Qxe6] White's bishop on c4 is gone, and after [w:f5] [b:Qc4] Black's queen hits e4 and Black stays a pawn up. [f5], taking e6 from the knight first, was the move. }
9... Ne6 10. Bxe6 Qxe6 ) ) ) 8. Bh4
{ The bishop keeps the pin. [d6] calmly holds e5 and prepares [b:Bg4] or [b:Qe7], and Black is a pawn up with a solid game: it is the engines' choice. [g5], the most played move, breaks the pin by force but is riskier: after [w:f4]! White gives the bishop to open the f-file, and Black must find one of the two good answers, [b:gxh4] or [b:d5]. The natural captures with the other pawns, or [b:d6] there, lose. In club games, this is where the line is decided. [%cal Gd7d6,Gg7g5,Rf2f4] }
8... d6 ( 8... g5
{ Black has broken the pin, and White's bishop is attacked. [f4]! is the move: White leaves the bishop and opens the f-file, and after [b:d5] [w:exd5] [b:gxh4] [w:fxe5] White's rook and queen join in fast, with the game roughly level. Retreating the bishop to g3 is too slow: Black's kingside pawns have gained space with tempo, the knight on d4 stays, and Black is a sound pawn up. [%cal Gf2f4,Rg5h4] }
9. f4
{ White has given the bishop on h4 to open the f-file. Black should take it with [gxh4], or first play [d5] to open lines for Black's own pieces: after [w:exd5] [b:gxh4] [w:fxe5] [b:Nxd5] [w:Qxd4] the game is level. Everything else loses. Taking on f4 with either pawn, the slow [b:d6] and the grab on e4 all open the f-file onto the knight on f6 and f7 while Black's king has no shelter, and White's queen joins the attack from d4. [%csl Rf4][%cal Gg5h4,Gd7d5] }
( 9. Bg3 $2
{ The bishop retreats to safety, which looks careful. But Black's pawn on g5 has gained space for free, and after [d6 f3 Ne6] Black is a pawn up, the knight heads for f4, and White has no attack left. White had to strike with [w:f4] while Black's kingside was loose. }
9... d6 10. f3 Ne6 ) 9... gxh4 ( 9... d5 ) ( 9... gxf4 $4
{ Taking the pawn looks natural, and nearly a quarter of the players here do it. But after [Rxf4 exf4 Qxd4] White wins the knight on d4, and the knight on f6 is pinned against the rook on h8: [w:Bxf6] wins more. White comes out far ahead. [b:gxh4] or [b:d5] was the move. }
10. Rxf4 exf4 11. Qxd4 ) ( 9... exf4 $4
{ Taking with the e-pawn keeps the g-pawn in place, but it gives up the knight on d4's support: after [Qxd4] White's queen takes it and lines up on the long diagonal against f6 and the rook on h8, with the bishop on h4 still pinning. White's attack is crushing. [b:gxh4] or [b:d5] was the move. }
10. Qxd4 ) ( 9... d6 $4
{ Holding e5 with the pawn looks solid, but it leaves the pawn on g5 hanging: after [fxg5 Bg4 Qe1] White takes on f6 next and the f-file opens onto f7. Black's raid on c2 wins only a rook while White's attack decides. [b:gxh4] or [b:d5] was the move. }
10. fxg5 Bg4 11. Qe1 ) ( 9... Nxe4 $2
{ The knight grabs a pawn and leaves the pin, which looks clever. But after [fxe5 d5 Qxd4] White's queen takes the knight on d4, the bishop on h4 is safe for now, and Black's king is exposed on the open f-file. White is clearly better. [b:gxh4] or [b:d5] was the move. }
10. fxe5 d5 11. Qxd4 ) ) ) ( 4. b4 Bxb4 ( 4... Bb6
{ The Evans declined. The bishop is safe on b6, and White's extra queenside space is the only gain. [a4] threatens [w:a5], trapping the bishop, so Black answers [b:a6] or [b:a5]. [b5] kicks the knight: it goes to a5 or d4, and the play becomes sharp. Black should not take on b4 now that it can be met by [w:a5]. Declining is a good practical choice against a player who knows the gambit well. [%csl Yb6][%cal Ga2a4,Gb4b5] }
5. a4
{ White threatens [w:a5], and the bishop on b6 would be lost. Black gives it a7 with [a6], or stops the pawn with [a5]. After [a6] [w:Nc3] [b:Nf6] [w:Nd5] White trades off the bishop or gains space, and the knight on d5 is strong. After [a5] [w:b5] the knight goes to d4, with a calm game. Taking the pawn on b4 now is a mistake: the a-pawn chases the bishop. [%cal Ga7a6,Ga7a5,Ra4a5,Rc6b4] }
( 5. b5
{ The knight is attacked. [Na5] hits the bishop on c4, and after [w:Nxe5] Black answers with [b:Nh6] or [b:Qg5], with chances for both sides. [Nd4] is the other square, Nimzowitsch's move, but after [w:Nxd4] [b:Bxd4] [w:c3] White gains time on the bishop and keeps a small edge. The knight must not go to e7, where it blocks Black's pieces and drops the e-pawn. In these lines the knight on the rim is not a problem, because it gains time on White's bishop. [%cal Gc6a5,Gc6d4,Rc6e7] }
5... Na5
{ Black's knight hits the bishop. White's right answer is [Nxe5], taking a pawn and threatening f7. Black replies [b:Nh6], covering f7, or [b:Qg5], hitting e5 and g2. Then White plays [w:d4] or [w:Be2], and the game is balanced. The flashy capture on f7 with the bishop instead throws away material: the king takes, and the knight check on e5 does not win anything back. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gf3e5,Rc4f7] }
( 5... Nce7 $2
{ The knight looks safe on e7, but it leaves e5 without a defender. [Nxe5] wins the pawn, and after [Nxe5 d5 exd5] White is a pawn up with the better game; the knight on e7 even blocks Black's own bishop and queen. [Na5] kept the knight active. }
6. Nxe5 d5 7. exd5 ) ( 5... Nd4 6. Nxe5 Qg5
{ Black's queen hits e5 and g2 together. White's best is [Bxf7+], and after [b:Kf8] the game is sharp: White's bishop must go back, and Black's queen takes on e5 or g2. Taking on f7 with the knight to fork the king's rook is a famous trap. Black's queen takes on g2 and mates with the knight on f3, the same picture as in the Blackburne Shilling Gambit. [%cal Gc4f7,Re5f7,Rg5g2] }
7. Bxf7+ ( 7. Nxf7 $4
{ The knight forks the queen and the rook, and it looks like a big win. But [Qxg2 Rf1 Qxe4+ Be2 Nf3#] is a smothered mate: White's own pieces block the king's escape. Before taking material, check what the opponent's queen can reach, and which squares around your own king are already blocked. The bishop check on f7 was the move. }
7... Qxg2 8. Rf1 Qxe4+ 9. Be2 Nf3# ) ) 6. Nxe5
{ White has taken e5 and hits f7 twice. [Nh6]! is the best defence: the knight guards f7, and after [w:d4] [b:d6] Black regains the pawn. Other natural moves lose: [d6] and [Qe7] allow the bishop sacrifice on f7, and the check on f2 gives a piece for nothing. This is the most forcing line of the declined Evans, and one careless move here costs Black the game. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gg8h6,Rc4f7] }
( 6. Bxf7+ $2
{ A sacrifice that looks forcing, but after [Kxf7 Nxe5+ Kf8] White has given a bishop for two pawns, and Black's king is safe on f8. Black is a piece up, and the knight on a5 is no longer attacking anything that matters. [Nxe5+] was the move. }
6... Kxf7 7. Nxe5+ Kf8 ) 6... Nh6
{ Black's knight guards f7, and White's bishop on c4 is attacked by the knight on a5. [Be2] is the best retreat, keeping the bishop safe; after [b:d6] [w:Nf3] the game is level. Pushing the d-pawn or bringing out the queen loses: the bishop on c4 or the knight on e5 falls. White has taken a pawn, but its pieces are loose, and the safe retreat matters more than a fast attack. [%csl Yc4,Ye5][%cal Gc4e2,Rd2d4] }
( 6... d6 $2
{ Kicking the knight looks natural, but [Bxf7+] comes first. After [Bxf7+ Kf8 Bxg8] White has taken the pawn on f7 and the knight on g8, and Black's king has lost the right to castle. Black can take back on g8, but White is two pawns up and the knight escapes from e5. [Nh6] defended f7. }
7. Bxf7+ Kf8 8. Bxg8 ) ( 6... Qe7 $2
{ The queen hits the knight on e5, which looks like winning it back. But [Bxf7+] comes first, and after [Bxf7+ Kf8 Ba3] the queen on e7 is pinned to the king, which has lost the right to castle. Black has to give back material, and White is two pawns up with an attack. [Nh6] was the move. }
7. Bxf7+ Kf8 8. Ba3 ) ( 6... Bxf2+ $2
{ The check on f2 looks forcing and drags the king out. But after [Kxf2 Nh6 Rf1] White's king is safe enough, the rook comes to the f-file, and White is simply a piece up. The bishop on b6 was Black's best piece, and nothing follows the check. [Nh6+] was the move. }
7. Kxf2 Nh6 8. Rf1 ) ( 6... Nxc4 $2
{ Taking the bishop on c4 is what the knight came for, and half the players here do it. But White recaptures with the knight, which escapes from e5 with tempo: after [Nxc4 d5 Nxb6 axb6 exd5] White has kept the extra pawn and Black's king's knight is still at home. White is better. [b:Nh6], covering f7 first, was the move. }
7. Nxc4 d5 8. Nxb6 axb6 9. exd5 ) 7. Be2 ( 7. d4 $2
{ The pawn protects the knight and opens the bishop on c1, so it looks like the right move. But [d6] hits the knight, and after [d6 Bxh6 dxe5] the bishop on b6 hits d4 and the knight on a5 hits c4: Black wins back material with the better game. [Be2] kept everything safe. }
7... d6 8. Bxh6 dxe5 ) ( 7. d3 $2
{ The pawn protects the bishop, which looks solid. But after [Qf6 d4 d6] the knight on e5 is attacked and must retreat, and the bishop on c4 has lost the pawn on d3 that guarded it: the knight on a5 takes it. White is left a piece down. Retreating the bishop to e2 was the move. }
7... Qf6 8. d4 d6 ) ( 7. Qh5 $2
{ The queen attacks f7 once more, which looks strong, but after [Qf6 Be2 Bxf2+] Black's queen guards f7, the bishop takes f2 with check, and White's king has to run to d1. Black wins back the pawn and keeps the attack. Retreating the bishop to e2 at once was the move. }
7... Qf6 8. Be2 Bxf2+ ) ( 7. Qf3 $2
{ The queen adds a third attacker on f7, which looks strong. But f7 is now well guarded by the knight on h6, and after [O-O Qc3 Re8] Black's rook hits the knight on e5 while the bishop on c4 is still attacked by the knight on a5. Black wins back the pawn with a big lead, and White's king is stuck in the centre. [Be2] was the move. }
7... O-O 8. Qc3 Re8 ) ) 5... a6 ( 5... a5 ) ( 5... Nxb4 $2
{ The knight grabs the pawn, which looks free. But [a5] attacks the bishop, which must retreat to c5, and White follows with [w:c3] and [w:d4], gaining time on the knight and the bishop. Black's pieces are chased around while White's centre grows. [a6] kept everything safe. }
6. a5 ) 6. Nc3
{ White develops and prepares [w:Nd5] against the bishop on b6. Black's best is [Nf6], and after [w:Nd5] [b:Nxd5] [w:exd5] [b:Nd4] the game is balanced: Black's knight on d4 is as good as White's space on the queenside. This is the calm side of the Evans, where the pawn on b4 gains space instead of being sacrificed. Grabbing it now runs into a knight jump on e5, because the knight on c6 is the only defender of e5. [%cal Gg8f6,Bc3d5,Rc6b4] }
6... Nf6 ( 6... Nxb4 $2
{ The knight takes the pawn, which no longer seems protected. But it has left e5, and [Nxe5] hits f7 twice. After [Nxe5 Qg5 Qf3] White threatens mate on f7; Black can grab more with the knight on c2, but White's queen gets to f7 and f8, and Black's king is caught. [Nf6] was the move, covering the kingside. }
7. Nxe5 Qg5 8. Qf3 ) ) 5. c3 Ba5 ( 5... Bc5
{ The bishop goes back to its old diagonal, but there [d4] hits it again, so White gains a full tempo over the [b:Ba5] lines. After [b:exd4] [w:O-O] or [w:cxd4] White's centre and development give strong compensation. Black must not take a third pawn on c3 here: [w:Bxf7+] and [w:Qd5+] would win the bishop on c5. This is the line McDonnell played against Evans himself. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gd2d4,Rd1b3] }
6. d4
{ The centre is opened with gain of time on the bishop on c5. Black takes: [exd4], and after [w:O-O] or [w:cxd4] White's development and centre give strong compensation for the pawn. The retreat [b:Bb6] at once also holds, but after [w:dxe5] White's pawn gains space and the knight on g8 has no good square. In this old line Black is a pawn up, but every White move comes with a threat against the bishop on c5 or the pawn on f7, so Black must play accurately. [%csl Yc5][%cal Ge5d4,Gc5b6] }
( 6. Qb3 $2
{ The queen goes for f7 at once, which is the usual target in the Evans. But [Qf6] covers it, and Black stays a pawn up with a safe game: the queen on f6 also eyes c3 and f2. White has lost the chance to gain time on the bishop. [d4] was the move: it makes the bishop move again and opens the centre. }
6... Qf6 ) 6... exd4
{ White's best is [cxd4], building the centre with gain of time on the bishop. [O-O] is also played, keeping the gambit spirit: White offers a second pawn on c3 for a faster attack. The queen raid on b3 instead lets Black guard f7 with the queen and stay two pawns up. In this old line the bishop on c5 is exposed, and White's moves should hit it. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gc3d4,Ge1g1] }
7. cxd4
{ White has rebuilt the centre, and the bishop on c5 is hit for the third time. [Bb4+] is the most played: after [w:Bd2] [b:Bxd2+] the dark bishops are traded, and White's attack loses one of its best pieces. [Bb6] keeps the bishop and reaches the Normal Position after [b:d6]. Taking the pawn on d4 with the knight is a blunder: after [w:Nxd4] [b:Bxd4] [w:Qxd4] White is a piece up. In this line Black has two pawns for nothing, so the safe moves are enough. [%cal Gc5b4,Gc5b6,Rc6d4] }
( 7. O-O
{ White has castled, two pawns down for development. Black should play [d6] or [Bb6], keeping the bishop safe, and must not take a third pawn on c3: [w:Bxf7+] and [w:Qd5+] would win the bishop on c5. Slow or careless moves let [w:cxd4] and [w:e5] roll over Black, because the bishop on c5 is hit first and the knight on f6 next. [%cal Gc5b6,Gd7d6,Rd4c3] }
7... Bb6 ( 7... d6
{ Black holds e5 and supports the bishop. White takes back with [cxd4], gaining time on the bishop, which goes to b6: the Normal Position, reached by another move order. The queen move to b3 first lets Black guard f7 with the queen and keep both pawns, and White's centre is never rebuilt. White's lead in development is the compensation for the pawn, and it has to be used before Black castles. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gc3d4,Rd1b3] }
8. cxd4
{ The bishop is attacked again. [Bb6] is the only good square: it keeps the bishop safe and aims at d4, and White's centre is then ready for [w:d5] or [w:e5]. The check on b4 looks active, but after [w:d5] the knight on c6 has no good square, as every retreat allows a queen fork on a4. This is a trap that costs Black a whole piece. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gc5b6,Rc5b4] }
( 8. Qb3 $2
{ The queen goes for f7, which looks like the quickest attack, but after [Qe7 Bd3 Nf6] it is guarded, Black's pieces come out, and Black is still two pawns up. The pawn on d4 can even take on c3 later. [cxd4] was the move, gaining time on the bishop and rebuilding the centre. }
8... Qe7 9. Bd3 Nf6 ) 8... Bb6 ( 8... Bb4 $4
{ The bishop goes to b4, where it looks active and safe from the pawns. But [d5] hits the knight, and after [d5 Nge7 dxc6] White has won a piece: the knight could not move to e5 or a5 without a queen fork on a4 against the king and the bishop. [Bb6] was the move. }
9. d5 Nge7 10. dxc6 ) ) ( 7... h6 $2
{ A quiet move, but it lets White rebuild the centre with tempo. After [cxd4 b5 Bxb5] Black has had to give back a pawn to save the bishop on c5, and when it drops to b6, [w:d5] chases the knight. Black's extra pawns are gone and nothing is developed. [Bb6] or [d6] was the move. }
8. cxd4 b5 9. Bxb5 ) ( 7... Nf6 $2
{ Developing the knight looks natural, but after [cxd4 Be7 e5] it is kicked and has only g8 to go back to, while [w:d5] chases the other knight. The pawns on d4 and e5 take over the board, and White is winning. [Bb6] or [d6] first was needed, so that the bishop is not hit with gain of time. }
8. cxd4 Be7 9. e5 ) ( 7... dxc3 $2
{ Taking a third pawn looks greedy but free, as in the Compromised Defence. With the bishop on c5, though, it is loose: after [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qd5+] the queen checks and takes it next. Black still has two extra pawns, but the king has been dragged out and White's pieces flood in, so White is clearly better. [b:d6] or [b:Bb6], keeping the bishop safe, was the move. }
8. Bxf7+ Kxf7 9. Qd5+ ) ) ( 7. Qb3 $2
{ The queen hits f7, the usual Evans target, and it looks like the fastest attack. But after [Qe7 O-O Nf6] f7 is covered, Black is developing, and the pawn on d4 still stands: Black is two pawns up. White has spent a tempo while the bishop on c5 is left in peace. [cxd4] was the move. }
7... Qe7 8. O-O Nf6 ) 7... Bb4+
{ White must answer the check. [Bd2] is the most played: the dark bishops come off, and White plays for the centre and development a pawn down. [Kf1] keeps the bishop for the attack and gives up castling: the bishop on b4 is then loose, and [w:Qa4+] or [w:d5] hit it. [Nbd2] blocks with the knight and keeps the bishop on c1 for a3 or b2. In every line White's compensation is the centre and the lead in development, and Black must not grab more pawns before castling. [%cal Gc1d2,Ge1f1,Gb1d2] }
( 7... Bb6 ) 8. Bd2
{ White blocks the check, and Black should trade at once: [Bxd2+] and then [b:d6] or [b:Nf6]. Any other move leaves the bishop on b4 loose, and [w:Bxb4] or [w:d5] with [w:Qa4+] wins it. With the dark bishops off, White's centre is less dangerous and Black keeps the pawn. The queen's check on a4 is the trick to know here: it hits the king and a loose piece on b4 or a5 together. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gb4d2,Rd7d6] }
( 8. Kf1 ) ( 8. Nbd2 ) 8... Bxd2+
{ In the Evans Gambit, trading on d2 gives White an important extra option compared with the Giuoco Piano: because Black's g8 knight is still at home rather than on f6, e4 is not under attack! [Qxd2] is therefore completely sound and very natural, keeping the b1 knight free to jump to its ideal c3 square and connecting the rooks fast. [Nbxd2] is playable too, aiming to meet [Nbxd2 d6] with [w:O-O] and central pressure. [%cal Gd1d2,Gb1d2] }
( 8... d6 $4
{ Holding e5 looks solid, but now the bishop on b4 hangs: after [Bxb4 Nxb4 Qa4+] the queen checks and hits the knight. Black can block with [b:Nc6], but then [w:d5] attacks the pinned knight, and Black loses a piece. [Bxd2+] first was the move, so that nothing is left on b4. }
9. Bxb4 Nxb4 10. Qa4+ ) ( 8... Nf6 $2
{ Developing the knight looks natural, but [d5] chases the other knight first. After [d5 Bxd2+ Qxd2] the knight has to leave c6, and then [w:e5] kicks the one on f6 too. Black's knights have no good squares, and White's centre is huge. [Bxd2+] at once was better, before the pawn could come to d5 with tempo. }
9. d5 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 ) ( 8... Qe7 $2
{ The queen guards b4 and hits e4, which looks active. But it blocks Black's own development: after [O-O Bxd2 Nbxd2] White's pieces are out, and [w:d5] and [w:e5] come with tempo against the queen and the knights. Black's extra pawn is not enough. [Bxd2+] at once was the move. }
9. O-O Bxd2 10. Nbxd2 ) 9. Qxd2 ( 9. Nbxd2 ) ) 6. d4 exd4 7. O-O d6 8. cxd4
{ White has rebuilt the centre with gain of time. Black develops with [Nf6] or plays [Bb6], reaching the Normal Position of the Evans, where Black is a pawn up and White has the centre. Slow moves are punished: White's queen comes to b3 and the knight to g5, both against f7, while Black's kingside pieces are still at home. The pawns on d4 and e4 are the whole point of White's gambit. [%cal Gg8f6,Ga5b6,Rh7h6] }
8... Nf6 ( 8... Bb6
{ The Normal Position of the Evans. White has the centre and a lead in development for the pawn. [h3] keeps the bishop from g4, [Nc3] develops, and [d5] gains space. Black plans [b:Nf6] or [b:Na5] to trade the bishop on c4. Each side has a clear plan: White pushes in the centre with [w:d5] or [w:e5], and Black trades the bishop on c4 and keeps the extra pawn. An early knight jump to g5 achieves nothing. [%cal Gh2h3,Gb1c3,Gd4d5,Bc6a5] }
9. h3 ( 9. Nc3
{ The Normal Position with White's knight developed. Black's best plans deal with the bishop on c4 and the pressure on d4 and e5: [Na5] trades the bishop off, and [Bg4] pins the knight on f3. Black must not let [w:e5] or [w:Ng5] land while the king is still on e8: that is why the natural [b:Nf6] and [b:Nge7] are mistakes here, and [b:a6] is a tempo too slow. Each natural developing move runs into a tactic, so Black has to know this position. [%cal Gc6a5,Ga5c4] }
9... Na5 ( 9... Bg4
{ Black pins the knight on f3, which guards d4 and e5. White answers with a pin of its own: [Qa4] pins the knight on c6 against Black's king, so [w:d5] would win it, and Black replies [b:Bd7]. [Bb5] does the same with the bishop. Breaking the pin with [w:h3] looks logical, but Black just takes on f3 and the queens come off, which suits the side a pawn up. Attacking f7 with the queen or the bishop fails while the knight on f3 is pinned. [%cal Gd1a4,Ga4e8] }
10. Qa4 ( 10. Bb5 ) ( 10. h3 $6
{ Asking the bishop the question is a club habit, and a quarter of the players here do it. But Black simply takes: after [Bxf3 Qxf3 Qf6] the queens come off and Black is a pawn up in an endgame, which is the last thing a gambit player wants. [Qa4] or [Bb5], pinning the knight on c6, kept the initiative. }
10... Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Qf6 ) ( 10. Qb3 $2
{ The queen hits f7 and b7 at once, but it leaves d1, so it no longer guards f3. After [Bxf3 Bxf7+ Kf8] White must take back on f3 with the g-pawn, the king's cover is wrecked, and [b:Nxd4] hits the queen and wins the centre pawn. Black is clearly better. [Qa4] was the move. }
10... Bxf3 11. Bxf7+ Kf8 ) ( 10. Bxf7+ $2
{ The bishop sacrifice on f7 is a typical Italian shot, but it needs the knight on f3 to follow up, and the knight is pinned. After [Kxf7 Nd5 Bxf3] Black takes it off, the king walks to safety, and White has given a piece for two pawns with no attack left. [Qa4+] was the move. }
10... Kxf7 11. Nd5 Bxf3 ) ( 10. Qd3 $2
{ The queen guards f3 and eyes h7, which looks like useful development. But it lets Black trade on f3 with gain of time: after [Bxf3 Qxf3 Qf6] the queens face each other and Black, a pawn up, is happy to swap them. White's initiative fizzles out. [Qa4] or [Bb5], pinning the knight on c6, was the move. }
10... Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Qf6 ) ( 10. d5 $6
{ The pawn hits the knight on c6, but the knight has a fine square: after [Nd4 Be2 Nxe2+] it lands in the centre, trades itself for White's good bishop, and Black's pieces are the active ones. The pawn on d5 has also left e5 free for Black. [Qa4] or [Bb5] was the move. }
10... Nd4 11. Be2 Nxe2+ ) ) ( 9... Nf6 $2
{ The most natural developing move, and a fifth of the players here choose it. But it walks into [e5]: after [e5 dxe5 Ba3] the bishop keeps Black's king in the centre, the e-file is open, and White wins back the pawn with a strong attack. [b:Na5] or [b:Bg4] was the move. }
10. e5 dxe5 11. Ba3 ) ( 9... Nge7 $2
{ The knight heads for g6, but it leaves f7 and h7 guarded by little more than the king. After [Ng5 Rf8 Qh5] White hits f7 and h7 at once, and [w:Nxh7] comes next. Black's position falls apart. [b:Na5], trading the bishop on c4 first, was the move. }
10. Ng5 Rf8 11. Qh5 ) ( 9... a6 $2
{ Making room on a7 for the bishop looks useful, but it is a tempo White uses at once. After [Nd5 h6 Nxb6 cxb6] White has the bishop pair, Black's b-pawns are doubled, and [w:d5] will drive the knight from c6. White's centre rolls on. [b:Na5] or [b:Bg4] kept Black's position together. }
10. Nd5 h6 11. Nxb6 cxb6 ) ) ( 9. d5
{ The pawn hits the knight on c6, and Black must choose its square. [Na5] is best: it hits the bishop on c4, and after [w:Bd3] [b:Ne7] Black's pieces come out safely. [Nce7] is solid too. The most played move, [b:Ne5], lets White win the gambit pawn back on e5. Another trap is the poisoned rook: [b:Qf6], hitting a1 along the open diagonal, loses to [w:dxc6] and an attack on f7 if Black grabs the rook. [%cal Gc6a5,Gc6e7,Rc6e5] }
9... Na5 ( 9... Nce7 ) ( 9... Ne5 $6
{ The knight jumps to the centre and offers a trade, which looks active. But after [Nxe5 dxe5 Nd2] the pawn on e5 can't be held: the knight heads for c4 and the bishop for b2, White wins back the gambit pawn, and the pawn on d5 keeps Black cramped. [b:Na5], hitting the bishop on c4, was the move. }
