{ White must answer the check. [Bd2] is the safe, modern choice, leading to an equal game with an isolated d-pawn. [Nc3] offers the pawn on e4: this is the old Greco Gambit and the Moller Attack, full of traps on both sides. [Nbd2] also allows [b:Nxe4] or [b:d5], with a quiet equal game. [Kf1], the Krakow line, gives up castling to keep the pieces on the board; it was analysed by the Krakow chess club in 1909. In every line Black aims to play [b:d5] at the right moment, and the first player to put a pawn on d5 usually comes out well. [%cal Gc1d2,Gb1c3,Gb1d2,Ge1f1] }
( 6... Bb6 $2
{ The bishop is safe on b6, and it still aims at f2, so the retreat looks fine. But White now pushes [e5]. The knight on f6 is kicked and has no good square: on g4 it can be trapped with [w:h3], and on e4 it is chased by [w:d5] and [w:Bd3]. White gains space with every move, and Black is cramped for a long time. [Bb4+] first forces White to block the check and gives Black time to strike with [b:d5] or [b:Nxe4]. }
