{ White has won back the piece, and the rook on e4 eyes Black's king. Black is a pawn up. [d6] is the main move: it stops the pawn on d5 from advancing and frees the bishop on c8. The old books call castling first a mistake because of [w:d6], which wedges a pawn into Black's position, but the engines find that [O-O] holds too. After [d6] the main line goes [w:Bg5] [b:Bxg5] [w:Nxg5], with sharp play around h7 and f7. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd7d6,Ge8g8] }
