{ The Krakow line: White's king steps aside to keep the bishops. Black's best is [d5]!, hitting the centre at once: after [w:exd5] [b:Nxd5] Black's pieces are active and White's king can't castle. The bishop on b4 is loose, so Black must act before [w:d5] and [w:Qa4+] come. Quiet moves give White that time. White has given up castling to keep the dark bishop and hopes the centre pawns will be worth more than a safe king; [d5] breaks them up before they can advance. [%csl Yb4,Yf1][%cal Gd7d5] }
