{ The bishop takes on f7 with check, and [Kxf7] is the only good answer: stepping aside leaves the bishop alive in Black's camp and gives nothing back. Once the king is on f7, White's real threat comes: [w:Qb3+] checks it and hits the knight on b4 at once, so White wins back the piece. After [w:Qxb4] material is level, and Black's king can no longer castle, though with the queens likely to come off it is safe enough. [%csl Yb4][%cal Ge8f7,Rd1b3] }
9... Kxf7
{ [Qb3+] is the point of the sacrifice: the queen checks the king and hits the knight on b4, and White wins back the piece next move. After [b:d5] [w:Qxb4] or [b:Kf8] [w:Qxb4+] material is level, Black's king has lost the right to castle, and White's pieces are easier to develop. The engines call it equal. Any slower move gives Black time to tuck the two knights away and keep the extra pawn, so the check must come at once. [%cal Gd1b3,Gb3f7,Gb3b4] }
( 9... Kf8 $2
{ Sidestepping the check with [Kf8] tries to keep the king sheltered, yet refusing the bishop leaves Black a pawn down with a permanently misplaced king and zero compensation! White simply tucks the bishop back with [Bb3], castles [w:O-O], and dominates the open lines. Capture the bishop with [Kxf7] to stay equal. [%csl Yf8][%cal Rf7b3] }
10. Bb3 d5 11. O-O g6 12. Qe1 ) 10. Qb3+
{ Black has to answer the check. [d5] is the most played: it blocks the queen's line to the king, keeps the knight on e4 protected, and after [w:Qxb4] Black has a good centre. [Kf8] and [Ke8] are also fine: after [w:Qxb4] the queens often come off with [b:Qe7], and the endgame is level. Black should not try to save both knights: one of them is lost whatever Black does, and the game is about who develops faster afterwards. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gd7d5,Gf7f8] }
( 10. Ne5+ $4
{ Checking with the knight before bringing the queen to b3 gets the move order fatally backwards: after [Kf8], Black's king is safe and [Kf8 Qb3] no longer comes with check! Black defends b4 and threatens mate on f2 or [Qe7], staying a full piece ahead. Play [Qb3+] immediately to fork king and b4 knight. [%csl Ye5][%cal Rf7f8] }
10... Kf8 11. Qb3 Qe7 12. O-O d6 ) 10... d5 ( 10... Kf8 ) ( 10... Ke8 ) ) 8.
Nbxd2
{ The bishops are gone, and now [d5]! is the freeing move. After [d5 exd5 Nxd5] White is left with an isolated pawn on d4. It gives White open lines and active pieces, and an outpost on e5, but in the endgame it can become a weakness that Black's pieces gang up on. Black's plan is to blockade it with a knight on d5 and trade pieces. White usually plays [w:Qb3] to press on d5 and b7, then castles and brings the rooks to the centre. The position is balanced. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd7d5] }
8... d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Qb3
{ The queen hits the knight on d5 and the pawn on b7 together. [Na5] chases it at once: after [w:Qa4+] [b:Nc6] [w:Qb3] [b:Na5] the moves repeat, and many games end in a quick draw. [Nce7] is the other good move, keeping the knight on d5 protected and preparing [b:O-O] and [b:c6], after which Black's blockade on d5 is very solid. White's isolated pawn gives both sides chances: White attacks on the kingside with pieces, Black aims at d4 and trades. [%csl Yd5,Yb7][%cal Gc6a5,Gc6e7] }
10... Na5 ( 10... Nce7 ) ) ( 5. b4
{ Bird's idea: White gains space on the queenside and threatens [w:b5] against the knight. Black retreats with [Be7], the engine's choice, which keeps the option of [b:d5], or [Bb6], after which [w:d3] [b:d6] [w:a4] [b:a5] gives a balanced closed game. The pawn on b4 can later become a target, and White has weakened c3 and c4. White gets less from [b4] than from [w:d3] or [w:d4], but it is a reasonable surprise. [%cal Gc5e7,Gc5b6,Rb4b5] }
5... Be7 ( 5... Bb6 ) ( 5... Bxf2+ $2
{ The sacrifice on f2 wins two pawns and drags the king out, and it is a pattern worth knowing. But here there is no follow-up: after [Kxf2 Nxe4+ Kg1] the king is safe in the corner, Black's queen can't reach h4 with any effect, and White is a piece for two pawns up. Before sacrificing on f2, check that more pieces can join the attack. Here [Be7] or [Bb6] was the move. }
6. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 7. Kg1 ) ) ( 5. O-O
{ White castles before deciding on [w:d3] or [w:d4], and leaves the pawn on e4 unprotected. Black can take it with [Nxe4]: after [w:d4] or [w:Bd5] White regains the pawn and the game is equal. [d6] is the solid move. Castling is playable, but White then plays [w:d4] with a good version of the centre. What Black must avoid is a slow move or an early [b:d5] here: White's [w:d4] and [w:Qb3] would come with gain of time. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf6e4,Gd7d6] }
5... Nxe4 ( 5... d6 ) ( 5... d5 $2
{ The central break seems to free Black's game, but it opens lines for White's better-placed pieces. After [exd5 Nxd5 Qb3] the queen hits the knight on d5 and the pawn on f7 together, and Black can't hold both without giving up the right to castle or a pawn. In this structure [b:d5] only works when Black is fully developed and castled. Here [Nxe4] or [d6] was better. }
6. exd5 Nxd5 7. Qb3 ) ( 5... h6 $2
{ A natural move against [w:Bg5], but it gives White the tempo for [d4]. In the line [d4 Bb6 Nxe5 Nxe5 dxe5 Nxe4] Black seems to win back the pawn, yet then [w:Bxf7+] and [w:Qd5+] fork the king and the knight on e4. White wins a piece. Black must keep the pawn on e5 protected before waiting moves like this one: [d6] first, or take the pawn on e4 at once. }
6. d4 Bb6 7. Nxe5 Nxe5 8. dxe5 Nxe4 9. Bxf7+ Kxf7 10. Qd5+ ) ( 5... a6 $2
{ Black wants the bishop on a7, out of reach of the pawns, but this costs the tempo White uses for [d4]. After [d4 Ba7 dxe5 Ng4 Bg5] the bishop on g5 hits the queen and Black's knight is stuck on g4 facing the pawn on e5. After [b:f6] [w:exf6] Black's kingside is wrecked. [d6] would have held the centre, and then [b:a6] and [b:Ba7] make sense. }
6. d4 Ba7 7. dxe5 Ng4 8. Bg5 ) ) 5... d6 ( 5... O-O
{ Black castles early while keeping d7 flexible: because White committed the d-pawn to d3, Black holds [d5] in reserve as a one-step central break whenever White plays slowly. Castling [O-O] is White's standard reply, tucking the king away before [Re1], [h3], and [Nbd2]. Developing [Nbd2] right away (inviting [Nbd2 d5 exd5 Nxd5 Ne4 Be7]) or claiming queenside space with [a4] to restrain [a5] and [Ba7] are equally rich grandmaster choices. [%csl Yd7,Yd3][%cal Ge1g1] }
6. O-O ( 6. Nbd2 ) ( 6. a4 ) ) ( 5... a6
{ Black gives the bishop the a7 square in advance, so that [w:d4] won't hit it, and takes b5 from White's bishop, so [b:Na5] becomes a threat. White answers with [Bb3] or [a4], giving the bishop a safe retreat on c2 or a2, or simply plays [O-O] and [Nbd2] and waits. [w:b4] gains space against the bishop, which goes to a7 anyway. The game is a slow Pianissimo: Black plays [b:Ba7], [b:d6], [b:h6] and [b:O-O], and both sides manoeuvre before anyone opens the centre. [%cal Ge1g1,Gc4b3,Ga2a4,Gc5a7] }
6. O-O ( 6. Bb3 ) ( 6. a4 ) ( 6. Nbd2 ) ) ( 5... Ng4 $2
{ The knight jumps at f2, which is a real target in this opening, but here it is alone there: White's king can castle, and [d4] opens the centre at once. After [d4 exd4 cxd4] the bishop on c5 is attacked, the knight on g4 stands offside, and White has the centre and a lead in development. Black must spend more time getting the pieces back. Develop first with [d6] or [b:O-O], and keep [b:Ng4] for a moment when White's rook has left f1. }
6. d4 exd4 7. cxd4 ) ( 5... d5
{ Black opens the centre before castling. White should take: [exd5], and after [b:Nxd5] the queen jumps to [w:Qb3], hitting the knight on d5 and the pawn on f7 at once. Black has to give up a pawn or the right to castle. The bishop must not retreat first: on b3 it lets Black take on e4 with tempo, and the queens come off. In the Pianissimo, [b:d5] works for Black only once the king is safe on g8. [%csl Yf7][%cal Ge4d5,Gd1b3] }
6. exd5 ( 6. Bb3 $2
{ The bishop steps away from the attack, which looks careful, but now nothing guards e4. After [dxe4 dxe4 Qxd1+ Bxd1 Nxe4] Black has won a pawn, and the queens are gone, so White has no attack to make up for it. Take on d5 first: [exd5] keeps the bishop on its diagonal, prepares [w:Qb3], and leaves Black's king in the centre. }
6... dxe4 7. dxe4 Qxd1+ 8. Bxd1 Nxe4 ) ) *

[Event "Two Knights Defence"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6
{ The Two Knights Defence, known since Polerio around 1580. Black develops and attacks e4 at once, but the knight on f6 now blocks the queen's view of g5, so [Ng5] becomes possible, hitting f7 twice. That is the sharp road, and Tarrasch famously called it a duffer's move, though it is perfectly sound. [d3] is the quiet road and the most played at master level: e4 is held and the game becomes a slow manoeuvring fight. [d4] opens the centre, and [O-O] offers the e-pawn. [w:Nc3] looks natural but walks into a fork trick. [%cal Gf6e4,Gf3g5,Gd2d3,Gd2d4] }
4. d3
{ The quiet move, and the most common at master level. White holds e4 and avoids all the gambits. Black's choices are [Bc5], reaching the Pianissimo, [Be7], a solid set-up like a Ruy Lopez, and [h6] with [b:d6] and [b:g6]. White plays [w:O-O], [w:Re1], [w:c3] or [w:a4], and the knight tour [w:Nbd2]-f1-g3. There is no [w:Ng5] worry once Black has castled. [%cal Gf8c5,Gf8e7,Gh7h6] }
( 4. Ng5
{ The knight and the bishop both hit f7, which only the king defends. Black has two good answers. [d5]! blocks the bishop's diagonal and is the main line; after [w:exd5] Black has to choose how to deal with the pawn on d5, and the best way is not to take it back at once. [Bc5] is the Traxler, a counter-attack on f2 that gives up f7 and the rook in the corner for a wild attack. Everything else drops a pawn or worse: [Qe7] does not really cover f7, and [h6] comes too late. [%cal Gd7d5,Gf8c5,Rg5f7] }
4... d5
{ Black blocks the bishop's diagonal, and White takes: [exd5] is the only sensible move, as the pawn on d5 attacks both the bishop and the pawn on e4, and [w:Bxd5] would lose time after [b:Nxd5]. Now Black has three main ways. The natural recapture with the knight walks into the Fried Liver and the Lolli. [b:Na5]!, attacking the bishop, is the main line: a pawn for development. [b:b5] and [b:Nd4] are sharp tries that White must know. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge4d5,Bc6a5] }
( 4... Bc5
{ The Traxler, or Wilkes-Barre. Black ignores f7 and attacks f2. White's best is [Bxf7+]!: after [b:Ke7] the bishop goes back to d5 or b3, White is a pawn up, and Black's king has left its castle. [Nxf7] forks the queen and the rook but lets Black sacrifice on f2 for a dangerous attack. [d4]!? is a rare and good try. The defence is named after Karel Traxler, a Czech priest who won a famous game with it in 1890. [%cal Gc4f7,Gg5f7,Gd2d4,Rc5f2] }
5. Bxf7+
{ White takes the pawn with check. Black's king steps to [Ke7], which keeps the f-file open for the rook and protects the knight on f6. Now the bishop on f7 is attacked and must retreat, to d5, b3 or c4. Black hopes for [b:Rf8], [b:d6] and [b:h6] with pressure on the f-file. The king can also go to f8, when a clever trap awaits White if it grabs the rook. [%csl Yf7][%cal Ge8e7,Gh8f8] }
( 5. Nxf7
{ The knight forks the queen and the rook, and Black must hit back at once: [Bxf2+]! drags White's king out. If it takes the bishop, Black's knight takes e4 with check and Black's queen comes to h4. If it steps to f1, Black plays [b:Qe7], [b:d5] and [b:Nd4], aiming at the queen on d1. The slow defence of the queen loses: White takes the rook and Black has no attack. [%cal Gc5f2,Gf6e4,Rd8e7] }
5... Bxf2+
{ White's king has to move. [Kf1]! is the best: the king stays out of checks, and after [b:Qe7] [w:Nxh8] [b:d5] [w:exd5] [b:Nd4] White must find [w:d6]! to stay ahead. [Kxf2] takes the bishop, but after [b:Nxe4+] Black's queen jumps to h4 and Black has at least a perpetual check. The king must not go to e2, where a knight check on d4 drives it forward. [%cal Ge1f1,Ge1f2,Re1e2] }
( 5... Qe7 $2
{ Saving the queen looks natural, but it gives White time to take the rook: after [Nxh8 d5 Bxd5] White is a rook up, and Black has no check on f2 and no attack. The Traxler only works if Black keeps attacking: [Bxf2+] first, before White's king is safe. }
6. Nxh8 d5 7. Bxd5 ) 6. Kf1 ( 6. Kxf2
{ White has taken the bishop, and now the king stands in the open. [Nxe4+]! is the point of the Traxler: the knight takes a pawn with check, and Black's queen comes to h4 next with checks on the dark squares. After [w:Kg1] [b:Qh4] or [w:Ke3] [b:Qh4] Black has at least a perpetual check, and White must find exact moves to keep anything. Black is a rook and a bishop down for the moment, so slow moves lose: every Black move must be a check or a threat. [%csl Yf2][%cal Gf6e4,Gd8h4] }
6... Nxe4+
{ White's king is in the open. [Kg1] is the safest square, but after [b:Qh4] Black has at least a perpetual check, as [w:g3] [b:Nxg3] opens more lines. [Ke3] is also played: after [b:Qh4] [w:g3] [b:Nxg3] [w:hxg3] [b:Qd4+]! the attack goes on. Most players keep the king near g1. Either way White gives up the chance for an advantage that the king move to f1 kept. [%cal Gf2g1,Gf2e3,Rf2e1,Rf2e2] }
7. Kg1 ( 7. Ke3
{ The king has walked to e3, where it attacks the knight on e4. Black's best is [Qh4]!, hitting h2 and g3, and after [w:g3] [b:Nxg3] [w:hxg3] [b:Qd4+] Black's attack goes on and the engines call the game level. White is a rook up for the moment, so Black must keep checking or threatening. Slower queen moves give White time to bring the queen out and take the knight. [%csl Ye3][%cal Gd8h4,Rd8f6] }
7... Qh4 ( 7... Qf6 $2
{ The queen comes out with a hit on f7, which looks active. But it gives White a free move: after [Qf3 Rf8 Qxe4] White's queen takes the knight on e4 with the king covered, and keeps the extra pieces. Black's attack has run out. [Qh4] kept the attack going. }
8. Qf3 Rf8 9. Qxe4 ) ) ( 7. Ke1 $4
{ The king goes back home, which looks safest. But [Qh4+] comes with check, and after [Qh4+ g3 Nxg3] the knight takes the pawn and the rook on h1 falls too. Black wins back all the material and keeps an attack against a king that can't castle. [Kg1] was the move. }
7... Qh4+ 8. g3 Nxg3 ) ( 7. Kf1 $2
{ The king steps to f1, which looks safe enough. But after [Qh4 Qe1 Ng3+] the knight checks, and once White's queen takes it, [b:Qxc4+] wins the bishop with check. Black is back in material with the better game, and White's king is still in the open. [Kg1] kept the king safer. }
7... Qh4 8. Qe1 Ng3+ ) ( 7. Ke2 $4
{ The king goes forward to e2, where it seems to guard d2 and f2. But after [Nd4+ Ke3 Qh4] it is surrounded by Black's knights and queen, and the checks on f4 and g5 are coming. White's extra material does not matter while the king is in the middle of the board. [Kg1] was the move. }
7... Nd4+ 8. Ke3 Qh4 ) ) ( 6. Ke2 $4
{ The king steps aside, but on e2 it is in the open. [Nd4+] drives it further, and Black's queen and pieces join the hunt; Traxler's own game against Reinisch in 1890 ended in mate after the king went to d3. [Kf1] keeps the king safe in the corner. }
6... Nd4+ ) 6... Qe7 7. Nxh8 d5
{ Black has given up a rook and now opens the centre, hoping to reach White's king on f1. White takes with [exd5]: after [b:Nd4] White must play [w:d6]!, giving the pawn back to break up Black's attack. Taking with the bishop is a mistake: then [b:Bg4] attacks the queen on d1, and it has no square, as e1 and e2 are covered by Black's bishops. [%csl Yd1][%cal Ge4d5,Rc4d5] }
8. exd5 ( 8. Bxd5 $4
{ The bishop takes the pawn and attacks c6, but it leaves the queen on d1 alone. [Bg4] hits the queen, and it has no square: e2 is on the bishop's line and e1 is covered by the bishop on f2. Black wins the queen. Taking with the e-pawn was the right capture. }
8... Bg4 ) 8... Nd4
{ Black threatens [b:Bg4], trapping White's queen. [d6]! is the only good move: it gives back the pawn to open the diagonal from c4 to f7. After [b:Qxd6] [w:Nf7] [b:Qc5] [w:d3] White is a rook up and safe, and the engines give White a winning advantage. The natural [w:c3] loses to the bishop move to g4. It is the Traxler's critical line, and one where White has to know the moves. [%csl Yd1][%cal Gd5d6,Gc4f7,Rc2c3] }
9. d6 ( 9. c3 $4
{ Kicking the knight seems natural, but [Bg4] hits the queen. After [Bg4 Qa4+ Nd7] Black's attack on e2 and the f-file is unstoppable, and White's extra rook is out of play on h8. [d6] first opens a diagonal for White's bishop and breaks the attack. }
9... Bg4 10. Qa4+ Nd7 ) ) ( 5. d4
{ A rare but good try: White hits the centre before taking on f7. The only good reply is [d5]!, opening the bishop on c8 and attacking the bishop on c4. Taking on d4 instead lets [w:Nxf7] work, because Black's bishop no longer threatens f2 with check. After [d5] [w:Bxd5] White keeps a small edge; Naroditsky has recommended this line. It is far less known than the captures on f7, which is part of its appeal. [%csl Yc4,Yf7][%cal Gd7d5] }
5... d5 ) 5... Ke7
{ The bishop must leave f7. [Bd5] is the main line: it blocks the d-pawn and eyes c6. [Bb3] and [Bc4] are also good, keeping the bishop near the kingside. White is a pawn up, and should play [w:c3], [w:d4] and [w:O-O] quickly before Black's [b:Rf8], [b:d6] and [b:h6] build up on the f-file. With the bishop back on c4 it can later drop to e2 to defend. [%cal Gf7d5,Gf7b3,Gf7c4] }
6. Bd5 ( 6. Bb3 ) ( 6. Bc4 ) ) ( 4... Nxe4 $2
{ A counter-attack that looks logical: if White's knight goes to f7, Black's goes to f2. But [Bxf7+]! comes first. After [Bxf7+ Ke7 d4] Black's king is stuck on e7, the knight on e4 is loose, and White threatens [w:Nxe4] or [w:d5] with a strong attack; Black is clearly worse. The greedy [w:Nxf7] instead would let Black's queen into h4 with a counter-attack. The way to defend f7 here is [d5], which cuts the bishop off from it. }
5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. d4 ) ( 4... Qe7 $2
{ The queen seems to guard f7 from e7, so the move looks like a simple defence. But it does not stop the capture with the bishop. After [Bxf7+ Kd8 Bb3] White is a clean pawn up, Black's king has lost the right to castle and blocks the rook on h8, and the queen on e7 hems in the bishop on f8. Against the double attack on f7 the right reply is to block the bishop's diagonal with [d5], or to counter-attack with [Bc5]. }
5. Bxf7+ Kd8 6. Bb3 ) ( 4... h6 $4
{ Kicking the knight seems natural, but it is too late: [Nxf7] takes the pawn and forks the queen and the rook on h8. After [Nxf7 Qe7 Nxh8] Black has lost the exchange and a pawn, and the king is open, though White's knight in the corner may be trapped later. A move like [h6] only works before the knight arrives. When f7 is attacked twice, Black needs a forcing answer: [d5], or the Traxler's [Bc5]. }
5. Nxf7 Qe7 6. Nxh8 ) 5. exd5
{ Black should not take back on d5 with the knight: it lets White attack f7 and d5 at once, either by sacrificing on f7 (the Fried Liver) or with [w:d4] (the Lolli). [Na5]! is the main line: it hits the bishop on c4, and Black gives a pawn for quick development and a coming attack on the knight on g5. Chigorin showed that Black's play is worth the pawn. [b5], the Ulvestad, and [Nd4], the Fritz, are sharper alternatives that give White an edge with the right reply. [%cal Gc6a5,Gb7b5,Gc6d4,Rf6d5] }
5... Na5
{ The main line. The knight attacks the bishop, and White has to keep it: [Bb5+] is the main move, and after [b:c6] [w:dxc6] [b:bxc6] the bishop must decide where to go. [d3], Morphy's move, is quieter: after [b:h6] [w:Nf3] [b:e4] White's queen goes to e2. Retreating the bishop to b3 or defending it with the queen gives up the bishop pair or blocks White's development, and Black wins back the pawn with a good game. [%cal Gc4b5,Gd2d3,Rc4b3] }
( 5... b5
{ The Ulvestad, named after Olav Ulvestad, who published it in 1941. Black attacks the bishop and gives a pawn to deflect it from d5. The engines' choice is [Bf1]!: the bishop steps all the way back and keeps the pawn on d5 guarded by the queen, so Black has to work to win it back. [Bxb5] is the other way: after [b:Qxd5] [w:Bxc6+] [b:Qxc6] White is a pawn up, but Black's queen and the bishop coming to b7 aim at g2, and Black has full compensation. Taking on c6 with the pawn lets Black open lines and gain time. Carlsen has tried this line as Black. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc4f1,Gc4b5] }
6. Bf1
{ The bishop's retreat looks odd, but it keeps the pawn on d5 defended by the queen. [Nxd5] is Black's main move: after [w:Bxb5] White is a pawn up with a safe position, and Black has some activity; [w:Nc3] hits the knight at once and keeps the same edge. [Qxd5] is just as good for the engines, though [w:Nc3] gains a tempo on the queen. [Nd4] transposes to the Fritz. Slow moves such as [b:h6] lose more material. White's king stays home for a move, but no Black piece can reach it. [%cal Gf6d5,Gd8d5,Gc6d4,Rh7h6] }
( 6. Bxb5 ) 6... Nxd5 ( 6... Qxd5 ) ( 6... Nd4 ) ( 6... h6 $6
{ Kicking the knight looks useful, but [Nxf7]! sacrifices it: after [Nxf7 Kxf7 dxc6] White has three pawns for the piece, Black's king is exposed, and the pawn on c6 is strong. Black should first win back the pawn with [Nxd5], which also covers f6 and e7. }
7. Nxf7 Kxf7 8. dxc6 ) ) ( 5... Nd4
{ The Fritz Variation, named after Alexander Fritz, who suggested it to Schlechter in 1904. The knight comes to d4, threatening [b:Nxc2+] and preparing [b:b5]. White's best is [c3]!, kicking it at once, a move found by Leonhardt in 1907. After [b:b5] [w:Bf1] [b:Nxd5] White can take with [w:cxd4] or play [w:Ne4], keeping an edge. The pawn sacrifice [w:d6] is playable, but after [b:Qxd6] the greedy [w:Nxf7]? runs into [b:Qc6]!, which hits c4 and g2 at once. [%cal Gc2c3,Bd4c2,Rg5f7] }
6. c3
{ The knight on d4 is attacked, and Black has three ways to go on. [b5] kicks the bishop first and is the main line: after [w:Bf1] [b:Nxd5] Black wins back the pawn with active play. [h6]!? is the engines' choice: after [w:cxd4] [b:hxg5] [w:dxe5] [b:Nxd5] Black has given up the knight on d4 for White's knight and keeps an open game. [Nf5] retreats safely and is the most played, but it gives White time to keep the extra pawn. Black must not leave the knight on d4 to be taken. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb7b5,Gh7h6,Gd4f5] }
( 6. Nxf7 $4
{ Playing Fried Liver fashion with [Nxf7] against the Fritz Variation is a catastrophic blunder! With Black's knight on d4 guarding f5 and blocking the d-file, White has no [Qf3] follow-up to win the piece back; [Kxf7] simply pockets a full knight with a deadly attack down the f-file. Play [c3] instead! [%csl Yd4,Yf5][%cal Rg5f7,Ge8f7] }
6... Kxf7 7. a4 Bd6 8. d3 Rf8 ) ( 6. d6 Qxd6
{ The pawn on d6 is gone, and White must not take on f7 with the knight: Black's queen would jump to c6, hitting the bishop and g2. [Bxf7+] [b:Ke7] [w:Bb3] keeps the game balanced; slower moves such as castling give Black the better game. Black's knight on d4 and queen on d6 are active, and White has to watch c2, e2 and g2. The move [w:d6] has cost White the extra pawn and the initiative. [%cal Gc4f7,Rg5f7,Rd6c6] }
