5. exd5 ) *

[Event "Scotch Game (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4
{ The Scotch Game. White doesn't wait: the d-pawn strikes at e5 on move three and opens the center at once. Black can't hold e5 comfortably, so the main answer is [exd4], giving up the strong point to open lines for the pieces. After [exd4 Nxd4], you get the classic Scotch picture: White has a pawn on e4 and Black has no pawn in the center, which gives White a little more space. Black's task is to use the time White spends recapturing to develop with threats, usually [Nf6] hitting e4 or [Bc5] hitting the knight. [d6] holds e5 but leaves Black cramped, and [Nxd4], the Lolli Variation, trades off a knight that White is happy to see go. The opening takes its name from a correspondence match between Edinburgh and London in the 1820s; Kasparov brought it back to the top in his 1990 world championship match against Karpov. [%cal Gd4e5] }
3... exd4
{ Black has given up the center to open lines. White's main move is [Nxd4]: the knight recaptures and stands in the middle of the board, and White keeps the pawn on e4 against nothing in Black's center, the classic Scotch structure with a little more space. Two gambits are also popular. [Bc4], the Scotch Gambit, leaves the pawn and develops toward f7, often leading to Italian-style positions. [c3], the Göring Gambit, offers one or two pawns for a big lead in development, the same idea as the Danish Gambit. Whatever you choose, don't take back with the queen: the knight on c6 is waiting for it. [%cal Gc4f7] }
( 3... d6
{ Black holds e5 with a pawn, steering for a Philidor-like setup. It is solid, but passive: the bishop on f8 is shut in, and the knight on c6 stands in front of the c-pawn, so Black has little room. White has two clear plans. [d5] gains space and kicks the knight to a poor square, leaving Black cramped for a long time. [dxe5] is the simple practical choice: after [dxe5 dxe5 Qxd8+ Kxd8], the queens are gone but Black's king has lost the right to castle and blocks its own rooks, while White develops with [Be3], [Nc3] and long castling, which brings a rook to the d-file with tempo. [Nc3] develops and keeps both ideas in hand. [%cal Gd4e5] }
4. d5
{ The pawn on d5 gains space and chases the knight from c6, and Black has no good square for it. [Nce7] is the most common: the knight can reroute to g6, and Black can later strike at White's center with [f5]. [Nb8] looks strange, but the knight can come back to the game via d7. Either way Black is cramped, and White has a long-term plan: [c4], [Nc3], and later [c5] to open lines on the queenside, where White has more space and Black's pieces can't come to help. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc6e7,Gc6b8] }
( 4. dxe5
{ White exchanges at once, the most direct way to show that [d6] was passive. Black has two recaptures. [dxe5] keeps a pawn on e5, but the d-file opens and White can trade queens with check: after [dxe5 Qxd8+ Kxd8], Black's king can no longer castle. [Nxe5] keeps the queens on, and after [Nxe5 Nxe5 dxe5] White can again trade on d8. White can also keep pieces with [Nc3] and [Bf4], hitting the knight. Neither recapture is a disaster, but White keeps an easy, risk-free edge in both: more active pieces and a king that castles normally. [%csl Yd8][%cal Gd6e5,Gc6e5] }
4... dxe5 ( 4... Nxe5 ) ( 4... f6 $2
{ Propping up the centre with the f-pawn doesn't work here. White takes again with [exf6 Nxf6], and Black is simply a pawn down, with the diagonal from c4 to g8 open toward the king and a hole on e6. Take back on e5 with the pawn or the knight and accept a slightly worse position: it is much better than a pawn less. }
5. exf6 Nxf6 ) 5. Qxd8+
{ White trades queens with check. Take back with the king: [Kxd8] keeps e5 guarded by the knight on c6. The king loses the right to castle, but with the queens gone it is safe enough in the middle, and it can walk to e8 or c7 later. Black's real problem is a slightly passive position, not the king. [%csl Ye5,Yc6][%cal Ge8d8] }
5... Kxd8
{ The queens are off, but this is no dull endgame. Black's king on d8 has lost castling, keeps the rook on a8 from joining the one on h8, and stands on the open d-file. White's plan is simple: develop with [Nc3], [Be3] or [Bc4], castle long so the rook lands on d1 with tempo against the king, and use the extra time to press on f7 and the d-file. Black will spend moves just walking the king to e8 or e7. Note that the pawn on e5 only looks loose: the knight on c6 protects it, so taking it costs a piece. [%cal Gf1c4,Gc4f7] }
( 5... Nxd8 $2
{ Taking back with the knight looks tidy, since the king keeps castling, but the knight was the only guard of e5. [Nxe5] wins a pawn, and Black's knight on d8 is out of play. In queenless positions a pawn is a big deal: Black has to suffer for a long time to hold this. }
6. Nxe5 ) 6. Nc3 ( 6. Bc4
{ The bishop aims at f7, the soft spot next to Black's displaced king. Black should take care of that diagonal before anything else. [f6] gives the king air, shuts out a knight on g5 and solidly protects e5; [Ke8] tucks the king back and covers f7. White will go on with [Nc3], [Be3] and long castling, keeping a small but lasting edge: Black's lost castling rights are a nuisance, though the position stays solid with care. Developing the kingside pieces before dealing with f7 is where most players go wrong here. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gf7f6,Gd8e8] }
6... f6 ( 6... Ke8 ) ( 6... Nf6 $2
{ Natural development, but it ignores the battery on f7. [Ng5] attacks the pawn a second time and threatens to fork the king and the rook on h8 with the knight. Black can't hold everything: after [Ng5 Ke8 Nxf7], White has won a pawn and the knight is deep in Black's camp. Before developing the knight, Black had to deal with f7, with [Ke8] for example. }
7. Ng5 Ke8 8. Nxf7 ) ( 6... Be6 $2
{ Offering to trade the bishop seems logical, but it ruins Black's pawns. After [Bxe6 fxe6], Black has doubled, isolated e-pawns, and e5 and e6 are long-term targets for White's knights and rooks. With the queens already off, structure is what matters most, and White is clearly better. [f6] or [Ke8] kept everything solid. Before you offer a trade, look at the pawn that will take back and what it leaves behind. }
7. Bxe6 fxe6 ) ) ( 6. Be3 ) ( 6. Nxe5 $4
{ The pawn on e5 looked free, but the knight on c6 defends it. After [Nxe5], White has given a knight for a pawn and has no follow-up. In these queenless positions every piece counts, and White's real trumps were development and long castling with tempo on Black's king. Before grabbing a pawn, always count who defends it: one look at c6 was enough here. }
6... Nxe5 ) ( 6. Bb5
{ The bishop pins the knight on c6, and that knight is all that holds e5. Black has to keep the pin harmless: [Bd6] guards e5 a second time, and [f6] props it up with a pawn. Both keep the material level. The natural [Bd7] to unpin is the move that fails. [%csl Yc6,Ye5][%cal Gf8d6,Gf7f6] }
