7. Qh5+ g6 8. Qxg6+ Ke7 9. Qf7+ Kd6 10. Bf4+ Ne5 11. Qd5+ Ke7 12. Qxe5# ) ) ) (
4. Bb5
{ The Relfsson Gambit. White pins the knight on c6 instead of taking back on d4, hoping to win the pawn later with [Nxd4] or to castle and attack. It isn't dangerous: on b5 the bishop doesn't aim at f7, so it does less than in the Scotch Gambit. Black can keep the pawn with [Bc5], ask the bishop what it wants with [a6], or develop with [Nf6], hitting e4. The simplest plan is to develop the kingside quickly, castle, and only then worry about the pawn on d4. Black is at least equal. [%cal Gf8c5,Ga7a6,Gg8f6] }
