5. O-O ) ) 4... Nf6
{ The Schmidt Variation, the most common answer at every level. The knight attacks e4, and White must decide how to deal with it. The main move is [Nxc6]: after [Nxc6 bxc6], Black has doubled c-pawns, and White can push [e5] to chase the knight from f6, the sharp Mieses Variation, or defend calmly with [Bd3] or [Nd2]. [Nc3] defends the pawn with a piece and leads to the Scotch Four Knights, where Black usually pins with [Bb4+]. One rule to remember: don't defend e4 with a piece that blocks the queen's protection of the knight on d4, which so far has no other defender. [%csl Ye4,Yd4][%cal Gd4c6,Gb1c3] }
( 4... Bc5 5. Nxc6
{ White trades knights to end the pressure on d4. Black has a choice of recaptures, and an in-between move. [Qf6], the Intermezzo Variation, is the most popular: it threatens mate on f2, so White has no time to save the knight, and Black recaptures next move. [bxc6] is natural too, aiming for [d5] with an active bishop on c5. [dxc6] opens the bishop on c8 but allows [dxc6 Qxd8+ Kxd8], a slightly worse endgame where Black can't castle. Watch out for flashy checks on f2: they only work with backup. [%cal Gf6f2] }
( 5. Be3 Qf6 6. c3 Nge7 7. Bc4
{ The main line. White's bishop controls d5, so Black's freeing [d5] is harder to arrange, and it eyes f7. Black has several good setups. [Ne5] attacks the bishop and keeps the knights active: after [Ne5 Be2], Black often goes on with [Ne5 Be2 Qg6]. [O-O] and [b6] with the bishop to b7 are calm developing plans, and [Bb6] removes the bishop from any trick on d4. White's plan is to castle, play [Nd2] and sometimes [f4] to gain space, while Black looks for [d5] or [f5] to free the position. [%cal Gc6e5,Ge8g8,Gb7b6,Gc5b6] }
