{ White castles and leaves the pawn on e4 hanging on purpose. Black's queen and knight are active, but Black's king is still in the center. The solid answers are [d6], [O-O] or [d5], finishing development first. After that, White's typical plan is [f3] to secure e4, [Kh1], and a later [f4] to push the knight away, while Black looks for [f5] to open the f-file. Engines rate it about equal, with White having a little more space. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d6,Ge8g8,Gd7d5] }
( 9. f4 $2
{ The pawn hits the knight, but it opens the way for Black's queen. [Qxg2] attacks the rook, and after [Qxg2 Rg1 Qxe4] Black has won two pawns and White's king has no safe place left. Castle first, then push the f-pawn. }
