{ Grabbing e4 is the well-known poisoned pawn of this line. After [Nd2], the queen has to keep moving: [Nd2 Qg6 Bh5] hits it again, and White's knights and rooks join the attack with tempo while Black's king is still on e8. White has plenty for the pawn. Castle or play [d6] first. Pawns taken in front of an undeveloped king often cost more than they bring. }
10. Nd2 Qg6 11. Bh5 ) 10. f3 ( 10. f4 ) ) 5... Qf6
{ The Intermezzo. Black threatens [Qxf2#], and the knight on c6 is still attacked twice, so White has to stop the mate first and give the knight back. [Qf3] offers a queen trade, [Qd2] keeps the queens on and covers f2 from the side, [Qe2] does the same, and [f4] blocks the queen's line. Black then takes on c6, usually with the d-pawn, as in [Qd2 dxc6], to open the bishop on c8. Trying to save the knight, or blocking the bishop's line with another piece, loses material or the structure. [%csl Rf2][%cal Gd1f3,Gd1d2,Gd1e2] }
