{ Trading on c6 against 4.[Bc5] lacks the bite it carries against 4.[Nf6], because Black's bishop is already actively posted on c5 eyeing f2 while the c6 pawn supports a future [b:d5] break. White's most accurate continuation is [Bd3], solidly defending e4 before castling short and playing [w:Nc3] or [w:Nd2] and [w:Qe2]. [Nc3] first is also completely sound, whereas putting the bishop on c4 leaves e4 unguarded and invites the energetic [b:Qh4] sortie. [%cal Gf1d3,Gb1c3] }
