6... Qxf2# ) ) ( 4... Qh4
{ The Steinitz Variation. The queen attacks e4 at once, and the pawn is harder to defend than it looks. The main move is [Nc3], defending with a piece, though after [Nc3 Bb4] the knight is pinned and White usually gives up the pawn for development and attacking chances. [Nb5], the Horwitz Attack, counterattacks c7 at once, and [Nf3], the Fraser Variation, and [Be3], the Braune Variation, offer the pawn in other ways. Black's queen comes out early and can become a target, which is White's main compensation. Watch the knight on d4: it is defended only by the queen. [%csl Ye4,Yd4][%cal Gb1c3,Gd4b5] }
5. Nc3
{ White defends e4 with the knight. Black's main reply is [Bb4], pinning that knight so that e4 is really attacked again: after [Bb4 Be2 Qxe4], Black wins the pawn, but White gets real compensation with [Bb4 Be2 Qxe4 Nb5], hitting c7, and rapid development while Black's queen and king are exposed. Other moves let White consolidate with [Be2] or [Be3] and castling, leaving Black's queen misplaced on h4. Don't take on e4 before the pin: the knight on c3 still guards it. [%csl Yc3,Ye4][%cal Gf8b4] }
( 5. Nb5
{ The Horwitz Attack. White doesn't defend e4 and instead threatens [Nxc7+], a fork. Black has three good answers. [Bb4+] gains time, and after [Bb4+ Bd2 Qxe4+] takes the pawn with check. [Bc5] defends c7 indirectly: if White grabs it, Black mates on f2, so White must look after f2 first. [Qxe4+] takes at once, and after [Qxe4+ Be2] Black should go on with [Qxe4+ Be2 Bb4+] rather than walking the king. Guarding c7 with the king, [Kd8], is playable but passive: Black gives up castling and gets nothing for it. [%csl Yc7][%cal Gf8b4,Gf8c5,Gh4e4] }
