6... Qxe4+
{ Black has taken e4 with check. [Be2] blocks and keeps the queens on: the threat on c7 and White's lead in development are the compensation for the pawn, and they only work with queens on the board. White castles next and brings the rook to e1. [%csl Ye4,Yc7][%cal Gf1e2] }
( 6... Bc5 ) ( 6... Bxd2+ $2
{ Trading bishops brings White's knight from b1 into play with gain of time. After [Nxd2], e4 is protected by the knight, the fork on c7 is still threatened, and Black's queen has nothing to attack. Black has lost time and has nothing to show for the early queen trip. The right move was to take on e4 with check first, and trade bishops only once White's king had castled. }
