5... Qxe4+ 6. Ne3 ) 5... Bb4
{ The knight on c3 is pinned, so the pawn on e4 is really attacked. White's main move is [Be2], giving up e4 for development: after [Be2 Qxe4 Nb5], the knight hits c7 and White gets a lead in development and pressure against Black's king. Black usually gives up the bishop on c3 and plays the king to d8, keeping the extra pawn. [Qd3] defends e4 and keeps the material, at the cost of a less harmonious setup. Be careful with the knight on d4: it is defended only by the queen, so a bishop on d2 or d3 hangs it. [%csl Yc3,Ye4][%cal Gf1e2,Gd1d3] }
