6. Be3
{ White has defended the knight and set a trap: a knight jump to f3 or f5 would hit the queen and uncover the bishop on e3 against c5. Black has to defuse it now. [Bb4] gets the bishop out of the line and pins the knight on c3, reaching the main line a move late. Any quiet move that leaves the bishop on c5 loses a piece. [%csl Rc5,Rh4][%cal Gc5b4] }
6... Bb4 ( 6... Nge7 $4
{ Development, but it ignores the discovered attack. [Nf3] hits the queen and uncovers the bishop on e3 against c5. After [Nf3 Qh5 g4] the queen is chased again, and the bishop on c5 falls. Get the bishop out of the line with a move to b4 first. }
7. Nf3 Qh5 8. g4 ) ) ( 5... Nf6 $4
{ The knight develops, but it blocks the queen's way back along the diagonal to d8. White hunts her down: [g3] or [Nf5] hits her, and after [Nf5 Qh5 Be2 Qg6 Nh4] she has no square left. With the queen out early, always keep a retreat open for her. }
6. Nf5 Qh5 7. Be2 Qg6 8. Nh4 ) 6. Be2 ( 6. Qd3 ) ( 6. Bd2 $4
{ Unpinning with the bishop cuts the queen off from the knight on d4, which was defended only by the queen. [Nxd4] wins the knight, and White's whole setup collapses, with e4 still loose too. With a knight on d4, neither d2 nor d3 is a square for a bishop. White should give up the pawn with the bishop to e2, getting fast development, or defend it with the queen from d3. }
6... Nxd4 ) ( 6. Bd3 $4
{ The bishop defends e4, but it closes the d-file, and now nothing defends the knight on d4: [Nxd4] wins a piece. With a knight on d4, the d-file must stay open for the queen. The two good moves were the bishop to e2, which gives up the pawn on e4 for development and attacking chances, and the queen to d3, which defends the pawn while keeping an eye on d4. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gc6d4] }
6... Nxd4 ) ( 6. g3 $2
{ [g3] kicks the queen, but it doesn't defend e4, and the knight on c3 is pinned, so it can't either. [Qxe4+] takes the pawn with check, and after [Qxe4+ Qe2 Qxe2+] Black is a clean pawn up without any of the usual risks. Give up e4 with the bishop on e2 instead, and get development for it. }
6... Qxe4+ 7. Qe2 Qxe2+ ) 6... Qxe4
{ Black has won the pawn, and White must use the time. [Nb5] is the main line: the knight from d4 jumps out and attacks c7, and Black's best is to give up the bishop on c3 with check and then play the king to d8. [Be3] is a calmer alternative that develops and protects d4, keeping more pieces on. Don't forget that the knight on d4 is still attacked by the knight on c6 and the queen on e4, and defended only by White's queen, so castling right now is a blunder. [%csl Yd4,Yc7][%cal Gd4b5,Gc1e3] }
7. Nb5
{ The knight threatens [Nxc7+], forking the king and the rook on a8. Black's main defense is [Bxc3+], and after [Bxc3+ bxc3 Kd8] the king covers c7 itself. It looks odd, but Black keeps an extra pawn and the king is fairly safe in the middle as long as Black doesn't get greedy. [Kd8] at once also works. White's compensation is the bishop pair, quick castling and open lines against the king, with the bishop coming to f3 and a rook to e1 or b1. [%cal Gb5c7] }
( 7. Be3 ) ( 7. O-O $4
{ Castling forgets the knight on d4, attacked by the queen and the knight on c6 and defended only by White's queen. [Qxd4] wins it, and if White's queen takes back, the knight on c6 recaptures. Black is a piece and a pawn up. The knight had to move with gain of time first, to b5 where it hits c7, or be defended by the bishop from e3. }
7... Qxd4 ) 7... Bxc3+ ( 7... Kd8 ) ( 7... Qxg2 $2
{ Grabbing a second pawn leaves c7 to its fate. [Bf3] kicks the queen, and after [Bf3 Qh3 Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8], White has won the rook. The knight on a8 may be lost later, but White comes out ahead in material with a big lead in development. Cover c7 first. In gambit positions, ask what your opponent threatens before you take a second pawn. }
8. Bf3 Qh3 9. Nxc7+ Kd8 10. Nxa8 ) ( 7... Ba5 $4
{ The bishop covers c7 and keeps the pin on c3, but it can't do both jobs. [Nxc7+] forces the bishop to take on c7, the pin is gone, and the knight on c3 takes the queen: [Nxc7+ Bxc7 Nxe4]. Trade on c3 with check first, then the king guards c7. [%cal Gb5c7,Gc3e4] }
8. Nxc7+ Bxc7 9. Nxe4 ) 8. bxc3
{ White's pawns are doubled, but the threat on c7 is still there. Black's answer is [Kd8]: the king protects c7 and Black keeps the extra pawn. The price is a king stuck in the center for a while, and White's bishops and rooks will try to reach it with [O-O], [Re1] and [Bf3], hitting the queen with tempo. Black's job is to develop the knights, tuck the king toward c8 if needed, and trade pieces. Any queen move that ignores c7 loses the rook. [%csl Yc7][%cal Ge8d8] }
8... Kd8 ( 8... Qxg2 $4
{ Greed again, and this time White doesn't even need to hurry. The bishop kicks the queen and the knight takes on c7: after [Bf3 Qg6 Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8], White has won a whole rook, and Black's queen is far from the defense. Two pawns don't make up for it. The king had to cover c7 first with [Kd8], and only then could Black think about more material. }
9. Bf3 Qg6 10. Nxc7+ Kd8 11. Nxa8 ) ( 8... Qe5 $4
{ The queen retreats to a central square, but it doesn't protect c7, and the fork is still there. [f4] kicks it again, and after [f4 Qc5 Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8], White wins the rook on a8 while Black's queen is out of play. The king move to d8 was the only way to hold c7, even though it costs castling: in this line the king is safe enough in the middle. }
9. f4 Qc5 10. Nxc7+ Kd8 11. Nxa8 ) 9. O-O
{ The main line of the Steinitz. Black is a pawn up, but the king is on d8 and the queen on e4 is exposed. White's plan is to open lines quickly: [Bf3] hits the queen, [Re1] gains time, and the bishop on c1 comes to a3 or g5. Black's best is [Nf6], developing and covering the kingside, then [Re8] to fight for the e-file and trades to reach an endgame where the extra pawn counts. Slow moves like developing the knight to e7 let White's pieces in. In practice White's activity is often worth more than the pawn, which makes this line dangerous at club level. [%csl Ye4,Yd8][%cal Gg8f6] }
9... Nf6 ) ( 4... Bb4+
{ The Malaniuk Variation. The check looks odd, but it has a point: after [c3], White's pawn takes c3 away from the queen's knight, and the bishop retreats to c5 or e7 having lost no real time. White can also block with [Bd2], keeping c3 free and offering a trade of bishops, or with [Nc3], entering positions similar to the Four Knights. None of these refutes it, but the Malaniuk avoids the most heavily analyzed Scotch lines, which is its main practical appeal. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gc2c3,Gc1d2,Gb1c3] }
5. c3
{ The pawn on c3 gains a tempo on the bishop, but it also takes the knight's best square. Black's main move is [Bc5], putting the bishop where it attacks d4 and f2; White answers [Be3] or trades on c6, and the game resembles the Classical Variation with the extra move [c3] for White. [Be7] is a solid alternative, preparing [Nf6] and castling. White's queen's knight usually develops to d2, and the plan is quick castling and a central pawn advance. [%csl Yc3,Yd4][%cal Gb4c5,Gb4e7] }
( 5. Bd2 ) ( 5. Nc3 ) 5... Bc5
{ The bishop is back on c5, hitting d4 again, and White's pawn on c3 has taken the best square of the queen's knight. [Be3] defends and sets the same trap as in the Classical: a careless Black move, and [Nxc6] uncovers the bishop on e3 against c5. [Nxc6] at once, with [Bd3] and castling, is just as good, and [Nf5], hitting g7, is a sharper try. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc1e3,Gd4c6,Gd4f5] }
( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... Ba5 $6
{ On a5 the bishop is out of play and does nothing against d4. White trades on c6 and the queens with [Nxc6 dxc6 Qxd8+ Kxd8], and Black, unable to castle, faces a worse endgame with a poor bishop; [b4] can also harass it. The bishop belonged on c5 or e7. A bishop check is only useful if the bishop has a good square to go back to, and here it doesn't. }
6. Nxc6 dxc6 7. Qxd8+ Kxd8 ) 6. Be3
{ Black has to take the bishop out of the line of e3. [Bb6] does it, and Black goes on with [Nf6], castling, a rook to e8 and [d5]: on any trade, Black takes back toward the centre. Moves that leave the bishop on c5 behind the knight on c6 lose a piece. [%csl Yc5,Ye3][%cal Gc5b6] }
( 6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. Bd3
{ White has traded on c6 and guards e4 with the bishop. With no white knight on f3, Black's queen can go to h4 without being chased by a minor piece: [Qh4] hits f2 and eyes h2, and [Nf6] with [Ng4] can follow. White castles and keeps the e-pawn back until the kingside is safe. [%csl Yf2,Ye4][%cal Gd8h4] }
7... Qh4 8. O-O Nf6
{ With Black's queen on h4, bishop on c5, and knight on f6, Black threatens [b:Ng4] creating simultaneous mating threats on h2 and double attacks on f2! Pushing [e5] only accelerates disaster after [e5 Ng4], so White must reinforce the kingside dark and light squares right now. [Qf3] is very clean, guarding f2, stopping [b:Ng4] cold, and challenging Black's active queen, while [Qe2] defends e4 and f2 laterally so [Qe2 Ng4] can be met safely by [w:h3]. [%cal Gd1f3,Gd1e2] }
9. Qf3 ( 9. Qe2 ) ( 9. e5 $4
{ The pawn kicks the knight, but the knight was happy to go: [Ng4] hits h2 with mate and f2 with the bishop behind it. White can't cover both, and stopping the mate with [h3] lets Black take on f2. Guard the kingside first with the queen on f3 or e2. }
9... Ng4 ) ) ( 6. Nf5 ) 6... Bb6 ( 6... Nf6 $4
{ Natural, but the bishop on c5 is still loose behind the knight on c6. [Nxc6] attacks the queen and uncovers the bishop on e3: after [Nxc6 bxc6 Bxc5] White is a piece up. It is the same trick as in the Classical after [Be3], and it catches a lot of players. [%cal Gd4c6,Ge3c5] }
7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. Bxc5 ) ) ( 4... Qf6
{ The queen attacks the knight on d4 a second time, together with the knight on c6. White must react, and many natural moves lose a piece. [Nb5] counterattacks c7, so Black has to answer with [Bc5], threatening mate on f2. [Be3] defends the knight and usually transposes to the Classical after [Be3 Bc5]. [Nf3] and [Nxc6] simply avoid the pressure. The queen on f6 takes the best square from Black's knight, which is the main drawback of this setup, and White can gain time against it later. [%csl Yd4,Yf6][%cal Gd4b5,Gc1e3] }
5. Nb5
{ White threatens [Nxc7+], forking king and rook. Black's best is [Bc5], which answers a threat with a bigger one: mate on f2. White must defend with [Qe2] or [Qf3], and then Black covers c7 with the bishop retreat to b6 or with the king. [Qd8], going back home, is safe but admits that the queen trip was a waste of time. The position stays roughly balanced if Black keeps finding these active answers. [%csl Yc7,Yf2][%cal Gf8c5,Gf6d8] }
( 5. Be3 ) ( 5. Nf3 ) ( 5. Nxc6 ) ( 5. Nc3 $4
{ The knight develops, but d4 has two attackers, the queen and the knight on c6, and one defender. [Qxd4] wins the knight: after [Qxd4 Qxd4 Nxd4], the queens are traded and Black is a knight up. Count the attackers on d4 before every move here. White had to defend the knight with [Be3], move it with [Nb5], [Nf3] or [Nb3], or trade it on c6. }
5... Qxd4 ) ( 5. Bc4 $4
{ The bishop develops toward f7, but d4 is attacked twice and defended once, just as after a knight move to c3. [Qxd4] wins a knight, and the queen trade after [Qxd4 Qxd4 Nxd4] leaves Black a piece up. The queen on f6 was attacking something, so White had to answer the threat: defend the knight from e3, or move it, preferably to b5 with a counterattack on c7. }
5... Qxd4 ) 5... Bc5 ( 5... Qd8 ) ( 5... Kd8 $2
{ The king covers c7, but it gives up castling for good and gets in the way of Black's own pieces. White simply develops with [Be2] or [N1c3], and Black's king and queen are both misplaced, so White keeps gaining time against them. White is clearly better. The bishop move to c5, with its threat of mate on f2, defended c7 actively instead. }
6. Be2 ) ( 5... Bb4+ $2
{ The check gets the bishop out, but after [N1c3] it has no good follow-up, and the threat on c7 is back. Black has to keep defending while White develops with tempo. The threat of mate on f2 from c5 was the right way to gain time. In this line Black's queen and bishop must work together against f2, or the knight on b5 keeps the initiative. }
6. N1c3 ) ) ( 4... Nxd4
{ Trading knights on d4 is very common in club play, even though masters avoid it because it brings White's queen into the middle with gain of time. [Qxd4] is the only real move: any non-capture leaves Black a clean piece ahead. With the c6 knight gone, no minor piece can easily chase White's queen from d4, so she commands both wings while White prepares [w:Nc3], [w:Be3] or [w:Bf4], and long castling. [%cal Gd1d4] }
5. Qxd4
{ White's queen dominates the center, and the knight that usually chases it from c6 is gone. Black has no central pawn and must find a way to develop. [Qf6] is the most active answer: it offers a queen trade and takes the sting out of White's lead. If White avoids it, the queen goes to c3 or c4, or White pushes [e5], gaining space. [d6] is the solid choice, preparing [Nf6] and [Be7], but White keeps a pleasant edge with [Nc3], [Be3] or [Bf4], long castling and a kingside pawn storm. Moves that develop the knight to f6 at once, or chase the queen with a pawn, are the typical club mistakes here. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gd4g7] }
5... Qf6
{ Black offers a queen trade to take the sting out of White's lead. White usually keeps the queens with [Qc3] or [Qc4], where the queen still eyes the queenside and stays out of reach. [e5] gains space with tempo: after [e5 Qb6 Qxb6 axb6] the queens are gone, but the pawn on e5 stays a thorn in Black's side. Black can't easily play [d6], since after the exchanges on d6 Black is left with an isolated pawn there, and White's [Nc3], [Bf4] and long castling put pressure on it. White keeps a small edge in every line. [%cal Gd4c3,Gd4c4,Ge4e5] }
( 5... d6
{ Black plays solidly: the pawn covers e5, so the knight can come to f6 without being kicked, and the bishop on c8 can breathe. White is still better thanks to the centralized queen and the extra space. A clear plan is [Nc3], [Bf4] or [Be3], long castling, then a kingside pawn storm with [f3], [g4] and [g5] against a king that castles short; Black's counterplay on the other wing comes too slowly. [Bd3], the Ghulam-Kassim Variation, is a calmer setup with short castling, but it gives Black an easier game than the plans above. [%cal Ge1c1,Gg2g4,Gh2h4] }
6. Nc3
{ Developing [Nc3] guards e4, controls d5, and gets ready for [w:Bf4] or [w:Be3] followed by queenside castling. Because Black traded off the c6 knight early, White enjoys a lasting space edge and free piece play. Black's cleanest setup is [Nf6] followed by [b:Be7] and [b:O-O], keeping a compact defensive shell; [Ne7], planning [b:Nc6] to challenge the centralised queen on d4, and [Be6] are playable alternatives, though White stays comfortably better. [%cal Gg8f6,Gg8e7,Gc8e6] }
( 6. Bf4 ) ( 6. Bd3
{ The Ghulam-Kassim Variation. Placing the bishop on d3 overprotects e4 and clears the kingside for rapid short castling, though standing in front of the d-file is gentler than long-castling setups with [Nc3] and [Bf4]. Black develops naturally with [Nf6] followed by [Be7] and [O-O], aiming for [Be6] or [c5] to contest the centralized queen; [c6] is a solid companion idea that prepares [Qb6] to offer a queen trade. [%csl Yd3,Ye4][%cal Gg8f6] }
6... Nf6 ( 6... c6 ) ) ( 6. Bc4
{ Putting the bishop on c4 aims at f7 and clears the kingside for rapid short castling, though the bishop stands exposed to a later [b:Be6] or a pawn push to d5. Black equalises much more comfortably here than against queenside castling setups: [Ne7] prepares [b:Nc6] and [b:Be6] to contest both central pieces at once, [Nf6] followed by [b:Be7] and [b:O-O] develops smoothly, and [c6] blunts the diagonal while preparing [b:Be6]. [%cal Gg8e7,Gg8f6,Gc7c6] }
6... Ne7 ( 6... Nf6 ) ( 6... c6 ) ) 6... Nf6 ( 6... Ne7 ) ( 6... Be6 ) ) ( 5...
c5 $2
{ [c5] kicks the queen, but it develops nothing and leaves a hole on d5 and a backward pawn on d7. The queen steps back with [Qe3] or [Qd3], and Black's d-pawn can't safely reach d5, since e4 and soon a knight on c3 control that square. It will sit on d6 as a backward pawn on an open file, a target for [Nc3], [Bf4] and a rook on d1. [%csl Rd5,Rd6] }
6. Qe3 ) ( 5... Nf6 $2
{ The knight has no good square once White pushes [e5]. [e5 Nh5 g4] traps it on the edge, [e5 Ng4 Qxg4] loses it to the queen along the fourth rank, and [e5 Nd5 Qxd5] is no better. Only [e5 c5 Qe3], kicking the queen first, keeps the material, and even then the knight is stuck and the pawn on e5 cramps Black's whole position. Prepare the knight with [d6] first. [%cal Ge4e5] }
6. e5 c5 7. Qe3 ) ( 5... b6 $2
{ [b6] prepares the bishop on b7, but it costs a move while Black's kingside pieces are still at home and the queen on d4 still rules the board. After [Nc3 Bb7 Qe5+], Black has to block the check and is far behind in development. Develop the kingside first, with [d6] and [Nf6], or offer the queen trade with [Qf6]. }
6. Nc3 Bb7 7. Qe5+ ) ( 5... Be7 $4
{ The bishop develops, but it stops guarding g7, which the queen on d4 was already looking at. [Qxg7] takes the pawn, and after [Qxg7 Bf6 Qg3] White is a pawn up, the g-file is open in front of Black's kingside and short castling is no longer safe. In the Lolli, every Black move has to account for the queen on d4. }
6. Qxg7 Bf6 7. Qg3 ) ( 5... Qe7 $2
{ Putting the queen on e7 pins nothing and blocks Black's own dark-squared bishop on f8, turning development into a traffic jam. After [Nc3 c6 Be2 Qc5 Qd3], White's pieces pour out effortlessly while Black's kingside stays bottled up. Play [d6] or challenge the diagonal with [Qf6] instead. [%csl Ye7,Yf8][%cal Rd8e7,Gb1c3] }
6. Nc3 c6 7. Be2 Qc5 8. Qd3 ) 6. Qc3 ( 6. Qc4 ) ( 6. e5 ) ) ( 4... Nge7 $2
{ On e7 the knight blocks the bishop on f8 and puts no pressure on White at all. White answers with [Nc3], developing and eyeing d5 and b5, and is simply ahead in development: Black's pieces get in each other's way and the bishop needs extra moves to come out. In open positions like this one, the knight's natural home is f6, where it hits e4 at once. }
5. Nc3 ) ( 4... Qe7 $2
{ The queen blocks the bishop on f8 and does little on e7. White develops with [Nc3], then [Be2] and [Bg5] or a knight jump to d5 that hits the queen. Black has lost time and has no target, while White's pieces flow out naturally. Develop the minor pieces first, ideally with a threat: [Nf6] or [Bc5]. Every move spent on the queen now is a move your knights and bishops are still waiting for. }
5. Nc3 ) ( 4... Ne5 $2
{ The knight dodges the trade on c6, but it moves to a square where White's f-pawn kicks it with gain of time. After [f4 Nc6], the knight has spent three moves to come back, and White has two central pawns side by side on e4 and f4. Use the time to develop with a threat: [Nf6] or [Bc5]. }
5. f4 Nc6 ) 5. Nxc6 ( 5. Nc3
{ This is the Scotch Four Knights, also reached from the Four Knights Game. White defends e4 with a piece and keeps the knight on d4. Black's most natural move is [Bb4], pinning the knight on c3 so that e4 is attacked again. [Bc5] is the other active choice, hitting the knight on d4. A passive move like [d6] lets White build a comfortable game, either with [Be2] and short castling or with [Be3], [Qd2] and long castling, and White keeps a pleasant space advantage thanks to the pawn on e4. [%csl Yc3,Yd4][%cal Gf8b4,Gf8c5] }
5... Bb4
{ The pin on c3 means e4 is attacked by the knight on f6 and defended by nothing that can move. White's main move is [Nxc6]: after [Nxc6 bxc6 Bd3], the bishop defends e4 without blocking anything, because the knight on d4 is gone. That's the key point of the whole line: as long as the knight stands on d4, it needs the queen's protection. [Bg5] is an aggressive alternative that pins in return. After the trade on c6, Black usually plays [d5] and castles, with active pieces against White's better pawns. [%csl Ye4,Yd4][%cal Gd4c6,Gc1g5] }
( 5... Bc5
{ The bishop hits the knight on d4 and eyes f2. [Be3] defends, usually with [Bb6] from Black and [Qd2] with long castling from White. [Nxc6] with [Bd3] leads to quieter play: Black should take back with the b-pawn, since taking with the d-pawn allows a queen trade that leaves the king in the centre. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc1e3,Gd4c6,Gd4b3] }
6. Be3 ( 6. Nxc6 ) ( 6. Nb3 ) ) ( 5... d5 $2
{ Striking back in the center looks thematic, but with Black's king still on e8 the c6-knight cannot hold d5 under a pin. White plays [Bb5], threatening to win on c6, and after [Bb5 Bd7 exd5 Nxd4 Bxd7+ Qxd7 Qxd4] White emerges a clean pawn ahead with dominant central pieces. Pin with [b:Bb4] or develop [b:Bc5] instead. [%csl Rc6,Rd5][%cal Gf1b5] }
6. Bb5 Bd7 7. exd5 ) 6. Nxc6
{ Take back toward the centre: [bxc6] supports a later [d5] and keeps the queens on. The bishop on b4 still pins the knight on c3, so e4 stays under fire and White has to guard it with [Bd3]. Taking with the d-pawn lets White trade queens with check: Black's king loses castling, and White's four pawns against three on the kingside give a small, lasting edge. [%csl Yc6,Ye4][%cal Gb7c6] }
( 6. Bg5 ) ( 6. Bd3 $4
{ The bishop defends e4, but it blocks the queen's line to d4, and that knight had no other defender. [Nxd4] wins it, and the knight on c3 can't help because it is pinned by the bishop on b4. This is the key trap of the Scotch Four Knights. The right order is [Nxc6] first, removing the knight from d4, and only then the bishop to d3, where it no longer blocks anything. }
6... Nxd4 ) ( 6. Bd2 $4
{ Unpinning the knight looks sensible, but the bishop on d2 cuts the queen off from the knight on d4, just as a bishop on d3 would. [Nxd4] wins a piece, and e4 is still loose as well. With a knight on d4, neither d2 nor d3 is a square for a bishop. Trading on c6 first, or the active counter-pin with the bishop on g5, kept everything protected. }
6... Nxd4 ) 6... bxc6 7. Bd3
{ White defends e4 with the bishop, which is safe now that the knight on d4 is gone. Black's main plan is [d5], opening the bishop on c8 and using the extra central pawn, then castling. [O-O] first is just as good. Taking on c3 with [Bxc3+] is a tempting but dubious idea: it doubles White's pawns, but it gives up the dark-squared bishop in an open position, and White's two bishops are worth more than the damaged structure, especially since a later [c4] can undouble the pawns. Keep the bishop unless you win something for it. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5,Ge8g8] }
7... d5 ( 7... O-O
{ Black castles short before pushing the d-pawn, tucking the king out of the open e-file so [Re8] and [d5] will come with maximum force. White should match that immediately with [O-O]: once the king is safe, [Bg5], [Qf3], or rerouting the knight via [Ne2] and [Ng3] puts healthy pressure on Black's kingside and doubled c-pawns. Advancing the e-pawn prematurely while White's king still sits on e1 only hands Black a target along the e-file. [%csl Ye1][%cal Ge1g1] }
8. O-O ( 8. e5 $2
{ The pawn kicks the knight, but White isn't ready for it. [Re8] attacks it at once and pins it to White's king, and the knight can come back to d5. Black wins the pawn or gets a free game. Castle first. }
8... Re8 ) ) 8. exd5
{ Releasing the central tension with [exd5] opens the e-file toward Black's uncastled king on e8, so Black must choose the right order. Recapturing [cxd5] undoubles the pawns at once, gives Black a healthy central presence, and prepares [b:O-O] plus [b:c5]. Castling [O-O] first is equally accurate: if White grabs c6 with [O-O dxc6], [b:Re8+] forces an awkward block on the open file. [Qe7+] is a solid endgame-oriented option. [%cal Gc6d5,Ge8g8,Gd8e7] }
8... cxd5
{ Recapturing [cxd5] undoubles Black's pawns and establishes a firm stake in the centre, while the bishop on b4 keeps pressure on c3. White castles [O-O] to tuck the king away and prepare [w:Bg5] or [w:Qf3] against the d5 pawn and f6 knight. Starting with [h3] to rule out [b:Bg4] before castling, or checking with [Qe2+] to force [Qe2+ Be7] and misplace the dark-squared bishop slightly, both lead to balanced middlegames. [%cal Ge1g1,Gh2h3,Gd1e2] }
( 8... O-O ) ( 8... Qe7+ ) 9. O-O ( 9. h3 ) ( 9. Qe2+ ) 9... O-O 10. Bg5
{ A classical Scotch Four Knights position. White pins the knight on f6, and the usual plan is [Qf3] to add pressure on f6 and d5, then reroute the knight via e2 and d4 toward f5, next to Black's king. Black has active pieces and a pawn on d5 that is well supported. [c6] makes it rock solid and frees the queen from defending it; [Be6] develops and covers d5 too. Black's counterplay comes from the half-open b-file, with [Rb8] pressing on b2, and from the bishop on b4. The position is balanced; careful play by both sides is rewarded. [%csl Yd5,Yf6][%cal Gc7c6,Gc8e6] }
10... c6 ( 10... Be6 ) ( 10... h6 $2
{ Asking the bishop looks natural, but White just takes. After [Bxf6 Qxf6 Nxd5], the pawn on d5 is gone and the knight hits the queen. Grabbing b2 back with her runs into a rook on b1. Hold d5 first, with [c6] or [Be6]. }
11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. Nxd5 ) ) ( 5. Bd3 $4
{ The bishop protects e4, which was attacked, but it stands on the d-file between the queen and the knight on d4. That knight had only one defender, the queen, and now it has none: [Nxd4] wins a piece on the spot. This pattern appears all over the Scotch, so make it a habit: while a knight sits on d4, check the line from d1 to d4 before every move. Trading on c6 first, and only then developing the bishop to d3, was the way. }
5... Nxd4 ) ( 5. e5 $6
{ Pushing at once is the Alekhine Gambit, and it costs the e-pawn. With the knights still on the board, Black can simply take: after [Nxe5 Qe2 Qe7], White has no way to win the pawn back and Black is a clean pawn up. The push to e5 works only once the knights have been traded on c6, when no black piece can take it. }
5... Nxe5 6. Qe2 Qe7 ) ( 5. Bg5 $6
{ The pin looks active, but it leaves e4 without protection. After [h6], the bishop must decide: [h6 Bxf6 Qxf6] gives Black the bishop pair and a queen hitting d4 and f2, and [h6 Be3 Nxe4] simply wins a pawn. Deal with the attack on e4 first, by trading on c6 or defending with the knight from c3. Before you pin a piece, check what it was attacking: here the knight on f6 was already hitting e4. }
