[Event "Scotch Game (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 ( 4. Bc4
{ The Scotch Gambit. White doesn't hurry to take back on d4 and develops the bishop toward f7, the weakest point in Black's camp. Black can't really keep the extra pawn for long, so the question is how to develop. [Nf6] is the most common and leads to Two Knights positions, where Black hits e4 at once. [Bc5] protects d4 and invites [c3] or [Ng5]. [Bb4+] is the London Defense, and [Be7] a solid, Hungarian-style setup. In all lines Black must keep an eye on f7 and on knight jumps to g5. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gg8f6,Gf8c5,Gf8b4] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... Bc5
{ Black protects d4 with the bishop, which also eyes f2: this position is called the Haxo Gambit. White has three main plans. [c3] offers a second pawn to rebuild the center, and Black should answer [Nf6], reaching a well-known Italian line; the grab [c3 dxc3] is risky. [O-O] develops first and keeps [c3] in reserve. [Ng5], the Sarratt Variation, attacks f7 at once, and Black's knight must come to h6. Taking back on d4 with the knight right now is a mistake: it would be attacked twice and defended once. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc2c3,Ge1g1,Gf3g5] }
5. c3
{ White offers a second pawn to rebuild the center with [cxd4]. Black's best answer is [Nf6], which transposes to a main line of the Italian Game where Black is fine: after [Nf6 cxd4 Bb4+], Black gains time on the center. [d3] gives the pawn back in another way: the pawn on d3 gets in the way of White's development and leaves the bishop on c5 free to aim at f2. [d6] is solid. Taking the second pawn walks into a classic trick on f7. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg8f6,Gd4d3,Gd7d6] }
( 5. O-O
{ White castles first and keeps both [c3] and [e5] in reserve. If Black plays [Nf6], the push [Nf6 e5] leads to the Max Lange Attack, one of the sharpest lines of the open games. [d6] is the main alternative: it keeps e5 under control and prepares [Bg4] to pin the knight on f3, the Cochrane-Anderssen Variation once White plays [c3]. [Nge7] is solid. Black should keep an eye on f7: White's bishop and a knight on g5 can combine fast, and knight trips to the rim cost too much time. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd7d6,Gg8f6,Gg8e7] }
5... d6
{ Supporting e5 and c5 with [d6] sets up an old-school Scotch Gambit battle where Black holds onto the extra d4 pawn and prepares [b:Bg4] to pin White's f3 knight, avoiding the Two Knights transposition of 5.[Nf6]. White's sharpest test is [c3], challenging the d4 chain immediately so that [c3 dxc3 Nxc3] gives rapid piece play while [c3 Bg4] leads into the tactical Cochrane-Anderssen pin ([w:Qb3]). Do not sacrifice prematurely with [Bxf7+], which just hands Black two bishops after [Bxf7+ Kxf7]. [%cal Gc2c3] }
( 5... Nf6 ) ( 5... Nge7 ) ( 5... Na5 $2
{ Chasing the bishop on c4 costs too much time. White answers with [b4]: after [b4 Bxb4 Qxd4], White has the pawn back, the queen hits g7 and the knight on a5, and Black's pieces are scattered. A knight on the rim needs moves to come back, and in a gambit those moves decide the game. Bring your pieces toward the center first: hunting a bishop can wait until your king is safe. }
6. b4 Bxb4 7. Qxd4 ) ( 5... h6 $2
{ [h6] is a slow move while White's pieces are ready. [c3] offers a pawn to open the centre, and if Black takes, [Bxf7+] and a queen check on d5 win the bishop on c5 back with interest. Develop with [d6] or [Nf6] instead. }
6. c3 ) 6. c3 ( 6. Bxf7+ $4
{ The sacrifice has nothing behind it. After [Kxf7], a knight check on g5 sends the king to g8, and White's queen is too far away to follow up. White is a piece down for a pawn. }
6... Kxf7 ) ( 6. Ng5 $2
{ Going after f7 with [Ng5] comes too late once d6 has opened the c8-bishop's guard over g5 and cleared e5 for Black's knight. [Ne5] defends f7 while hitting White's c4-bishop with tempo, and after [Ne5 b4 Bb6 Bb3 h6] Black keeps the extra pawn comfortably. Undermine d4 with [c3] instead. [%csl Yf7,Yd6,Yg5][%cal Rf3g5,Gc6e5] }
6... Ne5 7. b4 Bb6 8. Bb3 h6 ) 6... Bg4
{ The Cochrane-Anderssen Variation. Black pins the knight on f3 and lets White open the center. [Qb3] is the most direct: the queen hits b7 and, together with the bishop, f7. After [Qb3 Bxf3 Bxf7+ Kf8 gxf3], both kings are exposed, and Black's pawn on d4 can become strong: accurate play is needed from both sides. [b4] gains space with tempo on the bishop, and [Be2] unpins calmly. Engines slightly prefer Black, but the positions are double-edged and practical chances are high. [%csl Yf3][%cal Gd1b3,Gb2b4,Gc4e2] }
7. Qb3 ( 7. b4 ) ( 7. Be2 ) ( 7. Bxf7+ $4
{ The bishop takes on f7 with check, but the knight check on g5 that should follow leads nowhere: after [Kxf7], the king steps to g8 and White has given a piece for a pawn. Hit f7 and b7 together with the queen on b3 first. }
7... Kxf7 ) ) ( 5. Ng5 ) ( 5. Nxd4 $4
{ The knight takes back, but on d4 it is attacked by the bishop and the knight on c6 and defended only by the queen. [Bxd4] wins a piece, since White's queen can't recapture: the knight on c6 would take it. In the Scotch Gambit, White recovers d4 only once the bishop on c5 is gone or with a pawn. Count attackers and defenders before you take back in the center: two against one loses the piece. }
5... Bxd4 ) ( 5. Bxf7+ $4
{ The bishop sacrifice has no follow-up. After [Kxf7 Ng5+ Ke8], White's knight and queen can't reach Black's king fast enough, the bishop on c5 is safe, and Black is simply a piece up. Sacrifices on f7 work when White can win the piece back, as with a queen check that also hits c5; here there is none. Before sacrificing on f7, make sure you can see how the material comes back. }
5... Kxf7 6. Ng5+ Ke8 ) 5... Nf6 ( 5... d3 ) ( 5... d6 ) ( 5... dxc3 $6
{ Taking the second pawn walks into a classic trick. [Bxf7+] draws the king out, and after [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qd5+], the queen checks and hits the loose bishop on c5. With [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qd5+ Kf8 Qxc5+], White wins back the piece, keeps a lead in development, and Black's king has lost castling. Return the pawn with [Nf6] instead. Greedy captures are risky when your own bishop stands loose on c5. [%cal Gc4f7,Gd1d5] }
6. Bxf7+ Kxf7 7. Qd5+
{ The queen checks and hits the bishop on c5. The king has to choose its square well. [Kf8] is the most reliable: White takes the bishop with check, Black blocks with [d6], and untangles later with [Nf6] and the king stepping back to f7. [Ke8] is also fine: White checks again on h5 before taking on c5. The king must not walk forward to g6 or f6. [%csl Rc5][%cal Gf7f8,Gf7e8] }
7... Kf8 ( 7... Ke8 ) ( 7... Kg6 $4
