8. Qf5# ) ( 7... Kf6 $4
{ The king walks into the open. [Bg5+] drives it up the board, and after [Bg5+ Kg6 Qf5+ Kh5] the pawn mates on g4. Go back to f8 or e8. }
8. Bg5+ Kg6 9. Qf5+ Kh5 10. g4# ) 8. Qxc5+ ) ) ( 4... Bb4+
{ The London Defense. Black checks to take advantage of c3 still being free. After [c3], Black can grab a second pawn with [c3 dxc3], giving White a big lead in development and open files in return. [Bd2] is a calmer answer that offers a trade of bishops and lets White take back on d4 later. In the gambit lines White's compensation is development, the bishop pair and attacking chances on the long diagonal, and Black must know exactly which pawns can be taken. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gc2c3,Gc1d2] }
5. c3
{ White offers a second pawn. Black usually takes it with [dxc3], and then White chooses between [dxc3 O-O], inviting a third grab on b2, and [dxc3 bxc3], recapturing toward the center and gaining time on the bishop. Retreating the bishop now is worse than it looks, because after [cxd4] White gets the ideal center for free and keeps all the benefits of the gambit without having given anything. If you play the London Defense with Black, taking on c3 is the consistent follow-up to the check. [%csl Yc3,Yb4][%cal Gd4c3] }
( 5. Bd2 ) 5... dxc3
{ Black is two pawns up for the moment. White's choice sets the tone. [O-O] offers a third pawn: if Black takes, [O-O cxb2 Bxb2], White's bishop rakes the long diagonal toward g7 with a dangerous attack. [bxc3] recaptures toward the center and gains time on the bishop, which usually retreats to a5. [Nxc3] develops but leaves the knight pinned. In all these lines White has rapid development and open files, and Black must develop the kingside quickly and give material back at the right moment. [%csl Yc3][%cal Ge1g1,Gb2c3,Gb1c3] }
( 5... Bc5 $2
{ The bishop goes back to c5, but [cxd4] hits it again and White has the ideal center. After [cxd4 Bb6 d5], the knight on c6 is kicked too and White's pawns roll forward. Black has lost several tempi and has no extra pawn to show for it. Take on c3 instead. Once you have checked with the bishop, follow it up: going back turns the check into a lost move. }
6. cxd4 Bb6 7. d5 ) ( 5... Ba5 $2
{ On a5 the bishop is offside, and White doesn't even need to hurry: after [O-O] and then [cxd4], White has the ideal center with pawns on d4 and e4 and a lead in development, and Black has no extra pawn to show for it. The bishop check was meant to grab material on c3: taking there was the consistent follow-up, and retreating just loses time. }
6. O-O ) 6. O-O
{ White offers a third pawn. Taking it with [cxb2] is the classic line: after the bishop takes back, White's bishops rake the board toward g7 and f7, and Black must defend accurately. [Nf6] first is a little safer: it develops and blocks the long diagonal in advance, and the pawn on c3 can wait. [d6] is also good. A slow move gives White time to open the lines. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gc3b2,Gg8f6,Gd7d6] }
( 6. bxc3
{ White takes back toward the center and attacks the bishop on b4. [Ba5] keeps it on the diagonal toward e1, where it pins the pawn on c3, and is the best retreat. Other retreats are passive and give White an easy attack, and one of them is a serious blunder, because it leaves f7 short of defenders. White's compensation for the pawn is development and open lines: castling, the queen to b3 to hit f7, and central pushes like [e5]. [%csl Yc3,Yf7][%cal Gb4a5] }
6... Ba5
{ Black keeps the extra pawn and the pin on c3. White's best is to castle with [O-O], then aim at f7 with [Qb3] or push in the center once the king is safe. Black will try to develop with [Bb6], [d6] and [Nge7], and hold the pawn while getting the king castled. White has fair play for it, thanks to the lead in development and the open b- and d-files. Pushing in the center before castling, though, lets Black open the e-file toward White's own king. [%csl Yc3,Yf7][%cal Ge1g1,Gd1b3] }
( 6... Be7 $4
{ The bishop retreats, but it leaves f7 defended only by the king. [Qd5] threatens mate on f7, and the knight on g8 can't help except by going to h6, where the bishop on c1 takes it. Black loses material by force. The bishop belonged on a5, keeping the pin on c3. In this line the bishop must keep its eye on c3, or f7 becomes a target at once. }
7. Qd5 ) 7. O-O ( 7. Qb3 ) ( 7. e5 $6
{ The Cochrane Variation pushes before castling. Black answers [d5], hitting the bishop on c4 and opening the center: after [d5 exd6 Qxd6], Black's queen and bishops come out with tempo, and it is White's king on e1 that is in danger, with the pin on c3 still there. Castling first was better, and only then the push to e5 or the queen to b3 against f7. }
7... d5 ) ) ( 6. Nxc3 ) 6... cxb2 ( 6... Nf6 ) ( 6... d6 ) ( 6... h6 $2
{ [h6] spends a move on the wrong wing. [a3] hits the bishop on b4, and White's pieces flood out while Black is still at home. Develop with [Nf6] or [d6]. }
7. a3 ) ( 6... Qf6 $2
{ The queen comes out early and gets hit at once: [e5] drives her off f6, and White's bishop on c4 and rook come into play with tempo. Develop the knight to f6 instead. }
7. e5 ) 7. Bxb2
{ Black is three pawns up, but White's bishops point straight at Black's king, and g7 is already attacked. The only good move is [Nf6]: it blocks the long diagonal and develops. White then has [e5] or [Ng5] with a strong attack, and Black must defend accurately, often by giving material back. The Hanneken Variation continues [Nf6 Ng5 O-O e5], when Black returns a piece to break the attack. Natural moves that leave g7 hanging lose at once. [%csl Rg7,Yb2,Yc4][%cal Gg8f6] }
7... Nf6 ( 7... d6 $4
{ Natural development, but it forgets that g7 is already attacked by the bishop on b2. [Bxg7] wins the pawn and then the rook on h8. After the bishop recaptures on b2, g7 must be covered at once, and the knight move to f6, which blocks the long diagonal and develops, is the way. In these gambits, always look at the long diagonals before making a quiet move. }
8. Bxg7 ) ( 7... Nge7 $4
