{ The bishop develops, but it walks into the pawn: [dxe4] hits it with tempo, and Black's pieces get active on the light squares. Develop with the knight first. }
11... dxe4 ) ) ( 7. Nc2 Bxe3
{ In the Meitner Variation the knight stepped back to c2, where it still guards e3, and Black has traded bishops at once. White must recapture the right way. [Nxe3] is correct: the knight lands on a good square, eyeing d5, f5 and g4, and White's pawns stay intact. Black usually goes on with [d6], then the bishop to e6 and castling, and the game is balanced. Taking with the f-pawn opens a dangerous diagonal to White's king. [%csl Ye3,Yd5][%cal Gc2e3] }
8. Nxe3 ( 8. fxe3 $4
{ Taking with the pawn opens the diagonal to White's king. [Qh4+] forces the king to move, and after [Qh4+ Kd2 Qxe4] Black has won a pawn while White's king is stuck in the center for good. When the f-pawn recaptures, always check the diagonal from h4 to e1. Taking back with the knight from c2 kept White's king safe and the pawns whole. }
8... Qh4+ 9. Kd2 Qxe4 ) ) ( 7. Be2 ) ( 7. Nb5 $2
{ The knight eyes c7, but leaving d4 opens Black's bishop onto e3. After [Bxe3 fxe3 Qh4+], White has wrecked pawns and a king that can't castle comfortably, and the knight on b5 has no real threat, since Black can cover c7 with the king. Black is clearly better. The knight on d4 is the piece that holds White's position together, so move it only for a real gain. }
7... Bxe3 8. fxe3 Qh4+ ) ( 7. Nf3 $2
{ Retreating the knight undoes the work of [c3]. Black trades on e3 with [Bxe3 fxe3], leaving White with doubled e-pawns and a weak pawn on e3, and Black's knights jump to the dark squares, to g6 and e5. Black is better, and White has wasted two moves with the knight. In this line the knight belongs on d4, where it blocks the bishop on c5 and eyes b5 and f5. }
7... Bxe3 8. fxe3 ) ( 7. Nxc6 $2
{ Trading on c6 lets Black answer with [Bxe3], with a threat on f2, so White has to take back with the f-pawn. After [Bxe3 fxe3 Nxc6], White has doubled e-pawns, and the pawn on c3 was played to support a knight that is now gone. Develop with [Bc4], [g3] or [Be2] instead. }
7... Bxe3 8. fxe3 Nxc6 ) ) ( 5. Nb3
{ The Potter Variation. The knight retreats with tempo on the bishop, avoiding every trick on d4 and f2. Black's bishop must move: [Bb6] keeps the long diagonal toward f2 and is the main move; [Bb4+], the Romanishin Variation, is usually met by [c3], and then the pawn takes away the queen's knight's best square; [Be7] is modest. White's usual plan is [Nc3], [Qe2] and [Be3] with long castling, or a queenside push with [a4] to harass the bishop on b6. Black develops with [Nf6] and [d6] and castles short. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gc5b6,Gc5b4,Gc5e7] }
5... Bb6
{ Black keeps the bishop on the diagonal to f2. White's main plans: [Nc3] with [Qe2], [Be3] and long castling, which often leads to races with kings on opposite wings; [a4], threatening [a5] to chase the bishop, which Black meets with a pawn to a5 or a6; and [Qe2], a flexible modern move. Black develops with [Nf6] or [Nge7], [d6] and castling. The bishop on b6 and Black's queen both look at f2, so be careful about pieces left loose on the kingside. [%cal Ga2a4,Ge1c1] }
( 5... Bb4+
{ The Romanishin Variation. Black checks before retreating, so that after [c3] White's pawn takes the best square from the queen's knight. The bishop then drops back, as in [c3 Be7], and Black has a solid, slightly passive game with [Nf6+], [d6+] and castling to follow. White can also block with [Bd2], keeping c3 free for the knight and offering a trade. White keeps a small edge thanks to the pawn on e4 and the extra space, but Black's position is very hard to crack. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gc2c3,Gc1d2] }
6. c3 ( 6. Bd2 ) ) ( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... Bxf2+ $4
{ Taking on f2 with check wins only a pawn, and [Kxf2] picks up the bishop. There is no follow-up: Black's queen can't join the attack quickly, White's king is quite safe on f2 for now, and it will tuck away on g1 later. Black is simply a piece for a pawn down. The bishop should retreat, preferably to b6, where it keeps the diagonal to f2 for later and is safe from the knight. }
6. Kxf2 ) 6. Nc3 ( 6. a4 ) ( 6. Qe2 ) ( 6. Bg5 $4
{ The bishop attacks the queen, but nothing protects it, and the diagonal from d8 to g5 is open. [Qxg5] simply takes it. Before attacking the queen, check that the attacking piece is protected, or that the queen can't capture it. White's useful moves were development like [Nc3] or [Qe2], or the queenside push [a4] to harass the bishop on b6. }
6... Qxg5 ) 6... Nf6
{ White's main plans meet here. [Qe2] with [Be3] and long castling heads for a race on opposite wings. [Bg5] pins the knight, and Black often answers [h6] and [Bh4+]. [a4] asks the bishop on b6. Against long castling, Black's queenside pawns are a weapon: a push to a5 and a4 hits the knight on b3. [%csl Yb6][%cal Gd1e2,Gc1g5,Ga2a4] }
7. Qe2 ( 7. Bg5 ) ( 7. a4 ) ) ( 5. Nc3 $4
{ The knight develops, but the knight on d4 is attacked twice, by the bishop and the knight on c6, and defended once, by the queen. [Bxd4] wins a piece, since [Bxd4 Qxd4 Nxd4] costs White the queen. In the Classical, count attackers and defenders on d4 before every move. White had to trade it with [Nxc6], defend it with [Be3], or move it with [Nb3]. }
5... Bxd4 ) ( 5. Bc4 $4
{ Development with the same problem: the knight on d4 has two attackers and one defender. [Bxd4] wins it cleanly. Taking with the knight instead is weaker, since White hits back with [Bxf7+] and then a queen check on h5 that picks up the bishop. Either way, White should have dealt with d4 first, with [Nxc6], [Be3] or [Nb3], and only then thought about developing the bishop to c4. }
5... Bxd4 ) ( 5. c3 $6
{ The pawn defends the knight, but it develops nothing and takes c3 away from the queen's knight. Black simply goes on developing: after [Nf6], e4 needs a defender and [d5] is coming. White has given up the Scotch's usual small edge. [Be3], [Nxc6] and [Nb3] all deal with the threat on d4 and develop or gain time. }
5... Nf6 ) ( 5. Nf5
{ The knight dodges the attack and eyes g7, but it has moved three times already. Black should hit back in the centre: [d5] opens the bishop on c8, and if White grabs with [d5 Nxg7+ Kf8], the knight has to run back while Black's queen comes to h4 with a big lead in development. Otherwise the bishop takes on f5 with gain of time. [%csl Yg7,Yf5][%cal Gd7d5] }
5... d5 ( 5... Nf6 $4
{ Natural development, but it leaves g7 to the knight. After [Nxg7+ Kf8 Bh6], the knight is defended and stands in front of Black's king: every knight move is now a discovered check from the bishop, and a jump to e6 would hit the queen as well. Strike with the d-pawn instead. }
6. Nxg7+ Kf8 7. Bh6 ) ) ) 5. Nxc6
{ White trades knights, and Black must choose how to take back. [bxc6] is the main move: the pawn heads for the center, the b-file opens for a rook, and Black hopes to free the game with [d5]. The price is a broken queenside, with pawns on a7, c6 and c7 that can become targets in an endgame. [dxc6] is solid and frees the bishop on c8, but it allows a queen trade: after [dxc6 Qxd8+ Kxd8], Black can't castle, and White's healthy kingside majority gives a small, lasting endgame edge. Recapture first: the knight on c6 is attacking the queen. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gb7c6,Gd7c6] }
5... bxc6
{ The basic Schmidt structure. Black has the half-open b-file and a pawn ready to support [d5]; White has the better pawns, but e4 is attacked. White's choice shapes the game. [e5], the Mieses Variation, chases the knight at once and gains space: it was Kasparov's favorite and is the most testing. [Bd3] defends calmly, and after [Bd3 d5] White can take or push past with [Bd3 d5 e5]. [Nd2], the Tartakower, defends with the knight. [Nc3] develops naturally and invites the pin [Bb4+]. Black's plan is the same in all of them: break with [d5], develop with [Bd6] or [Bc5], and castle. [Qe2] is a modern try: the queen guards e4, [e5] stays in reserve, and White can castle long. [%cal Ge4e5] }
( 5... dxc6 ) ( 5... Nxe4 $4
{ Taking the pawn on e4 looks like a free gain, but it forgets the knight on c6, which is attacking Black's queen. [Nxd8] wins the queen for a knight, and the extra pawn is no consolation. When the opponent has just captured with a threat, the first question is always whether you must take back. Here recapturing on c6, with the b-pawn or the d-pawn, was the only move, and only then could Black think about e4. }
6. Nxd8 ) 6. e5
{ The Mieses Variation. The pawn chases the knight from f6, and this is where many club players lose a piece: White's queen on d1 watches both g4 and h5. The main answer is [Qe7], a pin: the pawn on e5 can't take on f6, since it would open the e-file to White's king, so White defends it with [Qe2], and next move the knight goes to d5. [Nd5] at once is solid too: the knight is safe in the center and drops back to b6 when [c4] comes. [Ne4] is the riskiest choice: the knight is active but can be harassed. [%cal Ge7e5] }
( 6. Bd3
{ The classical way: White simply defends e4 with the bishop. Black's best is the central break [d5], using the extra center pawn that the capture toward the middle gave. After [d5 exd5 cxd5], Black has a fine pawn on d5 and open lines for the bishops, while White castles and puts pressure on that pawn. [d5 e5] instead kicks the knight and gains space, with a French-like structure where the knight usually goes to g4 or d7 and White aims at the kingside. Early bishop moves let White push [e5] with gain of time. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5] }
6... d5 7. e5 Ng4 8. O-O
{ Castling [O-O] leaves White's advanced e5 pawn looking undefended, yet it sets a classic e-file trap: [Nxe5] loses a piece on the spot to [Nxe5 Re1], pinning the knight to Black's uncastled king while [w:f4] is ready to finish it off! Black must bring pieces out or undermine the wedge with a pawn instead. [Bc5] develops with pressure against f2 alongside the g4 knight (threatening [b:Qh4]), while [f6] safely chips away at the e5 spearhead. [%cal Gf8c5,Gf7f6] }
8... Bc5 ( 8... f6 ) ( 8... Nxe5 $4
{ The pawn falls, but the knight lands on the e-file in front of the king. [Re1] pins it, and White wins it with [f4] next: the knight can't move, and a second defender comes too slowly. With White's rook able to reach e1, hit e5 with a pawn rather than a piece. }
9. Re1 ) ) ( 6. Nd2 ) ( 6. Nc3 ) ( 6. Qe2 ) ( 6. Bg5 $6
{ Pinning the knight with [Bg5] leaves e4 completely undefended, and here the tactical pin fails to hold the pawn. Black kicks the bishop first with [h6], and after [h6 Bf4 Nxe4 Qe2 Qe7] (or [h6 Bxf6 Qxf6]) Black wins material or takes over the bishop pair cleanly. Protect or advance e4 with [e5], [Bd3], or [Nc3]. [%csl Ye4][%cal Rc1g5,Gh7h6] }
6... h6 7. Bf4 Nxe4 8. Qe2 Qe7 ) ( 6. f3 $6
{ Defending e4 with [f3] weakens the e1-h4 diagonal right before White has developed a single remaining piece. Black blasts open the centre with [d5], and after [d5 exd5 Nxd5 Nc3 Qh4+] White has to push [g3] and endure lasting dark-square weaknesses. Play [e5], [Bd3], or [Nc3] instead. [%csl Ye4,Ye1,Yh4][%cal Rf2f3,Gd7d5] }
6... d5 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. Nc3 Qh4+ ) 6... Qe7
{ Black pins the pawn on e5 against White's king. White can't take on f6, and the pawn is attacked by the queen, so the main move is [Qe2], defending it and breaking the pin at once. After [Qe2 Nd5], White chases the knight with [c4] and plays for space, while Black prepares [d6] or [f6] to hit the pawn on e5 and brings the bishop to a6 to pin the c-pawn. Most other moves simply lose the pawn on e5 with check, so this is a moment to slow down. [%csl Ye5,Ye1][%cal Gd1e2] }
( 6... Nd5 ) ( 6... Ne4 ) ( 6... Nh5 $4
{ The knight runs to the edge, onto a square White's queen already controls along the diagonal from d1. [Qxh5] wins it for nothing. This is the most common way to lose a piece in the Mieses: after the push to e5, both g4 and h5 are covered by the queen, and g8 is passive. The good answers are pinning the pawn with the queen on e7, or the central knight moves to d5 and e4. }
7. Qxh5 ) ( 6... Ng4 $4
{ On g4 the knight attacks e5 and f2, but White's queen controls that square along the diagonal from d1. [Qxg4] wins the knight for nothing. After the push to e5, look at that diagonal first: g4 and h5 are both covered. The knight's safe squares are d5 and e4, or Black pins the pawn first with the queen on e7, which is the main line and keeps the knight on f6 for one more move. }
7. Qxg4 ) ( 6... Ng8 $2
{ The retreat keeps the material but undevelops a piece. White develops with [Nc3], then [Bf4] or [Qe2], and Black is far behind: the knight needs several moves to find a square, and the pawn on e5 takes away d6 and f6. Pinning the pawn with the queen on e7 was the active answer. A knight back on its starting square in an open position is a full move behind, and White has the time to use it. }
7. Nc3 ) 7. Qe2
{ White defends e5 and breaks the pin, so the knight on f6 is now attacked for real. The main move is [Nd5]: the knight takes the best central square, and Black is ready for [Ba6], pinning a pawn that comes to c4 against White's queen. Note the queens: Black's on e7 blocks the bishop on f8 for now, a known drawback that Black solves later with [g6] and [Bg7]. [Ng8] keeps the knight safe but is too passive: White develops freely and is comfortably better. [%csl Ye5,Yf6][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 7. Bd3 $2
{ The bishop develops, but it doesn't help with the real problem: the pawn on e5 is pinned to the king and attacked by the queen. [Qxe5+] wins it with check, and Black is a clean pawn up with an easy game, since the doubled c-pawns matter much less than a whole pawn. The only good move was [Qe2], which defends the pawn and breaks the pin at the same time; then the knight on f6 has to move. }
7... Qxe5+ ) ( 7. Nc3 $2
{ Natural development, but the pawn on e5 is pinned and attacked, and nothing defends it. [Qxe5+] picks it up with check, and after [Qxe5+ Qe2 Qxe2+] the queens come off with Black a healthy pawn up in the endgame. The pin on the e-file is the whole point of Black's last move, so White has to answer it at once with [Qe2], defending the pawn and unpinning it. }
7... Qxe5+ 8. Qe2 Qxe2+ ) 7... Nd5
{ The knight sits on d5, and White's most direct reply is [c4], kicking it and gaining space. That is the main line of the Mieses: after [c4 Ba6], Black pins the pawn on c4 against the queen, and after [c4 Nb6] the knight drops back to the queenside. Quieter moves keep more tension: [g3] prepares [Bg2] to hit d5 and the long diagonal, and [h4], a modern idea, gives the rook on h1 a way into the game via h3. White's trumps are space and the pawn on e5; Black's are active pieces and, once the bishop reaches a6, pressure on White's queenside. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gg2g3,Gh2h4] }
8. c4
{ The knight on d5 is attacked and must decide. [Ba6] counterattacks: the pawn on c4 is pinned to the queen on e2, so it can't take the knight, and White must spend a move like [b3] or [Nd2] to deal with the pin. [Nb6] retreats to a safe square, ready for [Ba6] or [d5] later. [Qb4+] offers a queen trade or forces White to block, and is a decent practical option. The knight must not wander to b4, though: from there it has no way back. [%cal Ga6c4] }
( 8. g3 ) ( 8. h4 ) ( 8. Nd2 ) 8... Ba6
{ The key idea of the Mieses: the bishop pins the pawn on c4 against White's queen, so the knight on d5 is safe. White has to unpin or defend. [b3] is the main move: it protects c4 and prepares [Bb2] to fight for the long diagonal and e5. [g3] heads for [Bg2] and short castling, and [Qe4] steps out of the pin with gain of time on the knight. Black usually goes on with [g6] and [Bg7], hitting e5, or castles long. The game is balanced and rich: Black's pieces are active, White's pawns are healthier. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gb2b3,Gg2g3,Ge2e4] }
( 8... Nb6 ) ( 8... Qb4+ ) ( 8... Nb4 $2
{ The knight looks active on b4, but it has no retreat. [a3] chases it, and after [a3 Na6] it sits on the rim doing nothing, while White's pawns on c4 and e5 control the center and White develops freely. In the Mieses the knight belongs on b6, or stays on d5 when the bishop on a6 pins the c-pawn. Before you jump forward with a piece, ask where it goes back if it is chased. }
9. a3 Na6 ) 9. b3
{ White has defended c4, and the bishop will go to b2 or a3. Black's standard plan is [g6] and [Bg7], adding an attacker on e5, then castling and a pawn to f6 or d6 against the centre. [Qh4] is a trickier try: it eyes d4 and the rook on a1, and sets a trap for a careless reply. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gg7g6,Ge7h4] }
( 9. g3 ) ( 9. Qe4 ) ( 9. cxd5 $4
{ Taking the knight looks natural, but the pawn on c4 was pinned: behind it, on the same diagonal, stands White's queen on e2. [Bxe2] takes the queen, and after White recaptures, Black is a queen for a knight up. This is exactly why Black put the bishop on a6. When a bishop lines up with your queen, ask what your pinned piece can really do, and deal with the pin first, here with [b3]. }
9... Bxe2 ) 9... g6
{ Black prepares [Bg7] against e5. White has two ways to hold it. [f4] supports the pawn with another pawn and cramps Black's pieces; Black answers by hitting the chain at once with [d6], [g5] or [f6]. [Bb2], then [g3] and [Bg2], develops first and keeps the pawn chain flexible. The game turns on e5: if it falls, Black's pieces come alive. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gf2f4,Gc1b2,Gg2g3] }
( 9... Qh4
{ In this sharp branch of the Mieses Scotch, [Qh4] steps off the e-file so White's e2 queen no longer pins her, while threatening [b:Qd4] to fork the loose rook on a1 and the central e5 pawn! Pushing [g3] walks straight into [g3 Qd4] because the long diagonal opens up. White neutralises every tactical threat calmly with [a3], keeping the d5 knight out of b4 while preparing [w:Ra2] or [w:Bb2], or with [Bb2] immediately to guard a1 and overprotect e5. [%cal Ga2a3,Gc1b2] }
10. a3 ( 10. Bb2 ) ( 10. g3 $4
{ The pawn chases the queen exactly where she wants to go. [Qd4] attacks the rook on a1 and the pawn on e5 at once, and White can't save both. Before chasing a queen, check where she lands. }
10... Qd4 ) ) 10. f4 ( 10. Bb2 ) ( 10. g3 ) *

[Event "Ruy Lopez (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5
{ The Ruy Lopez, named after the Spanish priest Ruy López de Segura, who analysed it in 1561. Developing the bishop to b5 gets White ready to castle and pressures the knight on c6 that guards e5 and d4. It does not threaten to win e5 right away, because [Bxc6 dxc6 Nxe5] runs into [b:Qd4], hitting the knight and e4 together; the threat becomes real once White defends e4 with [w:Re1] or [w:d3]. Black's two great answers are [a6], the Morphy Defence, asking the bishop its intentions while keeping [b:b5] in reserve, and [Nf6], the Berlin Defence, counterattacking e4 at once. [d6], [Bc5], [f5], [Nd4], [Nge7], and [g6] are playable sidelines. [%csl Yb5,Yc6,Ye5][%cal Ga7a6] }
3... a6 ( 3... Nf6
{ The Berlin Defence, one of the oldest and toughest answers to the Ruy Lopez, brought back to the summit of chess when Vladimir Kramnik used it in London 2000 to neutralize Garry Kasparov's [e4] and win the World Championship. Instead of spending a move on [b:a6], Black develops a piece and attacks e4 right away. White's main choice is [O-O], daring Black to take [b:Nxe4] and enter the Berlin Endgame or the open e-file lines; [d3], the Anti-Berlin, keeps e4 solid for a manoeuvring fight, and [Qe2] guards e4 with the queen. ([Nc3] transposes to the Spanish Four Knights.) [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge1g1] }
4. O-O
{ White castles, leaving e4 indirectly protected by the open e-file and inviting the main Berlin battle. Black's principled move is [Nxe4], capturing the pawn because [Nxe4 d4] can be met with tempo on the b5-bishop by [b:Nd6]. Black's main ways to keep more pieces on the board are [Bc5], the Beverwijk Variation eyeing f2, [d6], the Improved Steinitz holding e5 solidly, and the quiet [Be7]. [%csl Ye4,Yf2,Ye5][%cal Gf6e4] }
( 4. d3
{ The Anti-Berlin. White defends e4 with a pawn so queens stay on the board for a slow, strategic Spanish middlegame, a major battleground of modern tournament play. Black's most active reply is [Bc5], placing the bishop on its best diagonal before committing [b:d6], since taking on c6 and e5 would run into a queen fork on d4. [d6] is solid, though it shuts the dark-squared bishop inside the pawn chain, and [Ne7] is the tricky Mortimer Variation baiting e5. [%csl Ye4,Yc6,Ye5][%cal Gf8c5] }
4... Bc5
{ Black develops the bishop to the active a7 to g1 diagonal while e5 does not yet need [b:d6] to guard it, because capturing on c6 and e5 walks into [b:Qd4] hitting both e5 and f2. White's main move is [c3], preparing [w:d4] and giving the bishop on b5 a direct retreat to c2 if Black later plays [b:a6] and [b:b5], saving a move over the usual a4-b3-c2 tour. [Bxc6] is a popular modern choice that saddles Black with two pawn islands while White's knight on f6 blocks [b:f6], and [O-O] keeps both plans open. [%csl Ya7,Yg1,Ye5][%cal Gc2c3] }
( 4... d6
{ Black defends e5 with a pawn, accepting a compact, quieter position with the f8-bishop inside the chain, where it can either go to e7 or fianchetto later via [b:g6] and [b:Bg7]. White usually castles [O-O] and builds up slowly with [c3], [w:Re1], and the knight tour [w:Nbd2] to f1 and g3. Expanding on the queenside with Duras's [c4] to clamp down on d5 and b5, or trading [Bxc6+] to double Black's pawns before Black plays [b:Bd7], are good alternatives. [%csl Ye5,Ye7,Yf1][%cal Ge1g1] }
5. O-O ( 5. c3 ) ( 5. c4 ) ( 5. Bxc6+
{ Anderssen's Variation of the Anti-Berlin. Having waited for Black to commit the d-pawn to d6, White trades on c6 with check so Black cannot recapture toward the center with the d-pawn and must accept doubled c-pawns after [bxc6]. Unlike the Morphy Exchange, Black hasn't spent a tempo on [b:a6] and keeps e5 firmly guarded by d6, planning [b:c5] or [b:g6] and [b:Bg7] after [bxc6 O-O] to control d4 and use the semi-open b-file, while White often routes [bxc6 Nbd2] toward c4 to probe the rigid queenside pawns. [%csl Yc6,Yd6][%cal Gb7c6] }
5... bxc6 ) ) ( 4... Ne7
{ The Mortimer Variation, introduced by James Mortimer in 1883. Retreating the c6-knight to e7 looks awkward because it blocks Black's bishop on f8 and leaves e5 seemingly undefended, which is the bait of one of the oldest traps in the Ruy Lopez. White stays comfortably ahead by ignoring the bait on e5 and developing naturally with [O-O], [c3] (preparing [w:d4] while Black's knight takes another move to reach g6), or [Nc3]. [%csl Ye7,Yf8,Ye5][%cal Ge1g1] }
5. O-O ( 5. c3 ) ( 5. Nc3 ) ( 5. Nxe5 $2
{ The Mortimer Trap. Snatching the seemingly free e5-pawn walks into [c6], hitting the bishop on b5 and opening d8 to a5 for Black's queen: [c6 Bc4 Qa5+] forks White's king and the e5-knight, and [c6 Nc4 d6 Ba4 b5] traps a piece as well. Castle [w:O-O] or play [w:c3] instead. [%csl Rb5,Rd8][%cal Gc7c6] }
5... c6
{ The Mortimer Trap is sprung: [c6] attacks White's bishop on b5 while opening the diagonal to a5 for Black's queen, so any quiet bishop retreat loses the knight on e5 to [b:Qa5+]. White's best practical bailout is [Nxf7], giving up the piece after [Nxf7 Kxf7 Bc4+ d5 Bb3] in exchange for two pawns and an exposed black king on f7. Note that if White tries the swindle [w:Nc4], Black answers [b:d6] or [b:Ng6] rather than grabbing b5. [%csl Yb5,Ya5,Ye5][%cal Ge5f7] }
6. Nxf7 ( 6. Bc4 $2
{ Aiming the bishop at f7 runs into the exact same queen fork: [Qa5+] checks White's king on e1 and picks up the loose knight on e5 after [Qa5+ Nc3 Qxe5]. Bail out with [w:Nxf7] first to get two pawns for the piece. }
6... Qa5+ 7. Nc3 Qxe5 ) ( 6. Ba4 $2
{ Retreating the bishop along the Spanish diagonal walks straight into [Qa5+], forking White's king on e1 and loose knight on e5 while eyeing a4 to leave Black a clean piece ahead. Sacrifice on f7 with [w:Nxf7] instead for two pawns and an exposed king. }
6... Qa5+ 7. Nc3 Qxe5 ) ( 6. Nc4
{ White's last tactical trick inside the Mortimer Trap: leaving the bishop on b5 sets up a one-move smothered checkmate on d6 if Black captures the bishop carelessly. Black sidesteps the trap and keeps a winning advantage by playing [d6] or [Ng6] first, covering d6 and opening the c8-bishop while renewing the threat of [b:b5] to fork White's two loose minor pieces on b5 and c4. [%csl Yb5,Yd6,Yc4][%cal Gd7d6] }
