15... Bg4 16. f3 Bxg3 ) 15... Bg4
{ Black's bishop attacks White's queen on d1 and threatens [b:Bf3] followed by checkmate on g2. White has only one square for the queen: [Qd3], stepping off d1 while keeping a clear path to f1 so [b:Bf3] is met by [w:Qf1] defending g2 and trading queens. After [Qd3 Rae8 Nd2 Re6], White counterattacks on the queenside with [w:a4] in the classic Spassky Variation. [%csl Yd1,Yg2,Yf1][%cal Gd1d3] }
16. Qd3 ( 16. Qd2 $4
{ Stepping the queen to d2 dodges the bishop, yet blocks White's b1-knight and fails to cover f1: [Bf3] threatens checkmate on g2 with no way to stop it. Put the queen on [w:Qd3] instead so [w:Qf1] parries [b:Bf3]. [%csl Rd2,Rf1][%cal Gg4f3] }
16... Bf3 ) ( 16. f3 $4
