16... Bxg3 17. hxg3 Qxg3+ 18. Kh1 Bxf3+ ) ) ( 7... Bb7 ) 8. c3
{ One of the defining moves of the Closed Ruy Lopez: [c3] prepares the central break [w:d4], blunts [b:Nd4], and opens c2 so [Na5] can always be met by [w:Bc2], keeping White's Spanish bishop aimed toward Black's kingside from behind the pawn chain. Black usually castles [O-O] now, though [Na5] right away transposes to Chigorin lines after [Na5 Bc2 c5 d4], and [Bg4] pins f3 early. [%csl Yc2,Yf3][%cal Ge8g8] }
( 8. a4 ) ( 8. h3 ) ( 8. d3 ) ( 8. d4
{ Striking with [d4] on move eight without [w:c3] threatens both [w:dxe5] and [w:Bd5], yet steps straight toward Black's Noah's Ark sequence on the queenside. Black equalizes cleanly with [Nxd4], since after [Nxd4 Nxd4 exd4] White must play [w:a4] or [w:Bd5] rather than [w:Qxd4] (which loses the b3-bishop to [b:c5]); [exd4] first and [Bg4] are sound alternatives, whereas ignoring the central tension drops e5. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gc6d4] }
8... Nxd4 ( 8... exd4 ) ( 8... Bg4 ) ( 8... Na5 $2
{ Chasing White's b3-bishop automatically overlooks that e5 is attacked twice in the center: [dxe5 dxe5 Qxd8+ Bxd8 Nxe5] wins a clean pawn while threatening f7 as well. Capture [b:Nxd4] instead. }
9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Qxd8+ Bxd8 11. Nxe5 ) 9. Nxd4 exd4
{ Pushing [d4] on move eight of the Closed Spanish without inserting [w:c3] first revives the Noah's Ark Trap. Even with White castled and the rook on e1, White cannot recapture on d4 with the queen without losing the b3-bishop to [b:c5] and [b:c4]. White has to undermine the queenside first with [a4] ([a4 Bb7 axb5 axb5 Rxa8 Qxa8 Qxd4]) or jump [Bd5] hitting a8 to keep the bishop alive. [%csl Ye1,Yd4,Ya8][%cal Ga2a4] }
10. a4 ( 10. Bd5 ) ( 10. Qxd4 $4
{ The Noah's Ark Trap in the Closed Spanish. Because White never played [w:c3] to open c2, recapturing with the queen walks into [c5 Qd3 c4], trapping White's Spanish bishop on b3 cleanly. Undermine b5 with [w:a4] or jump [w:Bd5] instead. [%csl Rc2,Rb3][%cal Gc7c5] }
10... c5 11. Qd3 c4 ) ( 10. e5 $2
{ Pushing past d6 hopes to disrupt Black's f6-knight and clear the e-file, yet e5 is simply undefended: [dxe5] leaves Black two pawns ahead while White's b3-bishop still needs [a4] to avoid being trapped. Play [w:a4] or [w:Bd5] right away. }
10... dxe5 11. a4 Rb8 ) ) 8... O-O
{ Both kings are castled and White is poised to challenge the center. Almost all masters insert the quiet [h3] before pushing [d4], because immediate [d4] allows [d4 Bg4], the Yates (or Bogoljubow) Variation, pinning the knight on f3 to increase pressure against d4. Playing [h3] rules out [b:Bg4] for good so [w:d4] arrives next move with full force; [d4] at once and Pilnik's [d3] remain playable choices. [%csl Yf3,Yd4][%cal Gh2h3] }
( 8... Na5 9. Bc2
{ Black jumps to a5 on move eight before castling, driving White's bishop to c2 and entering the Chigorin without waiting for [w:h3]. Pushing [c5] right away challenges d4 ([c5 d4 Qc7], where White can either insert [w:h3] to transpose to the main Chigorin or play Balla's immediate [w:a4] while Black's rook on a8 is still undefended), while [O-O] reaches standard Closed lines after [O-O h3] or [O-O d4]. [%csl Ya5,Yc2,Yd4][%cal Gc7c5] }
9... c5 ( 9... O-O ) ) ( 8... Bg4 ) 9. h3
{ The grand tabiya of the Closed Ruy Lopez. With [h3] preventing [b:Bg4], White is ready for [w:d4] followed by the classic Spanish knight tour [w:Nbd2], [w:Nf1], and [w:Ng3], reinforcing e4 and eyeing f5 without blocking the c1-bishop, while keeping [w:a4] in reserve against b5. Black chooses a defensive system: [Na5] (Chigorin), [Nb8] (Breyer), [Bb7] (Zaitsev), [h6] (Smyslov), [Nd7] (Karpov), or [Be6] (Kholmov). [%csl Ye4,Yf5,Yb5][%cal Gc6a5] }
( 9. d4
{ The Yates (or Bogoljubow) Variation: White strikes in the center with [d4] right away, skipping the customary prophylactic [w:h3]. Saving that tempo claims central space a move faster, yet it hands Black the active pin [Bg4], increasing pressure against d4 and e4 so White has to play accurately with [Bg4 Be3] or [Bg4 d5]. If Black prefers not to commit the light-squared bishop outside the chain, [exd4] ([exd4 cxd4 Bg4] or [exd4 cxd4 d5]), [Re8], and Chigorin's [Na5] are all playable alternatives. [%csl Yd4,Yf3][%cal Gc8g4] }
9... Bg4
{ The Yates (or Bogoljubow) Variation. Because White omitted [w:h3], Black pins the knight on f3 to step up pressure on d4 and e4. White's sharpest continuation is [Be3], overprotecting d4 and setting a hidden trap if Black trades [Be3 exd4 cxd4] and grabs e4 carelessly. Pushing [d5] ([d5 Na5 Bc2 c6]) closes the center at once, and [a4] probes b5 on the queenside. [%csl Yf3,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gc1e3] }
( 9... exd4 ) ( 9... Re8 ) ( 9... Na5 ) 10. Be3 ( 10. d5 ) ( 10. a4 ) 10...
exd4 11. cxd4
{ Black has traded on d4 to open the e-file against White's pawn on e4, which looks unguarded now that [Be3] stepped in front of the rook on e1. In truth, e4 is poisoned because of a bishop fork on d5. Black should generate queenside counterplay with [Na5] ([Na5 Bc2 Nc4] or [Na5 Bc2 c5]) or strike directly at White's center with [d5]. [%csl Yd4,Ye4,Ye1][%cal Gc6a5] }
