12. Bd5 Qd7 13. Bxe4 ) ) ( 9. d3 ) 9... Na5
{ With [Na5], Mikhail Chigorin's classic manoeuvre, Black steps onto the rim for a concrete purpose: chasing White's valuable Spanish bishop off the a2-g8 diagonal while clearing the c-pawn to fight for d4 with [c5]. Preserving the light-squared bishop with [Bc2] is virtually mandatory ([Bc2 c5 d4 Qc7]); leaving the bishop on b3 lets [Nxb3] trade off White's pride and joy in the Ruy Lopez and hands Black the bishop pair. [%csl Ya2,Yg8,Yd4,Yb3][%cal Gb3c2] }
( 9... Nb8
{ The Breyer Variation, suggested by Gyula Breyer in 1911 and championed by Boris Spassky, Anatoly Karpov, and Magnus Carlsen. Retreating the knight home to b8 looks paradoxical, yet solves three problems at once: the knight had no future behind White's c3-pawn, moving it frees Black's c-pawn for [b:c5] and clears the a8 to h1 diagonal for [b:Bb7], and from d7 it bolsters e5 and eyes c5 without getting stranded on the rim. White plays [d4] or Karpov's [d3]. [%csl Yb8,Ya8,Yh1][%cal Gd2d4] }
10. d4
{ White claims the center with [d4] while Black's queenside knight sits back on b8, attacking e5 twice with pawn and f3-knight. Black completes the Breyer manoeuvre with [Nbd7], reinforcing e5 and f6 while keeping b7 open for the light-squared bishop and the c-pawn free to strike at White's center later with [b:c5]. (Playing [b:Bb7] before [Nbd7] invites the sharp [w:dxe5] and [w:e6] pawn sacrifice.) [%csl Yb8,Ye5,Yf6][%cal Gb8d7] }
( 10. d3 ) 10... Nbd7
{ Black completes the Breyer reroute to d7, reinforcing e5 and f6 harmoniously while leaving the c8-bishop clear for b7 and the c-pawn ready for [b:c5]. White's classical main line is [Nbd2], starting the knight tour via f1 to g3 after [Nbd2 Bb7 Bc2 Re8 Nf1 Bf8 Ng3 g6]. Geller's queenside strike [c4] ([c4 c6 Nc3 b4 Na4]) and [Nh4] eyeing f5 are sharp alternatives. [%csl Yd7,Ye5,Yf6][%cal Gb1d2] }
11. Nbd2 ( 11. c4 ) ( 11. Nh4 ) 11... Bb7
{ Fianchettoing the bishop to b7 puts a second attacker on White's e4-pawn alongside the f6-knight, so hurrying [w:Nf1] right away would let Black grab e4 for active counterplay. White therefore tucks the Spanish bishop back to [Bc2] first, overprotecting e4 from behind c3 while keeping aim along the b1 to h7 diagonal, so [Bc2 Re8 Nf1 Bf8 Ng3 g6] completes both sides' classic regrouping. [%csl Yb7,Ye4,Yc3][%cal Gb3c2] }
12. Bc2 Re8 13. Nf1 Bf8 14. Ng3 g6
{ The classic Breyer tabiya after both sides' signature Spanish manoeuvres: White's knight tour via d2 and f1 to g3, and Black's [Nb8] to d7 plus [Re8], [Bf8], and [g6] (taking f5 away from White's knights and preparing [b:Bg7]). A key Closed Spanish rule applies here: avoid pushing [w:d5] while Black's c-pawn is still on c7, because [b:c6] undermines the chain at once; probe b5 first with [a4] (or play [b3] or [Bg5]), and lock the center with [w:d5] once Black answers [a4 c5]. [%csl Yd2,Yf1,Yg3][%cal Ga2a4] }
