{ The Smyslov tabiya. Because Black inserted [h6] on move nine, White cannot jump to g5 to hit f7 now that Black's rook has vacated f8. White starts the knight tour with [Nbd2], and after [Nbd2 Bf8] White can play [w:Nf1] directly because [b:exd4], [w:cxd4], and [b:Nxe4] would lose a piece to [w:Bd5]; Black answers [Nbd2 Bf8 Nf1 Bb7 Ng3 Na5 Bc2 Nc4] for a rich three-flank struggle. [Bc2] and [Be3] are solid too. [%csl Yg5,Yf7,Yf8][%cal Gb1d2] }
11. Nbd2 ( 11. Bc2 ) ( 11. Be3 ) ) ( 9... Nd7
{ The Karpov Variation, adopted by Anatoly Karpov in his 1990 World Championship match against Kasparov. Retreating the f6-knight to d7 bolsters e5, clears the e7-bishop to step onto the long diagonal at f6, and prepares [b:Nb6] to challenge White's Spanish bishop on b3 without blocking the c8-bishop or c-pawn. White claims the center with [d4] ([d4 Bf6]) or plays [d3]. [%csl Yd7,Ye5,Yf6][%cal Gd2d4] }
10. d4
{ Against Karpov's [Nd7], White claims the center with [d4] and opens the diagonal for the c1-bishop. Black's signature follow-up is [Bf6], placing immediate pressure on White's d4-pawn along the long diagonal while keeping [b:Bb7], [b:Re8], and [b:Nb6] or [b:Na5] in reserve ([Bf6 a4 Bb7] or [Bf6 Be3 Na5 Bc2 Nc4]). Jumping to [Nb6] right away asks White's b3-bishop to declare its square first. [%cal Ge7f6] }
( 10. d3 ) 10... Bf6 ( 10... Nb6 ) ) ( 9... Be6
{ The Kholmov Variation challenges White's prized Spanish bishop on b3 directly on move nine. Trading [Bxe6] ([Bxe6 fxe6]) gives Black doubled e-pawns, yet that central pawn mass guards d5 and f5 firmly while opening the half-open f-file for Black's rook. White's sharpest reply is [d4], threatening [w:d5] to fork e6 and c6, while [Bc2] keeps both bishops on the board and threatens [w:d4] next. [%csl Yb3,Yd5,Yf5][%cal Gd2d4] }
10. d4
{ In the Kholmov Variation, White declines the bishop trade on e6 (which would strengthen Black's central light squares and open the f-file) and strikes with [d4] instead, threatening [w:d5] to fork Black's bishop on e6 and knight on c6. That forces [Bxb3], since after [Bxb3 axb3 exd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Ne4 Nc3] White's open a-file and active kingside space balance Black's queenside play. [%csl Ye6,Yc6][%cal Ge6b3] }
( 10. Bc2 ) ( 10. Bxe6 ) 10... Bxb3 ) 10. Bc2
{ The Chigorin Variation, introduced by Mikhail Chigorin and championed by Paul Keres. Jumping to a5 drives White's bishop back to c2 and clears Black's c-pawn to contest d4 with [c5] ([c5 d4 Qc7]). The strategic bargain: Black gains immediate queenside space and c-file play, at the risk of the knight on a5 getting sidelined if White closes the center with [w:d5] and stops [b:c4] with [w:b3]. ([c6] is Rossolimo's solid line, and [d5] is Gajewski's sharp gambit.) [%csl Ya5,Yc2,Yd4][%cal Gc7c5] }
10... c5
{ Chigorin's trademark follow-up: [c5] claims queenside space, opens c7 for Black's queen, and clamps down on d4 alongside e5. White should strike in the center right away with [d4] ([d4 Qc7 Nbd2], [d4 Nd7], or [d4 Nc6]) to open the c1-bishop and fight for central squares; delaying [d4] lets Black push [b:c4] in comfort and shut White's c2-bishop out of the game. [%csl Yc7,Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gd2d4] }
( 10... c6 ) ( 10... d5 ) 11. d4
{ White seizes the center and attacks e5 twice, threatening [w:dxe5]. Black's main move is [Qc7], defending e5 flexibly while stepping off the d-file and eyeing the c-file against c2. [Nd7] is Keres's solid alternative ([Nd7 Nbd2 cxd4 cxd4]), [Nc6] brings the knight straight back to provoke [Nc6 d5 Nb8], and [cxd4] opens the c-file early (whereas [b:Bb7] runs into [w:d5], shutting the bishop in). [%csl Ye5,Yc2][%cal Gd8c7] }
11... Qc7
{ The classic Chigorin tabiya. White usually maintains central tension with [Nbd2], beginning the knight's route via f1 to g3 or e3 while waiting to see how Black handles the a5-knight. Closing the center right away with [d5] is the other big plan, as in Karpov's famous 1974 Nice Olympiad win over Unzicker: White restricts the knight on a5 and b7 with [w:b3] while preparing [w:a4] or a kingside pawn storm. [%csl Yf1,Yg3,Ye3][%cal Gb1d2] }
( 11... Nd7 ) ( 11... Nc6 ) ( 11... cxd4 ) 12. Nbd2
{ From d2, White's knight supports e4 and prepares [w:Nf1] toward g3 or e3. Black's main continuation is the Panov System [cxd4] ([cxd4 cxd4]), opening the c-file against White's bishop on c2 before returning the knight to c6 or developing [Bd7] and [b:Rac8]. [Nc6] ([Nc6 d5 Nd8], rerouting the knight via b7 or f7 in a closed center) and [Bd7], the Yugoslav System, keep central tension intact. [%csl Yd2,Ye4,Yg3][%cal Gc5d4] }
( 12. d5 ) 12... cxd4 ( 12... Nc6 ) ( 12... Bd7 ) 13. cxd4
{ The Panov System of the Chigorin Variation: Black trades on d4 to open the half-open c-file against White's bishop on c2 before White can lock the center with [w:d5] on White's own terms. Black's two main plans are [Nc6] ([Nc6 Nb3 a5 Be3 a4 Nbd2 Bd7 Rc1 Qb7], returning the rim knight to the center) and [Bd7] ([Bd7 Nf1 Rac8 Ne3]), piling heavy pieces onto c2 while keeping d5 under control; [Bb7] is playable too. [%csl Yd4,Yc2,Yd5][%cal Ga5c6] }
13... Nc6 ( 13... Bd7 ) ( 13... Bb7 ) *

[Event "Ruy Lopez (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6
{ The Morphy Defence, popularized by Paul Morphy and played in the majority of Ruy Lopez games ever since. Kicking the bishop on move three forces White to declare a plan right away while giving Black the permanent option to break the pin later with [b:b5] whenever White threatens e5. White almost always drops back to [Ba4] to maintain the pin on c6, or trades [Bxc6], the Exchange Variation, surrendering the bishop pair to saddle Black with doubled c-pawns. [%csl Ye5,Yc6][%cal Gb5a4] }
( 3... d6
{ The Old Steinitz Defence, a favourite of first World Champion Wilhelm Steinitz. Black holds e5 with a pawn without spending a move on [b:a6], building a sturdy central stronghold at the cost of shutting the f8-bishop inside the pawn chain and leaving c6 pinned to the king. White's most testing plan is [d4] at once, claiming central space while c6 cannot move; [O-O], [c3], and [Bxc6+] are solid, quieter ways to play. [%csl Ye5,Yc6][%cal Gd2d4] }
4. d4
{ White challenges e5 immediately while Black's knight on c6 is pinned to the king on e8, threatening both [dxe5] and [d5] to win material. Black has to break the pin with [Bd7] or release the central tension with [exd4]. After [Bd7 Nc3 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1], Black reaches a cramped but resilient hedgehog-like structure, whereas careless flank moves drop a pawn or wreck Black's queenside. [%csl Ye5,Yc6,Ye8][%cal Gc8d7] }
( 4. O-O
{ Castling [O-O] against 3.[d6] tucks White's king away and threatens to open the centre with [w:d4] while c6 is still pinned to e8. Black must break or neutralise that pin before e5 falls under heavy pressure: [a6] puts the question to the bishop right away, [Bd7] unpins solidly while keeping the pawn structure intact, and [Nf6] develops actively, ready to meet [Nf6 d4] with [b:Bd7] or [b:exd4]. [%cal Ga7a6,Gc8d7,Gg8f6] }
4... a6 ( 4... Bd7 ) ( 4... Nf6 ) ) ( 4. c3
{ Playing [c3] against the Old Steinitz prepares a rock-solid [d4] pawn centre and gives the b5 bishop a safe retreat path to c2 if chased. Black's sharpest moment to ask the bishop its intentions is [a6], which allows the Siesta Counterattack ([a6 Ba4 f5]) as well as normal development via [Nf6] and [Be7]. Breaking the pin right away with [Bd7] or developing [Nf6] keeps a sturdy, compact Philidor-style shell. [%csl Yb5,Yc2][%cal Ga7a6] }
4... a6 ( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... Bd7 ) ) ( 4. Bxc6+
{ Trading [Bxc6+] against the Old Steinitz Defence hands over the bishop pair early in order to saddle Black with doubled c-pawns. Because d6 is already occupied by a pawn, Black has only one sensible recapture: [bxc6], pulling a flank pawn toward the centre and opening the half-open b-file and the a6-f1 diagonal for the light-squared bishop. Black follows up with [b:c5] or [b:Nf6] and [b:Be7], reaching a solid, level structure. [%cal Gb7c6] }
4... bxc6 ) 4... Bd7
{ Breaking the pin on c6 is the Steinitz's trademark move, renewing Black's threat to capture on d4 and freeing the knight to fight for the center. White usually develops [Nc3] to bolster e4 and d4, leading to the main line after [Nc3 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1] or Nimzowitsch's plan [Nc3 Nf6 Bxc6 Bxc6 Qd3] preparing long castling. [O-O] and [Bxc6] are sound alternatives, while [w:d5] closes the center early and lets Black manoeuvre on the kingside. [%csl Yc6,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 4... exd4
{ Black surrenders the strong point on e5 early to relieve the pressure on c6 and open the e-file. White has three good recaptures: [Nxd4] keeps pieces on the board with easy central space after [Nxd4 Bd7 Nc3 Nf6 O-O Be7]; [Qxd4] centralizes the queen and prepares [Qxd4 Bd7 Bxc6 Bxc6 Nc3] with long castling and pressure down the open d-file; and the Center Gambit [O-O] develops first before regaining d4. [%csl Ye5,Yc6,Yd4][%cal Gf3d4] }
5. Nxd4 ( 5. Qxd4 ) ( 5. O-O ) ) ( 4... a6 $2
{ Chasing the bishop before breaking the pin walks straight into a poor endgame: [Bxc6+ bxc6 dxe5 dxe5 Qxd8+ Kxd8 Nxe5] attacks both c6 and f7 at once, costing Black a clean pawn with the king stranded on d8. Unpin with [b:Bd7] or trade [b:exd4] first. [%csl Rc6,Rf7][%cal Gb5c6] }
5. Bxc6+ bxc6 6. dxe5 dxe5 7. Qxd8+ Kxd8 8. Nxe5 ) 5. Nc3
