{ White retreats the bishop to a4, preserving the pin on c6 and standing ready to drop back to b3 whenever Black pushes [b:b5]. Black's main continuation by a wide margin is [Nf6], developing with an attack on e4. Black also has several independent fourth-move systems: [b5], the Caro Variation chasing the bishop at once; [d6], the Modern Steinitz; [Bc5], the Classical Deferred; and [Nge7], the Cozio Deferred. [%csl Ya4,Yc6,Yb3][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 4. Bxc6
{ The Exchange Variation, played with great success by Emanuel Lasker and revived at the highest level by Bobby Fischer. White trades off the Spanish bishop on move four to damage Black's queenside pawn structure for the rest of the game. Black almost universally recaptures away from the center with [dxc6], opening diagonals for both bishops and the queen at once; [bxc6], the Lutikov Variation, keeps pawns pointing toward the middle, yet leaves a6 isolated and hems in the c8-bishop. [%csl Ya6][%cal Gd7c6] }
4... dxc6
{ The Exchange Ruy Lopez tabiya. Taking e5 right away with [Nxe5] fails to win a pawn because [Nxe5 Qd4] forks the knight on e5 and the pawn on e4, regaining the pawn with the two bishops on an open board. Instead White plays Fischer's [O-O] (making [w:Nxe5] a real threat next move), Lasker's [d4] to trade off e5 at once, Keres's [Nc3], or quiet [d3]. Once White trades the d-pawn for e5, White's healthy 4-vs-3 kingside majority can create a passed pawn in the endgame while Black's doubled c-pawns cripple the queenside majority, balanced in the middlegame by Black's bishop pair. [%csl Ye5,Ye4][%cal Ge1g1] }
( 4... bxc6
{ The Lutikov Variation. Capturing toward the center keeps e5 easy to support with [b:d6], [b:Qe7], or [b:Qf6], at the cost of a clogged bishop on c8 and a loose pawn on a6. White should resist grabbing e5 immediately with [w:Nxe5], which lets [b:Qg5] hit both e5 and g2 for sharp counterplay; castling [O-O], striking in the center with [d4], or developing [Nc3] gives White a clean, lasting positional edge. [%csl Ye5,Yc8,Ya6][%cal Ge1g1] }
