{ Barendregt and Fischer's move, used by Fischer with devastating effect at the 1966 Havana Olympiad. With White's king castled, [w:Re1] is ready to pin the e-file, turning [w:Nxe5] into a real threat to win a pawn. Black has five ways to meet it: [f6] (Gligorić's sturdy pawn barrier), [Bg4] (pinning the knight and inviting the Alapin Gambit), [Qd6] (Bronstein's queen defence clearing d8 for long castling), [Bd6], and [Qf6]. [%csl Yd8][%cal Gf7f6] }
( 5. d4
{ Lasker's classical treatment of the Exchange Variation, famous from his victories over Tarrasch in 1908 and Capablanca at St. Petersburg 1914. Challenging e5 on move five invites an immediate queen trade after [exd4 Qxd4 Qxd4 Nxd4], steering straight into an endgame battle between White's healthy 4-vs-3 kingside majority and Black's bishop pair. [Bg4] is Black's livelier alternative, pinning f3 to meet [Bg4 dxe5] with [Bg4 dxe5 Qxd1+ Kxd1 O-O-O+]. [%csl Ye5,Yf3][%cal Ge5d4] }
