{ In the Wormald Attack, Black breaks the pin on c6 so e5 is safe and now chooses where to place the bishops. [Bc5] puts immediate pressure along the a7 to g1 diagonal ([Bc5 a4 b4 d3]), [Be7] heads into classical Worrall structures after [Be7 O-O] or sharp central play after [Be7 c3 O-O d4], and [Bb7] eyes e4 down the long diagonal. [%csl Yc6,Ye5,Ya7][%cal Gf8c5] }
6... Bc5 ( 6... Be7 ) ( 6... Bb7 ) ) ( 5. d4
{ The Mackenzie Variation opens the center on move five before castling, bypassing Closed Spanish manoeuvring altogether. Black should avoid grabbing [b:Nxe4], which leaves White's d4-pawn strong; the clean equalizer is [exd4], surrendering e5 so [exd4 O-O Be7 e5 Ne4 Nxd4 O-O Nf5 d5] uses active piece play to neutralize White's space. Breaking the pin first with [b5] transposes to open lines after [b5 Bb3]. [%csl Ye5][%cal Ge5d4] }
5... exd4 ( 5... b5 ) 6. O-O
{ In the Mackenzie Variation, White castles and threatens both [w:e5] and [w:Re1] down the half-open e-file before recapturing on d4. Black's accurate equalizer is [Be7], shielding the king on e8 so [Be7 e5 Ne4 Nxd4 O-O Nf5 d5] liquidates the center cleanly with active pieces; snatching a second pawn on e4 or chasing the bishop with [b:b5] leaves Black's king dangerously exposed. [%csl Yd4,Ye8,Ye4][%cal Gf8e7] }
6... Be7 ( 6... Nxe4 $2
{ Snatching a second central pawn transposes into the Riga Variation with Black's king still on the open e-file: [Re1 d5 Nxd4 Bd6 Nxc6] forces Black into an endgame where White's two minor pieces outplay a rook. Shield the king first with [b:Be7]. [%cal Gf1e1] }
7. Re1 d5 8. Nxd4 Bd6 9. Nxc6 ) ( 6... b5 $2
{ Chasing White's bishop before developing the kingside leaves Black too far behind in development once the center opens: after [Bb3 Na5 Nxd4], White regains the pawn with rapid piece play against Black's uncastled king. Play [b:Be7] first. [%cal Ga4b3] }
7. Bb3 Na5 8. Nxd4 ) ) ( 5. Nc3
{ Tarrasch's Four Knights Spanish with [a6] and [Ba4] included. Developing to c3 guards e4 and threatens [w:Bxc6] followed by [w:Nxe5], at the cost of blocking White's c-pawn so White cannot build a [w:c3] and [w:d4] center. Black breaks the pin with [b5] ([b5 Bb3 Be7] or [b5 Bb3 Bc5]), develops [Bc5] (where [Bc5 Nxe5 Nxe5 d4] is the classic center fork trick), or plays [Be7]. [%csl Yc3,Ye4][%cal Gb7b5] }
5... b5 ( 5... Bc5 ) ( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... d5 $4
{ Lashing out in the center overlooks that the knight on c6 is pinned to the uncastled king on e8 and cannot defend e5 or d5. White captures [Nxe5] with an immediate threat on c6, and whether Black tries [Nxe5 d4] ([Nxe5 d4 Nxc6]) or [Nxe5 Bd7 Nxd7 Qxd7 exd5], Black drops a pawn and collapses in the center. Unpin with [b:b5] or [b:Be7], or play [b:Bc5] instead. [%csl Rc6,Re5][%cal Gf3e5] }
6. Nxe5 d4 ) ) ( 5. c3
{ The Jaffe Gambit plays [c3] a move before castling or defending e4. While popular in club games to prepare [w:d4], it leaves e4 unprotected: Black's sharpest reply is [Nxe4], since after [Nxe4 Qe2 Nc5] Black attacks the a4-bishop with tempo and holds active play even after White recovers e5. Black can also decline the pawn with [Be7] or [b5] ([b5 Bc2]), transposing into quiet Spanish lines. [%csl Ye4,Ye5][%cal Gf6e4] }
5... Nxe4 ( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... b5 ) ) ( 5. Bxc6
{ The Bayreuth Variation trades on c6 on move five right after Black develops [Nf6]. Compared with the fourth-move Exchange, Black's knight is already on f6, which attacks e4 right away and rules out [b:f6] as a pawn guard for e5. Black recaptures [dxc6] ([dxc6 d3 Bd6 Nbd2] or [dxc6 Nc3], since [dxc6 Nxe5 Qd4] still regains e4); [bxc6] is playable too. [%csl Yc6,Yf6,Ye4][%cal Gd7c6] }
5... dxc6 ( 5... bxc6 ) ) 5... Be7
{ The Closed Ruy Lopez, the royal road of classical chess from Steinitz and Capablanca to Fischer, Karpov, Kasparov, and Carlsen. Developing the bishop to e7 shields Black's king on the e-file and prepares castling, which turns [b:Nxe4] (or [b:b5] followed by [b:Nxe4]) into a real threat next move. White defends e4 with [Re1] (the classical main line), [d3] (the modern Martinez Variation), [Qe2] (the Worrall Attack), [Bxc6] (the Delayed Exchange), [d4] (the Center Attack), or [Nc3]. [%csl Ye7,Ye4][%cal Gf1e1] }
( 5... b5
{ Advancing [b5] on move five breaks the pin on c6 before Black commits the dark-squared bishop to e7, keeping the sharp Arkhangelsk ([Bb3 Bb7]) and Neo-Arkhangelsk ([Bb3 Bc5]) setups open alongside the Closed Ruy Lopez. [Bb3] is White's only move, tucking the Spanish bishop onto the strong a2-g8 diagonal aimed at f7; forgetting the bishop is attacked loses a full piece to [bxa4] with nothing in return. [%csl Yc6,Ye7,Ya2,Yg8][%cal Ga4b3] }
6. Bb3
{ Black breaks the pin on c6 on move five before committing the dark-squared bishop to e7, keeping c5 available for the f8-bishop and b7 ready for the c8-bishop. From here Black branches into four major systems: [Be7] (rejoining the Closed Spanish if White plays [Be7 Re1]), [Bc5] (the sharp Neo-Arkhangelsk), [Bb7] (the classic Arkhangelsk eyeing e4), and [d6] (guarding e5 while holding the bishops back). [%csl Yc6,Ye7,Yc5][%cal Gf8e7] }
6... Be7
{ Black has played [b5] on move five and [Be7] on move six, reaching the Closed Spanish if White answers [Re1], while giving White extra fifth- and sixth-move options because Black committed the queenside pawn early. Besides [Re1], White can strike in the center with [d4], challenge the b5-hook right away with [a4] (the Wing Attack), or play [d3]; note that [w:c3] allows [b:Nxe4] since e4 is still unguarded. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf1e1] }
( 6... Bc5
{ The Neo-Arkhangelsk (or Modern Archangel), championed at elite level by Alexei Shirov and Fabiano Caruana. Black places the dark-squared bishop on the active a7 to g1 diagonal while keeping the c8-bishop flexible for b7 or g4. White's main tries are [c3] ([c3 d6 d4 Bb6 a4]) to seize the center with tempo, [a4] right away ([a4 Rb8 c3 d6 d4 Bb6]) to probe the b5-hook, quiet [d3], or the fork trick [Nxe5]. [%csl Ya7,Yg1,Yb7][%cal Gc2c3] }
7. c3 ( 7. a4 ) ( 7. d3 ) ( 7. Nxe5 ) ) ( 6... Bb7
{ The Arkhangelsk Variation, developed by masters from the northern Russian city of Arkhangelsk. Fianchettoing on move six puts immediate pressure on e4 and often pairs with [b:Bc5] to aim both bishops at White's kingside. White usually answers [d3], blunting the b7-bishop with a sturdy pawn chain before challenging b5 via [d3 Be7 Nc3 O-O a4] or facing [d3 Bc5 Nc3 O-O a4]; [Re1] and [c3] are sharp alternatives. [%csl Ye4,Yb5][%cal Gd2d3] }
