9. Nxf7 Kxf7 10. Qf3+ Ke6 11. Nc3 ) ) ) 7. Re1 ( 7. d4 ) ( 7. a4 ) ( 7. d3 ) )
( 5... Nxe4
{ The Open Ruy Lopez, championed by Viktor Korchnoi, Artur Yusupov, and Viswanathan Anand. Unlike in the Berlin, Black already has [a6] on the board, so [d4] can be met with tempo by [d4 b5 Bb3 d5], breaking the pin on c6 and returning the extra pawn for free piece play and an active knight outpost on e4. White's critical move is [d4]; [Re1] ([Re1 Nc5 Bxc6 dxc6 Nxe5 Be7]) and [Qe2] are calmer alternatives. [%csl Yc6,Ye4][%cal Gd2d4] }
6. d4 ( 6. Re1
{ Attacking the knight on e4 right away sidesteps the deep forcing theory of [w:d4] while regaining the pawn cleanly along the open e-file. Black's accurate reply is [Nc5], counterattacking White's bishop on a4 with gain of time; after [Nc5 Bxc6 dxc6 d4 Ne6 Nxe5 Be7], White holds central space while Black's bishop pair and solid blockade on d5 keep the balance. [%csl Ye4,Ya4,Yd5][%cal Ge4c5] }
6... Nc5 ( 6... d5 $2
{ The Skipworth Gambit props up e4 with a pawn, yet it leaves c6 pinned and the e-file wide open: [Bxc6+ bxc6 d3] (taking on c6 before [w:d3] or [w:Nxe5]) shatters Black's queenside and wins e5 after [Bxc6+ bxc6 d3 Nf6 Nxe5]. Retreat to [b:Nc5] instead. }
7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. d3 Nf6 9. Nxe5 ) ( 6... Nd6 $2
{ Copying the Berlin retreat forgets that White's bishop already stands on a4 instead of b5: [Nd6] hits nothing and blocks Black's own d-pawn, letting [d4 Be7 dxe5 Nc4] give White a clear lead in space and development. Jump to [b:Nc5] to gain time on a4. }
7. d4 Be7 8. dxe5 Nc4 ) ( 6... f5 $2
{ Supporting the knight with the f-pawn weakens Black's king diagonals and the open e-file: [d3] chases the knight back at once, and [d3 Nf6 Bxc6 dxc6 Nxe5] threatens discovered checks against e8. Retreat the knight with tempo via [b:Nc5] instead. }
7. d3 Nf6 8. Bxc6 dxc6 9. Nxe5 ) ) ( 6. Qe2 ) ) ( 5... Bc5
{ The Møller Defence develops Black's dark-squared bishop actively to c5 without committing [b:b5] first. White's most testing reply is [c3], preparing [w:d4] with tempo (note that [c3 b5] lets White drop straight back to [w:Bc2] in one move, so Black often plays [c3 Ba7 d4]). [d3] and [Re1] are solid alternatives, while the fork trick [Nxe5] can be met sharply by [Nxe5 Nxe4] counterattacking f2. [%csl Yc5,Yf2][%cal Gc2c3] }
6. c3 ( 6. d3 ) ( 6. Re1 ) ( 6. Nxe5 ) ) ( 5... d6
{ The Steinitz Deferred (or Russian Defence) bolsters e5 solidly on move five while keeping [b:b5] ready whenever White threatens the c6-knight. White maintains a pleasant space advantage with [Re1], [c3] (securing c2 for the bishop before [w:d4]), or [Bxc6+] ([Bxc6+ bxc6 d4], challenging e5 before Black castles). Rushing [w:d4] without [c3] has to be handled carefully because [b:b5] and [b:Nxd4] revive the Noah's Ark Trap. [%csl Ye5,Yc2][%cal Gf1e1] }
6. Re1 ( 6. c3 ) ( 6. Bxc6+ ) ) ( 5... d5 $2
{ The Central Countergambit opens lines while Black's king is still on e8 and c6 is pinned: White takes [exd5] (stronger than [Bxc6+ bxc6 Nxe5], which allows [b:Bd6]), and [exd5 Qxd5 Nc3] gains a big lead in development. Play [b:Be7], [b:b5], or [b:Nxe4]. [%csl Re8,Rc6][%cal Ge4d5] }
6. exd5 Qxd5 7. Nc3 ) 6. Re1 ( 6. d3
{ The Martinez Variation, a modern favourite of Magnus Carlsen and elite players looking for a rich fight without Marshall or Berlin theory. Waiting until move six to play [d3] has a clear point over fifth-move Anderssen lines: Black has already committed the bishop to e7, ruling out the active [b:Bc5] diagonal and the [b:g6] fianchetto. Because e4 is now guarded, White threatens [w:Bxc6] and [w:Nxe5], so Black answers [b5] ([b5 Bb3 d6]) or [d6]. [%csl Ye7,Ye4][%cal Gb7b5] }
6... b5 ( 6... d6 ) 7. Bb3
{ Black breaks the pin on c6 and claims queenside space. After [d6] (or [O-O] first, inviting [O-O c3 d5] if White allows it), White does not rush [w:d4]: White usually probes Black's queenside hook with [w:a4], tucks [w:c3] in to give the b3-bishop a safe retreat on c2 against [b:Na5], and routes the b1-knight via d2 and f1 toward g3 or e3 for a slow manoeuvring battle. [%csl Yc6,Yc2,Yd2][%cal Gd7d6] }
7... d6 ( 7... O-O ) ) ( 6. Qe2
{ The Worrall Attack, championed by Paul Keres and used by Nigel Short in his 1992 Candidates match victory over Anatoly Karpov. Protecting e4 with the queen makes [w:Bxc6] followed by [w:Nxe5] an immediate threat while leaving d1 open for White's f1-rook, where it stands directly behind a later [w:d4] break. Black has to break the pin with [b5], leading after [b5 Bb3] to a choice between [b:d6] and [b:O-O]. [%csl Ye4,Yd1][%cal Gb7b5] }
6... b5 7. Bb3
{ In the Worrall Attack, Black chooses between two distinct setups. Playing [d6] enters a Closed Spanish structure where White places the king's rook on d1 rather than e1 ([d6 c3 O-O Rd1]) to support [w:d4] head-on. Castling [O-O] first keeps a Marshall-style [O-O c3 d5] pawn sacrifice in reserve, which White often meets solidly with [O-O c3 d5 d3] to hold the center together before bringing the rook to d1. [%csl Yd1,Ye1][%cal Gd7d6] }
7... d6 ( 7... O-O ) ) ( 6. Bxc6
{ The Delayed Exchange Variation. Waiting until move six to capture on c6 carries a subtle positional point: Black has already placed the knight on f6 and the bishop on e7, so after [dxc6] Black cannot bolster e5 effortlessly with [b:f6] as in the fourth-move Exchange. White follows [dxc6] with [dxc6 d3 Nd7 Nbd2 O-O Nc4] (forcing [b:f6] or [b:Bf6] after spending extra moves) or [dxc6 Re1 Nd7 d4] to target Black's doubled pawns. [%csl Yc6,Yf6,Ye7][%cal Gd7c6] }
6... dxc6 ) ( 6. d4
{ The Center Attack strikes in the middle before Black pushes [b:b5], aiming to open lines while Black's king is still uncastled. Black's cleanest reply is [exd4], surrendering e5 so [exd4 e5 Ne4] wins time for counterplay ([exd4 e5 Ne4 Nxd4 O-O Re1 Nc5] equalizes comfortably, while [exd4 e5 Ne4 c3 dxc3] is the sharp Basque Gambit). Breaking the pin with [b5] transposes to open lines after [b5 Bb3]. [%csl Ye5][%cal Ge5d4] }
6... exd4
{ In the Center Attack, Black gives up e5 to open the e-file while White's d-pawn has left d2. White's main move is [e5], kicking Black's knight forward so [e5 Ne4 Nxd4 O-O Re1 Nc5] leads to lively piece play (or [e5 Ne4 c3 dxc3], the Basque Gambit sacrificing a pawn for rapid development). [Re1] is the other testing try ([Re1 b5 e5 Nxe5 Rxe5 d6]), whereas recapturing on d4 right away hands Black free queenside tempos. [%csl Ye5,Yd2,Yd4][%cal Ge4e5] }
( 6... b5 ) ( 6... Nxd4 $2
{ Capturing on d4 with the knight looks active, yet [Nxd4 exd4 e5] gains crucial time on Black's f6-knight, driving it all the way back to g8 while White's queen dominates d4 after [Nxd4 exd4 e5 Ng8 Qxd4]. Capture with the pawn via [b:exd4] instead. }
7. Nxd4 exd4 8. e5 Ng8 9. Qxd4 ) 7. e5 ( 7. Re1 ) ( 7. Nxd4 $2
{ Recapturing on d4 right away looks natural, yet skipping [w:e5] lets Black trade knights and win queenside tempos with [Nxd4 e5 b5], costing White the initiative and forcing careful defence to stay level. Push [w:e5] or play [w:Re1] first. }
7... Nxd4 8. e5 b5 ) ) ( 6. Nc3
{ The Morphy Attack defends e4 with the knight and threatens [w:Bxc6] followed by [w:Nxe5] right away. The drawback is strategic: with the knight occupying c3, White can neither build the classical [w:c3] and [w:d4] pawn center nor tuck the Spanish bishop back to c2 if Black chases it with [b:b5] and [b:Na5]. Black breaks the pin with [b5] ([b5 Bb3 d6 d3 O-O]) or guards e5 solidly with [d6]. [%csl Ye4,Yc3,Yc2][%cal Gb7b5] }
6... b5 ( 6... d6 ) ) *

[Event "Four Knights Game"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3
{ The Three Knights Opening. White develops both knights before choosing a square for the f1 bishop, guarding e4 and d5 while keeping [w:d4], [w:Bb5], and [w:g3] in reserve. The trade-off is that the c3 knight blocks the c-pawn, so White gives up the classical plan of building a two-pawn center with [w:c3] and [w:d4]. Black's cleanest reply is [Nf6], reaching the Four Knights Game. [d6] holds e5 solidly, [g6] and [Bb4] (met by [w:Nd5]) break symmetry early, while routine [b:Bc5] allows the center fork trick [w:Nxe5]. [%csl Gf1,Ge4,Gd5][%cal Gg8f6] }
3... Nf6
{ The Four Knights Game, one of the oldest classical open games and a favorite of Paulsen, Rubinstein, and Capablanca before World War I. All four knights stand on their natural squares, defending their own e-pawns and attacking the opponent's. White has two main tries for an edge: [Bb5] (the Spanish Variation, threatening to remove the guard of e5) and [d4] (the Four Knights Scotch, opening the center at once). Quiet setups like [g3] (Glek's fianchetto), [a3] (Gunsberg's waiting move), and [h3] keep pieces on the board, while [Nd5] is Naroditsky's tricky blitz sideline. Avoid [w:Bc4] ([b:Nxe4], in the Two Knights chapter) and the unsound [Nxe5] Halloween Gambit. [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf1b5] }
( 3... d6
{ Answering the Three Knights Opening with [d6] avoids Four Knights symmetry and keeps Black's f-pawn or g-pawn free, at the price of locking Black's f8 bishop inside the pawn structure. White seizes the initiative with [d4], opening the centre while Black is passive, or [Bb5], pinning the c6 knight in a Steinitz Ruy Lopez setup where White's knight already reinforces e4 and d5. [Bc4] is a sound, quieter Italian option. [%cal Gd2d4,Gf1b5,Gf1c4] }
4. d4 ( 4. Bb5 ) ( 4. Bc4 ) ) ( 3... Bb4
{ Pinning the c3 knight on move three before White plays [w:d4] or [w:Bb5] breaks symmetry early, yet the bishop on b4 has no pawn threat behind it yet and stands exposed to White's knight. [Nd5] is the sharpest reply, hitting b4 and c7 with tempo to force [Nd5 Nf6] (Schlechter's counterattack) or [Nd5 Bc5]. [Bb5] transposes to the Double Spanish after [Bb5 Nf6], while [Bc4] and [a3] keep comfortable play. [%csl Gc3,Gb4,Gc7][%cal Gc3d5] }
4. Nd5
{ White punishes the early bishop sortie by leaping into d5 with tempo, taking aim at b4 and c7. Black has three sound ways to handle the hit on b4. [Nf6] is Schlechter's choice, counterattacking e4 and offering [Nf6 Nxb4 Nxb4] or [Nf6 Bc4]; [Be7] admits the bishop was misplaced and returns home to keep the kingside solid; and [Bc5] develops actively, though [w:c3] and [w:d4] soon gain space. Retreating to [b:Ba5] is playable too, but [w:Bc4] and [w:O-O] leave Black's bishop sidelined on the queenside. [%csl Gd5,Gb4,Gc7][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 4. Bb5 ) ( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. a3 ) 4... Nf6
{ Schlechter's Variation in the Three Knights: instead of wasting a tempo retreating the attacked b4 bishop, Black develops the kingside knight to counterattack e4 and challenge White's intruder on d5. White's cleanest way to keep a nagging pull is [Nxb4 Nxb4 c3 Nc6 d4], claiming the bishop pair and rolling the central pawns forward with tempo on the knight. [c3] first ([c3 Be7 Bd3 d6]) and [Bd3] are strong positional alternatives that preserve central tension while driving the bishop back. [%csl Yf6,Ye4][%cal Gd5b4,Gc2c3] }
( 4... Bc5 ) ( 4... Be7 ) 5. Nxb4 ( 5. c3 ) ( 5. Bd3 ) ) ( 3... Bc5
{ Played in nearly a quarter of club games out of Italian Game habit, [b:Bc5] overlooks a key detail: White's light-squared bishop is still tucked safely on f1 rather than sitting loose on c4. White seizes the initiative at once with the center fork trick [Nxe5]. After [Nxe5 Nxe5 d4], the d-pawn forks bishop and knight, winning back the piece while sweeping Black's central pawn off the board and opening lines for [w:Bd3], [w:O-O], and [w:f4]. [%csl Gf1,Gc4][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5
{ The center fork trick in action: White gives up the knight on e5 for one move because [w:d4] will fork c5 and e5 next. Black's best damage control is [Nxe5], intending [Nxe5 d4 Bd6] so the bishop stays alive and preserves the bishop pair even though White wins the center. Desperadoing on f2 with [Bxf2+] is a popular club try ([Bxf2+ Kxf2 Nxe5 d4]), yet handing over both bishops and the center leaves White comfortably on top once the king steps onto g1. [%csl Ge5,Gc5,Gf2][%cal Gc6e5] }
4... Nxe5 ( 4... Bxf2+ 5. Kxf2 Nxe5 6. d4
{ White claims the full center and kicks the knight. Black's best retreat is [Ng6], keeping the knight near the kingside to prepare [b:d6] and [b:Nf6] (in club games Black often throws in [b:Qf6+] [w:Kg1] first, which loses time and helps White's rook take the f-file). White's king is surprisingly safe once tucked onto g1: Black has traded off the dark-squared bishop that could probe the dark squares around it, while White develops with [w:Bc4] or [w:Be2], [w:Rf1], and [w:Kg1]. [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge5g6] }
6... Ng6 ( 6... Qf6+ 7. Kg1 Ne7
{ Black brings the second knight to e7 rather than moving the e5 knight twice, baiting White into taking the loose-looking piece on e5. With White's c1 bishop still on its home square and the king boxed in on g1, opening the a7-g1 diagonal too soon is fatal. [h4] gives the h1 rook air and prepares [w:Rh3] or [w:Bg5], while club players also play [w:Be3] to seal the a7-g1 diagonal before touching e5. [%csl Ge7,Ge5,Gc1][%cal Gh2h4] }
8. h4 ( 8. dxe5 $4
{ Taking the knight opens the a7-g1 diagonal with fatal effect: [Qb6+] checks the boxed-in king on g1, and every interposition ([Qb6+ Qd4 Qxd4+ Be3 Qxe3#]) ends in checkmate. Play [w:h4] or [w:Be3] first before capturing on e5. [%csl Ra7,Rg1][%cal Gf6b6] }
8... Qb6+ 9. Qd4 Qxd4+ 10. Be3 Qxe3# ) ) ) 5. d4
{ The point of White's knight sacrifice: the d-pawn forks bishop and knight and recovers the piece immediately. Black must retreat with [Bd6] rather than grabbing on d4. After [Bd6 dxe5 Bxe5], Black has lost the central pawn and a bit of time, yet keeps both bishops and avoids bringing White's queen into the middle of the board with tempo. White continues with [w:Bd3], [w:O-O], and a later [w:f4] to gain space on the kingside. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc5d6] }
5... Bd6 ( 5... Bxd4 $2
{ Capturing on d4 clings to material parity at a steep positional price. [Qxd4] plants White's queen unchallenged in the middle, hits the e5 knight, and x-rays g7 so the knight cannot move freely without dropping the g-pawn. Black loses the dark-squared bishop and suffers on the dark squares for good. Step back with [b:Bd6] to preserve the bishop. [%csl Rd4,Re5][%cal Gd1d4] }
