{ The Spanish Four Knights. White threatens [w:Bxc6] followed by [w:Nxe5], since unlike the pure Ruy Lopez White's e4 pawn is already guarded by the c3 knight. On the other hand, the c3 knight cannot be shielded by a c-pawn, which gives Black active answers besides copying: [Nd4] is Akiba Rubinstein's counterattack on the bishop, [Bb4] is the solid Double Spanish, [Bc5] develops actively while eyeing f2, [Bd6] overprotects e5 in modern style, and [a6] puts the question to the bishop at once. [%csl Ge4,Gc3,Gf2][%cal Gf8b4] }
( 4. d4
{ The Four Knights Scotch. White breaks the symmetrical deadlock immediately by striking at e5, opening lines for both bishops and the queen while the c3 knight stays out of the way. [exd4] is by far the best answer, surrendering e5 to challenge White's center with [b:Bb4] or [b:Bc5] (main lines after [exd4 Nxd4] continue in the Scotch Game chapter). [Bb4] pins c3 right away to threaten e4, inviting the sharp [w:Nxe5] Krause Gambit, whereas [d6] is passive and cramps Black's f8 bishop. [%csl Ge5,Gc3,Ge4][%cal Ge5d4] }
