{ The main structural tabiya of the Glek System. Black has traded knights on c3, giving White doubled c-pawns that reinforce d4 while opening the half-open b-file for [w:Rb1] alongside the g2 fianchetto bishop. Black develops the dark-squared bishop to [Bd6] ([Bd6 O-O O-O Re1] followed by [w:d3] or [w:d4]) or [Bc5], keeping solid central footing while White presses against b7 and e5. [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Gg2][%cal Gf8d6] }
7... Bd6 ( 7... Bc5 ) ) ( 4. a3
{ The Gunsberg Variation, a sly waiting move reviving a classic Three Knights trick: White prevents [b:Bb4], prepares [w:Bb5] or [w:d4], and dares Black to develop [Bc5] on autopilot. Black's cleanest reply is [d5], opening the center at once ([d5 exd5 Nxd5 Bb5 Nxc3 bxc3 Bd6]), while [Be7] and [d6] are solid quiet choices. Notice that [Bc5], played in nearly half of club games, allows the center fork trick [Bc5 Nxe5 Nxe5 d4 Bd6 dxe5 Bxe5] for a pleasant White edge. [%cal Gd7d5] }
