{ The Halloween (or Müller-Schulze) Gambit, named in the 1990s by German programmer Steffen Jakob because sacrificing a full knight on move four shocks unprepared defenders. White rolls d4 and e5 forward to chase both black knights back and cramp Black's camp. Black's strongest refutation is [Ng6], planning [Ng6 e5 Ng8 Bc4 d5]; retreating [b:Nc6] is common too ([b:Nc6] [w:d5] [b:Bb4]), whereas [b:Neg4] walks into [w:e5] and drops the extra piece back. [%csl Gd4,Ge5][%cal Ge5g6] }
