8... Bxc3 9. Bxc3 d5 ) ) ) ( 4. d3
{ Playing [d3] turns the Four Knights into a quiet, symmetrical manoeuvring game, locking down e4 while shutting White's light-squared bishop inside the pawn chain for the moment. Because White puts no immediate pressure on the centre, Black can seize the initiative straight away: [Bb4] pins the c3 knight to prepare [d5], [d5] strikes open the centre at once ([d5 exd5 Nxd5 Be2]), and [Bc5] reaches a calm Four Knights Italian. [%csl Ye4,Yc3][%cal Gf8b4] }
4... Bb4 ( 4... d5 ) ( 4... Bc5 ) ) 4... Bb4
{ The Double Spanish. Black mirrors the pin on c3, so if White captures on c6 and e5, Black can answer in kind on c3 and e4. [O-O] is the main line, getting the king safe so White's threat on c6 and e5 becomes real. [d3] is a solid quiet alternative, and [Bxc6] trades immediately (Paulsen's move, usually played after castling as Nimzowitsch did). Note that jumping early with [w:Nd5] achieves little because [b:Nxd5] [w:exd5] [b:e4] counterattacks the f3 knight and frees Black's game. [%csl Gc3,Gc6,Ge5][%cal Ge1g1] }
( 4... Nd4
{ The Rubinstein Variation, first tried by Emil Schallopp against Paulsen in 1881 and turned into a major weapon by Akiba Rubinstein before 1914. Because White has no pawn on c3, Black leaps into d4 to hit the b5 bishop and offer the e5 pawn as bait for [b:Qe7] counterplay down the e-file. White has a wide choice: [Ba4] is the sharpest test (inviting Black's [b:Bc5] gambit), [Bc4] eyes f7, [Nxd4] opens a positional squeeze after [Nxd4 exd4 e5], [O-O] is Henneberger's solid castling, and [Nxe5] leads to quick equality after [b:Qe7]. [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Gb5][%cal Gb5a4] }
5. Ba4
{ Paulsen's retreat keeps the bishop on the a4-e8 diagonal and renews the threat to take on e5 for real. Black's signature reply is [Bc5]: a Marshall-style pawn sacrifice where [Bc5 Nxe5 O-O] gives Black rapid piece fire against White's bottled-up queenside. For a quieter game, [c6] guards d5 and prepares [b:d6] or [b:Qc7], while [Nxf3+] [w:Qxf3] releases the tension early at the cost of activating White's queen. [%csl Ga4,Ge8,Ge5][%cal Gf8c5] }
( 5. Bc4
{ Bogoljubow's and Spielmann's retreat moves the bishop onto the Italian diagonal toward f7 while keeping e5 in view. Black's best reply is [Bc5], matching the bishop and meeting [Bc5 Nxe5] with [b:Qe7], when [Bc5 Nxe5 Qe7 Nf3 d5] gives Black vigorous central counterplay for the sacrificed pawn. [c6] is a solid alternative preparing [b:d5], whereas grabbing e4 with [b:Nxe4] backfires after [w:Nxe4] [b:d5] [w:Bxd5]. [%csl Gf7,Ge5,Ge4][%cal Gf8c5] }
5... Bc5 ( 5... c6 ) ) ( 5. Nxd4
{ The Rubinstein Variation Accepted, the most common move in club games. White trades off Black's active intruder on d4 rather than retreating the b5 bishop. Black must recapture with [exd4], kicking the c3 knight and opening the e-file so that passive knight retreats give Black easy equality, while White's only testing try is the sharp counter-thrust [exd4 e5 dxc3 exf6 Qxf6]. [%csl Gd4,Gb5,Gc3][%cal Ge5d4] }
5... exd4
{ The Rubinstein Accepted. Black recaptures with the e-pawn, kicking the c3 knight and opening the e-file toward White's king. Retreating the knight to e2 or b1 passively surrenders e4 and hands Black easy equality with [b:Bc5] and [b:d5], so White's only ambitious test is Karl Rosenkranz's and Alexander Alekhine's sharp counter-thrust [e5]! Both knights now hang to pawns, forcing [e5 dxc3 exf6 Qxf6]. [%csl Gc3,Ge2,Gb1][%cal Ge4e5] }
