[Event "Other open games (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 ( 2. d4 exd4
{ The Center Game Accepted. White has four distinct paths: [Qxd4] is the Center Game proper, accepting a tempo loss on the queen to clear the queenside for [w:Bd2] and [w:O-O-O]; [c3] is the swashbuckling Danish Gambit; [Nf3] is Kieseritzky's flexible move order (inviting [Nf3 Nc6], which transposes to the Scotch Game chapter, or independent [Nf3 c5] and [Nf3 Bc5]); and [Bc4] is von der Lasa's gambit. [%cal Gd1d4] }
3. Qxd4
{ White recaptures with the queen in the middle of the board. Bringing the queen out early carries a concrete price: Black wins a free developing tempo with [Nc6]. In return, White clears the d-file on move three, planning [Nc6 Qe3] followed by [w:Nc3], [w:Bd2], and fast queenside castling. Any Black reply other than [Nc6] lets White's centralized queen stay unchallenged for free. [%cal Gb8c6] }
( 3. c3
{ The Danish (or Nordic) Gambit, popularized in the 1850s and 1860s by Swedish master Hans Lindehn and Dane Martin Severin From, and later played by Blackburne, Marshall, and young Alekhine. White offers one or two queenside pawns to sweep both bishops onto open diagonals raking g7 and f7. Black has three sound answers: [d5] (Sörensen's and Capablanca's practical equalizer), [dxc3] (accepting), and [Qe7] ([Qe7 cxd4 Qxe4+ Be3]). [%csl Gg7,Gf7][%cal Gd4c3] }
3... dxc3
{ The Danish Gambit Accepted. White has two main choices: [Bc4] is Lindehn's Double Danish, offering a second pawn on b2 so both bishops slice across the board from c4 and b2; [Nxc3] is Alekhine's Single Danish, keeping b2 intact for a one-pawn gambit (notice that [Nxc3 Nc6 Nf3] transposes to the Göring Gambit in the Scotch chapter, while independent [Nxc3 Bc5 Bc4 d6 Qb3] is met by [b:Qh4]). Avoid recapturing with [bxc3]. [%csl Gb2,Gc4][%cal Gf1c4] }
( 3... d5
{ The Sörensen (or Capablanca) Defense, the cleanest practical way to take the sting out of the Danish Gambit. Because White's c-pawn stands on c3, after [exd5 Qxd5 cxd4] White has no immediate [w:Nc3] tempo on the queen, and Black reaches a comfortable isolated-queen's-pawn game with [b:Nc6], [b:Bb4+], and [b:Bg4] ([exd5 Nf6] is good too). White must resolve d5 first ([Qxd4] [b:Nf6] is playable too). [%csl Gc3,Gd5][%cal Ge4d5] }
4. exd5 ( 4. Qxd4 ) ( 4. e5 $2
{ Pushing past with [w:e5] lets Black pocket the c3 pawn safely ([dxc3 Nxc3 c6]), since the closed center shields Black's king from any Danish diagonal fire and leaves White a clean pawn down. Trade with [w:exd5] instead. [%csl Rc3][%cal Gd4c3] }
4... dxc3 5. Nxc3 c6 ) ( 4. Bc4 $4
{ Pre-moving [w:Bc4] expecting [b:dxc3] walks straight into [dxc4], dropping a whole bishop on move four because Black pushed the d-pawn instead of taking on c3. Always check Black's move and play [w:exd5]. [%csl Rc3][%cal Gd5c4] }
4... dxc4 5. cxd4 Nf6 ) ( 4. Nf3 $2
{ Developing on autopilot ignores the central tension on e4 and lets Black snatch a clean pawn. After [dxe4 Nxd4 Nf6], White's knight is chased or neutralized while Black keeps the extra central pawn without giving up any open attacking files. Resolve d5 first with [w:exd5] or [w:Qxd4]. [%csl Rf3,Re4][%cal Gd5e4] }
4... dxe4 5. Nxd4 Nf6 ) 4... Qxd5 5. cxd4 Nc6 6. Nf3
{ Declining the Danish has transposed into an Isolated Queen's Pawn position where Black's light-squared bishop is still outside the pawn chain. Capablanca's model plan is [Bb4+] followed by [Bb4+ Nc3 Bg4 Be2 Bxf3 Bxf3 Qc4], hitting d4 and c3 while stopping White from castling for a pleasant endgame. [Bg4] right away works too, though after [Bg4 Be2 Bxf3 Bxf3] grabbing d4 blunders the queen to [w:Bxc6+]. [%csl Gd4,Gc3][%cal Gf8b4] }
6... Bb4+ ( 6... Bg4 ) ) ( 3... Qe7 ) ( 3... d3 $6
{ Returning the pawn with [b:d3] without forcing any concession just develops White's bishop with tempo: after [Bxd3 d6 Nf3], White gets an open game a tempo ahead with c3 supporting [w:Nd2] or [w:Bc2]. Decline actively with [b:d5] or accept with [b:dxc3]. [%csl Rc3][%cal Gf1d3] }
4. Bxd3 d6 5. Nf3 ) 4. Bc4
{ White offers the b2 pawn to enter the Double Danish. Accepting with [cxb2] ([cxb2 Bxb2]) is completely sound as long as Black follows it immediately with an active central counter-strike. Declining b2 with [Nf6] ([Nf6 Nxc3 Bb4 e5 d5 exf6 dxc4]) or [Nc6] ([Nc6 Nxc3]) is playable too, whereas developing [Bc5] or passive [Be7] walks into immediate tactics against f7 and g7. [%csl Gb2,Gf7,Gg7][%cal Gc3b2] }
( 4. Nxc3 ) ( 4. bxc3 $2
{ Recapturing with [w:bxc3] gives away a pawn for zero developing tempo, leaves White with split queenside islands instead of an open b2 diagonal, and lets [Nf6 e5 Qe7] seize the initiative. Offer b2 with [w:Bc4] or develop [w:Nxc3]. [%csl Rb2][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 5. e5 Qe7 ) 4... cxb2
{ Accepting the third pawn with [cxb2] enters the full Danish Gambit Accepted. White recaptures [Bxb2], stationing twin bishops side by side on c4 and b2 raking right across Black's kingside dark and light squares toward f7 and g7 while Black has not developed a single piece! Although engines neutralise the gambit with cold accuracy via [Bxb2 d5] (Schlechter's antidote) or [Bxb2 Nf6], Black cannot afford a single passive move. [%cal Gc1b2] }
( 4... Nf6
{ Black declines the second pawn on b2 to develop with tempo on e4. White must capture on c3 first with [Nxc3], renewing the threat of [w:e5] while clearing b2 (after [Nxc3 Bb4 e5 d5 exf6 dxc4 Qxd8+ Kxd8 fxg7 Rg8], avoid Black's blunder [b:Qxf6] which runs into [w:Bxd5]). Pushing [e5] immediately lets Black advance [b:c2] or [b:d5] with gain of time. [%csl Gb2,Ge4,Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] }
5. Nxc3 ( 5. e5 $2
{ Rushing [w:e5] while Black's pawn still sits on c3 lets [c2] deflect White's queen and [c2 Qxc2 d5] counterattack c4 at once, leaving Black a clean pawn up with free development. Take [w:Nxc3] first. [%csl Rc3,Rc4][%cal Gc3c2] }
5... c2 6. Qxc2 d5 ) ) ( 4... Nc6 ) ( 4... d6 ) ( 4... Bc5 $2
{ Developing [b:Bc5] leaves it undefended on the fifth rank: [Bxf7+] drags Black's king onto f7, and [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qd5+] (or [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qh5+ g6 Qxc5]) recovers the bishop with check while stripping Black's castling right. Play [b:cxb2] or [b:Nf6]. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gc4f7] }
5. Bxf7+ Kxf7 6. Qd5+ ) ( 4... Be7 $2
{ Developing [b:Be7] blocks Black's queen from covering f7 and h4: [Qd5] threatens mate on f7, forcing [Qd5 Nh6 Bxh6 O-O] where Black drops material or gets the kingside torn open. Play [b:cxb2] or [b:Nf6]. [%csl Rf7,Rh4][%cal Gd1d5] }
5. Qd5 Nh6 6. Bxh6 ) ( 4... Qf6 $6
{ Eyeing c3 and f2 with the queen blocks the g8 knight's natural square and lets White recapture on c3 with full compensation and zero risk. After [Nxc3 Bb4 Ne2 Ne7 O-O], White threatens [Nd5] against the exposed queen. Accept the challenge with [cxb2], or develop [Nc6] or [Nf6]. [%csl Rf6,Yg8][%cal Gb1c3] }
5. Nxc3 Bb4 6. Ne2 Ne7 7. O-O ) ( 4... Qe7 $2
{ Trying to hit e4 with [Qe7] shuts Black's dark-squared bishop inside on f8 and gives back the c3 pawn without solving development. After [Nxc3 Nf6 Nge2 d6 O-O], White castles smoothly with a big lead in piece activity while Black struggles to untangle. Play [Nc6], [d6], or [cxb2] instead. [%csl Re7,Rf8][%cal Gb1c3] }
5. Nxc3 Nf6 6. Nge2 d6 7. O-O ) 5. Bxb2
{ The Double Danish tabiya: White is two pawns down, yet the twin bishops on c4 and b2 sweep g7 and f7. Engines favor [Nf6] (the Classical Defense, meeting [Nf6 e5] with [b:d5] or [b:Bb4+]), [Bb4+] (the Copenhagen check), and [d6]; human masters also like Carl Schlechter's [b:d5], handing back both pawns to trade queens into equality, while Chigorin's [b:Qe7] forces White's exact reply [w:Nc3]. [%csl Gc4,Gb2,Gg7][%cal Gg8f6] }
5... Nf6
{ The Classical Defense to the Double Danish: Black develops a kingside defender and hits e4 immediately. White's critical test is [e5], chasing the knight to force an open crisis before Black castles; [Nf3] ([Nf3 Bb4+ Nbd2 O-O O-O Nxe4]) and [Nc3] ([Nc3 Nc6] or [Nc3 Bb4]) are playable developing tries, whereas early queen sorties to f3 or b3 let Black counter-strike in the center. [%csl Ge4,Gf3,Gb3][%cal Ge4e5] }
( 5... Bb4+
{ The Copenhagen Defense. Black checks on b4 on move five to disrupt White's coordination before committing the g8 knight. [Nc3] is White's strongest block, inviting [Nc3 Nf6] (covered in the Scotch chapter) or punishing passive [Nc3 d6] with [Nc3 d6 Qb3] hitting b4 and f7 together (club players also block with [w:Nd2], though it concedes some initiative). Moving the king with [Kf1] forfeits castling needlessly. [%csl Gb4,Gg8,Gf7][%cal Gb1c3] }
6. Nc3 ( 6. Kf1 $2
{ Giving up castling with [w:Kf1] when two clean blocks exist strands the h1 rook in the corner and walks into [Nf6 e5 d5] anyway, leaving White worse on both wings. Block cleanly with [w:Nc3] or [w:Nd2]. [%csl Rh1][%cal Gg8f6] }
6... Nf6 7. e5 d5 ) ) ( 5... d6
{ A cautious club defense: Black keeps both extra pawns, stops [w:e5] from hitting f6 with tempo, and prepares [b:Nc6] and [b:Be6]. White builds steady pressure with [Nf3] (followed by [w:O-O], [w:Nc3], and [w:Qb3]), watching for two common club blunders: [Nf3 Bg4] loses to [Nf3 Bg4 Bxf7+ Kxf7 Ne5+] (the d-pawn is pinned to d8), and [e5 dxe5] drops Black's queen to [w:Bxf7+]. [%csl Gf6,Gd8][%cal Gg1f3] }
6. Nf3 ( 6. Qb3 ) ( 6. Ne2 ) ) ( 5... Nc6 ) ( 5... Qg5 $2
{ Raiding g2 with [b:Qg5] walks into a famous Bird vs Emanuel Lasker trap: White calmly develops [Nf3], and after [Nf3 Qxg2 Rg1 Qh3] Black's queen is shut out while [w:Bxf7+] and [w:Rg3] overwhelm Black's uncastled king. Neutralize the diagonals with [b:d5] or [b:Nf6] instead. [%csl Rg2][%cal Gg1f3] }
6. Nf3 Qxg2 7. Rg1 ) ( 5... f6 $2
{ Blunting the b2 bishop with [b:f6] weakens the a2-g8 diagonal where White's other bishop sits, blocks the g8 knight, and leaves Black's king stuck in the middle after [Nc3 Nc6 Nf3]. Strike back with [b:d5] or [b:Nf6]. [%csl Rb2,Ra2][%cal Gb1c3] }
6. Nc3 Nc6 7. Nf3 ) ( 5... d5
{ Carl Schlechter's Defense, the most reliable practical antidote to the Double Danish. Black hands back one pawn immediately to block the c4 bishop and free the c8 bishop and queen. White must capture with [Bxd5] to keep pressure on f7 and g7 (taking with [w:exd5] lets [b:Nf6] and [b:Bd6] castle smoothly a clear pawn ahead), and Black answers with [Bxd5 Nf6]. [%csl Gc4,Gc8,Gf7][%cal Gc4d5] }
6. Bxd5
{ White eyes f7 with two bishops and threatens [w:Bxf7+] deflecting Black's king from the d8 queen. Black's exact reply is [Nf6], developing with tempo against d5 while vacating g8. If White plays [Nf6 Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qxd8], [b:Bb4+] checks e1 and empties f8 so Black's h8 rook attacks White's queen on d8 across the eighth rank, forcing [w:Qd2] [b:Bxd2+] [w:Nxd2] [b:Re8] with equality. Never block with [c6]. [%csl Gf7,Gd8,Gd5][%cal Gg8f6] }
6... Nf6
{ Schlechter's antidote in full view: Black's knight on f6 attacks d5 while vacating g8. Watch how the trap shuts on [Bxf7+]: after [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qxd8 Bb4+], Black's king on f7, knight on f6, and bishop on b4 have emptied e8, f8, and g8, uncovering the h8 rook's attack across the eighth rank onto White's queen on d8. White must interpose [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qxd8 Bb4+ Qd2] with equality (keeping queens on with [w:Nc3] concedes a slight edge after [b:Nxd5] [w:Nxd5] [b:Nd7]). [%csl Gf6,Gd5,Gg8][%cal Gd5f7] }
( 6... c6 $4
{ Trying to kick the bishop with [b:c6] forgets that d8 is undefended once f7 lures the king away: [Bxf7+] forces [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qxd8] winning Black's queen cleanly (since [Bxf7+ Ke7] loses the queen to [w:Ba3+]), and without [b:Nf6] played g8 is still blocked so [b:Bb4+] recovers nothing. Play [b:Nf6]. [%csl Rd8,Rf7][%cal Gd5f7] }
7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Qxd8 ) 7. Bxf7+ ) ( 5... Qe7
{ Chigorin's Defense to the Double Danish. Black threatens [b:Qb4+], forking king, c4 bishop, and b2 bishop, while also attacking the e4 pawn. Because of that dual threat, White has only one good move: [Nc3], guarding e4 and taking b4 away from Black's queen ([Nc3 c6 Nge2] followed by [w:O-O]). Other developing moves drop a bishop to [b:Qb4+] or let Black liquidate. [%csl Gc4,Gb2,Ge4][%cal Gb1c3] }
6. Nc3 ( 6. Nf3 $4
{ Developing [w:Nf3] overlooks Chigorin's threat: [Qb4+] forks White's king and both bishops on c4 and b2, winning a clean piece on the spot. Always play [w:Nc3] against Black's e7 queen to cover b4 and e4 together. [%csl Rc4,Rb2][%cal Ge7b4] }
6... Qb4+ 7. Nbd2 Qxb2 ) ( 6. Ne2 $4
{ Shielding e4 with [w:Ne2] still leaves b4 wide open: [Qb4+] forks White's king along with the c4 and b2 bishops, winning one of White's attacking bishops for free. Play [w:Nc3] instead. [%csl Re4,Rb4][%cal Ge7b4] }
6... Qb4+ 7. Nd2 Qxb2 ) ( 6. e5 $4
{ Pushing [w:e5] saves the e-pawn yet walks straight into the triple fork [Qb4+], dropping the b2 or c4 bishop immediately while leaving White three pawns and a piece behind. Develop [w:Nc3] instead. [%csl Rb2,Rc4][%cal Ge7b4] }
6... Qb4+ 7. Nd2 Qxb2 ) ( 6. Nd2 $2
{ While [w:Nd2] blocks b4 and guards c4, it cuts off White's queen on d1 from the d-file so [d5] liquidates the center without allowing Schlechter's queen trade, leaving Black a solid pawn ahead. Play [w:Nc3] instead. [%csl Rb4,Rc4][%cal Gd7d5] }
6... d5 7. Bxd5 Nf6 ) ( 6. Qb3 $2
{ Aiming at f7 with [w:Qb3] forgets that e4 is hanging and c4 is a target: [Nc6] develops with tempo for [Nc6 Nd2 Na5], trading off White's attacking bishop while Black stays two pawns ahead. Guard e4 and b4 with [w:Nc3]. [%csl Rf7,Re4][%cal Gb8c6] }
6... Nc6 7. Nd2 Na5 ) ( 6. Qf3 $2
{ Bringing the queen to f3 steals f3 from White's knight and lets [Nf6 Nc3 Nc6] develop smoothly with tempo threats against e4 and f3. Play [w:Nc3] first instead. [%csl Rf3,Re4][%cal Gg8f6] }
6... Nf6 7. Nc3 Nc6 ) ) 6. e5
{ White's e-pawn attacks the f6 knight, and retreating passively to g8 leaves Black badly cramped. Black has three active counter-blows: [d5] (the classical equalizer: [d5 exf6 Bb4+ Bc3 Bxc3+ Nxc3 dxc4 fxg7 Rg8] into a balanced endgame), [Bb4+] right away (setting a sharp trap where only [Bb4+ Bc3] holds, as [Bb4+ Nc3] runs into [b:d5] [w:exf6] [b:Qxf6]), and [Ne4] ([Ne4 Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qf3+ Ke8]). [%csl Gf6,Gg8][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 6. Nf3 ) ( 6. Nc3 ) ( 6. Qf3 $2
{ Bringing the queen to f3 takes the g1 knight's natural square and lets Black seize the initiative with [Bb4+ Nc3 Nc6 O-O-O Ne5] hitting both queen and c4 bishop. Push [w:e5] to challenge f6 directly. [%csl Rf3,Rg1][%cal Gf8b4] }
6... Bb4+ 7. Nc3 Nc6 ) ( 6. Qb3 $6
{ Stacking on f7 with [w:Qb3] leaves the center unguarded: [d5] gives back one pawn to shut the c4 diagonal, and after [d5 exd5 Bd6] Black castles safely a healthy pawn up. Play [w:e5] instead. [%csl Rf7,Rc4][%cal Gd7d5] }
6... d5 7. exd5 Bd6 ) 6... d5 ( 6... Bb4+
{ A sharp test of Danish Gambit preparation. Almost every club player blocks with a knight on c3 or d2, yet only [Bc3] holds the balance: after [Bc3 Qe7 Qe2 Ng8 Nf3], White keeps c4 protected, avoids any pin on a knight, and maintains active piece play for the two sacrificed pawns. Blocking with [Nc3] or [Nd2] walks straight into [b:d5]. [%csl Gc3,Gd2,Gc4][%cal Gb2c3] }
7. Bc3 ( 7. Nc3 $6
{ The most popular club move walks into a cross-pin trap: after [d5 exf6 Qxf6], Black's queen attacks the pinned c3 knight and x-rays b2 and a1 while the d-pawn hits c4 ([d5 exf6 Qxf6 Qe2+ Be6 Bxd5 Bxc3+]), forcing White onto the defensive. Block with [w:Bc3] instead. [%csl Rc3,Rb2][%cal Gd7d5] }
7... d5 8. exf6 Qxf6 ) ( 7. Nd2 $2
{ Blocking with [w:Nd2] leaves c4 exposed to [d5]: after [d5 exf6 dxc4 fxg7 Rg8], Black trades off White's active light-squared bishop and stands better in a four-on-one queenside endgame. Interpose [w:Bc3] instead. [%csl Rc4][%cal Gd7d5] }
7... d5 8. exf6 dxc4 ) ( 7. Kf1 $2
{ Side-stepping with [w:Kf1] forfeits castling, strands the h1 rook, and still runs into [d5 exf6 dxc4 Qxd8+ Kxd8 fxg7 Rg8], where Black's four-to-one queenside pawn majority carries the endgame. Interpose [w:Bc3] instead. [%csl Rh1][%cal Gd7d5] }
7... d5 8. exf6 dxc4 ) ) ( 6... Ne4 ) ) ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. Bc4
{ The von der Lasa Gambit: White develops the bishop to c4 before touching the knights, aiming straight at f7. Black's cleanest replies are [Nf6] (attacking e4; [Nf6 Nf3] reaches the Urusov Gambit, while [Nf6 Qxd4 Nc6 Qe3 Bb4+] gives Black active play), [Nc6] ([Nc6 Nf3] transposes into the Scotch Gambit), or [Bb4+] ([Bb4+ c3 dxc3 bxc3 Be7]). Avoid [Bc5], which drops f7 to [w:Bxf7+]. [%csl Gc4,Gf7,Ge4][%cal Gg8f6] }
3... Nf6 ( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... Bb4+ ) ( 3... Bc5 $2
{ Guarding d4 with [b:Bc5] walks into the classic decoy combination [Bxf7+]: after [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qh5+ g6 Qxc5], White recovers the piece with check, strips Black's castling right, and weakens Black's dark squares. Develop [b:Nf6] or [b:Nc6] instead. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gc4f7] }
4. Bxf7+ Kxf7 5. Qh5+ ) ( 3... c6 $6
{ Playing [c6] against the von der Lasa Gambit takes away the c6 square from Black's knight, so White can recapture [Qxd4] without ever losing a tempo to [b:Nc6]! After [Qxd4 d6 Nf3 Nd7 Nc3], White gets an ideal Center Game with the bishop already active on c4. Develop [Nf6] or [Nc6] instead. [%csl Rc6,Yc4][%cal Gd1d4] }
4. Qxd4 d6 5. Nf3 Nd7 6. Nc3 ) ) ( 3. f4 $2
{ The Halasz-McDonnell Gambit weakens the e1-h4 diagonal on move three for insufficient compensation: after [Bc5 Nf3 Nc6 Bd3 d6], Black holds the extra pawn solidly while White struggles to contest c5. Prefer [w:Qxd4], [w:c3], or [w:Nf3]. [%csl Re1,Rh4][%cal Gf8c5] }
3... Bc5 4. Nf3 Nc6 ) ( 3. Bd3
{ The Ross Gambit in the Center Game. White places the bishop on d3 to guard e4 and clear f1 for [w:Nf3] and [w:O-O], accepting that the bishop blocks the d-file and the c1 bishop for now. Black keeps the extra pawn comfortably by developing [Nc6] ([Nc6 Nf3 Bc5 O-O d6]), supporting d4 with [c5], or checking with [Bb4+]. [%csl Gd3,Ge4,Gf1][%cal Gb8c6] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... c5 ) ( 3... Bb4+ ) ) 3... Nc6
{ The Normal Variation of the Center Game: Black develops with tempo on the queen. Louis Paulsen's [Qe3] is the theoretical main line: from e3 the queen stays safe from minor pieces, discourages [b:d5], clears d1 and d2 for [w:Bd2] and [w:O-O-O], and is ready to slide to g3 against Black's kingside. [Qa4] (a Scandinavian in reverse with an extra tempo) and [Qd1] are solid quieter retreats, and [Qc4] is Hall's sideline. Never put the queen on c3. [%csl Ge3,Gd1,Gd2][%cal Gd4e3] }
( 3... d6 $6
{ Playing [b:d6] instead of [b:Nc6] forfeits Black's free tempo against the d4 queen and locks in the f8 bishop, handing White an effortless lead in development after [Nc3 Nf6 Bg5]. Hit the queen at once with [b:Nc6]. [%csl Rd4,Rf8][%cal Gb1c3] }
4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bg5 ) ( 3... c5 $2
{ Chasing the centralized queen with a pawn looks tempting, yet [c5] permanently creates a gaping hole on d5 and leaves the d7 pawn backward. White checks with [Qe5+ Qe7 Qxe7+ Bxe7 Nc3], steering into a queenless middlegame where [Nd5] dominates the board. Chase the queen with [Nc6] instead. [%csl Rc5,Rd5][%cal Gd4e5] }
4. Qe5+ Qe7 5. Qxe7+ Bxe7 6. Nc3 ) 4. Qe3
{ Paulsen's Attack. White's queen looks slightly awkward in front of the c1 bishop, yet White intends [w:Bd2], [w:Nc3], and [w:O-O-O], after which the queen glides to g3 to target g7 while White's kingside pawns storm forward. Black has three good setups: [Nf6] (the Berger Variation, preparing [b:Bb4] or [b:Be7]), [Bb4+] (provoking [Bb4+ c3] so c3 is taken from White's knight before retreating [b:Be7] or [b:Ba5]), and [g6] ([b:Bg7], armoring g7 against [w:Qg3]). [%csl Gc1,Gg3,Gg7][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 4. Qa4 ) ( 4. Qd1
{ Dropping the queen all the way back to d1 in the Center Game avoids exposing her on e3 or a4, essentially playing a Scandinavian with colours reversed and an extra tempo that White has already spent moving the queen twice. Black takes a clean, effortless lead in development: [Nf6] attacks e4 at once, [Bc5] eyes f2 before developing the second knight, and [Bb4+] forces a concession on the a5-e1 diagonal. [%cal Gg8f6,Gf8c5,Gf8b4] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... Bc5 ) ( 4... Bb4+ ) ) ( 4. Qc4
{ The Hall Variation of the Center Game, played in nearly nine hundred thousand online games. White puts the queen on the Italian diagonal toward f7, planning [w:Nc3], [w:Bf4] or [w:Bd2], and [w:O-O-O]. The drawback is that the queen occupies c4 and stays exposed to minor pieces: Black develops smoothly with [Nf6] (preparing [b:Bb4+] or [b:d5]), [d6], or [Bb4+]. [%csl Gf7,Gc4][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... d6 ) ( 4... Bb4+ ) ) ( 4. Qc3 $4
{ Stepping to c3 walks into an immediate royal skewer on move four: [Bb4] pins White's queen to the e1 king along the a5-e1 diagonal, winning the queen for a bishop on the spot. Tuck the queen onto e3 instead. [%csl Rc3,Re1][%cal Gf8b4] }
4... Bb4 5. Bd2 Bxc3 ) ( 4. Qd5 $2
