8. Bxg6 Nxd1 9. Bxf7+ Ke7 10. Bg5+ ) ) *

[Event "Other open games (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. d4
{ The Center Game, refined by Louis Paulsen in the 19th century and occasionally revived at the elite level by Shirov, Morozevich, and Nepomniachtchi. White challenges e5 on move two to open the d-file before either knight moves. Black's cleanest reply by far is [exd4], forcing White either to bring the queen out early ([exd4 Qxd4 Nc6]) or gambit pawns ([exd4 c3]). Other second moves like [b:Nc6] ([w:d5] or [w:dxe5]), passive [b:d6], and [b:d5] (the Beyer Gambit, met by [w:dxe5]) concede space or castling right away. [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge5d4] }
( 2. Qf3
{ The Napoleon Attack, popularized by legends of Napoleon Bonaparte's casual games. It is weaker than [w:Qh5]: from f3 the queen puts zero pressure on e5 and steals the g1 knight's natural square. Black develops freely with [Nf6] (preparing [b:Nc6], [b:Bc5], and [b:d5]) or [Nc6] (meeting [Nc6 Bc4] with [Nc6 Bc4 Nf6] or [Nc6 Bc4 Nd4], avoiding careless [b:Bc5]). [%csl Gf3,Ge5,Gg1][%cal Gg8f6] }
2... Nf6
{ Developing [Nf6] right away puts a natural blocker on the kingside, covers h5 and f7 tactics in advance, and highlights how awkwardly White's queen sits on f3, stealing the g1 knight's best square. White usually continues [Bc4], aiming down the a2-g8 diagonal while planning [w:Ne2] and [w:d3], or [Nc3] to guard e4. Black answers either with [b:Nc6] and [b:Bc5], or [b:c6] and [b:d5] to seize the centre. [%cal Gf1c4,Gb1c3] }
( 2... Nc6
{ Against Napoleon's 2.[Qf3], developing [Nc6] calmly defends e5 and eyes d4, exploiting how White's early queen sortie blocks the g1 knight's natural f3 square. White's most consistent follow-up is [Bc4] to renew the Scholar's Mate threat on f7, which Black neutralises effortlessly with [Bc4 Nf6], threatening [b:Nd4] next to fork queen and c2. Preparing [c3] first to keep the knight out of d4 is White's safer alternative. [%cal Gf1c4,Gc2c3] }
3. Bc4 ( 3. c3 ) ) ( 2... Bc5 ) ( 2... f6 $2
{ Blocking f7 with [b:f6] is completely unnecessary since nothing was threatened yet, and it weakens Black's kingside light squares and g8 knight: after [Bc4 Nc6 Ne2], Black cannot castle short comfortably. Simply develop [b:Nf6] or [b:Nc6]. [%csl Rf7,Rg8][%cal Gf1c4] }
3. Bc4 Nc6 4. Ne2 ) 3. Bc4
{ Bringing out [Bc4] completes Napoleon's Scholar-like battery against f7, yet with Black's knight already standing on f6 the queen's path to f7 is firmly blocked. Black has an enviable choice of active plans: [Nc6] threatens [Nd4] hitting both the queen on f3 and c2 (forcing [Nc6 Ne2] or [Nc6 c3]), [Bc5] develops smoothly toward f2, and [c6] prepares [d5] or [b5] to seize the initiative with gain of time. [%csl Yf7,Yf6,Yf3,Yc2][%cal Gb8c6] }
( 3. Nc3 ) 3... Nc6 ( 3... Bc5 ) ( 3... c6 ) ) ( 2. d3
{ The Leonardis Variation, played in over thirty million online games by cautious players who want to lock down e4 before bringing pieces out. Because pushing the d-pawn one square shuts in the f1 bishop and puts zero pressure on e5, Black equalizes on move two with [Nf6] (planning [b:d5] or [b:Bc5]), [d5] immediately ([d5 exd5 Qxd5 Nc3 Bb4]), or [Bc5]. [%csl Ge4,Gf1,Ge5][%cal Gg8f6] }
2... Nf6
{ Black develops naturally and prepares [b:d5] or [b:Bc5]. White usually steers into a quiet King's Indian Attack or Giuoco Pianissimo with [Nf3] ([Nf3 Nc6]), [Nc3], or [g3]; some players try a deferred King's Gambit with [f4] (the Clam Variation, well met by [f4 d5] or [f4 exf4]). Avoid weakening [f3], which opens the king's diagonals and blocks g1. [%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3,Yd7d5] }
( 2... d5
{ When Black meets White's cautious [w:d3] directly in the center, White should trade [exd5] ([exd5 Qxd5 Nc3 Bb4 Bd2], reaching a reversed Scandinavian with an extra tempo for Black's active pieces) or hold the e4 point solidly with [Nf3] and [Nd2] in King's Indian Attack style. Never weaken the kingside diagonals with [f3] while the king sits in the middle. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge4d5,Gg1f3] }
3. exd5
{ White resolves the central tension right away rather than letting Black push past or capture on e4. With d3 already on the board, the position turns into a reversed Scandinavian where Black enjoys easy, unhindered development. [Qxd5] is the direct recapture ([Qxd5 Nc3 Bb4 Bd2 Bxc3 Bxc3 Nc6]), bringing the queen out safely because c3 can always be pinned. [Nf6] is a clean modern alternative that invites [Nf6 Nf3 Nxd5], while [c6] is the sharp Weber Gambit, offering a pawn for open diagonals. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd8d5,Gg8f6] }
( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. Nd2 ) ( 3. f3 $2
{ Bolstering e4 with [w:f3] blocks g1, weakens the a7-g1 and e1-h4 diagonals, and lets [Nf6 Ne2 Bc5] keep White's king stuck in the center. Play [w:exd5] or [w:Nf3] instead. [%cal Gg8f6,Rf8c5] }
3... Nf6 4. Ne2 Bc5 ) ( 3. Qf3 $6
{ Bringing the queen to f3 steals f3 from White's knight and walks straight into future tempo hits along the long diagonal. After [Nf6 Bg5 Be6 exd5 Bxd5], Black's light-squared bishop seizes d5 while hitting White's misplaced queen with gain of time. Capture [exd5] or develop [Nf3+] instead. [%csl Rf3,Yd5][%cal Gg8f6] }
3... Nf6 4. Bg5 Be6 5. exd5 Bxd5 ) 3... Qxd5 ( 3... Nf6 ) ( 3... c6 4. dxc6
{ The Weber Gambit: Black offered a pawn on c6 hoping for Danish-style diagonals in reverse, and White has accepted. Black's only sound continuation is to recapture on c6 with [Nxc6] ([Nxc6 Nf3 Bd6 Be2 Nge7 O-O], playing a clean one-pawn gambit for rapid piece development). Offering a second pawn on b7 fails because White's pawn on d3 already blunts the c5 diagonal. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gb8c6] }
4... Nxc6 ( 4... Bc5 $2
{ Played in nearly forty percent of club games to copy the reversed Danish, [b:Bc5] sacrifices a second pawn when White has already played d3 to blunt the c5 diagonal: [cxb7 Bxb7 Nf3] leaves White two solid pawns ahead. Recapture with [b:Nxc6]. [%csl Rb7,Rc5][%cal Gc6b7] }
5. cxb7 Bxb7 6. Nf3 ) ( 4... Nf6 $2
{ Leaving c6 alive lets [cxb7 Bxb7 Nf3] pocket a second pawn with a rock-solid defensive shell around d3. Take back on c6 with [b:Nxc6]. [%csl Rb7][%cal Gc6b7] }
5. cxb7 Bxb7 6. Nf3 ) ( 4... Bd6 $2
{ Leaving c6 alive to develop [b:Bd6] lets [cxb7 Bxb7 Nf3] win a second pawn while White's d3 structure keeps the kingside secure. Recapture first with [b:Nxc6]. [%csl Rb7,Rd6][%cal Gc6b7] }
5. cxb7 Bxb7 6. Nf3 ) ) ) ( 2... Nc6
{ After White's quiet 2.[d3], developing [Nc6] controls d4 and keeps Black's setup completely flexible while waiting to see where White's pieces go. White usually plays [Nf3], transposing toward a Giuoco Pianissimo or Four Knights once the bishops emerge, or [Nc3] to steer toward a Closed Vienna formation where White builds up on the kingside; fianchettoing with [g3] before [Nf3] is less accurate because Black can strike immediately in the middle with [g3 d5]. [%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3] }
3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3 ) ) ( 2... Bc5
{ Developing [Bc5] against the Leonardis Variation seizes the open a7-g1 diagonal immediately: with White's d-pawn already sitting on d3, pushing [c3] followed by [w:d4] to hit c5 would cost an extra move. White usually develops [Nf3] (reaching a Philidor Reversed or Giuoco Pianissimo after [Nf3 d6] or [Nf3 Nc6]), brings out [Nc3], or plays [Be2] to prepare safe kingside castling. [%csl Ya7,Yg1,Yd3,Yc5][%cal Gg1f3] }
3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. Be2 ) ) ( 2... f5
{ The King's Gambit Reversed against White's passive Leonardis setup: Black takes advantage of White's locked-in f1 bishop to challenge e4 from the flank on move two. White's cleanest replies are [exf5] ([exf5 Nf6 Nf3 Nc6]) and [Nc3] (keeping e4 bolstered while developing a piece; [w:Nf3] allows [b:fxe4] [w:Nxe5] [b:Qf6]). Never touch [f3], which weakens the king's diagonals. [%csl Gf1,Ge4][%cal Ge4f5] }
3. exf5 ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. f3 $6
{ Defending e4 with [w:f3] wrecks White's kingside diagonals, steals f3 from the knight, and lets [Bc5 Nc3 Nc6] clamp down on White's king before it can castle. Play [w:Nf3] or [w:exf5] instead. [%csl Re4,Rf3][%cal Gf8c5] }
3... Bc5 4. Nc3 Nc6 ) ) 3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. g3 ) ( 3. f4
{ The Clam Variation (a deferred King's Gambit with d3 played first to shield e4). Because d3 is already committed and shuts in the f1 bishop, Black has two clean answers: [d5] strikes the center immediately ([d5 fxe5 Nxe4 dxe4 Qh4+]), and [exf4] ([exf4 Bxf4 d5]) opens lines with easy equality. Developing [Bc5] carelessly leaves the e5 pawn hanging. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gd7d5,Ge5f4] }
3... d5 ( 3... exf4 ) ( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... Bc5 $2
