5. Be3 ) ) ( 2... f6 $2
{ Guarding e5 with [b:f6] opens the e8-h5 diagonal ([dxe5 fxe5] walks straight into [w:Qh5+] winning material) and steals the g8 knight's best square. Simply play [b:exd4]. [%csl Re5,Re8][%cal Gd4e5] }
3. dxe5 Nc6 4. Bc4 ) ( 2... d5
{ The Beyer Gambit: Black copies White's pawn thrust on move two, yet White captures first. [dxe5] is the refutation: after [dxe5 dxe4 Qxd8+ Kxd8 Nc3], White trades queens with check, strips Black's castling right, and attacks the isolated e4 pawn with [w:Bg5+] and [w:O-O-O]. Note that taking the wrong pawn with [w:exd5] [b:Qxd5] lets Black centralize the queen with no [w:Nc3] tempo. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd4e5] }
3. dxe5
{ Winning e5 with [dxe5] exposes why the Beyer Gambit is unsound: unlike the Elephant Gambit, White has not yet placed a knight on f3 for Black's pawn to kick with tempo. Recapturing [dxe4] walks into [dxe4 Qxd8+ Kxd8 Nc3], where Black loses castling rights and struggles to hold e4 against [Bg5] and queenside castling. Gambiting the pawn via [Ne7] or [c6] avoids the queen trade, though White stays a healthy pawn up. [%csl Ye5,Yf3,Ye4][%cal Gd5e4] }
( 3. c3 $2
{ Treating Black's d-pawn thrust like a Danish Gambit drops e4 for nothing after [dxe4 Bb5+ Bd7], since Black's e-pawn is still alive on e5 to anchor the center. Capture [w:dxe5] instead. [%csl Re4,Re5][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4 4. Bb5+ Bd7 ) 3... dxe4 ( 3... Ne7 ) ( 3... c6 ) ) 3. Nf3
{ The Kieseritzky Variation: White delays recapturing on d4 so Black cannot gain a free tempo with [Nc6] against White's queen. [Nc6] is still Black's cleanest reply, transposing directly into the Scotch Game (covered in the Scotch chapter), while [Nf6] reaches the Petrov. Players trying to keep the extra pawn often play [c5] or [Bc5], both of which invite dangerous gambits if Black is careless. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gb8c6] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... Nf6 ) ( 3... Bb4+ ) ( 3... Bc5
{ Black guards d4 with the dark-squared bishop, preparing to transpose into the Scotch Game if White plays [Nxd4 Nc6]. Instead of obliging, White can strike at the d4 pawn chain immediately with [c3] (the Lanc-Arnold Gambit: after [c3 dxc3 Bc4] or [c3 dxc3 Nxc3], Black's early bishop on c5 becomes a tactical target) or play [Bc4] ([Bc4 Nc6] reaches the Scotch Gambit). [%csl Gd4,Gc5][%cal Gf3d4] }
4. Nxd4
{ Recapturing [Nxd4] recovers the pawn while avoiding the main Scotch move order when Black omitted [Nc6] earlier (whereas [Nc6] here transposes directly into the Classical Scotch). Black's sharpest independent move is [Nf6], attacking e4 at once ([Nf6 Nc3 d6] or [Nf6 Nb3 Nxe4 Nxc5 Nxc5]); [d6] builds a solid Philidor-like barrier before [Nf6], and [Qf6] puts immediate pressure on d4 in Blumenfeld fashion. [%csl Ye4,Yd4][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. c3
{ The Lanc-Arnold Gambit: White challenges Black's d4 chain immediately while the c5 bishop stands exposed to [w:cxd4] with tempo. Black's cleanest reply is to accept with [dxc3] ([dxc3 Nxc3 d6 Bc4 Nf6] or [dxc3 Bc4 d6], watching out for f7 tactics). Leaving d4 in place with [Nc6] or pushing [b:d3] [w:Bxd3] hands White a strong center with tempo. [%csl Gd4,Gc5,Gf7][%cal Gd4c3] }
4... dxc3 ( 4... Nc6 $2
{ Defending d4 with [b:Nc6] walks straight into [cxd4], winning a tempo on the c5 bishop and handing White an ideal two-pawn center ([cxd4 Bb4+ Nc3 d5 exd5 Qxd5 Be2]). Capture [b:dxc3] instead. [%csl Rd4,Rc5][%cal Gc3d4] }
