5... d6 ( 5... cxb2 ) ( 5... d5 ) ( 5... Qf6 $2
{ Bringing the queen to f6 blocks g8 and walks into [Nxc3] with [w:Nd5] or [w:Bg5] tempo hits on the exposed queen while Black's kingside lags behind. Guard c5 solidly with [b:d6] instead. [%csl Rf6,Rg8][%cal Gb1c3] }
6. Nxc3 Ne7 7. O-O ) ( 5... h6 $2
{ Stopping [w:Ng5] with [b:h6] misses the real threat: [Bxf7+] drags the king out anyway ([Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qd5+ Kf8 Qxc5+] wins back the bishop and leaves Black's king stranded). Protect c5 with [b:d6]. [%csl Rc5][%cal Gc4f7] }
6. Bxf7+ Kf8 7. Nxc3 ) ( 5... Bxf2+ $4
{ Sacrificing on f2 hands White the two bishops and open files for zero attacking follow-up: after [Kxf2 cxb2 Bxb2], White's rook on f1 and twin bishops rake Black's uncastled camp. Play [b:d6] instead. [%csl Rf2,Rf1][%cal Ge1f2] }
6. Kxf2 cxb2 7. Bxb2 ) ( 5... Nc6 $2
{ Developing [b:Nc6] forgets that the c5 bishop is unguarded on the fifth rank: [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qd5+ Kf8 Qxc5+] recovers the bishop with check and exposes Black's king. Guard c5 with [b:d6] first. [%csl Rc5][%cal Gc4f7] }
6. Bxf7+ Kxf7 7. Qd5+ ) ) ( 4. Ng5 $2
{ Leaping to g5 without a bishop on c4 creates zero threat on f7 and wastes two moves with the same knight while still a pawn down. Black calmly develops [Nf6], threatening [h6] and keeping the extra d4 pawn after [Nf6 Bd3 d6 O-O O-O]. Recapture [Nxd4] or play [c3] instead. [%csl Rg5,Yf7][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 5. Bd3 d6 6. O-O O-O ) 4... Nf6 ( 4... d6 ) ( 4... Qf6 ) ) ( 3... c5
{ Trying to cling to the extra d4 pawn with the c-pawn creates a permanent hole on d5, weakens d6, and leaves Black behind in kingside development. White develops actively with [Bc4], targeting f7 and d5 before Black's minor pieces ever leave the back rank, or challenges the pawn chain at once with [c3] for open c- and d-file play. [%csl Gd4,Gd5,Gd6][%cal Gf1c4] }
4. Bc4
{ White eyes f7 and prepares [w:c3] or [w:O-O] followed by [w:Ng5] or [w:Ne5]. Black has to defend carefully: [Nc6] (followed by [Nc6 c3 Nf6]) and [d6] ([d6 c3 Nc6]) are the only ways to keep the kingside together while bringing pieces out. Passive or weakening moves like [Be7], [h6], or [f6] leave f7 and the central light squares exposed. [%csl Gf7][%cal Gb8c6] }
( 4. c3 ) ( 4. Ng5 $2
{ Lunging to g5 with a lone knight achieves nothing without a bishop on c4: [h6] kicks the knight right back to f3, gifting Black a free tempo to consolidate the extra pawn. Develop [w:Bc4] or challenge d4 with [w:c3]. [%csl Rg5,Rc4][%cal Gh7h6] }
4... h6 5. Nf3 Nc6 ) 4... Nc6 ( 4... d6 ) ( 4... Be7 $4
{ Developing [b:Be7] blocks the queen from g5 and h4 while leaving f7 unguarded against [Ne5]: Black's knight is forced to the rim ([Ne5 Nh6 Bxh6 gxh6 Qh5]) or drops f7 outright. Guard d5 and e5 with [b:Nc6] or [b:d6]. [%csl Rg5,Rh4][%cal Gf3e5] }
5. Ne5 Nh6 6. Bxh6 ) ( 4... h6 $2
{ Stopping [w:Ng5] with [b:h6] forgets that the knight can jump to the other square attacking f7: [Ne5] hits f7 with zero black pieces out to challenge it, forcing awkward [Ne5 Qe7 f4] self-blocks. Develop [b:Nc6] instead. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf3e5] }
5. Ne5 Qe7 6. f4 ) ( 4... f6 $2
{ Blunting f7 with [b:f6] leaves Black's king stranded on the weakened a2-g8 diagonal with the g8 knight deprived of its best square: [c3] opens the center with strong effect after [c3 Nc6 O-O]. Play [b:Nc6] instead. [%csl Rf7,Ra2][%cal Gc2c3] }
5. c3 Nc6 6. O-O ) ) *

[Event "Petrov's Defence (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6
{ Petrov's Defence, also known as the Russian Game after nineteenth-century masters Alexander Petrov and Carl Jaenisch, who rehabilitated an opening Pedro Damiano had analysed around 1500. Instead of guarding e5 with [Nc6], Black counterattacks e4 right away and invites an early pawn exchange that sidesteps the Ruy Lopez and Italian Game. [Nxe5] is the critical test, [d4] is Steinitz's Modern Attack, [Nc3] steers toward the Three or Four Knights, and [d3] builds a reversed Hanham Philidor. [%csl Ye5,Ye4][%cal Gf3e5] }
3. Nxe5
{ White accepts the challenge and captures on e5. Now Black must chase the knight from the e-file with [d6] before recapturing on e4: once the white knight retreats to f3, a later queen pin with [Qe2] can be met safely by [Qe7]. Taking on e4 immediately walks into dangerous pins along the open e-file, while [Nc6], the Stafford Gambit, sacrifices a full pawn for rapid piece activity and blitz traps against f2. [%csl Ye5,Ye4,Yf3][%cal Gd7d6] }
( 3. d4
{ The Modern or Steinitz Attack, first analysed by Alexander Petrov himself, championed by Wilhelm Steinitz in his 1889 treatise and revived by Garry Kasparov and Vasyl Ivanchuk in the 1990s. By striking with [d4] on move three, White dodges the Stafford Gambit altogether and asks Black which central pawn to take: [Nxe4] is the elite main line, while [exd4] invites [w:e5] chasing the knight. [%cal Gf6e4] }
3... Nxe4 ( 3... exd4
{ Club players' favourite capture against [d4], though rarer at top level than [Nxe4] because half-opening the e-file exposes Black's king while the knight on f6 gets kicked around. White's principled response is [e5], chasing that knight immediately before recapturing on d4 so Black never gets a quiet developing tempo. Once [e5] forces the knight to commit, White recovers the d4 pawn with clear central space and open diagonals. [%csl Yf6,Yd4][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5
{ Pushing [w:e5] unseats Black's knight on f6 before White takes back on d4. Only one square keeps Black on solid footing: [Ne4], jumping forward into the centre so [Ne4 Qxd4] can be met by [d5] with tempo. Every passive retreat or flank jump loses precious time while White scoops up d4. Centralizing on e4 lets Black fight back immediately with [d5] rather than getting pushed onto the back rank. [%csl Yf6,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gf6e4] }
( 4. Nxd4 $6
{ Recapturing [Nxd4] forgets to guard e4. Black snatches a central pawn with [Nxe4], and after [Nxe4 Qe2 Qe7] Black breaks the pin cleanly and keeps the extra pawn unless White repeats with [Nf5]. Push [w:e5] first. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf6e4] }
4... Nxe4 5. Qe2 Qe7 ) ( 4. Bc4 $6
{ Offering e4 enters the Urusov Gambit: while tricky in blitz after [Nxe4 Qxd4 Nf6], Panov's [d5] equalises comfortably and accurate defence leaves Black fine. Push [w:e5] first to chase the knight and keep a risk-free pull. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf6e4] }
4... Nxe4 5. Qxd4 Nf6 ) ( 4. c3 $2
{ Offering a Danish-style pawn with [c3] forgets that Black's knight is already on f6 attacking e4! Instead of taking on c3 to open White's lines, Black snatches [Nxe4], emerging a solid central pawn ahead after [Nxe4 Bd3 Nc5 O-O Nxd3]. Push [e5] to kick the f6 knight first. [%csl Rc3,Re4][%cal Gf6e4] }
4... Nxe4 5. Bd3 Nc5 6. O-O Nxd3 ) 4... Ne4
{ From e4, Black's knight stays active and prepares to meet White's recapture with [d5]. [Qxd4] is the main Center Attack, hitting the knight at once so [Qxd4 d5 exd6 Nxd6] clears the centre cleanly; [Qe2] is Steinitz's own 1889 preference, pinning the knight to stop [d5]; and [Bb5] is Tal's check luring [c6]. In all three continuations, the open e- and d-files reward whichever side connects the rooks fastest. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd1d4] }
( 4... Nd5 $2
{ Stepping to d5 puts the knight in the path of [Qxd4], forcing the knight to move a third time to b6 while White develops [Nc3] and [Bf4] with complete central control. Jump forward to [Ne4] instead. [%csl Rd5,Rb6][%cal Gd1d4] }
5. Qxd4 Nb6 6. Nc3 ) ( 4... Ng4 $2
{ Attacking e5 from g4 walks into [h3 Nh6 Qxd4], stranding Black's knight on the rim at h6 while White recovers the pawn with a huge lead in space and development. Centralize with [Ne4]. [%csl Re5,Rg4][%cal Gh2h3] }
5. h3 Nh6 6. Qxd4 ) 5. Qxd4
{ The Center Attack: White recovers the d4 pawn with tempo on Black's loose knight on e4. Black answers with [d5], defending the knight while opening the c8 bishop's diagonal and inviting the en passant exchange [d5 exd6 Nxd6], after which Black develops [Nc6], [Be7] and [Bf5] or [b:Be6] smoothly. Once the en passant capture clears both central files, White often castles queenside to seize the d-file with tempo. [%csl Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 5. Qe2
{ Steinitz's own pet line from 1889: White pins the knight on e4 along the e-file so Black cannot push [d5] (because en passant [w:exd6] would leave the knight pinned to Black's king). Instead, Black sidesteps with [Nc5] ([Nc5 Nxd4 Nc6], Bardeleben's Variation) or disrupts White first with [Bb4+] for clean equality. Although computers hold the balance for Black, club opponents frequently stumble into Steinitz's e-file pin. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4c5,Gf8b4] }
5... Nc5 ( 5... Bb4+ ) ( 5... d5 $2
{ Steinitz's trap. Defending the knight with [d5] walks right into en passant [exd6]: Black cannot recapture on d6 because the knight on e4 is pinned to the king on e8, leaving Black tangled and losing material after [exd6 f5 Nbd2]. Play [Nc5] or [Bb4+]. [%csl Rd6,Ye8][%cal Ge5d6] }
6. exd6 f5 7. Nbd2 ) ) ( 5. Bb5 ) 5... d5
{ By pushing [d5], Black defends the centralized knight and challenges White's cramping e5 wedge. White almost always captures en passant with [exd6]: after [exd6 Nxd6 Nc3 Nc6 Qf4], all four central pawns have vanished and White's slight lead in piece activity and long-castling potential meet Black's solid [Bf5] or [Be7] defence. With no pawns left on the d- or e-files, active piece placement and bishop diagonals matter far more than static structure. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge5d6,Gb1c3] }
( 5... f5 $6
{ Trying to cement the knight with [f5] forgets en passant. [exf6 Nxf6 Bd3] removes the pawn, strips Black's kingside shelter and leaves Black behind in development on an open e-file. Push [d5] instead. [%cal Ge5f6] }
6. exf6 Nxf6 7. Bd3 ) 6. exd6 ) ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... d5
{ The Symmetrical Variation of the Modern Attack. Because Black pushed [d5] without waiting for White to capture on e5, White keeps the move in hand: [Nxe5] is best, reaching the main line after [Nxe5 Nxe4 Bd3] with an extra tempo while punishing [Nxe5 dxe4] via [Bc4]; [exd5] ([exd5 exd4 Qxd4 Qxd5]) is a quiet equal alternative. Symmetrical play almost always favours the side with the move when the e-file is wide open. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5 ( 4. exd5 ) ) ) ( 3. Nc3
{ The Three Knights Variation: White defends e4 calmly on move three, dodging both Petrov main lines and the Stafford Gambit in one stroke. [Nc6] is Black's most common reply and transposes into the Four Knights Game; Petrov players who want an unbalanced fight choose [Bb4], pressuring c3 and e4 in reversed-Spanish style, while [d6] steers into a Lion or Philidor setup. [%csl Ye4,Yc3][%cal Gb8c6] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... Bb4
{ By threatening to trade off the knight on c3 that guards e4, [Bb4] renews Black's pressure on the centre without entering Four Knights symmetry. White's principled reply is [Nxe5], grabbing the central pawn right now because [Bxc3] [w:dxc3] [Nxe4] runs into [Qg4] hitting both e4 and g7. [Bc4] is a quiet Italian-style alternative. The resulting imbalance—White's bishop pair against Black's cleaner queenside pawn structure—gives both sides real winning chances. [%csl Yc3,Ye4,Yg7][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5
{ White captures e5, counting on [Qg4] tactics against g7 if Black tries to win back e4 prematurely. Black must castle first with [O-O], tucking the king away and defending g7 so that [Re8] and [Bxc3] followed by [Nxe4] become genuine threats on the very next turn. Patience on move four pays off: once castled, Black wins the pawn back cleanly with active rook play. [%csl Ye5,Yg7,Ye4][%cal Ge8g8] }
( 4. Bc4 ) 4... O-O
{ Now that Black has castled and protected g7, Black threatens both [Re8] along the e-file and [Bxc3] followed by [Nxe4]. Clinging greedily to the extra pawn lands White's uncastled king in serious trouble, so White develops [Be2] calmly, preparing to castle short and return the pawn on White's own terms for the bishop pair. Giving back the extra pawn on schedule transforms White's temporary material lead into safe, lasting bishop-pair pressure. [%csl Yg1,Ye5][%cal Gf1e2] }
( 4... Nxe4 $2
{ Grabbing e4 before castling leaves f7 wide open to [Qh5]. Black's knight has to scramble back with [Qh5 Nd6 Nd5], where both the b4 bishop and c7 come under crushing fire. Castle [O-O] first so f7 is protected. [%csl Re4,Rf7][%cal Gd1h5] }
5. Qh5 Nd6 6. Nd5 ) 5. Be2
{ Developing [Be2] shields the e-file and prepares [w:O-O]. Black regains the pawn cleanly with [Re8], when [Re8 Nd3 Bxc3 dxc3 Nxe4 O-O] preserves White's two bishops and keeps the f-pawn unblocked against Black's healthier queenside structure; immediate [Bxc3] followed by [Nxe4] or [Qe7] also equalises. White's bishop pair shines if the game opens up, while Black aims for knight outposts and clean endgames. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gf8e8,Gb4c3] }
( 5. d3 $6
{ Trying to hold onto the extra pawn with [d3] walks straight into [d5], opening the e-file while White's king is still in the middle (and [w:exd5] is a blunder due to [b:Nxd5] threatening c3 twice). Develop [Be2] and castle instead. [%csl Rd3,Ye5][%cal Gd7d5] }
5... d5 6. Bd2 Re8 ) 5... Re8 ( 5... Bxc3 ) ( 5... Qe7 ) ) ( 3... d6
{ Instead of entering the Four Knights with [b:Nc6] or pinning via [b:Bb4], Black holds e5 solidly with [d6], transposing into a Nimzowitsch or Lion Philidor setup where White has already committed [Nc3]. White's strongest plan is [d4], challenging the centre at once so [d4 Nbd7] reaches the main Lion tabiya and [d4 exd4 Nxd4] gives White free Exchange-Philidor space; [Bc4] eyes f7 in quieter Italian style. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gd2d4,Gf1c4] }
4. d4 ( 4. Bc4 ) ) ( 3... Bc5 $2
{ Developing [Bc5] leaves the e5 pawn undefended while White's e4 pawn is already guarded by c3. [Nxe5] wins a clean pawn (since [Bxf2+] [w:Kxf2] [Nxe4] [Nxe4] fails completely with c3 guarding e4). Play [Nc6] or [Bb4]. [%csl Re5,Re4][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5 Nc6 5. Nxc6 dxc6 ) ( 3... Ng4 $2
{ The dubious Vladimir Variation wastes two moves with the same knight: [h3] chases it right back to f6, and [h3 Nf6 Nxe5] wins Black's central pawn with a big lead in development. Play [Nc6] or [Bb4]. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gh2h3] }
4. h3 Nf6 5. Nxe5 ) ) ( 3. d3
{ A deceptive, quiet weapon: by defending e4 with [d3], White threatens [Nxe5] for real and steers into a reversed Hanham Philidor or King's Indian Attack ([g3] and [Bg2]) with an extra move in hand. Black defends e5 via [Nc6] (aiming for [d5] and [Bc5]) or strikes immediately in the centre with [d5]. Because White avoids early pawn trades, the game turns into a rich, manoeuvring battle where understanding beats memorization. [%csl Ye4,Ye5][%cal Gb8c6] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... d5 ) ( 3... Bc5 $2
{ Developing [Bc5] forgets that [d3] just protected e4, so e5 is now hanging for real. [Nxe5] pockets a clean central pawn with zero compensation for Black after [Nxe5 d6 Nf3]. Defend e5 with [Nc6] or counterstrike with [d5]. [%csl Re4,Re5][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5 d6 5. Nf3 ) ) ( 3. Bc4
{ The Italian Variation of Petrov's Defence, also frequently reached via the Bishop's Opening. White aims directly at f7 and leaves the central e4 pawn hanging on purpose. Black can either accept the gauntlet with [Nxe4]—inviting the razor-sharp Boden-Kieseritzky Gambit [Nc3]—or play [Nc6] to transpose safely into the Two Knights Defence. Unprepared opponents often grab e4 and then stumble straight into White's fierce f7-and-e-file traps. [%csl Yf7,Ye4][%cal Gf6e4] }
3... Nxe4
{ Black snatches the undefended e4 pawn, temporarily going a pawn ahead. Now White's signature continuation is [Nc3], the Boden-Kieseritzky Gambit—effectively a reversed Stafford Gambit where White already has [Bc4] eyeing f7. [d3] is a quieter alternative that chases the knight from e4 and aims to recover e5 safely. Just remember not to snatch e5 prematurely on move four, which hands Black [d5] with tempo on your bishop. [%csl Ye4,Yf7,Ye5][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... Bc5 $2
{ Copying White's bishop move leaves e5 hanging while f7 is attacked. After [Nxe5 O-O d4], White pockets a clean central pawn with tempo on the bishop (and [Bxf2+] [w:Kxf2] [Nxe4] [Kg1] leaves Black a piece down). Play [Nxe4] or [Nc6]. [%csl Re5,Rf7][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5 O-O 5. d4 ) ( 3... h6 $2
{ Playing [h6] leaves e5 completely undefended. [Nxe5] snatches the central pawn and attacks f7 twice, forcing Black into a desperate pawn deficit after [Nxe5 d5 exd5]. Take on e4 with [Nxe4] or defend with [Nc6]. [%csl Re5,Rf7][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5 d5 5. exd5 ) ( 3... c6 $2
{ Preparing [d5] with [c6] works in the Bishop's Opening before [Nf3], yet here White's knight is already on f3 and snaps off [Nxe5] with a double threat on f7. Play [Nxe4] or [Nc6] instead. [%csl Rf3,Rf7][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5 d5 5. exd5 ) 4. Nc3
{ The Boden-Kieseritzky Gambit, played by Samuel Boden and Lionel Kieseritzky in the 1850s. Because White already has [Bc4] eyeing f7, accepting with [Nxc3 dxc3] gives White a far more venomous attack than Black ever gets in the Stafford. Bobby Fischer's clean antidote is [Nc6], inviting [Nxe4 d5] (the centre fork trick) for dead-even play, while [Nd6] ([Bb3 Nc6]) is solid too. [%csl Yf7][%cal Ge4c3] }
( 4. Nxe5 ) ( 4. d3
{ Rather than gambit a pawn with [w:Nc3], White attacks the centralized knight calmly with [d3] to win back e5 without structural risk. Black should retreat to [Nd6] (hitting the c4 bishop so [Nd6 Bb3 Nc6] keeps the extra pawn under pressure) or [Nf6] ([Nf6 Nxe5 d5 Bb3 Bd6]), whereas careless piece moves along the e-file walk into [w:Nxe5] and f7 tactics. [%csl Ye5][%cal Ge4d6,Ge4f6] }
4... Nd6 ( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... Ng5 $4
{ Jumping to g5 loses a piece or collapses on f7: after [Nxg5 d5 Qf3], White threatens mate on f7 while saving the c4 bishop, leaving Black down a clean piece with a wrecked kingside. Retreat to [b:Nd6] or [b:Nf6]. [%csl Rg5,Rf7][%cal Gf3g5] }
5. Nxg5 d5 6. Qf3 ) ) ( 4. Bxf7+ $4
{ The unsound Trébuchet Gambit throws away a full bishop for two pawns: after [Kxf7 Nxe5+ Kg8], Black tucks the king away behind [b:d5] and [Nf6] and keeps the extra piece. Gambit a single pawn with [Nc3+], not a bishop. [%cal Ge8f7] }
4... Kxf7 5. Nxe5+ Kg8 ) 4... Nxc3
{ Black accepts the Boden-Kieseritzky challenge and trades knights on c3. Just like the Nimzowitsch Petrov and the Stafford Gambit, White must recapture away from the corner with [dxc3], opening both the d-file for [Qd5] and the c1-h6 diagonal for the dark-squared bishop in a single stroke so every white piece joins the attack. Just as in the Nimzowitsch Attack, fast piece mobilization and open central files matter far more than doubled c-pawns. [%csl Yc3,Yc1,Yh6][%cal Gd2c3] }
( 4... Nc6 ) ( 4... d6 $2
{ Supporting e5 passively with [d6] drops e4 for free or worse: if White plays [Nxe5] and Black takes [dxe5], [Bxf7+] wins Black's queen on d8, while [Nxe4 d5 Bxd5 Qxd5 Qe2] regains the pawn with a crushing pin and lead in development. Play [Nxc3] or [Nc6]. [%csl Re5,Re4][%cal Gc3e4] }
5. Nxe4 d5 6. Bxd5 Qxd5 7. Qe2 ) ( 4... Bc5 $4
{ Developing [Bc5] hangs the knight on e4 and puts the bishop where [Nxe4] hits c5 too. After [Nxe4], Black loses a full minor piece because c5 falls with tempo. Play [Nxc3] or [Nc6]. [%csl Re4,Rc5][%cal Gc3e4] }
5. Nxe4 d5 6. Nxc5 ) 5. dxc3
{ Every white piece is coiled to pounce on e5, f7 and g5. Black's only reliable defensive move here is [f6]: although ugly at first sight because it weakens light squares, it solidly guards e5 and keeps White's knight out of g5 at the same time. Almost every natural developing move loses on the spot. Once you remember [f6], Black survives the initial storm and forces White to prove long-term compensation for the pawn. [%csl Ye5,Yf7,Yg5][%cal Gf7f6] }
( 5. bxc3 $2
{ Recapturing [bxc3] leaves the d2 pawn blocking both White's queen and dark-squared bishop, letting Black seize the centre with [d5 Bb3 e4] a clean pawn up. Always recapture [w:dxc3] to open both diagonals. [%cal Gd7d5] }
5... d5 6. Bb3 e4 ) 5... f6
{ With [f6], Black erects a sturdy barrier over e5 and g5 while preparing [Nc6], [Qe7], [d6] and long castling behind four healthy queenside pawns. White's sharpest try is [Nh4], threatening [Qh5+] to provoke [g6] and following with [f4] (met best by [Qe7] or [c6]), while [O-O] and [Be3] develop smoothly. Black's goal is to weather the kingside wave, trade off an attacker and reach safety on the queenside. [%csl Ye5,Yg5][%cal Gf3h4] }
( 5... d6 $4
{ Defending e5 with [d6] leaves g5 and f7 wide open: [Ng5] attacks f7 twice with no clean defence (and even [w:Nxe5] wins Black's queen if Black takes [b:dxe5] because of [w:Bxf7+]). After [Ng5 Be6 Bxe6 fxe6 Qf3], b7 and f8 collapse. Play [f6] instead. [%csl Re5,Rg5][%cal Gf3g5] }
6. Ng5 Be6 7. Bxe6 fxe6 8. Qf3 ) ( 5... Bc5 $2
{ Developing [Bc5] ignores both e5 and g5: [Ng5] hits f7 twice while eyeing h7 (and [w:Qd5] forks mate on f7 and c5 as well). After [Ng5 Rf8 O-O], Black's kingside is paralysed. Guard e5 and g5 with [f6]. [%csl Re5,Rg5][%cal Gf3g5] }
6. Ng5 Rf8 7. O-O ) ( 5... Nc6 $4
{ Defending e5 with [Nc6] forgets to cover g5: [Ng5] attacks f7 twice with no good defence, and after [Ng5 Qf6 Nxf7] White wins the h8 rook or wrecks Black's kingside. Only [f6] stops both [Nxe5] and [Ng5]. [%csl Re5,Rg5][%cal Gf3g5] }
6. Ng5 Qf6 7. Nxf7 ) ( 5... Be7 $6
{ Covering g5 with [Be7] leaves the e5 pawn completely undefended. [Nxe5] wins it back with tempo on f7, and after [Nxe5 O-O Qh5] White launches a fierce assault against Black's castled king. Play [f6] instead. [%csl Rg5,Re5][%cal Gf3e5] }
6. Nxe5 O-O 7. Qh5 ) ( 5... d5 $2
{ Pushing [d5] overlooks that [w:dxc3] just opened White's queen on d1 against d5. [Bxd5] wins a central pawn with threats on e5 and f7 (if [b:c6], [w:Bxf7+] [b:Kxf7] [w:Qxd8] wins Black's queen, and [Bxd5 Be7 Nxe5 O-O] drops a second pawn). Play [f6] instead. [%csl Rd1,Rd5][%cal Gc4d5] }
6. Bxd5 Be7 7. Nxe5 O-O ) 6. Nh4 ( 6. O-O
{ White castles short, clears the e-file for [w:Re1] and dares Black to untangle while the c4 bishop stops Black from castling kingside. Black must play accurately with [d6], overprotecting e5 and preparing [b:Qe7] plus [b:Be6] to neutralise the diagonal before castling long, or develop [Nc6] carefully. Trying to block the c4 bishop immediately with [c6] walks straight into a knight sacrifice on e5. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gd7d6] }
6... d6 ( 6... Nc6 ) ( 6... c6 $4
{ Preparing [b:d5] with [c6] is the most common club blunder here: [Nxe5] sacrifices the knight to rip open the e8-h5 diagonal, and after [Nxe5 fxe5 Qh5+ g6 Qxe5+] White forks king and h8 rook with a winning attack. Overprotect e5 with [b:d6] first. [%csl Re8,Rh5][%cal Gf3e5] }
7. Nxe5 fxe5 8. Qh5+ g6 9. Qxe5+ ) ) ( 6. Be3 ) ) ( 3. Qe2
{ Putting the queen on e2 protects e4 and threatens [Nxe5] while keeping the d-pawn ready for a later [d4] (the Moody Gambit if played against [Nc6]) or [g3] and [Bg2] in Chigorin King's-Indian-Attack style. Because White's f1 bishop is temporarily blocked, Black develops naturally with [Nc6], guarding e5 with easy equality. Later White often fianchettoes the f1 bishop on g2 and plays d3 plus c3 to build a slow kingside pawn roller. [%csl Ye2,Ye4,Ye5][%cal Gb8c6] }
3... Nc6 ) 3... d6
{ Pushing [d6] forces White's knight off the e-file before Black touches e4, which is the foundation of Petrov's Defence. Almost everyone retreats with [Nf3], keeping pieces flexible and preparing to challenge Black's own knight once it lands on e4. Two sound offbeat retreats also see grandmaster play: [Nd3], the Karklins-Martinovsky Variation used by Carlsen, and [Nc4], Paulsen's retreat eyeing d6 and e3. Because Black has not yet regained e4, White keeps a slight initiative in every line. [%csl Ye4,Yd6,Ye3][%cal Ge5f3] }
( 3... Nxe4
{ Damiano's Variation, often called the copycat line because Pedro Damiano analysed it in his 1512 treatise and warned against Black's greedy capture. Snatching e4 before chasing White's knight off e5 opens the e-file while White has the move. [Qe2] is the classic refutation, pinning the knight and threatening discovered checks that win material; [d4] is playable too and usually transposes to the Classical Attack after [d6]. [%csl Ye4,Ye5][%cal Gd1e2] }
4. Qe2
{ White lines the queen up against Black's king on e8 and attacks the knight on e4. Every natural retreat loses Black's queen on the spot to a discovered check. Black's one playable resource—already noted by Damiano in 1512 and later named the Kholmov Gambit—is [Qe7], mirroring White's queen move so that after [Qxe4] White's own knight on e5 becomes pinned. One glance down the open e-file shows why symmetry broke in White's favour: White acts first while Black's king is unshielded. [%csl Ye8,Ye4,Ye5][%cal Gd8e7] }
( 4. d4 ) ( 4. Bc4 $6
{ Aiming at f7 lets Black consolidate the knight on e4 with [d5], hitting the bishop with tempo. After [d5 Bb3 Be6], Black's central outpost is secure and White's knight on e5 will soon be questioned without White ever exploiting the open e-file. Play [Qe2] instead. [%csl Rf7,Re4][%cal Gd7d5] }
