{ The Kholmov Gambit, misattributed to Ratmir Kholmov from a 1974 game though known since Damiano. By opposing queens on e7, Black stops every discovered check and sets up a reverse pin: as soon as White captures [Qxe4], White's own knight on e5 is pinned to the queen and king. Taking that knight with [Qxe4] is still White's best path, winning a pawn after [d6]. [%csl Ye7,Ye5][%cal Ge2e4] }
( 4... Nf6 $4
{ Literally the oldest trap in modern chess literature. Retreating the knight uncovers the e-file for [Nc6+], a discovered check from the queen that simultaneously attacks Black's queen on d8. After [Nc6+ Be7 Nxd8], White wins the queen for a knight. Play [Qe7] instead. [%csl Rd8][%cal Ge5c6] }
