{ The most popular and reliable start against the Stafford Gambit: [d3] defends e4 solidly with a pawn so White's pieces stay free from babysitting duty, and leaves c3 vacant to build a [c3] and [d4] stone wall later. Black almost always continues [Bc5], aiming directly at White's tender f2 pawn and threatening [Ng4]. Keep your king in the centre until Black's knight on f6 and bishop on c5 are both neutralised. [%csl Ye4,Yc3,Yf2][%cal Gf8c5] }
( 5. Nc3
{ Developing [Nc3] defends e4 with a piece while keeping the d-pawn flexible for [d3] or [Be3], though it blocks the c-pawn so White cannot use the [c3]+[d4] wall against Black's bishop. Black plays [Bc5] almost without exception, taking aim at f2 and threatening [Ng4] on the very next turn. In this branch White must watch e4 and g4 together, since no pawn stands behind e4 yet. [%csl Ye4,Yf2,Yg4][%cal Gf8c5] }
