[Event "Petrov's Defence (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 ( 3. d4 Nxe4
{ Black captures e4 and leaves e5 loose in return. White's most accurate move order is the intermezzo [Bd3], developing with tempo against the knight so Black plays [b:d5] before White takes on e5, ruling out Black's [d6] kick. [dxe5] is a sharp, asymmetrical alternative eyeing [Qd5] and [Bc4], while immediate [Nxe5] allows [b:d5] or [d6]. With central files already open, brisk development sets the pace. [%csl Ye4,Ye5][%cal Gf1d3] }
4. Bd3
{ Developing [Bd3] attacks the knight on e4 while preparing kingside castling. Black almost always anchors the knight with [d5], opening the c8 bishop and keeping piece play balanced once White recaptures on e5; Murey's tricky sideline [Nc6] leaves the knight hanging because [Nc6 Bxe4 d5 Bd3 e4] forks bishop and knight right back. White gets free, natural development for every minor piece while challenging Black's centralized knight. [%csl Ye4,Ye5][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 4. dxe5
{ Capturing with the pawn avoids symmetrical d4/d5 structures altogether and opens the d-file for [Qd5], which threatens to fork e4 and f7 alongside [Bc4]. Black's solid answer is [d5], controlling d5 with the queen and anchoring e4 (often met by [Nbd2]), while [Bc5] counterattacks f2 immediately in wild tactical fashion. Because pawn symmetry is gone from move four, games in this variation rarely fizzle out into quiet draws. [%csl Ye4,Yf7,Yd5][%cal Gd7d5] }
4... d5 ( 4... Bc5 ) ( 4... d6 ) ) ( 4. Nxe5 ) ( 4. Bc4 $2
{ Developing [Bc4] walks straight into [d5], defending the knight on e4 with tempo on the bishop and leaving Black a clean central pawn ahead after [d5 Bb3 exd4]. Play [Bd3] instead so e4 is attacked. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd7d5] }
4... d5 5. Bb3 exd4 ) 4... d5
{ Black secures the knight on e4 with [d5], and now White recaptures the central pawn with [Nxe5]. Notice the key difference from the Classical Petrov main line after [Nxe5]: because neither side played [b:d6] and [w:Nf3], White's knight already stands on its aggressive e5 outpost while Black saved a full tempo developing d5. Both sides emerge with active pieces and clear central footholds, making this a favourite of dynamic grandmasters. [%csl Ge4,Gd5][%cal Gf3e5] }
( 4... Nc6
{ Murey's Variation: Black develops [Nc6] and leaves the knight on e4 untouched. It is no blunder: if White captures [Bxe4], Black answers [Bxe4 d5 Bd3 e4], regaining the piece with a pawn fork on bishop and knight for an unbalanced fight. [dxe5] is White's controlled alternative, keeping pieces on after [Nc5] or [b:d5]. That tactical fork makes Murey's move a lively surprise against Classical Petrov players. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd3e4] }
5. Bxe4 ( 5. dxe5 ) ) ( 4... Nf6 $2
{ Retreating [Nf6] lets [dxe5] chase the knight a third time while White castles and seizes central space after [dxe5 Nd5 O-O]. Anchor the knight in the middle with [b:d5] instead. [%cal Gd4e5] }
5. dxe5 Nd5 6. O-O ) ( 4... Nd6 $4
{ Dropping back to d6 is a notorious club mistake: [dxe5] attacks the knight again where its retreat clogs Black's kingside and d7 pawn, or drops material along the e-file after [dxe5 Qe7 O-O]. Anchor the knight with [b:d5]. [%csl Rd6][%cal Gd4e5] }
5. dxe5 Qe7 6. O-O ) ( 4... Nxf2 $4
{ Throwing the knight away on f2 gives Black only two pawns and opens the e-file straight at Black's uncastled king after [Kxf2 exd4 Re1+]. Defend the centralized knight soundly with [b:d5]. [%csl Rf2][%cal Ge1f2] }
5. Kxf2 exd4 6. Re1+ ) ( 4... f5 $6
{ Propping up e4 with [f5] opens the h5-e8 diagonal toward Black's king. After [Nxe5 Qf6 O-O], since [d6] runs into [Qh5] [g6] [w:Nxg6], Black's kingside stays wide open and behind in development. Play [b:d5] instead. [%csl Re4,Rh5][%cal Gf3e5] }
5. Nxe5 Qf6 6. O-O ) 5. Nxe5
{ Both knights stand on active fifth-rank outposts in the heart of the board. Black's two main grandmaster setups are [Nd7], immediately challenging White's strong knight on e5 before it can support [Qh5] or [f3], and [Bd6], developing rapidly to castle short and meet [c4] with [c6] or Trifunovic's clean trade [Bxe5]. White often aims [Qh5] or [c4] at Black's centre if Black delays challenging the e5 knight. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gb8d7,Gf8d6] }
5... Nd7
{ Challenging White's e5 outpost right away with [Nd7] is Konstantin Sakaev and Vladimir Kramnik's favourite equalizer against the Modern Attack. White can swap knights with [Nxd7] ([Nxd7 Bxd7 O-O Bd6 c4 c6]), develop ambitiously with [Nc3] inviting sharp play after [Nc3 Nxe5 dxe5 Nxc3 bxc3], or castle [O-O] keeping tension. Trading off that powerful e5 steed drains most of White's kingside attacking venom before it can build. [%cal Ge5d7,Gb1c3,Ge1g1] }
( 5... Bd6
{ Black develops the dark-squared bishop to d6, mirroring White's attacking diagonal and preparing kingside castling. White castles short with [O-O], keeping central breaks with [c4], [Nc3] and [Re1] ready while watching for tactical shots against f7 or h7 if Black's pieces lose coordination. Neither side has any structural blemishes, so piece coordination around e5 and e4 decides the middlegame. [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge1g1,Gc2c4] }
6. O-O
{ White's king is safely castled and [c4] is threatened next to undermine d5 while Black's king still sits on the open e-file. Black castles immediately with [O-O], tucking the king away and reaching the balanced main tabiya of the Modern Petrov. Once castled, Black is ready to meet [c4] either by reinforcing d5 with [c6] or by trading off e5. [%csl Yd5,Ye4][%cal Ge8g8] }
6... O-O
{ With both kings castled in a symmetrical central structure, White undermines d5 with [c4]—the critical test of Black's setup, asking whether Black reinforces with [c6] or trades on e5—or develops [Nc3] to force an immediate decision on e4 and d5, where [b:Nxc3] [w:bxc3] [c5] fights back vigorously. Even small inaccuracies around d5 can leave either side defending an isolated central pawn under heavy rook fire. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gb1c3] }
7. c4
{ White strikes at the d5 base supporting Black's knight on e4. Black either reinforces d5 solidly with [c6] (leading after [c6 cxd5 cxd5 Nc3 Nxc3 bxc3] to sharp play where [Qh5] tests Black's kingside) or liquidates cleanly with Trifunovic's [Bxe5]: after [Bxe5 dxe5 Nc6], Black trades off White's attacking knight and hits e5 and c4 for easy equality. Both replies give Black reliable grandmaster equality, though [c6] keeps far more tension on the board. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc7c6,Gd6e5] }
( 7. Nc3 ) 7... c6 ( 7... Bxe5 ) ) 6. Nxd7 ( 6. Nc3 ) ( 6. O-O ) ) 3... d6 4.
Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 ( 5. Nc3
{ The Nimzowitsch Attack, a favourite at club and grandmaster level alike whenever White wants an asymmetric fight instead of symmetrical main lines. White invites [Nxc3 dxc3] to open the d-file and the c1 bishop's diagonal, planning [Be3], [Qd2] and [w:O-O-O] with a kingside pawn storm. Black can accept that race with [Nxc3] or sidestep it calmly with [Nf6]. Once the c-pawns double, drawish symmetry disappears on move five. [%cal Ge4c3] }
5... Nxc3
{ Black accepts the trade on c3, clearing the centre before White builds more pressure against e4. White recaptures away from the corner with [dxc3]: although that doubles the c-pawns, it opens the d-file for [Qd2] and frees the bishop on c1 for [Be3] or [Bf4] so White can castle long in three moves. Meanwhile the extra pawn on c2 serves as a sturdy shield once White's king tucks onto b1. [%csl Yc3,Ye4,Yc1][%cal Gd2c3] }
( 5... Nf6
{ Rather than double White's pawns and open files for an opposite-side castling storm, Black retreats [Nf6]—a solid high-level antidote favoured by Fabiano Caruana and Chinese grandmasters. White claims the centre with [d4], enjoying an extra developing move on c3 compared to the Closed Classical Petrov, though White's c-pawn is now blocked behind the knight. Black gladly concedes that one tempo to keep all pawns symmetrical and steer the game into patient, strategic channels. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gd2d4] }
6. d4
{ White occupies d4 and prepares [Bd3], [O-O] and [w:Re1], often manoeuvring [Ne2] and [Ng3] toward the kingside while fianchettoing the queen's bishop if Black stays passive. Black chooses between [Be7], keeping a compact Hanham-like shell with [b:O-O], [b:Re8] and [c6], and [d5], transposing into an Exchange French structure where White already has [Nc3] in. White's extra developing move gives a slight pull, though Black's position has no weaknesses. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gf8e7] }
6... Be7 ( 6... d5 ) ) ( 5... Bf5 $4
{ Even future World Champion Viswanathan Anand fell into this famous six-move trap against Alonso Zapata at Biel 1988. Instead of trading on e4, White pins the knight with [Qe2], and after [Qe2 Qe7 Nd5] the double attack on e7 and c7 forces the queen off the e-file so [w:d3] wins the pinned knight. [%csl Re4,Re7][%cal Gd1e2] }
6. Qe2 Qe7 7. Nd5 ) ( 5... d5 $6
{ Supporting e4 with [d5] overlooks the e-file pin. After [Qe2 Be7 Nxe4 dxe4 Qxe4], White's knight was already poised to trade off the defender, so the e4 pawn falls cleanly and White emerges a healthy central pawn ahead. Trade with [Nxc3] or retreat [Nf6]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd1e2] }
6. Qe2 Be7 7. Nxe4 dxe4 8. Qxe4 ) ( 5... Qe7 $4
{ Trying to set a discovered-check trap with [Qe7] backfires at once: [Nd5] jumps out of the line of fire while hitting e7 and c7, forcing [Qd8], and [Qe2] then pins the stranded knight on e4. Take on c3 with [Nxc3+] instead. [%csl Re7,Rc7][%cal Gc3d5] }
6. Nd5 Qd8 7. Qe2 ) ( 5... Nc6 $4
{ Developing [Nc6] forgets that the knight on e4 is hanging. After [Nxe4], there is no centre fork trick on d5 because White has no bishop on c4 or b5, so [d5] is met calmly by [w:Ng3] and Black stays a full piece down. Trade with [Nxc3] or retreat [Nf6]. [%csl Re4,Rd5][%cal Gc3e4] }
6. Nxe4 ) 6. dxc3
{ White's queenside pawns are doubled, yet in return every diagonal and central file is wide open for [Be3] or [Bf4], [Qd2] and [w:O-O-O], while the extra pawn on c2 shelters the king once White castles long. Black develops solidly with [Be7], preparing short castling or a flexible setup with [Nd7] and [Nc5], or brings out [Nc6] to eye e5 and keep queenside castling in reserve. [%csl Yc2,Ye5][%cal Gf8e7] }
6... Be7
{ Developing [Be7] keeps Black's king flexible and prepares [O-O] alongside [Nd7] or [Nc6]. White chooses where to place the dark-squared bishop before [Qd2] and [w:O-O-O]: [Be3] is the modern main line, keeping the bishop stable on the a7-g1 diagonal and ready to support [Bd3] and [h4], whereas [Bf4] is the classic alternative controlling e5 and eyeing d6. Whichever square White picks, the plan stays fast and direct: queen to d2, long castling, and a pawn storm with h4. [%cal Gc1e3,Gc1f4,Gd1d2] }
( 6... Nc6 ) 7. Be3
{ On e3, White's bishop supports the centre and clears the way for [Qd2] and [O-O-O] without sitting exposed to [g5] or knight jumps. Black usually castles short with [O-O], entering an opposite-side castling race where Black counterattacks via [Nd7], [Re8], [c6] and [b:Qa5], or delays castling with [Nd7] or [Nc6] so queenside castling stays open for Black too. Either way White centralizes the rooks and probes with [Qd2] and [h4]. [%cal Ge8g8,Gb8d7,Yd1d2] }
( 7. Bf4
{ Developing to f4 claims the h2-b8 diagonal and stops Black from placing a piece comfortably on d6 or c7 before [Qd2] and [O-O-O]. Black answers with [O-O], [Nc6] or [Nd7]. One classic tactical motif to remember after [Nc6 Qd2 Be6 O-O-O]: Black must castle rather than grab [Bxa2], because [b3] traps the greedy bishop on a2. Compared with [Be3], the bishop on f4 presses d6 harder while needing an eye kept on [b:Nh5] or [g5] counter-punches. [%cal Ge8g8,Gb8c6,Yf4d6] }
7... O-O ( 7... Nc6 ) ( 7... Nd7 ) ) 7... O-O
{ Once Black castles kingside, the opposite-wing race of the Nimzowitsch Attack is officially on. White plays [Qd2] next to connect the rooks and castle queenside in one stroke, then launches [Bd3], [h4], [Ng5] or [g4] at Black's castled king. Black counters on the queenside and in the centre via [Nd7] or [Nc6], targeting White's doubled c-pawns. White's attack is intuitive to conduct over the board, whereas Black must defend h7 accurately before cashing in on White's queenside structure. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gd1d2,Gh2h4] }
( 7... Nd7 ) ( 7... Nc6 ) 8. Qd2
{ White is one move from [O-O-O], followed by [Bd3] or [Bc4], [h4] and [w:Rde1]. Black develops the queenside knight with [Nd7], aiming for f6 to fortify the kingside or c5 to harass White's light-squared bishop, or plays [Nc6] to contest e5 and prepare [Be6] and [Qd7]. In endgames Black's healthy queenside structure shines, so White plays for the initiative. White typically tucks the king onto b1 early so no bishop or queen check ever interrupts the kingside advance. [%csl Yf6,Yc5,Ye5][%cal Gb8d7] }
8... Nd7 ( 8... Nc6 ) ) 5... d5 6. Bd3 Bd6
{ Marshall's Variation: Black puts the bishop on the aggressive b8-h2 diagonal, mirroring White's bishop on d3 and racing to castle short. Because e7 is left empty, Black must stay alert to pins along the e-file once White castles. White usually plays [O-O] first, keeping [c4] and [Re1] ready, though [c4] at once is also playable. Compared with [Be7], the bishop on d6 exerts far more kingside bite at the price of leaving e4 slightly more exposed to pins. [%csl Yb8,Yh2,Yd3][%cal Ge1g1] }
7. O-O
{ White's king is tucked safely away and both [c4] and [Re1] are threatened next to pry loose Black's knight on e4. Black should match with [O-O] immediately, getting the king off the open e-file before White can pin the centralized knight. Delaying castling to develop queenside pieces leaves Black vulnerable to sudden central pawn breaks. Once both kings are tucked away on the kingside, every tempo in the battle around d5 and e4 counts. [%csl Ye4,Yd5][%cal Ge8g8] }
( 7. c4 ) 7... O-O
{ Both sides have castled and the symmetrical Marshall tabiya is set, with both light-squared bishops eyeing the opposing kingsides. White's main weapon is [c4], undermining d5 so the knight on e4 loses its pawn anchor, while [Re1] contests the open e-file first and asks Black to commit a piece to f5 or g4 before White touches the c-pawn. Black typically answers rook pressure with [Re8] or [Bf5], keeping piece activity completely symmetrical until White breaks with c4. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gf1e1] }
8. c4
{ Pushing [c4] attacks d5 and forces Black to reinforce the centre. [c6], the Staunton Variation, is the solid classical choice, keeping a pawn on d5 even if White trades on d5 so the knight on e4 stays supported. Marshall's gambit try [Bg4] sacrifices a pawn for tricky tactical counterplay against f3 and h2, but drops objective ground against accurate defence. Whichever side blinks first in the c4-d5 tension risks either an isolated central pawn or surrendering the e4 outpost. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc7c6,Gc8g4] }
( 8. Re1 ) 8... c6
{ The Staunton Variation: Black shores up d5 with [c6], forming a compact Caro-Slav pawn triangle behind the active knight on e4 and bishop on d6. White develops [Nc3] to pile a third attacker onto d5 and e4, plays [Qc2]—Anand's weapon against Caruana—to build a battery toward h7 and e4, or brings the rook to [Re1] to command the open file. Black often develops the b8 knight via a6 toward c7 or b4 so the c8 bishop stays unblocked. [%csl Yd5,Ye4,Yd6][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 8... Bg4
{ In Tarrasch and Marshall's aggressive gambit line, Black ignores the attack on d5 to pin the knight on f3 and build immediate piece pressure against White's kingside. White accepts the challenge with [cxd5], pocketing a central pawn and forcing Black to justify the sacrifice with [f5], or develops [Nc3] calmly to keep central tension. Because Black's queenside knight is still at home on b8, Black must rely on dynamic kingside threats to compensate for d5. [%csl Yd5,Yf3,Yb8][%cal Gc4d5] }
9. cxd5
{ White has won the central d5 pawn and simultaneously attacks Black's knight on e4 with the d3 bishop. Black's only consistent gambit follow-up is [f5], anchoring the knight firmly on e4, opening the f-file for the f8 rook and laying Marshall's famous Greek-Gift trap on h2 if White carelessly vacates f1. White's task from here is simple: keep f2 and h2 defended while bringing out the queenside knight to consolidate the extra pawn. [%csl Ge4,Yh2][%cal Gf7f5] }
( 9. Nc3 ) 9... f5
{ Black has sacrificed the d-pawn to clamp a knight on e4 with both bishops aimed directly at White's castled king. White stays safely on top by developing [Nc3] (preparing [Qb3] or [Be2] to break the pin) or questioning the bishop with [h3] while keeping the rook on f1 where it guards f2. Moving that rook away springs the Marshall Trap. Once White neutralises the pin on f3 without touching the f1 rook, Black's attacking compensation quickly fades. [%csl Yf2][%cal Gb1c3,Gh2h3] }
( 9... Bxf3 $2
{ Giving up the light-squared bishop on f3 just to save the knight lets White recapture [Qxf3] and stay a clean pawn up with the bishop pair after [Qxf3 Nf6 Nc3]. Anchor the knight with [f5] instead. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd1f3,Gb1c3] }
10. Qxf3 Nf6 11. Nc3 ) 10. Nc3 ( 10. h3 ) ( 10. Re1 $6
{ The classic Marshall Trap. Leaving f2 undefended allows [Bxh2+]: if [Kf1], Black keeps a fierce initiative, while after [Bxh2+ Kxh2 Nxf2 Qe2 Nxd3 Qxd3 Bxf3] White cannot recapture on f3 without dropping the rook on e1 to [Qh4]. Keep the rook on f1 and develop [w:Nc3]. [%csl Rh2,Rf2][%cal Gd6h2] }
10... Bxh2+ 11. Kxh2 Nxf2 12. Qe2 Nxd3 13. Qxd3 Bxf3 ) ) ( 8... dxc4 $4
{ Giving up d5 removes the only defender of Black's knight on e4. [Bxe4] wins a full minor piece on the spot, since after [Bxe4 Re8 Qc2] White guards the bishop and picks up c4 too. Reinforce d5 with [c6]. [%csl Re4,Rc4][%cal Gd3e4] }
9. Bxe4 Re8 10. Qc2 ) 9. Nc3
{ With three white pieces now bearing down on e4 and d5, Black has to resolve the central tension accurately. [Nxc3] is the cleanest path: after [Nxc3 bxc3 dxc4 Bxc4 Bg4], Black trades off White's queenside knight and eliminates the d5 target, reaching a dynamic position where White's central pawns face Black's clean piece play. [Bf5] is the main alternative, keeping pieces on. White's central pawn cluster controls d4 and e5, while Black targets the hanging pawns down the e- and c-files. [%csl Ye4,Yd5,Yd4][%cal Ge4c3] }
( 9. Qc2
{ Putting the queen on c2 builds a battery with the bishop on d3, hitting the knight on e4 twice while eyeing h7. White is not threatening [Bxe4] yet because [b:dxe4] would fork f3 and e5, so Black can meet the pressure actively with [Na6], preparing [Nb4] against the bishop on d3, or defend e4 along the file with [Re8]. Capturing prematurely on e4 with [w:Bxe4] only helps Black, whose recapturing pawn gains space and kicks f3. [%csl Yc2,Yd3,Ye4][%cal Gb8a6] }
