{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 ( 1... d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nbd7 4. Nf3 e5 5. Bc4 Be7 6. dxe5
{ Often reached from the Black Lion move order when Black delays [e5] until both knights are out, and White trades [w:dxe5] once [Bc4] and [Be7] sit on the board. Black must recapture with the pawn via [dxe5], never with the knight (which drops e5 after [w:Nxe5] [dxe5] [w:Qh5]), reaching an open-d-file structure where Black castles short and plays [c6] and [b:Qc7]. [%csl Ye5,Yf7][%cal Gd6e5] }
6... dxe5
{ With the d-file open, White castles [O-O] and builds pressure via [Qe2], [w:Rd1] and [Bg5] or the knight route [Nh4] to f5 against Black's compact kingside; [Ng5] ([Ng5 O-O]) is playable too, and some blitz players even try the double-edged [w:Bxf7+] [b:Kxf7] [w:Ng5+] [b:Kg6] [w:f4] sacrifice, though Black's pieces hold the balance with accurate defence. [%cal Ge1g1,Gf3g5,Gd1e2] }
7. O-O ( 7. Ng5 ) ( 7. Bxf7+ $6
{ Sacrificing on f7 tries to punish Black's cramped setup by dragging the king out, yet with Black's knights on f6 and d7 plus the bishop on e7 covering key dark squares, the king escapes safely via [Kxf7 Ng5+ Kg6]! Prepare pressure first with [Ng5+] or castle [O-O+] instead of giving up a piece. [%csl Yf7][%cal Re8f7] }
7... Kxf7 8. Ng5+ Kg6 9. f4 exf4 ) ) 2. Nf3 d6 ( 2... d5 3. Nxe5
{ Daniel Naroditsky's practical choice against the Elephant Gambit. Capturing e5 sidesteps Paulsen's [b:e4] lines altogether and plants White's knight on an active central post eyeing f7. Black's main tries are [Bd6], preparing to regain e4 after [w:d4] [dxe4], [dxe4] inviting the sharp Wasp Variation ([dxe4 Bc4 Qg5]), and [Qe7] pinning along the e-file. In every line White meets Black's counterattacks by developing pieces with tempo rather than clinging passively to extra pawns. [%csl Ye5,Yf7,Ye4][%cal Gf8d6] }
3... Bd6
{ The modern Elephant main line against White's knight capture: Black develops [Bd6] with tempo on e5 so Black can recover e4 next turn. Instead of retreating the knight passively to f3, White strikes right back in the centre with [d4], anchoring e5 with a pawn and opening both bishops so White stays ahead in development once the central pawns trade. [%csl Ye5,Ye4,Yf3][%cal Gd2d4] }
( 3... dxe4
{ Black takes e4 right away without [Bd6], steering into the sharpest tactical branch of the Elephant Gambit. White's strongest reply is [Bc4], aiming directly at f7 and challenging Black to enter the Wasp Variation with [Bc4 Qg5]. Avoid premature solo raids like [w:Nxf7] or [w:Qh5] before the light-squared bishop joins the attack. Developing [Bc4] forces Black either to enter the double-edged [b:Qg5] line or concede a ruined kingside structure. [%csl Ye4,Yf7][%cal Gf1c4] }
4. Bc4
{ With White's bishop on c4 and knight on e5 both trained on f7, Black has only one move that keeps the gambit playable: [Qg5], the Wasp Variation, counterattacking e5 and g2 at the same time. Passive blocks on e6 or h6 ruin Black's kingside structure, while [b:Nh6] runs into [w:d4] and [w:Bxh6]. Against [Qg5], capturing on f7 with the bishop rather than the knight is the tactical key that refutes Black's sortie. [%csl Yc4,Ye5,Yf7][%cal Gd8g5] }
( 4. Nxf7 $4
{ Sacrificing [Nxf7] without a second piece developed is pure hope chess: after [Kxf7 Bc4+ Be6 Qh5+ g6], Black blocks every check cleanly and stays a full piece ahead. Develop [Bc4] first. [%cal Ge8f7] }
4... Kxf7 5. Bc4+ Be6 ) ( 4. Qh5 $6
{ Bringing the queen to h5 alone without a bishop helper is easily parried by [Be6], guarding f7 while preparing [Nf6] to chase the exposed queen with tempo. Develop [Bc4] first. [%csl Rh5,Rf7][%cal Gc8e6] }
4... Be6 5. Bb5+ c6 ) 4... Qg5
{ The Wasp Variation: Black's queen hits both the knight on e5 and g2, setting a trap for players who grab the fork with [Nxf7]. White's refutation is to capture on f7 with the bishop via [Bxf7+], keeping the knight on e5 alive so [Bxf7+ Ke7 d4 Qxg2 Rf1 Bh3 Bc4] defends the rook and turns the tables on Black's exposed king; [d4] at once ([d4 Qxg2 Rf1]) is strong too. [%csl Ye5,Yg2,Yf7][%cal Gc4f7] }
( 4... Be6 $2
{ Blocking f7 with [Be6] walks into [Bxe6 fxe6 Qh5+ g6 Nxg6]: because the h-pawn is pinned to the corner rook, [Nf6] [w:Qh3] leaves Black's kingside in ruins two pawns down. Counterattack with [b:Qg5] instead. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gc4e6] }
5. Bxe6 fxe6 6. Qh5+ g6 7. Nxg6 ) ( 4... Nf6 $4
{ Developing [Nf6] overlooks the fork on f7: [Nxf7 Qe7 Nxh8] attacks Black's queen and corner rook simultaneously, winning a clean exchange while Black's king stays trapped in the centre. Play [b:Qg5] instead. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge5f7] }
5. Nxf7 Qe7 6. Nxh8 ) 5. Bxf7+
{ Capturing on f7 with the bishop, with check, is the key to refuting the Wasp Variation: White drags Black's king out to [Ke7] or [Kd8] without surrendering the central knight on e5, gaining the tempo needed to push [d4+] on the next move and open the c1-bishop against Black's queen on g5. Even when Black snatches g2 next, [w:Rf1] and [w:Bc4] shield White's king while Black's own king sits stranded in the middle. [%csl Yf7,Ye5,Yg5][%cal Ge8e7] }
( 5. d4 ) ( 5. Nxf7 $4
