3. Bc4 ( 3. d4 ) ( 3. Nc3 ) ) 3. d4
{ White strikes at e5 immediately, threatening [dxe5] followed by trading queens on d8 to win a central pawn and strip Black's castling rights. Black has three main replies: [exd4] is the solid Exchange Variation, surrendering the centre to free Black's pieces; [Nd7] is Hanham's Variation, holding the e5 strongpoint; and [Nf6] is Nimzowitsch's counterattack against e4. [Bg4] is the dubious pin from Morphy's 1858 Opera Game. [%csl Ye5,Yd8,Ye4][%cal Ge5d4] }
3... exd4
{ The Exchange Variation, accounting for more than half of all Philidor games. Black gives up the central e5 foothold early so the c8-bishop and e-file open up and Black avoids cramped Hanham traps. [Nxd4] is the classical main line, placing a knight in the middle behind the lone central pawn on e4, while [Qxd4] was Paul Morphy's active choice, planning [Bb5+] if Black chases the queen with [Nc6]. [Bc4] and [w:c3] offer gambit play. [%csl Ye5,Ye4][%cal Gf3d4] }
( 3... Nd7
{ The Hanham Variation, named after nineteenth-century American master James Moore Hanham: Black holds the e5 strongpoint rather than surrendering the centre, planning [c6], [Be7], [Ngf6] and [b:O-O]. White's sharpest test is [Bc4], setting a famous minefield of traps on f7 if Black develops on autopilot. [Nc3] reaches the Lion after [Ngf6], while [c4] builds a spatial bind on the queenside. [%csl Ye5,Yf7][%cal Gf1c4] }
4. Bc4
{ One of the sharpest trap positions in club chess. Because [Nd7] boxed in Black's c8-bishop and took away escape squares from the queen on d8, both routine kingside developing moves, [Be7] and [Ngf6], lose material by force after [dxe5]. Black must play [c6] first, covering d5 and opening c7 and b6 for the queen, or trade into an Exchange structure with [exd4]. [%csl Yd8,Yd5,Yc7][%cal Gc7c6] }
( 4. Nc3 Ngf6 5. g4
{ The Shirov Gambit, introduced by Alexei Shirov to blow open Black's quiet Lion setup on move five. White sacrifices the g-pawn so that [Nxg4 Rg1 Ngf6 Bc4] opens the g-file straight at Black's kingside before Black castles, followed by [Be3], [Qd2] and [w:O-O-O]. Black can accept the challenge with [Nxg4], decline solidly with [h6], or prepare a fianchetto with [g6]. [%cal Gh7h6] }
