{ Nimzowitsch's Variation: instead of defending e5 passively with [Nd7], Black counterattacks e4 right away, inviting [Nc3] followed by [Nbd7] to reach the Lion without allowing Hanham's early f7 traps. White's sharpest test of this move order is [dxe5], forcing [Nxe4] where Rellstab's [Qd5] seizes the initiative; [Nc3] enters the main Lion tabiya and [Bc4] is Klein's tricky sideline. Because neither side has fixed the central pawns yet, accurate move order on moves four and five decides the whole opening. [%csl Ye5,Ye4,Yf7][%cal Gd4e5] }
4. dxe5
{ White calls Nimzowitsch's bluff by capturing e5 immediately, which is why many Lion players prefer starting with [b:d6], [b:Nf6] and [b:Nbd7] before pushing [b:e5]. Black must counter-capture e4 with [Nxe4], accepting an open tactical game, because recapturing on e5 with the d-pawn loses a clean pawn after [Qxd8+] and [w:Nxe5]. Once Black recaptures on e4, White avoids routine d6-trades and plays for initiative against the loose e4-knight. [%csl Ye5,Ye4][%cal Gf6e4] }
