6. Kxf2 c6 7. Qd4 ) ) ( 3... Bg4
{ The third move of the 1858 Paris Opera Game between Paul Morphy and the consulting Duke Karl of Brunswick and Count Isouard. Black pins f3 hoping to hold e5 without giving up the centre, yet the pin fails tactically: White captures [dxe5] at once, forcing Black either to give up the bishop pair on f3 or drop a clean central pawn. [%csl Yf3,Ye5][%cal Gd4e5] }
4. dxe5
{ White opens the d-file, so Black cannot recapture [dxe5] without losing a pawn to [Qxd8+] and [Nxe5]. Black must either concede the bishop pair as in Morphy's Opera Game with [Bxf3] ([Bxf3 Qxf3 dxe5 Bc4]) or try the Albin-Blackburne Gambit [Nd7], sacrificing a pawn via [Nd7 exd6 Bxd6 Nc3 Ngf6 Be2] to catch up in kingside development. Whichever way Black plays on move four, White's open d-file and lead in development keep Black's king under pressure. [%cal Gg4f3] }
