{ As in Morphy's Opera Game, Black trades off the knight on f3 before recapturing on e5 so no pawn falls on the spot. White recaptures [Qxf3], bringing the queen to an active post that eyes both f7 and b3 without fear of harassment; Géza Maróczy's endgame squeeze [gxf3 dxe5 Qxd8+ Kxd8 f4] is a strong alternative that uses the two bishops in a queenless middlegame. [%csl Yf3,Ye5,Yf7][%cal Gd1f3] }
( 4... Nd7
{ The Albin-Blackburne Gambit: instead of giving up the bishop pair as in Morphy's Opera Game ([b:Bxf3]), Black develops [Nd7] and offers a central pawn to speed up kingside mobilisation. White accepts with [exd6] ([exd6 Bxd6 Be2 Ngf6 Nc3 O-O]), keeping a clean extra pawn and a solid kingside shield while Black relies on open central files and active piece play for compensation. Chasing the bishop first with [h3] ([h3 Bxf3 Qxf3 dxe5]) lets Black recover the pawn cleanly. [%csl Yd6,Yg4][%cal Ge5d6,Gf1e2] }
5. exd6 ( 5. h3 ) ) ( 4... dxe5 $2
{ The beginner mistake [Bg4] was trying to avoid. Because the queens see each other down the open d-file, [Qxd8+ Kxd8] unpins White's knight with check and [Nxe5] wins a clean central pawn while hitting f7 and g4. Play [Bxf3] first. [%csl Rf7,Rg4][%cal Gd1d8] }
5. Qxd8+ Kxd8 6. Nxe5 Be6 ) 5. Qxf3
{ White recaptures with the queen, keeping the kingside pawns whole while aiming down the f-file toward f7 and standing ready to slide to b3 once the light-squared bishop develops. Black recovers the central pawn with [dxe5], hoping to bring out [b:Nf6], [b:Bd6] or [b:Qf6] before White's unopposed light-squared bishop locks onto Black's weak light squares. Once Black recaptures on e5, [w:Bc4] seizes the a2-g8 diagonal with immediate mating and fork threats. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gd6e5,Gf3f7] }
5... dxe5
