[Event "Other replies to 2.Nf3 (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6
{ The Philidor Defence, recommended by Ruy López de Segura in 1561 to avoid the pin of [w:Bb5] after [b:Nc6] and made famous by François-André Danican Philidor in his 1749 treatise. Protecting e5 with [d6] builds a sturdy pawn chain and leaves Black's c-pawn free to advance, at the cost of boxing in the dark-squared bishop on f8. [d4] challenges the centre directly, [Bc4] targets f7 first, and [Nc3], [c3] or [d3] build more quietly. [%csl Ye5,Yf8,Yf7][%cal Gd2d4] }
( 2... d5
{ The Elephant Gambit, called the Queen's Pawn Countergambit in nineteenth-century manuals and tried occasionally by Joseph Henry Blackburne and Géza Maróczy. Black strikes at e4 before defending e5, sacrificing a central pawn for open files and quick traps. Both captures, [exd5] and [Nxe5], give White a clear advantage when met with active central moves, whereas passive pawn pushes hand Black easy play. [%csl Ye4,Ye5][%cal Ge4d5] }
