{ White captures d5 and asks how Black justifies the gambit. Recapturing [Qxd5] loses time to [Nc3], so Black almost always chooses between Paulsen's Countergambit [b:e4], chasing White's f3-knight to disrupt coordination, and Maróczy's [Bd6], sacrificing the pawn outright to develop the kingside rapidly and castle short. Against either branch, White prioritizes central pins and [w:d4] over early castling. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge5e4] }
( 3. Nxe5 ) ( 3. Nc3 $6
{ Defending e4 with [Nc3] walks straight into [d4], gaining central space with tempo and chasing White's knight back to e2 or b1. Never play passively against the Elephant Gambit: capture on d5 or e5. [%csl Re4,Re2][%cal Gd5d4] }
