3... dxe4 4. dxe4 Qxd1+ 5. Kxd1 ) 3... e4
{ Paulsen's Countergambit: Black pushes [b:e4] to harass White's knight on f3 and claim central space. Retreating the knight hands Black free initiative or drops d5 with tempo, so White's refutation is [Qe2], pinning the e4-pawn directly to Black's king on e8 so the knight on f3 stays put. Once the e-file is pinned, Black must spend a move unpinning before touching d5. [%csl Yf3,Yd5,Ye8][%cal Gd1e2] }
( 3... Bd6
{ The Maróczy Gambit: Black leaves d5 alone to place the dark-squared bishop on an attacking diagonal aimed at h2, planning [Nf6], [O-O] and [b:e4] with Greek-Gift sacrifices on h2 if White castles carelessly. White's cleanest refutation is [d4], challenging e5 at once so Black never builds a free attack; [Nc3] is playable too so long as White meets [Nc3 Nf6] with [w:Bb5+] or [w:d4] rather than [w:Bc4]. [%csl Yd5,Yh2,Ye5][%cal Gd2d4] }
