[Event "Vienna Game and Bishop's Opening (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 ( 2. Bc4 Nf6 3. d4
{ The Urusov (or Ponziani) Gambit, analysed by Ponziani in 1769, named after Prince Sergey Urusov and played by Keres: White strikes in the centre instead of defending e4. Black must capture the d4-pawn with [exd4]. Grabbing the e4-pawn via [Nxe4 dxe5] seals off the knight's d6 and f6 retreats and exposes f7, while passive [d6 dxe5] drops a pawn. [%csl Ge4,Gd6,Gf6,Gf7][%cal Ge5d4] }
3... exd4
{ After [d4] [exd4], White enters the Urusov Gambit proper with [Nf3], developing with control over e5 and d4 rather than exposing the queen early with [Qxd4 Nc6] or rushing Ponziani's [e5 d5] (where [Bb5] [Bd7] equalises). Avoid Danish-style [c3], which fails against a knight already on f6 because [c3 Nxe4] grabs a second pawn safely. With [Nf3], White threatens [w:e5] and [w:Qxd4] (or [w:O-O] and [w:Re1]), forcing Black to choose between Two Knights, Panov and Keidansky territory. [%csl Ge5,Gd4,Gf6][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 3... Nxe4
{ When Black mistakenly grabs [Nxe4] on move three against the Urusov Gambit, White immediately pushes [dxe5]. That advance seals off both natural retreat squares d6 and f6, leaving Black's knight stranded on e4 while f7 sits wide open to [w:Qd5] and [w:Bxf7+] tactics (note [dxe5 Bc5 Qd5] wins a piece, so Black has to scramble with [b:c6] or [b:Nc5]). Once d6 and f6 are cut off by [dxe5], Black's safest damage control is [b:c6] (preparing [b:d5] while opening a5) or [b:Nc5]. [%csl Gd6,Gf6,Ge4,Gf7][%cal Gd4e5] }
