{ Black plays the symmetrical Copycat bishop move, expecting a quiet Italian game. Because White has not put a knight on f3, the queen's diagonal is open and [Qg4] hits the undefended pawn on g7 immediately, forcing Black into [Qg4 g6] (creating dark-square holes) or [Qg4 Kf8] (giving up castling). Quiet [d3] is playable too, whereas [Nf3] lets Black off the hook. That single move-order difference: having [Nc3] instead of [Nf3]: makes symmetry a mistake for Black because White's queen is unblocked on the d1-h5 diagonal. [%csl Gf3,Gg7,Gd1,Gh5][%cal Gd1g4] }
