{ A famous crossroads in the Vienna Game. Defending e5 with [Nc6] enters the Frankenstein-Dracula Variation (named by Tim Harding in 1976), sacrificing the corner rook after [Nc6 Nb5 g6 Qf3 f5 Qd5 Qe7 Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8 b6] for a fierce bishop-pair attack. Players who prefer safety play [Be7] ([Nf3 Nc6 Nxe5] [O-O]), returning the e5-pawn cleanly. Avoid [Qf6 Nd5]. [%csl Ge5][%cal Gb8c6] }
5... Nc6
{ The Frankenstein-Dracula Variation: defending e5 with [Nc6] instead of [Be7] dares White to jump [Nb5], threatening [w:Nxd6+] [b:cxd6] [w:Qxf7#]. Developing quietly with [Nf3] fails here because Black's dark-squared bishop is still on f8 ready to fianchetto after [Nf3 g6 Qg4 h5], keeping the extra pawn, so White must enter the tactical storm with [Nb5]. [%csl Ge5,Gf8][%cal Gc3b5] }
( 5... Be7
{ The solid master antidote to 4.[Qh5]: instead of entering the Frankenstein-Dracula labyrinth with [Nc6], Black develops [Be7] to unblock g8 for short castling and return the e5-pawn on Black's own terms. White develops [Nf3] ([Nc6] [Nxe5] [O-O d4] [b:Nxd4] [w:Nxd4] [Bf6], reaching balanced open play) rather than taking [Qxe5 O-O] with the queen exposed. After [Nf3 Nc6 Nxe5 O-O], Black often trades on e5 or plays [b:Nd4] and [b:Bf6], neutralizing White's bishop while reaching comfortable equality. [%csl Gg8,Ge5][%cal Gg1f3] }
6. Nf3 ) ( 5... Qf6 $4
{ Trying to cling to e5 with [Qf6] walks straight into [Nd5 Qd8 Qxe5+ Be7 Qxg7 Rf8 Nf3], losing the pawn anyway with Black's king and rook crippled. Play [Nc6] or [Be7]. }
