{ White's jump to b5 threatens [w:Nxd6+] [b:cxd6] [w:Qxf7#]. Notice that capturing the knight with [Nxb5] is instant suicide, as it removes the only piece blocking the bishop's diagonal and allows [Nxb5 Qxf7#] checkmate, while [Qf6 Nxc7+] drops the corner rook cleanly. Black's only move is [g6], kicking White's queen off h5 first. Once [g6] kicks the queen to f3, Black blocks the f-file with [b:f5] and sacrifices the a8-rook for sweeping bishop diagonals. [%csl Gb5,Gh5,Gf3][%cal Gg7g6] }
( 6. Nf3 $2
{ Developing [Nf3] fails against 5.[Nc6] because Black's f8-bishop can still fianchetto: [g6 Qg4 f5 Qg3 e4] keeps the extra pawn and chases White's pieces back. Jump [Nb5]. [%cal Rg7g6] }
6... g6 7. Qg4 h5 ) 6... g6
{ In the Frankenstein-Dracula Variation, [Nb5] threatened [Nxd6+ cxd6] [Qxf7+], so Black kicks White's queen off h5 with [g6]. White slides the queen straight down the file to [Qf3], keeping the exact same mate battery trained on f7 through the d6-knight so [b:Nxb5] is still impossible and Black must block the f-file on the next move. After [Qf3 f5], White shifts the queen onto the a2-g8 diagonal with [w:Qd5], forcing [b:Qe7] before capturing on c7 and a8. [%csl Gh5,Gf7,Ga2,Gg8][%cal Gh5f3] }
( 6... Nxb5 $4
{ Grabbing the knight removes the only defender of f7: [Qxf7#] is immediate checkmate. Kick the queen first with [g6]. [%csl Rf7][%cal Rh5f7] }
7. Qxf7# ) ( 6... Qf6 $4
{ Guarding f7 with [Qf6] forgets c7: [Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8] wins the a8-rook WITHOUT giving Black any [f5] or [Bb7] counter-play. Play [g6]. }
