{ White's queen on f3 renews the threat of [w:Nxd6+] followed by [w:Qxf7#] (so [Nxb5 Qxf7#] is still immediate mate). Black's forced and resourceful defence is [f5], blocking the f-file with a pawn while preparing [b:e4]. Guarding f7 passively with [Qe7] instead lets White capture [Qe7 Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8] without giving Black any [f5] counter-play. [%csl Gf3,Gf7][%cal Gf7f5] }
7... f5
{ Black's [f5] blocks the f-file mate threat and threatens [b:e4] against White's queen. White immediately switches to the a2-g8 diagonal with [Qd5], joining the b3-bishop to renew [w:Nxd6+] followed by [w:Qf7#] checkmate while eyeing c7 as well. Any slower retreat would let Black trade on b5 and overrun the centre. That diagonal switch to d5 forces Black to play [b:Qe7], leaving c7 and the corner rook on a8 free for White's knight to take. [%csl Ga2,Gg8,Gc7,Gb5][%cal Gf3d5] }
( 7... Qe7 $2
{ Trying to guard f7 with [Qe7] drops c7 and the rook cleanly to [Nxc7+ Kd8 Nd5], hitting the queen while keeping the knight alive. Block with [f5]. }
8. Nxc7+ Kd8 9. Nxa8 ) ( 7... Nxb5 $4
{ Even with the queen on f3, [Nxb5] walks straight into [Qxf7#] checkmate. Block with [f5]. [%csl Rf3][%cal Rf3f7] }
8. Qxf7# ) 8. Qd5
{ With the f-file closed by f5, White's [Qd5] shifts the battery onto the a2-g8 diagonal and threatens [w:Nxd6+] followed by [w:Qf7#] once again. Black's only defence now is [Qe7], guarding both d6 and f7 laterally while deliberately sacrificing c7 and the corner rook on a8 for long-term piece pressure. Counter-attacking with [Qg5 d4] collapses. [%csl Gf5,Ga2,Gg8,Gd6][%cal Gd8e7] }
8... Qe7
{ Black's [Qe7] is the sole move that protects both d6 and f7, offering the c7-pawn and a8-rook as a positional exchange sacrifice. White cashes in with [Nxc7+], forcing Black's king to step to d8 before capturing the rook in the corner, since leaving c7 untouched would hand Black [b:Kd8] and a safe extra pawn. Once [Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8 b6] traps the cornered knight, White must play [w:d3] immediately so the queen on d5 does not get trapped too. [%csl Gd6,Gf7,Gd8,Gc7][%cal Gb5c7] }
( 8... Qg5 $4
{ Counter-attacking g2 forgets both c7 and d6: [Nxc7+ Kd8 d4] (followed by [Nxa8]) wins the corner rook with tempo against Black's exposed queen. Guard d6 and f7 with [Qe7] instead. }
9. Nxc7+ Kd8 10. d4 ) 9. Nxc7+
{ White's knight check on c7 forks Black's king and the corner rook on a8, leaving Black only one legal move: stepping aside with [Kd8]. Black gladly accepts losing the rook on a8, planning [Kd8 Nxa8 b6] and [b:Bb7] to trap White's cornered knight and sweep the long light diagonal against White's exposed queen and kingside. The resulting struggle pits White's extra rook against Black's two bishops, central pawn mass and White's uncastled king. [%csl Gc7,Ga8][%cal Ge8d8] }
9... Kd8
{ Black's king steps to d8, and White captures the corner rook with [Nxa8]. White knows the knight on a8 will be trapped once Black plays [Nxa8 b6] and [b:Bb7], yet counts on emerging an exchange ahead once that knight eventually falls: provided White opens a retreat square for the d5-queen before Black's pieces close the net. After [Nxa8 b6], White's top priority is [w:d3] followed by [w:h4] and retreating the queen to f3 before [b:Bb7] and [b:Nd4] close the net. [%csl Gd8,Ga8,Gf3][%cal Gc7a8] }
10. Nxa8
{ The main Frankenstein-Dracula tabiya: White has captured the rook on a8, and Black springs the trap shut with [b6]. Pushing [b6] entombs White's knight in the corner and prepares [b:Bb7] down the long a8-h1 diagonal straight at White's queen on d5, the central dark squares and g2. Delaying [b6] with [Bg7 Qc5] lets White untangle. [%csl Ga8,Gh1,Gd5,Gg2][%cal Gb7b6] }
10... b6
{ White's queen on d5 is in real danger of getting trapped in mid-board once [b:Bb7] and [b:Nd4] arrive. White must open a retreat path immediately with [d3] ([Bb7 h4 f4 Qf3], keeping f3 vacant for the queen before [Nd4] lands). Developing the g1-knight to [Nf3] steals the queen's escape square and gets crushed after [Nf3 Bb7]. [%csl Gd5,Gf3][%cal Gd2d3] }
( 10... Bg7 $2
{ Developing [Bg7] leaves the a8-knight alive to escape and lets White centralise with [Qc5], consolidating a full extra rook while Black's attack fizzles out. Trap the knight with [b6] first. }
11. Qc5 e4 12. Ne2 ) 11. d3 ( 11. Nf3 $2
{ Putting the knight on f3 steals the d5-queen's vital escape square and walks straight into [Bb7 d3 e4] (or [Nd4]), smashing White's kingside. Play [d3] first and keep f3 free for the queen. [%csl Rf3][%cal Rc8b7] }
11... Bb7 12. d3 Nd4 ) ) ( 3. g3
{ The Mieses Variation, championed by Jacques Mieses and Vassily Smyslov, prepares to fianchetto White's bishop on g2 to press e4 and d5 along the long light diagonal while keeping [w:Nge2] and [w:f4] in reserve. Black's sharpest equaliser is striking immediately in the centre with [d5] before the bishop reaches g2; [Bc5] and [Nc6] lead to slower positional play. [%csl Gg2,Ge4,Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] }
3... d5
{ The principled central strike against Mieses's [g3]: Black challenges e4 immediately before White's bishop reaches g2. White must trade [exd5] ([Nxd5] [Bg2]) to open the long h1-a8 diagonal against Black's centralised knight. Ignoring d5 to play [Bg2] lets Black push [Bg2 d4 Nb5 Bg4] (or [d4 Nce2 c5]), seizing a cramping pawn wedge that blunts the g2-bishop. [%csl Ge4,Gg2,Gh1,Ga8][%cal Ge4d5] }
( 3... Bc5
{ Black develops the dark-squared bishop onto the active a7-g1 diagonal before committing the d-pawn, eyeing d4 and discouraging an early [f4]. White completes the fianchetto with [Bg2], reinforcing e4 and d5 along the long diagonal ahead of [w:Nge2], [w:d3] and short castling, ready to meet [b:d6] with [w:Na4] if Black leaves the c5-bishop without a retreat square. After [Bg2 Nc6 Nge2], White prepares [w:h3], [w:Kh2] and [w:f4] to launch a kingside pawn break with the g2-bishop supporting the centre from afar. [%csl Ga7,Gg1,Gd4,Ge4][%cal Gf1g2] }
4. Bg2
{ With White's bishop on g2 reinforcing e4 and d5 along the long diagonal, Black develops smoothly with [Nc6] ([Nge2 O-O O-O a6 h3], preparing [w:Kh2] and [w:f4]), [O-O] or [d6]. Never jump prematurely to g4 with [Ng4]: because Black's d7-pawn still blocks the c8-bishop, the knight on g4 is completely unguarded and drops outright to [Ng4 Qxg4]. [%csl Gg2,Ge4,Gd5,Gg4][%cal Gb8c6] }
4... Nc6 ( 4... O-O ) ( 4... d6 ) ( 4... Ng4 $4
{ Attacking f2 before White has weakened anything backfires badly: [Qxg4] wins a full knight outright because Black's c8-bishop is still blocked by the d7-pawn. Develop [Nc6] or castle [O-O]. }
5. Qxg4 O-O 6. Qd1 ) ) ( 3... Nc6 ) 4. exd5
{ White trades on d5 to open both the half-open e-file and the h1-a8 long diagonal for the upcoming bishop on g2. Black recaptures [Nxd5], centralising the knight while asking White to spend [Nxd5 Bg2] attacking it; offering a pawn gambit with [c6 dxc6 Nxc6] instead leaves White a clean pawn ahead with a rock-solid fianchetto shelter after [w:Bg2] and [w:Nge2]. Once [Nxd5 Bg2] attacks the knight twice, Black either trades on c3 (giving White the open b-file) or retreats [b:Nb6] into a positional fight. [%csl Gd5,Gh1,Ga8,Gg2][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 4. Bg2 $2
{ Allowing [d4 Nce2 c5 d3 Nc6] gives Black a commanding King's Indian pawn wedge in reverse while White's g2-bishop bites on granite. Trade [exd5] first. [%cal Rd5d4] }
4... d4 5. Nb5 Bg4 ) 4... Nxd5
{ With Black's knight centralised on d5, White completes the fianchetto at once with [Bg2], putting immediate two-piece pressure on d5 from the c3-knight and g2-bishop. That forces Black to decide on the very next move between trading knights on c3 (opening White's b-file) and retreating the knight to b6 while White follows with [w:Nf3], [w:O-O] and [w:Re1]. In the main [Bg2 Nxc3 bxc3 Bd6 Nf3 O-O O-O] line, White uses [w:Re1] and [w:Rb1] to exert annoying pressure along both half-open files. [%csl Gd5,Gc3,Gb6][%cal Gf1g2] }
5. Bg2
{ White's bishop on g2 and knight on c3 attack Black's centralised knight on d5 twice. Black's two sound answers are [Nxc3] ([w:bxc3] [Bd6 Nf3 O-O], giving White the half-open b-file against Black's solid structure) and [Nb6] ([Nf3 Nc6 O-O Be7 Re1], keeping pieces on the board while White pressures e5). Supporting d5 with [Be6 Nf3 Nc6 O-O] requires care against [b:Bc5] [w:Nxe5]. [%csl Gg2,Gc3,Gd5,Ge5][%cal Gd5c3] }
5... Nxc3
{ Black trades off White's knight on c3 to relieve the pressure on d5. White must recapture towards the centre with [bxc3], opening the half-open b-file for [w:Rb1] against b7 while building a sturdy pawn base to support [w:Nf3], [w:O-O], [w:Re1] and a later [w:d4] break. Recapturing away from the centre with [dxc3 Qxd1+ Kxd1] kills White's initiative at once. Once [bxc3] is on the board, White follows up with [w:Nf3], [w:O-O] and [w:Re1], forcing Black to guard e5 carefully before developing the c8-bishop. [%csl Gc3,Gd5,Gb7,Ge5][%cal Gb2c3] }
( 5... Nb6 ) 6. bxc3
{ The signature Mieses tabiya, played by Smyslov: White plans [w:Nf3], [w:O-O], [w:Re1] (putting direct rook pressure on e5 while the g2-bishop sweeps through to c6 and b7) and [w:d3] or [w:d4]. Black defends e5 solidly with [Bd6] ([Nf3] [O-O] [O-O] [Nc6] [w:Re1] [Qf6], avoiding passive [b:Re8] [w:d4]), [Nc6] ([Nf3 Bd6]) or [Bc5]. White's plan after [Bd6 Nf3 O-O O-O Nc6 Re1 Qf6] is [w:d3] and [w:Nd2] (or [w:Rb1]), using the g2-bishop and b1-rook against Black's queenside. [%csl Ge5,Gc6,Gb7][%cal Gf8d6] }
6... Bd6 ( 6... Nc6 ) ( 6... Bc5 ) ) ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. a3
{ The Mengarini Variation of the Vienna Game: White plays a useful waiting move on a3 that rules out [Bb4] pins in advance, acting like a reversed Two Knights or Sicilian with an extra tempo on a3. Black equalises cleanly by striking in the centre immediately with [d5] ([w:exd5] [b:Nxd5] [Qf3] [Be6] [Bc4 c6]), developing [Bc5] ([Nf3 d6]), or mirroring with [Nc6] ([Nf3 d5] Four Knights Scotch). [%csl Ga3][%cal Gd7d5] }
3... d5 ( 3... Bc5 ) ( 3... Nc6 ) ) ( 3. f3 $2
{ Guarding e4 with [f3] blocks White's g1-knight and weakens the a7-g1 diagonal permanently: [Bc5] stops White from castling short while [d5] prepares to open the centre. Play [f4] or [Bc4]. [%csl Re4,Ra7,Rg1][%cal Rf8c5] }
3... Bc5 4. Na4 Be7 ) 3... d5
{ The critical line of the Vienna Gambit (also reachable via the Falkbeer [f4] [d5] [Nc3] [Nf6]): Black meets a flank gambit with an immediate central counter-strike. White's main weapon is [fxe5] ([Nxe4]), clearing the f-file ahead of [w:Qf3] or [w:Nf3]; [exd5] ([e4] or [Nxd5]) is a solid alternative. Never capture with the knight: [Nxd5 Nxe4] drops e4 and exposes h4. [%csl Ge4,Gh4][%cal Gf4e5] }
( 3... exf4
{ Accepting with [exf4] is Black's most common club error against the Vienna Gambit: unlike in the King's Gambit, White's knight already stands on c3 guarding both e4 and d5, so [e5] chases Black's f6-knight all the way back to g8. Delaying [e5] to play [d4 Bb4] or [Nf3 d5] lets Black counter-attack in the centre and throws away White's tempo. [%csl Gc3,Ge4,Gd5,Gg8][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5
{ Every forward square of Black's f6-knight is covered: d5 and e4 by White's knight on c3, g4 and h5 by White's queen on d1. Black's best damage control is swallowing pride and undeveloping immediately to [Ng8]. Trying to pin the pawn with [Qe7 Qe2 Ng8] still forces the knight home while leaving Black's queen blocking f8, and [Nh5 Qxh5] or [Nd5 Nxd5] drops the knight. [%csl Gd5,Ge4,Gc3,Gg4][%cal Gf6g8] }
( 4. d4 $6
{ Grabbing d4 before kicking the f6-knight lets Black pin with [Bb4] and counter-attack e4. Push [e5] immediately while the f6-knight has no squares. [%csl Rd4,Re4][%cal Rf8b4] }
