{ Black's knight sits back on g8, leaving Black with zero pieces developed while White's e5-pawn cramps the centre. One vital accuracy from White: develop [Nf3] first to cover h4. Rushing to grab the centre with [d4] before [Nf3] lets Black check with [d4 Qh4+ Ke2 d6 Nf3 Bg4], displacing White's king and spoiling a clean positional bind. Once [Nf3] shuts down h4, White follows with [w:d4] and [w:Bxf4], enjoying a three-piece development lead and complete central command. [%csl Gg8,Gh4][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 4... Nh5 $4
{ Forgetting that White has not played [Nf3] yet: so White's d1-queen still sees h5. [Qxh5] wins a full knight for nothing. Retreat [Ng8]. [%csl Rh5][%cal Rd1h5] }
