{ Jumping to d5 walks into White's c3-knight: [Nxd5] wins a piece (since [Qh4+ Ke2] leaves Black a whole knight down with zero follow-up). Retreat [Ng8]. [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc3d5] }
5. Nxd5 Qh4+ 6. Ke2 ) ( 4... Qe7
{ After mistakenly accepting the Vienna Gambit, Black tries to avoid retreating the f6-knight to g8 by pinning White's e5-pawn against the king with [Qe7]. White breaks the pin immediately with [Qe2], renewing the threat to take on f6 while leaving Black's queen awkwardly blocking her own dark-squared bishop on f8 and exposed to a future [w:Nd5] jump. Even after [Qe2 Ng8], Black's queen on e7 remains a tactical liability that invites [w:Nf3] and [w:Nd5] forks against c7. [%csl Gg8,Gf6,Gf8,Ge7][%cal Gd1e2] }
5. Qe2
{ With the e5-pawn unpinned by White's queen on e2, Black's knight on f6 still has zero safe forward squares (d5 and e4 are guarded by c3, g4 and h5 by White's queen) and is forced all the way home to [Ng8]. Compared with retreating on move four, Black has only managed to bottle up the f8-bishop behind the queen. Once [Ng8] retreats, White develops [w:Nf3] and threatens [w:Nd5] against the queen and c7, punishing Black's early queen sortie. [%csl Ge2,Gf6,Gd5,Ge4][%cal Gf6g8] }
5... Ng8
{ A dream anti-gambit picture for White: Black's knight has returned to g8 and Black's queen stands on e7 blocking the f8-bishop. White develops [Nf3] (covering h4 while threatening [w:Nd5] against the queen and c7, setting a queen-winning trap if Black plays [Nf3 d6]) or pushes [d4] at once to open the c1-bishop against f4. Watch how lethal [Nf3] is here: if Black plays [b:d6] on autopilot to free the c8-bishop, [w:Nd5] wins Black's queen or rook by force. [%csl Gg8,Ge7,Gh4,Gc7][%cal Gg1f3] }
