{ White develops [Nf3] and threatens [Nd5], hitting both Black's queen on e7 and the pawn on c7. Black is already much worse and must sidestep the fork with [Qe6] ([d4] [Bb4] [Qe4]) or cover d5 via [c6]. Natural-looking [d6] loses Black's queen on the spot: after [d6 Nd5 Qd8 Nxc7+ Qxc7 exd6+], the discovered check down the e-file picks up the queen. [%csl Ge7,Gc7,Gd5][%cal Ge7e6] }
( 6. d4 ) 6... Qe6 ( 6... d6 $4
{ Playing [d6] walks into a queen-winning fork: [Nd5] hits both the e7-queen and c7, and if [Qd8], [Nxc7+] [Qxc7] [exd6] wins Black's queen by discovered check. Sidestep the fork with [Qe6] instead. [%csl Rc7][%cal Rc3d5] }
