{ The passive Philidor-style decline of the Vienna Gambit: Black holds e5 firmly at the price of locking the dark-squared bishop on f8 behind d6. White develops [Nf3], covering h4 while renewing the threat to e5 rather than trading [fxe5 dxe5] and freeing Black's bishop; once Black defends e5 with [Nf3 Nc6], White pins with [w:Bb5]. Once [Nf3 Nc6 Bb5] is on the board, White enjoys all the pressure of a Ruy Lopez with the f-pawn already advanced to open the f-file. [%csl Ge5,Gf8,Gd6,Gh4][%cal Gg1f3] }
4. Nf3
{ With h4 secured by [Nf3], White genuinely threatens [fxe5] to win a central pawn. Black reinforces e5 with [Nc6] (the main line, met by [Nc6 Bb5 Bd7 d3]), [Nbd7] ([Bc4 Be7 d3 O-O O-O], keeping the c-pawn free while accepting a cramped position), or [exf4] ([d4 Be7] [w:Bxf4] [O-O] [Qd2] with opposite-side castling chances). Against [Nc6], pinning with [w:Bb5] ties Black down to e5 while White castles short, plays [w:d3] and prepares [w:fxe5] or [w:f5] on White's own terms. [%csl Gh4,Ge5][%cal Gb8c6] }
4... Nc6
{ Against Black's passive [d6] and [Nc6] setup, White pins the c6-knight with [Bb5], creating a charged Ruy Lopez structure where the f-pawn is already at f4. After [Bb5 Bd7 d3 Be7 O-O O-O], White keeps central tension until ready to trade on c6 or tuck the bishop to a4: avoid rushing [Bb5 Bd7 Bxc6 bxc6 fxe5 dxe5 Nxe5], where [b:Qd4+] forks g1 and e5. [%csl Gf4,Gc6,Ga4,Gg1][%cal Gf1b5] }
( 4... Nbd7 ) ( 4... exf4 ) 5. Bb5 ) 4. fxe5
{ White captures on e5, opening the half-open f-file while attacking Black's knight on f6. Black's principled recapture is [Nxe4], planting the knight in the heart of the board with immediate threats of [b:Qh4+] and [b:Nxc3]. Counter-attacking c3 with [d4] instead fails badly to [d4 exf6 dxc3 bxc3 Qxf6 Nf3], leaving White a clean pawn up with easy development. Once [Nxe4] lands, White must cover h4 or attack the knight immediately with [w:Qf3], [w:Nf3] or [w:d3]. [%csl Ge5,Gf6,Gc3,Gh4][%cal Gf6e4] }
( 4. exd5
{ White captures on d5 instead of e5. Black gets active play either by advancing [e4] (transposing into Staunton's Falkbeer line [f4] [d5] [exd5] [e4] [Nc3] [Nf6], where White undermines e4 with [w:d3]) or by recapturing [Nxd5] ([Nxd5] [Qxd5 Nf3 Nc6], reaching balanced open play where Black's centralised queen cannot be chased by a knight). Because d5 is already open, this branch avoids the sharp [fxe5] [b:Nxe4] complications and leads to clear central piece play. [%csl Gd5,Ge5,Ge4][%cal Ge5e4] }
