{ Black centralises the knight on d5 while keeping e5 defended. White equalises cleanly with [Nxd5 Qxd5 Nf3 Nc6] or [Nf3 Nc6 Bb5]. Avoid the tempting pawn grab [fxe5]: Black trades [fxe5 Nxc3 bxc3] first to open the diagonal and then fires [b:Qh4+], where [w:g3] [b:Qe4+] forks White's king and the h1-rook. After [Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 Nxc3 bxc3 Bd6], White castles short and uses the open e- and f-files to press Black's kingside. [%csl Gd5,Ge5][%cal Gc3d5] }
5. Nxd5 ( 5. Nf3 ) ) ) ( 4. Nxd5 $2
{ Capturing on d5 with the knight leaves e4 undefended: [Nxe4 d3 Qxd5] wins a central pawn while threatening [Bc5] and [Qh4] against White's loose kingside. Capture [fxe5] or [w:exd5] with a pawn. [%csl Rd5,Re4][%cal Rf6e4] }
4... Nxe4 5. d3 Qxd5 ) 4... Nxe4
{ The main crossroads of the Vienna Gambit. Because [b:Qh4+] is threatened along the open diagonal, White chooses between three classic continuations: Paulsen's modern main line [Qf3] (attacking e4 and f7 while guarding g3), Steinitz's classical [Nf3] (covering h4 solidly), and the Oxford Variation [d3] (setting the 1930 Würzburger Trap). Trading [Nxe4 dxe4] hands Black space and easy play. [%csl Ge4,Gf7,Gg3,Gh4][%cal Gd1f3] }
( 4... d4 $2
{ Counter-attacking c3 fails to [exf6 dxc3 bxc3 Qxf6 Nf3], leaving White a clean pawn up with easy development and the open f-file. Recapture [Nxe4]. [%csl Rc3][%cal Re5f6] }
5. exf6 dxc3 6. bxc3 ) 5. Qf3
{ Paulsen's 5.[Qf3] is the modern main line of the Vienna Gambit: White attacks Black's knight on e4 and eyes f7 down the half-open f-file, while guarding g3 in advance so [Qh4+ g3] carries no threat to h1. Black has three sound master defences: [Nc6] (ignoring e4 to counter-strike with [b:Nd4]), classical [Nxc3], and Bardeleben's [f5]. Avoid [Bc5 Nxe4] and [Ng5 Qxd5]. [%csl Ge4,Gf7,Gg3,Gh1][%cal Gb8c6] }
( 5. Nf3
{ Steinitz's classical Knight Variation covers h4 solidly and prepares [w:d3] or [w:Qe2] to challenge Black's outpost on e4. Black's cleanest equaliser is [Bc5], threatening [b:Bf2+] to provoke [Bc5 d4], which opens the a5-e1 diagonal for [b:Bb4] against c3; [Be7] ([d4] [O-O] [Bd3 f5]), [Bg4] ([Qe2]) and [Nc6] ([Bb5+]) are all sound too. Against [Be7], White plays [w:d4] and [w:Bd3], aiming to trade off or evict Black's e4-knight once short castling secures the king. [%csl Gh4,Ge4,Ga5,Ge1][%cal Gf8c5] }
5... Bc5
{ Black's [Bc5] is a clean equalising recipe against 5.[Nf3]: by eyeing f2 with both bishop and knight, Black threatens [b:Bf2+] ([w:Ke2]) and [b:Nf2], practically forcing White to shut the diagonal down with [d4]. Once White plays [d4], the a5-e1 diagonal opens toward White's king, letting Black swing the bishop across to b4 with tempo. After [d4 Bb4 Bd2], Black trades on d2 and develops [b:Be6] and [b:Be7], preparing [b:c5] and [b:f6] to chip away at White's centre. [%csl Gf2,Ga5,Ge1,Gb4][%cal Gd2d4] }
( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... Bg4
{ Black pins White's f3-knight to the queen so [b:Nc6] and [b:Nd4] loom. White breaks the pin accurately with [Qe2] ([Nxc3 dxc3 c6 Bf4 Be7] [w:O-O-O]), unpinning f3 while attacking Black's e4-knight twice to force a trade on c3. Avoid premature [d4 Bb4], and never kick the bishop with [h3 Bxf3 Qxf3 Qh4+], which drops the exchange via [w:g3] [b:Nxg3]. [%csl Gf3,Gc3][%cal Gd1e2] }
6. Qe2
{ White's [Qe2] unpins the f3-knight and attacks Black's e4-knight twice. Black's only clean equaliser is trading knights first with [Nxc3] ([dxc3] [c6] [Bf4] [Be7] [w:O-O-O] [Nd7], keeping a compact position against White's open d-file). Surrendering the bishop with [Bxf3 Qxf3] hands White the bishop pair, and leaving e4 hanging with [Bc5 Nxe4] drops a central pawn. [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge4c3] }
( 6. d4 $2
{ Opening the a5-e1 diagonal while f3 is pinned invites [Bb4 Bd2 Nxd2 Qxd2 Bxf3 gxf3 Qh4+], wrecking White's pawn structure and king safety. Unpin with [Qe2] first. }
