{ The tactical point behind 5.[Bc5]: instead of retreating passively to b6 (which blocks the b-pawn and lets White consolidate d4 and e5), Black swings the bishop across to [Bb4], pinning c3 and threatening [b:Nxc3]. After [Bb4 Bd2 Nxd2 Qxd2 Be6 Bd3 Be7], Black castles comfortably and undermines White's centre with [b:c5] and [b:f6]. [%csl Gb6,Gd4,Ge5,Gc3][%cal Gc5b4] }
6... Bb4 ) ( 5. d3
{ The Oxford Variation attacks Black's e4-knight directly with [d3] and sets the famous 1930 Würzburger Trap against anyone who checks on h4 ([Qh4+ g3 Nxg3 Nf3 Qh5 Nxd5]). Black's accurate equaliser is trading knights first with [Nxc3] ([w:bxc3] [d4], clamping down on White's doubled c-pawns) or pinning with [Bb4] ([dxe4] [Bxc3+] [w:bxc3] [Qh4+]). [%csl Gh4][%cal Ge4c3] }
