{ In the Oxford Variation, Black sidesteps the Würzburger Trap by trading knights on c3 first, doubling White's queenside pawns before pushing [b:d4]. White recaptures [bxc3], opening the half-open b-file and aiming to build a broad pawn centre with [w:d4] and [w:Nf3] unless Black blocks d4 immediately on the next move. Once [bxc3] is played, Black's best plan is pushing [b:d4] immediately to clamp down on c3 and stop White from building a broad pawn centre. [%csl Gc3,Gd4][%cal Gb2c3] }
( 5... Qh4+
{ Black falls into the 1930 Würzburger Trap: assuming [Qh4+] wins material via the h-file pin just as in the King's Gambit, Black checks while the e4-knight is already under attack by d3. White calmly blocks with [g3], attacking the queen so Black is forced into [g3 Nxg3 Nf3], where White's knights turn the tables. Because both the e4-knight and h4-queen end up attacked at once after [g3], Black is dragged by force into the Würzburger Trap. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gg2g3] }
6. g3
{ White's pawn on g3 blocks the check and attacks Black's queen on h4 at the same time that White's d3-pawn attacks Black's knight on e4. With two pieces hanging at once, Black has no choice left but to capture [Nxg3], counting on the h-file pin against White's rook on h1 to save the knight. After [Nxg3], White does not recapture on g3 (which is pinned to h1), but develops [w:Nf3] with tempo on the queen first. [%csl Gg3,Gh4,Ge4,Gh1][%cal Ge4g3] }
6... Nxg3
{ Because White's h2-pawn is pinned to the corner rook on h1, recapturing [hxg3 Qxh1] would drop the rook with tempo against g1. Instead White plays the crucial intermezzo [Nf3], developing with gain of time against Black's queen on h4 so Black must spend a move keeping the h-file pin alive while White prepares [w:Nxd5]. Once [Nf3] forces Black's queen to slide to h5 to keep the h-file pin alive, [w:Nxd5] hits c7 and springs the trap. [%csl Gh1,Gg1,Gh4,Gh5][%cal Gg1f3] }
7. Nf3
{ In the Würzburger Trap line, [Nf3] attacks Black's queen on h4 while White's h2-pawn remains pinned to h1. Black's only move to keep the g3-knight alive is sliding to [Qh5], preserving the h-file pin. Stepping off the h-file with [Qg4 Rg1] (or [Qg4 hxg3]) loses a piece, and counter-pinning with [Bg4 Nxh4] hangs Black's queen. [%csl Gh4,Gh1][%cal Gh4h5] }
( 7. hxg3 $2
{ Forgetting the pin along the h-file drops White's h1-rook with tempo to [Qxh1] (threatening [Qxg1]). Interpose [Nf3] first to chase the queen. [%cal Rh4h1] }
