9. Nf4 Qh6 10. Ne2 ) ) 6. bxc3
{ With White's pawns doubled on c2 and c3, Black's strongest move is [d4], physically stopping White from playing [d4] while fixing the c3-pawn on a half-open file and eyeing [b:Qd5] or [b:Nc6]. Developing [Be7] or [c5] ([Nf3 Nc6]) is solid as well, though both allow White to establish [w:d4] and [w:Bd3] for kingside pressure. If Black plays [d4], White must resist [w:cxd4] [b:Qxd4] (which forks a1 and e5) and develop [w:Nf3] or [w:Bb2] calmly instead. [%csl Gc2,Gc3,Ga1,Ge5][%cal Gd5d4] }
