[Event "King's Gambit (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. f4
{ The King's Gambit, the signature attacking weapon of romantic chess from Ruy Lopez in the sixteenth century to Anderssen, Morphy and Spassky. White offers the f-pawn on move two to deflect e5 from the centre, aiming to occupy d4 and e4 with pawns, develop rapidly and use the half-open f-file against f7 after castling short. The drawback is a lasting draught along the e1-h4 and a7-g1 diagonals toward White's king. Accepting with [exf4] is the sharpest test; [Bc5] and [d5] are the principal ways to decline, while [Nc6] and [d6] hold e5 more quietly. [%csl Ge5,Gd4,Ge4,Gf7][%cal Ge5f4] }
2... exf4
{ The King's Gambit Accepted: Fischer famously wrote that the way to refute a gambit is to start by taking the pawn. Black captures on f4 and immediately threatens [b:Qh4+], using the open e1-h4 diagonal where [g3] would drop the h1-rook to [b:fxg3]. White has two serious continuations: the main line [Nf3] (covering h4 while developing) and the Bishop's Gambit [Bc4] (vacating f1 for [Qh4+] [Kf1]). [%csl Gf4,Ge1,Gh4,Gf1][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 2... Bc5 ) ( 2... d5 3. exd5
{ White accepts the countergambit pawn on d5 and asks Black to justify sacrificing the centre. Black has three main systems: the modern master choice [exf4], which reaches the Modern KGA while ruling out White's 3.[Bc4] Bishop's Gambit; the Nimzowitsch-Marshall [c6], offering a second pawn to open queenside lines; and Staunton's classical push [e4], clamping down on f3 to hinder White's king's knight. Across all three Falkbeer branches, Black trades a pawn for time and open central files, so White's priority is covering h4 and neutralising the a7-g1 diagonal. [%csl Gd5,Gf3,Gh4,Ga7][%cal Ge5e4] }
3... e4
{ Staunton's Classical Falkbeer sacrifices the d5-pawn to plant an advanced thorn on e4, taking the natural f3 square away from White's knight so [b:Qh4+] stays a constant threat. White's main antidote is Rubinstein's [d3], undermining the e4-spearhead right away before Black's bishops and queen swarm the kingside; [Nc3 Nf6 d3] is an equally reliable move order. Because the e4-pawn cuts White's camp in half and stops [Nf3], White's entire strategy is to attack and trade off e4 before Black castles and brings a rook to e8. [%csl Ge4,Gf3,Ge8][%cal Gd2d3] }
( 3... exf4
{ The Modern Transfer, championed by Tarrasch and Keres: Black trades off e4 so White can never build the ideal d4 and e4 central pawn duo, while creating the immediate threat of [b:Qh4+] to dictate White's next move. White must cover h4 with [Nf3] (transposing into the Modern Defense of the KGA) or check [Bb5+] ([Bd7 Qe2+]); skipping [Nf3] invites a painful check on h4. [%csl Ge4,Gd4,Gh4][%cal Gg1f3] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. Bb5+ ) ( 4. Nc3 $2
{ Developing [Nc3] leaves h4 unguarded: [Qh4+ g3 fxg3 Qe2+ Be7] wrecks White's kingside via the h1-pin (and [Ke2 Bg4+ Nf3 Nd7] hunts the king). Play [Nf3] first. [%csl Rh4][%cal Rd8h4] }
4... Qh4+ 5. Ke2 Bg4+ ) ( 4. c4 $2
{ Defending d5 before guarding h4 invites [Qh4+ Ke2 Bg4+ Nf3 Nd7] with queenside castling and a fierce attack against White's stranded king. Play [Nf3] first. [%csl Rd5,Rh4][%cal Rd8h4] }
4... Qh4+ 5. Ke2 Bg4+ ) ( 4. Bc4 $2
{ Giving the king f1 still concedes castling rights after [Qh4+ Kf1 Bd6 Nf3 Qh5], where Black consolidates f4 effortlessly. Play [Nf3] instead. [%csl Rf1,Rf4][%cal Rd8h4] }
4... Qh4+ 5. Kf1 Bd6 ) ) ( 3... c6
{ The Nimzowitsch-Marshall Countergambit offers a second pawn on c6 to open the queenside files for Black's knights and bishops while setting a trap on e5. White's best answers are [Nc3] (developing while keeping central tension), Keres's [Qe2] (pinning e5 to the king), and [Nf3]. Never grab [fxe5], which loses the h1-rook on the spot to [fxe5 Qh4+ g3 Qe4+]. Accepting the second pawn with [dxc6 Nxc6] is playable too, though White must then defend accurately with [w:Bb5] while Black's bishops rake the board. [%csl Gc6,Ge5][%cal Gb1c3] }
4. Nc3
{ The main line against the Nimzowitsch-Marshall Countergambit: White develops a piece and supports d5 without blocking the f1-bishop. Black's cleanest equaliser is [exf4], creating the immediate threat of [b:Qh4+] to force [exf4 Nf3] before recovering d5 with [b:Nf6] or [b:Bd6]. Recapturing [cxd5] right away is playable too, though [cxd5 fxe5 d4] gives White a strong central bind. After [exf4 Nf3], the position turns into an open IQP battle where White's queenside pawn majority faces Black's active minor pieces. [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge5f4] }
( 4. Qe2
{ Keres's sharp pin freezes Black's e5-pawn against the king on e8 along the half-open e-file, so Black can neither capture [exf4] nor push [e4]. Black's best reply is recapturing [cxd5], reclaiming a central foothold and daring White's queen to capture on e5 in front of her own uncastled king, where Black will gain rapid developing tempi with [b:Be7], [b:Nc6] and [b:Nf6]. Pinning along the e-file takes the sting out of Black's gambit and steers toward clear, open structures where Black's isolated d5-pawn can be targeted. [%csl Ge8,Ge5][%cal Gc6d5] }
4... cxd5
{ Black recovers the d5-pawn and offers e5 as bait to lure White's queen onto the open e-file in front of her own king. White's cleanest continuation is [fxe5] ([Nc6 Nf3 Bg4 Nc3]), keeping the queen shielded behind the e5-pawn while playing against Black's isolated pawn on d5. Taking with the queen via [Qxe5+] wins the pawn with check, but hands Black fast piece play after [Be7]. In the [fxe5 Nc6 Nf3] main line, White unblocks the f1-bishop with [w:d4] or [w:g3] and [w:Bg2], pressing d5 while keeping the extra pawn. [%csl Ge5,Gd5][%cal Gf4e5] }
5. fxe5 ( 5. Qxe5+
{ White captures the central pawn with check, accepting a congested e-file where the queen blocks the f1-bishop while White's king sits behind her on e1. Black interposes [Be7] to shield the king, prepare [b:Nf6], [b:O-O] and [b:Nc6] with tempo on the queen, and set a queen trap on g7; [Ne7] aiming for [b:Nbc6] and [b:Bg4] is a sharp alternative. Because White's queen on e5 stands in front of the uncastled king on e1, White must develop with tempo on the next move rather than grab more pawns. [%csl Ge1,Gg7,Ge5][%cal Gf8e7] }
5... Be7
{ Black shields the king and prepares [b:Nf6], [b:O-O] and [b:Nc6] to use the open e-file against White's queen and king. White should develop with tempo via [Bb5+] ([Nc6 Nf3 Nf6]) or cover h4 and e5 with [Nf3] ([Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Nbxd7] [O-O]). Never snatch the poisoned pawn on g7: [Qxg7 Bf6 Qg3 Bh4] pins White's queen to the king on e1 and wins her. After [Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6], White aims to trade dark-squared bishops or castle quickly so Black's [b:O-O] and [b:Re8] never catch the queen and king aligned. [%csl Gh4,Ge5,Gg7,Ge1][%cal Gf1b5] }
( 5... Ne7 ) 6. Bb5+ ( 6. Qxg7 $4
{ A 2300-level tournament victim fell right into this trap: [Bf6] chases the queen to her only safe square [Qg3], and [Bh4+] pins her to the e1-king, winning the queen. Develop [Nf3] or [Bb5+] instead. [%csl Rg7,Re1][%cal Re7f6] }
6... Bf6 7. Qg3 Bh4 ) ) ) ( 4. Nf3
{ Reached from both [d5] [exd5] [c6] [Nf3] and [c6] [Nf3] [d5] [exd5]: White guards h4 early, which allows Black's thematic space push [e4]. Advancing [e4] kicks the f3-knight away from its natural square and builds a sturdy d5-e4 pawn chain once [e4 Ne5 cxd5] follows. White will then chip at the head of Black's chain with [w:d3] while keeping a close eye on the a7-g1 diagonal. Once Black pushes [e4 Ne5 cxd5], White's knight on e5 is active but Black's central chain cramps White's pieces until [w:Bb5+] and [w:d3] break it up. [%csl Gh4,Ga7,Gg1,Ge5][%cal Ge5e4] }
4... e4
{ Black's e4-pawn evicts the knight from f3 and claims central dark squares. White must leap forward to [Ne5], pressing c6 and f7 from the middle of the board; retreating the knight to g1 or d4 surrenders the initiative completely and leaves White's kingside cramped and vulnerable along both the e1-h4 and a7-g1 diagonals while Black recaptures on d5. From e5, White's knight controls c6, d7 and f7, buying the time White needs to check on b5 and chip at Black's pawn chain with [w:d3]. [%csl Gf3,Gc6,Gf7,Gg1][%cal Gf3e5] }
