{ Defending e5 with [f6] repeats Damiano's classic mistake in an even looser setting: the pawn on f6 strips Black's king along the e8-h5 diagonal and takes the g8-knight's best square away. White's strongest punishment is developing [Nf3] first, covering h4 while threatening [fxe5] and [Bc4] so Black has no counter-play. Immediate [fxe5] is tempting too: most club players recapture [fxe5] and lose a rook to [Qh5+], though [fxe5 Nc6] lets Black gambit the pawn for piece activity. Because f6 deprives Black's knight of its natural home and weakens the light squares around e8, White plays to pry open the e- and f-files before Black can hide the king. [%csl Ge5,Gf6,Ge8,Gh5][%cal Gg1f3] }
