{ The King's Knight's Gambit has been the main road of the King's Gambit since the sixteenth century. Developing the knight to f3 shuts down [Qh4+] and threatens to seize the whole centre with [w:d4] followed by [w:Bxf4]. Black chooses between two philosophies: holding the extra f4-pawn behind a kingside wall ([g5], Fischer's [d6], or Becker's [h6]), or counter-attacking with [d5], Schallopp's [Nf6], Cunningham's [Be7], or [Ne7]. [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg7g5] }
( 3. Bc4
{ The Bishop's Gambit, a favourite of Anderssen, Bogoljubow, Fischer (who switched to it as White to sidestep his own [d6] defence) and Judit Polgar. Developing the bishop first vacates f1 so the king can meet [Qh4+] with [Kf1], avoids [g5] and [g4] kicking a knight off f3 with tempo, and answers [d5] directly. Black's main replies are Cozio's [Nf6], Bledow's [d5], and [Qh4+]. In all lines of the Bishop's Gambit, White's goal is to establish [w:d4], regain f4 with the c1-bishop, and use [w:Nf3] and [w:Nc3] to dominate the central squares. [%csl Gf1,Gf3,Gf4][%cal Gg8f6] }
