[Event "King's Gambit (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 ( 2... Bc5 3. Nf3 d6 4. c3
{ Marshall's [c3] is White's most testing plan against the Classical Declined: White prepares [d4] in one burst to shut down the a7-g1 diagonal with tempo on the c5-bishop, build an ideal central pawn duo and unlock short castling. Black's main reply is [Nf6], counter-attacking e4 as soon as [d4] lands; [Bg4] pins the knight that supports d4, and Svenonius's [f5] challenges White's centre from the flank. [%csl Ga7,Gg1,Ge4,Gd4][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6
{ The main line of the Classical KGD: Black develops with a direct attack on e4 so White cannot hesitate. White answers on principle with [d4], seizing the centre with tempo on the c5-bishop and opening the c1-bishop's diagonal; [fxe5 dxe5 d4] reaches similar open play. Leaving e4 undefended to develop [Bc4] allows [Bc4 Nxe4], where [Bxf7+] [b:Kxf7] [fxe5] [Nf2] leaves Black winning material. Once [d4 exd4 cxd4] lands, White reclaims full castling rights and owns a proud central pawn duo while Black counter-attacks e4 and d4. [%csl Ge4,Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] }
( 4... Bg4
{ Black pins the f3-knight to undermine White's control of d4 and e4. White's sharpest continuation is [fxe5 dxe5 Qa4+], using the diagonal opened by [c3] to fork Black's king on e8 and the undefended bishop on g4. Quieter [Be2] breaks the pin solidly ahead of [d4], and [h3] forces the bishop to declare its intentions at once ([Bxf3] [Bxf3], watching h4 and g1). Notice how [c3] works double duty here: it prepares the [w:d4] break and vacates the d1-a4 diagonal so White's queen can punish Black's loose bishop on g4. [%csl Gd4,Ge4,Ge8,Gg4][%cal Gf4e5] }
5. fxe5
{ White opens the f-file and clears the d1-a4 diagonal to prepare [w:Qa4+] against Black's undefended bishop on g4. Black recaptures [dxe5] to keep material level and preserve the bishop's pressure down the a7-g1 diagonal. Trading [Bxf3 Qxf3 dxe5] first would surrender the bishop pair unprovoked and let White follow with [w:Bc4] targeting f7 along both the diagonal and the f-file. Once Black recaptures [dxe5], White springs [w:Qa4+] immediately to force the g4-bishop back to d7 before Black can pile up on the pinned f3-knight. [%csl Gd1,Ga4,Gg4,Ga7][%cal Gd6e5] }
