{ Black checks on h4, assuming White's kingside is collapsing because [g3] normally drops the corner rook on h1 to [b:fxg3]. White calmly interposes [g3] anyway: because Black's g-pawn advanced to g4 where it sits undefended, [g3 fxg3 Qxg4] counter-attacks Black's queen on h4 while keeping the h2-pawn shielding h1. Stepping up with [Ke2] instead would be fatal. That hidden counter-attack on g4 is why [Nc3] on move four turned [g4] and [Qh4+] from a refutation into a double-edged trap. [%csl Gh4,Gh1,Gg4][%cal Gg2g3] }
