[Event "King's Gambit (3)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 ( 2... Bc5
{ The Classical King's Gambit Declined is a favourite of players who want active piece play without memorising twenty moves of [exf4] tactics. By placing the bishop on the a7-g1 diagonal on move two, Black prevents White from castling short and keeps e5 intact. White's essential move is [Nf3], covering h4 while attacking e5. Grabbing [fxe5] falls into the notorious Idiot Trap [fxe5 Qh4+], which mates after [Ke2] [b:Qxe4#] or wins the h1-rook after [g3] [b:Qxe4+]. [%csl Ga7,Gg1,Ge5,Gh4][%cal Gg1f3] }
3. Nf3
{ With h4 secured by the knight on f3, White genuinely threatens [fxe5]. Black's standard and strongest reply is [d6], holding the e5-point firmly while keeping the c5-bishop trained down the a7-g1 diagonal so White cannot castle short. Accepting a move late with [exf4 d4] hands White a free central tempo against the bishop, while [Nf6 fxe5 Nxe4 d4] gives White a mobile centre and open f-file. Once [d6] bolsters e5, White's central task is neutralising the c5-bishop along the a7-g1 diagonal so the king can castle short onto the f-file. [%csl Gh4,Gf3,Ga7,Gg1][%cal Gd7d6] }
( 3. Qh5
{ White's early queen sortie eyes e5 and f7, hoping Black panics or weakens the kingside. Black refutes the raid cleanly with [Nc6], defending e5 with a piece while preparing [Nf6] on the next turn to chase White's exposed queen and seize the initiative ([Nc6 Nf3 d6]). Taking [Bxg1 Rxg1] opens the g-file for White's rook, [Qf6] and [Qe7] drop e5, and [exf4 Qxc5] hangs the c5-bishop outright. [%csl Rh5,Ge5][%cal Gb8c6,Gg8f6] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... Qf6 $2
{ Defending e5 with [Qf6] forgets that e5 is attacked twice once [fxe5] opens lines, and even simpler [Qxe5+ Qxe5 fxe5] wins a clean central pawn into an endgame. Defend e5 with [Nc6] instead. [%csl Re5][%cal Rh5e5,Rf4e5] }
4. Qxe5+ Qxe5 5. fxe5 ) ( 3... Bxg1 $2
{ Trading off Black's active dark-squared bishop for the knight on g1 hands White [Qxe5+ Ne7 Rxg1], winning a central pawn while opening the g-file for White's rook. Defend e5 with [Nc6] instead. [%csl Rg1][%cal Rh5e5,Rh1g1] }
4. Qxe5+ Ne7 5. Rxg1 ) ( 3... Qe7 $2
