4... exd4 5. Bd3 Nf6 6. O-O O-O ) ) ( 2... d5
{ The Falkbeer Countergambit, introduced by Ernst Falkbeer against Anderssen in 1851: instead of defending e5 passively or nursing f4 under fire, Black strikes back in the centre to pry open the e-file before White can castle. White must capture [exd5] (the main line), develop [Nf3], or play [Nc3]. Capturing [fxe5] is the signature club blunder, losing to [fxe5 Qh4+]. [%csl Ge5,Gf4][%cal Ge4d5] }
3. exd5 ( 3. Nf3
{ Blackburne's flexible move order against the Falkbeer covers h4 first and asks Black to clarify the central tension. Black gets comfortable play either by capturing [dxe4] ([Nxe5 Nd7], challenging White's centralised knight before it settles) or by transposing into the Modern Defense of the KGA with [exf4] ([exd5 Nf6]), where both sides hold doubled pawns and race to develop. Delaying the capture on d5 rules out Staunton's [e4] wedge against f3 and keeps the game in open piece-play channels where White's king is safer. [%csl Gh4,Gd5,Gf3][%cal Gd5e4] }
