( 3... exf4 ) 4. Nxe5
{ White's knight sits actively on e5 pressing f7, while Black's advanced pawn on e4 cramps White's kingside. Black neutralises the outpost cleanly with [Nd7] to challenge White's best piece at once, [Bc5] to seize the a7-g1 diagonal and stop short castling, or [Be6] to bolster f7 and d5 together. Prematurely centralising the queen with [Qd4] loses time to [Qd4 c3] and [w:Bc4]. Once Black challenges e5 or develops [Bc5], White's key break is [w:Nc3] followed by [w:Qe2] or [w:d3], trading off the e4-pawn to free the c1-bishop. [%csl Ge5,Gf7,Ge4,Ga7][%cal Gb8d7] }
4... Nd7 ( 4... Bc5
{ Black seizes the a7-g1 diagonal to stop White from castling short and prepares [Nf6] followed by [O-O] or [Qd4]. White's soundest continuations are [Nc3], attacking the e4-pawn immediately to force [Nc3 Nf6] before playing [w:Qe2] or [w:Bc4], and [Bc4] counter-attacking f7 ([Bc4 Nh6 Nc3]). Playing [Qe2] before [Nc3] shuts the f1-bishop in too early. Because Black's bishop on c5 stops [O-O], White wants to trade off the e4-pawn via [Nc3 Nf6 Qe2] and then challenge c5 with [w:Be3] or [w:Na4]. [%csl Ga7,Gg1,Gf7,Gc5][%cal Gb1c3] }
