[Event "Sicilian Defence (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 c5
{ The Sicilian Defence, Black's most popular and most combative answer to [e4]. The pawn on c5 fights for d4 from the side instead of meeting White head-on, so the position is unbalanced from the start. If White opens the centre with [Nf3] and [d4], Black trades the c-pawn for White's d-pawn, keeps two central pawns against one and gets the half-open c-file; White gets space and a lead in development. White usually attacks on the kingside, Black on the queenside. Quieter set-ups such as [c3], the Alapin, or [Nc3], the Closed Sicilian, avoid the theory. It is the choice of more than a third of Lichess players facing [e4]. [%cal Gc5d4,Gg1f3,Gd2d4] }
2. Nf3
{ The knight heads for the centre and prepares [d4], the Open Sicilian, where White gives a centre pawn for Black's c-pawn to gain space and a lead in development. Black now chooses a family of systems. [d6] keeps e5 under control and leads to the Najdorf, the Dragon and the Classical. [Nc6] develops and adds pressure on d4, heading for the Sveshnikov or the Accelerated Dragon. [e6] prepares a flexible set-up with [d5] in reserve, as in the Taimanov and the Kan. Rarer tries are [g6], [a6] and [Nf6]; the pawn moves [e5], [b5] and [f5] just give material away. [%cal Gd7d6,Gb8c6,Ge7e6] }
( 2. Nc3 ) ( 2. c3 ) ( 2. d4 cxd4 3. c3
{ The Smith-Morra Gambit. White gives a pawn for fast development: after [dxc3 Nxc3], White's pieces come quickly to c4 and f3, with the c- and d-files open for the rooks. Accepting is the critical test, but Black must then defend precisely. Declining is also good: [Nf6] reaches an Alapin after [Nf6 e5 Nd5], and [d3] gives the pawn back at once, leaving White's pawn on c3, where it takes the queen's knight's best square. [%cal Gc4f7,Gd1d6] }
3... dxc3
{ Black has accepted the Smith-Morra Gambit. The usual recapture is [Nxc3]: the knight develops toward d5 and b5, and White is a pawn down for a lead in development and the open c- and d-files. [Bc4] first is a sharper try that offers a second pawn: after [Bc4 cxb2 Bxb2], both bishops aim at Black's king, but Black, two pawns up, holds with careful play. Against both, Black's job is the same: develop quickly and keep an eye on f7 and d6. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb1c3,Gg1f3] }
( 3... Nf6
{ The main way to decline the Smith-Morra Gambit without conceding the centre. Rather than taking on c3 and handing White's queen's knight an open board, Black develops with tempo against e4. White has only one testing continuation, [e5], kicking the knight to d5 and recapturing [e5 Nd5 cxd4] on the next turn: that reaches the main line of the [Nf6] Alapin, where White's space faces Black's blockade on d5. [%csl Yc3,Ye4,Ye5][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5 ) ( 3... d3
{ Black gives the pawn back with [d3], so that the c-pawn can't take back and the Morra's open files never appear. White takes, usually with [Bxd3], and keeps a small lead in development, but the pawn on c3 now takes the best square from the queen's knight. Black develops with [Nc6], [g6] and [Bg7], or [e6] and [Nf6], and gets an easy game. It is a practical choice against players who know the Morra well. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gf1d3,Gd1d3] }
4. Bxd3 ( 4. Qxd3 ) ( 4. c4
{ The Dubois Variation against Black's [d3] push in the Smith-Morra Declined! By returning the pawn onto d3, Black clogged the d-file; instead of capturing it immediately, White locks down a Maroczy Bind first with [c4] so Black can never free the position with [b:d5]! The d3-pawn isn't going anywhere—White will scoop it up next move with [Bxd3] while enjoying a dominant grip on d5. Black fights back on the dark squares via [Nc6], [g6], [Bg7] and [d6], eyeing the d4 hole. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6,Gg7g6,Rf1d3] }
4... Nc6 ( 4... d6 ) ( 4... g6 ) ) ) ( 3... e5
{ Black declines the Smith-Morra by bolstering d4 with [e5], opening the f8-bishop's diagonal at the cost of a permanent backward d-pawn and a hole on d5. Developing [Nf3] first is sharpest, hitting e5 so [Nf3 Nc6 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4] (or [w:Bc4]) targets f7 and d5 with tempo. [cxd4] right away is also good: [cxd4 exd4 Nf3] (or [w:Qxd4] [b:Nc6] [w:Qe3]) recovers the pawn with easy piece play. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gg1f3,Gc3d4] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. cxd4 ) ) ( 3... d5
{ The Scandinavian Formation declines the Smith-Morra by striking straight back in the centre, a practical weapon recommended by Yasser Seirawan to steer gambit players into calmer waters. [exd5] is White's only way to fight for an edge: after [exd5 Qxd5 cxd4], the game transposes into the main line of the [b:d5] Alapin, where White gets an isolated queen's pawn and active piece play with [w:Nf3] and [w:Nc3]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ge4d5,Gd8d5] }
4. exd5 ) 4. Nxc3
{ The Smith-Morra Gambit accepted. White is a pawn down but ahead in development, with two open files for the rooks and the knight already on c3. White's set-up is almost always the same: [Nf3], [Bc4], castling, [Qe2] and the rooks to d1 and c1. Black usually develops with [Nc6], [d6], [e6], [Nf6] and the bishop on e7, or with [a6] and the knight to e7. Black's job is to survive the opening safely: after that, the extra pawn often counts in the endgame. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb8c6,Gd7d6,Ge7e6] }
( 4. Nf3
{ The Danish Variation inside the Smith-Morra Gambit! Instead of recapturing [w:Nxc3] for one sacrificed pawn, White leaves c3 untouched and develops [Nf3], daring Black to go all the way to b2 just like a full Danish Gambit! Black should accept the challenge with [cxb2]! After [cxb2 Bxb2], White controls sweeping diagonals with both bishops, [Bc4] and [w:Nc3], for two pawns, but Black stays very solid with [cxb2 Bxb2 d6 Bc4 e6]—shutting down both bishops behind a granite pawn wall. [%cal Gc3b2,Bc1b2] }
4... cxb2 ) 4... Nc6 ( 4... d6
{ Black plays [d6] early in the Smith-Morra Gambit, controlling e5 so White can never harass a future f6-knight with an e-pawn lunge. At the same time, delaying [Nc6] keeps Black's setup flexible—either transposing to a Scheveningen hull with [e6], [Nc6] and [Nf6], or heading into a Fianchetto defense via [g6] and [Bg7]. White develops rapidly with [Nf3] and [Bc4], preparing short castling, [Qe2] and [w:Rd1] to line up against Black's backward d6-pawn down the open d-file. [%csl Rd6][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1c4] }
5. Nf3
{ Black has a Scheveningen-like set-up in mind, with [d6], [e6], [Nf6] and [Be7]. The best moves now are [Nc6] or [Nf6], developing and keeping d5 covered. White's set-up is the usual [Bc4], castling, [Qe2] and the rooks to d1 and c1. Pushing the e-pawn leaves d5 to White's knight for good and blocks Black's own bishop. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb8c6,Gg8f6,Gg7g6] }
( 5. Bc4 ) 5... Nc6 ( 5... Nf6 ) ( 5... g6
{ The Fianchetto Defense against the Smith-Morra Gambit—a dangerous mistake if Black plays Dragon moves on autopilot! Because the c-file and d-file are already wide open and Black has NOT developed [Nc6] yet, White has the explosive central thrust [e5] (threatening [e5 dxe5 Qxd8+ Kxd8 Nxe5] hitting f7!) as well as [Bc4], where [Bc4 Bg7] walks straight into [Bc4 Bg7 e5] or [Ng5]! Black has to tread on eggshells with [Bc4 Nc6] to cover d4 and e5. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf1c4,Ge4e5] }
6. Bc4 ( 6. e5 ) ) ( 5... e5 $2
{ The pawn takes space, but d5 becomes a permanent hole. [Bc4] aims at it and at f7, and after [Bc4 Nc6 Ng5], White's pieces are already in Black's camp. Develop with [b:Nc6] instead. [%csl Rd5,Rf7][%cal Gf1c4] }
6. Bc4 ) ( 5... Bg4 $6
{ Bringing the bishop out to g4 abandons the b7-pawn too early in the wide-open Smith-Morra center. [Qb3 Bd7] forces the bishop straight back (or [Qc8] passively) while White eyes both b7 and f7 via [Bc4] and [Ng5]. Develop solidly with [b:Nc6] or [b:e6] first. [%cal Gd1b3,Rb3b7,Rb3f7] }
6. Qb3 Bd7 ) ) ( 4... e6
{ Black chooses the set-up with [e6], which blunts a bishop on c4 and prepares [d5] or a knight to e7. White goes on with [Nf3], [Bc4], castling and [Qe2]. Black's main plans are [a6] with [b5] and [Bb7], chasing the bishop, or [Nc6] with [Qc7] and [Nf6]. The danger in this set-up is d6: with the e-pawn on e6 and the d-pawn at home, a knight from b5 or a rook on d1 can hit it, so Black must keep it covered. [%csl Yd6][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1c4] }
5. Nf3
{ Black has taken the Morra pawn and chooses a set-up. [Nc6] leads to the main lines, and [a6] keeps White's knight off b5 before the bishop comes out. [d6] keeps White's e-pawn from coming to e5; then the king's knight goes to e7 or f6. [Bb4] pins the knight and plans to take on c3, a direct way to slow White's development. In every line, Black's job is to finish development and castle: the extra pawn counts only in the endgame. [%cal Gb8c6,Ga7a6,Gd7d6] }
( 5. Bc4
{ White puts the bishop on c4 while Black has committed only [e6], creating a subtle move-order battle in the Smith-Morra. Black often plays [a6], taking away the b5 jump from White's c3-knight and preparing [b5] with tempo against the c4-bishop followed by [Bb7] and [Qc7]. Developing [Nc6] reaches the standard Morra main line, while [Bb4] pins the c3-knight immediately to disrupt White's piece harmony and fight for control over e4 and d5. [%cal Ga7a6,Gb8c6,Bf8b4] }
5... a6 ( 5... Nc6 ) ( 5... Bb4 ) 6. Nf3
{ Both sides have laid their cards on the table in this Taimanov/Kan defense to the Smith-Morra. With [a6] already played, Black can kick the bishop at once with [b5], gaining queenside space for [b5 Bb3 Bb7] to hit White's e4-pawn while keeping d6 solid, or develop [Nc6] and [Qc7] smoothly without fearing [Nb5]. White welcomes [b5 Bb3] because the bishop remains safely tucked on the a2-g8 diagonal while [O-O], [Qe2] and [w:Rd1] build dangerous momentum in the center. [%cal Gb7b5,Gb8c6,Bd8c7] }
6... b5 ( 6... Nc6 ) ( 6... Qc7 ) ) 5... Nc6
{ Black has the knight on c6 and the pawn on e6. White's standard move is [Bc4], followed by castling and [Qe2]. Black then chooses a plan: [Qc7] and [Nf6], or [a6] and [b5], or [d6] with [Nf6] and [Be7]. Two traps live in these positions: the Siberian Trap, when White kicks a knight on g4 with [h3] too early, and the knight jump to d5 when Black's queen sits on c7. Knowing both is half the battle in the Morra. [%csl Yd5,Yf7][%cal Gf1c4,Gf1b5] }
( 5... a6
{ The Kan Formation against the Smith-Morra takes b5 away from White's knight and bishop on move five, preparing [b:b5], [b:Bb7] and [b:Qc7] or [b:Ne7] without giving White easy tactical targets. [Bc4] remains the overwhelming club choice, accepting [Bc4 b5 Bd3 Bb7] to aim at the kingside, while [Bf4] targets Black's dark-square holes along the h2-b8 diagonal and coordinates smoothly with [w:Qd2] and [w:Rd1]. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gc1f4,Gf1c4] }
6. Bf4 ( 6. Bc4 ) ) ( 5... d6 ) 6. Bc4 ( 6. Bb5 ) ) ( 4... g6
{ The Fianchetto Defense against the Smith-Morra puts Black's bishop on g7 to command the dark squares and shield the kingside without moving the e- or d-pawns, blunting standard [Nd5] and [w:Bxe6] sacrifices. White develops naturally with [Nf3] and [Bc4] in either order: against [b:Bg7], White often pushes [w:e5] (stopping [b:Nf6]) or sets up [w:Bf4], [w:Qe2] and [w:Rd1] to squeeze Black's cramped camp. [%cal Gg1f3,Gf1c4,Bf8g7] }
5. Nf3 ( 5. Bc4 ) ) ) ( 2. f4 ) ( 2. Bc4
{ The Bowdler Attack, common in club games: the bishop aims at f7 at once. It is harmless if Black answers calmly. [e6] blunts the bishop and prepares [d5], which hits it with gain of time; [Nc6] and [d6] with [Nf6] are also good. Black must just keep f7 in mind while developing, and White's bishop usually ends up being chased. [%cal Ge7e6,Gb8c6,Rc4f7] }
2... e6
{ Black blunts the bishop's diagonal along a2-g8 and prepares [d5] to gain a clean tempo in the centre—or [a6] threatening [b5] right away while keeping ideal Taimanov development ([Nc6], [Qc7], [Nf6]) ready in reserve. Since the bishop on c4 bites on granite and will soon have to retreat or guard itself, White should get pieces out briskly with [Nf3] or [Nc3], or play [Qe2] and [d3] for a compact King's Indian Attack setup. [%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3,Bd7d5] }
( 2... Nc6
{ Black develops naturally, clamps down on d4 and keeps both [e6] and [g6] in reserve against the Bowdler bishop. White continues with [Nf3] and [Nc3], often tucking the d-pawn to [d3] so d4 is shielded from Black's knight jump. Black enjoys an easy game: [e6] followed by [Nf6] and [d5] seizes central space with tempo against c4, while [g6] and [Bg7] build a strong dark-square grip. [%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3,Gd2d3] }
3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. d3 ) ) ( 2... d6
{ Black secures e5 and opens the c8-h3 diagonal without committing the e-pawn yet. White usually brings the knights out with [Nf3] and [Nc3], either keeping a quiet Italian-like structure with [d3] and short castling or breaking with a later [d4] into a Sozin position. Black answers smoothly with [Nf6] and [Nc6], ready to meet slow play with [e6] and [Be7] or a queenside push via [a6] and [b5]. [%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3,Gd2d3] }
3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. d3 ) ) 3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. d3 ) ( 3. Qe2 ) ) ( 2. Ne2
{ The Keres or Chameleon Variation. By putting the knight on e2 instead of f3, White keeps two very different plans open: a late [d4] into an Open Sicilian, or a Closed Sicilian and Grand Prix structure where the f-pawn is still free to advance. Black can answer flexibly with [Nc6], [d6] or [e6], waiting to see White's cards, while [Nf6] attacks e4 right away and invites [Nbc3] followed by [d5]. [%csl Ye2,Yf3,Yd4][%cal Gb8c6] }
2... Nc6 ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... e6 ) ( 2... Nf6 ) ) ( 2. a4
{ The Myers Attack with [a4]. White plays a quirky flank thrust on move two, ruling out early [b5] Wing-countergambit or Kan/O'Kelly queenside expansions while keeping [Ra3] rook-lift ideas or a quiet [Nc3] and [Bc4] setup in mind. The drawback is obvious: [a4] surrenders the b4 outpost and spends a tempo on the edge while the center is untouched. Black equalizes effortlessly by developing normally—[Nc6] eyes b4 and d4 right away, while [e6] and [d6] prepare [Nf6] and [d5]. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gb8c6,Ge7e6] }
2... Nc6 ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... e6 ) ) ( 2. h4
{ The Myers / Keres-flank provocation [h4]. White throws the h-pawn forward on move two to discourage Dragon fianchettoes ([g6] invites [h5]) and prepare a quirky rook lift via [Rh3] or space grab on the kingside, hoping to rattle Sicilian specialists out of their book. Because [h4] weakens g4 and kingside castling while doing nothing for the center, Black simply ignores the flank gesture: [Nc6], [e6] (preparing [d5] immediately!) or [d6] with [Nf6] give Black an easy, sound game. [%csl Yg4][%cal Gb8c6,Ge7e6] }
2... Nc6 ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... e6 ) ) ( 2. Na3
{ The Kronberger Variation ([Na3]). Though putting a knight on the rim looks bizarre at first glance, White has a concrete strategic idea: keep the c-pawn free to push [c3] and [d4] (Alapin-style, without blocking c3!) or jump [Nc4] to eye d6 and e5 while holding [f4] in reserve. Still, a3 exerts zero pressure on d5 or e4 right now, so Black gets comfortable play with [Nc6] (clamping down on d4), [e6] (preparing [d5] before White coordinates), or flexible [d6]. [%cal Gb8c6,Ge7e6,Ba3c4] }
2... Nc6 ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... e6 ) ) ( 2. g3
{ White fianchettoes at once, keeping the Closed Sicilian set-up of [Bg2], [d3] and [Nc3] or [Ne2]. The plan is slow, so Black can develop freely: [Nc6] with [g6] and [Bg7] mirrors White, and [e6] with [d5] grabs the centre. [d5] straight away is also good: after [d5 exd5 Qxd5], the queen hits the rook on h1 along the long diagonal, and White has to block with [Nf3]. Black's usual plan later is queenside space with [Rb8], [b5] and [b4]. [%cal Gb8c6,Gd7d5,Bf1g2] }
2... Nc6 ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... e6 ) ( 2... d5 ) ) ( 2. a3
{ The Mengarini Variation. The pawn on a3 prepares [b4], a Wing Gambit with the a-pawn ready to take back, and it keeps Black's pieces off b4. It is a slow move, so Black can simply develop: [Nc6] with [g6], [e6] with [d5], or [d6] with [Nf6]. If White follows with [b4], Black can take or ignore it; after [b4 cxb4 axb4], White has the open a-file and an extra central pawn, but has spent two moves on it. [%cal Gb8c6,Ge7e6,Bb2b4] }
2... Nc6 ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... e6 ) ( 2... g6 ) ) ( 2. d3
{ A modest move, often the start of a King's Indian Attack with [Nf3], [g3], [Bg2] and short castling, or of a Closed Sicilian with [Nc3]. White keeps the centre closed and plans a later kingside push with [f4] or [e5]. Black has no problems here: [Nc6] with [g6] and [Bg7] fights for d4 and e5, and [e6] with [d5] takes the centre while White is slow. A good plan for Black is to gain space on the queenside with [b5], where White has nothing going on. [%cal Gb8c6,Ge7e6,Gd7d6] }
2... Nc6
{ Black develops and keeps an eye on d4 and e5. White picks a set-up: [Nf3] with [g3] and [Bg2] is the King's Indian Attack, [Nc3] with [g3] is a Closed Sicilian, and [f4] aims for a Grand Prix-style attack with the pawn already on d3. All of them are slow and aimed at the kingside, so Black can take space: [g6] with [Bg7] and [d6], or [e6] with [d5], then [b5] on the queenside. Black has no reason to open the centre early. [%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3,Gg2g3] }
( 2... d6
{ Black meets White's quiet setup in kind, staking out e5 with [d6] while keeping every kingside arrangement open. White usually fianchettoes with [g3] and [Bg2], aiming for a King's Indian Attack after [Nf3] and short castling or for a Closed Sicilian after [Nc3] and [Nge2]; [f4] is a sharper kingside plan. Black answers smoothly with [b:Nc6], [b:g6] and [b:Bg7] to clamp down on the central dark squares. [%cal Gg2g3,Gg1f3,Gb1c3] }
3. g3 ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. Nc3 ) ) ( 2... e6 ) ( 2... g6 ) 3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3
{ A Closed Sicilian set-up, with [w:g3] and the bishop to g2 next, or the f-pawn to f4. Black has an easy game: [g6] and [b:Bg7] put the bishop on the long diagonal, against the knight on c3 and the pawn on b2. [e6] with [b:Nge7] keeps the f-pawn free, and [d6] keeps e5 under control. Black's plan is the queenside: a rook to b8 and the b-pawn forward, while White plays on the kingside. [%cal Gg7g6,Ge7e6,Gd7d6] }
3... g6 ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... d6 ) ) ( 3. g3 ) ( 3. f4 ) ( 3. c3
{ White holds back the pieces and builds a compact pawn wedge on c3 and d3, keeping [d4] in reserve while blunting the knight on c6. Black has three sound ways to organize counterplay: [e6] prepares a prompt central strike with [d5], [g6] fianchettoes the dark-squared bishop to g7 to bear down on d4 and the long diagonal, and [d6] keeps the center flexible while waiting for [Nf3] and [Be2]. Passive moves let White expand slowly with [Nf3], [Be2], [w:O-O] and a well-timed [d4]. [%csl Bd5][%cal Ge7e6,Gg7g6] }
3... e6 ( 3... g6 ) ( 3... d6 ) ) ) ( 2. b3
{ The Snyder set-up. White prepares [Bb2], which will press along the long diagonal toward e5 and g7, and keeps the centre flexible. The plan is slow, so Black has easy choices. [Nc6] with [d6] and [e5], or with [g6], takes the key squares of that diagonal away from the bishop. [d5] straight away hits e4 before the bishop is out: White should take on d5, since pushing past it gives Black a good French with the light-squared bishop free. Either way Black is comfortable from the start. [%cal Gb8c6,Gd7d5,Bc1b2] }
2... Nc6 ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... d5
{ Black hits e4 at once, before the bishop on c1 reaches b2. White should take with [exd5]: after [exd5 Qxd5] the queen is a little exposed to [Nc3], but Black develops quickly with [Nf6], [Nc6] and a bishop on f5 or g4. Pushing the e-pawn instead gives Black a French Advance with an extra tempo and the light-squared bishop free, and the pawn on e5 has no pawn on d4 to support it. [%cal Ge4d5,Rd5e4] }
3. exd5 ( 3. e5 $2
{ The pawn gains space, but it gives Black a better version of the French Advance. After [Nc6] and [Bf5] or [Bg4], Black's light-squared bishop is free and White's pawn on e5 is hard to keep supported, since the bishop on b2 won't help it. Take with [w:exd5] instead. [%csl Re5][%cal Gb8c6,Bc8f5] }
3... a6 ) ) ) ( 2. c4 ) ( 2. b4 ) 2... d6 ( 2... Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 (
4... g6 5. Nc3
{ White develops instead of setting up the Maroczy Bind with [c4]. Black goes on with [Bg7], the move that gives the line its point: the bishop hits the knight on d4 at once, so White must guard it or trade it. [Nf6] first allows [Nxc6 bxc6 e5], which kicks the knight back, and [d6] transposes to a normal Dragon, where White gets the Yugoslav Attack the accelerated move order was meant to avoid. [%cal Gf8g7,Rg7d4] }
5... Bg7
{ The bishop on g7 now hits d4 through the empty f6 square, so the knight there is attacked twice and defended only by the queen. White must deal with it first. [Be3] guards it and is the main line, heading for [Bc4] and short castling. [Nxc6 bxc6] trades it, and Black then plays for [d5]. [Nb3] steps away and keeps pieces on. A developing move that ignores the threat, such as [Bc4] or [Be2], drops the knight to [Nxd4]. [%cal Gc1e3,Gd4c6,Gd4b3,Rg7d4] }
6. Be3
{ White reinforces the central knight with [Be3] and threatens dangerous knight leaps into Black's queenside dark squares if Black plays carelessly. [Nf6] is practically forced: it develops with tempo on e4, covers d5 alongside the g7-bishop and prepares castling. Slow pawn moves get punished immediately—[a6] surrenders b6 to [a6 Nd5], while [e6] leaves a gaping hole on d6 that [e6 Ndb5] exploits on the spot. [%csl Yd5,Ye4][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 6. Nxc6 ) ( 6. Nb3 ) ( 6. Bc4 $4
{ The bishop heads for its best diagonal, but the knight on d4 was attacked twice. [Nxd4] takes it, and [w:Qxd4] would lose the queen to [b:Bxd4], so White is simply a piece down. In the Accelerated Dragon, count the attackers of d4 as soon as the bishop reaches g7: [w:Be3] or [w:Nb3] first. [%cal Gc6d4,Rg7d4] }
6... Nxd4 ) 6... Nf6
{ The Accelerated Dragon's main line with [Nc3]. White develops as in the Yugoslav Attack, but here [f3] and long castling lose much of their bite, because Black can hit back with [d5] in one move. So White usually plays [Bc4], which keeps d5 under control, then [Bb3] and short castling. Black answers with castling or [Qa5], pinning the knight on c3, and later [d6], [Bd7] and play on the c-file. [Be2] with short castling is the calm option. [%cal Gc4f7,Gf1c4,Gf1e2] }
( 6... a6 $2
{ The pawn keeps the knight off b5, but it lets the other one in. [Nd5] lands in Black's camp, and with [w:Nb6] or [w:Bb6] ideas against the rook and the queen, Black is already in trouble. Develop with [b:Nf6], which covers d5. [%csl Rb6][%cal Gc3d5] }
7. Nd5 ) ( 6... e6 $4
{ The pawn covers d5 but leaves d6 open. [Ndb5] heads straight for it, and after [Ndb5 Bf8] or a king move Black can't develop normally. Play [b:Nf6] first. [%csl Rd6][%cal Gd4b5] }
7. Ndb5 ) 7. Bc4
