{ White chooses how to take back. [Nxd4] is normal: after [Nc6] the game is an Accelerated Dragon, and after [Nf6] White defends e4 with [Nc3]. [Qxd4] is a real option here: the queen hits the rook on h8 through the empty g7 square, so Black has to block with [Nf6] before chasing it, and White keeps the queen centralised a little longer. After [Nf6], the push [e5] and [Nc6] follow, and the queen usually goes to a4 or h4. [%csl Yh8][%cal Gf3d4,Gd1d4] }
( 3... Bg7
{ Black keeps the tension and puts the bishop on the long diagonal, aiming at d4. White can build a centre with [c3], gain space with [d5], or take the pawn with [dxc5], which Black answers with [Qa5+] and wins it back. [Nc3] invites [cxd4 Nxd4 Nc6], an Accelerated Dragon. After [c3], Black can take on d4 and hit the centre with [d5], or develop with [Nf6] and [Qb6], pressing on d4 and b2. [%cal Gc2c3,Gd4c5,Gd4d5,Gg7d4] }
4. c3
{ White wants a big pawn centre with d4 and e4 both guarded. Black takes first: after [cxd4 cxd4], [b:d5] hits e4 at once, and the bishop on g7 eyes d4. [Nc6] adds pressure on d4 too, and [b:Qb6] can follow. [d6] is a slip: [w:dxc5] opens the d-file, and after the queens come off Black has lost a pawn and the right to castle. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc5d4,Gb8c6] }
( 4. dxc5
{ In the Hyperaccelerated Dragon, Black bypassed [b:cxd4] with [Bg7], inviting [dxc5], and now recovers the pawn cleanly via [Qa5+]! After [Qa5+ Nc3 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qxc3+ Bd2 Qxc5], White sacrifices queenside pawn symmetry for the two bishops and dark-square open lines, or plays [Qa5+ Bd2 Qxc5 Nc3] where the b2-pawn is taboo ([Qa5+ Bd2 Qxc5 Bc3] trades off Black's Dragon bishop!). Developing [Nc6] first as a temporary gambit is also tricky in blitz. [%cal Gd8a5,Gb8c6] }
