{ The Pterodactyl check recovers the c5 pawn while trying to disrupt White's queenside coordination. [c3] is White's sharpest reply: after [c3 Qxc5], the c3-pawn blunts the g7-bishop down the long diagonal and prepares [Be3+] with tempo on the queen, followed by [Na3] or [Nbd2]. [Bd2] is the safe alternative ([Bd2 Qxc5 Nc3]), eyeing the exposed queen if c3 later opens, while [Nc3] invites [Nc3 Bxc3+ bxc3]. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gc2c3,Gc1d2,Gb1c3] }
( 4... Nc6 ) ( 4... Nf6 ) 5. c3 ( 5. Bd2 ) ( 5. Nc3
{ In the Sicilian Pterodactyl, Black offered the c5-pawn to activate [Qa5+] with tempo and open the h8-a1 diagonal for the g7-bishop, and White blocks the check with [Nc3]. To recover the pawn cleanly without losing time, Black must capture on c3 first: [Bxc3+] forces [Bxc3+ bxc3 Qxc3+ Bd2 Qxc5], regaining the pawn with check while leaving White's queenside shattered. Taking immediately with [Qxc5] lets [Qxc5 Nd5] seize a dangerous initiative against c7. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gg7c3,Ga5c3] }
