{ The O'Kelly Variation. Black waits with [a6], a useful move in many Sicilian lines, and plans to choose a set-up after seeing White's. Its point shows after [d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e5]: the knight can't retreat to b5, and the bishop is ready to pin on b4. White's best answers avoid the Open Sicilian: [c3], an Alapin in which [a6] is not much use, and [c4], which stops [b5] and grips d5. After [d4 cxd4], [c3] is a gambit worth knowing, and declining it with a waiting move hands White the centre. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gc2c3,Gc2c4,Gd2d4] }
3. c3
{ The Venice System is a major test of the O'Kelly: since Black spent move two on [a6], White prepares [d4] without fearing a pin on b4 and turns Black's wing pawn into a quiet tempo when the centre opens. [e6] preparing [b:d5] in French style and [d5] striking at e4 immediately are Black's soundest equalisers, whereas slower moves such as [d6] allow [d6 d4] with an unchallenged central grip. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd7d5,Ge7e6] }
( 3. c4
{ White sets up the Maroczy Bind on move three, clamping down on d5 and inviting [Nc6] [w:d4] [b:cxd4] [w:Nxd4], where Black's early [a6] has limited value if the game stays quiet. Black can play [Nc6] anyway and fight for d4 with [b:e5], choose [d6] preparing [b:Bg4] to trade off a dark-square defender before [b:Nc6], or play [e6] aiming for a Hedgehog or a timely [b:b5] queenside break. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb8c6,Gd7d6,Ge7e6] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... e6 ) ) ( 3. d4
{ White opens the centre against the O'Kelly. Black usually takes with [cxd4]: after [cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e5], the point of [a6] shows, as the knight can't drop back to b5 and has to choose a worse square. White's sharper tries after [cxd4] are the gambit with [c3] and the bishop move [Bc4]. Not taking with [e6] is playable, but gives White the choice between [d5] and [dxc5]. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gc5d4,Ge7e6] }
3... cxd4
{ White can take back with the knight, reaching the O'Kelly's main position, or gambit with [c3], against which the O'Kelly's [a6] is not much use. [Nxd4] invites [Nf6] and [e5], Black's most active plan, where [a6] keeps the knight off b5. The bishop move [Bc4], aiming at f7, is also played: Black then blunts it with [e6] and keeps the extra pawn for a while. Slow moves let Black keep the pawn for free. [%cal Gf3d4,Gc2c3,Gd1d4] }
( 3... e6
{ The Drazic Variation, a provocative sideline where Black delays [cxd4] hoping to reach a Benoni or Kan structure on favourable terms. White punishes the slow pawn moves best by pushing [d5] right away: after [d5 exd5 exd5], Black's queenside lags behind and d6 needs careful defence, while [d5 d6] gives White a roomy Modern Benoni where [a6] came before piece development. Developing [Nc3] transposes back into the Kan after [Nc3 cxd4]. [%csl Yd5,Yd6,Ya6][%cal Gd4d5] }
