{ The Yerevan System against the O'Kelly. By developing the queen's knight instead of playing [w:c3] or [w:c4], White gives up the chance to build a Maroczy Bind or an Alapin centre and heads back toward Open Sicilian territory. Black gets comfortable play with [e6] toward a Kan or Taimanov, [d6] toward a Najdorf, [Nc6], or an immediate [b5] to seize queenside space and eye e4 from b7. [%csl Yc3,Yc4,Ye6][%cal Ge7e6] }
3... e6 ( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... b5 ) ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... g6 $2
{ Combining the O'Kelly [a6] with a Dragon fianchetto [g6] leaves the h8 rook undefended on the long diagonal and wastes too much time on the flanks. [d4 cxd4 Qxd4] centralizes White's queen with an immediate threat against h8, forcing awkward concessions before [b:Nc6] can chase her. Play [b:e6] or [b:b5] instead. [%cal Gd2d4,Rd4h8] }
4. d4 cxd4 5. Qxd4 ) ) ( 3. d3
{ Facing the O'Kelly Variation ([a6]—where [d4] lets Black play [d4 cxd4 Nxd4 e5] without [w:Nb5] or [e6] and [Qc7] Kan style, and [c4] allows [b5] gambit ideas), White steers calmly into a King's Indian Attack setup with [d3]. White plans [g3], [w:Bg2], [w:O-O] and [Nbd2], rendering Black's early [a6] somewhat slow unless Black uses it actively! Black does just that with [Nc6] and [g6] (Closed Sicilian with extra queenside readiness for [b5]), or [b5] and [Bb7] immediately. [%cal Gb8c6,Gb7b5,Ge7e6] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... b5 ) ) ( 3. g3
{ The Réti / King's Indian Attack system against the O'Kelly Variation. Instead of walking into Black's prepared traps after [d4] or [c4], White fianchettoes with [g3] and [Bg2], keeping [d3] or even a delayed [d4] in hand. Black puts the [a6] move to work on the queenside right away with [b5], grabbing space for [Bb7] to attack White's undefended e4-pawn (forcing [b5 Bg2 Bb7 d3]), or develops [Nc6] and [g6] for a symmetrical fianchetto duel. [%cal Gb7b5,Gb8c6,Bf1g2] }
