4. d4 ) ) ( 2... Nf6
{ The Nimzowitsch Variation. The knight attacks e4 and dares White to push the pawn forward, as in Alekhine's Defence. White's most testing answer is [e5], gaining space with tempo: after [e5 Nd5 Nc3], White offers to trade knights and then gain more time on Black's pieces. [Nc3] is a calm alternative that defends the pawn. Ignoring the attack on e4 simply loses it. [%cal Ge4e5,Gb1c3,Rf6e4] }
3. e5
{ Nimzowitsch's 2...[Nf6] deliberately provokes White's e-pawn forward, mirroring Alekhine's Defence with the extra Sicilian move [c5] already in place. Once [e5] attacks the knight, [Nd5] is the only sound destination: from d5 the knight blockades the centre, eyes b4 and f4, and can drop to c7 if kicked by [w:c4] while Black undermines e5 with [Nc6] and [d6]. Jumping forward to e4 gets the knight trapped by [Ne4 d3]. [%csl Ye5,Yb4][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 3. Nc3
{ White defends e4 with the knight instead of pushing it. Black should keep e5 covered so that the knight on f6 can't be chased: [d6] or [Nc6] return to normal Sicilian lines after [d4], and [e6] with [d5] is a French-style option. Two moves that look free here lose ground: the push to e5, which drops a pawn, and the fianchetto, which lets White's pawn kick the knight. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gd7d6,Gb8c6,Ge7e6] }
3... d6
{ Having met the Nimzowitsch [Nf6] calmly with [Nc3] instead of lunging forward with [w:e5], White watches Black step back into orthodox Sicilian waters with [d6]. Almost every game now transposes straight into the main Open Sicilian via [d4 cxd4 Nxd4], reaching the Classical Sicilian crossroads before Black has chosen Najdorf ([a6]), Dragon ([g6]), Scheveningen ([e6]) or Classical ([Nc6]). Anti-Sicilian players can still sidestep that theory with [Bb5+ Bd7] (a Rossolimo/Moscow hybrid) or [Bc4]. [%cal Gd2d4,Gf1b5,Gf1c4] }
( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... e5 $2
{ The pawn takes space, but it has no defender. [Nxe5] wins it, and after [Nxe5 Nc6 Nxc6 bxc6] Black has only a broken queenside to show. Cover e5 with [b:Nc6] or [b:d6] instead. [%csl Re5][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5 ) ( 3... g6 $6
{ The fianchetto ignores the knight's problem. [e5] kicks it back to g8, and after [e5 Ng8 d4] White has a big centre and a lead in development. With a knight on f6, keep e5 covered first. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5 Ng8 5. d4 ) 4. d4
{ White opens the center with [d4], completing the transposition from the Nimzowitsch Sicilian ([Nf6]) into the main Open Sicilian. Black has only one good move here: [cxd4] trades a flank c-pawn for White's central d-pawn, opening the c-file for queenside counterplay while retaining the d6+e7 central pawn duo. Any hesitation—such as [Nc6] or [Bg4]—lets White push [d5] or capture [dxc5] with a commanding structural and spatial advantage. [%cal Gc5d4,Rd4c5] }
( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. Bb5+ ) 4... cxd4
{ Black captures [cxd4], trading flank pawn for central pawn, and White recaptures to reach the main Open Sicilian tabiya. [Nxd4] is overwhelming main-line theory, centralizing the knight on d4 where it eyes b5, c6, e6 and f5, and asking Black to pick between the Najdorf ([Nxd4 a6]), Dragon ([Nxd4 g6]), Classical ([Nxd4 Nc6]) and Scheveningen ([Nxd4 e6]). Recapturing with the queen via [Qxd4] is a solid Chekhover sideline, met by [Qxd4 Nc6 Bb5 Bd7]. [%cal Gf3d4,Gd1d4] }
5. Nxd4 ( 5. Qxd4 ) ) ( 3. Bc4 $2
