3... Nxe4 ) ( 3. d4 $6
{ Opening the centre drops the pawn on e4: [Nxe4] takes it, and after [Nxe4 d5] White has space but is a pawn down. Push it first with [w:e5], or guard it with [w:Nc3]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf6e4] }
3... Nxe4 ) 3... Nd5
{ In the Nimzowitsch Sicilian ([Nf6]), Black provokes White's e-pawn to e5—just like Alekhine's Defense—with the extra Sicilian trump of a pawn already on c5 to chip away at d4. White has several testing plans: [Nc3] challenges the centralized knight directly ([Nc3 Nxc3 dxc3] gives White rapid piece activity and easy development, while [Nc3 e6] enters a rich tactical fight), [c4] grabs a Maroczy-style space edge after [c4 Nc7 d4], and [d4] opens the center right away. [%cal Gc2c4,Gf1c4,Gd2d4] }
( 3... Ne4 $4
{ The knight steps forward, but it has no way back. [d3] hits it, and every square is covered: g5 by the knight on f3, c3 and d2 by White's pieces, f6 and d6 by the pawn on e5. A check from a5 is met by [w:c3], and after [b:Nxf2] [w:Kxf2] Black has lost a piece for a pawn. Go to d5. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd2d3] }
4. d3 ) 4. Nc3 ( 4. c4 ) ( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. d4 ) 4... e6 5. Nxd5 exd5 6. d4
{ White has a strong centre after [d4], and Black's pawn on d5 stands alone in the middle of the board. Black should develop with [d6] or [Nc6], hitting e5 and d4 before White can support them with the bishop and castling. The pawn on e5 is White's pride, but also Black's target. Taking on d4 at once is a mistake: the queen takes back, sits in the centre and hits d5, and Black has to spend time guarding it. [%csl Yd5,Ye5][%cal Gd7d6,Gb8c6] }
6... d6 ( 6... Nc6
{ Black develops and hits d4. White's best is [dxc5]: after [dxc5 Bxc5 Qxd5] White has won the pawn on d5, and Black's quick development, with [b:Qb6] lining up against f2, is only partial compensation. The pin with the bishop on b5 looks natural but loses it, as the queen checks from a5 and hits the bishop at the same time. [%csl Yd5,Rb5][%cal Gd4c5] }
