{ Stefan Bücker's provocative [h6] sideline. Black spends an early tempo denying g5 to White's pieces, intending either a wild Kingside/Basman structure with [g5] or a delayed Dragon/Najdorf where [Bg5] attacks never exist. Because Black has developed nothing and left the center unattended, White seizes the initiative with classical principles: [d4 cxd4 Nxd4] opens the game with an extra tempo in hand (followed by [Nc3] and [Bc4] or [c4] Maroczy style!), while [c3] and [d4] claim the full center. [%cal Gd2d4,Gf1c4,Gb1c3] }
3. d4 ( 3. c3 ) ( 3. Bc4 ) ( 3. Nc3 ) ) ( 2... e5 $2
{ Black copies White's centre, but the pawn on e5 has no defender: [Nxe5] simply takes it. [Qa5] wins nothing back, and [Qe7] gets the pawn only after [w:Nf3] and [b:Qxe4+], a queen trek that leaves White far ahead in development. In the Sicilian, the knight on f3 already eyes e5: develop first with [b:d6] or [b:Nc6]. [%cal Gf3e5] }
3. Nxe5 ) ( 2... b5 $2
{ A wing gambit with the colours reversed, but here it gives a pawn for nothing. [Bxb5] takes it with a developing move, and the bishop even gets ready to castle. Black has no lead in development to show for it. Keep the b-pawn home and develop. [%csl Rb5][%cal Gf1b5] }
3. Bxb5 ) ( 2... f5 $2
{ The Brussels Gambit opens Black's own king. [exf5] takes the pawn, and the squares e6 and g6 are weak at once: [w:Qh5+] and [w:Ne5] tricks follow, and the natural [b:d5] runs into [w:Bb5+]. White is a pawn up with the better position. Never push the f-pawn this early. [%csl Rf5][%cal Ge4f5] }
3. exf5 ) ( 2... Qc7
{ The Quinteros. The queen covers e5 and watches the c-file and the h2-b8 diagonal, but it comes out early. White's simplest answer is [c3] and [d4], a big centre, or [Nc3] with [d4], after which the queen on c7 is a target for [Nb5] or a bishop on f4. Black usually goes on with [Nc6], [e6] and [a6], a Taimanov-style set-up, so the move is not bad, just committal. White should simply develop and open the centre. [%cal Gc2c3,Gd2d4,Gb1c3] }
3. c3 ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. d4 ) ) ( 2... b6
{ The Katalimov. Black wants the bishop on b7 against e4, but the light squares around the king get thin. White plays [d4] and develops naturally. There is a known trap: after [d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Bb7 Nc3 Nc6 Be3 Nxd4 Bxd4 d6 Bb5+], Black can only block the check, with the queen or with the bishop on c6, and both lose the queen. The pawn move to d6 is what opened the diagonal: in this line Black should develop with the e-pawn and the king's knight instead. [%cal Gd2d4,Gb1c3,Rb5e8] }
3. d4 ( 3. Nc3 ) ) 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4
{ The basic position of the Open Sicilian with [d6]. White's knight dominates the center, the d-file is half-open for White and the c-file for Black. Black's natural move is [Nf6], which attacks e4 and makes White defend it, usually with [Nc3], before Black commits to a system. That waiting is deliberate: only after [Nc3] does Black choose between [a6], [g6], [Nc6] and [e6], so that White's set-up can't be tailored in advance. [Nc6] at once is also possible and often transposes to the Classical. [%cal Gg8f6,Gb8c6,Gg7g6] }
4... Nf6
{ The knight on f6 attacks e4, so White has to deal with it before anything else. [Nc3] is by far the main move: it defends the pawn, develops a piece and reaches the starting point of all of Black's big systems. [f3], the Prins Variation, defends with a pawn and keeps the c-pawn free for [c4], a Maroczy Bind that cramps Black; its drawbacks are slower development and a weakened diagonal toward the white king. Black usually answers it with [f3 e5], hitting the knight at once. [%cal Gb1c3,Gf2f3,Rf6e4] }
( 4... Nc6
{ Developing [Nc6] before [b:Nf6] keeps White guessing, yet it omits the immediate attack on e4 and gives White extra freedom on move five. [Nc3] is the main path, normally transposing to the Classical Sicilian after [Nc3 Nf6] or the Dragon after [Nc3 g6]. Because e4 is not under fire, White can also establish a Maroczy Bind with [c4], clamping down on d5 before bringing the b1-knight out, or play [f3]. [%cal Gb1c3,Gc2c4,Gf2f3] }
5. Nc3 ( 5. c4 ) ( 5. f3 ) ) ( 4... g6
{ An imprecise Dragon move order: by playing [g6] before [Nf6], Black leaves e4 unattacked and gives White a free hand in the centre. Unlike the Accelerated Dragon, Black has already spent a tempo on [d6], so a single-step [d5] break is gone. White can clamp down on d5 with [c4] for a favourable Maroczy Bind, set up [Be3] and [f3] without worrying about [Ng4], or simply play [Nc3] into a regular Dragon. [%csl Yg6,Ye4,Yd6][%cal Gb1c3] }
5. Nc3 ( 5. Be3 ) ( 5. c4 ) ( 5. Bc4 ) ) 5. Nc3
{ The great crossroads of the Open Sicilian. Each of Black's choices starts a different system with its own pawn structure. [a6], the Najdorf, controls b5 and keeps every option open. [g6], the Dragon, puts the bishop on the long diagonal. [Nc6], the Classical, develops and adds pressure on d4. [e6], the Scheveningen, builds a small, sturdy centre. Pushing [e5] at once is less accurate: without [a6] first, [Bb5+] or a knight jump to b5 makes the holes on d5 and d6 easier to use. [%cal Ga7a6,Gg7g6,Gb8c6,Ge7e6] }
( 5. f3
{ The Prins Variation. White guards e4 with a pawn instead of the knight, so that [c4] can follow and build a Maroczy Bind, where Black's freeing [d5] never comes. The cost is a slower development and a weakened diagonal toward the white king. Black's best answer is [e5], kicking the knight before [c4] is played; [Nc6] and [e6] are also fine. The Najdorf move [a6] is slower: after [a6 c4] White has the bind, though the engine sees only a small edge. [%cal Gc2c4,Ge7e5,Gb8c6] }
5... e5
{ In the Prins Variation (5.[f3]), White holds back the queen's knight hoping to squeeze in [c4] for a Maroczy Bind, so Black strikes immediately with [e5] to deny it and force the d4-knight to declare itself. Retreating [Nb3] is the main line, heading for a solid English Attack or Maroczy hybrid after [Nb3 Be6 c4]; flicking in [Bb5+] first to draw [Bb5+ Nbd7] or [Bb5+ Bd7] is good too. [Nf5] walks right into [Nf5 d5]. [%cal Gd4b3,Gf1b5,Re5d4] }
( 5... Nc6 ) ( 5... e6 ) 6. Nb3 ( 6. Bb5+ ) ( 6. Nf5 $6
{ The knight looks active, but it stands where Black can attack it with gain of time. [d5] breaks in the centre and opens Black's pieces, and the bishop on c8 can also take on f5 when it wants. White has moved the knight three times and has nothing to show for it. Drop it back to b3 instead. [%csl Rf5][%cal Gd6d5] }
6... d5 ) ) ( 5. Bc4
{ The Ginsberg Gambit leaves e4 undefended to rush the bishop to c4 and set traps around f7. Black can safely accept with [Nxe4], meeting [Nxe4 Qh5] with [e6] (never [b:g6] because [w:Qd5] forks e4 and f7), after which Black stands well. Declining with [e6] or [a6] is equally reliable: [e6] blunts the c4-bishop cold and turns White's skipped [Nc3] into a mild inaccuracy once [w:Nc3] follows anyway. [%csl Yf7][%cal Ge7e6,Gf6e4,Ga7a6] }
5... e6 ( 5... Nxe4 ) ( 5... a6 ) ) 5... a6 ( 5... g6 ) ( 5... Nc6 ) ( 5... e6
{ The Scheveningen. Black builds a compact centre: the pawns on d6 and e6 cover e5, d5, c5 and f5 and keep the position flexible. The pieces come out behind them: [Be7], castling, [a6], [Qc7] and a knight to c6 or d7, waiting for the right moment to break with [d5] or [e5]. White's sharpest reply is [g4], the Keres Attack, which kicks the knight before Black has castled. [Be2] with [f4] and short castling, or [Be3] with long castling, are the main other plans. [%cal Gd6d5,Ge6e5] }
6. g4
{ The Keres Attack. White pushes the g-pawn before Black has castled, threatening to push on to g5 and drive the knight from f6 and win time for a kingside attack. Black's main answer is [h6], slowing the pawn down; White then often keeps pushing with [h4] and [Rg1], or pushes to g5 anyway as a pawn sacrifice. [Nc6] and [a6] are the other replies, letting the pawn come to g5 and rerouting the knight to d7. Black usually keeps the king in the center for a while, since short castling would walk into the storm. [%cal Gg4g5,Gh7h6,Gb8c6] }
( 6. Be2
{ The Classical Scheveningen. White castles short and plays [f4], [Kh1], [Be3] and often the queen to e1 and g3, aiming for [e5] or [f5]; [a4] stops Black's [b5]. Black develops with [Be7], castling, [a6], [Qc7] and [Nc6]. The diagonal from b6 to g1 matters: with the king on g1, a black queen on b6 pins the knight on d4. [%cal Gf4f5,Gf8e7,Ga7a6] }
