) ) ( 5... e5
{ Without waiting for [a6] (Najdorf) or [Nc6] (Sveshnikov/Boleslavsky), Black lunges straight into the center with [e5]! Because b5 is NOT covered yet, White has a critical extra weapon here: [Bb5+]! After [Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Qxd7 Nf5], White trades off Black's good light-squared bishop—the key defender of d5 and f5—and plants a monster knight on f5 while d5 stays chronically weak! Retreating [Nb3] or [Nf3] lets Black get away with skipping [a6], whereas [Nf5] directly runs into [Nf5 d5]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gf1b5,Gd4b3] }
6. Bb5+ ( 6. Nf3 ) ( 6. Nb3 ) ( 6. Nf5 ) ) ( 5... Bd7
{ Viktor Kupreichik's cunning [Bd7] sideline! Instead of committing to [a6], [g6], [Nc6] or [e6] on move five, Black develops the light-squared bishop first—keeping Accelerated-style Dragon ([g6] without fearing [Bb5]), Classical ([Nc6]) and Scheveningen ([e6]) setups all alive! White's sharpest try is [Bg5], threatening to shatter Black's kingside pawns with [Bxf6] unless Black plays [Bg5 e6] or [Bg5 Nc6]; [Bc4] and [Be3] (aiming for [f3] and [Qd2]) also build active central pressure. [%cal Gc1g5,Gf1c4,Gc1e3] }
6. Bg5 ( 6. Bc4 ) ( 6. Be3 ) ) 6. Bg5
{ The old main line of the Najdorf, and one of the sharpest openings in chess. The bishop bears on the knight on f6, the piece that guards d5 and e4, and prepares [f4] with the threat of [e5]. Black nearly always answers [e6], so that a capture on f6 can be met without wrecking the pawns. [Nbd7] is the main alternative, keeping a second piece behind the knight. Either way, the game becomes a direct fight very quickly. [%cal Gg5f6,Ge7e6,Gb8d7] }
6... e6
{ Black meets the bishop with a solid pawn on e6, and the knight on f6 is now pinned against the queen. The standard plan is [f4], adding a pawn to the attack and preparing [e5] against the pinned knight. [Qf3] and [Qd2], followed by long castling, are other ways to bring the pieces quickly. Black is a little behind in development here, and must keep an eye on the classic piece sacrifices on e6, b5 and d5. [%cal Gf2f4,Gd1f3,Rg5d8] }
( 6... Nbd7
{ The knight goes to d7, so that a capture on f6 can be met by a piece and Black keeps the pawns healthy. White's most direct answer is [Bc4], aiming at e6 and f7; [f4] and [Qe2] are the other main moves. Black often follows with [Qa5], pinning the knight on c3 and eyeing g5, or with [e6] and [b5]. [%cal Gf1c4,Gf2f4,Bd8a5] }
