{ In this sharp Ivkov Najdorf line, [Qa5] pins the c3-knight to White's king and sets two immediate tactical traps: [Qxg5] hangs in one move, while careless moves allow either [Nxe4] (exploiting the pinned c3-knight and loose g5-bishop) or [Qc5] (forking the undefended bishop on c4 and knight on d4). [Qd2] is White's essential multipurpose reply, defending the g5-bishop, breaking the pin on c3 and preparing [O-O-O]; Black then plays [b:e6] and [b:b5] ([w:Bb3] [b:Bb7]), aiming at e4 before castling. [%csl Yd6][%cal Gd1d2,Ra5c3] }
8. Qd2 ( 8. Bd2 $4
{ Unpinning the knight looks sensible, but it gives the queen a double attack. [Qc5] hits the bishop on c4 and the knight on d4 together, and after [Qc5 Qe2 Qxd4] Black has won a piece. The bishop belonged on g5: [w:Qd2] guards everything at once. [%csl Rc4,Rd4][%cal Ga5c5] }
8... Qc5 ) ( 8. f4 $2
{ The pawn prepares [e5], but it takes away the last guard of e4. [Nxe4] grabs the pawn, and the knight on c3 can't take back because it is pinned. After [Nxe4 Nb3 Nxc3], Black has won material with a good game. Defend c3 and e4 first, with [w:Qd2]. [%cal Gf6e4,Ra5e1] }
