9. O-O-O
{ White has castled long and the kings are on opposite wings, so it is a race. Black's plan is [b5]: the bishop drops to b3, then [Bb7] and [b4] open lines toward White's king, with the queen on a5 already in place. White pushes in the centre or on the kingside, often with sacrifices on e6 or d5. The knight on d7 should stay where it is for now, guarding f6 and c5. [%cal Gb7b5,Bc4b3,Bc8b7] }
( 9. f4 $2
{ The pawn push is one move too early. [b5] chases the bishop from c4, and after it drops back the pawn on e4 has lost a defender, so Black's pieces get active with tempo. Castling long first, with [w:O-O-O], keeps everything protected. [%csl Rc4,Re4][%cal Gb7b5] }
9... b5 ) 9... b5 ( 9... Ne5 $2
{ The knight looks for c4, but it gives White a free tempo. [Bb3] drops the bishop back out of reach, and the knight on e5 stands in the way of Black's own pieces while White prepares [w:f4] with gain of time. Black's plan in these lines is [b:b5], not knight hops. [%csl Re5][%cal Gc4b3] }
10. Bb3 ) ) ( 6... e5 $6
{ The pawn claims space, but against the bishop on g5 it leaves Black short of guards on d5 and f5. [Nf5] jumps in at once, and after [Nf5 h6 Bxf6 Qxf6] White has traded the knight that covered d5 and has a strong knight on f5. Against [w:Bg5], [b:e6] is the move that keeps everything covered. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd4f5] }
7. Nf5 ) 7. f4
{ The main crossroads of the Najdorf with [Bg5]. White wants to push [e5] and hit the pinned knight, so Black must react. [Be7] breaks the pin and prepares castling, the classical line. [Qb6] attacks b2 and eyes the knight on d4: the famous Poisoned Pawn. [Nbd7] and [h6] are other sharp options. [Nc6] looks natural, but after [Nc6 Nxc6 bxc6 e5] the pinned knight is hit at once and Black is already clearly worse. [%cal Gf8e7,Gd8b6,Gb8d7] }
( 7. Qf3 ) ( 7. Qd2 ) 7... Be7
{ The classical [Be7] main line of the 6.[Bg5] Najdorf. Black calmly unpins the f6-knight with [Be7] and prepares kingside castling rather than entering the wild Complications of the Poisoned Pawn [Qb6]. White's overwhelming choice is [Qf3], building a battery behind the f-pawn, discouraging an immediate [b5] because of [e5] tactics against the unguarded a8 rook, and getting ready to castle queenside on the next move. [%csl Yf6,Yb5,Ye5][%cal Gd1f3] }
( 7... Qb6
{ The Poisoned Pawn Variation. The queen attacks b2 and also eyes the knight on d4. White's main answer gives the pawn: after [Qd2 Qxb2 Rb1 Qa3], Black is a pawn up but the queen is far from home and White has a big lead in development, the open b-file and the break [f5] or [e5]. These lines are very sharp and analysed deeply; one slip on either side is often fatal. [Nb3] protects the pawn more quietly, at the cost of taking the knight out of the centre. [%cal Gd1d2,Gd4b3,Rb6b2] }
8. Qd2 ( 8. Nb3 ) 8... Qxb2 9. Rb1 Qa3
{ The main line of the Poisoned Pawn. Black is a pawn up and the queen is out of play on a3; White must use the lead in development at once. The critical moves are [f5], hitting e6, and [e5], against the pinned knight. Slow moves such as [Bc4] or [Rb3] give Black time for [b:Nbd7] and [b:Be7], and then the extra pawn starts to count: the engine already prefers Black after them. [%csl Re6][%cal Gf4f5,Ge4e5] }
