Nc6
{ The knight hits the one on d4, which now has a single defender. The sharp answer is [Nxc6]: after [Nxc6 bxc6 e5] White chases the pinned knight before Black is organised. [Nb3] is a calm retreat, about equal. What White must not do is ignore the threat: any queen move that leaves d4 alone loses the knight. [%cal Gd4c6,Rc6d4] }
8. Nxc6 ( 8. Qf3 $4
{ The queen joins the attack, but it was the knight's only defender. [Nxd4] wins a piece outright, and the bishop on g5 doesn't help. In the Sicilian, always count the guards of the knight on d4 when Black puts a knight on c6. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gc6d4] }
8... Nxd4 ) ) 8. Qf3
{ With [Qf3], White completes the classical attacking alignment, eyeing e5 and preparing [O-O-O] followed by [g4] or [Bd3]. Black must avoid castling blindly into the storm before creating queenside counterplay. [Qc7] is the traditional move, controlling e5, guarding the b7-pawn so [b5] and [Nbd7] become safe, and watching the c-file; [h6] to question the bishop first and [Nbd7] are strong modern alternatives. [%cal Gd8c7,Gb8d7,Gh7h6] }
( 8. Bd3 $6
{ Developing the bishop looks natural, yet it blocks White's queen from watching d4 and walks into a tactical trick on e4. [h6] asks the dark-squared bishop to commit, and [h6 Bh4 Nxe4 Bxe7 Nxc3] wins a clean pawn with tempo on the queen. Play [Qf3] first to guard e4 and prepare long castling. [%csl Yd4,Ye4,Yh6][%cal Rh7h6] }
8... h6 9. Bh4 Nxe4 10. Bxe7 Nxc3 ) 8... Qc7
{ The Traditional Line of the 6.[Bg5] Najdorf. Black's [Qc7] controls the c-file, watches e5 and gets off the back rank so [Nbd7] and [b5] can launch queenside counterplay. White castles long with [O-O-O], bringing the queen's rook straight onto the half-open d-file opposite d6, and prepares either the surging [g4] pawn storm or the classic [Bd3] and [w:Rhe1] piece buildup. [%csl Ye5,Yb5,Yd6][%cal Ge1c1] }
( 8... Nbd7 ) ( 8... h6 ) 9. O-O-O
{ The classical main line. White has castled long and the rook on d1 lines up with the pawn on d6. Black should finish with [Nbd7], so that [e5] can be met by a knight; only then does [b5] make sense. Pushing the b-pawn first is the most common club mistake in this position. [%cal Gb8d7,Re4e5] }
9... Nbd7
{ The iconic starting tabiya of the Old Main Line Najdorf: [Nbd7] backs up the f6-knight so [w:e5] no longer wins a piece and prepares [b:b5] with [b:Bb7] for a ferocious opposite-side race. [g4] is the sharpest continuation, storming straight at f6 ([g4 b5 Bxf6 Nxf6 g5 Nd7 f5]), while [Bd3] develops the last minor piece first to prepare [w:Rhe1] and potential [w:Nd5] or [w:Bxe6] piece sacrifices. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gg2g4,Gf1d3] }
( 9... b5 $4
{ Black wants the queenside race, but the centre is not ready. [e5] hits the knight on f6, and it has no good square: after [e5 Bb7 Qg3], the queen also hits g7, and taking on e5 only opens the f-file once the pawn is back on e5. White wins the knight or breaks through on the kingside. Play [b:Nbd7] first, so the knight on f6 has a friend. [%csl Rf6,Ra8][%cal Ge4e5] }
10. e5 ) ( 9... Nc6 $6
{ Developing the knight to c6 instead of d7 fails to cover f6 and invites an immediate central rupture! [Nxc6 bxc6 e5 dxe5 fxe5 Nd5 Bxe7 Qxe7 Ne4] blasts open the d- and f-files while Black's king is still uncastled and lets White's knight dominate d6. Develop to d7 with [b:Nbd7] so the f6-knight is backed up. [%csl Yc6][%cal Rd4c6] }
