[Event "Sicilian Defence (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 ( 2. Nc3
{ The knight defends e4 and keeps White's plans open. If White follows with [Nf3] and [d4], the game returns to the Open Sicilian; if not, it becomes the Closed Sicilian with [g3], [Bg2] and [d3], or a Grand Prix Attack with [f4]. Black usually develops with [Nc6], keeping an eye on d4; [d6], [e6] and [a6] also keep many transpositions open. Against the closed set-ups, Black's simplest plans are to fianchetto with [g6] as well, or to take central space with [e6] and [d5]. [%cal Gf2f4,Gg2g3] }
2... Nc6
{ Both sides have developed a knight and kept their options. White chooses here. [Nf3] with [d4] heads back to the Open Sicilian. [g3] is the Closed Sicilian, a slow build-up with [Bg2], [d3] and a later kingside attack. [f4] is the Grand Prix Attack, which aims for a quick attack with [Nf3] and a bishop on b5 or c4. Black's main plans are to fianchetto with [g6] and fight for d4, or to set up with [e6] and [d5], hitting the center before White's attack gets going. [%cal Gg1f3,Gg2g3,Gf2f4,Gf1b5] }
( 2... d6 ) ( 2... e6
{ Black prepares [d5], a central break that suits slow set-ups. White can go back to the Open Sicilian with [Nf3] and [d4], stay closed with [g3] and [Bg2], or play [f4] for a Grand Prix. Against all of them Black's plans are much the same: [a6] and [b5] on the queenside, [Nc6] and [Nge7] or [Nf6], and [d5] when it comes with gain of time. The pawn on e6 also blunts a bishop that comes to c4, which then only bites on a pawn. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gg1f3,Gf2f4,Gg2g3] }
3. Nf3
{ White keeps [d4] in reserve. Black's best moves keep the options open: [Nc6] or [a6] develop and wait, and after [d4 cxd4 Nxd4] the game becomes a Taimanov or a Kan. [a6] also stops [Nb5] and prepares [b5]. [d5] at once is a French-style try that gives Black free piece play. The move order helps Black: with the knight already on c3, White can no longer set up a Maroczy Bind with [c4], so Black's [a6] and [Qc7] lines are easier to play. [%cal Gb8c6,Ga7a6,Gd7d5] }
( 3. f4
{ A Grand Prix against [e6]. Black's most direct reply is [d5], hitting e4 before White has castled, with free development whether White takes on d5 or lets Black take on e4. [Nc6] and [a6] keep things flexible, and [a6] with [b5] also takes b5 away from White's bishop. With the pawn on e6 in place, a white bishop on c4 has little to bite on, so White usually plays [Nf3] with [Bb5] or [Be2]. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5,Gb8c6,Ga7a6] }
3... d5 ( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... a6 ) ) ( 3. g3
{ The Closed Sicilian against an [e6] set-up. Because Black has not fianchettoed on g7, White's bishop on g2 will press cleanly along the long dark diagonal toward b7 and d5. Black has three sound ways to answer: [Nc6] followed by [g6] and [Bg7] to contest that diagonal directly, [d5] right away to challenge the centre while White's king's knight is still at home, or [a6] and [b5] for immediate queenside space. [%csl Ye6,Yg7,Yg2][%cal Gb8c6] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... a6 ) ( 3... d5
{ Korchnoi's [d5] strikes at the centre before White's bishop reaches g2, asking an awkward question of e4. Taking with [exd5] is cleanest: after [exd5 exd5 d4] (or [Bg2 Nf6 Nge2]), White targets the isolated d5-pawn down the long h1-a8 diagonal. Playing [Bg2] immediately allows [Bg2 d4 Nce2 Nc6 d3], where Black's space wedge on d4 blunts the fianchettoed bishop until White breaks with [w:f4]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge4d5,Gf1g2] }
4. exd5 ( 4. Bg2 ) ) ( 3... Be7 ) ) ( 3. Bc4 ) ( 3. Nge2 a6
{ White's Chameleon setup with [Nc3] and [Nge2] keeps Black guessing between a Closed Sicilian fianchetto and an Open Sicilian transposition, and Black's [a6] prepares [b5] while keeping White's pieces off b5. White's sharpest continuation is [d4], opening the centre into a Kan or Taimanov where the e2-knight recaptures cleanly on d4; [g3] for [Bg2] is the patient path. Drifting to g3 with the knight hands Black [Ng3 b5] for free. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gd2d4,Gg2g3] }
4. d4 ( 4. g3 ) ( 4. Ng3 $6
{ The knight eyes f5 and h5, but it gives Black a free hand on the queenside. [b5] gains space at once, and with [b:Bb7] and [b:Nc6] Black is the one playing for more. Open the centre with [w:d4] while the knights are ready. [%csl Yb7][%cal Gb7b5] }
4... b5 ) ) 3... Nc6 ( 3... a6
{ Black keeps every option: [a6] stops [Nb5] and prepares [b5]. White's main move is [d4], reaching a Kan after [d4 cxd4 Nxd4], where Black often follows with [Qc7] or [b5]. [g3] keeps a closed set-up, [Bc4] aims at e6 and invites [b5], and [a4] stops [b5] for good but spends a tempo. Black's set-up is flexible: [d6] or [d5], [Nc6] or [Nf6], and the queen on c7. If White plays slowly, [d5] hits the centre with every piece ready. [%csl Yb5,Yd5][%cal Gd2d4,Gg2g3] }
4. d4 ( 4. g3 ) ( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. a4 ) ) ( 3... d5 ) ) 3. Nf3 ( 3. g3
{ The Closed Sicilian, long a favourite of Spassky. White leaves the center closed: the plan is [Bg2], [d3], then [f4] with [Nf3] or [Nge2], and a kingside pawn storm, often with [Be3] and [Qd2]. Black's mirror answer is [g6] with [Bg7], then [d6] and play on the queenside with [Rb8] and [b5]. [e6] followed by [d5] is the other approach, grabbing central space while White is still building up. The game is slower than in the Open Sicilian, and understanding the plans matters more than theory. [%cal Gb7b5,Gg7g6,Ge7e6] }
3... g6
{ The symmetrical fianchetto in the Closed Sicilian, the most common path at club and master level alike. By mirroring White with [g6] and [Bg7], Black contests the h1-a8 diagonal and reinforces the clamp on d4 so White cannot easily open the centre. White completes the fianchetto with [Bg2], planning [d3], [f4] and either [Nf3] or [Nge2] for a kingside attack while Black prepares [Rb8] and [b5] on the queenside. [%csl Yg6,Yh1,Ya8][%cal Gf1g2] }
( 3... e6 ) ( 3... d6 ) 4. Bg2
{ Both sides fianchetto their king's bishops in the main line of the Closed Sicilian, turning the central dark squares d5 and d4 into the strategic prize of the opening. Black answers naturally with [Bg7], completing the fianchetto so the g7-bishop neutralizes White's counterpart along the long diagonal and clamps down on d4. From there, White plays [d3], [f4] (or [Be3] and [Qd2]) and [Nf3]/[Nge2] for a steady kingside buildup, while Black counters on the queenside with [d6], [Rb8] and [b5]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gf8g7,Bg2d5] }
4... Bg7
{ Both dark-squared bishops now face off along the long diagonal, clamping down on d4 and d5 from opposite ends. White almost always anchors the centre with [d3], followed by [Nge2] (keeping the f-pawn unblocked for [f4]) or [w:Be3] and [Qd2] to trade off Black's prized g7 bishop via [w:Bh6]. Black answers with [d6], [e6] and [Nge7], keeping the kingside sturdy while rolling [Rb8] and [b5] down the queenside. [%csl Yd4,Yd5,Yd3][%cal Gd2d3] }
5. d3
{ This is the starting tabiya of the Classical Closed Sicilian. White gives up any immediate [d4] break in favor of a slow, coiled kingside attack with [f4], [Nf3] (or [Nge2] to keep the f-pawn and g2-bishop unobstructed) and [O-O], aiming to push [w:f5] or [g4] and [w:g5] later on. Black has two main setups: [d6] with [e5] (or [Nf6]) plus [Rb8] and [b5] to uproot the c3-knight on the queenside, or [e6] and [Nge7], keeping [d5] in reserve in the center. [%cal Gd7d6,Ge7e6,Bf2f4] }
( 5. Nge2 ) 5... d6
{ The main position of the Closed Sicilian. Both sides have fianchettoed, and the center is fixed enough for slow plans. White chooses between [Be3] with [Qd2], aiming to trade the dark-squared bishops with [Bh6], and [f4] with [Nf3] and castling, preparing a push to f5 and a kingside attack. [Nge2] keeps the f-pawn free to advance. Black plays on the other wing with [Rb8], [b5] and a push to b4 to chase the knight from c3, or sets up in the center with [e6] and [Nge7], or with a pawn on e5 against the [f4] plan. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gc1e3,Gf2f4,Gg1e2] }
( 5... e6 ) ( 5... Nf6 ) 6. Be3
{ Instead of committing the f-pawn to f4 immediately, White plays [Be3] first—preparing [Qd2] so a battery on the c1-h6 diagonal can trade off Black's prized g7-bishop with [Bh6], while keeping d4 reinforced and [f4] or [Nge2] flexible! Black can stop [Bh6] schemes cold with [e5] (clamping down on d4 and f4, followed by [Nge7] and [Nd4]), play [e6] and [Nge7] calmly, or develop [Nf6]—just watching out for [Nf6 h3] so [b:Ng4] never harasses the e3-bishop. [%cal Ge7e5,Gg8f6,Be3h6] }
( 6. f4
