{ With [Bb5], White plays a Rossolimo-style pin with the queen's knight already on c3, eyeing [Bxc6] to saddle Black with doubled c-pawns. Because White has skipped [w:Nf3] and [w:c3], Black's sharpest reply is [Nd4], attacking the bishop at once and forcing [Bc4] or [Nf3] before White can wreck the structure. [e6] preparing [b:Nge7] and [g6] heading for a fianchetto on g7 are solid positional alternatives. [%cal Gc6d4,Ge7e6,Gg7g6] }
3... Nd4 ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... g6 ) ) ( 3. Nge2
{ The Chameleon Variation keeps both doors open for White: [d4] transposes into an Open Sicilian right after Black commits to a setup, while [g3] and [Bg2] steer into a Closed Sicilian with the f-pawn still unblocked for [f4]. Black answers calmly with [g6] and [b:Bg7] to fight for d4, [e6] preparing [b:d5] or a Taimanov shape, or [d6] and [Nf6] keeping flexible Classical and Sveshnikov options. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg7g6,Ge7e6,Gd7d6] }
3... g6 ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... Nf6 ) ) ( 3. d3 g6
{ Both sides adopt Classical Closed Sicilian geometry: White locks in a solid d3-e4 pawn chain while Black prepares to fianchetto on g7, aiming to dominate the dark squares d4 and e5 and expand on the queenside with [Rb8] and [b5]. White has three harmonious setups from here: [g3] and [Bg2] for the main Closed Sicilian kingside buildup, [Be3] and [Qd2] (the Chameleon/Spassky system, eyeing [Bh6] to trade off Black's prized g7-bishop), or [Nf3] keeping [Bg5] open. [%cal Gg2g3,Gc1e3,Bg1f3] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. Be3 ) ( 4. g3 ) ) 3... e5
{ Black takes d4 with a pawn so that the Open Sicilian never comes, an anti-Sveshnikov set-up. The price is a hole on d5, where White's knight on c3 is ready to jump. White's best plan is [Bc4], aiming at f7 and d5, with [d3], castling and [Nd5]; [Bb5] with [Bxc6] is also good. Black must be careful with the king's knight: after [Bc4], [Nf6] lets [Ng5] hit f7, so [Be7] or [d6] comes first. [%csl Yd5,Yf7][%cal Gf1c4,Gf1b5] }
