{ Black declines to take on d4 and gets ready for [b:d5], the French way. White's most flexible answer is [Nf3]: after [Nf3 cxd4 Nxd4] the game is an Open Sicilian, and [Nf3 d5] gives a French-like centre. [d5] grabs space and makes the pawn on e6 look odd, though Black can trade it off with [b:exd5]. [c3] is an Alapin with the d-pawn already in the centre. Taking on c5 gives the centre away: [dxc5 Bxc5] develops Black's bishop with tempo. [%cal Gg1f3,Gd4d5,Gc2c3] }
3. Nf3 ( 3. d5
{ White grabs space, and the pawn on d5 cramps Black's game. The simplest answer is [exd5]: after [exd5 exd5 d6], White's pawn on d5 gains space but needs support, and Black develops easily with [b:Nf6] and [b:Be7]. [d6] keeps the tension, and Black can take on d5 later. Leaving the pawn alone for long is risky: after [w:dxe6] or [w:c4], Black is either left with a weak pawn or squeezed. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge6d5,Gd7d6] }
3... exd5
{ Black trades off the cramped e-pawn to reach a Modern Benoni pawn structure. Recapturing with [exd5] is White's strongest continuation: after [exd5 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3], White holds a clean central space advantage and enjoys smooth development because White has not committed the c-pawn to c4, leaving c4 free for a bishop or knight. [Qxd5] keeps pieces active without central tension, and [b:Nf6] equalises. [%csl Yc4][%cal Ge4d5,Gd1d5] }
( 3... d6 ) ( 3... e5 $6
