3... cxd4 4. c3 Qa5 5. f4 d6 ) 3... d5
{ Black has set up a French with the c-pawn already on c5. [exd5] is the usual answer: after [exd5 exd5] the centre opens, and a check on b5 with quick castling puts pressure on the pawn on d5; Black often ends up with an isolated d-pawn. [e5] is less good than it looks: it gives a French Advance in which no pawn on c3 holds d4, so [b:cxd4] and [b:Nc6] put it under fire at once. [dxc5] lets the bishop take back with tempo. [%csl Yd5,Ye4][%cal Ge4d5] }
4. exd5 ( 4. c3 $2
{ Trying to bolster d4 Alapin-style with [c3] forgets that the e4-pawn is hanging! [dxe4 Ne5] drops a clean central pawn while White's c3-pawn blocks the b1-knight from ever recapturing or attacking cleanly. Resolve the central tension first with [w:exd5] or [w:Bb5+]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd5e4] }
4... dxe4 5. Ne5 ) ( 4. e5
{ A French Advance in which White hasn't played [w:c3], so d4 has only the knight and the queen behind it. Black hits it at once: [Nc6] brings a second attacker, with [b:Qb6] to follow, and after [w:c3] the game is the main line of the French Advance. [cxd4] is just as good: the pawn on e5 loses its base, and the knight comes to c6 with tempo on whatever takes back. The bishop on c8 is the problem piece, shut in behind e6: it usually goes to d7, and Black is glad to trade it. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6,Gc5d4] }
4... Nc6
{ Black brings the knight out to c6 to pile up on White's d4 base, reaching the Nimzowitsch move order of the French Advance. Because [Nf3] came before [c3], White can either bolster the pawn chain with [c3]—transposing into the main French Advance where Black answers [Qb6] and [Bd7]—or gambit the d4-pawn with [Bd3] (the Milner-Barry plan), castling fast and using the e5 wedge for dark-square kingside pressure. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gf1d3,Gc2c3] }
( 4... cxd4
{ Black has taken on d4, and White takes back. [Nxd4] is natural, but after [Nxd4 Nc6] the pawn on e5 needs help, and a trade on c6 frees Black's game. [Qxd4] keeps a firmer grip on e5, though [b:Nc6] gains a tempo on the queen, which usually drops to f4. [Bd3] offers the pawn for development, a gambit for attacking players. Either way the pawn on e5 is White's pride and Black's target: [b:Qc7], [b:Nge7] and [b:f6] all go after it. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gf3d4,Gd1d4] }
5. Nxd4
{ By recapturing on d4 with the knight after trading c-pawns, White reaches an Advance French structure without [w:c3], putting pieces on central squares while e5 lacks pawn support. Black develops smoothly with [Nc6] to challenge the d4-knight and hit e5 ([Nc6 Nxc6 bxc6 Bd3 Ne7]), or [Ne7] first heading for g6 or c6 to pile up on White's advanced e5-pawn while White bolsters it with [w:f4]. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gb8c6,Gg8e7] }
( 5. Qxd4 ) 5... Nc6 ( 5... Ne7 ) ) ( 4... c4 $2
