{ The Opocensky Variation, White's most positional answer to the Najdorf. The bishop prepares short castling, and White plays for a steady edge rather than a direct attack. Black's most popular reply is [e5]: after the knight drops back to b3, Black plays [Be7], castles, then [Be6] and [Nbd7], and the game turns on d5. [e6] instead leads to a Scheveningen. The knight from d7 often goes to b6 or f6 to fight for d5, and [b5] must wait until a knight can't land there. [%cal Gd6d5,Ge7e5,Ge7e6] }
