{ The Amsterdam Variation. The pawn on f4 prepares [e5] against the knight on f6 and adds pressure on the kingside. Black's most direct answer is [e5], hitting the knight and the pawn on f4; after the knight drops back to f3, Black plays [Nbd7], [Be7] and castles. [e6] and [g6] are also good. What Black must avoid is a slow queenside move that lets [e5] come at once. [%cal Ge7e5,Ge7e6,Be4e5] }
6... e5 ( 6... e6 7. Be2 Qc7 8. O-O Nc6
{ A classical Najdorf-Scheveningen tabiya where Black puts pressure on White's d4-knight and threatens [b:Nxd4] or [Qb6] along the open g1-a7 diagonal toward White's castled king. Before launching any pawn advance, White must neutralize that diagonal: [Be3] overprotects d4 while [Kh1] steps the king safely out of pins and checks. Rushing with [f5] surrenders the e5 square and allows [f5 Qb6] with nasty tactical pressure. [%csl Yd4,Yg1,Ya7][%cal Gg1h1] }
9. Kh1 ( 9. Be3 ) ( 9. f5 $2
{ The pawn attacks e6, but it gives Black the e5 square and opens the diagonal of the king. [Qb6] pins the knight on d4 against the king on g1: after [Qb6 Be3 e5], the knight can't move and White loses material. Play [w:Kh1] or [w:Be3] first, then push. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gc7b6,Rb6g1] }
9... Qb6 ) ) ( 6... g6 ) ( 6... b5 $2
{ Gaining queenside space is a normal Najdorf plan, but here it gives White the move [e5]. After [e5 dxe5 fxe5], the knight on f6 is attacked, and the pawn on b5 has also loosened the long diagonal toward a8. Against [w:f4], answer [b:e5] or [b:e6] first, so the push has no target. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge4e5] }
7. e5 ) ) ( 6. Bc4
{ The Fischer-Sozin Attack. The bishop aims at e6 and f7, the weak points around the black king, and White often follows with [Bb3], castling and [f4] with [f5]. Black's main answer is [e6], blunting the diagonal, then [b5] to chase the bishop; it drops back to b3 and keeps its pressure through the pawn. White's typical weapons are piece sacrifices on e6 and d5, so Black has to check them at every move. [e5] is a sharper alternative that leaves d5 weak. [%csl Yf7][%cal Ge7e6,Ge7e5] }
6... e6
{ The main position of the Fischer-Sozin. Black has blunted the bishop with [e6] and will chase it with [b5], so White usually drops it to b3 at once, where it keeps its eye on e6. White follows with castling and [f4] with the idea [f5], or with [Be3], [Qe2] and long castling. Black develops with [Be7], castling, [b5] and [Bb7], and a knight to c6 or d7. Sacrifices on e6 and d5 are White's main weapons, so Black should check them before every quiet move. [%cal Gc4b3,Ge1h1,Rb3e6] }
( 6... e5 ) 7. Bb3
{ The Fischer-Sozin / Najdorf [Bc4] hybrid. After [e6] blunted the diagonal toward f7, White tucks the bishop proactively back onto b3 before Black gains a free tempo with [b5]! From b3, the bishop still eyes e6 and f7—always threatening [f4] and [w:f5] or sacrifices on e6—while clearing the c-file and dodging [Nc6] trades. Black responds vigorously on the queenside with [b5] (preparing [Bb7] against e4 and [Nbd7] toward c5), or develops [Nbd7] and [Be7] solidly first. [%cal Gb7b5,Gf8e7,Rb3e6] }
( 7. O-O ) ( 7. Be3 ) 7... Be7 ( 7... b5
{ The Flank Variation of the Najdorf Sozin ([b5]). Black wastes zero time on the queenside, threatening [b4] right now to kick White's c3-knight away from defending the e4-pawn! White must reckon with e4 immediately: [Bg5] pins f6 (leading to razor-sharp theory after [Bg5 Be7 Qf3 Qc7] where [b4] is met by [w:Nce2] or piece sacrifices!), [f3] bolsters e4 calmly in English Attack style ahead of [Be3] and [Qd2], while [O-O] offers a dangerous pawn gambit after [O-O Be7 Qf3]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gc1g5,Gf2f3,Rb5b4] }
8. Bg5 ( 8. f3 ) ( 8. O-O ) ( 8. a3 ) ) ( 7... Nbd7 ) ) ( 6. g3
{ The Zagreb Variation fianchettoes on g2 against the Najdorf to control d5 down the long light diagonal and tuck the king away safely with [Bg2] and [O-O]. [e5] is Black's most direct challenge, kicking the d4-knight to e2 ([e5 Nde2 Be7 Bg2 O-O]) so White can plan [w:Nd5] or [w:h3] and [w:g4]; [e6] (Scheveningen fianchetto) and [g6] (symmetrical fianchetto) are solid alternatives. [%cal Ge7e5,Gg7g6,Ge7e6,Bf1g2] }
6... e5 ( 6... g6 ) ( 6... e6 ) ) ( 6. Rg1
{ The Petronić / Freak Attack against the Najdorf ([Rg1] on move six)! White puts the rook directly behind the g-pawn before moving a single bishop, preparing an instant [g4] and [w:g5] bayonet lunge that sidesteps the main Keres/English lines! Black has three crisp antidotes: [e5] kicks the d4-knight first ([e5 Nb3 Be6 g4 d5] blasts open the center while White's king is still on e1!), [h5] stops [g4] in its tracks, and [e6] builds a solid Scheveningen shield. [%cal Ge7e5,Gh7h5,Bg2g4] }
6... e5 ( 6... e6 ) ( 6... h5 ) ) ( 6. Bd3
{ The Yates Variation, a quiet line. The bishop on d3 guards e4 solidly, so White can play castling, [f4] or [Nde2] without worrying about the pawn, but it blocks the d-file and does little else. Black has a free choice: [e5] kicks the knight and gains space, [e6] sets up a Scheveningen, and [g6] a Dragon. After [e5], the knight's retreat to f3 or e2 leaves Black comfortable, with [Be7], castling and [Be6] to come. [%cal Ge7e5,Ge7e6,Gg7g6] }
6... e5 ( 6... e6 ) ( 6... g6 ) ( 6... Nc6 ) ) ( 6. g4
{ A direct pawn storm before any piece has moved. The pawn on g4 is attacked twice, by the knight and the bishop, and defended only by the queen, so Black can simply take it with [Bxg4]: the engine likes Black after it. [e5] is the solid choice, with [b:h5] to follow against the pawn, and the game is level. What Black should not do is let the pawn advance for free: after a slow move, [g5] kicks the knight and White's attack runs itself. [%csl Rg4][%cal Gc8g4,Ge7e5] }
6... Bxg4 ( 6... e5 ) ( 6... e6 $2
{ This is the normal Scheveningen move, but it lets the g-pawn do its job. [g5] kicks the knight back to d7, and with [w:Be3], [w:h4] and [w:Qd2] White gets a fast attack without having spent anything. The pawn on g4 was there for the taking: [b:Bxg4] or [b:Nxg4] wins it. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gg4g5] }
7. g5 ) ) *

[Event "Sicilian Defence (3)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 ( 2. Nc3 d6
{ Black keeps e5 covered and waits for White's plan. [Nf3] with [d4] returns to the Open Sicilian with [d6], where Black can choose the Najdorf or the Dragon. [f4] heads for a Grand Prix and [g3] for a Closed Sicilian, both with a slow kingside build-up. [Bb5+] is a Moscow-style check, best met by [Bd7]. Against the closed set-ups, Black does well with [Nc6] and [g6], or with [a6], [b5] and [Bb7] against e4, and can answer an early [f4] with a well-timed [e5]. [%cal Gf1b5,Gc8d7] }
3. Nf3
{ White brings out the king's knight and offers to transpose straight back into the Open Sicilian on [d4]. Black can accept the invitation with [Nf6], [Nc6] or [a6], reaching familiar Najdorf, Classical or Dragon waters if White opens the centre. Because White delayed [d4] by a move, Black also has the independent [e5], clamping down on d4 for good and steering the game into a quiet, manoeuvring structure where [Be7] and [Nf6] follow smoothly. [%cal Gg8f6,Gb8c6,Ga7a6,Ge7e5] }
( 3. f4
{ A Grand Prix with Black's pawn already on d6. Black's sound replies are [Nc6] and [g6], aiming the pieces at d4 and the long diagonal, or [Nf6], which develops and hits e4. [a6] with [b5] and [Bb7] is another good plan: it takes b5 away from White's bishop and puts pressure on e4. Later Black can strike with [e5]: the hole on d5 is covered by Black's pieces, and White's f-pawn becomes a target. [%csl Gd5][%cal Ga7a6,Gb7b5,Ge7e5] }
3... Nc6 ( 3... Nf6 ) ( 3... g6 ) ( 3... a6 ) ( 3... e5 $6
{ Pushing [e5] after already spending a move on [d6] wastes a full tempo and tears open a permanent hole on d5 with zero pieces developed to guard it! After [Nf3 Nc6 Bb5], White pins the sole defender of d5 and castles quickly with a lasting positional grip. Develop pieces first with [b:Nc6], [b:g6] or [b:Nf6]. [%csl Ye5][%cal Rg1f3] }
4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Bb5 Ne7 6. O-O ) 4. Nf3
{ A Grand Prix set-up with the pawn on d6. Black's best is the fianchetto, [g6] with [Bg7], which puts pressure on d4 and the long diagonal; [Nf6] with [e6] or [g6] is also sound. White plans [Bb5] or [Bc4], castling, [d3] and the queen to e1 and h4. Pushing [e5] now opens the f-file for White's attack, since the f-pawn can take on e5. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg7g6,Gg8f6] }
4... g6
{ This Grand Prix Attack position includes an early [d6] by Black instead of [e6], steering toward a Closed Dragon defense. Because Black has already spent a tempo on [d6], a single-step [d5] break is off the table; instead, Black plans [Bg7], [Nf6] (or [e6] and [Nge7]), [b:O-O] and a fast queenside pawn storm with [Rb8] and [b5]. White's best try is [Bb5], pinning c6 so [Bb5 Bg7 Bxc6+ bxc6] leaves Black with chronically weak doubled c-pawns, or [Bc4] eyeing the open a2-g8 diagonal. [%cal Gf1b5,Gf1c4,Rc4f7] }
( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... e5 $6
{ The pawn hits f4, but the trade helps White. [Bb5] pins the knight first, and after [Bb5 Bd7 fxe5 dxe5] the f-file and the d5 square are White's. Play [b:g6] and fight for the dark squares instead. [%csl Rd5,Yf4][%cal Gf1b5] }
5. Bb5 ) 5. Bb5 ( 5. Bc4 ) ( 5. d3 ) ( 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 Bg7 7. Be3
{ White has transposed from a Grand Prix move order into an Open Sicilian against the Dragon with [f4] and [Be3] already established. Black's king's knight must come out to [Nf6] right now: that attacks e4, guards d5 and gets Black ready to castle kingside on the next turn. Touching the e-pawn prematurely is disastrous here, as both [e5] and [e6] leave d6 chronically weak and allow [Ndb5] straight away. [%csl Ye4,Yd5][%cal Gg8f6] }
7... Nf6
{ Black has developed and hits e4. White should develop too, with [Be2] or [Nxc6], and castle, then play for [e5] or [f5] with the pieces behind it. Pushing [e5] at once opens the d-file before White is ready, and the queens come off with White's pawn on f4 the weaker for it. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf1e2,Gd4c6] }
( 7... e5 $4
{ The pawn hits the knight and f4, but it opens d6. [Ndb5] heads straight there: after [Ndb5 exf4 Nxd6+] the knight checks and Black's king has to move. Develop with [b:Nf6] first. [%csl Rd6][%cal Gd4b5] }
8. Ndb5 ) ( 7... e6 $2
{ The pawn covers d5 but leaves d6 with no guard. [Ndb5] hits it, and the bishop must go back to f8 to hold it. Black is tied up for a long time. [b:Nf6] was the move. [%csl Rd6][%cal Gd4b5] }
8. Ndb5 ) 8. Be2 ( 8. Nxc6 ) ( 8. e5 $2
{ The push looks strong, but the d-file opens with the queens facing each other. After [dxe5 Nxc6 Qxd1+ Rxd1 bxc6], the queens are gone and White's pawn on f4 is a weakness. Develop with [w:Be2] first. [%csl Rd1,Rf4][%cal Gd6e5] }
8... dxe5 ) 8... O-O
{ Having started with a Grand Prix move order before opening the centre with [d4], White has reached a Classical Dragon with [f4] already in place. Now that Black is castled and ready to generate counterplay along the c-file or via [b:e5] and [Qb6], White should castle short with [O-O] or retreat [Nb3] to avoid simplifying exchanges. Throwing [g4] forward unguarded blunders a clean pawn to [g4 Bxg4]. [%csl Rg4][%cal Ge1g1,Gd4b3] }
9. O-O ( 9. Nb3 ) ( 9. g4 $2
{ The storm comes too early: the pawn on g4 has no defender. [Bxg4 Bxg4 Nxg4] follows, and the knight hits the bishop on e3. White is a pawn down. Castle first. [%csl Rg4,Re3][%cal Gc8g4] }
9... Bxg4 ) ) ) ( 3. g3 ) ( 3. Bb5+ ) ( 3. d4
{ After starting with the Closed Sicilian move [Nc3], White switches gears and opens the center with [d4] anyway! Black should snap off [cxd4] without hesitation: leaving c5 undefended or playing passively would let White capture [dxc5] and saddle Black with a fractured queenside or a worse endgame after a queen trade on d8. Once [cxd4] is played, White has no knight on f3 yet, which points straight toward recapturing with the queen via [cxd4 Qxd4], a Chekhover-style Open Sicilian. [%cal Gc5d4,Rd4c5] }
3... cxd4
{ Black trades on d4 and asks how White intends to justify delaying [Nf3]. Recapturing with the queen via [Qxd4] is the only sound continuation, placing the queen centrally while keeping [Qxd4 Nc6 Bb5] or [Bg5] and [w:O-O-O] ready for rapid piece pressure. Avoid the tempting gambit [Nf3], since [Nf3 dxc3] simply pockets a clean extra pawn with no real Morra-style open c-file compensation because White's knight is already gone from b1. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd1d4] }
4. Qxd4
{ With [Qxd4] and the knight already on c3, White reaches an accelerated Chekhover/Carlsen Sicilian while keeping f4 unblocked on the kingside! Black immediately challenges the exposed queen with [Nc6], forcing White to choose between pinning with [Nc6 Bb5 Bd7] and dropping the queen back to d2 ([Nc6 Qd2], Magnus Carlsen's signature setup, preparing [b3] and [Bb2] down the long diagonal). Playing [a6] first denies b5 before [Nc6], while [Nf6] develops calmly. [%cal Gb8c6,Gg8f6,Ga7a6] }
4... Nc6 ( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... a6 ) 5. Qd2
{ Magnus Carlsen's signature Sicilian weapon! Instead of pinning on b5 as in the Chekhover, White tucks the queen calmly onto d2, keeping the bishop pair and planning [b3] plus [Bb2] to aim down the long a1-h8 diagonal while [w:O-O-O] and [f4] prepare a flexible kingside buildup. Black develops naturally with [Nf6] and either [g6] + [Bg7] (neutralizing b2 head-on along the long diagonal) or [a6] and [e6] (building a compact Scheveningen/Najdorf shell against e4). [%cal Gg8f6,Gg7g6,Bb2g7] }
5... Nf6 ( 5... g6 ) ( 5... a6 ) ) 3... Nf6 ( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... a6 ) ( 3... e5
) ) ( 2. c3
{ The Alapin. White prepares [d4] so that the c-pawn can take back on d4, keeping two pawns in the center. It avoids most of the Open Sicilian's theory and leads to open, positional games. Black's two main answers hit the center before [d4] is ready: [d5] attacks e4 with a pawn, and [Nf6] attacks it with a piece, provoking White's pawn forward. [e6] with [d5], a French-style set-up, is the third option. The drawback of [c3] is that it takes the best square away from White's queen's knight. [%cal Gc3d4,Gg8f6,Gd7d5] }
2... Nf6
{ Black attacks e4 with a piece, asking White to push it forward. [e5] is the main answer, since the knight can't defend from c3: the pawn gains space and kicks the knight to d5, where it stands well but can be harassed later. White then builds the center with [d4] and [Nf3], and if Black takes on d4, the c-pawn recaptures. Defending with [d3] is too passive to promise anything. Black's main idea afterwards is to undermine the pawn on e5 with [d6]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge4e5,Rf6e4] }
( 2... d5
{ Black challenges e4 at once. White almost always takes with [exd5], and Black takes back with the queen, which is safe in the center because the pawn on c3 stops [Nc3] from chasing it. [e5] instead closes the center, but here Black's light-squared bishop can still come out to f5 or g4, which makes the position easier for Black than a similar Advance French. That free bishop is the main reason [d5] is so popular against the Alapin. [%csl Yc3][%cal Ge4d5,Ge4e5] }
3. exd5
{ Black chooses how to take back. [Qxd5] is the main line: the queen is safe on d5 since no knight can reach c3, and after [d4], Black develops with [Nf6], [e6] or [Nc6], often with the bishop on g4. [Nf6] is a gambit-like alternative: Black recaptures later with the knight, but White can hold on to the pawn for a while with a pawn on c4 or [Bb5+], in exchange for falling behind in development. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd8d5,Gg8f6] }
( 3. e5 ) 3... Qxd5
{ Black's queen stands in the centre, and here it is safe: the pawn on c3 keeps White's knight off c3, the square it would use to chase the queen. White's main move is [d4], building the classical Alapin centre; [Nf3] first keeps more options. Black's plan is the same either way: develop with [Nf6], [e6] or [Nc6], put the bishop on g4 or f5, and press on d4. The queen often stays on d5, and drops back to d8 or d6 when a knight on c3 or a pawn on c4 hits it. [%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3,Gd5d4] }
( 3... Nf6
{ The Anti-Alapin Gambit ([Nf6] instead of recapturing [b:Qxd5]). Black offers a pawn—or invites White to overextend trying to cling to d5—in order to recover it with the knight on d5 and save the queen tempo that [b:Qxd5] usually concedes to [w:Nc3]! White's soundest response is [d4], transposing into an IQP/Grünfeld-like fight after [d4 Nxd5 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4] (or [d4 Qxd5]), whereas greedy [c4] lets Black undermine the chain fiercely with [c4 e6] (a sharp Icelandic/Marshall-style gambit). [%csl Yc4][%cal Gd2d4,Rf6d5] }
4. d4 ( 4. c4 ) ( 4. Nf3 ) ) 4. d4
{ White has the classical Alapin center: the pawn on d4 is backed by c3, and if Black takes on d4, the c-pawn recaptures, often leaving White with an isolated pawn but free piece play. Black develops with [Nf6], then [e6] or a bishop on g4 that pins a knight on f3 and adds pressure on d4. [Nc6] hits d4 at once. White's knights often come to f3 and a3, the second one heading for b5 or c2 and sometimes gaining time on the queen. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg8f6,Gb8c6,Ge7e6] }
( 4. Nf3 ) 4... Nf6
{ Instead of putting the queen's knight on c6 right away, Black plays [Nf6] to keep the c-pawn mobile and maintain the option of [Bg4] or [e6] without committing to a specific setup. White develops calmly with [Nf3], after which Black can pin with [Nf3 Bg4], lock down the light squares via [Nf3 e6], or transpose back to the main line with [Nf3 Nc6]. Grabbing [dxc5] allows [dxc5 Qxc5] (avoiding a queen trade if Black wishes) where Black recovers the pawn smoothly with active pieces. [%cal Gg1f3,Gc1e3,Bc8g4] }
( 4... Nc6
{ In the Barmen Alapin, Black develops [Nc6] immediately to pile pressure onto White's d4-pawn alongside the centralized queen on d5 and c5-pawn. White almost always plays [Nf3], defending d4 and inviting [Nf3 Bg4] (where [Nf3 Bg4 Be2] unpins cleanly) or [Nf3 cxd4 cxd4], which enters the classic isolated queen's pawn tabiya. Supporting d4 with [Be3] is tricky too, daring Black to trade on d4 while eyeing c5, whereas [dxc5 Qxd1+ Kxd1] trades into a harmless endgame. [%cal Gg1f3,Rc6d4,Rd5d4] }
5. Nf3
{ The Barmen Defence in the [d5] Alapin. Black develops the queen's knight early to put immediate pressure on d4 while White's king's knight steps to f3. Black's sharpest plan is [Bg4], pinning the defender of d4 and threatening [Bxf3] followed by [Qxd4], or preparing [e6] and long castling for central piece pressure. [cxd4] followed by [e5] opens the game briskly, while [Nf6] heads into classical IQP waters. [%csl Yd5,Yd4,Yf3][%cal Gc5d4] }
( 5. Be3 ) ( 5. dxc5 ) 5... cxd4
{ Having developed [Nc6] and provoked [Nf3], Black captures on d4 to enter the main isolated queen's pawn battleground of the Alapin. White should recapture with [cxd4], unlocking the c3-square so [w:Nc3] will hit Black's queen on d5 with tempo and seize active central play. Taking with the knight via [Nxd4] avoids the isolated pawn, but after [Nxd4 Nxd4 Qxd4 Qxd4 cxd4] (or [Nxd4 e5]), Black equalizes effortlessly in a symmetrical, tension-free endgame. [%cal Gc3d4,Gb1c3] }
( 5... Bg4 ) ( 5... Nf6 ) 6. cxd4 ( 6. Nxd4 ) 6... e5 7. Nc3
{ The knight hits the queen. Black should play [Bb4], pinning the knight and keeping the queen in the centre. After [Bb4], the fight is about d4 and e5: Black takes on d4 at the right moment and gets active pieces against White's isolated pawn. The queen's retreats are worse: on a5 it runs into [w:d5], and on d6 into a knight jump to b5. [%csl Rc3,Yd4][%cal Gf8b4] }
7... Bb4 ( 7... Qa5 $4
{ The queen steps aside, but [d5] kicks the knight on c6 off its guard of e5. After [d5 Nb8 Nxe5], White has won a pawn with a big centre. Pin with [b:Bb4] instead. [%csl Rc6,Re5][%cal Gd4d5] }
8. d5 ) ( 7... Qd6 $4
{ The queen stays close to d4, but it stands in the knight's path. [Nb5] hits it with tempo, and after [b:Qd8] the push [w:d5] attacks the knight on c6 while the knight on b5 eyes c7: Black can't meet both threats and loses material. [b:Bb4] pinned the knight first. [%csl Rd6,Rc7][%cal Gc3b5] }
8. Nb5 ) 8. Be2
{ In the Milner-Barry line of the Barmen Alapin, White unpins with [Be2] and gets ready to castle short while central tension peaks. Black should liquidate immediately with [exd4]: because the c3-knight is still pinned until White castles, [exd4 O-O Nf6] gives Black smooth piece play against White's isolated d4-pawn. Pushing [e4] instead locks the centre while Black's king is still uncastled and hands White's knight e5 or g5. [%csl Yc3,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Ge5d4] }
8... exd4 ( 8... e4 $2
{ The pawn hits the knight on f3, but it closes the centre with Black behind. [O-O] comes, and after [O-O Bxc3 bxc3 Nf6 Ba3], White's bishop takes the a3-f8 diagonal and the pawn on e4 is a target. [b:exd4] keeps the game open. [%csl Re4,Ya3][%cal Ge1g1] }
9. O-O ) ) ( 4... e6 ) ( 4... cxd4 ) ( 4... g6 5. Nf3
{ The Fianchetto Barmen Defense against the Alapin ([g6]). Since the queen is out on d5, Black fianchettoes the dark-squared bishop onto g7 to turn up maximum Grünfeld-like heat on White's d4-pawn! Black usually plays [Bg7] right away, preparing [Nh6] toward f5 (keeping the g7-bishop's diagonal wide open!) plus [Nc6] and [cxd4] against d4. White meets [Bg7] energetically with [Bg7 Be3 cxd4 cxd4] followed by [w:Nc3], chasing the queen off d5 to seize active central play. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gf8g7,Gc5d4] }
5... Bg7 ( 5... cxd4 ) ( 5... Nc6 ) ) 5. Nf3
{ The main line of the Alapin with [Qxd5]. Black chooses where the light-squared bishop goes: [Bg4] pins the knight and adds pressure on d4; [e6] keeps the bishop in and prepares a solid set-up with [Be7] and castling; [Nc6] brings one more piece against d4. White usually aims for [Be2], castling and a well-timed push to c4 to gain time on the queen. When Black trades on d4, White often gets an isolated pawn there and plays for activity with [Nc3]. [%cal Ge7e6,Gc8g4,Gb8c6] }
( 5. Be3 ) ( 5. dxc5 ) 5... e6
{ Playing [e6] in the [Qxd5] Alapin shuts the c8-bishop inside the pawn chain in exchange for rock-solid control of d5 and fast kingside castling via [b:Be7] and [b:Nc6]. [Be2] and [Bd3] prepare [O-O] before White recaptures [w:cxd4] into a classic isolated-queen's-pawn middlegame, [Be3] hits c5 directly to force [b:cxd4], and [Na3] eyes b5 and c4 with uncomfortable tempo threats against Black's queen. [%cal Gb1a3,Gf1e2,Gc1e3,Gf1d3] }
( 5... Bg4
{ The Barmen Modern Line posts the c8-bishop actively on g4 ahead of [b:e6], pinning the f3-knight to heighten pressure on d4 without locking the bishop in. [Be2] is the classical main line: after [Be2 e6] (or [Be2 cxd4 cxd4 e6 Nc3]), White unpins, castles short and plays an active IQP position with [w:h3] and [w:Be3]. [dxc5] is the endgame test, inviting [dxc5 Qxd1+ Kxd1] (or [dxc5 Bxf3 Qxf3 Qxf3 gxf3]). [%csl Yd4][%cal Gf1e2,Gd4c5] }
6. Be2 ( 6. dxc5 ) ) ( 5... Nc6 ) 6. Na3 ( 6. Be2 ) ( 6. Be3 ) ( 6. Bd3 ) ) (
2... e6
{ Black prepares [d5], a French-style answer to the Alapin. After [d4 d5], White chooses between taking on d5, an open position where Black usually recaptures with the pawn and gets easy development, and [e5], which leads to an Advance French. Because the pawn on c3 takes away the knight's best square, White's pieces are a little less active than usual in these lines. Black should be ready to transpose to the French Defense, as that is where many games end up. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3] }
3. d4
{ White claims the full pawn centre, and Black's most direct response is [d5], turning the Alapin into a French-type battle. White then chooses between [d5 exd5 exd5], an open isolated-queen's-pawn game where Black's light-squared bishop is free, and [d5 e5], an Advance French where Black already has [c5] in to pressure d4 with [b:Nc6] and [b:Qb6]. Taking with [cxd4] instead gives White the ideal [cxd4] recapture. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5,Gc5d4] }
( 3. Nf3 ) 3... d5 ( 3... cxd4 ) ) ( 2... Nc6
{ Black develops a piece rather than challenging e4 immediately, which lets White build the full pawn centre that [c3] prepared. Pushing [d4] right away forces Black to react: after [d4 cxd4 cxd4], Black has to strike back with [d5] or [b:e5] before White's pawns roll forward with [w:d5]. Developing [Nf3] first is playable too, usually transposing once Black answers [Nf6] or [d5]. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3] }
3. d4 ( 3. Nf3 ) ) ( 2... d6
{ A passive reply to the Alapin that concedes the central squares without a fight. Because Black has committed the d-pawn to d6, a later [d5] break will cost an extra tempo, so White claims the centre immediately with [d4]. After [d4 Nf6 Bd3], White keeps both central pawns intact, brings the knights to f3 and d2 or e2, and enjoys a lasting space advantage with easy kingside development. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd2d4,Gf1d3] }
3. d4 ) 3. e5
{ The pawn kicks the knight. [Nd5] is the only good square: the knight stands in the centre, hits c3 and b4, and can't be chased by a pawn at once, since [c4] would leave d4 weak. [Ng8] hands back all the time Black gained. Black's next moves are [Nc6], [d6] or [e6], all aimed at the pawn on e5. [%csl Ye5,Yc3][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 3. d4 $6
{ The central push leaves e4 without a guard. [Nxe4] takes it, and [w:d5] only chases the knight. Push the pawn first with [w:e5], or the knight on f6 keeps hitting it. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf6e4] }
3... Nxe4 ) 3... Nd5
{ The knight is centralized on d5 and can't easily be chased. White's main move is [d4], offering a pawn trade that brings the c-pawn to d4; [Nf3] keeps the d-pawn's options for one more move, and [Bc4] hits the knight at once. Black usually answers [d4 cxd4], then [Nc6] or [d6] to hit e5 before White is comfortable, or a pawn on e6 for a solid French-like structure. White's space on the kingside is the trump; Black's is pressure against e5 and d4. [%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3,Gf1c4] }
