5. d4 ( 5. Bc4 Nb6
{ In the Stoltz Attack against the [Nf6] Alapin, White's bishop develops with tempo on the d5-knight, and Black retreats to b6 to counter-attack the bishop and clear the d-pawn's path. White should drop the bishop back to [Bb3], keeping pressure along the a2-g8 diagonal while awaiting Black's choice between [d5] and the flank squeeze [c4+] plus [Qc7]. Flashy sacrifices on f7 fail completely because Black's king tucks safely onto g8. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gc4b3,Rb6c4] }
6. Bb3
{ The Stoltz Attack in the [Nf6] Alapin! White spent a tempo with [Bc4] to drive Black's centralized knight from d5 out to b6, then tucked the bishop safely onto b3 where it eyes f7 and supports [d4]. Black has three sharp ways to strike back: [c4] locks the b3-bishop out of the game ([c4 Bc2 Qc7 Qe2] followed by [b:g5] is a famous modern line targeting e5), [d5] grabs central space ([d5 exd6 Qxd6]), and [d6] undermines e5 directly ([d6 exd6 Qxd6]). [%cal Gc5c4,Gd7d5,Gd7d6] }
( 6. Bxf7+ $4
