{ White develops as usual, and Black picks a defensive set-up. [d6] with [e6] and [Nf6] is the classical approach, keeping the knight on c3 out of b5 and d5 as far as possible. [e6] first, with [a6] and the knight to e7, is another reliable plan. [g6] with a fianchetto is also played. Black has to watch d6 and f7, where most of White's tactics land, and should avoid opening lines while behind in development. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd7d6,Ge7e6,Gg7g6] }
( 5. Bc4
{ In the Smith-Morra Gambit Accepted, White sends the bishop straight to c4 before developing the g1-knight, taking aim at the vulnerable f7 square along the a2-g8 diagonal. Black must neutralize that diagonal first: [e6] is the cleanest answer, blunting c4 at once and preparing [a6], [Nf6] and [Qc7] (the Siberian setup) or [d6] and [Be7]. Playing [d6] keeps Classical and Scheveningen structures open, whereas careless development with [Nf6] walks straight into the unpleasant thrust [Nf6 e5]. [%cal Ge7e6,Gd7d6,Rc4f7] }
5... e6
{ Black's [e6] shuts down the bishop's line toward f7 and prepares to build a compact fortress on the sixth rank. White nearly always develops [Nf3], transposing into the main Smith-Morra tabiya where [Nf3], [Qe2], [O-O] and [w:Rd1] generate tremendous central pressure for the sacrificed pawn. Bringing out [Bf4] right away is an enterprising alternative that eyes the dark-square hole on d6 and threatens [Nb5], forcing Black to watch c7 and d6 carefully with [a6] or [d6]. [%csl Yd6][%cal Gg1f3,Gc1f4] }
( 5... d6 ) ( 5... g6 ) 6. Nf3 ( 6. Bf4 ) 6... d6 7. O-O
{ A classical Scheveningen defensive shell against the Smith-Morra Gambit. Black builds a sturdy barrier on d6 and e6 to blunt White's c4-bishop and cover e5 and d5, at the price of slightly slower development. Black can bring the king's knight out via [Nf6], unpin and prepare castling with [Be7], or play [a6] and [b5]—heading into the flexible Chicago system where [Nge7] and [Bb7] follow. [%cal Ga7a6,Gg8f6,Gf8e7] }
7... a6 ( 7... Nf6 ) ( 7... Be7 ) 8. Qe2 b5 9. Bb3
{ The starting tabiya of the Chicago Defence in the Smith-Morra Gambit. Having nudged White's bishop back to b3 with [a6] and [b5], Black aims for a flexible hedgehog-like setup where the queen's rook sometimes lifts to a7 and d7 while the king's knight goes to e7 and g6. Timing is critical: Black must play [Nge7] or [Bb7] first to get pieces out, because an immediate [Ra7] walks into [Ra7 Be3] with loss of time. [%csl Ya7][%cal Gg8e7,Gc8b7] }
9... Nge7 ( 9... Bb7 ) ( 9... Ra7 $2
{ The rook lift is the Chicago's signature, but it comes before the pieces are out. [Be3] hits the rook at once, and after [Be3 Ra8] Black has lost two tempi in a gambit where tempi are everything. [%csl Ra7][%cal Gc1e3] }
