{ The pawn on e6 blunts the bishop on c4 and keeps the d-pawn flexible. Black often plays [a6], so that [b5] can gain time on the bishop, and [Qc7], eyeing c4 and the long diagonal toward h2. That diagonal is the key to the Siberian Trap: after [Qc7 O-O Nf6 Qe2 Ng4 h3 Nd4], the knight hits the queen and the knight on f3, the only guard of h2. White must avoid that careless [h3]. [%csl Yh2][%cal Ga7a6,Gd8c7,Gg8f6] }
6... a6
{ The Paulsen Formation against the Smith-Morra Gambit. Black plays [a6] early to keep White's knights out of b5, stop [Bb5] pins and prepare [b5] with tempo against the c4-bishop. White castles short with [O-O]—or plays [Qe2] first—planning [Qe2], [w:Rd1] and [Bf4] or [Bg5] to bring every piece into the attack before Black's kingside knights and dark-squared bishop can untangle and contest the open central files. [%csl Ya6,Yb5,Yc4][%cal Ge1g1] }
( 6... Qc7
{ The Siberian Variation ([Qc7]) of the Smith-Morra Gambit—one of Black's most venomous counter-systems! Black covers e5, controls the c-file, and sets up the famous Siberian Trap: if White castles carelessly, [O-O Nf6 Qe2 Ng4] threatens [b:Nd4] deflecting the f3-knight from h2 for mate! White prevents all [b:Ng4] + [b:Nd4] tricks cleanly with [Qe2 Nf6 O-O a6 Rd1] (or kicking the queen via [Nb5] when Black omits [a6], or retreating [Bb3] proactively). [%cal Gd1e2,Gf1b3,Gc3b5] }
