7. Ng5 Nh6 ) ) ( 2. c4
{ White grips d5 with two pawns at once, a Maroczy-style set-up without the Open Sicilian. It costs the d4 square: no white pawn can ever cover it again, and Black's knight is welcome there after [Nc6], [g6] and [Bg7]. Black develops normally and aims the pieces at d4; [e6] with [Nc6] and the other knight on e7 is another sound plan. White usually goes on with [Nc3], [d3] and [g3], a slow game where good piece placement matters more than theory. [%csl Yd4,Gd5][%cal Gb8c6,Ge7e6] }
2... Nc6
{ White has clamped d5 with pawns on c4 and e4, but has given up d4, which no white pawn can cover any more. Black's ideal set-up uses that hole: [g6], [Bg7] and a knight on d4, with [e6] and [Nge7], or [e5] to fix the pawns. White usually plays [Nc3], [d3] and [g3], a closed set-up like the Botvinnik English. The game stays slow, and the side that makes better use of its outpost is usually the one that comes out on top. [%csl Yd4,Gd5][%cal Gb1c3,Gd2d3] }
( 2... e6 ) ( 2... d6
{ Against White's early Maroczy grip on c4 and e4, Black plays [d6] to stake out the dark squares c5, d6 and e5 without committing the knights yet. Because White has left a permanent hole on d4 by pushing both c- and e-pawns, Black plans [Nc6], [g6] and [Bg7]—or [e5] to lock the center and maneuver via d4. White develops naturally with [Nc3] and [Nf3], often fianchettoing on g2 and preparing queenside expansion with [a3] and [Rb1] or kingside play with [d3] and [f4]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb1c3,Bg1f3] }
3. Nc3
{ White brings the queen's knight to c3, tightening control over d5 while keeping the kingside flexible between [Nf3] and [Nge2]. Black can develop [Nc6] directly to contest d4 and head for a Botvinnik-style setup with [Nc6 g3 g6 Bg2 Bg7], bring out [Nf6] to eye e4 and keep [e6] and [Be7] available, or play [e6] to cover d5 before deciding where the knights belong. Rushing [e5] before [Nc6] is slightly premature, as White still has easy dark-square play. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gb8c6,Gg8f6] }
