{ White has pawns on c4 and e4, a Botvinnik-style clamp on d5. Black's simplest is [g6] and [b:Bg7]: the bishop bites on the long diagonal, and the knight can later jump to d4. [e5] copies the bind, so each side gets a hole, on d4 for Black and d5 for White. [e6] gets ready to hit d5 with the d-pawn. The clamp costs White something: the pawn on d3, when it comes, is backward, and d4 is Black's square for good. [%csl Yd4,Gd5][%cal Gg7g6,Ge7e5] }
( 3. d3 ) ( 3. Nf3 ) 3... g6 ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... e5 ) ) 2... d6
{ The pawn on d6 guards e5, so White's knights can never settle there, and it opens the diagonal of the light-squared bishop. It is the gateway to the Najdorf, the Dragon, the Classical and the Scheveningen, all reached after [d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3] and a fifth move of Black's choice. White's main move is [d4]. Players who want to sidestep the theory choose [Bb5+], the Moscow Variation, or [c3], so that White can take back on d4 with a pawn; [Bc4] is common at club level. [%cal Gd6e5,Gd2d4,Gf1b5] }
