{ The Accelerated Dragon. Black fianchettoes without first playing [d6], so that the d-pawn can later go to d5 in one move, the best antidote to a Yugoslav-style attack. The price is that White gets an extra option: [c4], the Maroczy Bind, clamps d5 with pawns on c4 and e4 and leaves Black cramped, with play on the dark squares. [Nc3] is the other main move, aiming for the usual Open Sicilian set-up with [Be3] and [Bc4]. [%cal Gd7d5,Gc2c4] }
5. Nc3 ( 5. c4
{ The Maroczy Bind. With pawns on c4 and e4, White controls d5 for good, and Black's freeing breaks [d5] and [b5] become very hard to arrange. Black has to play patiently: [Bg7], [Nf6], [d6] and castling, then a trade of knights to ease the cramp and a later break with [b5] or [f5]. The bind is not a refutation: Black's pieces stand well on the dark squares, and a careless White can find the pawns on c4 and e4 turning into targets. [%cal Gc4d5,Ge4d5] }
