{ With [Bg7], Black immediately attacks the central knight on d4, forcing White to defend or move it before completing the Maroczy Bind. White almost always plays [Be3], keeping the knight centralized and preparing [Nc3] and [Be2] to squeeze Black for space. Trading with [Nxc6] relieves the tension too early, since [Nxc6 bxc6] gives Black the open b-file and extra central pawn mass to prepare [d5] or [f5]. [%csl Yd4,Yd5,Yf5][%cal Gc1e3] }
( 5... Nf6
{ The knight hits e4, which the pawn on c4 can't defend. White must develop with [Nc3] first, after which the Maroczy main lines begin: [Be2] or [Be3], castling, and a slow squeeze. The bishop move that looks natural here drops the pawn. Black's plan in the bind is [d6], castling, a trade of knights on d4 and [Be6] or [Nd7], then [a5] and a knight on c5, or a break with [b5] or [f5] later. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb1c3,Rf6e4] }
