{ The Open Sicilian with [e6]. Black takes with [cxd4 Nxd4] and keeps a light, flexible centre: the d-pawn can go to d6 in Scheveningen style, or to d5 in one move. Compared with the [d6] lines, Black's dark-squared bishop has more options, not only e7 but also c5 or b4. Developing the knight to c6 first is a slip: the pawn on d4 pushes on. [%csl Yd5,Yb4][%cal Gc5d4] }
3... cxd4
{ The same choice with Black's pawn on e6. [Nxd4] leads to the Taimanov after [Nc6], the Kan after [a6] and the Four Knights after [Nf6]. With the e-pawn moved, Black's dark-squared bishop is free, so a bishop check or pin from b4 and a jump to c5 against the knight on d4 come up often. [c3], a Morra-style gambit, is the main alternative. [Qxd4] is playable, but [Nc6] gains time on the queen at once. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gf3d4,Gc2c3] }
( 3... Nc6 $2
{ The knight develops, but it lets the d-pawn advance with tempo. [d5] kicks it, and after the knight moves White has space and Black's pawn on e6 is hit. Take on d4 first with [b:cxd4]. [%csl Rc6][%cal Gd4d5] }
