{ Black's main set-ups split here. [Nc6] is the Taimanov, which challenges the knight on d4 and keeps the d-pawn's choice open. [a6], the Kan, keeps the white knights off b5 and prepares [b5] and a bishop on b7, with the queen often on c7. [Nf6] hits e4 and, after [Nc3], leads to the Four Knights, the Pin Variation with the bishop on b4, or a Scheveningen with [d6]. In all of them Black delays the d-pawn, so White can't build the ideal attacking set-up in advance. [%cal Gd7d5,Gb8c6,Ga7a6] }
( 4. c3
