{ The Kan, one of Black's most flexible systems. The pawn on a6 keeps the white knights off b5, and Black can choose later between [Qc7], [Nf6], [b5] and [Bb7], or a bishop on c5. White's most popular reply is [Bd3], guarding e4 and keeping [c4] available; [Nc3] is more direct, and [c4] at once sets up a Maroczy Bind. The cost of Black's flexibility is a slight lag in development, so White should try to open the game before Black is ready. [%cal Gf1d3,Gb1c3,Gc2c4] }
5. Bd3
{ White guards e4 with the bishop and keeps the c-pawn free to go to c4, a Maroczy Bind that cramps the Kan. Black has three main ideas. [Bc5] chases the knight from d4 before White has castled. [Nf6], often followed by [Qc7], develops calmly. And a hedgehog set-up with [d6], [b6] and the bishop on b7 keeps Black's pawns on the third rank, waiting for [b5] or [d5] to break out. White usually castles, plays [c4] and [Nc3], and tries to keep Black squeezed. [%cal Gf8c5,Gg8f6,Gd8c7] }
( 5. Nc3
{ White develops naturally and leaves the defense of e4 to the knight. Black usually plays [Qc7], which watches e5 and c3 and prepares [Nf6] or [b5]. [b5] at once gains queenside space and prepares a bishop on b7, adding pressure on e4. [Nc6] transposes to the Taimanov. White's main plans are [Bd3] with short castling, or [Be3], [Qd2] and long castling in the spirit of the English Attack, with a kingside pawn storm. [%cal Gd8c7,Gb7b5,Gb8c6] }
5... Qc7
{ The main tabiya of the Kan Variation: Black's queen controls e5 and pressures the c-file while Black holds back both knights to stay flexible against White's setup. [Bd3] is White's most popular grandmaster choice, supporting e4 so [f4] and [O-O] follow harmoniously. [Be2] is the solid positional approach preparing [O-O] and [Be3], while [Qf3] prepares [Qg3] and fast kingside pressure before Black finishes developing. [%cal Gf1d3,Gf1e2,Gd1f3] }
( 5... b5
{ The Wing Attack of the Kan Variation. Black delays developing both knights to grab queenside space with [b5], threatening [b:b4] to kick White's c3-knight away from its guard duty over e4 while [Bb7] adds fire along the long diagonal. White answers solidly with [Bd3], guarding e4 so the knight can move if nudged, [Be3] to coordinate with [f3] and [Qd2], or [a3] to halt [b:b4] in its tracks. [%csl Yb5,Yb4,Yc3][%cal Ga2a3] }
6. a3 ( 6. Be3 ) ( 6. Bd3 ) ) ( 5... Nc6 ) 6. Bd3 ( 6. Be2 ) ( 6. Qf3 ) ) ( 5.
c4
{ White establishes the Maroczy Bind against the Kan Variation, planting pawns on c4 and e4 to clamp down on d5 and [b5]. Black develops [Nf6] to attack e4 and force [Nf6 Nc3], from where Black can either adopt the patient Hedgehog structure—with [Qc7], [b6], [Bb7], [d6] and [Be7] waiting for a well-timed [b5] or [d5] break—or pin immediately with [Bb4+] to disrupt White's smooth coordination. [%cal Gb6b5,Gd6d5] }
