{ Black attacks e4 at once. White defends with [Nc3], the natural move, after which Black chooses between the Four Knights, a Scheveningen with [d6], and the Pin Variation with the bishop on b4. [Bd3] also defends the pawn, more quietly. The tempting [e5] is a known mistake: after [e5 Qa5+], the queen checks the king and attacks the pawn on e5 at the same time, and Black wins it. That check is worth remembering, as it recurs in many Sicilian lines. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gb1c3,Gf1d3] }
