{ With [Nf6] and [Nc3] on the board in the [e6] Sicilian, Black stands at a major crossroads because White threatens to push [w:e5] and chase the f6-knight around. Black has three classical answers that all address e5: [Nc6] reaches the Four Knights Sicilian, [d6] sets up the resilient Scheveningen pawn centre, and [Bb4] pins the c3-knight to fight for e4 directly. Playing [a6] here fails to stop [a6 e5]. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gb8c6,Gd7d6,Gf8b4] }
( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. e5 $2
{ The pawn chases the knight, but [Qa5+] checks and attacks e5 at the same time. After [Qa5+ Nc3 Qxe5+], Black is a pawn up and White's king is still in the centre. Guard e4 with [w:Nc3] instead. [%csl Re5,Re1][%cal Gd8a5] }
