10. f3 ) ) ( 6... e6 ) 7. Nb3 ( 7. Nf3 ) ( 7. Ndb5 ) ) ( 6. Be3 ) ( 6. f3 ) )
6. Be3
{ White starts the Yugoslav Attack with the bishop on e3, where it supports d4 and can later go to h6 to trade off the dragon bishop. Black's natural reply is [Bg7]. Hitting the bishop with [Ng4] first is a known mistake. Once the bishop stands on g7, though, [Ng4] becomes a real threat, which is why White plays [f3] soon after. [%cal Gh2h4,Ge3h6] }
( 6. Be2
{ The Classical Dragon. White passes on the race of the Yugoslav and plays for a positional edge: the bishop on e2 prepares short castling, and White follows with [Be3], [Nb3] and [f4], aiming to control d5. Black is comfortable here: after [Bg7], castling and [Nc6], the dragon bishop presses on the long diagonal, and Black plays [Be6], a rook to c8 and [a5] or [a6] with [b5]. Understanding the structure matters more than memory. [%cal Gf8g7,Gb8c6,Be1g1] }
6... Bg7
{ The Classical Variation of the Sicilian Dragon. Instead of the cutthroat Yugoslav Attack ([Be3], [f3], [Qd2], [w:O-O-O]), White develops [Be2] and prepares short castling for a positional, controlled battle where the kings stay on the same flank. White plans [O-O], [Be3] and [Nb3] (avoiding tactical hits on d4) followed by [f4] and [w:Bf3] to neutralize the g7-bishop. Black develops [Nc6] and [O-O], aiming for [Bd7], [b:Rc8] and counterplay down the half-open c-file or a timely [d5] break. [%cal Ge1g1,Gc1e3,Bg7d4] }
( 6... Nc6 ) 7. O-O ( 7. Be3 ) ( 7. Bg5 ) ( 7. h4 ) 7... Nc6
{ White has castled and wants [Be3], [Nb3] and [f4], the classical plan against the Dragon. The move order matters: the knight on d4 has only the queen behind it, and the pawn on e4 only the knight on c3. [Be3] or [Nb3] first keeps everything protected. Black castles and plays [Be6], [a6] or [a5], and the knight on c6 often goes to a5 and c4. [%cal Gc1e3,Gd4b3,Rc6d4] }
( 7... O-O 8. Be3 Nc6
{ The main starting position of the Classical Dragon. With both sides castled short, White's Attack is strategic rather than a mating race: [Nb3] sidesteps simplifying trades on d4 so Black has less room, [Qd2] eyes h6 and connects the rooks, [f4] claims kingside space, and [h3] rules out [Ng4]. Black answers with [Be6], [Rc8] and [a6] or [Qa5], aiming a knight toward the c4 outpost. [%cal Gd4b3,Gf2f4,Gd1d2] }
9. Nb3 ( 9. f4 ) ( 9. Qd2
{ The Battery Variation of the Classical Dragon ([Be3] and [Qd2] instead of retreating [Nb3]). By setting up the queen-bishop battery right away while castled short, White invites [Ng4]! After [Ng4 Bxg4 Bxg4], White plays [Nd5] or [f4] to seize space while Black enjoys the unopposed Dragon bishop on g7 and light-square control! If Black prefers to keep tension rather than trade minor pieces, [Bd7] followed by [Rc8] and [Ne5] (heading for c4) leads to a rich strategic battle. [%cal Gc8d7,Gf6g4,Be3h6] }
9... Bd7 ( 9... Ng4 ) ( 9... a6 ) ) ( 9. h3 ) 9... Be6 10. f4
{ The classical tabiya. White has set up [f4] with the idea of [f5] or [g4], hitting the bishop on e6. Black's plans are [Rc8] and [Qc8] with play on the c-file, or [Na5] toward c4, where a knight trades off one of White's good pieces. With less space, Black is happy to trade: a knight on c4, the c-file against c2 and a healthy pawn structure make the endgames pleasant for Black. The central break [d5] looks liberating but is not ready yet. [%csl Gc4,Rc2][%cal Gc6a5,Ga5c4,Ga8c8] }
