{ A key Classical Dragon tabiya where Black has held back castling for a move ([Nc6] on move eight) or prepares queenside activity right away! Usually [O-O] reaches the main Classical Dragon ([O-O Nb3 Be6]), while [Bd7] and [a6] prepare [Rc8] and [b5]—and also keep [Ng4] on the radar if White leaves e3 exposed instead of retreating [Nb3]! White's positional blueprint is [Nb3] (dodging [Nxd4] simplifications and [Ng4] hits) followed by [f4] and [Bf3]. [%cal Gc8d7,Ga7a6,Bd4b3] }
( 8. Nb3 ) ( 8. f4 $2
{ The f-pawn push looks aggressive, but it opens a fork trick. [Nxe4 Nxe4 Nxd4] follows: Black wins the pawn back with the knight, which now hits the bishop on e2, and the long diagonal opens for the dragon bishop. Black is a clean pawn up. Play [w:Be3] first, so the knight on d4 has two guards. [%cal Gf6e4,Rc6d4] }
8... Nxe4 ) 8... O-O ( 8... Bd7 ) ( 8... a6 ) ) ( 6. f3 ) ( 6. f4
{ The Levenfish Attack. The pawn on f4 prepares [e5] to kick the knight from f6, before Black's bishop reaches g7. Black's best answers are [Nc6] and [Nbd7], which cover e5 and make the push harmless. After [Bg7], the push [e5] comes at once, and Black must know the one good answer. [%cal Ge4e5,Gb8c6,Gf8g7] }
6... Nc6 ( 6... Nbd7 ) ( 6... Bg7
{ The natural move, but now [e5] hits the knight. It is not a mistake yet: after [e5 Nh5], the knight finds a square and Black is fine. The danger is in the other knight moves, because a check from b5 follows and the knight on d7 or g4 gets in the way. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge4e5] }
7. e5
{ The knight on f6 is attacked. [Nh5] is the safest answer: after [Bb5+ Bd7] the knight stays out of trouble. Taking first with [dxe5] is just as good, but after [dxe5 fxe5] the knight must go to d7 and Black has to meet the [w:e6] break with care. Retreating to d7 at once is worse: [w:e6] comes with more force, and White keeps a clear edge. Every other knight move runs into [Bb5+], [e6] or both. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gf6h5,Gd6e5] }
( 7. Be2 ) 7... Nh5 ( 7... dxe5 8. fxe5
{ Levenfish's sharp [e5] strike against the Dragon move order reaches its critical test once White recaptures on e5 and attacks the f6-knight. Black has only one move that holds the position together, [Nfd7], keeping e5 under fire and blocking the d-file so that after [Nfd7 e6 Ne5] Black survives the tactical storm with solid counterplay. Active-looking hops like [Ng4] or retreating [Ng8] collapse to [Bb5+] and [w:e6] ideas. [%cal Gf6d7,Re5e6] }
