9. Bb5+ ) ( 8... Ng8 $2
{ Back home, the knight is safe but Black's position isn't. [Bf4] and then [w:Bb5+] or [w:e6] follow, and Black can't develop the kingside at all. After [fxe5], only [b:Nfd7] holds. [%cal Gc1f4,Re5e6] }
9. Bf4 ) ) ) ) ( 6. g3
{ The Fianchetto Variation against the Sicilian Dragon ([g3]). By opposing Black's Dragon bishop along the long diagonal from g2 and castling short, White takes all the sting out of Black's usual half-open c-file mating counterattacks—turning the Dragon into a technical, positional grind! Black develops [Nc6] (careful: [Bg7] first allows the trick [Bg7 Nde2] or [Bg7 Bg2 Nc6 Nde2], avoiding trades on d4!) so that if White retreats [Nc6 Nde2], Black hasn't lost flexibility, then completes [Bg7], [b:O-O] and [Bd7]. [%cal Gf8g7,Gb8c6,Bf1g2] }
