[Event "Sicilian Defence (4)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 ( 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. f4
{ The Grand Prix Attack. White aims straight at the black king: [Nf3], then the bishop to b5 or c4, short castling, and an attack with the f-pawn and the queen swinging over to h4. Black's best plans fight back in the center. [g6] with [Bg7] is solid and puts pressure on d4 and b2. [e6] with [d5] hits e4 before White's attack gets going, and it also blunts a bishop aimed at f7. Black should not rush to castle short into a ready-made attack. [%cal Gg1f3,Gf1b5,Gf4f5] }
3... g6
{ Via the [Nc3] move order, the game enters the main tabiya of the Grand Prix Attack. Black's [g6] prepares [Bg7] to clamp down on d4 and e5, followed by [e6] and [Nge7] so the f-pawn stays free and [d5] breaks open the center whenever White lunges too aggressively on the kingside. White's main replies are [Nf3] (keeping [Bb5] and [Bc4] both on the table for one more move), [Bb5] immediately to double Black's c-pawns after [Bb5 Bg7 Bxc6], and [Bc4] aiming at f7. [%cal Gg1f3,Gf1b5,Gf1c4] }
( 3... e6
{ Black prepares [b:d5] to hit the centre before White's attack gets going. [Nf3] is the main move, and after [Nf3 d5] White can choose between taking on d5 and pushing to e5, a French-like position where White's f-pawn is already in. [Bb5] pins the knight first, so that a later [w:Bxc6] doubles Black's pawns. [Bc4] aims at e6, but the push to d5 would hit it with tempo, so it's best kept for later. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1b5] }
4. Nf3
{ Black has a choice of set-ups. [d5] is the most direct: it hits e4 at once, and the knight on c3 has to decide. [Nf6] develops and also eyes e4, and [a6] keeps White's bishop off b5 before the d-pawn moves. Slow moves give White time for [w:Bb5] or [w:Bc4] and a kingside attack with castling and [w:Qe1]. Black's plan is the centre: [b:d5], the bishop to e7 and short castling. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5,Gg8f6,Ga7a6] }
( 4. Bb5 ) 4... d5
{ Black strikes at e4 with [d5], the principled antidote to the [e6] Grand Prix Attack. [Bb5] is White's best continuation: pinning the c6-knight preserves tension in the centre and threatens [Bxc6+] to ruin Black's queenside pawns before White castles short. [d3] holds the chain together quietly, whereas [e5] hands Black the f5 square for [b:Nh6] and [b:Nf5] and [exd5 exd5] frees Black's c8-bishop completely. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf1b5,Gd2d3] }
( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... a6 ) 5. Bb5
{ Pinning the c6-knight is White's critical move in the [e6]/[d5] Grand Prix Attack, keeping central tension alive while preparing [Bxc6+] and [O-O]. [Ne7] is Black's cleanest answer, supporting the pinned knight so [b:a6] will force a decision without ruining Black's queenside pawns. Pushing [d4] closes the centre and kicks the c3-knight ([d4 Bxc6+ bxc6 Ne2]), giving Black the bishop pair against static pawns. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gg8e7,Gd5d4] }
( 5. d3 ) 5... Ne7 ( 5... d4 ) ) ( 3... d6
{ Black keeps e5 covered against the Grand Prix. White plays [Nf3], then [Bb5] or [Bc4], castling and [d3], with [Qe1] and [Qh4+] to come for a kingside attack. [Bb5] at once pins the knight and keeps [Bxc6+] in reserve. Black's best set-up is [g6] and [Bg7] with [Nf6] and castling, or [e6] with [Nge7]: with the pawn on d6, White's push to e5 is harder to arrange, and a later [e5] or [f5] by Black frees the game. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1b5] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. Bb5 ) ) 4. Nf3
{ White completes knight development with [Nf3], guarding e5 and d4 before committing the light-squared bishop to b5 or c4. Black almost always finishes the fianchetto with [Bg7], seizing the long dark-squared diagonal and inviting White's main try [Bg7 Bb5] or the classical attacking setup [Bg7 Bc4 e6 d3 Nge7 O-O] followed by [Qe1] toward h4. Playing [d6] first is playable too, steering toward a Closed Dragon structure where Black attacks on the queenside with [Rb8] and [b5]. [%csl Bd4][%cal Gf8g7] }
( 4. Bb5 ) ( 4. Bc4 ) 4... Bg7
{ The main line of the Grand Prix Attack against the fianchetto. [Bb5] is the modern choice: White is ready to take on c6, double Black's pawns and then attack with [f5] or play on the light squares. [Bc4] aims at f7 for a direct attack, and Black usually answers with [e6] to blunt the bishop, then [Nge7] and [d5]. [d3] with short castling is the slower plan. Black's dark-squared bishop is a strong piece here, and trading it lightly would leave the king's position weak. [%csl Rh7][%cal Gd1e1,Ge1h4,Gf4f5] }
( 4... d6 ) 5. Bb5 ( 5. Bc4
{ In the classical Tal/Larsen branch of the Grand Prix Attack, White points [Bc4] straight at f7 and dreams of castling short, swinging [Qe1] over to h4 on the kingside and crashing through with [f5]. Black's most accurate antidote is [e6], blunting the c4-bishop's diagonal at once and preparing [Nge7] plus [d5] in the center to counterattack before White's kingside attack gets off the ground. [d6] is playable as well, though it gives [Bc4] a freer diagonal and slows down [d5]. [%csl Bd5][%cal Ge7e6,Rc4f7] }
5... e6
{ Black's [e6] builds a rock-solid barrier against c4 and threatens to gain a clean tempo in the center with [Nge7] and [d5] (or even [b5] after [a6]). White's soundest course is [d3], anchoring e4 and c4 ahead of [O-O] and the signature Grand Prix maneuver [Qe1] to h4, keeping [f5] in reserve until the rooks and bishop join in. Pushing [f5] prematurely right now lets Black capture [f5 gxf5 exf5 d5] with a tremendous central preponderance. [%csl Re6][%cal Gd2d3,Bd1e1] }
( 5... d6 ) ( 5... a6 ) 6. d3 ( 6. e5 ) ( 6. O-O ) ) ( 5. d3 ) ) ( 2. c3 d5 3.
exd5 Qxd5 4. d4 cxd4
{ Black resolves the central pawn tension early in the Alapin by capturing on d4, clarifying the pawn structure before developing the minor pieces. White's natural recapture is [cxd4], accepting an isolated queen's pawn on d4 in exchange for the half-open c-file, easy piece development and the key tempo gain [cxd4 Nc6 Nf3] followed by [w:Nc3] hitting the queen on d5. Playing [Nf3] first as an intermezzo is fine as well, usually transposing right back after Black develops [Nf3 Nc6 cxd4]. [%cal Gc3d4,Gg1f3] }
5. cxd4
{ White recaptures [cxd4], liberating the b1-square for the queen's knight—the whole strategic point of the Alapin! White now has an isolated d4-pawn that grants space and outposts on e5 and c5, while Black aims to blockade d5 and attack d4 directly. Black's main move is [Nc6], hitting d4 at once so White cannot gain a free tempo with [Nc3] first; [Nf6] is solid too, and the sharp strike [e5] tries to liquidate the isolated pawn structure immediately at the cost of open lines. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6,Gg8f6] }
( 5. Nf3 ) ( 5. Qxd4 ) 5... Nc6
{ Black's [Nc6] attacks the unprotected d4-pawn with both queen and knight, forcing White to defend before chasing the queen away with [Nc3]. [Nf3] is the main line, guarding d4 so [Nc3] becomes a real tempo threat on the next move: Black then chooses between the active pin [Nf3 Bg4] (looking to trade on f3 and win d4 unless White plays [Nf3 Bg4 Be2] or [Nf3 Bg4 Nc3]) and the solid [Nf3 e6]. Defending with [Be3] instead stops [e5] ideas and prepares [Nc3] immediately. [%cal Gg1f3,Gc1e3,Rc6d4] }
( 5... Nf6 ) ( 5... e5 ) 6. Nf3
{ White has an isolated pawn on d4, and Black's queen still stands on d5. Black must keep up the pressure on d4 before [Nc3] comes with tempo: [Bg4] pins the knight on f3, and [e5] strikes at once. With the quieter [e6], Black accepts an IQP middlegame: White plays [Nc3], [Bd3] or [Be2], castling and a kingside attack, with the pawn on d4 supporting a knight on e5; Black blockades d5, trades pieces and aims for an endgame against the weak pawn. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc8g4,Ge7e5,Ge7e6] }
( 6. Be3 ) 6... Bg4
{ Having liquidated on d4 early, Black pins the f3-knight to threaten [b:Bxf3] followed by [b:Qxd4]. [Be2] is the positional response, shielding the knight so White keeps an intact kingside and builds an active isolated-queen's-pawn game with [Be2 e6 Nc3]. [Nc3] is the sharp gambit test: after [Nc3 Bxf3 gxf3 Qxd4 Qxd4 Nxd4 Bb5+], White uses the lead in development to recover material quickly. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb1c3,Gf1e2] }
( 6... e5 ) ( 6... e6 ) ( 6... Nf6 $2
{ Natural development, but it gives White [Nc3] with tempo on the queen. After [Nc3 Qd8 d5], the knight on c6 is kicked and White is well ahead in development. Put pressure on d4 first with [b:Bg4]. [%csl Rd5,Rc6][%cal Gb1c3] }
7. Nc3 ) 7. Nc3
{ White's [Nc3] develops with tempo against Black's centralized queen while piling counter-pressure onto d5, creating the sharpest tactical moment of the [Bg4] Alapin. Black has no time to retreat the queen: every single queen move allows [d5] to crush the c6-knight on the spot. Instead, Black must capture first with [Bxf3], so that after [Bxf3 gxf3 Qxd4] Black eliminates the dangerous d-pawn and reaches a dynamic endgame via [Qxd4 Qxd4 Nxd4]. [%cal Gg4f3,Gd5d4] }
( 7. Be2 ) 7... Bxf3 ( 7... Qa5 $4
{ The queen steps aside, but [d5] hits the knight on c6. After [d5 Rd8 Bd2], Black's pieces are tied up and the pin on a5 is broken. Take on f3 first, then on d4. [%csl Rc6][%cal Gd4d5] }
8. d5 ) ( 7... Qd7 $4
{ The queen keeps an eye on d4, but [d5] wins the knight on c6: after [d5 Bxf3 gxf3], the knight has nowhere to go and the queen on d7 is in the way. Take on f3 first. [%csl Rc6][%cal Gd4d5] }
8. d5 ) ( 7... Qd8 $4
