10. Nb5 ( 10. Bd3 $2
{ The bishop develops, but [Nxf3+] takes a second pawn with check. Black is two pawns up in the endgame. [w:Nb5] was the active move. [%csl Re1][%cal Gd4f3] }
10... Nxf3+ ) ( 10. Bg2 $4
{ The bishop guards f3, but it leaves c2 alone. [Nc2+] forks the king and the rook on a1, and Black wins the exchange on top of the pawn. [w:Nb5] hit d4 first. [%csl Ra1,Re1][%cal Gd4c2] }
10... Nc2+ ) ) ( 2. b4
{ The Wing Gambit. White offers the b-pawn to deflect Black's c-pawn from the center, hoping to follow with [a3] and [d4] and build a big center with quick development. Black should accept: after [cxb4], the extra pawn is real, and Black can later return it for a good position, often with [d5] hitting e4. The gambit is rare at master level, as Black's best defenses are well known, but it can be dangerous in fast games. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb4c5,Gc5b4] }
2... cxb4
{ Black has taken the pawn. White's usual follow-up is [a3], offering a second pawn to open lines for the bishops and the rook. Black's most respected answer is [d5], hitting back in the center, and there is a trap to know: in [a3 d5 exd5 Qxd5 axb4 Qe5+], White grabs the pawn back too soon, and the queen check wins the rook on a1 down the open long diagonal. [d4] instead keeps the gambit in a central style. [%cal Ga2a3,Gd2d4,Gc1b2] }
3. a3
{ The Wing Gambit's main idea: White offers a second pawn to open the a-file and the long diagonal for a bishop on b2. Black has a choice. [d5] is the most respected answer, hitting e4 at once. [bxa3] grabs the pawn: after [bxa3 Nxa3] or [bxa3 Bxa3], White has fast development and open lines on the queenside, and Black must be careful. [e6] and [e5] develop normally and keep the extra pawn on b4. Whatever Black picks, quick central counterplay matters more than a second pawn. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5,Gb4a3,Ge7e6] }
( 3. d4 ) ( 3. Bb2
{ The Abrahams Variation of the Sicilian Wing Gambit ([b4], [cxb4] and [Bb2]). Instead of playing [a3] to open the a-file, White fianchettoes the dark-squared bishop immediately onto b2 to sweep the long diagonal toward e5 and g7 while planning [d4] in the center! Black neutralizes the diagonal cleanly with [d5] ([d5 exd5 Qxd5] centralizes the queen while blocking b2, or [e6] and [Nf6] followed by [d5]), whereas [Nc6] and [d6] hold the extra pawn more cautiously. [%cal Ge7e6,Gb8c6,Bb2g7] }
3... d5 ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... d6 ) ) 3... bxa3
{ The Carlsbad Variation accepts the second Wing Gambit pawn, giving White wide-open queenside files and rapid piece play. [d4] grabs the centre before recapturing on a3, while [Nxa3] brings the knight out with ideas of [Nb5] and [c3] to build a full pawn duo on d4 and e4. [Bxa3] is the most common club choice, activating the bishop along the a3-f8 diagonal, which Black counters with [b:d6], [b:Nf6] and [b:g6]. [%cal Gb1a3,Gd2d4,Gc1a3] }
( 3... d5 4. exd5 Qxd5
{ Black strikes back in the centre against the Wing Gambit and recaptures on d5 with the queen, centralizing powerfully while eyeing e5 and a2. White must resist grabbing the b4-pawn back immediately: [axb4] leaves e5 unguarded and drops the a1 rook to the fork [axb4 Qe5+] down the open long diagonal. Instead, White develops with [Nf3], covering e5 and preparing [Bb2] or [c4] to gain rapid tempos against Black's exposed queen. [%cal Gg1f3,Bd5e5,Re5a1] }
