d4 ( 3. Bb5+
{ The Moscow Variation, a solid way for White to avoid the main lines after [d6]. The check forces Black to block, and White is often happy to trade the light-squared bishops, develop quickly and keep a light grip on the centre, sometimes with a pawn on c4 for a Maroczy-style bind. Black has three blocks: [Bd7] offers the trade and is the most common; [Nd7] keeps both bishops; [Nc6] develops and reaches a Rossolimo position. [%cal Gc8d7,Gb8d7,Gb8c6] }
3... Bd7
{ Black offers to trade the light-squared bishops, a piece that often struggles to find a good role in the Sicilian. White nearly always accepts with [Bxd7+]. Black usually takes back with the queen, keeping the knight free for c6. White then chooses between [c4], which clamps d5, and short castling with [c3] and [d4] for a classical center. The structure is calm, and the game is more about placing the pieces well than about early tactics. [%cal Gb5d7,Ga2a4] }
( 3... Nd7
{ Black blocks with the knight, keeping the light-squared bishop and avoiding the trade White was hoping for. The knight is less active on d7, where it also shuts in the bishop on c8, so Black usually follows with [Nf6] and sometimes [a6] to ask the bishop what it wants. White's most direct reply is [d4], opening the center while Black is a little behind in development. Castling first, then [c3] and [d4], is the steady alternative. [%cal Gd2d4,Ge1h1,Gc2c3] }
4. d4 ( 4. O-O ) ( 4. c3 ) ) ( 3... Nc6
{ Black blocks with the knight, which develops naturally; the position is a Rossolimo in which Black has played [d6]. The knight on c6 is pinned for now, so Black often follows with [Bd7], and sometimes [a6] to question the bishop. If White takes on c6, Black recaptures with the b-pawn and plays [e5]: the extra central pawn and the bishop pair make up for the doubled pawns. White usually castles and plays [c3] and [d4], or opens the centre at once: after [d4 cxd4 Qxd4], the pinned knight can't chase the queen, which stays strong in the centre. [%cal Ge1h1,Gd2d4,Bb5c6] }
