{ In the Moscow Check ([Bb5+]) met by [Nc6], White castles quickly on move four while keeping the pin alive on the a4-e8 diagonal. White threatens both [Re1], [c3] and [d4] for a strong Ruy Lopez-style center and [Bxc6+] at the right moment to saddle Black with doubled c-pawns. Black usually breaks the pin immediately with [Bd7], preparing [Nf6] and [g6] or [e6] without structural damage, or plays [Nf6] directly to hit e4 and provoke [Nf6 Re1] or [Nf6 Bxc6+ bxc6 e5]. [%cal Gc8d7,Gg8f6,Bb5c6] }
