{ White has added the f-pawn and threatens [e5], which would hit the knight on f6 with the d-file open behind it. Black's best is [Nxd4], taking the knight before the push, then [Qa5] or [h6]; [h6] at once asks the bishop on g5 to decide. The queen trip to b6 looks active but lets White open the centre with a knight trade first. [%cal Gc6d4,Gh7h6,Re4e5] }
9... Nxd4 ( 9... h6 ) ( 9... Qb6 $2
{ The queen eyes d4 and b2, but the knight on d4 simply trades. After [Nxc6 bxc6 e5] the knight on f6 is hit, and once the pawns are traded on e5, the bishop takes on e7 and White's queen and rook own the d-file. Trade on d4 yourself with [b:Nxd4] before [w:e5]. [%cal Gd4c6,Ge4e5] }
10. Nxc6 ) ) 8. O-O-O ( 8. f4 ) ( 8. f3 ) ( 8. Nxc6 ) ) 6. Be3
{ The English Attack. The bishop on e3 guards d4, and White plans [f3], [Qd2], long castling and a pawn storm with [g4] and [g5]. The pawn on f3 also has a second job: it keeps a knight off g4, where it would hit this bishop. Black picks the centre first: [e5] kicks the knight and claims space at the cost of a hole on d5, [e6] keeps a compact Scheveningen centre, and [Ng4] chases the bishop before [f3] comes. In every line it is a race: White storms the kingside, Black answers with [b5] and [b4]. [%cal Gf2f3,Gg2g4] }
6... e5
{ The pawn on e5 gains time on the knight and takes d4 and f4 away from White. The price is lasting: d5 can no longer be guarded by a pawn, and d6 is backward. The whole game now turns around d5: White wants a knight there, Black covers it with [Be6] and dreams of [d5], which frees the position at once. The knight usually drops back to b3; [Nf3] and [Nde2] are the other ways. [%csl Gd5,Rd6][%cal Gd4b3] }
( 6... e6
{ Black chooses a Scheveningen centre: the pawns on d6 and e6 cover the key central squares and keep the position compact. White's main plan is the English Attack with [f3], [Qd2], long castling, then [g4] and [g5]. Black does not wait: [b5], [Bb7] and [Nbd7] come quickly, aiming at e4 and at the white king with [b4]. White can also push [g4] at once, the Perenyi Attack, which hits the knight before Black has castled. [%cal Gf2f3,Gg2g4,Bb7b5] }
7. f3
{ The English Attack against the [e6] Najdorf, reaching a pure Scheveningen structure. With [f3], White stops [Ng4] from harassing the e3-bishop and prepares [Qd2], [g4] and [O-O-O] for an all-out kingside pawn storm. Black must generate rapid queenside counterplay: [b5] grabs space at once to prepare [Bb7] and [Nbd7], while [Be7] and [Qc7] keep development flexible before committing the b-pawn. [%csl Ye6,Yf3,Ye3][%cal Gf8e7] }
( 7. g4
{ The Perenyi Attack. White throws the g-pawn at the knight before anything else: [g5] is the threat, and it would send the knight to d7 and let White's pawns roll. Black has to react at once. [e5] hits the knight on d4 first and asks it to move, and [h6] slows the pawn down. Quiet developing moves are too slow here. [%cal Ge6e5,Gh7h6,Rg4g5] }
7... e5 ( 7... h6 ) ( 7... Be7 $2
{ A natural developing move, but it ignores the pawn on g4. [g5] kicks the knight back to d7, and White follows with [w:h4] or [w:f4] with a big space advantage and a ready-made attack. Against an early [w:g4], hit the knight on d4 first with [b:e5], or slow the pawn with [b:h6]. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gg4g5] }
8. g5 ) ( 7... Nc6 $2
{ The knight develops, but it gives White the time to push. [g5] drives the knight from f6 to d7, and Black's centre has lost its best guard. Meet [w:g4] with [b:e5] or [b:h6] first, before you develop. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gg4g5] }
8. g5 ) ( 7... Qc7 $2
{ The queen move is useful in the Najdorf, but here it gives the g-pawn a free run. [g5] drives the knight back to d7, and White's space grows with [w:h4] and [w:f4]. Hit back first with [b:e5]. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gg4g5] }
8. g5 ) ( 7... b5 $2
{ Gaining space on the queenside is Black's usual plan, but here it comes too late. [g5] chases the knight to d7 at once, and White's pawns keep rolling. In the Perenyi, [b:e5] or [b:h6] is the answer: deal with the pawn on g4 first. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gg4g5] }
8. g5 ) 8. Nf5
{ The knight jumps to f5, where it looks dangerous, and the g-pawn is ready to push. Black's answer is [g6]: the knight is asked to leave, and if White pushes [g5] the knight on f6 is lost, but Black takes the knight on f5 in return. Taking on f5 with the bishop instead gives White a pawn on f5 and the d5 square. [%csl Rf5][%cal Gg7g6] }
8... g6
{ The knight has to decide. [g5] is the sharp main line: after [g5 gxf5 exf5], White has given the knight, but the pawn on g5 still hits the one on f6, and the position explodes. Retreating the knight is too slow, because Black then takes on g4 or blocks the pawns with [Be6]. Both sides must know the lines here: one quiet move can lose the game. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gg4g5,Rg6f5] }
( 8... Bxf5 $2
{ Trading the bishop for the knight looks simple, but it hands White the light squares. After [exf5] the pawn on f5 cramps Black, d5 has lost its last light-squared guard, and [w:g5] still comes to chase the knight. Black keeps more chances with [b:g6], asking the knight to decide first. [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge4f5] }
9. exf5 ) 9. g5
{ Both knights are attacked. Black's only good move is [gxf5], taking the attacker first: after [gxf5 exf5 d5], White wins the knight on f6 back, and Black's big centre faces White's open lines and the pawn on f5. Any other move loses material at once. [%cal Gg6f5,Rg5f6] }
( 9. Ng3 $2
{ The retreat is too timid. [Nxg4] takes the pawn that White pushed, and the knight on g4 eyes the bishop on e3. White has spent three tempi on the g-pawn and lost it. Once you start the Perenyi, the sharp [w:g5] is the only consistent move. [%cal Gf6g4,Rg4e3] }
9... Nxg4 ) 9... gxf5 ( 9... Nh5 $4
{ The knight saves itself, but it leaves White's knight alive on f5. [Nd5] brings the other knight in, and with two knights on the fifth rank and the pawn on g5, Black's kingside is full of holes. The right answer was [b:gxf5], taking the attacker first. [%csl Rf5][%cal Gc3d5] }
10. Nd5 ) ( 9... Nxe4 $4
{ Grabbing the pawn walks into a combination. [Nxe4 Bxf5 Nf6+] follows: the knight checks from f6, and Black's king is stuck in the centre with nothing developed. When both knights are attacked, take the one that does the damage, here with [b:gxf5]. [%cal Gc3e4,Re4f6] }
10. Nxe4 ) ) ( 7. Be2 ) ( 7. Qd2
{ In the English Attack against the Najdorf/Scheveningen ([Be3] and [e6]), White plays [Qd2] before [f3]—accelerating [O-O-O] and keeping the option of [f4] open, at the price of allowing [Ng4]! However, in this exact setup with a6 and e6 on the board, [Ng4 Bg5] forces [Ng4 Bg5 Qb6] or [Be7] with messy play, so Black usually sticks to thematic queenside expansion with [b5] (threatening [b:b4] to uproot c3 and hit e4!) or solid [Be7] and [Qc7]. [%cal Gb7b5,Gf8e7,Gd8c7] }
7... Be7 ( 7... b5 ) ( 7... Qc7 ) ) 7... Be7
{ Black develops the dark-squared bishop conservatively to e7 before pushing [b5], getting ready to castle short in the Scheveningen English Attack. White seizes the moment with [g4], launching the pawn storm right away to threaten [w:g5] and drive the f6-knight from its central post, or plays [Qd2] first to keep [O-O-O] and [h4] smoothly coordinated against Black's [b5] and [Bb7] counterplay. [%cal Gg2g4,Gd1d2,Bb7b5] }
( 7... b5 ) ( 7... Qc7 ) 8. g4 ( 8. Qd2 ) ) ( 6... Ng4
{ Black attacks the bishop before [f3] can stop it. The bishop must go somewhere it stays safe: [Bg5] is the main line, and after [Bg5 h6 Bh4 g5 Bg3 Bg7] Black has gained space on the kingside and put the bishop on the long diagonal, at the cost of a looser king. White then often plays [h3] or [Be2] to chase the knight from g4. [Bc1] sends the bishop home and offers a repetition after the knight goes back to f6. [%cal Ge3g5,Ge3c1,Rg4e3] }
7. Bg5 ( 7. Bc1 ) ( 7. Be2 $2
{ It looks like natural development, but it leaves the bishop on e3 where the knight wanted it. [Nxe3 fxe3] gives Black the bishop pair and leaves White with doubled e-pawns and a weak diagonal toward the king. When a knight lands on g4 next to your bishop, move the bishop first, with [w:Bg5] or [w:Bc1]. [%csl Re3][%cal Gg4e3] }
7... Nxe3 ) ( 7. Bf4 $2
{ The bishop runs into the next pawn. [e5] hits it again with gain of time, and after [e5 Nf5] the knight on g4 eyes f2, so White is already struggling to hold everything together. [w:Bg5] is the only active square: it keeps the bishop out of reach of Black's pawns. [%csl Rf4][%cal Ge7e5] }
7... e5 ) ) 7. Nb3
{ The main line of the English Attack against [e5]. Black brings the bishop to e6 to fight for d5, then plays [Be7], castles and adds [Nbd7]. White answers with [f3], [Qd2] and long castling, then pushes [g4] and [g5] to drive the knight from f6, the main guard of d5. Black's counterplay is [b5] and [b4], with the c-file. Move order matters: with [Be7] first, Black must not rush [b5] before the bishop covers d5. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc8e6,Gf8e7] }
( 7. Nf3 ) ( 7. Nde2 ) ( 7. Nf5 $6
{ The knight looks active on f5, but it has moved twice and stands where Black can hit it. [d5] breaks in the centre at once: it opens Black's pieces and attacks e4, and the knight on f5 can be traded off by the bishop from c8. Against [e5], keep the knight on a safe square such as [w:Nb3] and keep your eye on d5. [%csl Rf5][%cal Gd6d5] }
7... d5 ) 7... Be6
{ Black posts the light-squared bishop on its ideal square e6, contesting the d5 outpost and eyeing b3 to slow down White's queenside castling. White's main continuation is [f3], the sharp English Attack, keeping Black's knight out of g4 and preparing [Qd2], [g4] and [O-O-O]. [Be2] steers the game into Karpov's positional system with short castling and [Qd2] or [f4], while [Qd2] invites [Qd2 Ng4]. [%cal Gf2f3,Gf1e2,Be6d5] }
( 7... Be7 8. f3
{ White has set up [f3], and Black still has to finish developing. [Be6] comes first, to cover d5, then castling and [Nbd7]. The queenside push with [b5] is Black's main plan, but only when d5 is guarded: a white knight on d5 would trade off Black's best defender and leave the hole for good. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc8e6,Ge8h8] }
8... Be6
{ Reached via either 7...[Be7] or 7...[Be6], this is the central tabiya of the Najdorf English Attack. Here [Qd2] is essential before pushing [g4]: rushing with [g4] while the queen still stands on d1 walks into [d5], because [b:d4] will fork c3 and e3 and Black's bishop on e7 watches h4. With [Qd2], White prepares [O-O-O] and [g4], and Black answers [O-O] and [Nbd7]. [%cal Gd1d2,Bg2g4,Be8h8] }
( 8... O-O ) ( 8... b5 $2
{ The pawn push is Black's plan, but it comes one move too early. [Nd5] lands on the hole at once: it trades off the knight on f6 or the bishop on e7, and White owns d5 with the c-pawn ready to follow. Black also has the pawn on b5 to look after. Play [b:Be6] first, so that [w:Nd5] can be met by a capture. [%csl Rb5][%cal Gc3d5] }
9. Nd5 ) 9. Qd2 ) ( 7... b5 $2
{ Rushing [b5] before developing [b:Be6] leaves the c6 and d5 squares unguarded and overextends the queenside pawns. [Nd5 Nbd7 a4] seizes the central outpost and undermines b5 immediately before Black's pieces are coordinated. Control d5 first with [b:Be6] or [b:Be7]. [%csl Rb5][%cal Gc3d5,Ga2a4] }
8. Nd5 Nbd7 9. a4 ) 8. f3
{ The tabiya of the Najdorf English Attack. White has secured e4 and g4 with [f3], preparing [Qd2], [O-O-O] and a fast [g4]–[w:g5] pawn storm aimed at dislodging the f6-knight that guards d5. Black has three major ways to fight back: [Be7], preparing short castling and a timely [d5] or [b5]; [Nbd7], keeping [b5] and [Rc8] flexible before moving the f8-bishop; and [h5], halting [g4] on the spot. [%csl Ye4,Yg4,Yf3][%cal Gf8e7] }
( 8. Be2 ) ( 8. Qd2 ) 8... Be7 ( 8... Nbd7
{ By developing [Nbd7] before [Be7], Black keeps the king in the centre for the moment and gets ready to meet White's kingside storm with immediate queenside play via [Rc8], [b5] and [Nb6] or [Qc7]. Because no black bishop stands on e7 to support a quick [h6] or [Nh5] check trick, White can launch [g4] right away—aiming for [w:g5] to kick the f6-knight—or play [Qd2] first to prepare [w:O-O-O]. [%cal Gg2g4,Gd1d2,Bb7b5] }
9. g4 ( 9. Qd2 ) ) ( 8... h5 ) 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O Nbd7
{ The main position of the English Attack. White's plan is [g4] and [g5]: the pawn drives the knight from f6, and a white knight then lands on d5. Black races with [b5], [b4] and the rooks on the c-file, and often trades on d5 to keep the hole covered. The kings sit on opposite wings, so every move counts: the side that opens a file toward the enemy king first is usually the one who wins. [%cal Gg4g5,Gc3d5,Gb7b5] }
11. g4 *

[Event "French Defence (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e6
{ The French Defence. Black prepares [d5] with a pawn already standing behind it, so after [d4 d5] a trade on d5 can always be met by a pawn and Black keeps a foothold in the centre. The price is the bishop on c8: the pawn on e6 shuts it in, and freeing or trading this "French bishop" runs through the whole opening. In return f7 is covered at once, so the early bishop and queen raids of 1...e5 games lead nowhere. White's main answer is [d4]; [Nf3] and [Nc3] usually come back to the same positions, and [d3] sets up a King's Indian Attack. The name comes from Paris, who played it against London in an 1834 correspondence match. [%csl Yc8,Ye6,Yf7][%cal Gd2d4] }
2. d4
{ White builds the two-pawn centre. [d5] is the French: Black attacks e4 at once and White must decide whether to defend it, push it or trade it. [c5] is the Franco-Benoni, which often turns into a Sicilian after [w:Nf3]. Other moves let White keep the big centre: ...b6 and ...d6 are playable but passive. Kicking moves like ...Nf6 or ...f5 only help White gain space. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 2. Nf3
{ White develops before deciding on the centre. [d5] is the French answer: if White adds [w:d4] we are back in a main line, while [w:e5] and [w:Nc3] lead to independent but harmless systems. [c5] is also good and turns the game into a Sicilian. Avoid the half-measures: [Nf6] invites [w:e5], and with no pawn on d5 the knight is chased to d5 and kicked again by [w:c4]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ Black strikes at e4. White has four ways to go on. [e5] gains space and can lead to the Wing Gambit with [w:b4]; [Nc3] is the Two Knights; [exd5] gives an Exchange French where White has committed the knight early; and [d4] is a gambit, the Perseus, that gives the e4 pawn. Black's plans don't change: hit the centre with [b:c5], bring the knights to c6 and f6, and keep an eye on the c8 bishop. [%csl Ye4,Yc6,Yf6][%cal Ge4e5] }
( 2... c5 ) ( 2... f5 $2
{ The Franco-Hiva Gambit: Black wants a Dutch-style grip on e4, but the f-pawn leaves e6 weak and the king's diagonal open. [exf5] wins a pawn, and after [exf5 exf5] White can follow with [w:d4] and [w:Bd3], with holes on e5 and e6 to use. The slower [w:Nc3], [w:d3] or [w:e5] let Black off lightly. Play [b:d5] instead. [%csl Re4,Re6][%cal Ge4f5] }
3. exf5 ) ( 2... Nf6 $6
{ This looks like natural development, but [e5] drives the knight away and Black has no pawn on d5 to lean on. After [e5 Nd5 c4 Nb6 d4] White has a big centre for free. Play [b:d5] first, then bring the knight out. [%csl Rf6,Rd5][%cal Ge4e5] }
3. e5 ) 3. e5
{ With no pawn on d4, the pawn on e5 is supported only by the knight. [c5] is the right reaction: it grabs d4 before White can occupy it and opens the way for [b:Nc6]. White then chooses between [w:d4], a Nimzowitsch Advance, and the [w:b4] gambit. Blocking the c-pawn with [Nc6] is less precise: it lets White play [w:d4] and [w:c3] with a solid Advance chain and no ...c5 break available. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gc7c5] }
( 3. Nc3
{ The Two Knights Variation. White develops pieces instead of building a pawn centre, so Black has a comfortable choice. [Nf6] invites [w:e5], and after [b:Nfd7] with [w:d4] and [b:c5] we are in a Steinitz French; [d4] grabs space and kicks the knight, though White gets play against the advanced pawn with [w:Ne2] and [w:c3]. [dxe4] gives a Rubinstein-type game, but here White has not played d4, which gives the knight on e4 more freedom. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gg8f6] }
3... Nf6 ( 3... d4
{ Black takes space and drives the knight back. White's best is [Ne2], from where it joins the [w:Nf3] in hitting d4, and then [w:c3] undermines the pawn. Black supports it with [b:c5] and [b:Nc6], but should be ready to give up the outpost rather than overextend: after [w:c3] and a trade on d4 the game opens, and Black needs development, not a pawn on d4 at any price. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc3e2] }
4. Ne2
{ Both knights now look at d4. Black supports the pawn with [c5], and after [w:c3] develops with [b:Nc6] or [b:Nf6] rather than defending d4 at all costs. A trick to know: after [c5 c3 Nc6 cxd4 cxd4], the queen move [w:Qa4] pins the knight on c6 to the king and hits the d4 pawn three times: Black has to push it on to d3, where White picks it up later. Taking on d4 with the e2 knight at once would be a blunder: after ...Nxd4 White is a piece down. A pawn on d4 supported by ...c5 and ...e5 cramps White, so White often plays c3 at once to break it up. [%csl Yd4,Yc6][%cal Gc7c5] }
4... c5 ( 4... Nc6 ) ) ( 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4
{ A Rubinstein structure, but White can still choose where to put the d-pawn. Black develops as in the Rubinstein: [Nd7] then [b:Ngf6], or [Be7] first. The careless [Nf6] invites [w:Nxf6+], and after the queen takes back it becomes a target: [w:d4], [w:Bd3] and [w:Bg5] come with gain of time, and with ...Bd6 the queen on f6 can even be trapped. After ...Nd7 the game is a Rubinstein. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gb8d7] }
4... Nd7 ( 4... Be7 ) ) ) ( 3. exd5
{ White liquidates the central tension on move three, steering into the Exchange French with the king's knight already committed to f3. Black recaptures [exd5] to open the c8-h3 diagonal for the light-squared bishop and equalize effortlessly: [Bd6], [Nf6] (or [Ne7] to sidestep a pin on g5), and [b:Bf5] follow smoothly. Recapturing [Qxd5] is inferior, handing White a free developing tempo with [Nc3] against the queen. [%csl Yc8,Yd5][%cal Ge6d5] }
3... exd5 ) 3... c5
{ Now [d4] transposes to the Advance with Nf3, a Nimzowitsch system, and [c3] keeps it flexible; but [c3] allows [b:d4], gaining space, so it is less accurate. The point of this move order is [b4], the Wing Gambit: White gives a pawn to drag the c-pawn off the centre, then plays [w:a3] and [w:d4] with a big pawn wedge and quick development. It is a dangerous surprise but not sound: Black can take and keep the extra pawn, or decline with [b:d4]. [%cal Gd2d4] }
( 3... Ne7 ) ( 3... d4 ) 4. d4 ( 4. b4
{ The Wing Gambit. White wants to deflect the c-pawn so that [w:d4] can be played without any pressure on it. Accepting with [cxb4] is the principled answer, after which White usually offers a second pawn with [w:a3]; [d4] is a good way to decline, keeping the centre closed and grabbing space. ...Nc6 is playable but gives the pawn back at once to bxc5. The gambit is a fun surprise weapon, but engines give Black a clear plus after [cxb4]. [%cal Gc5b4] }
4... cxb4
{ Black has the pawn. White's usual plan is [a3], offering the a-pawn as well to open lines, followed by [w:d4], [w:Bd3] and kingside castling, and later an attack with [w:h4] and [w:h5] behind the pawn wedge. [d4] at once is the other way to build the centre. Black should keep developing with [b:Nc6] and [b:Nge7] and not chase every pawn: a second pawn is fine to take, a third one usually costs too much time. [%cal Ga2a3] }
( 4... d4 ) 5. a3 ( 5. d4 ) ) ( 4. c3
{ Preparing [w:d4] via [Nf3] and [c3] has one important drawback compared with the standard Advance order: because White's pawn has not reached d4 yet, Black can push [d4] right here to seize dark-square space and cramp White's queenside! Developing [Nc6] or [Bd7] is completely sound too, inviting [Nc6 d4] to transpose straight back into the main line of the [Nf3] Advance French. [%cal Gd5d4] }
4... d4 ( 4... Nc6 ) ( 4... Bd7 ) ) ) ( 2. Nc3
{ 2.Nc3 has little life of its own. After [d5] White usually goes back to the main lines with [w:d4], or plays [w:Nf3], the Two Knights, bringing the pieces out before the centre is fixed. A sharper try is [w:f4], aiming for e5 and a kingside attack. [c5] is the other good answer and leads to a Closed Sicilian, where White follows with g3, Bg2, d3 and f4. Whatever White chooses, Black's plan is the French one: ...d5, then ...c5 against the centre and the queen's knight to c6. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ White should go on with [d4], back to the main lines, or [Nf3], the Two Knights. [f4] is a sharp try: White wants e5 and a kingside attack, and Black's best answer is to hit the centre at once with ...d4 or ...dxe4, before White's pieces are out. [exd5] gives a quiet Exchange French. The push [w:e5] with no pawn on d4 behind it is too early: ...d4 kicks the knight away, Black gains space, and the e5 pawn soon needs defending. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gd2d4] }
( 2... c5 ) 3. d4 ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. f4
{ White wants e5 and a kingside attack, with the f-pawn already behind it. Black's best is to hit the centre at once. [d4] kicks the knight and gains space: after [w:Nce2] Black plays ...c5 and ...Nc6, and White's centre is gone before it is built. [dxe4] takes the pawn: after [w:Nxe4] Black develops with ...Nd7 and ...Ngf6, and the f4 pawn only weakens White's king. [c5] is also good. Slow moves like ...Nf6 allow [w:e5] and a closed position that suits White. [%csl Yf4][%cal Gd5d4,Gd5e4,Gc7c5] }
3... d4 ( 3... dxe4 ) ( 3... c5 ) ) ( 3. exd5
{ Trading on d5 with the queen's knight already on c3 reaches a quiet Exchange French where White has blocked the c-pawn, ruling out an early [c4] isolated-queen's-pawn push. Black recaptures [exd5] cleanly, freeing the c8 bishop at once and preparing [Nf6], [Bd6] (or [Bb4] pinning c3), [b:O-O] and [c6] with dead-even symmetry. Gambiting a central pawn with [Nf6] or [c6] is unsound here. [%csl Yc8,Yc3][%cal Ge6d5] }
3... exd5 ) ( 3. e5 $6
{ The pawn on e5 has no d-pawn behind it, and [d4] gains a tempo on the knight. After [d4 Ne4] the knight is in the way, and Black follows with ...Nc6, ...Qd5 or ...f6 against e5. Develop with [w:Nf3] or take the centre with [w:d4] instead. [%csl Rc3,Re5][%cal Gd5d4] }
3... d4 ) ) ( 2. d3
{ White chooses the King's Indian Attack: [w:Nd2], [w:Ngf3], [w:g3], [w:Bg2] and castling, a set-up Fischer liked against the French. White avoids French theory and later pushes [w:e5] to attack on the kingside. Black has no problems: [d5] takes the centre, and [c5] with [b:Nc6] first is just as good, keeping the option of a Sicilian-style set-up with [b:g6] and [b:Bg7]. In both cases Black's play comes on the queenside, with ...b5-b4, while White's comes on the kingside. [%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ [Nd2] is the King's Indian Attack proper: the knight protects e4 and leaves the c-pawn free for [w:c3]. [Qe2] is an older way to do the same, and [exd5] simply gives a symmetrical position. White's plan is slow, so Black can pick a set-up: [b:Nf6], [b:c5], [b:Nc6], [b:Be7] and castling is the classical one; [b:dxe4] followed by [b:e5] aims to blunt the bishop that will come to g2. [%csl Ye4,Yg2][%cal Gb1d2] }
( 2... c5 ) 3. Nd2
{ Black develops. [Nf6] and [c5] are the main moves and usually transpose; the classical set-up is [b:c5], [b:Nc6], [b:Be7] and short castling, then queenside play with [b:b5] and [b:b4]. Taking on e4 is also fine, but only with a plan: ...dxe4 followed by ...e5 leaves the coming bishop on g2 staring at a blocked diagonal. White's whole set-up is aimed at e5 and a kingside attack with h4, Nf1 and Bf4, so Black's counterplay must come fast on the queenside. [%csl Yg2][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 3. Qe2 ) ( 3. exd5 ) 3... Nf6
{ White carries on with the set-up: [Ngf3], then [w:g3], [w:Bg2] and castling. [g3] first keeps the option of putting the knight on e2 and pushing f4 later. The early [w:e5] is premature: after [b:Nfd7] the pawn on e5 has no f- or d-pawn support yet and Black hits it with [b:c5] and [b:Nc6], and later [b:f6]. Black's quickest plan is ...c5, ...Nc6, ...b5 and ...b4, which gains space where White has no pieces. [%csl Ye2,Ye5][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 3... c5 ) 4. Ngf3
{ The main position of the King's Indian Attack against the French. Black has three good set-ups: [c5] with [b:Nc6], [b:Be7] and castling, the most common; [Be7] first, keeping ...c5 in reserve; and [Nc6] with the idea of [b:dxe4] and [b:e5], which shuts the long diagonal. Taking on e4 at once gives up the centre for nothing, and fianchettoing with ...b6 is slow here: White's [w:e5] and kingside play come faster. [%cal Gc7c5] }
( 4. g3 ) 4... c5
{ White completes the fianchetto with [g3]. Putting the bishop on e2 is too modest: without the bishop on g2, White has no pressure on d5 and nothing behind a later [w:e5]. The plan is [w:Bg2], castling, [w:Re1] and [w:e5], then a kingside attack with [w:Nf1], [w:h4] and a knight jump to g5, or the h-pawn's march to h5. Black meets it with ...Nc6, ...Be7, castling and a queenside pawn storm with ...b5-b4. [%csl Ye2,Yg2,Yd5][%cal Gg2g3] }
( 4... Be7 ) ( 4... Nc6 ) 5. g3
{ Black goes on with [Nc6], then [b:Be7] and castling; [Be7] first comes to the same thing. An early trade on e4 is a small concession: after [b:dxe4] [w:dxe4] the g2 bishop's diagonal opens and White gets e4 for the pieces, so Black usually keeps the tension. Black's play is on the queenside: [b:b5], [b:a5] and [b:b4], a rook to the c-file and the bishop to a6 or b7. Once White plays e5, the f6 knight goes to d7, where it covers e5 and c5. [%csl Ye4,Ya6,Yb7][%cal Gb8c6] }
5... Nc6
{ White completes the fianchetto with [Bg2], then castles and plays Re1 and e5. Black follows with ...Be7 and short castling, or ...Bd6 to watch e5. A trade on e4 at the moment helps White: after ...dxe4 dxe4 the bishop on g2 sees the long diagonal. The race begins after castling: White's e5 and kingside attack against Black's ...b5-b4 and ...a5. [%csl Ye5,Yb5][%cal Gf1g2] }
( 5... Be7 ) 6. Bg2
{ The standard set-up is [Be7] and short castling. [Bd6] is a more active square: it watches e5, so White needs more preparation before pushing [w:e5], but the push will hit the bishop when it comes. Black's plan does not change: queenside space with [b:b5], [b:a5] and [b:b4], the bishop to b7 or a6 and a rook to the c-file. Keep the centre tense: trading on e4 early only helps the g2 bishop. [%csl Ye5,Yb7,Ya6][%cal Gf8e7] }
6... Be7
{ [O-O] completes White's development; then Re1 and e5 are the plan. Black castles too, and the race begins. White's attacking pieces are the knights, which go to f1, h2 or g4, and the h-pawn, which marches to h5 to weaken Black's king. Black plays ...b5, ...a5 and ...b4 to open lines on the queenside, where White's c-pawn is a target. [%csl Ye5,Yb5][%cal Ge1g1] }
( 6... Bd6 ) 7. O-O
{ [O-O] is the natural move: Black's king is safe for now, and the game will be decided by which side's pawns arrive first. [b5] at once is also played, keeping castling for later. Black should not trade on e4 now, which would help the bishop on g2. After ...O-O White plays Re1 and e5, and the f6 knight goes to d7. [%csl Yd7][%cal Ge8g8,Gb7b5] }
7... O-O
{ The typical King's Indian Attack position. White's plan is [Re1] and [w:e5], then [w:Nf1], [w:h4] and pieces towards Black's king: a knight on g5, a bishop on f4, the queen to h5. [e5] at once is also played. Black races on the other wing with [b:b5], [b:b4], [b:a5]-a4 and a rook on the c-file, aiming to open lines before White's attack arrives. The side whose play comes first usually wins. [%csl Yg5,Yf4,Yh5][%cal Gf1e1] }
( 7... b5 ) 8. Re1
{ White prepares e5. Black has several plans: [b5] at once, starting the queenside race; [Qc7], which watches e5 and makes White's push harder; and ...dxe4 dxe4 with ...e5, which closes the long diagonal and turns the game into a slower fight. After e5 the f6 knight goes to d7, and Black's ...b4 and ...a5-a4 must come fast. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gb7b5,Gd8c7] }
( 8. e5 ) 8... b5 ( 8... Qc7 ) ) ( 2. Bc4 $6
{ The bishop aims at f7, but the pawn on e6 already guards it, and [d5] hits the bishop at once. After [d5 exd5 exd5] the bishop must move again and Black has the free development of an Exchange French with an extra tempo. Take the centre first with [w:d4]. [%csl Rf7,Re6][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5 ) ( 2. Qe2
{ The Chigorin Variation. The queen on e2 makes ...d5 awkward: after [d5 exd5] the e6 pawn is pinned to the king, so Black must take back with the queen, and [w:Nc3] hits it at once. That is why Black's main answers are [c5], a Closed Sicilian type of game where White fianchettoes the king's bishop, and [Be7], which gets off the e-file in advance so that ...d5 works. [e5] is also fine: the queen on e2 blocks White's own light bishop, and Black develops with ...Nc6 and ...Nf6. [%csl Ye2,Ye6][%cal Gc7c5] }
2... c5
{ With the c-pawn on c5 White cannot play [w:d4], so the usual plan is [Nf3], [w:g3] and [w:Bg2], like a Closed Sicilian with the queen on e2. [g3] first keeps the option of [w:f4]. Black develops with [b:Nc6], [b:g6] and [b:Bg7], or [b:Nf6] and [b:Be7]; ...d5 is good once the e-pawn is no longer pinned. Black's most solid set-up is ...Nc6, ...g6, ...Bg7 and ...Nge7, which keeps the long diagonal closed and the d4 square under control. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 2... Be7 ) ( 2... e5 ) 3. Nf3 ( 3. g3 ) ) ( 2. f4
{ The La Bourdonnais Variation, named after the 19th-century French master. White prepares [w:e5] with f-pawn support, at the cost of loosening the king. [d5] is the best answer, and after [w:e5] Black plays [b:c5], [b:Nc6] and [b:Nge7]-f5, hitting d4 before White can cover it. [c5] is also fine. The f-pawn also takes the f4 square from White's own pieces and weakens the e1-h4 diagonal, which Black's queen and bishop can use. [%csl Yf5,Yd4][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ [e5] is White's idea: an Advance French with f4 already played, but White still needs [w:d4] and [w:c3] to hold d4, and the open g1-a7 diagonal gives ...Qb6 extra force. [Nc3] and [d3] are calmer. Taking on d5 gives an Exchange French where f4 is just a weakness. Don't play [w:Nf3] yet: ...dxe4 wins a pawn, the Reuter Gambit, because the knight has nowhere good to go. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ge4e5] }
( 2... c5 ) 3. e5
{ Black attacks the chain at once with [c5]. White needs [w:Nf3], [w:c3] and [w:d4] to build the centre; a typical line is [c5 Nf3 Nc6 c3 Nge7], with the knight going to f5 to hit d4. Because the f-pawn is on f4, White can never support the centre with it from f2, and the g1-a7 diagonal is open for ...Qb6. Black's ...c5 and ...Nc6 hit d4. [%csl Yf5,Yd4,Yf4][%cal Gc7c5] }
( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. d3 ) ( 3. exd5
{ By capturing on d5 in the Labourdonnais [f4] French, White enters an Exchange French structure with the f-pawn already advanced to f4. [exd5] is Black's clear best recapture: the open e-file and loosened e1-h4 diagonal make White's early [f4] a slight liability until [w:Nf3] and [w:d4] catch up in development, and Black develops comfortably with [b:Nf6], [b:Bd6] and [b:O-O] (or [b:c5] against [w:d4]). [%cal Ge6d5] }
3... exd5 ) ( 3. Nf3 $2
{ The knight develops, but e4 is now attacked and not defended. After [dxe4] the knight must move again, to g5 or e5, and Black is simply a pawn up with ...Nf6 and ...c5 to come; ...c5 or ...Nf6 at once would let White off. Defend the pawn with [w:Nc3] or [w:d3], or push [w:e5]. [%csl Rf3,Re4][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4
{ The Reuter Gambit: White has given the e4 pawn and must move the knight. [Ng5] and [Ne5] are the two tries: from g5 the knight attacks e4, but ...Nf6 holds it and ...c5 and ...Nc6 follow; from e5 it blocks the centre and eyes f7. Black stays a sound pawn up with ...Nf6, ...Be7 and ...c5 in both lines. The f4 pawn has taken the knight's best square and loosened White's king. [%csl Ye4,Yf4][%cal Gf3g5,Gf3e5] }
4. Ng5 ( 4. Ne5 ) ) 3... c5 ) ( 2. c4 ) ( 2. b3 ) ( 2. e5
{ 2.e5 grabs space at once, but with no pawn on d4 behind it the e5 pawn has little support. [d6] hits it straight away: after [w:exd6] [b:Bxd6] Black develops freely, and after [w:d4] [b:dxe5] [w:dxe5] [b:Qxd1+] the queens come off in a level position. [d5] is the other good answer, and after [w:d4] the game is an Advance French; [c5] first is fine too. Black's king's knight can't use f6 for now, so it goes to e7 or h6, and the queen's knight to c6 to hit the e5 pawn. [%csl Yd4,Yf6,Ye7][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ Black stakes a claim on the light squares at once and questions White's unsupported e5 spearhead. Bolstering the chain with [d4] transposes straight into the Advance French, where Black immediately attacks the base with [c5]. [Nf3] and [c3] keep d4 flexible for a moment—often aiming to recapture on d4 with a piece or pawn after [c5]—whereas an early [f4] loosens the dark squares before White's knights are out. [%csl Ye5,Yd5][%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3] }
( 2... d6 ) ( 2... c5 ) ( 2... f6 $6
{ Attacking the spearhead with [f6] on move two opens the fatal e1-h5 diagonal toward Black's king and robs the g8-knight of its natural square! [d4] reinforces e5 so [w:Qh5+] is already in the air, forcing [d4 c5 Bd3 g6 h4] with scary kingside weaknesses. Challenge the centre with [b:d5] or [b:c5] instead. [%csl Yf6][%cal Rd2d4] }
3. d4 c5 4. Bd3 g6 5. h4 ) 3. d4 ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. c3 ) ) ( 2. g3
{ White plans a fianchetto: [w:Bg2], then [w:Ne2] or [w:Nf3], castling and d3, a quiet King's Indian set-up. [d5] is the natural answer: after [w:exd5] [b:exd5] Black has a free game with an open c8 bishop, and after [w:d3] the game becomes a King's Indian Attack. [c5] and [b6] are also fine. White's g2 bishop aims at d5 and b7, so Black keeps the centre with ...d5 and ...c5 and develops the knights to c6 and f6. [%csl Yg2,Yd5][%cal Gd7d5,Gc7c5] }
2... d5 ( 2... c5 ) ) ( 2. Bb5
{ The Bird Invitation: the bishop comes out early and asks ...c6 or ...a6. [c6] kicks it, and after [w:Ba4] or [w:Be2] Black plays ...d5 with gain of time, a cross between the Caro-Kann and the French in which White's bishop has wasted moves. [a6] works the same way. White's idea is to provoke a pawn move on the queenside, but Black is comfortable either way; just don't ignore the bishop and let it settle. [%cal Gc7c6] }
2... c6 ( 2... a6 ) ) 2... d5
{ The French tabiya. The e4 pawn is attacked and White chooses the type of game. [e5], the Advance, gains space and closes the centre; [exd5], the Exchange, gives an open, symmetrical game; [Nc3] is the most testing move and allows the Winawer, the Classical and the Rubinstein; [Nd2], the Tarrasch, keeps c3 free and sidesteps the pin ...Bb4. Gambits that give the e-pawn for nothing, like an early Nf3, Be3 or c4, are less sound than they look: Black takes on e4 and keeps the pawn with good play. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4e5] }
( 2... c5 ) ( 2... c6
{ The Franco-Slav (or Caro-French) move order builds a small pawn wall before striking at the centre, hoping to transpose into a Caro-Kann or Fort Knox setup after [b:d5]. Because Black has spent two pawn moves without challenging e4 or d4, White develops freely with [Nf3], [Nc3] or [Bd3] and meets [b:d5] with [w:e5] or [Nc3], leaving the c8 bishop shut in behind e6. [%csl Yc8,Ye6][%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3] }
3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. Bd3 ) ) ( 2... f5 $2
{ The Franco-Hiva Gambit. After [exf5] Black must take back, and ...exf5 leaves a ragged king side with e5 and e6 weak for good: White develops with Nf3, Bd3 and O-O and is clearly better. Pushing [w:e5], or [w:Bd3] before taking, lets Black off lightly. Play [b:d5]. [%csl Re5,Re6][%cal Ge4f5] }
3. exf5
{ Black has to take back, and [exf5] is the way. The structure is a poor Dutch: the e-file is open, e5 and e6 are weak, and White develops with [w:Nf3], [w:Bd3] and castles, then puts a rook on the e-file. Black should castle quickly and play ...d5 to hold e4. The check on h5 is a lasting threat while ...g6 is the only block, and that would leave holes around Black's king. [%csl Ye5,Ye6][%cal Ge6f5] }
3... exf5 ) ( 2... b5 $2
{ The Baeuerle Gambit gives a pawn for a fianchetto on b7, but [Bxb5] just takes it, and Black has nothing for it: the queenside is loose and White keeps the big centre. White is a clean pawn up. Play [b:d5]. [%csl Rb7][%cal Gf1b5] }
3. Bxb5 ) ( 2... Nf6
{ The Mediterranean Defence: Black hits e4 before playing ...d5. The most testing answer is [e5], gaining space with tempo: after ...Nd5 [w:c4] [b:Nb6] White has a big centre and the knight on b6 is out of play, and Black's counter is ...d6 against the chain, as in an Alekhine Defence where the c8 bishop is already shut in. [Nc3] is also good: ...d5 then gives a normal Classical French, and ...Bb4 a Winawer-like game. [Bd3] defends e4 more slowly. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4e5] }
3. e5
{ The knight must move. [Nd5] is the natural square: if [w:c4] it goes on to b6, and Black follows with ...d6 to undermine e5. Retreating with ...Ng8 just loses time, and ...Ne4 leaves the knight short of squares once [w:Bd3] and [w:f3] hit it. After ...Nd5 the game looks like an Alekhine Defence with ...e6 played: Black is solid but cramped, and needs ...d6, ...Nc6 and ...c5 to get play against White's big centre. [%csl Yb6,Ye5][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. Bd3 ) 3... Nd5 ) ( 2... a6
{ A waiting move: Black keeps every set-up open and stops a later Bb5 or Nb5. It is playable but gives White an easy choice. [Nf3], [c4] and [Nc3] are all good: White takes the centre and develops. Black usually goes on with ...d5, reaching French positions where ...a6 is a useful extra move, or with ...b5 and ...Bb7, hitting e4 from the side. Against ...b5, White's simplest plan is to hit the pawn with a4 or c4 and keep developing. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gg1f3,Gc2c4,Gb1c3] }
3. Nf3
{ Black chooses the set-up. [d5] is the most solid: a French where ...a6 already stops Nb5 and Bb5 ideas, and ...c5 can follow without a check on b5. [b5] prepares ...Bb7 to hit e4 from the side, but White answers with [w:Bd3], [w:a4] or [w:c4] and the queenside pawns can become targets. [c5] is also played. Slow moves like ...h6 give White a free hand: [w:Bd3], [w:O-O] and [w:c4] with a big centre. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5,Gb7b5,Gc7c5] }
( 3. c4
{ Against Black's creeping [a6], [c4] builds a towering three-pawn centre on c4, d4 and e4. Black's best reply is [d5] at once, challenging the centre before White locks everything down: after [d5 exd5 exd5 Nc3 Nf6], the game becomes a Monte Carlo Exchange French where Black's extra [a6] keeps pieces off b5. Passive flank moves like [b:b6] or [b:h6] leave White dominating the whole board. [%cal Gd7d5] }
3... d5 ) ( 3. Nc3 ) 3... d5 ( 3... b5 ) ( 3... c5 ) ) 3. e5
{ The Advance. White's pawn on e5 takes away f6 from Black's knight and gives White space on the kingside. The chain d4-e5 has a base, d4, and Black strikes it at once with [c5], often backed by ...Nc6 and ...Qb6. The second break, ...f6, hits the head of the chain later. Waiting moves like [b6] or [Bd7] are playable plans to trade the light bishop, but a slow ...Nc6 or an early ...f6 lets White develop calmly with [w:Nf3] and [w:Bd3]. [%csl Ye5,Yf6,Yd4][%cal Gc7c5] }
( 3. exd5 ) ( 3. Nc3 Nf6
{ The Classical. White can close the centre with [e5], the Steinitz, or pin the knight with [Bg5], the old main line, which threatens e5 with a gain of time. [exd5] gives an Exchange where Black's knight is already on f6. Defending e4 with [Bd3] lets ...c5 or ...dxe4 gain time, and [Be3] gives up the centre: ...dxe4 wins a pawn for a while. Against all of them Black's plan is the same: ...c5 against d4, with ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 once the centre closes. [%csl Yf6,Ye4,Yd4][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5
{ The Steinitz. The knight must move, and [Nfd7] is the right square: from d7 it supports both French breaks, ...c5 and ...f6, and keeps an eye on e5. ...Ne4 looks active but after Nxe4 dxe4 the pawn on e4 is weak and Black's centre is gone. ...Ng8 undevelops. Black will follow with ...c5 and ...Nc6 against d4. White's space on the kingside gives an attack later with f4-f5 or Qg4; Black's counterplay comes from ...c5 and ...f6. [%csl Yd7,Ye5,Yd4][%cal Gf6d7] }
( 4. Bg5
{ The old main line. The pin threatens to take on f6 and win d5. [Be7] breaks the pin; [dxe4] is the Burn, a Rubinstein with Bg5 thrown in; [Bb4] is the MacCutcheon, a counter-pin. ...h6 at once is less good: after Bxf6 Black must take back with the queen or the g-pawn. The pin also prepares e5: then the knight must move and White trades the dark bishops. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gf8e7] }
4... Be7
{ [e5] is the main line: the knight is kicked and White trades the dark bishops. [Bxf6] first is another try, and [exd5] gives a quiet game. Slow moves give Black a tactic on e4: after [w:Nf3] the knight takes it at once, and [w:Bxe7] ...Qxe7 [w:Nxe4] ...dxe4 leaves Black a pawn up with the knight on f3 attacked. After [w:e5] [b:Nfd7] White trades the bishops or plays the Alekhine-Chatard h4, and Black answers with ...c5 or ...a6. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4e5] }
( 4... dxe4
{ The Burn: Black gives up the centre at a moment when White's bishop has already gone to g5. White takes back with [Nxe4], the only good move, and then Black chooses between ...Be7 and ...Nbd7, often with ...h6 to ask the bishop. The point is a Rubinstein-like position where the bishop on g5 can be traded or hit. Black's goal is the ...c5 break, which frees the dark bishop and balances White's central d-pawn. [%cal Gc3e4] }
5. Nxe4
{ The Burn: Black gives up the centre to free the pieces. [Be7] is the main move, and after Bxf6 Black chooses ...Bxf6 or ...gxf6. [Nbd7] first, with ...h6 and ...c5, is the other plan. The key break in every line is ...c5, which activates Black's pieces and the central majority. Black has given up the centre but the knight on e4 will be challenged by ...Nbd7-f6 or traded. [%cal Gf8e7] }
5... Be7
{ [Bxf6] and [Nxf6+] are the main moves. The natural [w:Nf3] is a serious error: the pin on f6 is not a real one, so Black takes on e4 with the knight, and after [w:Bxe7] ...Qxe7 Black is simply a piece up. White must trade first. After [w:Bxf6] Black chooses between the solid ...Bxf6, which keeps the pawns healthy, and the dynamic ...gxf6, which keeps the dark bishop and opens the g-file. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gg5f6] }
( 5... Nbd7 6. Nf3 h6 7. Nxf6+ Nxf6 8. Bh4
{ [c5] is the key move: it frees the dark bishop and fights for the centre, and it is the break that makes the Burn work. Without it Black is passive. After [w:Bb5+] [b:Bd7] the trade of light bishops helps Black too. Black continues with ...Be7 and short castling, or ...cxd4 and ...Qa5+ to put the queen on the open diagonal. White's best set-up is Bb5+, Qe2 and long castling. [%cal Gc7c5] }
8... c5 ( 8... Be7 ) ) 6. Bxf6
{ [Bxf6] is solid and keeps a healthy structure, and White plays Nf3 with Qd2 or Bc4. [gxf6] is the fighting choice: Black keeps the dark bishop and gets the g-file, and plays ...f5, ...a6, ...b5 and ...Bb7 with the light bishop on the long diagonal. In both cases ...c5 or ...e5 frees Black's game later, and the bishop pair is Black's long-term trump. [%cal Ge7f6] }
( 6. Nxf6+ ) ( 6. Nf3 $4
{ Developing the knight is natural, but e4 and g5 are both loose. [Nxe4] and after [w:Bxe7] ...Qxe7 Black is a piece up, because the knight on e4 is no longer attacked by the bishop. Trade first with [w:Bxf6]. [%csl Re4,Rg5][%cal Gf6e4] }
6... Nxe4 ) ( 6. Qf3 $4
{ Bringing the queen out to eye f6 and prepare long castling misses that the pin is an illusion. [Nxe4 Bxe7 Qxe7] unpins the knight with tempo, and recapturing with [w:Qxe4] walks straight into [b:Qb4+], forking the king and b2 with c1 and c3 both vacated—while leaving e4 drops a clean piece. Trade first with [w:Bxf6] or [w:Nxf6+]. [%csl Rb2,Rg5][%cal Gf6e4,Gd8b4] }
6... Nxe4 7. Bxe7 Qxe7 8. Qxe4 Qb4+ ) 6... Bxf6
{ Black has the bishop pair and a solid structure. [Nf3] is the main move, followed by [w:Qd2] and long castling, or [w:Bc4] and [w:Qe2]. Taking on f6 with the knight gives Black an easy game after ...Qxf6, with the queen active. Black's plan is ...O-O, ...Nd7 and ...c5 or ...e5 to free the game, and ...b6 and ...Bb7 for the light bishop. [%csl Yb7,Yf6][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 6... gxf6
{ Morozevich's fighting recapture in the Burn Variation. Black accepts doubled f-pawns to hold on to the bishop pair, open the g-file against White's kingside, and control e5 and g5 with pawns. Black's plan is [b:f5] to kick the e4 knight, followed by [b:b6], [b:Bb7] and [b:Nd7], often castling queenside alongside White. [Nf3] is White's flexible main move; [c3] builds a solid barrier against the future b7 bishop, [g3] prepares [w:Bg2] to contest the long diagonal head-on, and [Qd2] aims for fast long castling. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gg1f3,Gd1d2,Gc2c3] }
7. Nf3
{ [f5] pushes the knight back and gives the light bishop the long diagonal after ...b6 or ...b5 and ...Bb7. [b6] and [a6] are also played. Black's plan is ...a6, ...b5 and ...Bb7, with ...c5 as the central break, and the king often stays in the centre or goes long. White plays [w:g3], [w:Bg2] and castles, or puts the queen on d2 and castles long. The bishop pair and the g-file give Black dynamic play for the broken pawns. [%cal Gf6f5] }
( 7. c3 ) ( 7. g3 ) ( 7. Qd2 ) 7... f5 ( 7... b6 ) ( 7... a6 ) ) 7. Nf3
{ Black has a solid position with the bishop pair. [O-O] and [Nd7] are the main moves; then ...c5 or ...e5 frees the game. White often castles long after Qd2 and aims at the d-file and Black's king. Black should not leave the light bishop on c8 for ever: ...b6 and ...Bb7 is the standard plan, and ...e5 opens the long diagonal for the f6 bishop. [%cal Ge8g8] }
7... O-O
{ The main line of the Burn Variation. Black has castled safely with an intact kingside and the bishop pair, waiting to free the game with [b:Nd7] and [b:c5], or [b:b6] and [b:Bb7] followed by [b:e5]. White holds a slight lead in space and development: [Qd2] is the accurate main move, defending d4 while preparing [w:O-O-O] and a kingside attack, and [c3] blunts the f6 bishop's diagonal first. [Bd3] eyes h7 at once, gambiting d4 to [b:Bxd4] for [w:c3] and quick piece play, whereas trading with [Nxf6+] relieves the tension early after [b:Qxf6]. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gd1d2,Gf1d3,Gc2c3] }
( 7... Nd7 ) ( 7... b6 ) 8. Qd2 ( 8. Bd3 ) ( 8. c3 ) ( 8. Nxf6+ ) ) ( 4... Bb4
{ The MacCutcheon: Black pins the knight in return, so e4 is attacked twice and defended once. [e5] is the main move; then ...h6 is forced, to hit the bishop on g5 before White can use the pin. [exd5] gives a calmer game, and [Ne2] keeps the pawns. Black's plan in the main line is ...h6, ...Bxc3 and ...Ne4, with play against the doubled c-pawns. John MacCutcheon beat Steinitz with it in a simultaneous display in 1885. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4e5] }
5. e5
{ [h6] is the only good move: it hits the bishop before White can use the pin. Taking on c3 at once, ...Bxc3+?, gives up the dark bishop without gaining anything: White takes with the pawn and the g7 square is weak. The point is that White's bishop on g5 and knight on c3 are both pinned or hit, so White cannot keep everything. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gh7h6] }
( 5. exd5 ) ( 5. Ne2 ) 5... h6
{ White chooses where the bishop goes. [Bd2] is the main move; then ...Bxc3 [w:bxc3] ...Ne4 and the knight hits d2 and c3. [exf6] trades the bishop for the knight and grabs g7: after ...hxg5 [w:fxg7] ...Rg8 the pawn on g7 is doomed, and Black gets the half-open h-file and the bishop pair. [Be3], [Bc1] and [Bh4] are the other retreats; against Bh4 Black has ...g5 and ...Ne4. In all lines Black's knight comes to e4, where the pin on c3 makes it strong. [%csl Yd2,Yc3,Yg7][%cal Gg5d2] }
( 5... Bxc3+ $6
{ The capture seems to win the knight with check, but it gives away the dark bishop. [bxc3] and after ...h6 [w:Bc1] or Bh4 White has the bishop pair and Qg4 against the weak g7. Play [b:h6] first. [%csl Rc3,Rg7][%cal Gb2c3] }
6. bxc3 ) 6. Bd2
{ The bishop steps back to d2, where it breaks the pin: the knight on c3 is no longer tied to the king. Black must take on c3 first, [Bxc3], and only then jump to e4, where the knight hits d2 and c3. Going to e4 at once loses a piece. The retreat ...Nfd7, the Tartakower, is passive but playable. After [Bxc3] White chooses between bxc3, with the bishop pair and doubled pawns, and Bxc3, and Black's play is ...Ne4, ...c5 and ...Nc6. [%csl Yd2,Yc3][%cal Gb4c3] }
( 6. exf6
{ Chigorin's sharp piece trade in the McCutcheon. Rather than retreating the g5 bishop and letting Black plant a knight on e4, White takes on f6 to wreck Black's kingside shelter. [hxg5] is forced—[b:gxf6] drops the h6 pawn after [w:Bh4] and leaves Black's king draughty on both wings, while [b:Bxc3+] first only helps White strengthen the centre before the capture on g5. After [hxg5 fxg7 Rg8], White's g7 thorn will fall, leaving an unbalanced fight between Black's bishop pair and active rook on the g-file and White's safer king and h-pawn push. [%csl Yg5,Yf6][%cal Gh6g5] }
6... hxg5 7. fxg7
{ [Rg8] is the move: the rook attacks the g7 pawn and will take it soon. Black has the bishop pair and the half-open h-file, and White a slight lead in development. Black should not hurry: the g7 pawn can't be saved, so ...Rg8, ...Rxg7 or ...Nd7 and ...c5 come with an easy game. The bishop on b4 still pins the knight on c3, which makes it hard for White to use the extra time. [%cal Gh8g8] }
7... Rg8
{ White has won a pawn on g7, but it will fall. Black has the half-open h-file, the bishop pair and a solid centre. [h4] reopens lines for White's rook; [Qh5] and [Nf3] are also played. Black's plan is ...Rxg7 or ...Nd7 and ...c5, with the king often heading for the queenside. White must play fast, because once the g7 pawn is gone the bishops and the open files favour Black. [%cal Gh2h4] }
8. h4 ( 8. Nf3 ) ( 8. Qh5 ) ( 8. a3 ) ) ( 6. Be3
{ [Ne4] is the move: the knight takes the active square and hits c3, which the bishop on b4 already pins. Giving up the bishop with ...Bxc3+ is weaker: it leaves Black without a dark-square defender. As in the Bd2 line, Black follows with ...c5 and ...Nc6. After ...Ne4 White plays [w:Qg4] and Black answers ...Kf8 or ...g6. White's bishop on e3 holds d4 well, so Black's ...c5 and ...Qa5 against c3 are the main source of play. [%csl Yc3,Yd4][%cal Gf6e4] }
6... Ne4 ( 6... Bxc3+ $2
{ It gives up the bishop for no reason: [bxc3] and after ...Ng8 or ...Ne4 White plays [w:Qg4] with the dark squares weak. Play [b:Ne4]. Black hopes to double White's pawns, but in the MacCutcheon it is the knight on e4, backed by the pin on c3, that does the work. [%csl Rg7,Re4][%cal Gb2c3] }
7. bxc3 ) ) ( 6. Bc1
{ The bishop goes home, where it still eyes h6 and g5. [Ne4] is the right move: the knight hits c3, which the bishop on b4 pins, and f3 can't chase it yet. Taking on c3 at once, ...Bxc3+, hands White the bishop pair and the dark squares, and [w:Qg4] comes next. After ...Ne4 the plan is ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qa5 with pressure on c3. White answers [w:Qg4], and Black meets it with ...Kf8 or ...g6. [%csl Yc3,Yh6][%cal Gf6e4] }
6... Ne4 ( 6... Bxc3+ $6
{ The trade gives White two bishops and weak dark squares to aim at: [bxc3] and [w:Qg4] hits g7. Play [b:Ne4]. Black wanted to double White's pawns, but in this line the bishop is worth more than that: it is the only defender of the dark squares. [%csl Rg7,Re4][%cal Gb2c3] }
7. bxc3 ) ) ( 6. Bh4 ) 6... Bxc3
{ [bxc3] is the main recapture: White gets the bishop pair and the b-file, and the c-pawns are doubled. Taking with the bishop, [w:Bxc3], keeps the structure, but after ...Ne4 the bishop is hit again; if it goes to b4, ...c5 hits it at once. In both cases Black's plan is ...Ne4, ...c5 and ...Nc6, and White's is Qg4 and the dark squares. [%cal Gb2c3] }
( 6... Ne4 $4
{ The knight wants e4, but not yet: with the bishop on d2 the c3 knight is free, and [Nxe4] takes it. After ...dxe4 [w:Bxb4] or ...Bxd2+ [w:Nxd2] Black is a piece down. Take on c3 first with [b:Bxc3], then go to e4. [%cal Gc3e4] }
7. Nxe4 ) 7. bxc3
{ [Ne4] is the move: the knight hits d2 and c3 and blocks the e-file. White then plays [w:Qg4], hitting g7, and Black defends with ...Kf8 or ...g6. A retreat like ...Nfd7 is passive: White gets Qg4 and the dark squares for free. Black's counterplay comes from the knight on e4 and the ...c5 break against the doubled pawns. [%csl Yd2,Yc3,Yg7][%cal Gf6e4] }
( 7. Bxc3 ) 7... Ne4
{ [Qg4] attacks g7, and Black answers ...Kf8 or ...g6. [Bd3] is calmer and challenges the knight at once. The game is a fight between White's dark-square bishop and kingside pressure and Black's better structure, with the doubled c-pawns to aim at. Kings often stay in the centre. Black's plan is ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qa5 or ...Qc7 against c3, and the king walks to the queenside if the kingside opens. [%csl Yg7,Yc3][%cal Gd1g4] }
8. Qg4
{ [Kf8] or [g6] defends g7. Taking the bishop on d2 is the mistake: ...Nxd2 lets [w:Qxg7], the rook on h8 is hit, and after [w:Kxd2] White has the knight back and the h6 pawn falls too. After ...Kf8 or ...g6 Black plays ...c5 and ...Nc6, and the king often walks to the queenside later, where it is safe behind the pawns. White's best set-up is [w:Bd3], [w:Nf3] or [w:h4], and Rh3 to bring the rook over. [%csl Yg7,Yh6][%cal Ge8f8] }
( 8. Bd3 ) ( 8. Bc1 ) 8... Kf8 ( 8... g6 ) ( 8... Nxd2 $2
{ The knight takes the bishop, but g7 is undefended. [Qxg7] hits the rook on h8, and after ...Rf8 [w:Kxd2] White wins the h6 pawn too. Defend first with [b:Kf8] or [b:g6]. [%csl Rh8,Rh6][%cal Gg4g7] }
9. Qxg7 ) ) 5. e5 ( 5. Bxf6
{ Black chooses the recapture. [Bxf6] is natural and keeps the structure: White then plays [w:e5] and the bishop goes back to e7, or [w:Nf3]. Taking with the g-pawn is rarer and worse here: with the d5 pawn still standing, the broken pawns give Black less play than in the Burn, and the engine rates it about a pawn down. After ...Bxf6 Black has the bishop pair, and ...c5 and ...Nc6 are the plan once the centre closes. [%csl Ye5,Ye7][%cal Ge7f6] }
5... Bxf6
{ [e5] is the main move: it gains time on the bishop and closes the centre, and after ...Be7 [w:Qg4] hits g7. [Nf3] keeps the tension, and ...dxe4 [w:Nxe4] then gives a Burn-type position. Black has the bishop pair and White more space. Black's plans are ...O-O and ...c5, or ...c5 at once, with ...Nc6 against d4; the f7-f6 break comes later. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ge4e5,Gg1f3] }
6. e5
{ [Be7] is the main square: the bishop keeps an eye on g5, d6 and c5. Black then castles and plays ...c5 or ...f6. After [w:Qg4] Black must castle or play ...g6 first, since the natural ...c5 lets the queen take on g7 and hit the rook. White's knight comes to f3 and the bishop to d3, and the game turns on Black's breaks against the d4-e5 chain. [%csl Ye7,Yg7][%cal Gf6e7] }
( 6. Nf3 ) 6... Be7 7. Qg4
{ The queen attacks g7 at once. [O-O] is the simplest answer: the king is safe enough and the dark bishop still covers the kingside from e7. [g6] is playable too, while ...Kf8 gives up castling for little. The natural French break ...c5 is a mistake here, because Qxg7 wins a pawn and traps the rook in the corner. Black should castle first and play ...c5 or ...f6 next, when White's queen has nothing to take. [%csl Yg7,Ye7][%cal Ge8g8,Gg7g6] }
7... O-O ( 7... g6 ) ( 7... c5 $2
{ Hitting the centre is the usual French reflex, but g7 is undefended: [Qxg7] attacks the rook on h8, and after ...Rf8 [w:dxc5] White is two pawns up in practice, with Black's king stuck in the centre. Castle first with [b:O-O]. [%csl Rh8,Rc5][%cal Gg4g7] }
8. Qxg7 ) ) ( 5. exd5 ) ( 5. Nf3 $2
{ Normal development, but e4 now has one defender too few. [Nxe4] takes it at once: after [w:Bxe7] [b:Qxe7] [w:Nxe4] [b:dxe4] the knight on f3 is hit and Black is a clean pawn up. White's pin on f6 has achieved nothing. Close the centre with [w:e5] first. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf6e4] }
5... Nxe4 ) ) ( 4. exd5
{ [exd5] gives a symmetrical Exchange, the same position as after 3.exd5 exd5 4.Nc3 Nf6. [Nxd5] is also possible: Black avoids symmetry, but after [w:Nxd5] ...Qxd5 White gains time on the queen. In both cases Black's c8 bishop is no longer the problem it is in the closed lines, and the game is level. Black develops with ...Bd6 or ...Be7, castles and puts a rook on the e-file. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge6d5,Gf6d5] }
4... exd5 ( 4... Nxd5 ) ) ( 4. Bd3
{ The bishop defends e4, but it lets Black hit the centre with gain of time. [c5] strikes at once: if [w:e5] the knight goes to d7 and Black has a good Tarrasch-like position, and if [w:exd5] Black takes back and develops quickly. [dxe4] with ...Nxe4 also gains time. Pinning with ...Bb4 or developing with ...Be7 is too slow: [w:e5] drives the knight back and White gets the attack with [w:Qg4]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc7c5,Gd5e4] }
4... c5 ( 4... dxe4 ) ( 4... Nc6 ) ( 4... Bb4 $2
{ The pin is the Winawer idea, but here the knight is already on f6 and gets kicked: [e5] and after ...Nfd7 or ...Ne4 White plays [w:Qg4], hitting g7 with the dark bishop gone from the kingside. Play [b:c5] instead. The bishop would be fine on b4 if White's centre were not ready to advance with tempo. [%csl Rf6,Rg7][%cal Ge4e5] }
5. e5 ) ( 4... Be7 $2
{ A solid-looking move, but it lets White close the centre with tempo: [e5] and after ...Nfd7 [w:Qg4] or [w:h4] gives White a Steinitz with an extra move for the attack. Hit the centre with [b:c5]. [%csl Rf6,Rg7][%cal Ge4e5] }
5. e5 ) ) ( 4. Be3
{ White protects d4 and offers e4. [dxe4] is the best answer: the pawn on e4 is weak, and Black can keep it with ...Bb4 or give it back for development. [Nxe4] is also fine. Slow moves are mistakes here: ...Be7 and ...c5 let [w:e5] kick the knight with the bishop already on e3 guarding d4, and ...Bb4 runs into the same push. After ...dxe4 Black's plan is ...b6, ...Bb7 and ...Nbd7, keeping the pawn. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd5e4,Gf6e4] }
4... dxe4
{ White has given the e4 pawn. [f3] is the gambit way, to open the f-file; [Nge2] and [w:Ng3] win the pawn back more slowly, and [a3] stops ...Bb4. Black keeps the extra pawn with ...b6, ...Bb7 and ...Nbd7, or returns it with ...exf3 and develops. White's best chance is quick development and long castling; Black should not hurry to give the pawn back while ...b6 and ...Bb7 hold it. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf2f3,Gg1e2] }
( 4... Nxe4 ) ( 4... Bb4 $6
{ The pin looks natural, but [e5] drives the knight to e4, and after [w:Qg4] the g7 pawn is weak without the dark bishop. Take the pawn with [b:dxe4]. Taking the pawn on e4 first is both safer and better. [%csl Rf6,Rg7][%cal Ge4e5] }
5. e5 ) ( 4... Be7 $2
{ Too slow: [e5] kicks the knight, and with the bishop on e3 already guarding d4, [w:Qg4] comes next with an easy attack on g7. Take the pawn with [b:dxe4]. [%csl Rf6,Rg7][%cal Ge4e5] }
5. e5 ) ( 4... c5 $2
{ It strikes at d4, but after [exd5] the centre opens while Black is behind: ...cxd4 [w:Qxd4] ...exd5 [w:Bb5+] and White's queen and bishops are the active pieces. Take on e4 instead with [b:dxe4]. [%csl Rd5,Re8][%cal Ge4d5] }
5. exd5 ) 5. f3 ( 5. Nge2 ) ( 5. a3 ) ) 4... Nfd7
{ White has several plans. [f4] builds the big centre d4-e5-f4 and is the main line. [Nce2] with c3 and f4 keeps d4 supported by pawns, as Shirov played. [Nf3] develops quickly, and [Be3] is also played. The queen raid Qg4 is weaker: Black answers ...c5, ignoring g7 for a moment, and gets a good game. In every line Black strikes with ...c5 and then decides whether to castle or to play ...f6. [%csl Yd4,Yg7][%cal Gf2f4] }
5. f4
{ White has the big centre. [c5] attacks it straight away; the knight on d7 already supports it. [a6] first is a waiting move that stops Nb5 tricks. [Be7] is also played. Black's plan is ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 against d4, and often ...cxd4 to open the c-file. The weak point in White's camp is d4; Black's is e6. White's pieces come out later: Nf3, Be3, Qd2 and O-O-O. [%csl Yd4,Ye6][%cal Gc7c5] }
( 5. Nf3
{ White develops and keeps f4 for later. [c5] is the normal answer; the knight on f3 defends d4, and Black adds ...Nc6 and ...Qb6. After [w:dxc5] the play opens up, and after [w:Bg5] Black must know the trap: the bishop hits the queen on d8, so ...Nc6 drops it, and ...Qb6 is the right answer. [Be7] is calmer. ...b6, aiming for ...Ba6 to trade the bad bishop, is too slow here: the engine rates it a pawn worse than ...c5. [%csl Yd4,Yd8][%cal Gc7c5] }
5... c5 ( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... a6 ) 6. Bg5
{ The bishop attacks the queen on d8. [Qb6] is the right answer: it hits b2 and d4. The careless ...Nc6?? drops the queen to Bxd8. ...Be7 looks like it solves the problem, but after Bxe7 Qxe7 Nb5 White threatens Nc7+ and Nd6+, and the dark squares are weak. After [b:Qb6] White plays dxc5 and Black takes back on c5 with the knight or the bishop; the pawn on b2 hangs in some lines but taking it is often dangerous. [%csl Yd8,Yb2,Yd4][%cal Gd8b6] }
6... Qb6
{ The queen gets out of the bishop's attack and hits b2 and the d4 pawn. [dxc5] is the main move: it opens the dark squares, and after ...Bxc5 or ...Nxc5 White develops with Qd2 or Bd3. [Qd2] at once is as good, and [Bd3] is playable. The b2 pawn is a trap all through this line: whenever the queen takes it, Nb5 and Rb1 come with threats against c7 and the queen. Black's plan is ...Nc6, ...Bxc5 and short castling. [%csl Yb2,Yc7][%cal Gd4c5] }
( 6... Nc6 $4
{ Developing with a threat on d4 is normal, but the queen is attacked: [Bxd8] wins it. Thousands of blitz games on Lichess end this way. Play [b:Qb6]. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gg5d8] }
7. Bxd8 ) ( 6... Be7 $2
{ It blocks the attack on the queen, but after [Bxe7 Qxe7] the dark squares are empty: [w:Nb5] threatens Nc7+ and Nd6+. If Black castles, Nc7 wins the exchange. Play [b:Qb6]. [%cal Gg5e7] }
7. Bxe7 ) 7. dxc5
{ [Bxc5] is the main move: the bishop develops with a hit on f2. [Nxc5] also works and is close to equal. Grabbing the pawn with [Qxb2] is a known trap: [w:Nb5] hits c7 and threatens a fork, and the queen on b2 can be chased by Rb1. Black should develop first and leave b2 alone. [%csl Yf2,Yc7,Yb2][%cal Gf8c5] }
( 7. Qd2 ) ( 7. Bd3 ) 7... Bxc5 ( 7... Nxc5 ) ( 7... Qxb2 $2
{ The queen takes a pawn and hits the knight on c3, but [Nb5] jumps in with threats on c7 and d6, and the queen has few squares: Rb1 comes next. Black's development is far behind. Develop with [b:Bxc5] instead. [%csl Rc7,Rd6][%cal Gc3b5] }
8. Nb5 ) 8. Qd2
{ [Nc6] is the right move: develop and keep the queen where it is. The two greedy captures are traps. ...Qxb2 runs into [w:Rb1] and [w:Nb5], and the queen is chased while c7 is hit. ...Bxf2+ wins a pawn, but after [w:Qxf2] ...Qxb2 White can give the rook with [w:Kd2] and hunt Black's king with Bb5 and Qc5. After ...Nc6 White has the bishop pair for Black's active pieces. [%csl Yc7][%cal Gb8c6] }
8... Nc6 ( 8... Qxb2 $2
{ The pawn looks free, but the queen is now far from home: [Rb1] chases it to a3, and [w:Nb5] follows with threats against c7. White is ahead in development and Black's queen can be trapped. Develop with [b:Nc6] instead. [%csl Rc7][%cal Ga1b1] }
9. Rb1 ) ( 8... Bxf2+ $2
{ Black gives the bishop to open the long diagonal: after [Qxf2] ...Qxb2 [w:Kd2] the queen can even take both rooks, but White's pieces then hunt the black king in the centre, and White is better. Develop with [b:Nc6] and leave both pawns alone. [%csl Rf2,Re8][%cal Gd2f2] }
9. Qxf2 ) ) ( 5. Nce2
{ The knight makes room for c3, so d4 will be defended by pawns. [c5] is the natural answer; after [w:c3] Black plays ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 as in the Advance. White adds f4 and Nf3 later. Black should keep up the pressure on d4 and avoid ...a6 waiting moves. Black's queen on b6 and knight on c6 are the usual pressure pieces. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc7c5] }
5... c5 ) ( 5. Be3 ) ( 5. Qg4
{ The queen hits g7, but Black has a simple answer: [c5], attacking the base of the chain, and if White takes on g7 the rook comes to g8 with play on the g-file. Moves that defend g7 at once, like ...g6, ...f5 or ...a6, are too slow or weaken the kingside: after ...g6 White plays Bg5 and h4-h5 with a strong attack on the dark squares. [%csl Yd4,Yg7][%cal Gc7c5] }
5... c5 ( 5... g6 $2
{ Protecting g7 with a pawn looks safe, but it weakens f6 and h6. [Bg5] hits the queen, and after ...Be7 [w:h4] and h5 opens the h-file. Black's dark squares cannot be defended. Ignore the threat with [b:c5]. [%csl Rf6,Rh6][%cal Gc1g5] }
6. Bg5 ) ( 5... a6 $2
{ A waiting move while the queen sits on g4. [Bg5] hits the queen on d8: ...f6 drops e6 with check, and after ...Be7 [w:Bxe7] [b:Qxe7] the queen takes on g7 and attacks the rook. Black has no time for slow moves here. Play [b:c5] at once. [%csl Rd8,Rg7][%cal Gc1g5] }
6. Bg5 ) ) 5... c5
{ [Nf3] is the main move, defending d4 and e5. [dxc5] gives up the base of the chain but gains space; Black takes back with the bishop or the knight and gets active pieces against e5. [Nce2] keeps the pawn chain with c3, and [Be3] first is another order. Black's knight on d7 can go to b6 or c5 later, and the queen to b6. [%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yb6][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 5... a6 ) ( 5... Be7 ) 6. Nf3
{ [Nc6] brings the third attacker against d4. [cxd4] first is also good, and [a6] and [Be7] are common. Black should not hurry with ...Qb6, which allows [w:Na4] with tempo on the queen. White's dark bishop is needed on e3 to protect d4 in most lines, so the usual next move is [w:Be3], and Black chooses among ...a6, ...cxd4, ...Qb6 and ...Be7. [%csl Yd4,Ye3][%cal Gb8c6] }
( 6. dxc5
{ White gives up the base of the chain for space and play on the dark squares. [Nc6] and [Bxc5] are the main replies; [Nxc5] also works. After ...Nc6 White should develop with [w:Nf3] or [w:a3]; the queen raid [w:Qg4] hits g7, but ...f5 shuts it out and the c5 pawn falls. Black's pieces come out quickly: the dark bishop takes on c5 with a view of g1, and ...Qb6 and ...f6 hit White's loose centre. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gb8c6,Gf8c5] }
6... Nc6
{ White has given up the base of the chain, so e5 needs pieces. [Nf3] is the natural move, and [a3] keeps the bishop off b4. The queen raid [w:Qg4] is a typical club error: ...f5 shuts the queen out of the kingside and Black takes on c5 with a fine game. Black's plan is ...Bxc5 or ...Nxc5, ...Qb6 and ...f6, hitting the head of the chain once the knight on f3 is pinned or traded. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gg1f3,Ga2a3,Gc1e3] }
( 6... Bxc5 ) ( 6... Nxc5 ) 7. Nf3 ( 7. a3 ) ( 7. Be3 ) ( 7. Qg4 $6
{ The queen hits g7, a natural target when Black's dark bishop is still at home. But [f5] blocks the queen and after [w:Qe2] or [w:Qg3] Black plays ...Nxc5 and ...Bd7 with easy development, while White's centre has lost its base. Develop with [w:Nf3]. [%csl Rg4,Rc5][%cal Gf7f5] }
7... f5 ) ) ( 6. Be3 ) ( 6. Nce2 ) 6... Nc6
{ [Be3] is the main line: the bishop defends d4 and keeps the dark squares. [Ne2] holds d4 with c3. The natural [w:Be2] is a known mistake: after ...cxd4 [w:Nxd4] Black plays ...Ndxe5, and if [w:fxe5] then ...Qh4+ and ...Qxd4 wins the knight back, leaving Black a pawn up. After [w:Be3] the game reaches the main Steinitz position. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc1e3] }
( 6... cxd4 ) ( 6... a6 ) ( 6... Be7 ) 7. Be3
{ The Steinitz main line. Black chooses a plan: [a6] and ...b5 gains space on the queenside and stops Nb5; [cxd4] with ...Bc5 trades White's good dark bishop; [Qb6] hits b2 and d4; and [Be7] develops calmly before castling. White often castles long and attacks with f5 or g4-g5. White's king usually goes long after Qd2, and Black's pawns roll on the queenside: whoever is faster wins. [%csl Yb2,Yd4][%cal Ga7a6] }
( 7. Ne2 ) ( 7. Be2 $2
{ The bishop prepares castling, but it leaves the knight on f3 with all the work. [cxd4] and after [w:Nxd4] the shot ...Ndxe5! wins a pawn: if fxe5 then ...Qh4+ picks up the knight on d4. Defend d4 first with [w:Be3]. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gc5d4] }
7... cxd4 ) 7... a6
{ [Qd2] is the main reply. Black continues with ...b5 to gain queenside space and to make a long castle risky. Later plans include ...Qb6 or ...cxd4, ...Bc5 to trade the dark bishops, and ...b4 to chase the knight. White can switch plans with dxc5 and Qf2. Black's king often stays in the centre or goes to the kingside after ...Be7 and ...O-O. [%cal Gd1d2] }
( 7... cxd4 8. Nxd4
{ [Bc5] is the key idea: Black offers to trade the dark bishops, and if White takes on c6, ...bxc6 strengthens Black's centre. [Qb6] hits b2 and d4. The plan is ...O-O, ...a6, ...b5-b4 and ...Ba6 to trade the bad bishop. Black's position is very solid. The knight on d4 is White's best piece, and Black's ...Bc5 challenges it at once. [%csl Yb2,Yd4][%cal Gf8c5] }
8... Bc5 ( 8... Qb6 ) 9. Qd2
{ [O-O] and [Bxd4] with ...Nxd4 and ...Qb6 both work. After castling long White attacks with h4-h5, but Black's play with ...a6, ...b5 and ...b4 comes quickly. The endgame after a trade of queens, with Black's knight coming to c4, is good for Black. Black's knight on d7 often goes to b6 and c4 in the endgame. [%csl Yc4][%cal Ge8g8] }
9... O-O ( 9... Bxd4 ) ) ( 7... Qb6
{ The queen hits b2 and adds to the pressure on d4. [Na4] gains time on it, and after ...Qa5+ [w:c3] ...cxd4 [w:b4] the game is sharp. [Qd2] is calmer: Black can take on b2 only at great risk, since Rb1 and Nb5 come with tempo. [a3] prepares b4. After [w:Na4] the queen goes to a5 with check, and the knight on a4 is offside but annoying. [%csl Yb2,Yd4,Ya5][%cal Gc3a4] }
8. Na4 ( 8. Qd2 ) ( 8. a3 ) ) ( 7... Be7
{ Black develops and castles before choosing a plan. After [Qd2] [b:O-O] White should not castle long: the answer ...c4 closes the centre, and Black's queenside pawns roll with ...b5-b4 even at the cost of a pawn. [Be2] with short castling and [dxc5], which stops ...c4, are the safer plans. Black's breaks are ...cxd4 and ...f6, and the light bishop often goes to b7 after ...b6. [%csl Yb7][%cal Gd1d2] }
8. Qd2 ( 8. Be2 ) ( 8. dxc5 ) 8... O-O
{ [Be2] and short castling or [dxc5] are the safe moves. Castling long is the trap: ...c4 locks the centre, the d4 pawn is safe, and Black's queenside attack with ...b5, ...Rb8 and ...Qa5 comes fast, even at the cost of a pawn. White's kingside attack is much slower because the centre is closed. After [Be2] Black plays ...a6, ...b5 and ...Qb6, or ...cxd4 and ...f6; after [dxc5] the knight takes back on c5 and ...f6 hits the chain. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gf1e2] }
9. Be2 ( 9. dxc5 ) ( 9. O-O-O $2
{ Castling long looks natural with the queen on d2, but the king walks into Black's attack. [c4] shuts the centre so that White's pieces cannot help, and ...b5-b4, ...Qa5 and ...Nb4 arrive quickly. Play [w:Be2] or [w:dxc5]. [%cal Gc5c4] }
9... c4 ) ) 8. Qd2 ( 8. Be2 ) ( 8. dxc5 ) 8... b5
{ White chooses where to play. [a3] stops ...b4; [dxc5] with Bxc5 and Qf2 trades off Black's good dark bishop; [Nd1] and c3 reroute the knight to hold d4. After [dxc5] [b:Bxc5] the trade of dark bishops leaves Black's light bishop as its worst piece, so Black plans ...Bb7, ...b4 and ...a5, or ...Ba6 to trade it. White's king rarely castles long here: Black's pawns are already on the move. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ga2a3] }
9. a3 ( 9. dxc5 ) ( 9. Nd1 ) ) ( 3. Nd2
{ The Tarrasch. The knight on d2 sidesteps the pin ...Bb4 and keeps c3 free for a pawn, so d4 can be held without doubled pawns. The drawback is that it blocks the c1 bishop and does not hit d5. Black's main answers are [Nf6], which leads to closed positions, [c5], which opens the centre, and [dxe4], the Rubinstein. [Be7], [Nc6] and [a6] are flexible waiting moves, each aimed at making one of White's set-ups less effective. [%csl Yc3,Yd4,Yd5][%cal Gg8f6] }
3... Nf6 ( 3... c5
{ The open Tarrasch. [exd5] is the main move; Black then takes back with the pawn, accepting an isolated pawn, or with the queen. [Ngf3] and [c3] keep the tension; [dxc5] gives up the centre. In all these lines the d2 knight goes to f3 or b3, and White's light bishop to c4 or b5. Black's choice is about structure: the isolated pawn gives active pieces, the queen recapture a healthier but slower game. [%csl Yc4,Yb5][%cal Ge4d5] }
4. exd5
{ Black chooses the structure. [exd5] gives an isolated pawn but active pieces and an open e-file; it was a battleground of the Karpov-Korchnoi matches. [Qxd5] keeps the structure healthy and the queen active, at the cost of some time. ...cxd4 lets [w:Bb5+] gain time and open lines while Black's king is still in the middle, so it is the one capture to avoid. [%cal Ge6d5] }
( 4. Ngf3
{ [Nc6] and [cxd4] are the main moves, and [Nf6] is also good. ...dxe4 gives up the centre too soon: after [w:Nxe4] White's pieces are well placed. White keeps the option of exd5 for later. After ...Nc6 White often plays exd5 and Bb5, reaching the main isolated-pawn lines, and after ...cxd4 the knight takes back on d4 with an open centre. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6] }
4... Nc6 ( 4... cxd4 ) ( 4... Nf6 ) ) ( 4. c3
{ White backs up d4 with a pawn. [Nc6] and [cxd4] are fine, and [dxe4] is also played. ...c4 is a mistake: it takes the pressure off the centre, and White hits the chain at once with [w:b3]. After ...Nc6 White plays [w:Ngf3], and after ...cxd4 [w:cxd4] Black may take on e4 or keep the tension, with White's d-pawn the long-term target. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6] }
