{ Black has the centre trade and can develop with gain of time: [Nf6] hits the bishop, which goes to f3 or d3. Black then plays [b:c5] or [b:Nbd7] and [b:c5]. With White's bishop on f3, the queenside needs a little care, as the bishop looks at b7 and a knight on c6 can be pinned. The open centre suits Black's development, and White's bishop will be hit again. [%csl Yf3,Yd3,Yb7][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6
{ The bishop is hit. [Bf3] keeps it on the long diagonal, aiming at b7, and [Bd3] keeps it pointed at the kingside. After [w:Bf3] Black plays [b:c5]: if White trades queens with dxc5, Black's queenside development needs a little care, as ...Nc6 can be pinned and ...Bd7 drops b7; the simplest is ...Nbd7 and ...Nxc5. Black is comfortable in both cases; the light bishop still needs a plan, often ...b6 and ...Bb7. [%csl Yb7][%cal Ge4f3] }
( 4... c5 ) 5. Bf3 ( 5. Bd3 ) ) ( 3. Nf3 $2
{ The Perseus Gambit gives the e4 pawn for little: [dxe4] and the knight has no good square. [Ne5] is the only try, and Black stays a pawn up with ...Nf6 or ...Nd7; [Ng5] or [Nfd2] to win the pawn back fail. Black must take, though: ...c5 or ...Nf6 lets White off. Defend the pawn with [w:Nc3] or [w:Nd2]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4
{ White must move the knight. [Ne5] is the only good square: it can't easily be kicked, and the obvious ...f6 is a trap. Winning the pawn back with [w:Ng5] or [w:Nfd2] fails: after ...Nf6, ...Bb4+ and ...Qxd4, Black wins a second pawn. In practice the knight on e5 is a nuisance, but with ...Nd7 or ...Nf6 Black keeps the pawn and develops. [%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Ne5
{ White has given the e4 pawn for a knight on e5, where no pawn can easily chase it, and hopes for Bc4 and Qh5 against f7. Black should develop: [Nf6] protects e4 and shuts the queen out of h5, [Nd7] offers to trade the knight, and [Nc6] is also fine. The obvious ...f6 loses: [w:Qh5+] and after ...g6 [w:Nxg6]. With calm development Black keeps the extra pawn and is better. [%csl Ye4,Ye5,Yf7][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 4. Ng5 $2
{ The knight attacks e4, but Black simply defends it: [Nf6], and after [Nf6 Nc3 Bb4] the d4 pawn falls too, as ...Qxd4 is coming. Play [w:Ne5]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ) ( 4. Nfd2 $6
{ The knight goes back to win e4, but d4 is no longer guarded: [Qxd4] takes a second pawn, and after [Qxd4 c3 Qd5] Black keeps both. Play [w:Ne5]. [%csl Rd2,Re4][%cal Gd8d4] }
4... Qxd4 ) 4... Nf6 ( 4... Nd7 ) ( 4... Nc6 ) ( 4... f6 $4
{ The pawn kicks the knight, but it opens the e8-h5 diagonal: [Qh5+ g6 Nxg6 hxg6 Qxh8] wins the rook cleanly. Develop with [b:Nf6] instead. [%cal Gd1h5] }
5. Qh5+ ) ( 4... Bd6 $6
{ The bishop challenges the knight, but it leaves g7 without a defender. [Qg4] hits g7 and e4 at once, and after ...Nd7 [w:Qxg7] White wins the pawn back with the better position. Play [b:Nf6] or [b:Nd7], which keep the extra pawn safely. [%csl Rg7,Re4][%cal Gd1g4] }
5. Qg4 ) ( 4... f5 $2
{ It keeps the e4 pawn, but like ...f6 it opens the e8-h5 diagonal: [Qh5+] ...g6 [w:Nxg6], and taking the knight with the h-pawn opens the file to the rook on h8. White wins material. Develop with [b:Nf6] or [b:Nd7]. [%csl Rg6,Rh8][%cal Gd1h5] }
5. Qh5+ ) ) ( 3. Be3 $6
{ The Alapin Gambit. White means to meet [dxe4] with [w:f3] and open the f-file, but Black keeps the extra pawn and a sound position with ...Nf6 and ...Nd5 or ...c5, and the e3 bishop is easily hit. Declining with ...Nf6 or ...c5 lets White off: take the pawn. [%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4
{ White has given the e-pawn. [f3] is the gambit idea: after [b:exf3] [w:Nxf3] White gets quick development with [w:Bd3] and castling, and after [b:Nf6] [w:fxe4] [b:Nxe4] [w:Bd3] Black must not grab with ...Qh4+: [w:g3] and Bf2 turn it against the queen. [Nd2] first also aims at f3. Black's best plan is to develop with ...Nf6, ...Nd5 or ...c5 and keep the pawn. Black can also return the pawn with ...e5 or ...c5 later to open the game at a good moment. [%cal Gb1d2] }
4. Nd2 ( 4. f3 ) ) ( 3. c4 $2
{ The Diemer-Duhm Gambit. After [dxe4] White needs [w:Nc3] and [w:f3] or [w:Be3] to get anything, and Black can return the pawn later with ...c5 or ...e5 to open lines. The trick to know comes after [dxe4 Nc3 Nf6]: ...Bb4 pins the knight and the e4 pawn is safe. Take the pawn: the alternatives give White a good Queen's Gambit. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4
{ White is a pawn down and must develop fast. [Nc3] is the right move, hitting e4 and preparing [w:f3] or [w:Be3]. After [Nc3 Nf6] Black meets threats with ...Bb4, pinning the knight. The two pawn moves are worse: [w:f3] loosens the king before the pieces are out, and [w:d5] spends another tempo while Black develops with ...Nf6 and ...Bc5. Black should keep the pawn only while it costs nothing; ...c5 and ...e5 are good ways to give it back with interest. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gb1c3] }
4. Nc3 ( 4. d5 $2
{ The push aims to cramp Black, but it spends another move while White is a pawn down and nothing is developed. [Nf6] and ...Bc5 come with ease, and after [w:Nc3] [b:O-O] Black is ready to hit the d5 pawn with ...exd5 or ...c6 at the best moment. Develop with [w:Nc3] first. [%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ) ( 4. f3 $2
{ The pawn hits e4, but it loosens White's king before a single piece is out. Black need not take: [Nh6] heads for f5 to hit d4 and eye e3, while [c5] strikes at the centre too, and ...exf3 would only help White's knight out. Develop with [w:Nc3] first. [%csl Rf5,Rd4][%cal Gg8h6] }
4... Nh6 ) ) 3... c5
{ White must decide how to hold d4. [c3] backs it with a pawn and is the main line; [Nf3] is Nimzowitsch's idea of letting d4 go and keeping e5 with pieces. [dxc5] is Steinitz's old try, which gives up the centre's base. Two early queen or wing adventures cost White: [w:Qg4] hits g7 but after ...cxd4 the d4 pawn is gone, and the Wing Gambit [w:b4] gives a pawn that Black is happy to take on d4 instead. [%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yg7][%cal Gc2c3] }
( 3... b6
{ A plan from the start: ...b6 and ...Ba6 trade the bad bishop. But the order matters. Against [c3] the bishop cannot go to a6 at once, because Bxa6 Nxa6 Qa4+ forks the king and the knight. Black plays ...Qd7 first. White's best set-up is [c3], [Nf3] and Bd3 or Ne2 with f4-f5 ideas. With the pawn already on b6, ...Ba6 will trade White's best bishop. [%cal Gc2c3] }
4. c3
{ [Qd7] prepares ...Ba6 so that the queen covers the knight on a6. [c5] and [Ne7] are also played. The direct ...Ba6 loses a piece: [w:Bxa6] [b:Nxa6] [w:Qa4+] attacks the king and the knight at once. The queen on d7 stops that check. A common plan for White is Nh3-f4, Bd3 and Nd2-f3, aimed at e6 and d5, while Black trades the light bishops and plays ...c5. [%csl Ya6,Yd7,Ye6][%cal Gd8d7] }
( 4. Nf3 ) 4... Qd7 ( 4... c5 ) ( 4... Ne7 ) ( 4... Ba6 $4
{ Trading the bad bishop is the right idea, but not yet. [Bxa6 Nxa6 Qa4+] forks the king and the knight on a6, which has no defender. Black loses a piece. Prepare the trade with [b:Qd7]. [%csl Ra6][%cal Gf1a6] }
5. Bxa6 ) ) ( 3... Bd7
{ Black heads for the bishop trade with ...Bb5, without ...c5 first. White's best is [Nf3] and [c3]; [Be2] lets the trade on e2 happen. The critical line is [w:Nf3] ...a6 [w:Bd3] ...Bb5 and then c4!, which uses White's lead in development. In the main line Black's bishop trade is worth the time it costs, and Black gets a comfortable game. [%cal Gg1f3] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. c3 ) ( 4. Be2 ) ) 4. c3 ( 4. Nf3 cxd4 5. Bd3
{ White gives the d4 pawn and develops: [w:O-O], [w:Re1], and perhaps [w:Nbd2]-b3 to win it back. [Nc6] is natural and hits e5; Black can then hold the extra pawn with ...Bc5 or ...Nge7. If White recaptures on d4 later with a piece, e5 has lost its pawn support and becomes a target for ...Qc7 and ...f6. Black can also play ...Bc5 first, hitting f2 and covering d4. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gb8c6] }
5... Nc6 ( 5... Bc5 ) ) ( 4. dxc5
{ Steinitz's 4.dxc5 gives up the base of the chain, so e5 has to be held by pieces. [Bxc5] develops at once, and the answer [w:Qg4] is harmless: ...Ne7 covers g7 and ...Nbc6 follows with a fine game. [Nc6] first is just as good, taking on c5 next move. In both lines Black's dark bishop gets the open a7-g1 diagonal, which it never has in the main lines, and e5 becomes a target for ...Nc6 and ...Qc7. After [b:Bxc5] White's best is Nf3 and Bd3, but Black has easy development. [%csl Ye5,Yg7,Yc5][%cal Gf8c5] }
4... Bxc5 ( 4... Nc6 ) ) ( 4. Qg4 $2
{ The queen eyes g7, which looks like the right target with Black's knight unable to reach f6. But the centre falls first: [cxd4] and the queen cannot take back without being hit by ...Nc6, so White has to gambit with [w:Nf3]. After ...Nc6 and ...h5 or ...Qc7 Black is a sound pawn up. Black's one poor answer is ...Qb6, which lets White take on c5. Play [w:c3] to keep d4. [%csl Rg7,Rd4][%cal Gc5d4] }
4... cxd4
{ White has to give the pawn: [Nf3] develops and keeps the attack on g7 going. [Qxd4] is met by ...Nc6, hitting the queen with tempo, and Black is comfortable. In the Nimzowitsch Attack [w:Nf3] [b:Nc6] [w:Bd3], Black's best is ...h5: the queen must leave the g-file, and after [w:Qf4] ...f6 Black hits the head of the chain. The quieter ...Qc7, hitting e5, only keeps the balance. White's compensation is development and the e5 wedge, but Black's extra pawn on d4 is healthy. [%csl Yg7,Ye5][%cal Gg1f3] }
5. Nf3 ( 5. Bd3 ) ) ( 4. b4 $2
{ This Frenkel Gambit hopes for ...cxb4 and a big centre with [w:a3]. Black declines in the best way: [cxd4] wins the d4 pawn instead, and White has neither the centre nor the pawn. The e5 pawn is now loose and ...Nc6 or ...Qc7 attacks it. Support the centre with [w:c3] instead. [%csl Rd4,Re5][%cal Gc5d4] }
4... cxd4 ) 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6
{ The queen lines up behind the c5 pawn on d4 and hits b2, so White's dark bishop is tied to defending b2. White has three main replies: [Bd3] offers the Milner-Barry Gambit, [a3] prepares b4 so the bishop can develop, and [Be2] is quiet. [Na3] heads for c2. [Be3] drops b2 to ...Qxb2, and [dxc5] gives up the chain's base and lets Black's dark bishop out with tempo. White's queen sometimes goes to b3 to offer a trade. [%csl Yd4,Yb2][%cal Gf1d3] }
6. Bd3
{ White develops the bishop to its best diagonal, but now the queen on d1 no longer defends d4, so the pawn has two defenders against three attackers. The trap is famous: after [cxd4] [w:cxd4], the pawn on d4 looks free, but ...Nxd4 [w:Nxd4] ...Qxd4 loses the queen to [w:Bb5+]. Black plays [Bd7] first, or [cxd4] followed by ...Bd7, to cover b5 before taking. ...Nge7 lets White take on c5 with tempo. [%csl Yd4,Yb5][%cal Gc5d4] }
( 6. a3
{ White wants b4: the pawn will take space and free the dark bishop from guarding b2. [c4] is the main answer: it stops b4 for good (b4 is met by ...cxb3 en passant) and keeps a White bishop out of d3. [Nh6] heads for f5; [Bd7], [a5] and [Nge7] are also played. Taking on d4 at once lets White recapture and play b4 with gain of space. [%csl Yb2,Yd3,Yf5][%cal Gc5c4] }
6... c4
{ The queenside is closed. White's plans are on the kingside: [Nbd2] with Be2, O-O and a later g3, h4 or Ne1-g2 and f4-f5. Another idea is [w:b3], opening the a-file against the chain's new base. Black brings the knight to a5 to watch b3 and c4, develops with ...Bd7 and ...Ne7, and may castle long. The pawn on c4 is the base of Black's queenside, and b3 is White's lever. [%csl Ya5,Yb3,Yc4][%cal Gb1d2] }
( 6... Nh6
{ The knight goes to f5 via h6. [b4] is the main reply: it takes space and frees the dark bishop from guarding b2. Taking on h6 at once is a mistake here: ...Qxb2 hits a1, and after Nbd2 and ...gxh6 Black has two extra pawns and the g-file. Black's knight on f5 after ...cxd4 will press d4 again. After [w:b4] [b:cxd4] [w:cxd4] [b:Nf5] White plays Bb2 and Black ...Bd7 and ...Rc8. [%csl Yf5,Yb2,Yh6][%cal Gb2b4] }
7. b4 ( 7. Bd3 ) ( 7. Be2 ) ( 7. Bxh6 $2
{ Doubling the h-pawns looks like a free bonus, but the bishop was the only piece guarding b2. [Qxb2] attacks the rook on a1, and after [Qxb2 Nbd2 gxh6] Black has won a pawn and keeps the bishop pair with the g-file open. Play [w:b4] first, which frees the bishop. [%csl Rb2][%cal Gb6b2] }
7... Qxb2 ) 7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5
{ The knight on f5 presses d4 with the queen and the c6 knight. [Bb2] is the main move and defends it; [Be3] also works but the knight can take it. Black plays ...Bd7, ...Rc8 and ...Na5-c4 on the light squares, while White can gain space on the kingside with g4. Taking d4 too early with the f5 knight can run into a pin by Be3. [%csl Yf5,Yd4][%cal Gc1b2] }
9. Bb2 ( 9. Be3 ) ) ( 6... Bd7 ) ( 6... a5 ) ( 6... Nge7 ) 7. Nbd2
{ [Na5] is the right move: it protects c4 and b3, so White cannot attack the pawn chain easily. The natural ...Nge7?? leaves c4 weak and loses to [w:Bxc4]: after ...dxc4 the knight retakes with an attack on the queen and then jumps to d6 with check. Black's plans are ...Bd7, ...Ne7-c6, ...O-O-O and ...h6-g5 on the kingside. After [b:Na5] White plays Rb1, Be2 and O-O, and Black plays ...Bd7 and ...Ne7. [%csl Yc4,Yb3][%cal Gc6a5] }
( 7. Be2 ) ( 7. g3 ) 7... Na5 ( 7... Qc7 ) ( 7... Nge7 $2
{ Developing the knight is normal elsewhere, but here c4 has only the d5 pawn defending it. [Bxc4] breaks the chain: after [Bxc4 dxc4 Nxc4] the knight hits the queen and threatens Nd6+, winning back more than the piece with Black's king stuck. Guard c4 first with [b:Na5]. [%csl Rc4,Rd5][%cal Gf1c4] }
8. Bxc4 ) ) ( 6. Be2
{ A quiet but solid move. [cxd4] and then ...Nh6 or ...Nge7 brings the knight to f5, where it hits d4. [Nh6] at once is also fine, and here White's best answer is [w:Bxh6]: after ...gxh6 Black has the bishop pair and the g-file, but the broken pawns give White a small edge. Taking on c5 is the error: ...Bxc5 and ...Ng4 hit f2 and e5. The knight should not go to f6, where the e5 pawn hits it. White's plan is O-O, Na3-c2 and b4; Black's is ...Nf5, ...Bd7 and ...Rc8. [%csl Yf5,Yd4,Yf2][%cal Gc5d4] }
6... cxd4 ( 6... Nh6 ) ( 6... Nge7 ) 7. cxd4
{ Black now develops the kingside knight. [Nh6] aims at f5 and dares [w:Bxh6], which would give up the bishop that guards b2. [Nge7] goes the same way; after [w:Nc3] [b:Nf5] Black hits d4 three times. White's knight goes to c3 and the dark bishop to e3 or, after b4, to b2, while Black plays ...Nf5, ...Bd7 and ...Rc8. [%csl Yf5,Yb2,Yd4][%cal Gg8h6] }
7... Nh6 ( 7... Nge7 ) ) ( 6. Na3 ) ( 6. Be3 $2
{ The bishop defends d4 a third time, but it leaves b2 with no defender. [Qxb2] wins a pawn and keeps up the threat ...Qxa1. The best White has is [w:Qb3] and a queen trade, and the natural [w:Nbd2] is worse because it allows ...Qxc3 and ...c4 ideas. Develop with [w:Bd3], [w:Be2] or [w:a3] first. [%csl Rd4,Rb2][%cal Gb6b2] }
6... Qxb2
{ White is a pawn down and must defend the rook on a1. [Qb3] offers a queen trade; after ...Qxa1 White gets some play with [w:dxc5] but objectively Black is better. The natural [Nbd2] is an error: it leaves c3 and the queen's escape route open, and after ...c4 Black threatens ...Qxa2 or ...Qxc3 with an extra pawn. Black's queen must then get back to safety with ...Qb6 or ...Qa5. [%csl Ya1,Yc3][%cal Gd1b3] }
7. Qb3 ( 7. dxc5 ) ( 7. Nbd2 $2
{ The knight protects the rook on a1 from b3 and develops, but it fixes nothing: [c4] keeps the queen safe and stops Nb3. Black then takes on a2 or c3 with a two-pawn lead. Try [w:Qb3] or [w:dxc5] for counterplay. [%csl Ra2,Rc3][%cal Gc5c4] }
7... c4 ) ) ( 6. dxc5 $6
{ This wins a tempo on the queen only on paper. [Bxc5] develops the dark bishop onto the a7-g1 diagonal, hits f2, and the e5 pawn has lost its base: ...f6 and ...Nge7 follow. Black's development is now better than White's. Keep the chain with [w:Bd3], [w:a3] or [w:Be2]. [%csl Rf2,Re5][%cal Gf8c5] }
6... Bxc5 ) 6... cxd4 ( 6... Bd7
{ Black covers b5 first and keeps the choice of taking on d4. White's best is often [dxc5]: after ...Bxc5 the bishop is active but e5 is White's, and b4 may gain space. [O-O] transposes to gambit lines after ...cxd4. A typical blunder comes after [dxc5 Bxc5 O-O]: ...Nge7?? blocks the bishop's retreat and [w:b4] traps it. Black's queen and bishop are ready for ...cxd4 and ...Nxd4 when White castles. [%csl Yb5,Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gd4c5] }
7. dxc5 ( 7. O-O ) ( 7. Nbd2 ) 7... Bxc5 8. O-O
{ The bishop on c5 is active but has few retreats. [a5] stops b4 at once and keeps the bishop safe; [Qc7] and [f6] are also good. The natural ...Nge7?? is the trap: [w:b4] attacks the bishop, and it cannot go back to f8 or e7 because the knight is there, so White wins a piece for a pawn or two. White's plan is b4 and Qe2 or Re1, with the e5 pawn as a wedge; Black's is ...f6 to break it. [%csl Yc5][%cal Ga7a5] }
8... a5 ( 8... Qc7 ) ( 8... f6 ) ( 8... Nge7 $4
{ Developing towards f5 looks normal, but the knight takes e7 from the bishop. [b4] attacks it, and with f8 and e7 blocked the bishop has no safe square: after ...Bxb4 [w:cxb4] or ...Bd4 [w:Nxd4] White is a piece up. Play [b:a5] first. [%csl Rf5,Re7][%cal Gb2b4] }
9. b4 ) ) ( 6... Nge7 $6
{ The knight heads for f5, but it shuts in the dark bishop just when White can open its diagonal. [dxc5] hits the queen and keeps an extra pawn, with b4 to support c5. After [dxc5 Qxc5 O-O] White is comfortably better. Play [b:cxd4] or [b:Bd7] first. [%csl Rb6,Rf8][%cal Gd4c5] }
7. dxc5 ) *

[Event "French Defence (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 ( 3. exd5
{ The Exchange Variation. [exd5] is the move: the pawn structure becomes symmetrical, the e-file opens and, best of all for Black, the c8 bishop is no longer shut in. Taking with the queen, ...Qxd5, lets [w:Nc3] gain time on it, and White gets a comfortable lead in development. With the pawn on d5 Black has the same space as White and can choose between quiet symmetry and castling on opposite sides. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge6d5] }
3... exd5
{ A symmetrical position with an open e-file and both sides' bishops free. White's moves decide the type of game: [Nf3] and [Bd3] are quiet and solid, [c4] gives an isolated pawn position like a Queen's Gambit, and [Nc3] invites ...Bb4 or ...Nf6. Steinitz called this the dullest opening of all, but either side can avoid symmetry by castling long or by playing c4 or ...c5. Black should never feel worse here: White's extra tempo is all there is. [%cal Gg1f3] }
4. Nf3
{ Black's simplest moves are [Nf6] and [Bd6], keeping the symmetry and castling short. [Nc6] is more ambitious: with the c-pawn still behind the knight, Black can follow with ...Bg4, ...Qd7 and ...O-O-O for an opposite-side attack. [Bg4] and [c6] are also good. Breaking with ...c5 at once gives White a target: after [w:Bb5+] and dxc5 Black is left with an isolated pawn on d5. Black's choice of castling side decides the type of game. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 4. Bd3
{ White keeps the knight's options open, so it can go to e2 and avoid ...Bg4. Black's most flexible moves are [Nc6], eyeing b4 and d4, [Bd6] and [c5]. After [Nc6] a later ...Nge7 and ...Bf5 trade White's good bishop. [c5] leads to an open game where Black's pieces are active. Against ...Nc6, White's c3 keeps the knight out of b4. [%csl Ye2][%cal Gb8c6] }
4... Nc6
{ The knight eyes b4 and d4. [c3] covers both and keeps the bishop on its diagonal; then ...Bd6, ...Nge7 and ...Bf5 trade it off. [Nf3] allows ...Nb4 or ...Bg4, and [Ne2] keeps the f-pawn free. White's plan is often Bf4 to trade the dark bishops and then castle. After ...Bf5 the trade of light bishops leaves both sides with few winning chances. [%csl Yb4,Yd4][%cal Gc2c3] }
( 4... Bd6 ) ( 4... c5 ) ( 4... Nf6 ) 5. c3 ( 5. Nf3 ) ( 5. Ne2 ) ) ( 4. c4
{ The Monte Carlo variation: White gives the game an isolated-pawn character, as in a Queen's Gambit. [Nf6] is the main move, then ...Be7 or ...Bb4 and castling. [c6] and [dxc4] are also good. When Black waits for White's bishop to move before taking on c4, the capture wins a tempo, and later ...Bg4 puts pressure on d4. White's isolated d-pawn after cxd5 or dxc4 is a target, but gives White space. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6
{ In the Monte Carlo [c4] system against the Exchange French, [Nf6] reinforces d5 with a piece so Black never has to concede the centre with [b:dxc4] until ready. White develops [Nc3] or [Nf3] (usually both), welcoming an isolated queen's pawn if Black later captures on c4 because the open e-file and free c1-bishop give White easy kingside activity against [b:Bb4] or [b:Be7] and [b:O-O]. [%cal Gb1c3] }
( 4... c6 ) ( 4... dxc4 ) ( 4... Bb4+ ) 5. Nc3
{ [Bb4] pins the knight, so ...Ne4 becomes a strong idea; [Be7] is calmer. In both lines Black castles quickly, then either takes on c4 to leave White with an isolated d4 pawn or keeps the tension. White's pieces become active, so Black should not grab material carelessly: Bxf7+ and Nxd5 tricks appear as soon as the e-file opens. After ...dxc4 Bxc4 O-O, Black plays for a blockade on d5. [%csl Yd4,Yd5][%cal Gf8b4] }
( 5. Nf3 ) 5... Bb4 ( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... c6 ) 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Bd3 Re8+ 8. Be3 Ng4
{ The knight hits the bishop on e3. White's best is to castle, [O-O], and let the bishop be taken: after ...Nxe3 fxe3 the f-file opens for White's rook, and the pawn on e3 is poisoned. If ...Rxe3 then Nxd5 forks the rook and the bishop on b4. Keeping the bishop with Qd2 is about a pawn worse. After [w:O-O] Black's best is ...Nxe3 fxe3 and ...dxc4, not grabbing e3 at once. [%csl Ye3][%cal Ge1g1] }
9. O-O Nxe3 10. fxe3
{ White has a half-open f-file and the e3 pawn looks weak. [dxc4] first, then ...Bxc3 and ...Rxe3, is the way to win it; [Bxc3] at once is another. Taking at once with ...Rxe3 is the trap: [w:Nxd5] attacks the rook on e3 and the bishop on b4 together, and Black loses a piece. After ...dxc4 Bxc4 Bxc3 bxc3 Rxe3 Black has won a pawn and White has the bishop and some pressure. [%csl Ye3][%cal Gd5c4] }
10... dxc4 ( 10... Bxc3 ) ( 10... Rxe3 $4
