{ The most testing move: White develops and defends e4. Black has three main replies: [Nf6], the Classical, which leads to the Steinitz or the 4.Bg5 lines; [Bb4], the Winawer, pinning the knight; and [dxe4], the Rubinstein, giving up the centre for solidity. [Nc6] and [a6] are playable waiting moves, but they let White pick a good set-up in peace. An early ...c5 is worse: the centre opens while Black is behind in development, and the d5 pawn ends up isolated. [%csl Ye4,Yd5][%cal Gg8f6] }
3... Nf6 ( 3... Bb4
{ The Winawer. The bishop pins the knight, so e4 loses a defender. [e5] is the main line: after ...c5 and a3 Black takes on c3 and White gets the bishop pair and a broken queenside. [exd5] gives a quiet Exchange. White can also deal with the pin first: [a3] at once, [Bd2], [Ne2] or [Bd3], each offering the e4 pawn in its own way. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5
{ [c5] is the main move, hitting the base of the chain at once. [Ne7] keeps options open, [b6] prepares ...Ba6 to trade the light bishops, and [Qd7] lets Black meet [w:Qg4] with ...f5, since the queen then protects e6. Taking on c3 before White asks, ...Bxc3+, throws away the tempo White would spend on a3 and leaves the dark squares weak. White's plans are a3, Qg4 and Nf3, always with an eye on g7. [%csl Ye6,Yg7][%cal Gc7c5] }
( 4. exd5
{ [exd5] gives a symmetrical structure in which Black's bishop is already active on b4, and the pin on c3 makes the game easy for Black. Taking with the queen, ...Qxd5, lets [w:Nf3] and [w:Bd2] gain time on it. After [b:exd5] Black develops with ...Ne7, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 and castles, and it is White who must be careful: the knight on c3 is pinned and d4 needs watching. [%csl Yb4,Yd4][%cal Ge6d5] }
4... exd5
{ The Exchange Winawer. Black's bishop on b4 makes the game easy, and it is level. [Bd3], [Nf3] and [Bd2] are common. Black can castle short, or go long after ...Nbc6, ...Bg4 and ...Qd7 for an opposite-side fight. The king's knight goes to e7 rather than f6, so Bg5 can't pin it, and from e7 it can reach f5 or g6. While the knight on c3 is pinned, d5 is safe. [%csl Ye7,Yb4][%cal Gf1d3,Gg1f3] }
5. Bd3
{ [Ne7] and [Nc6] are the main moves: the king's knight goes to e7 so that Bg5 can't pin it, and from there to f5 or g6. Black then castles, plays ...Bf5 or ...Bg4, and may trade on c3 to leave White with doubled pawns. White's plan is [w:Ne2], [w:O-O] and Bf4 or Qh5, aiming at the kingside. The position is level and symmetrical. [%csl Yf5][%cal Gg8e7,Gb8c6] }
( 5. Nf3 ) ( 5. Bd2 ) 5... Ne7 ( 5... Nc6 ) ) ( 4. a3
{ White asks the bishop at once. [Bxc3+] is the right answer: after [w:bxc3] Black takes on e4 and the game becomes a gambit, with the bishop pair and open lines as White's compensation. Retreating with ...Ba5 or ...Be7 loses time: after ...Ba5 [w:b4] White gains space with tempo and the bishop is shut out. Black should not delay the capture on e4 either: after the trade on c3, ...Nf6 lets [w:e5] kick the knight. [%csl Ye4,Yc3][%cal Gb4c3] }
