{ The bishop keeps the pin. [b4] is the critical answer; Black should reply ...cxd4, as ...cxb4 lets the knight jump to b5 with tempo. [dxc5] and [Qg4] are calmer, and [Nf3] develops. Vaganian and other Armenian players made this line popular. Black's idea is to keep the dark bishop, the best defender of g7 and the dark squares, at the cost of time. [%csl Yb5,Yg7][%cal Gb2b4] }
6. b4
{ [cxd4] is the main move: after Nb5 Bc7 or Qg4 Ne7 the play is sharp. [cxb4] is playable too, but the knight jumps to b5 at once, with Nd6+ in the air, and White's pieces are faster. The position is very sharp in both cases: White's knight wants b5 or d4, and Black's king may have to stay in the centre. [%csl Yb5,Yd4][%cal Gc5d4] }
( 6. dxc5 ) ( 6. Qg4 ) ( 6. Nf3 ) 6... cxd4 ( 6... cxb4 ) ) ( 5... cxd4
{ Black takes on d4 before moving the bishop, so that after [axb4] ...dxc3 the knight is gone and White's queenside is broken. White should take the bishop: after [axb4] [b:dxc3] White plays [w:Nf3], [w:bxc3] or [w:Qg4] with the bishop pair and the open a-file. The line is playable for Black but less popular than ...Bxc3+, because White's dark bishop gets a free diagonal. [%csl Yc3,Yg7][%cal Ga3b4] }
6. axb4 dxc3
{ Black has an extra pawn, while White has the bishop pair, the open a-file and the dark squares around Black's king, with no black bishop left to guard them; the engine gives White a clear edge. [Nf3] develops and keeps an eye on d4; [bxc3] takes the pawn back at once; [Qg4] hits g7 while the dark bishop is gone. Black follows with ...Ne7, ...Nbc6 and ...Qc7, hitting the e5 pawn, and must watch the dark squares around the king, where White's c1 bishop now rules. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gg1f3,Gb2c3,Gd1g4] }
7. Nf3 ( 7. bxc3 ) ( 7. Qg4 ) ) 6. bxc3
{ White has the bishop pair and a big centre; Black has the better structure, with doubled c-pawns to aim at. [Ne7] is the main move: the knight defends g6 and can go to f5. [Qc7] and [Qa5] are older; [Nc6] needs ...g6 after Qg4. Closing with ...c4 too early removes the pressure on d4 and leaves White free to attack. The doubled c-pawns make White's queenside weak in endgames, so Black is happy to trade pieces. White needs open lines for the bishops. [%csl Yg6,Yd4][%cal Gg8e7] }
6... Ne7
{ The Winawer tabiya. [Qg4] attacks g7 at once and is the sharpest. [Nf3] develops quietly; [a4] prepares Ba3 for the bad c1 bishop; [h4] wants h5-h6 to weaken the dark squares. In every line Black plays against the c-pawns with ...Qa5, ...Nbc6, ...Bd7-a4 and sometimes ...c4, while White looks for a diagonal for the c1 bishop. Black's king often stays in the centre or goes long in this line. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gd1g4] }
( 6... Qc7
{ The classical move: the queen watches e5 and c5. [Qg4] hits g7 and Black answers ...f5 or ...f6; [Nf3] is calmer, and [Bd2] keeps options. Black follows with ...Ne7, ...b6 and ...Ba6 to trade the bad bishop. White's plans are Qg4 with pressure on g7, or Nf3 with a4 and Ba3 for the dark bishop. Black's king often stays in the centre or goes long. [%csl Ye5,Yc5,Yg7][%cal Gd1g4] }
7. Qg4 ( 7. Nf3 ) ( 7. Bd2 ) ) ( 6... Qa5
{ The Portisch-Hook line. The queen pins the c3 pawn and hits a3. [Bd2] is the main reply, defending the pawn and the a5-e1 diagonal; then ...Qa4 fixes White's queenside pawns, and Black follows with ...Ne7, ...Nbc6, ...b6 and ...Ba6 or ...c4. [Qd2] and [Nf3] are the other moves. The queen on a4 is safe and annoying, but Black must not forget the kingside: White's Qg4 and h4-h5 still come. [%csl Ya3][%cal Gc1d2] }
7. Bd2 ( 7. Qd2 ) ( 7. Nf3 ) ) ( 6... Nc6 ) ( 6... c4 $6
{ Locking the queenside gains space, but it gives up the pressure on d4 that makes the Winawer work, and White's kingside plans go unhindered. [Qg4] hits g7 and Black has nothing to show for it. Develop with [b:Ne7] first. [%csl Rg7,Rd4][%cal Gd1g4] }
7. Qg4 ) 7. Qg4
{ Black chooses between two worlds. [Qc7] offers the g7 and h7 pawns for a counterattack in the centre: the Poisoned Pawn. [O-O] castles into the attack but keeps the pawns. [cxd4] first is another order, and [Kf8] gives up castling to keep g7 defended. Slow moves lose time, and White wins the race on the kingside. In every line Black's play comes from ...Qc7, ...cxd4 and pressure on c3 and e5. [%csl Yg7,Yh7,Yc3][%cal Gd8c7] }
( 7. Nf3
{ The positional line. Black has time to choose: [Nbc6], [Qa5], [Qc7], [b6] with ...Ba6 to trade the light bishops, or [Bd7] with ...Ba4 to hit c2. The c-pawns are weak, but White's bishops will be strong if the position opens, so Black usually keeps it closed with ...c4 when needed. A typical plan for Black is ...Bd7, ...Qa5 and ...Ba4 to fix c2, then ...c4 to lock the queenside; White plays a4 and Ba3 or h4-h5. [%csl Yc2][%cal Gb8c6] }
7... Nbc6 ( 7... Qa5 ) ( 7... Qc7 ) ( 7... b6 ) ( 7... Bd7 ) ) ( 7. a4
{ White prepares [w:Ba3] for the dark bishop, which has no other good diagonal, and stops ...Qa4 or ...Ba4 from fixing the queenside. [Qa5], [Nbc6] and [Qc7] are the main moves; [b6] with ...Ba6 trades the light bishops. After ...Qa5 [w:Bd2] Black adds ...Nbc6 and ...Bd7 against c3 and d4, and White answers with Nf3, Bd3 and O-O, keeping the king safe and the bishop pair. [%csl Yc3,Yd4][%cal Gd8a5] }
7... Qa5 ( 7... Nbc6 ) ( 7... Qc7 ) ( 7... b6 ) ) ( 7. h4
{ The h-pawn wants to reach h6 and weaken g7. Black must react: [Qc7], [Nbc6] or [Qa5], with ...Qa4 a good blockading idea. Castling short is risky while the pawn is coming. If h6 arrives, ...gxh6 or ...g6 and White's bishop should look for g5. White's rook may join by Rh3, and the bishop goes to g5 or d2. Black should not castle short before the h-pawn is blocked. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gd8c7] }
7... Qc7 ( 7... Nbc6 ) ( 7... Qa5 ) ( 7... h5 ) ) ( 7. Bd3
{ [Nbc6] and [Qa5] are the main replies; [Qc7] and [Bd7] are also played. Black's trick to know comes after ...Nbc6: if White plays Ne2, ...c4 traps the bishop on d3, because the knight blocks its retreat. White's bishop aims at h7, so Black keeps the king flexible: ...Qa5, ...Bd7 and ...O-O-O are common, and ...c4 is played once White's bishop has no safe retreat. [%csl Yd3,Yh7][%cal Gb8c6,Gd8a5] }
7... Nbc6
{ [Nf3] and [Qg4] are fine, and [Be3] protects d4. [w:Ne2] is a common blunder: ...c4 attacks the bishop on d3, and with e2 taken it has no way back. White gets a pawn at most for the piece. After [w:Nf3] Black plays ...Qa5 and ...Bd7, or ...c4 at the right moment to gain space with tempo on the bishop. [%csl Yd3][%cal Gg1f3,Gd1g4] }
( 7... Qa5 ) ( 7... Qc7
{ The queen covers c7 and hits e5, and keeps ...Qa5 and ...c4 ideas in reserve. [Nf3] develops; [Qg4] leads to Poisoned Pawn positions. Here too [w:Ne2] is a blunder: ...c4 hits the bishop on d3, and with e2 taken it has no way back. A bishop with one retreat square can be trapped by a single pawn move, so always look for ...c4 when White's bishop stands on d3 in the Winawer. [%csl Yd3,Ye5][%cal Gg1f3,Gd1g4] }
8. Nf3 ( 8. Qg4 ) ( 8. Ne2 $4
{ The knight comes to its natural Winawer square, but it shuts in the bishop: [c4] attacks it, and after [w:h4] [b:cxd3] Black is a piece up. Play [w:Nf3]. [%csl Rd3,Re2][%cal Gc5c4] }
8... c4 ) ) ( 7... Bd7 ) 8. Nf3 ( 8. Qg4 ) ( 8. Be3 ) ( 8. Ne2 $4
{ The knight goes to its usual Winawer square, but it blocks the bishop's retreat. [c4] hits the bishop on d3, and it has no safe square: after [w:h4] [b:cxd3] Black is a piece up. Play [w:Nf3]. It is easy to miss because the bishop on d3 looks safe; but a bishop with only one retreat square can be trapped by a pawn. [%csl Rd3,Re2][%cal Gc5c4] }
8... c4 ) ) 7... Qc7
{ [Qxg7] takes the offer; after ...Rg8 Qxh7 Black takes on d4 and attacks c3 and e5. [Bd3] keeps the pawn chain and attacks h7 instead. In the Poisoned Pawn White's king often stays in the centre and Black's on the queenside, and the side whose pieces come out first wins. After [w:Bd3] Black plays ...cxd4 or ...c4, and White's queen keeps up the pressure on g7. [%csl Yd4,Yc3,Ye5][%cal Gg4g7] }
( 7... O-O
{ Black castles into the attack, but the king is not easily reached. [Bd3] hits h7, and [Nf3] with Bd3 is the main line. Black's plan is ...Nbc6 and ...f5 to shut the b1-h7 diagonal, and later ...c4 or ...Qa5. Moves like ...h6 or ...g6 give White a target: Bxh6 or h4-h5. Black keeps the queen near the kingside, and the knight on e7 can come to f5. [%csl Yh7,Yf5][%cal Gf1d3] }
8. Bd3 ( 8. Nf3 Nbc6 9. Bd3
{ [f5] is the right move: it blocks the bishop's diagonal and, after exf6, the rook takes on f6 with play on the f-file. The tempting ...c4?? releases the bishop: Bxh7+ and Ng5+ give a mating attack. ...h6? is met by Bxh6, and ...g6 by h4-h5. With the f-file open, Black's rook can come to f7 or f6 to defend. [%cal Gf7f5] }
9... f5 ( 9... c4 $4
{ Gaining space with tempo on the bishop looks good, but it is the signal for the Greek gift: [Bxh7+] Kxh7 [w:Ng5+] and the queen joins on h4 or h5. Black's king has no defenders. Block the diagonal with [b:f5]. [%cal Gd3h7] }
10. Bxh7+ ) ) ( 8. h4 ) ) ( 7... cxd4 8. Qxg7 Rg8 9. Qxh7
{ [Qc7] is the right follow-up: it hits e5 and c3 and transposes to the main Poisoned Pawn. ...Qa5 is the slip: the rook comes to b1 with tempo and the queen runs short of squares. [Nbc6] is also good, keeping ...Qc7 for the next move. In every line Black's king heads for the queenside after ...Bd7 and ...O-O-O, while White pushes the h-pawn. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gd8c7,Gb8c6] }
9... Qc7 ( 9... Nbc6 ) ( 9... Qa5 $2
{ The queen eyes c3 and the a5-e1 diagonal, but [Rb1] activates the rook on the b-file at once, and after ...dxc3 [w:Nf3] and [w:h4] White's passed h-pawn and active pieces count for more than the pawns. Play [b:Qc7]. [%csl Ra5,Rc3][%cal Ga1b1] }
10. Rb1 ) ) ( 7... Kf8
{ Black gives up castling to keep g7 defended. White's plans are [h4]-h5 to gain space, [Nf3] and [a4] with Ba3 to use the bishops. Black plays ...Qa5, ...Nbc6 and ...b6 with ...Ba6, and the king often goes to g8 after ...h5. Black's king is safe on f8 or g8 while the centre is closed, but the rook on h8 needs ...h5 and ...Rh6 or a later ...Kg8 to come into play. [%csl Yg7,Yg8][%cal Gh2h4] }
8. h4 ( 8. Nf3 ) ( 8. a4 ) ( 8. Bd3 ) ) 8. Qxg7
{ [Rg8] is forced: the rook on h8 is attacked, and from g8 it hits the queen. After [w:Qxh7] [b:cxd4] Black's play against c3 and e5 begins: this is the main Poisoned Pawn. Black's compensation for the two pawns is the open g-file, active pieces and White's weak queenside; White's trump is the passed h-pawn. Black's king usually walks to the queenside after ...Bd7 and ...O-O-O, and the rook on g8 presses g2. [%csl Yc3,Ye5,Yg2][%cal Gh8g8] }
( 8. Bd3 ) ( 8. Bd2 ) 8... Rg8 9. Qxh7
{ [cxd4] is the move: the centre opens and Black's queen hits c3 and e5. The knight on e7 and the rook on g8 also join the attack. Slower moves like ...Nbc6 let White play [w:Nf3] and [w:Bf4] with two extra pawns and a solid centre. Black's queen and rook are active, and White's king will feel the open c-file. White's h-pawn is passed, so the endgame is often White's goal. [%csl Yc3,Ye5][%cal Gc5d4] }
9... cxd4
{ The Poisoned Pawn. [Ne2] is the main move: it defends c3 and covers d4. [Qd3] and [Kd1] are also played. The natural recapture [w:cxd4] is the trap: ...Qc3+ forks the king and the rook on a1. With the queen on c7 and the rook on g8, Black has real threats, and White must keep every piece protected. In all lines Black's king goes to the queenside after ...Bd7 and ...O-O-O. [%csl Yc3,Yd4,Ya1][%cal Gg1e2] }
10. Ne2
{ [Nbc6] develops and attacks e5; [dxc3] takes a pawn back at once. Grabbing the e5 pawn with the queen is a mistake: White's dark bishop comes out with tempo. Black usually follows with ...Bd7 and ...O-O-O, and White plays f4 to hold e5. White's plan is f4, Qd3 and Nxd4 or cxd4 at the right moment; Black's is ...Bd7, ...O-O-O and ...Nf5 against d4. [%csl Ye5,Yd4][%cal Gb8c6] }
( 10. Qd3 ) ( 10. Kd1
{ The king steps aside to defend c2 and d2. [dxc3] and [Nbc6] are the right answers. Taking on e5 with the queen lets [w:Nf3] develop with tempo, and Black's queen is hit again by Bf4 or Qh6. The king on d1 is safe for now, but it blocks the queen's rook. Black's quick ...Bd7 and ...O-O-O is the plan. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gd4c3,Gb8c6] }
10... dxc3 ( 10... Nbc6 ) ( 10... Qxe5 $2
{ A third pawn, but the queen is chased at once: [Nf3] and after ...Qf6 [w:cxd4] White's pieces come out with gain of time. Play [b:dxc3]. Black already has two pawns' worth of play; the third pawn is not worth the time it costs. [%csl Re5,Rd4][%cal Gg1f3] }
11. Nf3 ) ) ( 10. cxd4 $4
{ Taking back the pawn is natural, but the c-file is now open: [Qc3+] forks the king and the rook on a1, and after [w:Bd2] [b:Qxa1+] Black is a rook up. Play [w:Ne2], which keeps c3 defended. [%csl Ra1,Rc3][%cal Gc7c3] }
10... Qc3+ ) ( 10. Bd2 $6
{ The bishop defends c3, but the e5 pawn falls with check: [Qxe5+] and after [w:Ne2] [b:dxc3] Black's pieces are very active. Play [w:Ne2] first. The bishop does guard c3, but it leaves e5 hanging with check, and Black's pieces flood into the centre. [%csl Rc3,Re5][%cal Gc7e5] }
10... Qxe5+ ) 10... Nbc6
{ [f4] holds e5 and is the main move; then ...Bd7 and ...dxc3 or ...O-O-O. White's king stays on e1 or goes to d1, and Black's king castles long. The position is complex and level. The rook on g8 presses g2, and Black's knights aim at f5 and e5. White's passed h-pawn is the long-term trump, and in many endgames it decides. [%csl Ye5,Yg2,Yf5][%cal Gf2f4] }
( 10... dxc3 ) ( 10... Qxe5 $2
{ The pawn looks free, but it opens lines for White: after [cxd4] the queen is hit by Bf4 or dxe5 ideas, and White's bishops come out with tempo. Play [b:Nbc6] or [b:dxc3]. [%csl Re5,Rf4][%cal Gc3d4] }
11. cxd4 ) 11. f4 ( 11. cxd4 ) ) ( 3... dxe4
{ The Rubinstein. Black gives up the centre at once: the c8 bishop can now come out via d7 or b7, and the position is very solid, if a little passive. [Nxe4] is the only good move. The gambit [w:f3], in the style of the Blackmar-Diemer, gives Black an extra pawn and little to fear after ...exf3 and ...Nf6. After Nxe4 Black's plans are ...Nd7 and ...Ngf6, or ...Bd7-c6, and always the ...c5 break. [%cal Gc3e4] }
4. Nxe4
{ The Rubinstein tabiya. [Nd7], the Blackburne, prepares ...Ngf6 so that Black can take back on f6 with a knight. [Bd7], the Fort Knox, heads for c6 to trade the bad bishop. [Nf6] invites Nxf6+ and a choice of recaptures, and [Be7] waits. Early adventures like ...e5 or ...Qd5 lose time: White's development is already ahead. In every line the key break is ...c5, which Black can play in one move. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gb8d7] }
( 4. f3 $6
{ The gambit opens the f-file for White, but here Black's pieces are ready: after [exf3] [w:Nxf3] Black develops with ...Nf6, ...Be7 and ...c5, a pawn up with no weaknesses. Take back with [w:Nxe4]. [%cal Ge4f3] }
4... exf3 ) 4... Nd7
{ [Nf3] is the main move: then ...Ngf6 and the knights are traded on f6. [Bd3] and [c3] are also normal. Black's plan is ...Ngf6, ...Be7 or ...c5, and the bishop on c8 goes to b7 after ...b6. The pawn on e6 stops White's d4-d5 break. The knight on d7 will take back on f6 after Nxf6+, so Black's pawns stay healthy. The same position arises from 3.Nd2 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Nd7. [%csl Ye6][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 4... Bd7
{ The Fort Knox. The bishop goes to c6, where it hits e4 and later f3 and g2, and it is often traded for a knight. [Nf3] and [Bd3] are the main moves. Black solves the bad-bishop problem at once, but the position stays a little passive. After ...Bc6 the bishop is often traded on f3 or e4; then ...Nd7, ...Ngf6, ...Be7 and ...O-O give Black a sound Caro-Kann-like position. [%csl Ye4,Yf3,Yg2][%cal Gg1f3] }
5. Nf3
{ [Bc6] is the point of the Fort Knox: the bishop hits e4, and Black is ready to trade it for a knight on f3 or e4, solving the bad-bishop problem. White answers [w:Bd3], [w:Ng3] or [w:Nc3]. Black follows with ...Nd7, ...Ngf6 and ...Be7, a solid set-up whose only drawback is a slight lack of space. The bishop on c6 also eyes g2 once White's knights move. [%csl Ye4,Yf3,Yg2][%cal Gd7c6] }
( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. c3 ) 5... Bc6
{ [Bd3] defends the knight and is the main line. [Ng3] keeps the knight out of the trade. After [w:Bd3] [b:Nd7] Black covers e5 so that Ne5 does not hit the bishop, then ...Ngf6. The bishop on c6 hits e4; if White's knight moves, the bishop also eyes f3 and g2. Black can take on e4 or f3 whenever it suits. [%csl Ye5,Ye4,Yf3][%cal Gf1d3] }
6. Bd3
{ [Nd7] is the best move order: it covers e5 and keeps the f6 square for the other knight. [Bxe4] trades at once and gives a Caro-Kann structure a tempo down. The direct ...Nf6 lets Nxf6+ Qxf6 Bg5, when the queen is harassed. After ...Nd7 White castles and Black plays ...Ngf6; the knights then decide the fate of the bishop on c6. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gb8d7] }
( 6. Ng3 ) ( 6. Nc3 ) 6... Nd7 ( 6... Bxe4 ) ( 6... Nf6 $6
{ Natural development, but after [Nxf6+] Black must take with the queen or the g-pawn. After ...Qxf6 [w:Bg5] the queen is chased again, and with the knight still on b8 the b7 pawn and the rook on a8 are loose. Play [b:Nd7] first, so the other knight can take back on f6. [%csl Rb8,Rb7][%cal Ge4f6] }
7. Nxf6+ ) 7. O-O Ngf6
{ [Ng3] is the engine's choice: it avoids the trade on f6. [Ned2] and [Re1] are good too. [Nxf6+] lets the queen recapture with threats on d4 and f3: after ...Qxf6 the natural Bg5? runs into ...Bxf3. [Bg5] first keeps the option. After ...Be7 and ...O-O Black can trade on f3 or e4 to solve the bad bishop for good and play ...c5 or ...c6. White keeps a small space edge. [%csl Yd4,Yf3][%cal Ge4g3] }
8. Ng3 ( 8. Ned2 ) ( 8. Re1 ) ( 8. Bg5 ) ( 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6
{ The queen hits d4 and lines up with the bishop on f3. [c3] or [Be2] defend calmly. The trap is Bg5?: ...Bxf3! and after Bxf6 Bxd1 Black wins a piece; if the queen takes back, ...Qxd4 wins a pawn. Black's next moves are ...Bd6, ...O-O and ...e5 or ...c5; the queen on f6 is active as long as it cannot be hit with tempo. [%csl Yd4,Yf3][%cal Gc2c3] }
9. c3 ( 9. Be2 ) ( 9. Re1 ) ( 9. Bg5 $2
{ The bishop hits the queen, but [Bxf3] comes with a counter-threat: if [w:Bxf6] then ...Bxd1 and Black is a piece up; if [w:Qxf3] [b:Qxf3] [w:gxf3] White's structure is ruined. Defend with [w:c3]. [%cal Gc6f3] }
9... Bxf3 ) ) ) ( 4... Nf6
{ Black challenges the knight at once. [Nxf6+] is the main move; then ...Qxf6 keeps the pawns but brings the queen out early, and ...gxf6 is the dynamic choice with the g-file and the bishop pair. [Bd3] keeps the knights on. Compared with ...Nd7 first, this order gives up the option of taking back on f6 with a knight, so Black's queen or structure must make a concession. [%cal Ge4f6] }
5. Nxf6+
{ [Qxf6] keeps the structure but exposes the queen: after Nf3 Black plays ...h6 to stop Bg5. [gxf6] gives Black a half-open g-file, ...b6, ...Bb7, ...Qd7 and ...O-O-O: a sharp set-up. With the queen on f6 Black should not let it be chased: ...h6 stops Bg5, and ...Bd6, ...O-O and ...b6 follow. With ...gxf6 the king usually stays in the centre or goes long. [%cal Gd8f6] }
( 5. Bd3 ) 5... Qxf6 ( 5... gxf6 ) ) ( 4... Be7
{ A flexible move: Black waits to see where White's knights go before choosing ...Nd7 or ...Nf6. [Nf3] and [Bd3] are the main replies, and [c3] is solid. The bishop is ready to take back on f6 if a knight is traded there, so Black can play ...Nf6 next without fearing a broken structure. Black usually follows with ...Nf6 and ...O-O, then ...b6 and ...Bb7 or ...c5. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1d3] }
5. Nf3 ( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. c3 ) ) ( 4... e5 $2
{ The Ellis gambit hits the centre at once, but Black is not developed: [Nf3] and after ...exd4 [w:Qxd4] the queen centralises with tempo while Black's king is still in the middle. The capture [w:dxe5] is weaker because of ...Qxd1+. Develop with [b:Nd7] or [b:Bd7] first. [%cal Gg1f3] }
5. Nf3 ) 5. Nf3
{ [Ngf6] challenges the e4 knight; after Nxf6+ Black recaptures with the d7 knight and keeps the pawns healthy. [Be7] first is also played. The point of the Blackburne order is to avoid taking back on f6 with the queen or a pawn. White's bishop goes to d3 next, and Black's knight takes on e4 with tempo if White allows it. [%cal Gg8f6] }
( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. c3 ) 5... Ngf6
{ White decides about the knight on e4. [Nxf6+] trades and is the main line; [Bd3] lets ...Nxe4 Bxe4 Nf6 gain a tempo; [Bg5] and [Ng3] keep pieces on. The jump [w:Neg5] looks aggressive but the knight has nothing to do on g5. After [w:Nxf6+] [b:Nxf6] Black plays ...c5 or ...Be7 and ...O-O; the position is solid and roughly equal. [%cal Ge4f6] }
( 5... Be7 ) 6. Nxf6+
{ Black takes back with the knight, [Nxf6], the point of playing ...Nd7 first: the pawns stay healthy and the queen stays at home. Then ...c5, ...Be7 and ...O-O, or ...b6 and ...Bb7, give a solid position in which Black's task is to free the c8 bishop. Taking with the queen brings it out early, and ...gxf6 breaks the structure for little. [%csl Yc5,Yb7][%cal Gd7f6] }
( 6. Bd3 ) ( 6. Bg5 ) ( 6. Ng3 ) ( 6. Neg5 $2
{ The knight eyes e6 and f7, but [h6] drives it away. The sacrifice [w:Nxe6] gives too little for the piece once Black answers ...Qe7, and after a retreat to h3 White has lost time. Black then plays ...c5 with a good game. Trade with [w:Nxf6+] or play [w:Bd3]. [%csl Rg5,Re6][%cal Gh7h6] }
6... h6 ) 6... Nxf6
{ White's set-ups are many: [Bd3] and Qe2, [c3] with Bd3, [Bg5], [Ne5] or [Be3]. Black's plan is always the same: ...c5 in one move, which the Caro-Kann cannot do, then ...Be7, ...O-O and ...b6 with ...Bb7. A check on the a4-e8 diagonal is a typical White idea while Black's king is still on e8, so Black often castles before ...c5 opens the diagonal. White keeps a small space edge, no more. [%cal Gf1d3] }
7. Bd3
{ [c5] is the key move: it fights for the centre and frees the dark bishop. After dxc5 Bxc5 Black is fully developed. [Be7] first is also fine. Black later plays ...b6 and ...Bb7, and if White plays Bg5 and Bxf6, ...gxf6 gives Black the g-file. White wants Qe2, Bg5 and O-O-O in some lines; Black's quick ...c5 and ...O-O make such an attack less dangerous. [%cal Gc7c5] }
( 7. c3
{ [c5] again, or [Be7] first. After [w:Be3] White threatens dxc5, and Black can answer ...Nd5 hitting the bishop. Black should not allow a structure where White's d4 pawn is fixed and Black's pieces are passive. With c3 White plays slowly: Bd3, O-O and Qe2 or Re1. Black's ...c5 trades the d4 pawn off and gives Black free development. [%cal Gc7c5] }
7... c5 ( 7... Be7 ) 8. Be3 Nd5
{ The knight hits the bishop. [Ne5] is the smart reply: it prepares Bb5+ or Qa4+ where ...Bd7 is no longer possible. [Bd2] keeps the bishop. Taking on c5 with [w:dxc5] lets ...Nxe3, and after fxe3 Bxc5 Black has the bishop pair. If White keeps the bishop, Black plays ...Be7, ...O-O and ...b6 with a good game. [%cal Gf3e5] }
9. Ne5 ( 9. Bd2 ) ( 9. Bb5+ ) ) ( 7. Bg5 Be7 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Qe2
{ [c5] is the normal move. The trap is ...b6??: Bxf6 Bxf6 and Qe4 attacks h7 and the rook on a8 at once. Black should develop the bishop first with ...c5 and ...b6 only when the long diagonal is covered. A check on b5 or a4 is another White idea while Black's bishop is still on c8. [%csl Yh7,Ya8][%cal Gc7c5] }
9... c5 ( 9... h6 ) ( 9... b6 $4
{ It prepares ...Bb7, but the long diagonal is still open: [Bxf6 Bxf6 Qe4] threatens mate on h7 and the rook on a8 at once. Black loses the exchange. Play [b:c5] first. The fianchetto is the right plan in the Rubinstein, but it must come after the long diagonal is safe from a queen on e4. [%csl Rh7,Ra8][%cal Gg5f6] }
10. Bxf6 ) ) ( 7. Ne5 ) ( 7. Be3 ) 7... c5
{ [dxc5] and [O-O] are the main moves. After [w:dxc5] [b:Bxc5] Black's pieces are active, and the queens are often traded. White keeps a small edge in endgames thanks to the queenside majority. [Qe2] with Bg5 and O-O-O is a sharper plan. In the endgame White's queenside majority can matter, so Black should keep pieces on and aim for activity: ...b6, ...Bb7 and ...Rd8. [%cal Gd4c5] }
( 7... Be7 ) 8. dxc5 ( 8. O-O ) ( 8. Qe2 ) ) ( 3... Nc6
{ A rare set-up: the knight blocks the c-pawn, so Black gives up the usual ...c5 break and plans ...Nf6 with ...e5 or ...f6 instead. White's simplest answers are [Nf3], keeping a broad centre, and [e5], which takes space and leaves the knight on c6 without a target: Black then has to rely on ...f6. [exd5] is harmless, and [Bb5] pins the knight. Black's position is solid but passive, and White keeps an easy edge. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gg1f3,Ge4e5] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. e5 ) ( 4. exd5 ) ) ( 3... c5 $2
{ Hitting the centre at once is the French idea, but here it opens lines before Black is ready. After [exd5 exd5] White plays [w:dxc5] or [w:Bb5+], and the d5 pawn is isolated and hit by the knight on c3. White must take on d5 first: [w:dxc5], [w:Be3] or [w:e5] at once let Black off. Play [b:Nf6], [b:Bb4] or [b:dxe4]. [%csl Rd5][%cal Ge4d5] }
4. exd5 exd5
{ Black's 3...c5 has led to an open position with an isolated pawn on d5. [dxc5] opens the centre and attacks d5 again, and [Bb5+] disrupts Black's development first. Black is behind in development, and the d5 pawn needs defending by pieces: ...Nf6, ...Nc6 and ...Be7, then castling. White's plan is to blockade d4 with a knight and pile up on d5. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd4c5,Gf1b5] }
5. dxc5 ( 5. Bb5+ ) ) ( 3... a6
{ A waiting move that stops Nb5 and Bb5, squares that matter in many French lines. [Nf3] and [e5] are good: after [w:e5] [b:c5] Black has an Advance-type position where ...a6 lets ...b5 follow. [exd5] gives a quiet Exchange. The cost is time: White develops freely and keeps a small edge, and Black still has to choose between ...Nf6, ...dxe4 and ...c5 next move. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gg1f3,Ge4e5] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. e5 ) ( 4. exd5 ) 4... Nf6 5. e5 Nfd7
{ Reached via an early [a6] or from the Steinitz, Black has kept b5 covered before striking at the pawn chain. That stops [w:Nb5] jumps once the queen goes to b6, while [b:c5] and [b:b5] wait in reserve. [Ne2] is the classical Steinitz regrouping: White vacates c3 so [w:c3] can bolster d4 against [b:c5]. [Be3] develops naturally and reinforces the base of the chain before [b:c5] lands, whereas [Bg5] provokes [b:Be7] or [b:Qb6], hitting b2 as soon as the bishop leaves c1. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gc3e2,Gc1e3,Gc7c5] }
6. Ne2 ( 6. Be3 ) ( 6. Bg5 ) ) 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e5
{ The knight must move. [Nfd7] is the main line: it guards e5 and supports ...c5, and White now trades the dark bishops with Bxe7 or offers the bishop with h4. [Ne4] is the Tartakower line: the knight trades itself on c3 or e4, a simpler but more passive way for Black. ...Ng8 loses time. After ...Nfd7, Black's next move in nearly every line is ...c5, often prepared by ...a6. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gf6d7] }
5... Nfd7
{ White has two main moves. [Bxe7] trades the dark bishops: Black's bad light bishop stays, but White has lost the piece that guarded the dark squares. [h4] is the Alekhine-Chatard Attack: White offers the g5 bishop and a pawn to open the h-file. Both are good. The bishops face each other, so White must decide now whether to trade or to gambit. In every line Black's next break is ...c5, often prepared with ...a6 against a knight jump to b5. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gg5e7] }
( 5... Ne4
{ The Tartakower Variation. Instead of retreating to d7, Black jumps into e4 to trade off two pairs of minor pieces and ease the cramp behind the e5 wedge. Both captures lead to the same structure: after [Bxe7 Qxe7 Nxe4 dxe4] or [Nxe4 dxe4 Bxe7 Qxe7], Black's advanced e4 pawn is overextended and needs careful defence with [b:Nd7] or [b:b6] and [b:Bb7]. White hits e4 at once with [w:Qe2] while preparing [w:O-O-O] and [w:g3], or plays [w:c3] and [w:Ne2] towards g3, enjoying more space while Black counts on the reduced material. [%csl Ye4,Ye5][%cal Gg5e7,Gc3e4] }
6. Bxe7 ( 6. Nxe4 ) ) 6. Bxe7
{ Black recaptures. [Qxe7] is the main move: the queen guards d6 against a knight jump to b5, and c7 too once the d7 knight moves, and the king is free to castle short. [Kxe7] is played in some lines to keep the queen flexible, but the king stays in the centre. After ...Qxe7 White picks a plan: [w:f4] for the big centre, [w:Qd2] with long castling, or [w:Nb5] at once. [%csl Yd6,Yc7][%cal Gd8e7] }
( 6. h4
{ The Alekhine-Chatard Attack. White offers the bishop on g5 and the h-pawn: after ...Bxg5 hxg5 Qxg5 the h-file opens. Black can accept with [Bxg5], or decline with [h6] or [c5]. Slow moves let White's pieces join in: after ...a6 the queen comes to g4, and ...O-O is playable but puts the king where the h-file will open. Black's king rarely castles short in this line. [%cal Ge7g5] }
6... Bxg5 ( 6... h6 ) ( 6... c5
{ [Bxe7] is right: after the trade White's knight comes to b5 with threats. The immediate Nb5? is a mistake because the bishop on g5 is still hanging and Black can take it or play ...f6 with a good game. After [w:Bxe7] Black should take back with the king or the queen and then play ...a6 or ...Nc6, so that Nb5 has no targets. The centre stays tense. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gg5e7] }
7. Bxe7 ( 7. Nb5 $2
{ The knight jump is White's main idea in this structure, but here the bishop on g5 has not been traded: [f6] hits it and the e5 pawn at once, and Black opens the centre with a big lead. Trade first with [w:Bxe7]. [%csl Rg5,Re5][%cal Gf7f6] }
7... f6 ) ( 7. Nf3 $6
