7. Qg4 ) 7. hxg5
{ Black takes the pawn back: [Qxg5] leaves Black a pawn up, and White's compensation is the open h-file and quick development. After [w:Nh3] the queen goes back to e7, and Black should keep the king in the centre or castle long, because the h-file is open towards h7. ...c5 and ...Nc6 come next. Not taking at once lets White keep the g5 pawn as an attacking wedge. [%csl Ye7,Yh7][%cal Gd8g5] }
7... Qxg5
{ White is a pawn down but has the h-file and quick development. [Nh3] is the main move: it hits the queen and goes to f4. [Qd3] and [Nf3] are also played. Black's king cannot castle short, so it often stays in the centre or goes long. Engines give Black an edge, but in practice White's attack is dangerous. Black's best set-up is ...Qe7, ...a6 and ...c5, with the king on d8 or c7 later. [%cal Gg1h3] }
8. Nh3 ( 8. Qd3 ) ( 8. Nf3 ) ) 6... Qxe7
{ The old main line. [f4] builds the big centre; [Qd2], [Nb5], [Qg4] and [Nf3] are the other tries. Black plans ...a6 and ...c5, or ...O-O and ...c5, with ...Nc6 against d4. A knight on b5 is White's most annoying piece: it eyes c7 and d6, so Black often plays ...a6 first or keeps the queen on e7 to cover them. Black's king usually castles short, but in some lines it stays in the centre. [%csl Yd4,Yb5,Yc7][%cal Gf2f4] }
7. f4
{ [a6] first, to stop Nb5, and [O-O] are the safe moves. ...c5 at once is the classic error: [w:Nb5] hits c7 and d6, and Black's king has no safe place. Once b5 is covered, Black plays ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...b5-b4 against d4. With the d4-e5-f4 chain, White's light bishop is good and Black's is bad, so Black looks for ...b6 and ...Ba6, or the long trip ...Bd7-e8-h5, to trade it or bring it out. [%csl Yc7,Yd6,Yb5][%cal Ga7a6] }
( 7. Qd2
{ Rubinstein's move: White keeps f4 for later and may castle long. [a6] or [O-O] are the right replies; ...c5 too early allows [w:Nb5] with Nd6+ or Nc7+ ideas. Black then plays ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...b5 with a fast queenside attack. White's king usually goes long, so the race starts early: White plays f4, Nf3 and sometimes g4, while Black's pawns head for White's king. [%cal Ga7a6] }
7... a6 ( 7... O-O ) ( 7... c5 $2
{ It strikes at d4, but [Nb5] hits c7 and d6 with Nd6+ to come, and the king cannot castle safely. Play [b:a6] first. With the queen on d2 White is ready to meet ...c5 with Nb5 and Nd6+ or Nc7+, because the black king is still on e8. [%csl Rc7,Rd6][%cal Gc3b5] }
8. Nb5 ) ) ( 7. Nb5
{ The knight threatens Nc7+, forking the king and the rook on a8. [Nb6] moves the knight off d7, so the queen on e7 guards c7 along the rank; [Qd8] guards it directly. The obvious ...a6 loses at once to Nxc7+ and Nxa8, and ...Qb4+ only wastes time. White's knight is strong on b5 but can be chased by ...a6 later, once c7 is safe; then it goes back to c3, or to a3 and c2. Black's plan is ...a6, ...c5 and ...Nc6. [%csl Yc7][%cal Gd7b6] }
7... Nb6 ( 7... Qd8 ) ( 7... a6 $4
{ Black wants to chase the knight, but it takes c7 with check: [Nxc7+] and then the rook on a8 falls. Defend with [b:Nb6] or [b:Qd8]. Chasing the knight is natural, but the knight is not attacked yet: it takes first. [%csl Re8,Ra8][%cal Gb5c7] }
8. Nxc7+ ) ( 7... Qb4+ $2
{ The check looks active, but after [c3] the queen has one square: ...Qa5. On e7 it would still be blocked by the d7 knight, so Nxc7+ wins, and on a4 it is taken. Then b4 kicks it again and White's queenside space grows. Play [b:Nb6], which lets the queen guard c7. [%csl Rb4,Rc7][%cal Gc2c3] }
8. c3 ) ) ( 7. Qg4
{ The queen hits g7. [O-O] is the calm answer: the king is safe enough and Black plays ...c5 next. Weakening moves are worse: ...g6 and ...f5 leave holes on the dark squares, and ...Kf8 loses the right to castle. ...a6 is too slow. After [b:O-O] White plays Nf3, Bd3 and O-O-O, and Black answers ...c5 and ...f5 or ...f6. [%csl Yg7,Yc5][%cal Ge8g8] }
7... O-O ( 7... g6 $6
{ It protects g7 but weakens f6 and h6, and the knight on d7 cannot use f6. [Nf3], Bd3 and h4-h5 give White a lasting attack. Castle with [b:O-O]. Black's dark squares are already weak after the bishop trade, and ...g6 makes them worse. [%csl Rf6,Rh6][%cal Gg1f3] }
8. Nf3 ) ( 7... f5 $6
{ It shuts out the queen, but it kills Black's ...f6 break and weakens g6 and e6. [Qh5+] pushes Black into ...g6, and then [w:Qh6] sits on the dark squares and keeps the king from castling. Castle with [b:O-O]. [%csl Rg6,Re6][%cal Gg4h5] }
8. Qh5+ ) ) ( 7. Nf3 ) 7... a6 ( 7... O-O 8. Nf3 c5
{ [Bd3] aims at h7, and after it Black should not take on d4: ...cxd4 allows the Greek gift Bxh7+ and Ng5+, with a dangerous attack. [Nb5] heads for d6 and c7, [dxc5] gives the knight c5, and [Qd2] prepares long castling. Black meets Bd3 with ...f5 or ...f6, or with ...Nc6 keeping the tension. White's knight on f3 supports e5 and is ready for Ng5, so Black's knight on d7 often goes to f6 after ...f6 to cover h7. [%csl Yh7,Yd6,Yc7][%cal Gf1d3] }
9. Bd3
{ The Greek gift is in the air. Black should block the b1-h7 diagonal or hit the centre with [f5] or [f6]. Taking on d4 with ...cxd4? opens the way: Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ and the queen joins on h5, and Black's extra piece does not help. With ...f5 Black also stops f4-f5, White's main attacking lever, and ...Nc6 can follow once the diagonal is shut. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gf7f5] }
( 9. Nb5 ) ( 9. dxc5 ) ( 9. Qd2 ) 9... f5 ( 9... f6 ) ( 9... cxd4 $6
