8. Nb5 ) ) ( 3. Nd2 Nf6
{ The closed Tarrasch. [e5] is the main move; after ...Nfd7 Bd3 c5 c3 Nc6 White plays Ne2 so that both knights can support d4. [Bd3] keeps the centre and allows ...c5. After [w:e5] [b:Nfd7] the knight on d7 supports ...c5 and ...f6. If White does not play e5, Black can choose ...dxe4 or ...c5 with an open centre. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5
{ The knight must move. [Nfd7] is the right square: from d7 it supports both French breaks, ...c5 and ...f6. [Ne4] looks active, but after [w:Nxe4] [b:dxe4] the pawn on e4 is weak and Black's centre is gone. ...Ng8 loses time. After ...Nfd7 White chooses between [w:Bd3], the main line, [w:f4] and [w:c3], and Black answers ...c5 in every case. [%csl Yd7,Yc5][%cal Gf6d7] }
( 4. Bd3 ) 4... Nfd7
{ [Bd3] is the main line, aiming at h7. [f4] builds a big centre; [Ngf3] and [c3] are also played. Black answers with ...c5 in every line. The knight on d7 supports ...c5 and ...f6, the two breaks. White's own plans are a kingside attack with Bd3, Ne2-f4 and Qh5 or Qg4, and the queenside expansion a3-b4. [%csl Yh7][%cal Gf1d3] }
5. Bd3
{ [c5] is the only good move. ...Nc6 gives White time for c3, and then Black has no way to attack d4. ...f6? is met by Qh5+ and Bxg6+. With the bishop on d3 and no knight on f6, Black's kingside is sensitive: the h7 pawn is the target of Qh5 and Bxh7+ ideas later. So Black must create counterplay fast, and ...c5 does it. [%csl Yd4,Yd3][%cal Gc7c5] }
( 5. f4
{ White builds the big centre with d4, e5 and f4, as in the Steinitz, but the knight on d2 does less than one on c3. [c5] hits the base at once, and after [w:c3] [b:Nc6] [w:Ndf3] Black adds ...Qb6 and ...cxd4, or plays ...f6 against the head of the chain. White's pieces come out slowly: Ndf3, Ne2 and Bd3, so Black has time. Black often plays ...f6 to trade off the e5 pawn and open the f-file. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gc7c5] }
5... c5 ) ( 5. Ngf3 ) ( 5. c3 ) 5... c5
{ [c3] is the move: it keeps d4 solid, so ...cxd4 can be met by cxd4 and the chain stays. Other moves give something up: [w:dxc5] hands Black's dark bishop a free diagonal, and the d2 knight can't defend d4 once it moves. After [w:c3] Black adds ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 against d4, and White brings the g1 knight to e2, so that the d2 knight can go to f3 and both knights hold the centre. [%csl Yd4,Ye2][%cal Gc2c3] }
( 5... f6 $2
{ The break at the head of the chain is too early: [Qh5+] and after ...g6 [w:Bxg6+] the bishop sacrifice opens Black's king. Play [b:c5]. The ...f6 break is a key French idea, but it must wait until the kingside is safe: here the e8-h5 diagonal is open. [%cal Gd1h5] }
6. Qh5+ ) 6. c3
{ [Nc6] brings a third attacker against d4. ...b6 to trade the bad bishop is playable but slower: White gets Ne2 and f4 in with a big centre. Black also has ...cxd4 at once, then ...Nc6 and ...Qb6. The plan is always the same: three or four attackers on d4 and the break ...f6 when White's pieces have left the centre. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6] }
6... Nc6
{ [Ne2] is the main move: both knights can defend d4, and the d2 knight goes to f3 after ...Qb6. [Ngf3] is Korchnoi's gambit line, where ...Qb6 and ...cxd4 win a pawn for development. Black's main plans are ...cxd4 and ...f6, or ...Qb6 with pressure on d4 and b2. Black's worst piece is the light bishop; ...a5-a4 and a queenside clamp are other ideas. [%csl Yd4,Yb2][%cal Gg1e2] }
