{ [exf6] is the safe main line: after [w:exf6] [b:Nxf6] Black has a free game with ...Bd6, ...O-O and ...Qc7. [Nf4] is the sharp try: after ...Nxd4 [w:Qh5+] ...Ke7 [w:exf6+] ...Nxf6 [w:Ng6+] ...hxg6 [w:Qxh8] White has won the exchange, but Black has two central pawns and a king safer than it looks, and the engine calls it equal. It is a line for well-prepared players on both sides. [%cal Ge5f6] }
( 8... Qb6 ) ( 8... Bb4 $2
{ With the knight still on d2 this is no check, only a pin, and the bishop has no future on b4. [a3] asks it at once: ...Bxd2+ hands over the bishop pair, and after ...Be7 [w:Nf3] White has won a tempo for nothing and keeps the grip on e5. Break with [b:f6] instead. [%csl Re5][%cal Ga2a3] }
9. a3 ) 9. exf6 ( 9. Nf4 ) 9... Nxf6
{ [Nf3] and [O-O] are normal. Black has a backward pawn on e6 and a hole on e5, but active pieces: ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Qc7 and ...e5 when ready, which frees the c8 bishop and removes the weakness. With Black castled, White must watch the jumps ...Ne4 and ...Nb4. White's pieces aim at the kingside and e5: Bf4, Ne5 and Qc2 are typical. [%csl Ye6,Ye5][%cal Gd2f3] }
10. Nf3 ( 10. O-O ) ) 3... c5 4. c3
{ The Advance main line. Black adds pressure to d4: [Nc6] is the normal move, [Qb6] (the Wade) hits d4 and b2 at once, and [Bd7] or [Ne7] keep options open. Taking at once with [cxd4] is fine but releases the tension a move early. The knight on g8 goes to f5 by ...Ne7 or ...Nh6, never to f6, which e5 controls. The pawn on c3 also covers b4 and d4, so Black's knight has no outpost there. [%csl Yd4,Yb2,Yf5][%cal Gb8c6] }
4... Nc6
{ [Nf3] is the main move and the most natural: it defends d4 a third time and covers e5. [Be3] also guards d4 and invites ...Qb6, where the pawn on b2 is defended by nothing. [a3] prepares b4. An early [w:f4] looks like it adds support to e5, but it neglects development and takes the f3 square from the knight. Black's queen comes to b6 next, and the knight from g8 goes to e7 or h6. [%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yb2][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 4... Qb6
{ The Wade: the queen comes out before the knight. [Nf3] is the main reply; then ...Bd7 and ...Bb5 can trade off Black's bad bishop. Here the queen already watches b2, so the natural [w:Be3] drops a pawn. [a3], preparing b4, is a good alternative. Black can also go back to the main lines with ...Nc6. The queen on b6 is safe in this closed position, and Black's knights come to c6 and f5 later. [%csl Yb2][%cal Gg1f3] }
5. Nf3
{ [Nc6] goes back to the main lines. [Bd7] is the true Wade idea: ...Bb5 trades the bad bishop for White's good one. White meets it with [w:Be2], so the trade happens on e2, or with [w:a3] and b4 to gain space. The trade solves Black's worst problem, but White keeps more space. White's best set-up is then a3 and b4, which gains space and frees the dark bishop from b2's defence. [%cal Gb8c6] }
( 5. a3 ) ( 5. Be3 $2
{ The bishop defends d4 but leaves b2 alone: [Qxb2] wins a pawn and after [w:Nd2] Black also takes on c3. White gets some development but not enough for two pawns. Play [w:Nf3] first. [%csl Rd4,Rb2][%cal Gb6b2] }
5... Qxb2 ) 5... Nc6 ( 5... Bd7
{ White chooses how to meet ...Bb5. [Be2] allows the trade; [Bd3] and [a3] are the main other moves. [w:Be3] is a mistake again: the b2 pawn drops. After [w:Be2] [b:Bb5] White often takes on c5 and plays b4 to kick Black's dark bishop; then ...Bxf2+ and Kf1 is a known trick, and Black must follow it with ...Bc6, since ...Bxe2+ Qxe2 leaves the f2 bishop with no way out. Black's knights go to c6 and e7. [%csl Yb2,Yc5,Yc6][%cal Gf1e2] }
6. Be2 ( 6. Bd3 ) ( 6. a3 ) ( 6. Be3 $2
{ It guards d4 but leaves b2: [Qxb2] and after [w:Qb3] [b:Qxa1] Black has a rook for little. White's best is to trade queens a pawn down. Play [w:Be2] or [w:Bd3]. [%csl Rb2,Ra1][%cal Gb6b2] }
6... Qxb2 ) 6... Bb5
{ The bishop trade is offered. [dxc5] and b4 is White's most testing line, and [O-O] lets the trade happen. After [Bxb5+] [b:Qxb5] Black's queen is active but White can gain space with Na3-c2. In all lines Black has solved the bad-bishop problem; White keeps space and the better minor pieces. After the trade on e2, Black's queen and knight go to the queenside: ...Nc6, ...Nge7 and ...Nf5. [%cal Gd4c5] }
7. dxc5 ( 7. O-O ) ( 7. Bxb5+ ) ) ) ( 4... Bd7 ) ( 4... Ne7 5. Nf3
{ The knight goes to c6 by way of e7, leaving b8 free. [Nbc6] transposes to normal lines; [Nec6] with ...b6 and ...Ba6 aims to trade the light bishops, and [Bd7] keeps options. White has the usual space and plays [w:Bd3] or [w:Na3]-c2. The point of this order is flexibility: the e7 knight can also go to f5, and the b8 knight to d7 to support ...f6. [%csl Yc6,Yf5,Yd7][%cal Gb8c6] }
5... Nbc6 ( 5... Nec6 ) ( 5... Bd7 ) ) ( 4... cxd4
{ Clarifying the tension on d4 early relieves Black's queenside cramp and opens the c-file, though it also releases all pawn pressure on White's centre and hands White the c3 square back for the queen's knight! Recapturing is forced: after [cxd4 Nc6 Nf3] (or [cxd4 Bb4+ Nc3]), Black maneuvers [b:Nge7]-[b:Nf5] and [b:Qb6] against d4 while White builds smoothly with [w:Nc3], [w:Bd3] and [w:O-O]. [%cal Gc3d4] }
5. cxd4 ) ( 4... b6 5. Nf3
{ The same idea as 3...b6, a move later. [Ne7] heading for c6 or f5 and [Qd7] both prepare ...Ba6. A good plan is ...Ne7-c6, ...b6 and ...Ba6, trading the bishops. The direct ...Ba6? loses a piece to Bxa6 and Qa4+. The trade of light bishops is the key goal: without it Black's bishop has no future. White's best set-up is Bd3, O-O and Na3-c2 or a4-a5. [%cal Gg8e7] }
5... Ne7 ( 5... Qd7 ) ( 5... Nc6 ) ( 5... Ba6 $4
