6. Bxa6 ) ) 5. Nf3
{ The Paulsen position. Black has three good plans: [Qb6] piles up on d4 and b2; [Bd7], the Euwe, waits to see where White's bishop goes; and [Nge7] or [Nh6] send the knight to f5. Taking on d4 early with [cxd4] is fine too. The light bishop has no future on c8, so Black often prepares ...Bd7-b5 or ...Rc8 to make it useful on the queenside. [%csl Yd4,Yb2,Yf5][%cal Gd8b6] }
( 5. Be3
{ The bishop guards d4 a third time, but it leaves b2 without a defender. [Qb6] hits it at once: White must spend a move on [w:Qb3] or [w:Qd2], and Black's pressure grows with ...Nh6-f5 against the e3 bishop. [Nh6] first heads for f5. The bishop on e3 is a natural target for a knight on f5. [%csl Yb2,Yf5][%cal Gd8b6,Gg8h6] }
5... Qb6 ( 5... Nh6 ) ) ( 5. a3 ) ( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. Bb5
{ The pin on c6 does not last. [Bd7] unpins, and after [w:Nf3] the trick ...Nxe5! is in the air because the knight discovers an attack on b5. [Qb6] also works well, putting the question to the bishop at once. White has given up the idea of a light bishop on d3, and taking on c6 hands Black the light squares. After ...Bd7 White's bishop has to take on c6 or retreat. [%csl Yb5,Yd3][%cal Gc8d7] }
5... Bd7
{ Here [Nf3] is the natural move and a well-known slip: it lets ...Nxe5, with the knight discovering an attack on b5, the same trick as in the Euwe line. White should first deal with the pin: [Bxc6] gives up the light bishop but keeps e5. Slower moves such as Ne2 or Be3 leave the bishop loose on b5, and Black is already better after them. After [Bxc6 Bxc6 Nf3] Black should take on d4 before putting the bishop on b5, since an early ...Bb5 is met by [w:a4] and the bishop runs short of squares. [%csl Yb5,Ye5,Yd4][%cal Gb5c6] }
( 5... Qb6 ) ( 5... Qa5 ) 6. Bxc6 ) 5... Qb6 ( 5... Bd7
{ The Euwe: Black waits for White to commit before choosing ...Qb6 or ...Nge7. [Be2] and [a3] are the main replies, and [Bd3] meets ...cxd4 with a gambit. [Bb5] looks like a natural pin but is a known blunder: the knight on c6 takes e5 and uncovers the d7 bishop on b5. Black's plans include ...Nge7-f5 and ...Rc8. The bishop also gives the a8 rook the c8 square: ...Rc8 is a common plan. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gf1e2] }
6. Be2
{ [Nge7] is the most flexible: the knight goes to f5 or g6. Taking on d4 first with [cxd4] is also good. White's typical regrouping is Na3-c2, so that both knights defend d4. Black can answer with ...Nf5 and ...Be7, or use the c-file after ...Rc8. Black can also play ...Qb6 later, reaching the Paulsen with the bishop on d7. [%csl Yf5,Yd4][%cal Gg8e7] }
( 6. a3 ) ( 6. Bd3
{ Here [cxd4] and then ...Qb6 or ...Nge7 is the main line. Black can also set a trap with [Rc8]: after [w:O-O] ...cxd4 [w:cxd4] ...Nb4, the knight hits the bishop, and if White avoids the trade with Be2 then ...Nc2 wins the rook on a1, protected by the rook on c8. White's best answer to ...Rc8 is a3, which stops ...Nb4. [%csl Yc8][%cal Gc5d4] }
6... cxd4 ( 6... Rc8
{ The rook prepares ...cxd4 and ...Nb4, which wins the bishop pair after castling. [a3] is the most precise answer: it takes b4 from the knight. Castling at once allows ...cxd4 cxd4 Nb4, and the bishop must be given up, since Be2 drops the exchange to ...Nc2. After [w:a3] Black plays ...cxd4 and ...Qb6, and with the bishop on d7 covering b5, White has no Bb5+ trick to save d4. [%csl Yb4,Yb5,Yd4][%cal Ga2a3] }
7. a3 ( 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 Nb4
{ The knight attacks the bishop on d3. White should let it go: [Nc3] or [Bf4] keeps a good position after ...Nxd3 and [w:Qxd3]. Saving the bishop with [w:Be2] is the trap: ...Nc2 hits the rook on a1, and the knight is defended by the rook on c8 along the open file. After the trade on d3 Black has the bishop pair and White has development and the e5 wedge, roughly level. [%csl Yd3,Ye5][%cal Gb1c3] }
9. Nc3 ( 9. Bf4 ) ( 9. Bg5 ) ( 9. Be2 $2
{ The bishop dodges the trade, but it leaves c2 open. [Nc2] attacks the rook on a1, and the knight is protected by the rook on c8 along the open c-file. Black wins the exchange. Give up the bishop with [w:Nc3] or [w:Bf4]. [%csl Rc2,Ra1][%cal Gb4c2] }
9... Nc2 ) ) ) ( 6... Nge7 ) ) ( 6. Be3 ) ( 6. Bb5 $2
{ The pin on c6 seems strong, but the knight leaves with tempo: [Nxe5] takes the central pawn and uncovers an attack on the bishop on b5 from d7. After [Nxe5 Nxe5 Bxb5] Black has won a pawn and traded the bad bishop. Play [w:Be2], [w:a3] or [w:Bd3]. [%csl Rb5][%cal Gc6e5] }
6... Nxe5 ) 6... Nge7
{ [Na3] heads for c2 to guard d4. [O-O] is also normal; then ...Ng6 can be better than ...Nf5, aiming at e5. An attacking idea for White is g4 to chase a knight from f5, though it loosens the kingside. Black keeps the choice of ...cxd4 and ...Nf5. White should avoid letting the knight settle on f5 without a challenge: g4 or Bd3 are the ways to do it. [%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yf5][%cal Gb1a3] }
( 6... cxd4 ) ( 6... Rc8 ) 7. Na3
{ [cxd4] first and then ...Nf5 is the usual order: the knight on f5 presses d4 and White's knight on c2 defends it. [Nf5] at once is also good. Black follows with ...Be7, ...O-O and ...f6 or ...Qb6. A typical mistake is a slow ...a6 that gives White time for Nc2 and g4. Black can also castle long in some lines, after ...Qb6 and ...O-O-O. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc5d4] }
( 7. O-O ) 7... cxd4 ( 7... Nf5 ) ) ( 5... Nge7
{ The knight heads for f5. [Na3]-c2 is the most useful reply because the knight will defend d4; [Bd3] invites ...cxd4 and ...Nf5, when the bishop can trade itself for the knight. The drawback of ...Ne7 is that it blocks the dark bishop for a while. In some lines the knight also goes to g6, where it eyes e5 and the f4 square. [%csl Yf5,Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gb1a3] }
6. Na3 ( 6. Bd3 ) ( 6. a3 ) ) ( 5... Nh6
{ The knight goes to f5 by the edge, so the bishop on c8 is not blocked. [Bd3] and then ...cxd4 [w:cxd4] ...Nf5 [w:Bxf5] gives a typical structure: Black's doubled f-pawns cover e4 and g4, and White has the better minor piece in the long run. [Bxh6] gives Black two bishops; here it costs nothing material because the queen is not on b6. With the knight on f5, Black's pressure on d4 grows, and ...Qb6 and ...Be7 follow. [%csl Yf5,Ye4,Yg4][%cal Gf1d3] }
6. Bd3 ( 6. Bxh6 ) ( 6. a3 ) 6... cxd4 7. cxd4 Nf5 8. Bxf5 exf5
{ A typical French structure: the f5 pawn shuts down White's play on the kingside, and the light bishop will go to e6. White has the better minor piece in principle, a knight against the bad bishop, and plays [Nc3], [h4]-h5 or Ne2-f4. Black answers with ...Be6, ...Be7 and ...h6, with a solid but passive game. Black usually castles short and looks for play on the queenside with ...Rc8 and ...Qb6, or with ...f6 to open the f-file. [%csl Ye6][%cal Gb1c3] }
9. Nc3 ( 9. h4 ) ( 9. Kf1 ) ) ( 5... cxd4 ) 6. Bd3 cxd4
{ White chooses the form of the gambit. [cxd4] keeps the centre: then Black covers b5 with ...Bd7 before taking on d4, and after castling White is in the Milner-Barry. [O-O] first is the modern way: White castles and lets Black decide between ...dxc3 and ...Bd7. Taking with the knight, [w:Nxd4], gives up the support of e5: ...Nxe5 or ...Bc5 follows. [%csl Yb5,Ye5][%cal Gc3d4] }
7. cxd4
{ Now [Bd7] is the move: it covers b5 so that ...Nxd4 becomes a real threat. The greedy ...Nxd4? fails because after Nxd4 the queen recapture is met by Bb5+, a discovered attack from the queen on d1. A check on b4 with ...Bb4+ looks active, but [w:Kf1] keeps the bishop pair and ...Nge7 allows [w:Nc3] and Bxf5 ideas that win d5. White's knight on f3 defends d4, and Black wants to remove it. [%csl Yb5,Yb4,Yd5][%cal Gc8d7] }
( 7. O-O
{ A modern way to play the gambit: White castles before taking back on d4. If [dxc3] then [w:Nxc3] gives White three pieces in play for a pawn and Be3 hits the queen. [Bd7] is calmer and keeps the pawn on d4 for now. Black must watch b5 and the e-file, and should not take on b2 while White's pieces are so active. Black's safest is ...Bd7, then ...Nge7 and ...Ng6. [%csl Yd4,Yb5,Yb2][%cal Gc8d7] }
7... Bd7
{ Black covers b5 and keeps the pawn on d4 for now. White can take back, [cxd4], reaching the main Milner-Barry, or play [Re1] and Nbd2, developing while the d4 pawn stays under attack. White's compensation is development, the open c-file and the e5 wedge. Black's next moves are ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 or ...Nf5, and taking on c3 is possible but hands White a big lead in development. [%csl Yb5,Ye5][%cal Gc3d4] }
( 7... dxc3 8. Nxc3
{ White has a big lead in development for a pawn. [Bd7] and [Nge7] are the main moves; the knight on e7 heads for g6 or f5. [w:Be3] will hit the queen next, so Black should decide where the queen belongs: d8 or a5. Taking on b2 later is dangerous, because [w:Nb5] then threatens Nc7+ and the rooks join quickly. Black's pieces are behind, so the king should get safe before grabbing more. [%csl Yg6,Yf5,Yd8][%cal Gc8d7] }
8... Bd7 ( 8... Nge7 ) 9. Be3
{ The queen is hit. [Qd8] looks like a step back but is the safest move: it covers c7 and d7. The greedy ...Qxb2 lets [w:Nb5] in, threatening Nc7+ and Rb1 to trap the queen. ...Bc5 is met by [w:Na4], hitting queen and bishop. White's lead in development is worth the pawn, but Black has no structural problems. After [b:Qd8] White plays Rc1, Nb5 or Bg5, and Black develops with ...Nge7 and ...Ng6. [%csl Yc7,Yd7][%cal Gb6d8] }
9... Qd8 ( 9... Qa5 ) ( 9... Qxb2 $2
{ A second pawn, but the queen is now far from home. [Nb5] threatens Nc7+ and the queen has few squares: after ...Rc8 White plays [w:Re1] and [w:Rb1], and the queen on b2 is trapped or Black's position collapses. Play [b:Qd8]. [%cal Gc3b5] }
10. Nb5 ) ) ( 7... Nge7 ) 8. cxd4 ( 8. Re1
{ The Hector Variation of the Milner-Barry Gambit. Instead of recapturing on d4, White brings the rook to e1 to overprotect e5 and keep the gambit pure: the b1 knight now threatens to enter via [w:Nbd2] and b3 while Black's kingside is still bottled up. [Nge7] is Black's soundest response, heading for g6 or f5 to contest the dark squares before touching c3. [Rc8] develops with an eye on the c-file, while grabbing a second pawn with [dxc3 Nxc3] hands White tremendous piece activity, open central files, and dangerous sacrifices on d5 or e6. [%csl Ye5,Yd4][%cal Gg8e7,Ga8c8] }
8... Nge7 ( 8... Rc8 ) ( 8... dxc3 ) ) ) 7... Bd7
{ Black threatens ...Nxd4 at last, so White must choose. [O-O] is the Milner-Barry Gambit: two pawns for a lead in development and the open e-file. [Bc2] keeps d4 but runs into ...Nb4, trading off White's best bishop. [Be2] is solid but passive. [w:Be3] looks like the normal defence of d4, yet it drops b2 to ...Qxb2. Black's other threat is ...Nb4, trading the bishop. [%csl Yd4,Yb2][%cal Ge1g1] }
( 7... Nxd4 $4
{ The pawn looks free because White has only the knight to recapture. But after [Nxd4] the natural ...Qxd4 loses the queen to [w:Bb5+]: the bishop moves with check and uncovers the queen on d1. Without the recapture Black is simply a piece down. Cover b5 first with [b:Bd7]; then ...Nxd4 becomes a real threat. [%csl Rb5][%cal Gf3d4] }
8. Nxd4 ) ( 7... Bb4+ $2
{ Checks are tempting, but here it chases the king to a square where it is safe: [Kf1] and White will play [w:a3] and win time on the bishop, which has no good retreat. Black's play against d4 has stalled. [b:Bd7] is still the move. [%csl Rb4][%cal Ge1f1] }
8. Kf1 ) ( 7... Nge7 $6
{ The knight goes to f5 but leaves the centre loose: [Nc3] and if ...Nf5, [w:Bxf5] [b:exf5] [w:Nxd5] wins the d5 pawn. The knight on e7 also blocks the dark bishop. Play [b:Bd7] first. [%csl Rd5,Rf8][%cal Gb1c3] }
8. Nc3 ) ( 7... Nb4 $2
{ The knight goes for the bishop on d3, but [Be2] keeps it. On b4 the knight has no target, since the queen covers c2, and it must go back: Black has lost two moves. In the ...Bd7 and ...Rc8 lines the same jump works, because the rook backs up ...Nc2; here play [b:Bd7]. [%csl Rb4,Rc2][%cal Gd3e2] }
8. Be2 ) 8. O-O
{ The Milner-Barry Gambit. White offers d4 and then e5 for quick development. [Nxd4] takes the first pawn; after the knight trade White plays [w:Nc3] and the queen faces Nb5, Re1 and Qg4 ideas. Declining is also possible. What Black must not do is play slowly with ...Nge7, which gives White d4 back for free. The gambit idea is to open the e-file and use b5. [%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yb5][%cal Gc6d4] }
( 8. Bc2
{ The bishop keeps away from ...Nxd4 tricks, but it is short of squares. [Nb4] hits it at once: the bishop on c2 has no good retreat, so Black trades it and White loses the piece that would attack h7. After [Nb4 O-O Nxc2 Qxc2] Black plays ...Rc8 and ...Ne7 with a comfortable game. Other ideas are ...Bb5 to stop White castling and ...Qa6. After [b:Nb4] [w:Bb1] the bishop keeps its diagonal, but Black's ...Bb5 stops White from castling. [%csl Yc2,Yh7][%cal Gc6b4] }
8... Nb4 ) ( 8. Be2 ) ( 8. Be3 $4
{ The bishop guards d4 but takes away b2's only defender. [Qxb2] wins a pawn and keeps hitting the rook on a1; after [Qxb2 Nbd2 Nb4] Black threatens ...Qc3+ and ...Nxd3+. White has neither d4 nor a gambit. Play [w:O-O] or [w:Bc2]. [%csl Ra1,Rb2][%cal Gb6b2] }
8... Qxb2 ) 8... Nxd4
{ [Nxd4] is the most played: after the queen takes back White continues with Nc3. Strong players also like [Nbd2], which keeps the knights on and aims at a piece attack. [w:Be3] looks like it wins time, but after ...Nxf3+ the queen recaptures and Black takes b2. Do not let the knight on d4 take on f3 with check for nothing. After [w:Nxd4] [b:Qxd4] White plays Nc3 to offer the second pawn. [%csl Yb2,Yd4][%cal Gf3d4] }
( 8... a6 ) ( 8... Rc8 ) 9. Nxd4 ( 9. Nbd2 ) ( 9. Be3 $2
{ The bishop attacks the queen's defender and invites ...Qxb2, but the knight on d4 simply takes with check: [Nxf3+] and after the recapture Black plays ...Qxb2, two pawns up with White's queenside loose. Take back on d4 with the knight, or play [w:Nbd2]. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gd4f3] }
9... Nxf3+ ) 9... Qxd4
{ White is a pawn down and offers a second one. [Nc3] is the classical gambit: the knight eyes b5 and d5, and if Black takes e5 the rook goes to e1. [Qe2] keeps the e5 pawn and prepares Nc3 or Rd1. Engines rate Black better, but in practice White's pieces come out fast and every Black inaccuracy is punished. White's best is to develop fast: Nc3, Qe2, Kh1 and f4. [%csl Yb5,Yd5,Ye5][%cal Gb1c3] }
10. Nc3
{ Black's safest is [a6], which takes b5 from the knight, followed by ...Ne7-c6. [Qb6] also holds. Grabbing the second pawn with [Qxe5] is playable but means walking a tightrope after [w:Re1]: the e-file opens against the king on e8 and every queen move must be accurate. Club players who take everything usually lose to Nb5 or Nxd5 tricks. Black's plan is ...a6, ...Ne7-c6 and ...Bc5 or ...Be7, then castle. [%csl Yb5][%cal Ga7a6] }
( 10. Qe2 ) ( 10. Re1 ) 10... a6
{ Black keeps the extra pawn and stops Nb5. White's best set-up is [Qe2], with Kh1 and f4-f5 to attack the uncastled king, or [Re1] keeping an eye on e6. Black answers with ...Ne7, ...Ng6 or ...Nc6, and ...Bc5, then castles. The pawn on e5 is White's attacking spearhead, and once the queen or a rook stands on the e-file it is poisoned: ...Qxe5 simply loses the queen. Black leaves it and develops. [%csl Ye6,Ye5][%cal Gd1e2] }
( 10... Qb6 ) ( 10... Qxe5
{ Black has two pawns. [Re1] is the point of the gambit: the rook pins nothing yet, but it hits the queen and lines up on e6 and the king. Black must find the right square. White's other tries, like [w:Qe2], are slower. The knight jumps to b5 or d5 come next, so Black's queen has to guard c7 and d6. White's rook comes to e1 with tempo, and Black's queen has to choose a square carefully. [%csl Ye6,Yb5,Yd5][%cal Gf1e1] }
11. Re1
{ Only two queen moves hold: [Qb8], which defends against Nxd5 ideas from far away, and [Qd6], which meets Nb5 with ...Qb6. ...Qd4 looks safe, but [w:Rxe6+] is a crushing sacrifice. ...Qc7 is met by [w:Nxd5], when White wins the pawn back with a strong attack. In every line Black's king is in the centre, so the queen must keep an eye on e6. Each queen move has its own refutation if Black picks the wrong one. [%csl Ye6][%cal Ge5b8] }
11... Qb8 ( 11... Qd6 12. Nb5
{ The knight hits the queen and c7. [Qb6] is the only good square: it keeps the queen near the king and covers c7. Taking with ...Bxb5 opens the a4-e8 diagonal and the king cannot castle after Bxb5+; ...Qc5 runs into Be3 and Rc1. After [Qb6] White plays [w:Be3] and the queen goes to a5 and back. After ...Qb6 Be3 Qa5 Bd2 Qb6 the game is often drawn by repetition. [%csl Yc7,Ya5][%cal Gd6b6] }
12... Qb6 ( 12... Bxb5 $2
{ Trading the knight seems to end the danger, but it opens the a4-e8 diagonal: [Bxb5+] and the king has no good square. After ...Kd8 White plays [w:Qh5] with Bg5+ to come, and Black's king is caught in the centre. Keep the bishop and play [b:Qb6]. [%csl Re8,Rb6][%cal Gd3b5] }
13. Bxb5+ ) 13. Be3
{ The bishop hits the queen again. [Qa5] keeps it safe, and after [w:Bd2] [b:Qb6] the game often repeats. The natural ...Bc5 is a famous trap: White trades on c5 and plays [w:Rc1], and Nc7+ wins the rook on a8. ...Qd8 is also bad because [w:Bf4] hits b8 and c7. Black is two pawns up, so a draw by repetition is a fine result against a prepared opponent. [%csl Yc5,Ya8,Yb8][%cal Gb6a5] }
13... Qa5 ( 13... Bc5 $4
{ Offering the trade looks like simple development. But after [Bxc5 Qxc5 Rc1] the queen is attacked and must leave c7's defence: after any queen move, [w:Nc7+] forks the king and the rook on a8. Black should play [b:Qa5] and accept a draw by repetition. [%csl Rc7,Ra8][%cal Ge3c5] }
14. Bxc5 ) ) ( 11... Qd4 $2
{ The queen stays central and keeps d5 guarded, but it leaves e6 defended only by the bishop and the f-pawn. [Rxe6+] breaks through: if [b:fxe6] then [w:Bg6+] uncovers an attack on the queen from d1, and if ...Bxe6 then [w:Bb5+] does the same. Play [b:Qb8] or [b:Qd6]. [%csl Rd5,Re6][%cal Ge1e6] }
