[Event "French Defence (3)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 ( 2. c4
{ The Steiner Variation. White's c-pawn covers d5 to make ...d5 harder, but Black plays it anyway. After [d5 exd5 exd5 cxd5 Qxd5] the queen is a little exposed and [w:Nc3] gains a tempo, but Black's pieces come out freely. After [d5 cxd5 exd5] White chooses between [w:exd5], an open game where Black regains the pawn or gets quick play, and the Orthoschnapp Gambit [w:Qb3]. [c5] is a calm alternative that leads to English or Sicilian structures. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ White must decide about the tension. [cxd5] is the main move: after ...exd5 White takes with the e-pawn or gambits with [w:Qb3]. [exd5] is similar: after ...exd5 cxd5 Qxd5 the queen is active and Black develops quickly. The natural [w:Nc3] costs time: ...d4 kicks the knight, Black gains space and plays ...e5, ...Nf6 and ...Bc5, while the c4 pawn blocks White's light bishop. It does not lose a pawn, though: after ...dxe4 the knight simply takes back. [%cal Gc4d5] }
( 2... c5 ) 3. cxd5
{ [exd5] is the natural recapture, and nearly everyone plays it: the e-file opens and the c8 bishop is free. Now White chooses between [w:exd5], when ...Nf6 attacks the pawn and Black gets good play for it, and the Orthoschnapp Gambit [w:Qb3], hitting d5 and b7. Taking with the queen, ...Qxd5, is a plain blunder: the e4 pawn takes it. After ...exd5 Black's pieces come out quickly, and the only thing to watch is the light squares around the king. [%csl Yd5,Yb7][%cal Ge6d5] }
( 3. exd5 ) ( 3. Nc3 $6
{ Developing the knight looks natural, but it walks into a gain of space: [d4] kicks it, and after [w:Nce2] [b:e5] Black has a big centre and free development, while White's knight has gone backwards and the c4 pawn blocks the bishop's best diagonal. Taking on e4 is also good for Black. Trade on d5 first with [w:cxd5] or support the centre with [w:e5]. [%csl Rc3,Rc4][%cal Gd5d4] }
3... d4 ) 3... exd5
{ White's main move is [exd5]: after ...Nf6 White can hold the pawn only with [w:Bb5+], and Black gets good play for it. [Qb3], the Orthoschnapp Gambit, offers the e-pawn to hit d5 and b7 and aim at f7 with [w:Bc4]; it is a gamble, about a pawn worse by the engine. Black should accept with ...dxe4 and develop fast. The check [w:Qa4+] is worse still: after ...Bd7 the queen just gets in the way. Black's c8 bishop is no longer blocked, and ...Nf6, ...Be7 and ...O-O follow. [%csl Yd5,Yb7,Yf7][%cal Ge4d5] }
4. exd5 ( 4. Qb3
{ The Orthoschnapp Gambit. The queen hits d5 and b7, and the e-pawn is offered. [dxe4] is the right answer: after [w:Bc4] queen and bishop aim at f7, and Black defends with ...Qe7 or ...Nh6 before developing the rest. Declining with ...Nf6 or ...c6 gives White what the gambit wants, a weak d5 pawn to press. The careless ...Be6 looks like it holds d5, but it leaves b7 to the queen. Whatever Black chooses, the light squares around the king need care. [%csl Yd5,Yb7,Yf7][%cal Gd5e4] }
4... dxe4 ( 4... Be6 $6
{ The bishop guards d5, but it leaves b7 alone. [Nc3] adds a second attacker on d5, and after ...dxe4 the queen takes on b7 with an attack on the rook. Black is a pawn down, with only some activity for it. Answer the gambit with [b:dxe4] or [b:c6]. [%csl Rb7,Rd5][%cal Gb1c3] }
5. Nc3 ) 5. Bc4
{ Queen and bishop aim at f7. [Qe7] is the best defence: it guards f7 and also eyes e4. ...Nh6 covers f7 too but is less accurate. White's plan is [w:Nc3], [w:Nge2] or [w:d3] to open more lines. A trap to know: after [Nh6 d3], taking with ...exd3 lets [w:Bxh6] and [w:Bxf7+], and if the king goes to d7 then Qe6 is mate. [%csl Yf7,Ye4,Yd7][%cal Gd8e7] }
5... Qe7 ) ( 4. Qa4+ $6
{ The check gains nothing: [Bd7] blocks with a developing move and the queen must move again, while the e4 pawn is still hanging. Black stands better after ...dxe4 or ...Nf6. Play [w:exd5] instead. [%csl Ra4,Re4][%cal Gc8d7] }
4... Bd7 ) ) ( 2. b3
{ The Horwitz Attack. White fianchettoes the queen's bishop to aim down the long diagonal at g7. The best reply is [d5]: the e4 pawn is attacked at once, and after [w:Bb2] [b:dxe4] White must spend time winning it back with [w:Nc3], while Black develops with ...Nf6, ...Be7 and ...O-O. Keep an eye on the diagonal: until a knight stands on f6, a bishop that leaves f8 leaves g7 to the b2 bishop. Black can also play ...Nf6 and ...c5 and take the long diagonal over. [%csl Yg7,Ye4,Yf6][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ White chooses between [Bb2], which turns the opening into a gambit of the e4 pawn, and [exd5], a quiet symmetrical game where the b2 bishop has little to bite on. After [w:Bb2] Black should take with [b:dxe4]: White wins the pawn back with [w:Nc3] and [w:Qe2], but it costs time. [b:Nf6] is the solid alternative: after [w:e5] the knight goes to d7 and ...c5 gives Black a good Advance French, in which White's bishop on b2 stares at its own e5 pawn. [%csl Ye4,Yd7,Yb2][%cal Gc1b2] }
3. Bb2
{ Black chooses how to meet the gambit. [dxe4] takes the pawn: White wins it back with [w:Nc3] and [w:Qe2], and Black develops in peace with ...Nf6, ...Be7, ...O-O and ...c5. [Nf6] keeps the centre: if [w:e5], ...Nfd7 and ...c5 give a French Advance where White's bishop on b2 bites on its own pawn. The one thing to avoid is developing the dark bishop before a knight reaches f6: with f6 and e5 empty, the bishop on b2 hits g7. [%csl Yb2,Yf6,Ye5][%cal Gd5e4] }
( 3. exd5 ) 3... dxe4 ( 3... Nf6 ) 4. Nc3
{ White will win the pawn back on e4. [Nf6] is the natural reply: after [w:Qe2] Black plays [b:Be7], castles and strikes with [b:c5], and the knight on f6 shuts the long diagonal. White often castles long and pushes [w:g4], but Black's position is solid. Holding the pawn with [f5] is playable but loosens e6 and the king's diagonal: after [w:d3], [w:Qe2] and long castling the d-file opens, and Black must not castle into the trick shown in the next position. [%csl Ye4,Yf6,Ye6][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... f5 5. d3 exd3 6. Bxd3 Nf6 7. Qe2 Be7 8. O-O-O
{ A trap position. White has castled long, so the rook on d1 stands behind the d3 bishop and lines up with the queen on d8. Castling short now is dubious: after [O-O Bxf5] White uncovers the rook on the queen, and whether Black takes back or steps away with [O-O Bxf5 Qe8 Bxe6+], White comes out ahead. Black should first block the file with [Bd6]; ...Nc6 is slower and leaves White the initiative. The king stays in the centre until the d-file is closed. [%csl Yd1,Yd3,Yd8][%cal Ge7d6] }
8... Bd6 ( 8... O-O $6
{ The king looks safer, but [Bxf5] is a discovered attack: the bishop takes a pawn and the rook on d1 now hits the queen on d8. After [Bxf5 exf5 Rxd8] Black gives the queen for a rook and a bishop, while [Bxf5 Qe8 Bxe6+] drops a second pawn and exposes the king. Block the file first with [b:Bd6]. [%csl Rd1,Rd8][%cal Gd3f5] }
9. Bxf5 ) ) ) 2... d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Ne2
{ The Alekhine-Maroczy Gambit. White offers the e4 pawn so that after ...dxe4 and [w:a3], the knight on e2 can take back on c3 without doubled pawns. Accepting with [dxe4] is the main line; [Nf6] and [Nc6] decline. The natural ...c5 is a mistake here: [w:a3] forces ...Bxc3+, the knight recaptures and White's centre and development are better. In the main line Black keeps the extra pawn for a while, and White's compensation is the bishop pair and quick development. [%csl Ye4,Ye2][%cal Gd5e4] }
( 4. Bd3
{ The bishop defends e4, but it blocks the d-pawn and lets Black hit the centre. [c5] is the sharpest answer, and [dxe4] gains time on the bishop: after [dxe4 Bxe4 Nf6] it must move again and Black has an easy game. [Ne7] is solid. Two natural moves fail here: ...Nf6 is kicked by [w:e5] with gain of time, and ...Nc6 blocks the c-pawn, so White closes the centre with e5 and Black has no ...c5 break against it. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gc7c5] }
