5. a3 ) 5. a3
{ [Bxc3+] is the main move: the knight retakes and Black is a pawn up, while White has the bishop pair and the e4 pawn as a target. [Be7] keeps the bishop but gives the pawn back after [w:Nxe4], a calm Rubinstein-like game. In the main line Black must not hang on to e4 at any cost: ...Nf6, ...Nc6 and ...O-O come first, and the pawn can be returned with ...e5 or ...f5 to free the game if White's development grows too strong. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gb4c3] }
5... Bxc3+
{ The Alekhine Gambit Accepted. Black parts with the dark-squared bishop to stay a pawn ahead on e4, banking on a solid queenside structure without doubled c-pawns. [Nxc3] is the clear recapture, hitting e4 at once and bringing the knight back into the centre; [w:bxc3] would give up the knight's activity and clog the c-file for no gain. White follows up with [w:Bf4] or [w:Bg5], [w:Qd2] and long castling, using the bishop pair and open files, while Black develops briskly with [b:Nf6] and [b:Nc6], ready to return the extra pawn with [b:e5] if needed. [%csl Re4][%cal Ge2c3] }
( 5... Be7 ) 6. Nxc3
