[Event "Caro-Kann Defence (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 c6
{ The Caro-Kann. Black's c-pawn steps aside to support [d5]: if White ever takes on d5, Black takes back with a pawn and keeps a foothold in the centre, without bringing the queen out early as in the Scandinavian. Unlike the French, the light bishop isn't shut in: Black usually develops it to f5 or g4 first and only then plays ...e6. The price is time, since the c-pawn often moves again to c5. It takes its name from Horatio Caro and Marcus Kann, two players who analysed it in 1886. White's main move is [d4], building a full centre; [Nc3], [Nf3], [c4] and the quiet [d3] are the main alternatives. [%cal Gc6d5] }
2. d4
{ White takes the centre with two pawns, and Black answers [d5] at once: the pawn hits e4, and White has to choose between pushing it, trading it, defending it with a knight or propping it up with f3. Every main line of the Caro-Kann starts from that position. Other second moves for Black, such as ...g6 or ...d6, lead to different openings, and [Nf6], the Masi, is playable but lets White gain space with e5. The whole point of 1...c6 is to play ...d5 with the c-pawn ready to recapture. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 2. Nf3
{ Developing [Nf3] on move two sidesteps early d4 theory and keeps White's options open between the Two Knights ([Nc3]), the Exchange ([d5 exd5]), or King's Indian Attack setups ([d3] and [g3]). Black's plan does not budge: strike the center at once with [d5], challenging e4 before White can build a broad front. Because White's knight is already on f3, Black's light-squared bishop gets an ideal target on g4 as soon as the center stabilizes, followed by [e6], [b:Nf6] and [b:Nbd7] or [c5]. [%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ [exd5] usually goes into the Exchange after d4, though Ne5 is an independent try. [Nc3] is the Two Knights. [e5] is an Advance in which White's knight is already on f3, so Black can play ...Bg4 as well as ...Bf5. [d3] offers a queen trade after ...dxe4 dxe4 Qxd1+ Kxd1, a level endgame, or a King's Indian setup if Black keeps queens on. What White must avoid is d4 here: the e4 pawn is left hanging. [%cal Ge4d5,Gb1c3,Ge4e5,Gd2d3] }
3. exd5
{ Trading on d5 on move three steers the Two Knights Caro-Kann toward Exchange waters while keeping d4 flexible for one more turn. Black recaptures [cxd5], claiming an equal share of the center and opening the c6 square for the queen's knight. From here White usually plays [d4] into the Exchange Variation, [cxd5 Bb5+] to trade light-squared bishops, [c4] for a Panov-style isolated queen's pawn battle, or the tricky [Ne5] Apocalypse sortie—against all of which Black develops [Nc6], [Nf6] and the light-squared bishop outside e6. [%cal Gc6d5] }
( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. e5
{ An Advance in which White's knight is already on f3 and the d-pawn still at home. [Bg4] is good: the bishop pins the knight, and if White plays h3, Black can take on f3; then ...e6, ...c5 and ...Nc6 hit the centre. [Bf5] is also fine, and [c5] grabs space at once, making d4 harder. Black's plan is the same as in the Advance, but White's centre is weaker without a pawn on d4. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gc8g4,Gc8f5,Gc6c5] }
3... Bg4
{ [d4] gives a normal Advance with the bishop on g4, pinning the f3 knight: Black follows with ...e6, ...c5 and ...Nc6, and the pin adds pressure on d4. [Be2] breaks the pin in advance, and [h3] asks the bishop to decide: ...Bxf3 Qxf3 e6 leaves White with the bishop pair, ...Bh5 keeps the pin. Black's plan in all lines: ...e6, ...c5 and ...Nc6. [%csl Yg4][%cal Gd2d4,Gf1e2,Gh2h3] }
( 3... Bf5 ) ( 3... c5 ) 4. d4 ( 4. Be2 ) ( 4. h3 ) ) ( 3. d3
{ White keeps the centre closed for now. [dxe4] offers a queen trade: after dxe4 Qxd1+ Kxd1, the endgame is level, and Black's development is easy. [Bg4] pins the knight and keeps queens on, and [e6] is a solid French-like setup. The ambitious ...e5 is a slip here: the knight on f3 simply takes it. Against White's King's Indian Attack setup, Black's pieces have easy squares: ...Nf6, ...Bg4 and ...e6, with ...e5 once it is guarded. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd5e4,Gc8g4,Ge7e6] }
3... dxe4
{ [dxe4] accepts the queen trade: after ...Qxd1+ Kxd1, White's king is in the middle but the queens are off, and the game is level. [Ng5], the Dinic gambit, keeps queens on: after ...exd3 Bxd3, White's pieces aim at f7 and h7, with tricks Black must know. The jump to e5 loses the knight to a queen check. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd3e4,Gf3g5] }
( 3... Bg4 ) ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... e5 $6
{ Taking the centre looks natural, but the e5 pawn has no defender. [Nxe5] wins it, and ...dxe4 dxe4 Qxd1+ Kxd1 only gets Black a little activity for the pawn. Play ...dxe4, ...Bg4 or ...e6 first, and push ...e5 when a piece guards it. [%csl Re5][%cal Gf3e5] }
4. Nxe5 ) 4. dxe4
{ [Qxd1+] is the move: it trades queens while White's king, not Black's, has to take back. The other moves keep the queens on the d-file with White to move, and that turns the tables: after ...Bg4 or ...Nf6, Qxd8+ Kxd8 trades them anyway, and it is Black's king that loses the right to castle. Pushing ...e5 is worse still: Qxd8+ Kxd8 Nxe5 wins the pawn. After Qxd1+ Kxd1, the endgame is level and Black's pieces come out easily. [%csl Yd1,Ye4][%cal Gd8d1] }
( 4. Ng5
{ The Dinic gambit. White's knight attacks e4 and eyes f7. Black's most common move is [exd3], after which Bxd3 brings the bishop to the b1-h7 diagonal and opens the d-file between the queens. [e5] is also good, keeping the pawn on e4 for now. Black must be careful: the obvious developing moves can walk into a sacrifice on f7. [%csl Yf7,Yg5][%cal Ge4d3,Ge7e5] }
