{ [Bxd3]. The bishop and the g5 knight aim at f7, g6 and h7, and the queen on d1 stares down the open d-file at Black's queen, so a check on g6 can uncover an attack on d8. Black is a pawn up and has to be precise for a few moves. [%csl Yf7,Yd8][%cal Gf1d3] }
( 4... e5 ) 5. Bxd3
{ [Nd7]! blocks the d-file between the two queens, so a check on g6 can no longer uncover an attack on Black's queen, and ...Ngf6 comes next. It is the only good move. ...e6, which opens e7 for the king, lets the knight take on h7 and win the pawn back, and the natural ...Nf6 and ...h6 lose: White sacrifices on f7, and a bishop check on g6 uncovers the queen against Black's queen. Black is a pawn up, but only after ...Nd7 and ...Ngf6 is the king safe. [%csl Yd8,Yf7][%cal Gb8d7] }
5... Nd7 ( 5... Nf6 $4
{ The most natural developing move, and a very common slip. [Nxf7 Kxf7 Bg6+]! is the point: taking the bishop with the king or the pawn lets Qxd8 win the queen down the open d-file, and other king moves leave Black a pawn down with a ruined position. Play ...Nd7, which blocks the d-file. [%csl Rd8,Rg6][%cal Gg5f7] }
