{ Black has hit back in the centre, and White has to look after e4 with a weakened kingside. The best answers keep the position closed: [e5] gains space, and Black plays ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...h5 to attack the g4 pawn; [d4] and [h3] are also fine. What White must not do is open the game: after exd5, the queen takes back on d5, hitting the rook on h1, and ...Bxg4 follows. The Spike only works if the centre stays shut. [%csl Yg4][%cal Ge4e5,Gd2d4] }
3. e5 ( 3. d4 ) ( 3. h3 ) ( 3. exd5 $2
{ The natural capture, but it opens the long diagonal toward White's rook. [Qxd5] hits h1, and after f3 or a knight move, ...Bxg4 picks up the loose pawn: Black is better at once. Keep the centre closed with e5 or d4. [%csl Rh1,Rg4][%cal Gd8d5] }
3... Qxd5 ) ( 3. f3 $2
{ The pawn props up e4, but it takes f3 from the knight and weakens the king even more. [e5] hits the centre, and White has nothing but holes. Push e5 or play d4 instead. [%csl Rf3,Rg4][%cal Ge7e5] }
3... e5 ) ( 3. Nc3 $6
{ The knight defends e4, but [d4] kicks it at once. After Nce2, ...Bxg4 picks up the loose pawn, and after Nb1 White has lost two tempi while Black develops with ...Nf6 and ...e5. Push e5 or play d4. [%csl Rc3,Rg4][%cal Gd5d4] }
3... d4 ) ) ( 2. b4
{ The Labahn gambit. White offers the b-pawn to pull Black's pieces away from the centre. [e5] hits back in the centre and opens the f8 bishop's diagonal toward b4, so White has to spend a move on Bb2 or a3. [d5] is just as good: Black hits e4 at once and treats the b4 pawn as a weakness for later, with ...a5. Black doesn't need to grab anything: normal development is strong against White's loose queenside. [%csl Yb4][%cal Ge7e5,Gd7d5] }
2... e5
{ [Bb2] defends the pawn indirectly by hitting e5: if Black takes on b4, Bxe5 wins the pawn back. [a3] guards it directly. Black then plays ...d5 or ...d6 and develops normally, and the b4 pawn stays a target for ...a5. Moves that ignore the pawn, like c4, Bc4 or Nc3, just let the f8 bishop take it for free. Of the two good moves, Bb2 is the more natural: the bishop stays on the long diagonal, aiming at e5 and g7. [%csl Ye5,Yb4][%cal Gc1b2,Ga2a3] }
( 2... d5
{ [e5] or [exd5] keeps White's position together. After e5, Black plays ...Bf5 and ...e6, and later ...a5 to hit the b4 pawn, which has no pawn to support it. After [exd5 cxd5], Black has the centre and the b4 pawn is loose: ...e5, ...Nf6 and ...a5 follow. Any other move leaves e4 to Black, and White is simply a pawn down with a weakened queenside. [%csl Yb4][%cal Ge4e5,Ge4d5] }
3. e5 ( 3. exd5 ) ( 3. Bb2 $6
{ The bishop takes the long diagonal, but it does nothing for e4. After [dxe4], Black is simply a pawn up, the bishop on b2 bites on Black's solid pawns, and the b4 pawn is still loose. Keep the centre together with e5 or trade on d5. [%csl Rb2,Rb4][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4 ) ( 3. f3 $2
{ The pawn props up e4, but it weakens White's king and leaves the queenside loose. [e5] hits the centre, and ...a5 against b4 follows: White's position is full of holes, and the gambit pawn on b4 is lost too. Hold the centre with e5 or exd5 instead. [%csl Rf3,Rb4][%cal Ge7e5] }
3... e5 ) ( 3. b5 $2
{ Pushing the b-pawn again ignores the centre. [dxe4] wins a pawn at once, and White's b5 pawn achieves nothing. Black should simply take: the common replies ...Nf6, ...cxb5 or ...e5 give White time to fix the centre. White's right moves were e5 or exd5. [%csl Rb5][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4 ) ) 3. Bb2
{ The bishop hits e5, and Black can let it go. [Nf6] is the best move: it develops and hits e4, and after Bxe5 Black plays ...d5 and then takes on b4. [d5] and [Bxb4] are also good, and after Bxb4 Bxe5 Nf6, Black is ahead in development. White has spent two moves with the b-pawn and the bishop, so Black should simply develop: weakening moves like ...f6 or an early queen move only help White. [%cal Gg8f6,Gd7d5,Gf8b4] }
( 3. a3 ) ( 3. c4 $2
{ The c-pawn move looks like it supports b4 from the side, but it doesn't defend it. [Bxb4] takes the pawn for free, and White's c4 has only weakened d4 and d3. Bb2 or a3 were the moves. [%csl Rd4,Rd3][%cal Gf8b4] }
3... Bxb4 ) ( 3. Bc4 $2
{ Natural development, but the b4 pawn hangs. [Bxb4] takes it, and the bishop on c4 runs into ...d5 with tempo soon after. Defend the gambit pawn first with Bb2 or a3. [%csl Rc4][%cal Gf8b4] }
3... Bxb4 ) ( 3. Nc3 $2
{ The knight develops, but the b4 pawn now has no defender, and the pin after [Bxb4] is unpleasant: Black is a pawn up and White's c3 knight is tied down. Bb2 or a3 kept the pawn. [%csl Rc3][%cal Gf8b4] }
3... Bxb4 ) 3... Nf6 ( 3... d5 ) ( 3... Bxb4 ) ( 3... f6 $6
{ The pawn guards e5, but it weakens the king's diagonal and takes f6 from the knight. After [a3], White keeps the b-pawn and follows with d4 or Nc3, and Black's position is loose. Develop with ...Nf6 or ...d5 instead. [%csl Rf6,Re8][%cal Ga2a3] }
4. a3 ) ( 3... Qf6 $2
{ The queen guards e5, but it comes out too early and takes f6 from the knight. After [Nf3], White develops and threatens the e5 pawn again, and the queen has to keep moving. Play ...Nf6 or ...d5. [%csl Rf6,Re5][%cal Gg1f3] }
4. Nf3 ) ) 2... d5
{ The starting point of the main lines. Black's pawn hits e4, and White must decide what to do with it. [e5] gains space and closes the centre: the Advance, White's most popular try today. [exd5] trades and opens the e-file: the Exchange, and with [c4] later, the Panov Attack. [Nc3] or [Nd2] defends with a piece and lets Black take, [b:dxe4], after which the knight recaptures and Black develops with gain of time: the Classical and Karpov lines. [f3], the Fantasy, props the pawn up so that a trade on e4 is met by fxe4 and a big centre. [Bd3] defends with the bishop, but after ...dxe4 Bxe4 Nf6 it has to move again. Moves that ignore the threat, like [Nf3], just give Black a pawn. [%cal Ge4e5,Ge4d5,Gb1c3,Gb1d2] }
( 2... Nf6
{ The Masi. Black attacks e4 with the knight instead of a pawn. White can push [e5], chasing the knight to d5; after c4 it goes on to c7 or b6, an Alekhine-like position where White has space and Black a solid but cramped game. [Nc3] simply defends, and after ...d5 e5 the knight is kicked anyway. Black is a little worse in these lines, but nothing is lost: ...d6 and ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 hit back at the advanced pawn. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge4e5,Gb1c3] }
3. e5 ( 3. Nc3 ) ) ( 2... f5 $2
{ Black tries to stop e4 from ever being comfortable, but this just gives a pawn. After [exf5], the queen can check on a5 and take back on f5 only at the cost of more queen moves, while White develops with Nc3, Nf3 and Bd3, and Black's king is left with holes on e6 and g6. White's natural e5 and f3 let Black off; the capture is the answer. Play ...d5, the Caro-Kann move. [%csl Re6,Rg6][%cal Ge4f5] }
3. exf5 ) 3. e5
{ The Advance. White grabs space and takes f6 away from Black's king's knight, betting that the pawn on e5 will cramp Black for the whole game. Black's answer is to develop the light bishop at once with [Bf5], before ...e6 closes its diagonal. Then the usual plan is ...e6, ...c5 and ...Nc6, with ...Qb6 against d4 and b2, while the king's knight goes to e7 and then f5 or g6: an Advance French in which Black's problem bishop is already outside the chain. [c5] at once, the Botvinnik-Carls line, hits the base of the chain right away. [b:e6] transposes to a French in which the c8 bishop stays locked in. [%cal Gc8f5] }
( 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6
{ [Nf3] is the main move, defending d4 and preparing Bd3 or Be2. [Bg5] pins the knight on f6 and adds pressure on d5, since that knight is its defender. Black's replies: against Nf3, the active ...Bg4 or the solid ...e6; against Bg5, ...e6, ...Be6 or the sharp ...dxc4. Black's knight on c6 hits d4 and makes the isolated pawn a target from the start. [%csl Yd4,Yd5][%cal Gg1f3,Gc1g5] }
6. Nf3
{ [Bg4] is the active line, pinning the knight that guards d4. White's best answer is cxd5 Nxd5 and then Qb3, a long forcing line that ends in a level endgame. [e6] is quieter: Black develops with ...Be7 and castling, and White chooses between c5, cxd5 and Bd3, with typical isolated-pawn play. Both moves are good; the choice is about whether Black wants a forcing line or a slower game. [%csl Yd4,Yf3][%cal Gc8g4,Ge7e6] }
( 6. Bg5
{ White pins the f6 knight, the main defender of d5. [e6] is solid: Black reaches isolated-pawn positions with ...Be7, and White must spend a move on Nf3. [Be6] is a sturdy alternative, defending d5 with a piece and keeping the e-pawn flexible. [dxc4] is the sharp line: after [dxc4 d5 Na5], White must find the right way to win back the pawn, while Black's knight on the rim has tricks of its own. In the quiet lines, ...Be7, ...O-O and pressure on d4 give Black a solid game. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge7e6,Gd5c4,Gc8e6] }
6... e6
{ Black keeps d5 solid and frees the f8 bishop. White develops with [Nf3], then Bd3 or Be2 and castling, and plays c5 or cxd5 when it suits; Black answers ...Be7 and ...O-O with a typical isolated-pawn position, where the pin on f6 is broken by ...Be7 or ...h6. [c5] at once grabs space on the queenside, met by ...h6 and ...Be7, then ...e5 or ...b6 to break the chain. Black's aims: blockade d5, trade pieces and target the d4 pawn. [%csl Yd5,Yf6][%cal Gg1f3,Gc4c5] }
( 6... dxc4
{ Black has taken a pawn and given up the fight for d5. [d5] kicks the knight before Black castles, and Black has to choose where it goes: a5 and e5 keep it active, while passive retreats lose ground. [Bxc4] wins the pawn back calmly, reaching an isolated-pawn position in which Black's pieces are more active than usual. In both lines White's lead in development matters, so Black must hurry to castle. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gd4d5,Gf1c4] }
7. d5
{ Keep the knight active: [Na5] defends the c4 pawn and keeps the extra pawn for a while, though it stands on the rim; [Ne5] centralises it, and White will hit it with Qd4 or f4. Retreating to b8 is too passive, and b4 walks into a queen check. White's plan is to win back the c4 pawn and use the passed pawn on d5, so Black must develop fast with ...g6 or ...e6 and castle. [%csl Yc4,Yd5][%cal Gc6a5,Gc6e5] }
( 7. Bxc4 ) 7... Na5
{ White wants the c4 pawn back with interest. [b4] attacks the knight on the rim: if Black takes en passant, the a-file opens and White's pieces come into play. [Qd4] hits a7, c4 and the pinned knight on f6 while staying central. Black's best chances lie in quick development with ...g6, ...Bg7 and castling, keeping the extra pawn as long as possible. White should keep the bishop on g5 for now: giving it up on f6 releases the pin. [%csl Yc4,Yf6][%cal Gb2b4,Gd1d4] }
( 7... Ne5 ) ( 7... Nb4 $4
{ The knight looks for an active square, but on b4 it has no defender. [Qa4+] checks along the open diagonal and hits the knight at the same time: White wins a piece. Keep the knight active on a5 or e5. [%csl Rb4,Re8][%cal Gd1a4] }
8. Qa4+ ) ( 7... Nb8 $2
{ A safe-looking retreat, but far too passive. After [Bxc4], White has the pawn back, a passed pawn on d5 and a big lead in development. Keep the knight in play on a5 or e5. [%csl Rb8,Rd5][%cal Gf1c4] }
8. Bxc4 ) 8. b4 ( 8. Qd4 ) ( 8. Bxf6 $2
{ Taking the knight looks like it wins time against d5, but it gives up the pin and the bishop. After [exf6], Black's pieces come out quickly, the f8 bishop gets a fine diagonal, and Black stays a pawn up on c4. White should go for the c4 pawn with b4 or Qd4. [%csl Rc4][%cal Ge7f6] }
8... exf6 ) ) ( 6... Be6 ) 7. Nf3 ( 7. c5 ) ) 6... Bg4
{ White's best is [cxd5], and only after ...Nxd5, Qb3. It leads to a well-known forcing line: [cxd5 Nxd5 Qb3 Bxf3 gxf3 e6 Qxb7 Nxd4 Bb5+] with an endgame that is close to equal. Be2 is a calmer choice: Black then takes on c4 or plays ...e6. Black's light bishop should not drop back if White plays h3: taking on f3 removes a defender of d4 or damages White's pawns. [%csl Yd4,Yf3][%cal Gc4d5] }
( 6... e6
{ A key position of the Panov, reached by several move orders. White has three plans. [cxd5] gives Black a choice: ...Nxd5 keeps the isolated-pawn game, ...exd5 gives a symmetrical structure where Bb5 pins the knight and Ne5 follows. [c5] grabs space and fixes the queenside: Black hits back with ...e5 or ...b6, and White wants Bb5 to control e5. [Bd3] keeps the tension and invites ...dxc4 Bxc4, a classic isolated-pawn position. Black's light bishop goes to d7 or b7, or to g4 when White's knight is pinned. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc4d5,Gc4c5,Gf1d3] }
7. cxd5
{ Black chooses the structure. [Nxd5] is the most common: the knight blockades the isolated pawn, and Black plays ...Be7, ...O-O and ...Bf6 against d4, while White builds up with Bd3, O-O and Re1. [exd5] keeps the knight on f6 and gives a symmetrical pawn structure: then Bb5 pins the c6 knight and Ne5 hits it, so Black has to be precise with ...Bd7 and ...Bd6. Both are sound; the knight recapture gives a more active game. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gf6d5,Ge6d5] }
( 7. c5
{ White closes the queenside and gives up the isolated pawn for space: the c5 pawn takes d6 and b6 from Black's pieces, and Bb5 with Ne5 can follow. Black must hit back before the bind is complete. [Be7] and castling prepare ...b6 or ...e5, the two breaks that undermine the chain; [Ne4] hits c3 and makes room for ...f5 or ...e5. If Black achieves ...e5, White's centre falls apart and Black's pieces become very active. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gf8e7,Gf6e4,Gb7b6] }
7... Be7 ( 7... Ne4 ) ( 7... b6 ) ) ( 7. Bd3 ) 7... Nxd5 ( 7... exd5
{ A symmetrical structure: both sides have an isolated-looking d-pawn, but Black's is not isolated, since the pawns on d5 and d4 face each other on an open e-file and a half-open c-file. White's main idea is [Bb5], pinning the knight on c6 and preparing Ne5 to hit it again; [Be2] and [Bd3] are calmer. Black answers Bb5 with ...Bd6 and ...O-O, or ...Bd7, and gets enough activity: the bishops come to d6 and g4, and ...Qc7 or ...Re8 use the open e-file. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gf1b5,Gf1e2,Gf1d3] }
8. Bb5
{ The bishop pins the knight on c6, and Ne5 next would add a second attacker. [Bd6] develops and gets ready to castle, so that after Ne5 the king is out of the pin; [Bd7] breaks the pin at once. Black often allows Bxc6 bxc6: the doubled pawns are compensated by the half-open b-file and the bishop pair. Then ...O-O, ...Bg4 and ...Qc7 or ...Re8 give Black active play. Avoid long thinking with the king in the centre: castle early. [%csl Yc6,Ye5][%cal Gf8d6,Gc8d7] }
( 8. Be2 ) ( 8. Bd3 ) 8... Bd6 ( 8... Bd7 ) ) ) 7. cxd5
{ Take back with [Nxd5]: the knight lands on the blockading square in front of the d4 pawn and hits the c3 knight. White's most testing reply is Qb3, hitting b7 and the knight, after which Black must know the forcing ...Bxf3 line. Other moves give Black an easy game with ...e6 and pressure on d4. [%csl Yc3,Yd4][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 7. Qb3 $2
{ The queen hits b7 and d5 as in other lines, but here it comes one move too early. [Bxf3] removes the knight that guards d4, and after gxf3 Black can even take on d4, with White's pawns broken. Take on d5 first, and only then play Qb3. [%csl Rd4,Rf3][%cal Gg4f3] }
7... Bxf3 ) 7... Nxd5
{ [Qb3] hits b7 and the knight on d5. Black answers ...Bxf3, removing the knight that guards d4, and after gxf3, ...e6 guards the d5 knight; after Qxb7 Nxd4 the play gets sharp but stays balanced. Calmer moves like Be2 or Bb5 give an ordinary isolated-pawn game. After gxf3, White's structure is damaged, but the bishop pair and open lines make up for it. [%csl Yb7,Yd5][%cal Gd1b3] }
8. Qb3
{ [Bxf3] removes the knight before Black's pieces get tied up, then ...e6. After [Bxf3 gxf3 e6 Qxb7 Nxd4 Bb5+ Nxb5 Qc6+ Ke7 Qxb5], the material is level, the queens usually come off soon, and Black's king is fine in the middle. This forcing line is known to be about equal, so it's a good choice for a well-prepared Black player. Without the trade on f3, White keeps pressure on b7 and d5 for free. [%csl Yd4,Yb7][%cal Gg4f3] }
8... Bxf3 ) ( 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ( 4... Nd7
{ Black prepares ...Ngf6: if White trades on f6, the d7 knight takes back and Black's pawns stay intact. It's slower than ...Bf5, and the c8 bishop has to wait, so White gets chances: [Bc4] and [Ng5] aim at f7 and e6, in lines where Black must know a few traps. [Nf3] is the solid main move, after which Black reaches a sound position with ...Ngf6 and then ...e6 or ...Bf5. [Bd3] usually transposes to the Ng5 lines, and [Qe2] sets a trap: after it, ...Ngf6?? walks into a famous mate. Karpov played this line for decades, hence its name. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1c4,Ge4g5] }
5. Nf3
{ [Ngf6] challenges the knight on e4: a trade on f6 is met by ...Nxf6, and Black's pawns stay healthy. If White keeps the knight with Ng3, Black plays ...e6 and ...c5, hitting d4. The bishop comes out to f5 or g4 once White's plans are clear. The strength of 4...Nd7 is its flexibility: no weaknesses, and a choice of setups. [%csl Ye4,Yd4][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 5. Bc4
{ [Ngf6] develops and controls e4, g4 and d5. White's most direct try is Ng5, aiming at f7 with bishop and knight together: then ...e6 is forced, and after Qe2 Black must find ...Nb6. Watch the f7 square: the bishop on c4 and a knight on g5 can mate if Black's king has no air and f7 has no defender. [%csl Yc4,Yf7][%cal Gg8f6] }
5... Ngf6
{ [Ng5] aims at f7 together with the bishop on c4. Now Black must play ...e6, blocking the bishop's diagonal; kicking the knight with ...h6 at once is a famous mate in one. [Nxf6+] is calmer: after ...Nxf6, Black develops with ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 and ...e6. The Ng5 line is full of traps, so Black needs to know the order: ...e6, then ...Nb6 after Qe2. [%csl Yf7][%cal Ge4g5,Ge4f6] }
6. Ng5
{ Play [e6], closing the bishop's diagonal toward f7. White goes on with Qe2, adding the queen against e6, and Black's precise answer is ...Nb6. [Nd5] is also possible, blocking the bishop's line from c4. The one thing not to do is kick the knight with ...h6 at once: the bishop takes on f7 and it's mate, since Black's own pieces block d7 and e7. [%csl Yf7][%cal Ge7e6,Gf6d5] }
( 6. Nxf6+ ) 6... e6
{ [Qe2] adds the queen to the attack on e6 and f7. Now Nxf7 is a real threat: after ...Kxf7, Qxe6+ drives the king into the open. This is the critical test of 4...Nd7, and Black has one good answer, ...Nb6; most other natural moves lose. White's other moves are weaker: N1f3 or Bd3 let Black kick the knight with ...h6 before the queen arrives. [%csl Ye6,Yf7][%cal Gd1e2] }
( 6... Nd5 ) ( 6... h6 $4
{ Kicking the knight is the natural reflex, and it walks into [Bxf7#]. The bishop is guarded by the g5 knight, and Black's king has no square: d7 and e7 are taken by Black's own pieces. Close the diagonal with ...e6 first, and only then kick the knight. [%csl Re8][%cal Gc4f7] }
7. Bxf7# ) 7. Qe2
