( 7. N1f3 $6
{ Developing the other knight is natural, but it lets Black kick the g5 knight with [h6] before White's queen reaches e2, and the Ng5 idea loses its point: after Nh3 or Ne4, Black has a comfortable game. Qe2 first is what makes the line dangerous. [%csl Rg5][%cal Gh7h6] }
7... h6 ) 7... Nb6
{ The bishop has to move: [Bd3] or [Bb3]. After Bd3, ...h6 kicks the knight back to f3, and Black follows with ...c5 and ...Qc7 with a solid game. After Bb3, ...h6 or ...a5 chase the bishop and the knight together. Leaving the bishop on c4 drops it to ...Nxc4. The main line after Bd3 h6 N5f3 c5 is one of the most played positions of the Karpov. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gc4d3,Gc4b3] }
( 7... Be7 $4
{ Developing and getting ready to castle looks sensible, but e6 has only one defender. [Nxf7 Kxf7 Qxe6+] drives the king out, and the attack goes on with Bd3+ and Qh3: Black is lost. With White's queen on e2, first cover e6 with ...Nb6, which also opens the c8 bishop's diagonal. [%csl Re6,Re8][%cal Gg5f7] }
8. Nxf7 Kxf7 9. Qxe6+ ) ( 7... h6 $4
{ Kicking the knight now is too late: [Nxf7] breaks in, and after ...Kxf7 Qxe6+ the king is hunted. Even declining the knight leaves Black a pawn down with a broken position. Play ...Nb6 first, then ...h6. [%csl Re6,Rf7][%cal Gg5f7] }
8. Nxf7 ) 8. Bd3
{ Having nudged White's bishop off the a2-g8 diagonal with [Nb6], Black must question the intrusive g5-knight at once with [h6]. Any sacrifice on f7 or e6 fails flatly now because the bishop on d3 no longer eyes f7 and the c8-bishop still guards e6. After [h6 N5f3], and Black strikes at the centre with [b:c5] ([w:dxc5] [b:Bxc5]), followed by [b:Qc7] (or [b:Nbd5]), [b:Bd7] and short castling, reaching a rock-solid Karpov Caro-Kann middlegame where White's slight space edge is balanced by Black's sturdy structure. [%cal Gh7h6] }
( 8. Bb3
{ [h6] kicks the knight, which goes back to f3; then ...c5, ...Bd7 or ...a5 and ...a4 against the bishop. [a5] at once is also played, threatening ...a4 to chase the bishop on b3: White answers a4 or a3. Grabbing the d4 pawn is greedy: White's knights and queen are already aimed at f7 and e6. Black should first finish development: ...h6, ...Be7 or ...c5 and castling. [%csl Yg5,Yb3][%cal Gh7h6,Ga7a5] }
8... h6 ( 8... a5 ) ( 8... Qxd4 $2
{ Grabbing the centre pawn looks free, but it costs Black's development. After [N1f3], the queen is chased with Ne5 or Be3 next, and f7 is still weak: Black falls far behind. Finish development first with ...h6, ...a5 or ...c5. [%csl Rd4,Rf7][%cal Gg1f3] }
9. N1f3 ) ) ( 8. N1f3 $2
{ Bringing out the last knight looks natural, but the bishop on c4 is still attacked. After [h6], White must deal with the knight on g5 and the bishop at once, and loses either time or the bishop pair. Move the bishop first, to d3 or b3. [%csl Rc4,Rg5][%cal Gh7h6] }
8... h6 ) 8... h6
{ [N5f3] goes back to its natural square. After it, Black plays ...c5, ...Bd7 or ...Qc7, and the game is level. White's plans are Bf4 with long castling, or dxc5 to free d4 for a knight. Black's light bishop goes to d7, and the knight on b6 can come to d5 once the c-pawn has moved. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gg5f3,Gc6c5] }
( 8... Qxd4 $2
{ Grabbing the pawn opens lines while Black is behind in development. After [N1f3], the queen is hit and has to move again, and Ne5 with f4 follows, aiming everything at f7 and e6 while Black's king is still in the centre. Kick the g5 knight first with ...h6, the main move. [%csl Rd4,Rf7][%cal Gg1f3] }
9. N1f3 ) 9. N5f3 ) ( 5. Ng5 ) ( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. Qe2
{ White sets a well-known trap: ...Ngf6?? Nd6 is mate, since the e7 pawn is pinned to the king by the queen. Play [Ndf6], hitting the knight on e4 with the other knight, or [e6], which blocks the e-file before developing. After ...Ndf6, if White trades on f6, the g8 knight takes back, and the bishop comes out to g4 or f5. The queen on e2 blocks White's light bishop: the queen move is a cheap trick, not a plan. [%csl Yd6,Ye2][%cal Gd7f6,Ge7e6] }
5... Ndf6 ( 5... e6 ) ( 5... Ngf6 $4
{ The natural developing move, and the most famous trap of the Caro-Kann: [Nd6#]. The e7 pawn is pinned by the queen on e2, so it can't take the knight, and the king has no square: d7 and e7 are blocked by Black's own pieces. Play ...Ndf6 or ...e6. [%csl Re8,Re7][%cal Ge4d6] }
6. Nd6# ) ) 5... Ngf6
{ White decides about the knight. [Nxf6+] trades, and Black recaptures with the knight from d7, keeping a healthy structure; then the c8 bishop comes out to f5 or g4. [Ng3] keeps pieces and space: Black answers ...e6 and ...c5, hitting d4 before White finishes development. Both are fine for Black. Jumping to g5 with the e4 knight only invites ...h6 and a retreat. [%csl Yf6][%cal Ge4g3,Ge4f6] }
6. Ng3
{ [e6] and then ...c5: Black hits d4 before White finishes development, and the knight on g3 isn't helping in the centre. ...Be7 or ...Bd6 and castling follow. [c5] at once is also fine. After Bd3 c5, Black often takes on d4 and develops with ...Bc5 or ...Be7. The c8 bishop can stay home a little longer, going to d7, or to b7 after ...b6. [%csl Yd4,Yg3][%cal Ge7e6,Gc6c5] }
( 6. Nxf6+
{ Take back with [Nxf6]: no doubled pawns. The knight on f6 guards d5 and h7, and the c8 bishop now has free squares on f5 and g4. Taking with a pawn is playable but throws away the main idea of 4...Nd7. White usually goes on with Ne5, c3 or Bc4, aiming at f7 and the e-file, while Black plays ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 and ...e6: a solid position, with a little less space. [%cal Gd7f6,Gc8f5,Gc8g4] }
6... Nxf6
{ White plays [Bc4], [Ne5] or [c3]. Against Bc4, develop the bishop to f5 before ...e6, so it isn't shut in. Against Ne5, ...Be6 is good: the bishop is safe there and controls d5 and c4. Against c3, Black chooses between ...Bg4 and ...Bf5. Black's structure is sound; the plan is to finish development with ...e6, ...Bd6 or ...Be7, castle and break with ...c5. [%csl Yf5][%cal Gf1c4,Gf3e5,Gc2c3] }
7. Bc4
{ [Bf5]! Develop the bishop before ...e6, so it isn't shut in. After O-O e6, Black plays ...Be7 or ...Bd6 and castles, with a sound position. If White plays Nh4 to chase the bishop, ...Bg6 is fine, and Nxg6 hxg6 opens the h-file for Black's rook. White's bishop on c4 looks at e6 and f7, so ...e6 should come soon after the bishop is out, to close that diagonal. [%csl Yc4,Yf7][%cal Gc8f5,Ge7e6] }
( 7. Ne5
{ [Be6]! The bishop is safe on e6 and covers d5 and c4, so White's light bishop has no easy good square. Then ...g6, ...Bg7 and castling, or ...Nd7 to trade the strong e5 knight. The knight on e5 looks active but has no targets: Black's pawns on f7 and c6 are well covered. White usually plays c3, Be2 and O-O, and the position is balanced. [%csl Ye5,Yc4][%cal Gc8e6] }
7... Be6 ) ( 7. c3 ) 7... Bf5 ) ( 6. Neg5 $6
{ The knight eyes f7 and e6, but nothing supports it there yet. After [h6], it has to go back, and Black is ready for ...e6 and ...Bd6 or ...c5 with an easy game. Trade on f6 or keep the knight with Ng3. [%csl Rg5][%cal Gh7h6] }
6... h6 ) 6... e6 ( 6... c5 ) ) 5. Ng3 Bg6
{ [h4] is the main line: White threatens h5, when the bishop would have to leave the b1-h7 diagonal. Black answers ...h6 so that the bishop can go to h7; then Nf3, ...Nd7 and h5 lead to the main position. [Nf3] develops first and keeps h4 for later. [N1e2], [Nh3] and [f4] aim a knight or a pawn at the bishop: Nf4 or f5 can drive it away. [Bc4] develops and aims at f7 and e6. Against all of them, the plan is ...e6, ...Nd7, ...Ngf6, ...Bd6 or ...Be7 and castling; the bishop needs watching only when a knight lands on f4 or h5. [%csl Yg6][%cal Gh2h4,Gg1f3,Gg1e2] }
6. h4
{ [h6] gives the bishop h7: now h5 just sends it there, and it keeps watching c2 and b1. The pawn on h6 also takes g5 away from White's pieces. It's the only good move: the others either leave the bishop without a retreat or weaken Black's kingside for good. After ...h6, White plays Nf3 and h5 (or h5 first), and Black follows with ...Nd7, ...e6, ...Ngf6 and ...Qc7. [%csl Yh7,Yg6][%cal Gh7h6] }
( 6. Nf3
{ [Nd7] covers e5, so White's knight can't jump there with tempo on the bishop. After h4 h6, the game reaches the main line. Against Nh4, Black plays ...e6, and if the knight takes on g6, ...hxg6 opens the h-file for Black's rook. Black's setup is the usual ...Ngf6, ...e6, ...Bd6 or ...Be7 and castling. [%csl Ye5,Yg6][%cal Gb8d7] }
6... Nd7 ) ( 6. N1e2
{ White aims Nf4 at the bishop on g6. Answer with [e6] or [Nf6], and then ...Bd6 so that a knight landing on f4 can be traded. If White takes on g6 with a knight, Black recaptures ...hxg6, and the h-file opens for Black's rook. Black's plan: ...Nd7, ...Ngf6, ...Bd6 and castling; White's plan: Nf4, h4-h5 and Bd3 or Bc4. [%csl Yf4,Yg6][%cal Ge7e6,Gg8f6] }
6... e6 ( 6... Nf6 ) ) ( 6. Nh3
{ The knight heads for f4, where it hits the bishop on g6. [e6] and ...Bd6 prepare for it: when the knight lands on f4, Black can trade it with ...Bxf4. [Nd7] is also fine. Black shouldn't waste time with ...h6, which only lets the knight come to f4 with tempo. If White trades on g6, ...hxg6 opens the h-file. White's plan is Nf4, h4-h5 and Bc4 or Bd3. [%csl Yf4,Yg6][%cal Ge7e6,Gb8d7] }
6... e6 ( 6... Nd7 ) ( 6... h6 $2
{ A useful move against h4, but here it is a lost tempo: the knight is already on its way to f4. [Nf4] hits the bishop at once, and h4-h5 can follow with gain of time. Play ...e6 and ...Bd6 to meet the knight. [%csl Rg6,Rh6][%cal Gh3f4] }
7. Nf4 ) ) ( 6. f4
{ White threatens f5, trapping the bishop on g6 or shutting it in on h7. Answer with [e6]: now f5 can be met by ...exf5, and the bishop is safe. Black then plays ...Nd7, ...Ngf6, ...Bd6 and castles, and the f4 pawn has weakened White's kingside and the e4 square. Moves that ignore the threat, like ...h6, let f5 bury the bishop. [%csl Yf5,Ye4][%cal Ge7e6] }
6... e6 ( 6... h6 $2
{ Making room on h7 looks wise, but it ignores the threat of f5. White develops with [Nf3], and f5 is still in the air, so Black has spent a move on a pawn that does nothing. If White pushes f5 at once, the bishop drops to h7 behind White's pawns. Play ...e6 first, so f5 can be taken. [%csl Rf5,Rh7][%cal Gg1f3] }
7. Nf3 ) ) ( 6. Bc4
{ [e6] closes the diagonal from c4 to f7 and frees the f8 bishop. Then ...Nf6, ...Nd7 and ...Bd6. White often plays N1e2-f4 to hit the bishop on g6, or h4-h5 as in the main line. Black's knight on d7 can later go to b6 with tempo on the c4 bishop. The setup is solid: the bishop on c4 has nothing to bite on once the pawn is on e6. [%csl Yc4,Yf7][%cal Ge7e6] }
6... e6 ) 6... h6
{ [Nf3] develops first and keeps the h-pawn's push for later; [h5] at once fixes Black's kingside pawns and sends the bishop to h7 right away. White's knight on f3 is heading for e5, so Black usually answers with ...Nd7, covering that square. With h5, White gains space, but the pawn can be lost in an endgame, so White usually keeps queens on and castles long. [%csl Ye5,Yh7][%cal Gg1f3,Gh4h5] }
( 6... h5 $6
{ This stops h5 for good, but it leaves g5 and g6 weak. White plays [Nh3] and Nf4 to hit the bishop and the h5 pawn, or Bg5 to take the square the pawn left. Black's kingside stays weaker for the rest of the game. ...h6 does the same job without the holes. [%csl Rg5,Rg6][%cal Gg1h3] }
7. Nh3 ) ( 6... Nd7 $2
{ Developing first seems natural, but now [h5] hits the bishop, which has no h7 square: Black's own pawn is still there. It must go to f5, where Nxf5 takes it, and White gets the bishop pair. Play ...h6 first, then ...Nd7. [%csl Rg6,Rh7][%cal Gh4h5] }
7. h5 ) ( 6... e6 $2
{ The same problem: after [h5], the bishop has to go to f5, since h7 is blocked by Black's pawn, and Nxf5 exf5 takes it, leaving Black with doubled pawns and no light bishop. Play ...h6 first. [%csl Rg6,Rh7][%cal Gh4h5] }
7. h5 ) 7. Nf3
{ [Nd7] is the most accurate: it covers e5, so Ne5 can be met by ...Nxe5, and prepares ...Ngf6. [e6] and [Nf6] are playable, but after Ne5 the knight is annoying, and Bc4 with Qe2 aims at f7 and e6. White's next move is usually h5, fixing the pawns and sending the bishop to h7, then Bd3 to trade it off. Black follows with ...e6, ...Ngf6, ...Qc7 and castling. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gb8d7,Ge7e6,Gg8f6] }
( 7. h5 ) 7... Nd7
{ [h5] gains space and forces the bishop to h7. The pawn cramps Black's kingside and fixes g6 as a hole, but it can't be guarded by another pawn: in an endgame it is often lost. White's plan is Bd3 to trade the light bishops, then Bd2 or Bf4 and long castling. Black continues ...Bh7, ...e6, ...Ngf6, ...Qc7 and chooses which side to castle on. [%csl Yh7,Yg6][%cal Gh4h5] }
( 7... e6 ) ( 7... Nf6 ) 8. h5
{ Retreat with [Bh7]. Thanks to ...h6, the bishop still has a square on the b1-h7 diagonal, where it fights for c2 and e4. Without ...h6 earlier, h5 would have trapped the bishop on g6: that is why Black spent a move on it. White will usually offer to trade it with Bd3 at once, and Black accepts: it removes White's best attacking piece if White castles short, and Black's position has no bad bishop left. [%csl Yh7][%cal Gg6h7,Gf1d3] }
8... Bh7
{ [Bd3] offers to trade bishops: it's White's most natural plan, since the bishop on h7 would otherwise stare at White's king after long castling. After ...Bxd3 Qxd3, the queen is well placed on d3, covering e4 and f5 and eyeing g6. Other moves like Bc4 or Bf4 are possible, but then Black's bishop stays strong on its diagonal. [%csl Yh7,Ye4][%cal Gf1d3] }
9. Bd3
{ Take with [Bxd3]. The trade is fine for Black: the light bishop has done its job, and without it White's attack has one piece fewer. Retreating it wastes time and leaves White's bishop the better one. After Qxd3, Black continues ...e6 and ...Ngf6, then ...Be7 or ...Bd6 and ...Qc7. The pawn on h5 gives White space on the kingside, so Black thinks twice before castling short. [%csl Yd3][%cal Gh7d3,Ge7e6] }
9... Bxd3
{ Take back with [Qxd3]: the queen lands on a good square, covering e4 and the light squares the bishop left, and ready to go to e2 later. Taking with the pawn would spoil White's structure for nothing. Black continues ...e6, ...Ngf6 and ...Qc7, and White chooses between Bd2 and Bf4 with long castling. [%csl Ye4,Yg6][%cal Gd1d3] }
10. Qxd3
{ The main line of the Classical. White has space on the kingside, and the h5 pawn cramps Black's pawns there, but it can also become a target in an endgame. Black plays [e6], then ...Ngf6, ...Qc7 and ...Be7 or ...Bd6; [Ngf6] first and [Qc7] first are just as good. Both sides often castle long. Black's freeing break is ...c5, and ...Qa5 or ...Nd5 can trade White's active pieces. The queens' trade usually helps Black, as the h5 pawn then gets weak. [%csl Yh5][%cal Ge7e6,Gd8c7,Gg8f6] }
10... e6
{ White prepares long castling with [Bd2] or [Bf4]. [Bf4] takes the b8-h2 diagonal, stopping ...Bd6, and Black often answers ...Qa5+ to provoke a small concession. [Bd2] is quieter and keeps the bishop on the c1-h6 diagonal, where it can help a kingside attack. Black's setup: ...Ngf6, ...Qc7 or ...Be7, castling, and the ...c5 break. If White castles long and Black short, it's a race: White pushes Ne4 and g4, Black plays ...c5 and ...Qa5. [%cal Gc1d2,Gc1f4,Gg8f6] }
( 10... Qc7 ) ( 10... Ngf6
{ Black develops and keeps ...e6 for the next move. White plays [Bd2] or [Bf4], preparing long castling, and after ...e6 the game transposes to the main lines. White can also try Ne4, offering a knight trade that leaves Black's pieces a bit passive. Black's setup stays the same: ...e6, ...Qc7, ...Be7 or ...Bd6, castling, and ...c5 once White's king has chosen its side. [%cal Gc1d2,Gc1f4,Ge7e6] }
11. Bd2
{ [e6] transposes to the main line, which is what Black wants: the pawn frees the f8 bishop and keeps the knight on f6 solid. Then ...Qc7, ...Be7 or ...Bd6 and castling. White will castle long and choose between Ne4 to trade a defender and a kingside push with g4. Black's plan stays ...c5 to open the centre against White's king, and trading queens when possible, since the h5 pawn gets weak. [%csl Yc5,Yh5][%cal Ge7e6,Gd8c7] }
( 11. Bf4 ) 11... e6 ) 11. Bd2
{ Develop with [Ngf6]; [Qc7] also comes soon, and keeping both castling options open is part of the idea. White plans O-O-O, Ne4 to trade a defender, Qe2 and Kb1, and later a kingside push with g4-g5 if Black castles short. Black's answers are ...Be7 or ...Bd6, castling on the side White's attack isn't aimed at, and ...c5 to open the centre against White's king. In many endgames the h5 pawn falls, so Black keeps an eye on it. [%csl Ye4,Yh5][%cal Gg8f6,Gd8c7] }
( 11. Bf4
{ [Qa5+] provokes a concession: after Bd2, ...Bb4 asks for c3, and the bishop drops back to e7, leaving White with a pawn on c3 and a bishop that has spent an extra move. [Ngf6] is the simple alternative, met by O-O-O, and then ...Be7 or ...Qa5. Black's plan in both lines: ...Ngf6, ...Be7, castling, often long to stay away from White's kingside pawns, and ...c5. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gd8a5,Gg8f6] }
11... Qa5+
{ [Bd2] is the main move, and ...Bb4 asks for c3; then the queen goes back to c7 and the bishop to e7, and White has spent two moves to get the bishop to d2 and a pawn to c3. [c3] at once is also played: it blocks the check and keeps the bishop on f4, at the cost of a slightly looser queenside. Either way, Black has gained a small concession and continues ...Ngf6, ...Be7 and castling. [%csl Ya5][%cal Gf4d2,Gc2c3] }
( 11... Ngf6 ) 12. Bd2 ( 12. c3 ) ) 11... Ngf6
{ [O-O-O] is the main move: White's king goes behind the queenside pawns, and the rooks come to the centre. White's plans: Ne4 to trade a defender of Black's kingside, Qe2, Kb1, and a pawn storm with g4-g5 if Black castles short. Other moves like Qe2 or c4 are possible, but they give Black time for ...c5. Black now chooses the king's home: ...Be7 and ...O-O, or ...Qc7 and ...O-O-O. [%cal Ge1c1,Gf8e7,Gd8c7] }
( 11... Qc7 ) 12. O-O-O
{ Black chooses a home for the king. [Be7] with short castling: White attacks with Ne4, g4 and g5, while Black opens the centre with ...c5 and brings the queen to a5 against White's king. [Qc7] with long castling is the safer choice: with the kings on the same side, Black's plan is ...c5 or ...Nd5, and White has fewer attacking chances. Either way the h5 pawn can become weak once queens are traded, so Black welcomes simplification. [%csl Yh5][%cal Gf8e7,Gd8c7,Gf8d6] }
12... Be7
{ Black has chosen short castling, so the kings will be on opposite sides and both attack. White's main plans are [Kb1], a safe king first, then Ne4 to trade Black's best defender, and g4-g5 to open lines; [Ne4] at once is also played, as is [Qe2]. Black castles next and plays ...c5, ...Qa5 or ...Qc7, and sometimes ...b5, attacking White's king with the queenside pawns. In such races, speed matters more than a pawn. [%csl Yc5][%cal Gc1b1,Gg3e4,Gd3e2] }
( 12... Qc7 ) ( 12... Bd6 ) 13. Kb1 ( 13. Ne4 ) ( 13. Qe2 ) ) ( 3. Nd2
{ The same idea as 3.Nc3: after [dxe4 Nxe4] the game reaches exactly the same positions, with the Classical, the Karpov and the 4...Nf6 lines. The knight on d2 leaves the c-pawn free, so against setups that keep the pawn on d5, such as ...g6, White can support d4 with c3. That is why some players prefer 3.Nd2. Black's main answer stays [dxe4]: the knight recaptures, and Black's next move hits it. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4
{ Take back with [Nxe4]. The knight lands in the centre, where Black's next move will hit it or prepare to: ...Bf5, ...Nd7 or ...Nf6, the same three main lines as after 3.Nc3. From here the two move orders are the same opening. White picks 3.Nd2 to keep the c-pawn free for c3, which takes the sting out of sidelines such as ...g6. Any move other than the recapture leaves White a pawn down. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd2e4] }
( 3... Nf6 $2
{ Developing the knight before trading on e4 walks straight into [e5], kicking the knight to a passive square on d7 or back to g8 while locking Black's light-squared bishop inside the chain after [e6]. Take on e4 first with [b:dxe4]. [%cal Re4e5] }
4. e5 Nfd7 5. Bd3 ) 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3
Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Ngf6 11. Bd2 e6 12. O-O-O Bd6
{ The Seirawan Variation. Black posts the bishop aggressively on d6 to force a decision on the g3 knight before castling. Trading knights is White's sharpest try: after [Ne4 Nxe4 Qxe4] Black continues with [b:Nf6] and [b:Qd5], welcoming an endgame where the advanced h5 pawn can turn weak. [Kb1] tucks the king away first while keeping the tension, and [Rhe1] brings the rook into the centre, eyeing e6 in case Black castles short. Black answers with [b:Qc7] and long castling for safety, or [b:O-O] if ready to brave a kingside pawn storm. [%csl Yd6,Yh5][%cal Gg3e4,Gc1b1] }
13. Ne4 ( 13. Kb1 ) ( 13. Rhe1 ) ) ( 3. f3
{ The Fantasy. White props up e4 with a pawn, so that after ...dxe4 fxe4 White has a full centre and the half-open f-file. The cost: f3 takes f3 from the knight and weakens the dark squares around White's king, especially the a7-g1 and e1-h4 diagonals. [e6] is the solid answer, preparing ...c5 and ...Bb4 against the dark squares. [dxe4] followed by ...e5 is the sharp one, hitting d4 with ...Qh4+ in the air. [e5], [g6] and [Qb6] are rarer but playable. [%csl Yh4][%cal Ge7e6,Gd5e4,Ge7e5] }
3... e6
{ [Nc3] is the main move, defending e4 a second time; [Nd2] and [Be3] are also played. After Nc3, Black pins the knight with ...Bb4 and keeps ...dxe4 in reserve. Against Be3, ...Qb6 hits b2, and against Nd2, ...c5 opens the centre. Black's plan in all lines: ...c5 and pressure on the dark squares, with the light bishop going to a6 via ...b6 if it stays shut in. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gb1c3,Gb1d2,Gc1e3] }
( 3... dxe4
{ Take back with [fxe4]: that was the point of f3. White now has a full centre and the half-open f-file, but the dark squares around the king are weak, and ...Qh4+ is a theme. Black's next move must hit the centre at once, before White completes development: ...e5 is the main answer, striking at d4 and opening the f8 bishop's diagonal toward White's king, which can't castle yet. [%csl Yh4][%cal Gf3e4,Ge7e5] }
4. fxe4
{ Strike at once with [e5]! It hits d4, and ...Qh4+ is in the air, since the e1-h4 diagonal is open. White has only one good answer, Nf3, guarding h4 and hitting e5. Anything slower than ...e5 lets White build the centre for free: Nc3, Nf3, Bc4 or Bd3 and castling, with the f-file for the rook. The knight move ...Nf6 is the most common slip here. [%csl Yd4,Yh4][%cal Ge7e5] }
4... e5
{ [Nf3] is the only good move: it develops, guards h4 and hits e5. Other moves lose the e4 pawn or the centre: dxe5 and c3 run into ...Qh4+, picking up e4 with check, and Nc3 into ...exd4. After Nf3, Black chooses between ...Bg4, pinning the knight that guards e5, and ...exd4, grabbing a pawn and inviting the Maróczy gambit with Bc4. [%csl Yh4,Ye5][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 4... Nf6 $2
{ The most natural developing move, and the most common one here, but it walks into [e5]: the knight is kicked again, and White has a big centre and the open f-file for nothing. About a third of club players go this way. Strike first with ...e5. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge4e5] }
5. e5 ) ( 4... e6 $6
{ Playing [e6] out of habit locks Black's light-squared bishop inside the chain and fails to punish White's weakened e1-h4 diagonal! After [Nf3], White covers h4 and builds an ideal pawn centre plus an open f-file for free ([Nf3 Nd7 Nc3 e5 dxe5]). Strike back immediately with [b:e5] instead. [%csl Ye6][%cal Rg1f3] }
5. Nf3 Nd7 6. Nc3 e5 7. dxe5 ) 5. Nf3
{ Black chooses between [Bg4], pinning the knight that guards e5, and [exd4], taking a pawn: then 6.Bc4 is the Maróczy gambit, with a dangerous attack on the f-file. In both lines Black must watch f7 at every move, as White's bishop and rook can arrive there quickly. ...Nf6 and ...Nd7 are the safest developing moves. White's centre looks strong, but if the attack runs dry, Black's healthier structure tells. [%csl Yf7,Yf3][%cal Gc8g4,Ge5d4] }
( 5. dxe5 $2
{ Taking the pawn looks natural, but it opens the e1-h4 diagonal. After [Qh4+ g3 Qxe4+], the queen picks up e4 with check and hits the rook on h1. White's best answer to the check is to walk the king to d2, which says it all. Develop with Nf3, which guards h4. [%csl Re4,Rh1][%cal Gd8h4] }
5... Qh4+ 6. g3 Qxe4+ ) ( 5. c3 $2
{ Supporting d4 looks solid, but it ignores the queen check. After [Qh4+], g3 Qxe4+ forks king and rook, and other answers leave White's king in the middle with the centre falling. Nf3 was the only good move. [%csl Re4,Rh1][%cal Gd8h4] }
5... Qh4+ ) ( 5. Nc3 $2
{ The knight defends e4, but d4 falls: [exd4] hits the knight, and Black is a pawn up with White's centre broken. Nf3 was the only good move, guarding h4 and hitting e5. [%csl Rc3,Rd4][%cal Ge5d4] }
5... exd4 ) 5... Bg4
{ [Bc4] hits f7 at once; [c3] is the most testing, supporting d4 and preparing Qb3 against b7 and f7. Against Bc4, Black must play ...Nd7, guarding e5, since other moves walk into Bxf7+ and Nxe5+. Against c3, ...Nd7 and ...Bd6 or ...b5 hold. The pin on f3 is Black's main asset: the knight is the only guard of e5 and h4. [%csl Yf7,Yf3][%cal Gf1c4,Gc2c3] }
( 5... exd4
{ [Bc4] develops and aims at f7: White gives the d-pawn for an attack on the f-file. After O-O, Ng5 or Ne5 and the queen coming via e1, Black's king can come under heavy fire. Taking back with the knight looks natural but loses e4 to ...Qh4+. The bishop move is White's usual choice; c3, a second gambit pawn, is a rarer try. [%csl Yf7,Yh4][%cal Gf1c4] }
6. Bc4
{ [Nf6] or [Nd7]. Black must keep f7 covered: White's plan is O-O, Ng5 or Ne5 and the f-file, with the queen joining via e1. After ...Nf6 O-O, ...Nbd7 and ...Nb6 is a good way to hit the bishop and calm things down. Slow or loose moves lose quickly. Black is a pawn up, and if the attack runs dry, Black is better: patience and accurate development are what count. [%csl Yf7,Yc4][%cal Gg8f6,Gb8d7] }
( 6. Nxd4 $2
{ Taking back looks natural, but the knight has left the guard of h4. [Qh4+] and the e4 pawn drops: g3 Qxe4+ forks king and rook. The gambit with Bc4 is White's real try. [%csl Re4,Rh1][%cal Gd8h4] }
6... Qh4+ ) 6... Nf6
{ [O-O] brings the rook to the f-file. White is a pawn down and has to play fast: the f-file, the bishop on c4 and the push e5 are the weapons. Black's plan: ...Nbd7 and ...Nb6 to hit the bishop, ...Be7 or ...Bc5 and castling. White's queen often comes via e1 to g3 or h4, and e5 kicks the knight away from f6. [%csl Yf7,Ye5][%cal Ge1g1,Gb8d7] }
( 6... Nd7 ) ( 6... Bg4 $4
{ The pin looks strong, but the bishop on g4 is loose. [Bxf7+ Kxf7 Ne5+] checks and uncovers the queen against the bishop, and Nxg4 follows: White wins a pawn and keeps a raging attack. Play ...Nf6 or ...Nd7. [%csl Rg4,Re8][%cal Gc4f7] }
7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Ne5+ ) ( 6... c5 $4
{ Holding the extra pawn on d4 looks wise, but it is too slow. After [O-O], the rook and the bishop aim at f7, and e5 or Ng5 breaks in before Black has developed. Play ...Nf6 or ...Nd7. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge1g1] }
7. O-O ) ( 6... h6 $4
{ A prophylactic move against Ng5, but Black can't afford it. After [O-O], the f-file opens before Black is developed, and Ne5 or e5 crashes through on f7. Develop a knight first, ...Nf6 or ...Nd7. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge1g1] }
7. O-O ) ( 6... Be6 $2
{ Trading off the dangerous bishop looks sensible, and a quarter of club players do it, but after [Bxe6] fxe6 the f-file opens toward Black's king and the e6 pawn is weak. White castles, pushes e5 to drive away a knight on f6, and the extra pawn on d4 counts for little. Develop a knight first, ...Nf6 or ...Nd7. [%csl Rf7,Re6][%cal Gc4e6] }
7. Bxe6 ) 7. O-O
{ [Nbd7], then ...Nb6 to hit the bishop, or ...Be7 and castling. The knight on d7 also covers e5, so the push e5 isn't as strong. White's queen often comes via e1 to g3 or h4. Before each move, check what happens after Bxf7+ or a knight landing on g5 or e5: White's whole game is about f7. [%csl Yf7,Ye5][%cal Gb8d7,Gd7b6] }
7... Nbd7 ) 6. Bc4
{ [Nd7]! is the only good move: the knight guards e5, so Bxf7+ followed by Nxe5+ doesn't work. Black then develops with ...Ngf6 and ...Bd6 or ...Be7, and the pin on the f3 knight keeps White busy. Developing the other knight first is the classic mistake: Bxf7+ and Nxe5+ uncover the queen against the bishop on g4. [%csl Ye5,Yf7][%cal Gb8d7] }
( 6. c3 ) 6... Nd7
{ [O-O] keeps up the pressure on f7 and brings the rook to the f-file; [c3] prepares Qb3, hitting f7 and b7. In both lines Black needs one more accurate move: ...Ngf6 against castling, ...Bd6 or ...b5 against c3. White's centre is strong, but the pin on f3 and Black's pressure on d4 keep the balance. [%csl Yf7,Yb7][%cal Ge1g1,Gc2c3] }
( 6... Nf6 $4
{ The natural developing move, but e5 loses its guard. [Bxf7+] wins a pawn: after ...Kxf7, Nxe5+ checks and uncovers the queen against the bishop on g4, so Black has to play [Bxf7+ Ke7], with the king stuck in the middle and White a pawn up. Play ...Nd7, guarding e5. [%csl Rg4,Re5][%cal Gc4f7] }
7. Bxf7+ Ke7 ) 7. O-O
{ [Ngf6] blocks the f-file in front of f7; any other move walks into Bxf7+. Next come ...Bd6 or ...Be7 and castling. White can open the centre with dxe5 or prepare Qb3 with c3, but Black's pieces work together: the knight on d7 guards e5 and the bishop pins the f3 knight. [%csl Yf7,Ye5][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 7. c3
{ White's [c3] shores up d4 and clears b3 for the queen, threatening a double attack on b7 and f7 as soon as Black's guard slips! Black has two accurate ways to defuse [Qb3] in advance: [Bd6] develops smoothly while overprotecting e5 so [Bd6 Qb3 Qe7] holds everything cleanly, and [b5] kicks the bishop off the a2-g8 diagonal before the queen ever lands on b3. Once that battery is neutralized, [Ngf6] and [b:O-O] follow safely—whereas rushing [Ngf6] right now drops a pawn to [Ngf6 Qb3]. [%cal Gf8d6] }
7... Bd6 ( 7... b5 ) ( 7... Ngf6 $2
{ The usual developing move, but here [Qb3] hits b7 and f7 together, and Black can't cover both: White wins a pawn with a strong position. Answer c3 with ...Bd6 or ...b5 first. [%csl Rb7,Rf7][%cal Gd1b3] }
8. Qb3 ) ) 7... Ngf6
{ [c3] prepares Qb3 against b7 and f7; Black answers ...Bd6 or ...b5. [dxe5] forces Black to find the right recapture: the d7 knight can't take back at once, because Qxd8+ would end the pin on the f3 knight. White's other plans are the queen to e1 and g3, or h3 to question the bishop. Black is fine with accurate play: ...Bd6, ...O-O and ...Qe7. [%csl Yb7,Yf7][%cal Gc2c3,Gd4e5] }
( 7... Bd6 $4
{ The bishop develops and guards e5, but f7 has no blocker in front of the rook. [Bxf7+ Ke7]: White is a pawn up, and Black's king is stuck in the centre facing the f-file. Play ...Ngf6 first, closing the file. [%csl Rf7,Re8][%cal Gc4f7] }
8. Bxf7+ Ke7 ) 8. c3 ( 8. dxe5
{ [Qb6+] or [Bxf3] first, and only then take on e5. After ...Bxf3 and the recapture, ...Nxe5 hits the bishop and Black has the pawn back with a good game. The check on b6 gains a tempo before Black takes. Taking on e5 at once with the knight drops a piece: Qxd8+ ends the pin on the f3 knight with tempo, and the knight then takes on e5. [%csl Ye5,Yd8][%cal Gd8b6,Gg4f3] }
8... Qb6+ ( 8... Bxf3 ) ( 8... Nxe5 $4
{ Taking back with the knight looks obvious, but [Qxd8+] trades queens with check and frees the f3 knight from the pin: after ...Rxd8 Nxe5, White is a piece up. Check with ...Qb6+ or take on f3 first. [%csl Re5,Rf3][%cal Gd1d8] }
9. Qxd8+ ) ) ) ( 3... e5 ) ( 3... g6 ) ( 3... Qb6 ) 4. Nc3
{ [Bb4]! pins the knight and keeps ...dxe4 in reserve: with the knight pinned, e4 has one defender fewer. [Nf6] gives a French in which White's f-pawn can go on to f4: e5 Nfd7 f4 c5 is a sharp structure. After ...Bb4, White has to find a way to develop: Bf4, Ne2 or a3. Black's typical setup is ...Ne7, ...b6, ...Ba6 and ...c5. [%csl Yc3,Ye4][%cal Gf8b4,Gg8f6] }
( 4. Nd2 ) ( 4. Be3 ) 4... Bb4
{ White has to find a way to develop. [Bf4] with Qd3 and long castling is the main plan; [Ne2] guards c3 so the pawns aren't doubled after a trade; [a3] asks the bishop at once. Black's plan: ...Ne7, ...b6 and ...Ba6 to trade the light bishop that ...e6 shut in, ...c5 at the right moment, and ...dxe4 when White's pieces can't recapture well. If White castles long, ...c5 and ...Qa5 turn the game into Black's attack. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gc1f4,Gg1e2,Ga2a3] }
( 4... Nf6 ) 5. Bf4
{ [Ne7], then ...b6 and ...Ba6 to trade the light bishop that ...e6 shut in. The knight on e7 guards d5 and can go to g6 to hit the f4 bishop. If White castles long, ...c5 gets Black's attack going first, with the queen coming to a5. Taking on e4 early opens the f-file for White, so Black usually waits. [%cal Gg8e7,Gc8a6,Gc6c5] }
( 5. Ne2
{ [Ne7] again, with ...Nd7 and castling. The knight on e2 can recapture on c3, so White's pawns stay healthy, but it blocks the f1 bishop. If a3, keep the bishop with ...Ba5 or ...Bd6 rather than trading it: the trade frees e2 for White's bishop. [dxe4] is the other option, opening lines while White's pieces are tangled. [%cal Gb4a5] }
5... Ne7 ( 5... dxe4 ) ) ( 5. a3 ) 5... Ne7 ) ( 3. Bd3 ) ( 3. Nf3 $2
{ Developing is natural, but this leaves e4 without a guard. After [dxe4], the knight is hit and White needs Ne5 or Ng5 to get some play for the pawn; Black keeps the extra pawn with ...Nd7 or ...Bf5. Many club players miss the gift and pin with ...Bg4, or play ...e6 or ...Nf6, which let White push e5: take the pawn. If you play the knight to f3, defend e4 first with Nc3 or Nd2. [%csl Rf3][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4
{ White has given a pawn and must find play for it. [Ng5], the Ulysses gambit, hits e4 and keeps the knight active; [Ne5] is the other try, aiming at f7 and c6. Black is a pawn up and should keep the e4 pawn while it is safe: against Ng5, ...Bf5 holds it, and against Ne5, ...Nd7 challenges the knight first. White's natural-looking retreat to d2 is the worst of all: it lets Black grab d4 as well. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf3g5,Gf3e5] }
4. Ng5
{ The Ulysses gambit. White attacks e4 again and hopes for tricks on f7. [Bf5] is the best answer: it holds the e4 pawn, and after g4 the bishop drops to g6. [Nf6] is also good, developing and covering h7 and g4. Black is a pawn up with an easy game. Moves that hit the knight or the centre at once, like ...h6, ...e5 or ...f5, hand the pawn back or weaken Black's king. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gc8f5,Gg8f6] }
( 4. Ne5
{ The knight eyes f7 and c6 and keeps the e4 pawn under watch. [Nd7] is the answer: it challenges the knight at once, and after Bf4 Nxe5 Bxe5 or Nc3 Nxe5 dxe5, Black develops with ...Bf5 or ...Nf6 and stays a clean pawn up. The natural ...Bf5 is the mistake here: Bc4 aims at f7 with the bishop and the knight together. ...Be6 blocks the bishop's diagonal, but it is passive and gives White time to win the pawn back. [%csl Ye5,Yf7][%cal Gb8d7] }
4... Nd7 ( 4... Bf5 $2
{ The bishop protects e4, but it leaves f7 to White's pieces. After [Bc4], the bishop and the e5 knight both aim at f7, Black has to spend moves on ...e6 or ...Bg6, and White wins the e4 pawn back with Nc3, keeping the better game. Challenge the knight with ...Nd7 first. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf1c4] }
5. Bc4 ) ) ( 4. Nfd2 $2
{ The knight goes after the e4 pawn, but it leaves d4 without a defender. [Qxd4] wins a second pawn, and the queen stands in the centre, eyeing b2 and f2, with ...e3 coming to open White's king. Ng5 or Ne5 keeps some play for the pawn. [%csl Rb2,Rf2][%cal Gd8d4] }
4... Qxd4 ) 4... Bf5 ( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... h6 $6
{ Kicking the knight seems natural, but it lets it take the pawn back: after [Nxe4], the material is level and Black has spent a move on ...h6. Hold e4 with ...Bf5 first. [%csl Rh6][%cal Gg5e4] }
5. Nxe4 ) ( 4... e5 $2
{ Striking in the centre looks active, but it opens lines for White's pieces. After [Bc4], f7 is under fire from the bishop and the knight, and White wins the pawn back with a strong attack. Play ...Bf5 or ...Nf6. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf1c4] }
5. Bc4 ) ( 4... f5 $2
{ The pawn guards e4, but it weakens the diagonal to Black's king. [Bc4] hits g8 and e6, and White gets a dangerous attack for the pawn; Black's king can't castle safely. Play ...Bf5 or ...Nf6. [%csl Rg8,Re6][%cal Gf1c4] }
5. Bc4 ) ) ( 3. Be3 $6
{ The bishop develops but leaves e4 to its fate. After [dxe4], White is a pawn down and the compensation is thin. Black should just take: the common replies ...Nf6 and ...e6 let White off the hook with e5, a free Advance. Defend the pawn with a knight or push it. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4 ) 3... Bf5
{ The bishop is out, and White now picks a system. [Nf3] with [Be2] and castling is Short's quiet line: White develops, keeps d4 protected and waits for ...c5. [Nc3] with [g4] and Nge2, and [h4] (Tal's line), attack the bishop with pawns while it has few squares, and Black has to know a few precise moves. [Ne2], the Bronstein, heads for g3 with the same aim. [c3] and [Nd2] are calm, solid setups. [Bd3] offers a trade of light bishops, which mostly helps Black. Whatever White picks, Black's plan stays the same: ...e6, ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6, with a knight heading for f5. [%csl Yf5][%cal Gg1f3,Gh2h4,Gb1c3] }
( 3... c5
{ The Botvinnik-Carls line. Black hits the chain at once, spending a second move with the c-pawn but keeping the light bishop free for ...Bg4 or ...Bf5 later. White's best reply is [dxc5], taking the pawn; Black wins it back with ...e6 and ...Bxc5, or plays ...Nc6 to hit e5. Most club players answer [c3], which gives an Advance French in which the c8 bishop is free: a good deal for Black. [Nf3] is also common, met by ...cxd4 or ...Bg4. Note that the d5 pawn has lost its pawn support, so watch for tricks against it. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd4c5,Gc2c3,Gg1f3] }
4. dxc5
{ White has taken the pawn and may try to keep it with b4 or Nf3 and Be3. Black wins it back with [e6] and ...Bxc5, or develops with [Nc6], hitting e5: then ...e6 and ...Bxc5 follow, or ...Bg4 pins a knight that comes to f3. If White props up c5 with b4, the pawns become loose, and ...a5 or ...b6 attacks them. Black's d5 pawn has lost its c-pawn support, so be careful with early queen moves: the pawn needs its defenders. [%csl Yc5,Yd5][%cal Ge7e6,Gb8c6] }
( 4. c3
{ White props up d4, but this is an Advance French in which Black's light bishop is free. Develop with [Nc6], then ...Bg4 or ...Bf5, ...e6, ...Qb6 and ...Ne7-f5. Once Black takes on d4 and White recaptures with the c-pawn, no pawn can defend d4 any more, and all of Black's pieces aim at it. A pin with ...Bg4 against a knight on f3 is especially strong here, since that knight is d4's main defender. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6,Gc8g4] }
4... Nc6 ) ( 4. Nf3
{ White develops instead of taking on c5. Trading on d4 is strong: after [cxd4 Qxd4 Nc6], Black develops with tempo on the queen, and if White takes back with the knight instead, the e5 pawn loses its support. [Bg4] is also good: it pins the knight that guards d4, and if White plays h3, Black takes on f3 and keeps up the pressure, with ...e6 and ...Nc6 next. White's best is often dxc5 at the right moment, keeping the e5 pawn as a cramping wedge. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gc5d4,Gc8g4] }
4... cxd4 ( 4... Bg4 ) ) 4... e6 ( 4... Nc6 ) ( 4... Qa5+ $2
{ The queen check looks like the fastest way to win back the pawn, but it drops d5. After [Nc3 Qxc5 Qxd5], White wins a central pawn, and Black can't even trade queens there: Nxd5 would then threaten a fork on c7. Win the pawn back calmly with ...e6 and ...Bxc5, or develop with ...Nc6. [%csl Rd5,Rc7][%cal Gb1c3] }
5. Nc3 Qxc5 6. Qxd5 ) ) 4. Nf3 ( 4. h4
{ Tal's line. White threatens g4 and h5, taking every square from the bishop. Black's most solid answer is [h5]: it stops g4 for good, and though it leaves a hole on g5, White's pawn on h4 is stuck too. Then Black can play ...e6, ...c5 and ...Nc6 in peace. [h6] is the other try: it keeps h7 for the bishop, but after g4 Black usually has to drop it to d7, with a passive French. Tal played 4.h4 five times in his 1961 return match against Botvinnik, and Kramnik won with it against Leko in the last game of their 2004 match, the win that kept his world title. Whatever you play, don't answer h4 with ...e6. [%csl Yf5][%cal Gh7h5,Gh7h6] }
4... h5
{ Now g4 is impossible, so ...e6 is safe. The pawn on h5 costs Black the g5 square, which a white bishop or knight may use later, but White's own pawn on h4 is just as stuck. White picks a setup. [c4] hits d5; Black answers ...e6 and ...Ne7, and should not take on c4, since the d5 pawn holds Black's light squares. [Bd3] trades bishops: after ...Bxd3 Qxd3, ...Qa5+ and ...Qa6 offer a queen trade that suits Black, as White's pawns on e5 and h4 become targets. [c3] and Nd2 are quieter. Think about the f8 bishop early: it is often Black's worst piece in these lines. [%csl Yg5][%cal Gc2c4,Gf1d3,Gc2c3] }
( 4... h6
{ Black keeps h7 for the bishop, but now g4 is open for White's pawn. [g4] at once is the critical test: after ...Bd7, Black gets a French in which the bishop is passive, though the position is solid and ...e6, ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 still come. [c3] or Bd3 are calmer: Black plays ...e6 with a normal Advance structure, but the h-pawns stand on h4 and h6 instead of their home squares, which gives White extra space on the kingside. This is why most strong players prefer ...h5 against h4. [%csl Yh7][%cal Gg2g4,Gc2c3] }
5. g4
{ The bishop has to leave. [Be4] is the most accurate: it waits for f3 before dropping back to h7, so White's f-pawn has had to step forward and take f3 from the g1 knight. [Bd7] is the solid retreat: Black plays a French with ...e6, ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6, though the pawn on g4 takes f5 from Black's king's knight. [Bh7] at once keeps the bishop on its diagonal, but e6 can come at once, cramping Black. Either way White has extra space and Black needs patience. [%csl Yf3][%cal Gf5e4,Gf5d7,Gf5h7] }
( 5. c3 ) 5... Be4 ( 5... Bd7 ) ( 5... Bh7 ) ) ( 4... e6 $4
{ The natural move, and it loses the bishop. After [g4 Be4 f3 Bg6 h5], the bishop has no square left: f5 is covered by the g4 pawn and h7 is taken by Black's own pawn. About one game in eight on Lichess goes this way. Against h4, stop the pawns first with ...h5 or ...h6, and only then play ...e6. [%csl Rf5,Rh7][%cal Gg2g4] }
5. g4 Be4 6. f3 Bg6 7. h5 ) 5. c4 ( 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3
{ [Qa5+] or [e6]. The queen check is the more ambitious: after Bd2 or Nd2, ...Qa6 offers a queen trade, and without queens White's advanced pawns on d4, e5 and h4 are easy targets, while the hole on g5 matters much less. With [e6] the game stays more complex; watch out for Bg5, which uses the hole to annoy Black's queen and slow down ...Ne7. Black's plan in both lines is ...e6, ...c5 and ...Nc6, often with ...Ne7-f5, and once the queens are off, the king can stay in the centre. [%csl Yh4,Yg5][%cal Gd8a5,Ge7e6] }
6... Qa5+ ( 6... e6 ) ) ( 5. c3 ) 5... e6
{ [Nc3] adds pressure on d5. Black develops with ...Ne7, not ...Nd7: after cxd5 exd5 the structure suits White, whose pieces can use f4 and e3. If White trades on d5, Black prefers to recapture toward the centre. Another idea for White is Qb3: if Black meets it with ...Qb6, c5 forces a queen trade that leaves the f8 bishop short of squares, so plan its route early, often ...g6 and ...Bh6 to trade it off. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb1c3,Gg8e7] }
6. Nc3 ) ( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. Ne2
{ The Bronstein. The knight heads for g3 to chase the bishop, and h4 will follow. Black plays [e6]; after Ng3 Bg6 h4 h5, White's knight and pawns have done little. A typical continuation is Be2 and ...c5: Black offers the h5 pawn, but if White takes it with the bishop and then the knight, ...cxd4 and ...Qb6 hit d4, b2 and the loose knight at once. The knight on e2 also blocks White's light bishop, so Black gets time for ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6. [%cal Ge7e6,Gc6c5,Gd8b6] }
4... e6 ) ( 4. c3
{ White guards d4 with a pawn at once. Black goes on with [e6], then ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6: the d4 pawn is still the target, and if White ever takes on c5 the f8 bishop recaptures. ...Nd7 with ...Ne7 and ...c5 is the other setup. A typical White plan is Nf3, Be2 and O-O, reaching Short-like positions, or Bd3 to trade bishops. Black is comfortable: the bishop is active, the pawns are solid and the ...c5 break is ready. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ge7e6,Gc6c5] }
4... e6
{ White picks a setup. [Nf3] and [Be2] with castling is the most common, reaching Short-like positions with the pawn already on c3; [Be3] guards d4 and keeps the f-pawn free; [Bd3] trades the light bishops, which suits Black. Against all of them, Black's plan is ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6, with the king's knight going to e7 and f5. If Black takes on d4 and White recaptures with the c-pawn, the c-file opens for Black's rooks and b4 becomes a good square for Black's pieces. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1e2,Gc1e3] }
5. Nf3 ( 5. Be2 ) ( 5. Be3 ) ( 5. Bd3 ) ) ( 4. Nd2
{ White plans [w:Nb3] to control c5, the square of Black's main break, and keeps the c-pawn free to go to c3. Black plays [e6], then ...Nd7 and ...Ne7, or the sharp ...c5 at once, which challenges the plan before the knight arrives. If the knight reaches b3, Black can still prepare ...c5 with ...Nd7, or with ...b6. With no pressure on the bishop, Black can develop calmly: ...Nd7, ...Ne7, ...h6 and ...c5. [%csl Yc5][%cal Ge7e6,Gd2b3] }
4... e6 ) ( 4. Bd3
{ White offers to trade the light bishops. That's good news for Black: the c8 bishop is Black's problem piece in the Advance, and White's light bishop is one of its best. Take with [Bxd3]; after Qxd3, play ...e6, ...c5 and ...Nc6, and Black has no bad piece left. White's queen on d3 can even become a target for ...Nb4 or ...Qa6 later. Capablanca beat Nimzowitsch this way at New York 1927, and the Advance lost its reputation for decades. There is no reason to avoid the trade: a retreat only wastes time. [%csl Yd3][%cal Gf5d3] }
4... Bxd3 5. Qxd3
{ [e6] is the natural move: it completes the pawn triangle and frees the f8 bishop. Then Black plays ...Qb6 or ...Qa5+, ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Ne7-f5 against d4, with no bad piece left. White usually goes on with Nf3, O-O and c3, keeping the centre, but without the light bishop White has little to attack with on the kingside. The queen check on a5 followed by ...Qa6 can force a queen trade, which leaves White's advanced pawn on e5 weak in the endgame. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Ge7e6,Gd8a5] }
5... e6 ) ( 4. g4
{ The Bayonet. White kicks the bishop at once. [Be4] is the best answer: after f3 the bishop drops back to g6, and White's pawn on f3 has taken the knight's best square and weakened the diagonal to e1. Black then plays ...e6, ...c5 and ...Nc6 in a normal Advance structure in which White has pushed pawns instead of developing. [Bg6], the most popular move, is fine too: after Ne2 or h4, Black plays ...e6 and ...c5. [Bd7] is a calmer French-style retreat, where Black can later hit the centre with ...c5 and ...f6. Going back to c8 would lose the time White has spent and is too passive. [%csl Yf3][%cal Gf5e4,Gf5g6,Gf5d7] }
4... Be4 ( 4... Bg6 ) ( 4... Bd7 ) ) *

[Event "Caro-Kann Defence (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 c6 2. d4 ( 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4
{ Take back with [Nxe4]. Black has given up the centre to develop with tempo, as in the Classical, but with White's d-pawn still at home: d4 isn't a target, and White's queen and bishop can use e2, h5 and c4 quickly. Black's next move decides the setup: ...Nf6, ...Nd7, ...Bg4 or ...Bf5. [Ng5] is a gambit try that leaves the pawn for a while and aims at f7; it is playable, but Black gets a free game with ...e5. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gc3e4,Gf3g5] }
4. Nxe4
{ [Nf6] and [Nd7] are safe: Black offers a knight trade or prepares ...Ngf6. [Bg4] is fine too, pinning the knight before it reaches e5. [Bf5], the Classical reflex, is the move to be careful with: after Ng3 Bg6 h4 h6 Ne5, White's knight and queen go after the bishop and f7. After ...Nf6 or ...Nd7, Qe2 sets a cheap trap: the other knight's natural move to d7 or f6 allows Nd6 mate. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gg8f6,Gb8d7,Gc8g4] }
( 4. Ng5
{ A gambit in the Two Knights: White leaves the e4 pawn for now and aims the knight at f7. Black's best answers take the centre or develop: [e5], which grabs space and opens the f8 bishop's diagonal, [Bf5], which defends the e4 pawn, or [Nf6]. White usually wins the pawn back with Ngxe4, but Black has a free game. Don't defend with ...f5: it weakens the diagonal to Black's king. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge7e5,Gc8f5,Gg8f6] }
4... e5 ( 4... Bf5 ) ( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... f5 $2
{ The pawn defends e4, but it opens the a2-g8 diagonal. [Bc4] hits g8 and e6, the knight on g5 joins in, and Black can't castle safely: White's attack is worth much more than the pawn. Play ...e5, ...Bf5 or ...Nf6. [%csl Rg8,Re6][%cal Gf1c4] }
5. Bc4 ) ) 4... Nf6
{ [Nxf6+] gives a level, simple game: Black takes back with ...exf6 or ...gxf6 and develops quickly, as in the 4...Nf6 lines of the Classical. [Qe2] sets a trap: ...Nbd7?? Nd6 is mate, since the e7 pawn is pinned. After Qe2, the simple ...Nxe4 Qxe4 with ...Nd7 or ...Bf5 is fine. Other moves, like Ng3, keep pieces on, and Black answers ...h5 or ...e5. [%csl Yf6][%cal Ge4f6,Gd1e2] }
( 4... Nd7
{ [d4] returns to the 4...Nd7 Caro-Kann, with the knight on f3 already out. [Bc4] aims at f7 at once, and Black answers ...Ngf6 and, after Ng5, ...e6. [Qe2] sets the usual trap: ...Ngf6?? Nd6 is mate. Black's plan is the Karpov setup: ...Ngf6, then ...e6 or ...Bg4, with a solid game. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gd2d4,Gf1c4,Gd1e2] }
5. d4 ( 5. Bc4 ) ( 5. Qe2
{ [Ndf6] or [e6], and Black is fine: the queen on e2 blocks White's light bishop and has to move again later. After ...Ndf6, the g8 knight can recapture on f6 if White trades. The one move to avoid is the natural ...Ngf6, which runs into the same mate on d6 as in the 4...Nd7 Classical. Once the knights are sorted out, ...Bg4 pins the f3 knight against the queen, and Black develops with tempo. [%csl Yd6,Ye2][%cal Gd7f6,Ge7e6] }
5... Ndf6 ( 5... e6 ) ( 5... Ngf6 $4
{ The developing move every Caro-Kann player knows, and here it is mate: [Nd6#], with the e7 pawn pinned by the queen. Play ...Ndf6 or ...e6 first. [%csl Re8,Re7][%cal Ge4d6] }
6. Nd6# ) ) ) ( 4... Bg4 ) ( 4... Bf5
{ [Ng3] chases the bishop, preparing h4 and Ne5. Black has to choose between the reflex ...Bg6 and ...Bg4, which pins the f3 knight and keeps it out of e5. Ng3 is the most testing; quieter moves like d4 reach Classical positions, where the bishop on f5 is fine. Unlike in the Classical, White hasn't committed the d-pawn yet, so it can go to d3 or d4 later, as suits the position. [%csl Yf5,Ye5][%cal Ge4g3] }
5. Ng3
{ [Bg4] is more accurate here: the bishop pins the knight before it reaches e5, and h4-h5 no longer hits anything. Black follows with ...e6, ...Nf6 and ...Nbd7. [Bg6] is the reflex, and after h4 h6 Ne5 Black has to find the precise ...Qd6; the natural ...Bh7 runs into a famous trap with Qh5. [%csl Yf3,Ye5][%cal Gf5g4,Gf5g6] }
5... Bg4 ( 5... Bg6
{ [h4] threatens h5, taking away the bishop's retreat. Black must answer ...h6 or ...h5, and in this move order White then has Ne5 with the d-pawn still at home, so the queen's way to h5 is open. This is where the Two Knights differs from the Classical: Black's light squares are easier to attack. [%csl Yg6][%cal Gh2h4,Gf3e5] }
6. h4
{ [h6] gives the bishop h7 against h5; ...h5 is also possible, but it weakens g5 and g6 for good. After ...h6, White's Ne5 hits the bishop on g6 and prepares Qh5 and Bc4 against f7, so Black has to be ready with ...Qd6. Developing with ...Nd7 first loses the bishop pair after h5. [%csl Yh7,Yg6][%cal Gh7h6,Gf3e5] }
6... h6
{ [Ne5] hits the bishop. With the d-pawn still at home, the queen's way to h5 is open, and Bc4 can join the attack on f7 at once. Black must find ...Qd6, which hits the knight and keeps h7 available; the natural ...Bh7 walks into Qh5 and a trap. Other White moves, like h5, simply send the bishop to h7, where it is safe. [%csl Yg6,Yf7][%cal Gf3e5,Gd8d6] }
7. Ne5
{ [Qd6]! is the best defence: it challenges the knight on e5, defends the bishop on g6 along the sixth rank, and keeps h7 in reserve. After d4, Black continues ...Nd7 and ...Ngf6 with a solid game. The natural ...Bh7 is a trap: Qh5 and Bc4 bring White's pieces against f7, and two famous old games ended quickly that way. [%csl Ye5,Yg6][%cal Gd8d6] }
7... Qd6 ( 7... Bh7 $2
{ The natural retreat, and a well-known trap. After [Qh5 g6 Qf3], White's queen and knight hit f7 and the rook comes later; ...gxh5 is never possible while Bxf7 mate is in the air, and ...Qd5 can be met by Bc4. The older line Bc4 e6 Qe2 also wins material if Black misses ...Qe7. Play ...Qd6, hitting the knight. [%csl Rf7,Rh7][%cal Gd1h5] }
8. Qh5 g6 9. Qf3 ) ) ) 5. Nxf6+ ( 5. Qe2
{ [Nxe4] trades the knight before any trick arrives. After Qxe4, ...Nd7 develops and ...Qd5 offers a queen trade, and Black is fine; don't play ...Bf5, which just hangs the bishop to the queen. The queen on e2 blocks White's light bishop, so the trap is White's only real point. Developing the queen's knight to d7 here is the famous blunder: Nd6 is mate. [%csl Ye2,Ye4][%cal Gf6e4] }
5... Nxe4 ( 5... Nbd7 $4
{ The natural way to support the f6 knight, and it allows [Nd6#]: the e7 pawn is pinned by the queen on e2, and the king has no square. Paul Keres won a game this way. Take on e4 first with ...Nxe4. [%csl Re8,Re7][%cal Ge4d6] }
6. Nd6# ) ) ) ( 2. c4
{ The Accelerated Panov. White hits d5 before Black has played ...d5, hoping for a Panov in which Black has fewer options. [d5] challenges at once: after the trades on d5, Black wins the pawn back with ...Nf6. [e5] is the other answer: Black takes the centre and keeps d4 under control, and the c6 pawn can support ...d5 later. White's pawn on c4 leaves d4 slightly weak, so Black's pieces can use it. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd7d5,Ge7e5] }
2... d5
{ [exd5] or [cxd5]: either way, Black takes back with the c-pawn, and White can then play d4 into a Panov or try to keep the extra d-pawn by taking again. Black wins it back with ...Nf6 and ...Nxd5, or with ...Qxd5. Other moves let Black take on e4 or c4 with an easy game. Remember that the queen can recapture on d5 only once White's c-pawn has gone: until then, it guards that square. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge4d5,Gc4d5] }
( 2... e5
{ An open game in which the pawn on c6 has taken the square Black's queen's knight usually uses. [Nf3] attacks e5, and Black defends with ...d6, a solid setup with ...Nf6, ...Be7 and ...d5 later. [d4] opens the centre at once; after ...exd4 Qxd4, Black can't hit the queen with a knight from c6, so it develops with ...Nf6 and ...d5 instead. Black's plan: use the dark squares around d4 that the c4 pawn has weakened, and strike with ...d5 at the right moment. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gg1f3,Gd2d4] }
3. Nf3 ( 3. d4 ) ) 3. exd5
{ Take back with the pawn, [cxd5]. White can then play d4, a Panov, or take again with cxd5 and try to keep the pawn for a while. Taking with the queen is the classic blunder here: the c4 pawn guards d5. After the pawn recapture Black's position is easy: the knight comes to f6 and the light bishop waits until it is clear where White's queen goes. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gc6d5] }
( 3. cxd5
{ Take back with [cxd5]. White's best is then exd5, reaching the same position as after 3.exd5 cxd5 4.cxd5: White has an extra pawn on d5 for a moment, and Black wins it back with ...Nf6 and ...Nxd5. Black's light bishop stays home for now, and the c-file is open for Black's rooks later. Developing first with ...Nf6 is playable, but after dxc6 Nxc6 Black has given up the centre for nothing. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc6d5] }
3... cxd5
{ [exd5] is the natural move: White has an extra pawn on d5 for a moment, and Black wins it back with ...Nf6 and ...Nxd5, or with ...Qxd5 at once. [e5] is a different idea: White closes the centre and gets an Advance-like structure without the c-pawn, and Black answers ...Nc6, ...Bf5 and ...e6, with play on the open c-file. Both are fine; the pawn trade is more common. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge4d5,Ge4e5] }
4. exd5 ( 4. e5 ) ) ( 3. Nc3 $6
{ The knight defends e4, but it lets Black gain space with tempo. [d4] kicks the knight, and Black gets a comfortable bind with ...e5 and ...c5, where White's c4 pawn has only weakened d4. Taking on e4 is also good for Black. White should trade on d5. [%csl Rc3,Rd4][%cal Gd5d4] }
3... d4 ) 3... cxd5
{ White chooses. [cxd5] keeps an extra pawn for a moment: Black wins it back with ...Nf6 and ...Nxd5, or with ...Qxd5, when Nc3 gains a tempo on the queen. [d4] goes back into the Panov-Botvinnik with an isolated pawn. Black's light bishop stays home for now: Qb3 would hit b7 and d5. Black's queenside development comes first: ...Nf6 and ...Nc6. [%csl Yd5,Yb7][%cal Gc4d5,Gd2d4] }
( 3... Qxd5 $4
{ Taking back with the queen is the classic slip here: the c4 pawn guards d5, so [cxd5] wins the queen. This line is in the Lichess database only because so many people fall into it. Take back with the c-pawn. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gc4d5] }
4. cxd5 ) 4. cxd5
{ White has an extra pawn on d5, but it can't be held for long. [Nf6] is the main move: the knight hits the pawn and takes it back next with ...Nxd5, unless White holds it with Bb5+, Qa4+ or Nc3. [Qxd5] takes back at once and is often played: it lets White gain time with Nc3 hitting the queen, but after the queen retreats Black is solid and has an easy game. In both lines the result is an open position in which Black's pieces develop freely. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gg8f6,Gd8d5] }
( 4. d4 ) 4... Nf6
{ [Nc3] gives the pawn back at once: after ...Nxd5 and d4 or Nf3, the game turns into a Panov-like position, with Black's knight well placed on d5. [Bb5+] and [Qa4+] try to hold the pawn: Black blocks with a knight on d7 and wins the pawn back later with ...a6, ...b5 or ...Nb6. [Qb3] and [Bc4] are other tries; against them Black plays ...g6 and ...Bg7 and catches up on d5. White rarely keeps the pawn for good. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb1c3,Gf1b5,Gd1a4] }
( 4... Qxd5
{ The pawn is back, and Black's queen stands in the centre, as in a Scandinavian. White gains time with [Nc3], and the queen must move: d8 is the safest square, a5 and d6 are also played. Then Black develops with ...Nf6, ...e5 or ...e6 and the light bishop, with the c-file open for the rooks. White's pluses are a lead in development and the d-pawn, which can go to d4 to take space; a white knight on b5 or a bishop on c4 are things to watch for. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb1c3,Gd5d8,Gd5a5] }
5. Nc3 ) 5. Nc3 ( 5. Bb5+
{ Block with [Nbd7]. Then ...a6 and ...b5 chase the bishop away from d5's defence, or ...g6, ...Bg7 and ...O-O with ...Nb6 win the pawn back. Blocking with the bishop is playable too, but after Bc4 the bishop on d7 stands in the queen's way down the d-file, and the d5 pawn holds a little longer. White's extra pawn on d5 is the target: once it falls, Black has a free game with the light bishop still to come out. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb8d7,Ga7a6] }
5... Nbd7 ) ( 5. Qa4+
{ [Nbd7] again. Then ...g6, ...Bg7, castling and ...Nb6 win the pawn back, with the knight on b6 hitting the queen on a4 at the same time. Blocking with the bishop works too, but it lets White's queen go to b3 with gain of time. White has spent several moves with the queen, so Black is soon ahead in development. [%csl Yd5,Ya4][%cal Gb8d7,Gd7b6] }
5... Nbd7 ) ) ( 2. Bc4
{ The Hillbilly Attack. White's bishop aims at f7 but stands in the way of ...d5, so [d5] comes with tempo: after exd5 cxd5 the bishop has to move again, and Black has a strong centre. White's other try is Bb3, a gambit of the e4 pawn. The direct ...d5 is the simplest answer. [%csl Yc4,Yf7][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ [exd5] trades, and after ...cxd5 the bishop has to move again; [Bb3] keeps the bishop on the diagonal and gambits the e4 pawn. In the exd5 line, Black gets a central pawn and an easy game; in the Bb3 line, Black takes on e4 and must then watch f7 after Qh5. Other moves let Black take on c4 or e4 for free. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4d5,Gc4b3] }
3. exd5
{ Take back with [cxd5]: the bishop on c4 is hit again, and it has to choose between b5 with check, b3 and e2. White has traded the e-pawn for Black's c-pawn, which leaves Black two pawns in the centre against one, and the bishop's trips cost White time. Black follows with ...Nf6, ...Nc6 and ...Bg4 or ...Bf5, and has an easy game. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gc6d5] }
( 3. Bb3
{ The Hillbilly gambit. Take the pawn, [dxe4]: after Qh5, cover f7 with ...g6 or ...e6, and Black is a pawn up with a sound position. White's bishop on b3 stays aimed at f7, so Black must watch that square for a few moves. The knight move ...Nf6 is a common slip: e5 kicks it, and White gets a free Advance. [%csl Ye4,Yf7][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... dxe4
{ Once White retreats the bishop instead of trading on d5, Black snatches the undefended e4 pawn and challenges White to justify the gambit! [Qh5] threatens mate on f7 and forces [Qh5 g6] (where [Qh5 g6 Qe2] or [Qh5 g6 Qh4] tries to win e4 back, though Black holds comfortably with [Nf6] and [b:Bg7]) or [Qh5 e6]. [Nc3] develops naturally and targets e4 directly ([Nc3 Nf6 Qe2] or [f3]). By contrast, sacrificing the bishop on f7 gives up a full piece for a single pawn with no follow-up checkmate. [%cal Gd1h5] }
( 3... Nf6 $2
{ Developing with an attack on e4 looks natural, but [e5] kicks the knight, and White gets a free Advance with the bishop already on the a2-g8 diagonal. Take the pawn with ...dxe4 instead. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5 ) ( 3... e5 $6
{ Building a big centre looks ambitious, but Black is behind in development and f7 is weak. After [Nf3], the e5 pawn is hit, and opening up with ...dxe4 Nxe5 gives the pawn back and frees the b3 bishop. White develops with Nc3 and O-O, and the gambit has cost White nothing. Take on e4, ...dxe4, and keep the extra pawn. [%csl Re5,Rf7][%cal Gg1f3] }
4. Nf3 ) 4. Qh5
{ [g6] or [e6], covering f7. After ...g6, the queen has to move again, and Black is a pawn up with ...Bg7 and ...Nf6 to come. After ...e6, Black's light bishop is shut in for a while, but the position is solid. The knight move ...Nf6 hits the queen but loses f7 with check. [%csl Yf7,Yh5][%cal Gg7g6,Ge7e6] }
( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. Bxf7+ $4
{ The bishop sacrifice looks like it exposes Black's king, but after [Kxf7] White has given a piece for one pawn, and Qh5+ is met by ...g6 or ...Kf6 with nothing to follow. Play Qh5 or Nc3. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge8f7] }
4... Kxf7 ) 4... g6 ( 4... e6 ) ( 4... Nf6 $4
{ Hitting the queen with a developing move looks perfect, but f7 falls: [Qxf7+] with the bishop behind it, and the king has to walk to d7, where Be6+ or Qe6+ keeps the hunt going. Cover f7 first with ...g6 or ...e6. [%csl Rf7,Re8][%cal Gh5f7] }
5. Qxf7+ ) ) 3... cxd5
{ Trading on d5 hands Black an unchallenged central pawn with gain of time on the exposed c4 bishop—demonstrating why an early Bc4 poses zero problems for the Caro-Kann! White must either give check ([Bb5+ Nc6] or [Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Qxd7] leaves Black comfortably ahead in central control) or tuck the bishop away to [Bb3], followed by [d4] and [Nf3]. With White having spent extra moves on the bishop, Black enjoys effortless development via [Nc6], [Nf6], [Bf5] or [Bg4], and [e6]. [%cal Gc4b5] }
4. Bb5+
{ [Bd7] is the simplest block: if White trades bishops, Black takes back with development. [Nc6] is fine too, keeping the bishop for later. Either way Black has the centre, and White's bishop has wasted time. After ...Bd7 Bxd7+ Nxd7, Black goes on with ...Ngf6, ...e6 and ...Bd6, or ...e5 at once, a free game with a strong pawn on d5. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc8d7,Gb8c6] }
( 4. Bb3 ) 4... Bd7 ( 4... Nc6 ) ) 2... d5 3. e5 ( 3. exd5
{ White trades and opens the e-file. Take back with the pawn, [cxd5]. Black keeps a pawn in the centre, and the c6 square is free again for the knight: this is the point of 1...c6, as Black doesn't have to recapture with the queen as in the Scandinavian. The pawns are no longer symmetrical: Black's c-pawn has gone, so the c-file is half-open for Black's rooks, while White has the half-open e-file. Taking with the queen is playable but lets White gain time with Nc3 or c4. [%csl Yc6,Yd5][%cal Gc6d5] }
3... cxd5
{ The Exchange. The structure is a Queen's Gambit Exchange with colours reversed and White a move up: White has the half-open e-file and plays on the kingside and for e5, Black has the half-open c-file and plays on the queenside, often with a minority attack ...b5-b4. White's main plan is [Bd3] with [c3], keeping Black's light bishop out of f5 and making it hard to develop. [c4] is the Panov-Botvinnik Attack, a completely different game with an isolated pawn on d4. [Nf3], [Bf4] and [Bb5+] are other setups. Black's priorities are the same in all of them: ...Nc6, ...Nf6 and the light bishop out before ...e6. [%csl Yf5][%cal Gf1d3,Gc2c4,Gg1f3] }
4. Bd3
{ The bishop takes f5 away from Black's light bishop. Develop with [Nc6], hitting d4, then ...Nf6 and, if White allows it, ...Bg4. [Nf6] first is also good, with ...Nc6 or ...Bg4 next. Black's ideal setup puts the light bishop on g4, or on f5 after ...g6 to trade White's good bishop, the dark bishop on d6, the queen on c7, the king castled short and the rooks on the c-file. White's plan is c3, Bf4, Nf3 or Ne2, and a slow kingside build-up. [%csl Yf5,Yd4][%cal Gb8c6,Gg8f6] }
( 4. c4
{ The Panov-Botvinnik Attack. White takes on an isolated d-pawn to get quick development, a grip on e5 and chances against Black's king. Develop with [Nf6], adding a defender to d5; [e6] is also played. Don't take on c4 at once: after [dxc4 Bxc4] White's bishop develops in one move with a good game. Taking is better once White's light bishop has moved. Black's general plan: blockade d5 with a knight, trade pieces and attack the isolated pawn, while White wants a kingside attack before that happens. [%csl Yd5,Ye5][%cal Gg8f6,Ge7e6] }
4... Nf6
{ [Nc3] adds a second attacker on d5: this is the main line. Pushing past with [c5] gives up the pressure on d5 for a queenside majority, and Black hits the chain while White is undeveloped. Other moves allow ...dxc4 with an easy game. After Nc3, Black decides how to set up: ...e6, ...Nc6 or ...g6, and in all of them the light bishop waits, since Qb3 is coming against b7. [%csl Yd5,Yb7][%cal Gb1c3,Gc4c5] }
( 4... e6 ) ( 4... Nc6
{ [Nc3] develops and adds a second attacker on d5; Black should answer with ...Nf6, reaching the normal Panov lines. [Nf3] is the other way, also met by ...Nf6 or ...Bg4. The knight on c6 hits d4, but for one move d5 lacks the knight from f6, so don't hurry with the light bishop: on f5 or g4 before ...Nf6, it can lose the d5 pawn. [%csl Yd5,Yd4][%cal Gb1c3,Gg1f3] }
5. Nc3
{ [Nf6] guards d5, reaching the main Panov lines. Black can't develop the bishop first here: d5 has only the queen to defend it, and cxd5 would also hit the knight on c6. After ...Nf6, White picks between Nf3 and Bg5, which pins the knight and adds a third attacker on d5, and Black between ...Bg4 and ...e6 setups. Both lead to the typical middlegames with an isolated pawn on d4. [%csl Yd5,Yd4][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 5. Nf3 ) ( 5. c5 $6
{ Pushing [c5] tries to grab queenside space, yet releasing all tension on d5 hands Black a free central counterpunch! [e5] hits d4 with full force: after [e5 Bb5 exd4 Qxd4 Be7], Black opens diagonals for both bishops and turns White's early c5-pawn into an overextended target. Keep tension on d5 with [w:Nc3] or [w:Nf3] instead. [%csl Yc5][%cal Re7e5] }
5... e5 6. Bb5 exd4 7. Qxd4 Be7 ) 5... Nf6 ( 5... Bf5 $4
{ The bishop wants out, but d5 has only the queen to defend it. [cxd5] wins the pawn and hits the knight on c6, and after it moves White keeps a clean extra pawn. Play ...Nf6 first, and only then develop the bishop. [%csl Rc6,Rd5][%cal Gc4d5] }
6. cxd5 ) ) 5. Nc3
{ Three main moves. [e6] is solid: Black aims for an isolated-pawn position with ...Be7 or ...Bb4. [Nc6] is more active, hitting d4 and preparing ...Bg4. [g6] is a Grünfeld-like fianchetto, giving up d5 for quick development and pressure on d4. Keep the light bishop home for now: on f5 or g4 it leaves b7, and Qb3 hits b7 and d5 together. Taking on c4 is still premature: Bxc4 develops with tempo. [%csl Yb7][%cal Ge7e6,Gb8c6,Gg7g6] }
( 5. c5
{ The Gunderam. White releases the tension and grabs space on the queenside, so d5 is safe and the isolated pawn is gone, but White has spent time with pawns. Black's best plan is to hit the chain at once: ...b6 attacks c5, and ...e5 breaks in the centre, using the fact that White's d4 pawn has lost the c-pawn's support. ...Nc6 and ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 develop fast. White wants Bb5, Nf3, O-O and b4 to keep the c5 pawn, so Black should strike before that setup is ready. [%csl Yc5][%cal Ge7e5,Gb7b6,Gb8c6] }
5... e5 ( 5... b6 ) ( 5... Nc6 ) ) 5... e6
{ White develops with [Nf3]. Black has a choice of setups: ...Be7 and castling, ...Bb4 pinning the knight in Nimzo-Indian style, or ...Nc6. Black's long-term plan is to fight the isolated pawn: blockade d5, trade pieces, ...b6 and ...Bb7. White's plan is Bd3, O-O, Re1 and Ne5 with a kingside attack, and sometimes d5 to break open the centre. White can also play c5, grabbing space on the queenside. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gg1f3,Gf8e7,Gf8b4] }
( 5... Nc6 ) ( 5... g6
{ Black gives up the d5 pawn's support for a quick fianchetto. Hit d5 at once with [Qb3] or [cxd5], before the bishop reaches g7 and Black castles. White can win the pawn for a while, but Black gets fast development, pressure on d4 from the g7 bishop and a lead in time while White's queen and pawns are busy. Quiet moves like Nf3 let Black reach a good Grünfeld-like position with ...Bg7, ...O-O and ...dxc4. [%csl Yd5,Yg7][%cal Gd1b3,Gc4d5] }
6. Qb3
{ [Bg7] and castling: Black gives the d5 pawn for development. After [Bg7 cxd5 O-O], Black wins the pawn back with ...Nbd7-b6 or ...Na6-b4, or gets play against White's queen. Defending d5 with ...e6 is solid but passive, as the g7 bishop then bites on a closed diagonal. Taking on c4 first is also playable: after ...dxc4 Bxc4, Black continues ...Bg7 and ...O-O. [%csl Yd5,Yd4][%cal Gf8g7] }
( 6. cxd5
{ [Bg7] prepares to take back on d5 later; [Nxd5] at once is also played. If White tries to keep the pawn with Qb3 and Bc4, Black develops fast and attacks it with ...O-O, ...Nbd7-b6 and pressure on d4, and White's pieces are tied to the pawn. Usually Black wins the pawn back and gets a good Grünfeld-like position with pressure on d4 down the long diagonal. [%csl Yd5,Yd4][%cal Gf8g7,Gf6d5] }
6... Bg7 ( 6... Nxd5 ) ) 6... Bg7 ) ( 5... Bf5 $2
{ The bishop goes to its favourite square, but it leaves b7 without a guard. [Qb3] attacks b7 and d5 at once, and Black can't hold both: White wins a pawn. In the Panov the light bishop waits until b7 is safe; play ...e6, ...Nc6 or ...g6 first. [%csl Rb7,Rd5][%cal Gd1b3] }
6. Qb3 ) ( 5... Bg4 $2
{ Pinning nothing yet, the bishop leaves b7 for the same reason. [Qb3] hits b7 and d5, and f3 can kick the bishop later: Black loses a pawn. Develop with ...e6, ...Nc6 or ...g6, and bring the bishop out once the queenside is safe. [%csl Rb7,Rd5][%cal Gd1b3] }
6. Qb3 ) 6. Nf3
{ [Be7] is the classical move; [Bb4] pins the knight and leads to Nimzo-Indian positions after Bd3 or a3. [Nc6] is a little less precise: White can play c5 and then Bb5 or b4-b5, fighting for e5 with a queenside bind. Black's plan in all lines: castle, put a knight on d5 after the trade there, and trade pieces, since every trade makes the isolated pawn weaker. White wants to keep pieces on and attack with Bd3, O-O, Re1, Ne5 and Bg5. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gf8e7,Gf8b4,Gb8c6] }
6... Be7
{ White usually plays [cxd5] or [Bd3]. After cxd5, Black takes back with the knight, blockading the isolated pawn, and Bd3, O-O and Re1 follow. With Bd3, White keeps the tension and lets Black decide when to take on c4: ...dxc4 Bxc4 O-O gives a Queen's Gambit Accepted structure. White's plans: Re1, Ne5, Qe2 and a kingside attack, or d5 to open lines. Black's: blockade d5 with a knight, ...Bf6 to hit d4, ...b6 and ...Bb7, and trades. [%csl Yd5,Yd4][%cal Gc4d5,Gf1d3] }
( 6... Bb4
{ [cxd5] and [Bd3] are the main moves. After cxd5 Nxd5, Black hits the c3 knight twice, and White defends with Qc2 or Bd2. Black often trades on c3 to fix White's pawns, then attacks the c3 pawn, or keeps the bishop and castles. With Bd3, ...dxc4 Bxc4 O-O gives a Nimzo-Indian structure with an isolated pawn. The pin is Black's main asset: White can't use the knight on c3 freely, and e4 is weaker. [%csl Yc3,Ye4][%cal Gc4d5,Gf1d3] }
7. cxd5 ( 7. Bd3 ) ) ( 6... Nc6 ) 7. cxd5 ( 7. Bd3 ) ) ( 4. Nf3
{ The bishop isn't stopped yet. [Nc6], [Nf6] and [Bg4] all work. ...Bg4 is the most direct: it pins the knight that guards d4, and if White plays h3, Black can take on f3 or keep the pin with ...Bh5. A useful rule from many coaches: develop the light bishop before ...e6, preferably to g4 when White's knight is on f3. Black's setup then is ...e6, ...Bd6, ...Nf6, castling and the c-file. White can still play Bd3 and c3, or go into a Panov with c4. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6,Gg8f6,Gc8g4] }
4... Nc6
{ White can pin with [Bb5], play [c3] or [Bd3], or go into a Panov with [c4]. Against the pin, Black continues ...Bg4, ...e6 and ...Bd6, and if White takes on c6, Black gets doubled pawns but also the half-open b-file and a firm grip on the centre. If White ever plays h3 while the f3 knight is pinned, taking on f3 is often best. Against c3 or Bd3, Black plays ...Bg4 at once. The main risk is a passive setup with ...e6 before the bishop is out. [%cal Gf1b5,Gc2c3,Gf1d3,Gc2c4] }
( 4... Nf6
{ Black develops the king's knight first while keeping the light-squared bishop flexible between g4 and f5. White has four clean setups: [Bd3] eyes h7 and invites [Nc6], [c3] and [Bg4]; [Ne5] plants an immediate outpost that makes [Nbd7] awkward until [Nc6] challenges it; [Bf4] mirrors the Carlsbad bishop diagonal; and [Bb5+ Bd7 Bd3] trades off Black's good light-squared bishop before [e6] closes the chain. [%cal Gf1d3,Gf3e5,Gc1f4,Gf1b5] }
5. Bd3 ( 5. Ne5 ) ( 5. Bf4 ) ( 5. Bb5+ ) ) ( 4... Bg4
{ Bringing the bishop straight to g4 pins the knight that guards d4 before playing [e6], solving Black's classic Caro-Kann bishop problem in one stroke. Asking the bishop its intentions with [h3] is sharpest: after [h3 Bxf3 Qxf3] White gains the bishop pair and eyes d5, while [h3 Bh5] invites [Bb5+] or [c3] and [w:Qb3] targeting b7. Immediate [Bb5+] and the Panov transposition [c4] are strong alternatives. [%csl Yg4][%cal Gh2h3,Gf1b5,Gc2c4] }
5. h3 ( 5. Bb5+ ) ( 5. c4 ) ) ( 4... Bf5
{ Bringing the bishop to f5 in the Exchange Variation before playing [b:Nc6] or [b:Nf6] leaves b7 and c6 lightly guarded, and White can test it right away! [c4] transposes into a sharp Panov line where [w:Qb3] hits b7 and d5 together, checking on b5 forces [Bb5+ Nc6 Ne5] against the pinned knight, and [Ne5] at once threatens [w:Bb5+] to disrupt Black's smooth kingside development. [%cal Gc2c4] }
5. c4 ( 5. Bb5+ ) ( 5. Ne5 ) ) 5. Bb5
{ Pinning the c6-knight in the Exchange Caro-Kann stops Black from pressuring d4 and prepares [Ne5] or [O-O]. [Bg4] is the active classical reply, counter-pinning the f3-knight outside the pawn chain before playing [b:e6]. [e6] immediately is remarkably solid too (favoured by grandmasters seeking a clean Carlsbad fight): Black meets [w:Ne5] or [w:c4] with [b:Bd7] and [b:Bd6]/[b:Nge7] without weakening the kingside. [%cal Ge7e6] }
( 5. c3 ) ( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. c4 ) 5... e6 ( 5... Bg4 ) ) ( 4. c3 ) ( 4. Bf4
{ White develops the dark bishop first, before deciding between Bd3 and c4. Black answers [Nc6] or [Nf6], and the light bishop is free to come to f5 or g4, since White has not played Bd3. A good setup for Black is ...Nc6, ...Nf6, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4, ...e6 and ...Bd6 to challenge the f4 bishop. If White plays c3 and Bd3 later, the game reaches Rubinstein-like positions with Black's bishop already outside. White's other plan is c4, a Panov with the bishop on f4. [%csl Yf5,Yg4][%cal Gb8c6,Gg8f6] }
4... Nc6 ( 4... Nf6 ) ) ( 4. Nc3
{ The knight on c3 doesn't do much here: it blocks the c-pawn, so White can't support d4 with c3 or attack d5 with c4, and d5 is solid. Black develops with [Nf6] and [Nc6] and is comfortable. Next comes ...Bg4 or ...Bf5, then ...e6, ...Bd6 and castling. White's knight may go on to b5 or e2, but Black has no weaknesses for it to aim at. [%csl Yc3,Yd5][%cal Gg8f6,Gb8c6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... Nc6 ) ) ( 4. Bb5+
{ Block with [Bd7], the simplest: if the bishops are traded, Black's problem piece is gone, and the recapture develops a piece. [Nc6] is also fine: the knight develops, and if White takes on c6, the doubled pawns are made up for by the bishop pair and the half-open b-file. Black then plays ...Nf6, ...e6 and ...Bd6 with an easy game. White's check has achieved little: the bishop usually has to come back or be traded. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gc8d7,Gb8c6] }
4... Bd7 ( 4... Nc6 ) ) 4... Nc6
{ [c3] guards d4 and opens the way to b3 for White's queen, which can hit b7 once the light bishop leaves. [Nf3] is natural but allows ...Bg4 at once: the bishop is out with tempo, pinning the knight that guards d4. White also wants Bf4, taking the b8-h2 diagonal before Black's bishop reaches d6. Black's answers: ...Nf6 to support ...Bg4, or ...Qc7 to stop Bf4. White must watch the d4 pawn: ...Qb6 against d4 and b2 is a common idea. [%csl Yf4,Yd4][%cal Gc2c3,Gg1f3] }
( 4... Nf6
{ Because White's bishop on d3 already controls f5, Black develops [Nf6] first and waits to see whether White blocks g4 before committing the c8-bishop. [c3] is the classic Exchange tabiya move, cementing d4 and preparing [Bf4] to contest the h2-b8 diagonal ([c3 Nc6 Bf4 Bg4 Qb3]). developing the knight allows [Nf3 Bg4] (or [Nf3 Nc6 c3]), while [Bf4] grabs the key light diagonal immediately. [%cal Gc2c3] }
5. c3 ( 5. Nf3 ) ( 5. Bf4 ) ) 5. c3
{ Black chooses between [Nf6], which covers g4 so the bishop can go there after Bf4, and [Qc7], which stops Bf4 and watches e5 and h2. With ...Nf6, Black answers Bf4 with ...Bg4, and h3 with ...g6 and ...Bf5 to trade White's good bishop. With ...Qc7, White's best is h3 or Ne2 with Bf4 later, and Black follows with ...g6 and ...Bf5, or ...e5 if White neglects that square. Both moves are sound; what matters is getting the light bishop out before ...e6. [%csl Yf4,Yg4][%cal Gg8f6,Gd8c7] }
( 5. Nf3 ) 5... Nf6
{ [Bf4] is the main line, the Rubinstein: the bishop takes the b8-h2 diagonal before Black can cover it with ...Bd6, and Qb3 is coming against b7. [h3] stops ...Bg4 first, and [Nf3] allows it. Against all three, Black's light bishop comes out at once: to g4 if it can, or to f5 after ...g6. Then ...e6, ...Bd6 to trade the dark bishops, short castling and play on the c-file. This quiet-looking line still sets Black real problems, and many strong players use it with White. [%csl Yg4][%cal Gc1f4,Gh2h3,Gg1f3] }
( 5... Qc7
{ The queen stops Bf4 and keeps an eye on e5 and h2. White's best is [h3], keeping the bishop off g4, or [Ne2] with Bf4 later, so that the knight doesn't block the f-pawn. Black can then play ...g6 and ...Bf5, trading White's best bishop, or ...e5 if White neglects that square: after dxe5 Nxe5, Black has an isolated pawn on d5 but very active pieces. ...Nf6 and ...Bg4 are still possible against Ne2. [%csl Ye5,Yf4][%cal Gh2h3,Gg1e2] }
6. h3
{ [Nf6] or [g6]. With ...g6, the light bishop comes to f5 anyway to trade White's good bishop, and the dark bishop goes to g7, where it supports ...e5. Black keeps an eye on e5: if White doesn't cover it, ...e5 breaks the centre open with active pieces. White's plan is Nf3, O-O and Re1, keeping e5 under control, and maybe Qb3 against b7 and d5. After the light bishops are traded, the minority attack with ...b5 and ...b4 is Black's long-term plan. [%csl Yf5,Ye5][%cal Gg8f6,Gg7g6] }
( 6. Ne2 ) 6... Nf6 ( 6... g6 ) ) 6. Bf4
{ Play [Bg4] at once, before White plays h3. The bishop is active: it eyes d1 and e2 and makes Nf3 awkward, and it will be guarded by the queen after ...Qd7 or ...Qc8. White's most testing reply is Qb3, hitting b7 and adding pressure on d5. Black meets it calmly by defending b7, then plays ...e6, ...Bd6 to trade White's dark bishop, castles and starts the minority attack. Slower moves such as ...e6 now leave the light bishop inside the chain for good. [%csl Yb7][%cal Gc8g4,Gd1b3] }
( 6. h3
{ White has spent a move to keep the bishop off g4. Answer with [g6] and ...Bf5: the light bishop still gets out and trades White's best piece. ...Bg7 and castling follow, with ...Qc7 or ...Qb6 and a rook on the c-file. ...e6 is playable but passive: the c8 bishop stays shut in, and White's light bishop is the better one for the rest of the game. If White takes on f5, don't fear the doubled pawns after ...gxf5: they guard e4 for good, and ...e6 makes the structure solid. Black's minority attack ...b5-b4 comes later. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc8f5] }
6... g6 ) ( 6. Nf3 ) 6... Bg4
{ [Qb3] is White's most testing move: it hits b7 and adds pressure on d5, and the bishop on g4 has left the queenside without a guard. Calmer moves like Nf3, Ne2 or f3 give Black an easy game with ...e6 and ...Bd6. After Qb3, Black has to decide how to cover b7: with the queen, or by chasing White's queen with ...Na5. The one thing to avoid is a natural developing move that drops the b-pawn. [%csl Yb7,Yd5][%cal Gd1b3] }
7. Qb3
{ Defend b7 with [Qd7] or [Qc8], or chase the queen first with [Na5]. The queen on d7 guards the bishop on g4 and, unlike on c8, keeps d6 free for the f8 bishop after ...e6. Black follows with ...e6, ...Bd6 to trade the dark bishops, and castling. After ...Na5, the queen usually checks on a4 and Black blocks on d7: the knight on a5 can later come to c4. White's plan is Nd2, Ngf3, O-O and Ne5, while Black aims for the minority attack with ...Rab8, ...b5 and ...b4. [%cal Gd8d7,Gf8d6] }
7... Qd7
{ White develops with [Nd2], then Ngf3, O-O and Ne5 to hit the queen and the bishop. Taking on b7 now is risky: after Qxb7 Rb8 the rook arrives with tempo, and White's queen spends more moves getting out while Black develops. Black's next moves are ...e6, ...Bd6 and castling. White keeps the queen on b3 only while it ties Black down; it often drops back to c2 once Black has finished developing. [%csl Yb7,Ye5][%cal Gb1d2,Ge7e6] }
( 7... Qc8 ) ( 7... Na5 ) ( 7... e6 $4
{ A natural developing move, but it leaves b7 hanging. After [Qxb7], the queen hits the knight on c6 and the rook on a8, and Black is a pawn down with a broken queenside. Defend b7 first with ...Qd7 or ...Qc8, or chase the queen with ...Na5. [%csl Rc6,Ra8][%cal Gb3b7] }
8. Qxb7 ) ( 7... Qc7 $4
{ The queen defends b7, but on c7 it stands on the bishop's diagonal: [Bxc7] wins the queen. The f4 bishop covers every square from b8 to h2. Defend with ...Qd7 or ...Qc8. [%csl Rc7][%cal Gf4c7] }
8. Bxc7 ) 8. Nd2
{ [e6] develops the f8 bishop, and only now closes the light bishop's way back, which doesn't matter since it is already outside on g4. Black's plan is ...Bd6 to trade White's strong dark bishop, short castling, and play on the queenside with ...Rab8 and ...b5-b4, or with ...Na5-c4. White aims for Ngf3, O-O and Ne5, hitting the queen and the bishop, and starts kingside play. The position is balanced: White has the attack, Black the sounder structure. [%csl Ye5][%cal Ge7e6,Gf8d6] }
8... e6
{ White plays [Ngf3], ready for Ne5 to hit the queen on d7 and the bishop on g4, with castling next. For now the knight on f3 is pinned, which is why Black is happy to take on f3 at the right moment, giving up the bishop pair for a solid structure. Black's next move, ...Bd6, offers to trade White's dark bishop, the best minor piece on the board here. [%csl Ye5,Yg4][%cal Gg1f3,Gf8d6] }
9. Ngf3
{ [Bd6] offers to trade White's good dark bishop. After Bxd6 Qxd6, Black castles and plays the minority attack: ...Rab8, ...b5 and ...b4 to leave White with a weak pawn on c3 or an isolated one on d4. White's play is on the kingside: O-O, a rook to e1, Ne5 and sometimes f4. Another idea for Black is ...Bh5 and ...Bg6, trading the light bishops too. Once the bishops are gone, Black's structure is at least as good as White's. [%csl Yc3,Yd4][%cal Gf8d6,Gb7b5] }
9... Bd6 ) ( 3. Nc3
{ White defends e4 with the knight. Black's main move is [dxe4]: Black gives up the centre but develops with gain of time on the knight that recaptures, with ...Bf5, ...Nd7 or ...Nf6 next. That is the heart of the Caro-Kann: a solid position with no weaknesses, in which the light bishop gets out. [g6], the Gurgenidze, keeps the pawn on d5 and fianchettoes against d4, a rarer but sound choice. [b:e6] transposes to a French in which Black has spent a move on ...c6 and the c8 bishop is shut in. Moves that leave the tension, like ...Nf6, let White push e5 and gain time. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd5e4,Gg7g6] }
3... dxe4
{ Take back with [Nxe4]. The knight sits in the centre, and Black's next move will hit it. This is the starting point of the Classical lines, played in millions of games: ...Bf5 develops the bishop with tempo, ...Nd7 prepares ...Ngf6, and ...Nf6 offers a trade at once. Gambits with f3 or Bc4 here give a pawn for development, but Black stays solid and keeps the extra pawn with careful play. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gc3e4] }
( 3... g6
{ The Gurgenidze. Black keeps the pawn on d5 and fianchettoes the bishop, which will hit d4 from g7. White's simplest plan is [Nf3] and [h3], keeping a strong centre, or [e5], which closes the centre and turns the game into an Advance-like structure where the g7 bishop bites on the pawn chain. Black's play: ...Bg7, ...dxe4 or ...Nh6 and ...Nf5, and ...Bg4 against a knight on f3. With White's knight on c3, the c-pawn can't support d4, which is why some players meet this setup with 3.Nd2 instead. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gg1f3,Gh2h3,Ge4e5] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. h3 ) ( 4. e5 ) ) ( 3... Nf6 $2
{ A natural developing move, but it invites [e5]: the knight is kicked, and after ...Nfd7 or ...Ne4 Black is in a bad Advance, with no time for ...Bf5 and ...c5. Club players often miss the push and play Bd3, exd5 or f3, which lets Black reach a normal game. Take on e4 first. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5 ) ( 3... b5 $6
{ The idea is ...b4 to kick the knight, but it weakens c6 and a6. After [e5], ...b4 only sends the knight to a better square, Black's queenside is loose and the centre is White's. White should push at once: slower moves like Nf3 let ...b4 and ...dxe4 work. [%csl Rb5,Rc6][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5 ) 4. Nxe4
{ Black has three main moves. [Bf5], the Classical, develops the bishop with tempo before ...e6. [Nd7], the Karpov line, prepares ...Ngf6, so that a trade on f6 is met by the other knight and Black keeps a sound structure. [Nf6] lets White trade on f6 and accepts doubled pawns for fast development: ...exf6 (the Tartakower) or ...gxf6 (the Bronstein-Larsen). All three are sound: the Classical is the most solid, the Karpov the most flexible, and the 4...Nf6 lines the most dynamic. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gc8f5,Gb8d7,Gg8f6] }
( 4. f3 $6
{ The Rasa-Studier gambit, a Blackmar-Diemer idea. After [exf3 Nxf3], White has open lines and a lead in development, but Black has an extra pawn and no weaknesses. Develop calmly with ...Nf6, ...Bg4 and ...e6. Holding on with ...e3 or developing with ...Bf5 lets White take back on e4 with a good centre. [%csl Rf3][%cal Ge4f3] }
4... exf3 5. Nxf3 ) ( 4. Bc4 $6
{ The von Hennig gambit. Black keeps the pawn with [Nf6] and ...Bf5 and develops easily; White's bishop on c4 has no target. The passive ...e6 is a common reply that shuts in the light bishop and lets White win the pawn back. [%csl Rc4,Re4][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ) 4... Bf5
{ [Ng3] chases the bishop and is the main line: after ...Bg6, White gains space with h4 and h5, or develops with Nf3 and Bd3. [Nc5] hits b7 and eyes e6: Black answers ...b6, or the active ...e5. [Ng5] aims at e6 and f7, hoping for a sacrifice there later. In all lines the bishop is Black's best piece, so White's plans are about trading it or shutting it out, while Black's plan is ...e6, ...Nd7, ...Ngf6 and a solid, well-developed position. [%csl Yf5][%cal Ge4g3,Ge4c5,Ge4g5] }
( 4... Nd7 5. Ng5
{ Two moves are good. [Ngf6] is the safe one: it develops and guards h7, and after Bd3 Black plays ...e6, ...Bd6 and only then ...h6. [h6] kicks the knight at once, and here it works, but Black must know the trick that follows: Ne6!, when ...fxe6?? Qh5+ mates and the right answer is ...Qb6. The knight on g5 is there for tricks on e6 and f7, so in this line every move needs a reason. [%csl Ye6,Yf7][%cal Gg8f6,Gh7h6] }
5... Ngf6
{ [Bd3] with N1f3 and Qe2 is the main line: White's pieces aim at e6 and h7, and Black must be careful with ...h6. [Bc4] transposes to the lines above after ...e6 and Qe2. The knight on g5 isn't attacking anything yet; it waits for Black to kick it too early, so that it can sacrifice itself on e6 or f7. [%csl Ye6,Yf7][%cal Gf1d3,Gf1c4] }
( 5... h6
{ [Ne6] is the critical try: the knight can't be taken, since ...fxe6 Qh5+ mates, and it hits the queen and the bishop on f8. After ...Qb6 Nxf8 Nxf8, White has the bishop pair, but Black has developed and is fine. The quiet retreat [N5f3] is also playable: the knight has achieved little, and Black goes on with ...Ngf6 and ...e6 or ...c5, a normal Karpov position with a useful ...h6 thrown in. [%csl Yd8,Yf8][%cal Gg5e6,Gg5f3] }
6. Ne6
{ The knight attacks the queen and the bishop on f8, and it can't be taken. [Qb6] is the answer: the queen steps away with an eye on d4, and after Nxf8 Nxf8 Black has given up the bishop pair but develops quickly with ...Ngf6, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 and ...e6. The check on a5 looks just as good, but after Bd2 the queen has to move again, and White develops with Bd3 while the knight still sits on e6. [%csl Yd4,Ye6][%cal Gd8b6] }
( 6. N5f3 ) 6... Qb6 ( 6... fxe6 $4
{ Taking the knight is the reflex, and it is mate: [Qh5+ g6 Qxg6#]. With the pawn on h6 instead of h7, nothing can take back on g6. Answer the knight with ...Qb6. [%csl Rg6,Re8][%cal Gd1h5] }
7. Qh5+ g6 8. Qxg6# ) ( 6... Qa5+ $2
{ The check gains nothing. After [Bd2], the queen must move again, and with Bd3 and Nf3 White develops while the knight on e6 still cramps Black: the f8 bishop can't come out until it is dealt with. Play ...Qb6, and after Nxf8 Nxf8 Black is fine. [%csl Ra5,Re6][%cal Gc1d2] }
7. Bd2 ) ) 6. Bd3
{ [e6] is the move: it frees the f8 bishop and covers f5 and d5. Kicking the knight with ...h6 allows Ne6!, when ...fxe6?? Bg6 is mate, since the h-pawn has left h7 and nothing can take the bishop; even after the best reply, ...Qb6, White keeps a clear edge. After ...e6, White plays N1f3 and Qe2, and Black develops with ...Bd6 and only then ...h6. The bishop on d3 eyes h7 and g6, so watch for tricks on that diagonal. [%csl Yh7,Yg6][%cal Ge7e6] }
( 6. Bc4 ) ( 6. Nxf7 $2
{ The sacrifice looks tempting with the knight already on g5, but nothing backs it up yet. After [Kxf7], White has a pawn for the knight and only a few checks. The sacrifices on f7 and e6 work only when the queen and the bishop are already aimed there. Develop with Bd3 or Bc4. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge8f7] }
6... Kxf7 ) 6... e6
{ [N1f3] brings the second knight. White's pieces aim at e6 and f7, so Black must keep them guarded before kicking the g5 knight. White's usual setup is Qe2, then Ne4 after ...h6, or a sacrifice on e6 if Black plays ...h6 too early. Black's correct order is ...Bd6 first, then ...h6 once the queen is on e2. [%csl Ye6,Yf7][%cal Gg1f3,Gf8d6] }
7. N1f3
{ [Bd6] develops and keeps e5 under control. Then after Qe2, ...h6 is safe, and Ne4 trades a pair of knights. Kicking the knight first with ...h6 lets White sacrifice on e6: Deep Blue beat Kasparov that way in the last game of their 1997 match, 7...h6? 8.Nxe6!. ...Be7 is also playable, though more passive. Moving the d7 knight away now leaves e5 to White's knight. [%csl Ye5,Ye6][%cal Gf8d6] }
7... Bd6
{ [Qe2] brings the queen to the e-file behind the knight and prepares Ne4 after ...h6, trading a pair of knights while keeping the queen active. White's aim is long-term pressure on e6 and a kingside attack after castling. Black's plan is ...h6, ...Nxe4 or ...Nf6 regrouping, ...Qc7 and ...b6 with ...Bb7, or ...c5 to free the game. [%csl Ye6][%cal Gd1e2,Gh7h6] }
( 7... Nb6 $4
{ The knight heads for d5, but it takes a defender away from e5 and f7. After [Ne5], two white knights hit f7, and Black has no good way to cover it: White wins material. Play ...Bd6 and keep the d7 knight where it is. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf3e5] }
8. Ne5 ) 8. Qe2
{ Now [h6] is right: with the bishop out of f8 and the queen able to block on e7, the sacrifice on e6 doesn't work any more, because Black's king has room. White's best is Ne4, trading a pair of knights. Black follows with ...Nxe4 Qxe4 Nf6, gaining time on the queen, then ...c5 or ...Qc7 and castling. Other moves let White keep the knight on g5 and build up with Ne5 against f7. [%csl Yg5,Ye4][%cal Gh7h6] }
8... h6
{ [Ne4] trades a pair of knights and keeps the initiative: after ...Nxe4 Qxe4, the queen is centralised, but ...Nf6 gains time on it. Black is solid: ...c5, ...Qc7, ...b6 and ...Bb7, and the king can castle or even stay in the centre. The sacrifice on e6 fails now: Black's king can step to e7 or f8, and White is just a piece down. [%csl Yd6,Yf6][%cal Gg5e4] }
( 8... Nb6 $2
{ The knight leaves d7, and with it the guard of e5. [Ne5] lands with tempo, and White's pieces swarm toward f7, e6 and h7. Kick the knight with ...h6 first: now that the bishop is on d6, it is safe. [%csl Rf7,Re6][%cal Gf3e5] }
9. Ne5 ) 9. Ne4
{ Take, [Nxe4], then ...Nf6 to kick the queen. Black trades a pair of attackers and gains a tempo in return. Other moves let White keep the strong knight on e4, with Nxd6 for the bishop pair when it suits, or Nxf6+ and a direct attack. This is the main line of the Smyslov: Black stays a little cramped for a while, but there are no weaknesses, and ...c5 or ...b6 and ...Bb7 free the game later. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf6e4,Gd7f6] }
( 9. Nxe6 $2
{ The sacrifice works against ...h6 when the f8 bishop is still home, but not here. After [fxe6] and Qxe6+, Black blocks with the queen on e7, or the king steps aside, and the bishop on d6 covers the key squares: White is simply a piece down for two pawns. Ne4 is the right move. [%csl Re6,Rd6][%cal Gf7e6] }
9... fxe6 ) 9... Nxe4 10. Qxe4
{ [Nf6] hits the queen and develops the last knight. White chooses where the queen goes: h4 keeps an eye on Black's kingside, e2 and d3 are safer. Black's plan: ...b6 and ...Bb7, ...c5 to free the game, and short castling, or Karpov's ...Ke7 idea against Qh4. Black has a little less space, so the main task is to time the freeing break, ...c5 or ...e5, well. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7f6,Ge4h4,Ge4e2] }
10... Nf6
{ [Qh4] or [Qe2]. Against Qh4, Karpov's idea was ...Ke7!: the king defends the f6 knight against Bg5 and stays safe in the middle, and ...g5 or ...Qa5+ can follow to kick the queen. Against Qe2, Black plays ...c5 or ...b6 with ...Bb7, then castles short. White's plans: Bd2 and long castling, then g4-g5 against the f6 knight. Black's: ...c5, trades, and a solid endgame. [%csl Yf6,Yc5][%cal Ge4h4,Ge4e2] }
11. Qh4 ( 11. Qe2 ) ) ( 4... Nf6
{ Black offers a knight trade at once. [Nxf6+] is the main move, and Black chooses which pawn takes back: ...exf6 (the Tartakower) opens lines for fast development, ...gxf6 (the Bronstein-Larsen) opens the g-file. [Ng3] keeps pieces, and Black hits back with ...e5, ...h5 or ...c5. [Bd3] offers the d4 pawn as a gambit, and [Ng5] heads for f7: both are traps more than plans, and Black is fine if it knows the answers. [%csl Yf6][%cal Ge4f6,Ge4g3] }
5. Nxf6+
{ [exf6] (the Tartakower) is the solid choice: fast development, an open e-file for Black's rooks and a safe king behind the f-pawns. [gxf6] (the Bronstein-Larsen) is more combative: the g-file opens for Black's rooks, and the f6 pawn controls e5. Both give White a slightly better structure, so Black must play actively. In both, the light bishop comes out to f5 or g4; after ...exf6 Black usually castles short, after ...gxf6 often long. [%csl Yf6][%cal Ge7f6,Gg7f6] }
( 5. Ng3
{ White avoids the trade and keeps pieces on, but the knight on g3 is less useful than on e4. [e5] hits the centre at once. [h5] threatens ...h4 against the knight, and [c5] also works, hitting d4. Black is fine in all lines: the light bishop comes out to g4 or f5, and White's pieces lack targets. [%csl Yg3][%cal Ge7e5,Gh7h5,Gc6c5] }
5... e5 ( 5... h5
{ Tal's energetic [h5] push threatens [b:h4] to trap or misplace the g3 knight while seizing kingside space before castling! White's best and near-universal answer is [h4], halting the h-pawn in its tracks and securing g5 for White's dark-squared bishop or knight. Trying [Bg5] without stopping the pawn runs into [Bg5 h4] anyway, when [Bg5 h4 Bxf6 hxg3] opens the h-file dangerously for Black's h8 rook. After [h4], Black has secured the g4 square for the light-squared bishop and continues with [Bg4] (or [c5]), [Nbd7] and [e6]. [%cal Gh2h4] }
6. h4 ) ( 5... c5 ) ) ( 5. Bd3
{ The Alekhine gambit. White offers d4 for fast development. [Qxd4] is the test: the queen grabs a central pawn, and Black must then survive Nf3 and Be3 with gain of time against it. The most natural replies are worse: ...Bg4 loses a piece, and ...e6 leaves Black passive and behind. If you take, keep the queen safe: it can come back to d8 or d5. [%csl Yd4,Yd3][%cal Gd8d4] }
5... Qxd4 ( 5... Bg4 $4
{ Developing the bishop with an attack on the queen looks natural, but the bishop is guarded only by the f6 knight. [Nxf6+ gxf6 Qxg4] removes that knight with check, and the queen takes the bishop: White wins a piece. Take the pawn with ...Qxd4, or develop with ...Nbd7 or ...Nxe4. [%csl Rg4,Rf6][%cal Ge4f6] }
6. Nxf6+ gxf6 7. Qxg4 ) ( 5... e6 $2
{ Solid-looking, but passive in a gambit. After [Nxf6+ Qxf6 Nf3], or ...gxf6 instead, White is well ahead in development, Black's c8 bishop is shut in, and the gambit pawn was never taken. Test the gambit with ...Qxd4. [%csl Rc8,Rd4][%cal Ge4f6] }
6. Nxf6+ Qxf6 7. Nf3 ) ) ( 5. Ng5
{ The knight jumps to g5, looking at f7 and e6. [h6] kicks it at once, and the knight must retreat, as the sacrifice on f7 doesn't work. [c5] or ...e6 are also fine. White's idea is the Alien gambit, Nxf7, a knight for a pawn and some checks, which rarely works. [%csl Yg5,Yf7][%cal Gh7h6,Gc6c5] }
5... h6
{ Retreat with [N5f3]. The knight has achieved nothing on g5, but nothing is lost either, and ...h6 is a useful move for Black. Black continues ...Bf5 or ...Bg4, ...e6 and ...Nbd7, with a comfortable position. The knight sacrifice on f7, the Alien gambit, gives a pawn and some checks for a piece: not enough. [%csl Yf7,Yh6][%cal Gg5f3] }
( 5... c5 ) 6. N5f3 ( 6. Nxf7 $2
{ The Alien gambit. After [Kxf7], White has a pawn and some checks for the knight, not enough. Black keeps the king safe with ...Bg4 or ...e6 and ...Kg8, and develops: the extra piece wins in the long run. Retreat the knight to f3 instead. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge8f7] }
6... Kxf7 ) ) 5... exf6
{ Black has doubled f-pawns: the kingside majority can't make a passed pawn, while White's queenside majority can, so the endgame favours White. In return Black develops fast, the e-file is open for Black's rooks, and the f6 and f7 pawns guard the king. White plays [c3], then Bd3, Ne2 and Qc2, aiming at h7; [Bc4] and [Nf3] are also common. Black's plan: ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, the light bishop to f5, g4 or e6, and the knight via d7 to f8 or b6. Keep pieces on the board, as every trade brings the bad endgame closer. [%csl Yh7][%cal Gc2c3,Gf1c4,Gg1f3] }
( 5... gxf6
{ Black has the open g-file and a solid centre, at the cost of a broken structure and a less safe king. White plays [Nf3], [c3] or [Be2], aiming for a quiet game and a better structure. Black's plan is ...Bf5, ...Qc7, ...Nd7, long castling and ...Rg8, with play down the g-file. If White castles short, the g-file points right at White's king; if White castles long, Black's king becomes the target. Black's e-pawn often goes to e6 to support ...c5 later. [%csl Yg8][%cal Gg1f3,Gc2c3,Gf1e2] }
6. Nf3 ( 6. c3 ) ( 6. Be2 ) ) 6. c3
{ [Bd6] develops toward the kingside, aiming at h2. Then ...O-O, ...Re8 (the e-file is the only open one), ...Bg4 or ...Bf5, and the knight goes via d7 to f8, guarding h7. Keep pieces on the board: the more trades, the more White's healthy queenside majority counts. White's best squares: bishop on d3, knight on e2 or f3, queen on c2. [%csl Yh2,Yh7][%cal Gf8d6] }
( 6. Bc4
{ [Bd6] with castling, or [Qe7+], which asks White to block: after Qe2 the queens come off and the endgame suits White's structure, so the check is mostly a way to disrupt White's development when White blocks with a piece. Later, ...Be6 offers to trade the c4 bishop, which straightens Black's pawns if White takes. Black's setup is the same as against c3: ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, and the knight via d7. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gf8d6,Gd8e7] }
6... Bd6 ( 6... Qe7+ ) ) ( 6. Nf3
{ [Bd6] and castling; if White lets it, ...Bg4 pins the knight on f3. Then ...Re8 and ...Nd7-f8, the usual setup. White's knight on f3 can come to h4 or e5 later, but Black's f6 pawn controls e5 and g5. Black should keep pieces on: with queens on, Black's active pieces matter more than the pawns. [%csl Yf3,Ye5][%cal Gf8d6,Gc8g4] }
6... Bd6 ) 6... Bd6
{ [Bd3] aims at h7, and Qc2 will add a second attacker. Black answers by castling and ...Re8, then ...h6 or ...g6 if needed. White's knight usually goes to e2 rather than f3, so it can't be pinned by ...Bg4 and can go on to g3. Black's queen often goes to c7, and the light bishop to e6 or g4. [%csl Yh7][%cal Gf1d3,Ge8g8] }
7. Bd3
{ Castle, [O-O]. The f6 pawn shelters the king, and ...Re8 comes next. Then Black chooses between ...Qc7, ...Nd7-f8 and ...Bg4 or ...Be6. If White plays Qc2, Black blocks the diagonal to h7 with ...h6 or ...g6, or checks first with the rook on the e-file. Black's game is active and solid; the long-term problem is the structure, not the king. [%csl Yh7][%cal Ge8g8,Gf8e8] }
7... O-O
{ [Qc2] hits h7 together with the bishop; [Ne2] develops while keeping the knight safe from ...Bg4 and prepares castling. Against Qc2, the rook check on the e-file makes White commit, then ...h6 or ...g6 shuts the diagonal. White's other plan is Ne2, Qc2 and long castling, a slow kingside attack with g4. Black's counterplay: ...c5, ...Qc7, ...Nd7 and quick development. [%csl Yh7][%cal Gd1c2,Gg1e2] }
8. Qc2
{ [Re8+] first: White usually blocks with Ne2, which keeps the knight away from a pin on f3, and only then Black plays ...h6 or ...g6 to close the b1-h7 diagonal. Be3 and Kf1 are also possible answers to the check. Black's queen and bishop later aim at h2 together, and the knight goes via d7 to f8, an excellent defender of h7. [%csl Yh7][%cal Gf8e8,Gg1e2] }
