{ With the intermediate check on e8, Black forces White to block the e-file before touching the threatened h7 pawn, disrupting White's coordination ahead of [h6+] or [g6+]. [Ne2] is the standard Tartakower reply, keeping the knight unpinned and ready to castle on either wing while Black tucks h7 away with [h6+] and maneuvers [Nd7+] toward f8. [Be3] is the sharp alternative, preparing [O-O-O] with opposite-side attacks. [%csl Yh7][%cal Gg1e2,Gc1e3] }
9. Ne2 ( 9. Be3 ) ) 5. Ng3
{ Retreat with [Bg6]. The bishop stays on its diagonal, aiming at c2 and b1, but White's next idea is h4-h5 to chase it again. Other retreats are worse: on g4 the queen simply takes it, and on e6 it blocks the e-pawn. After ...Bg6, Black's usual plan is ...Nd7, ...Ngf6, ...e6 and ...Qc7, often with long castling. [%csl Yc2][%cal Gf5g6,Gh2h4] }
( 5. Nc5
{ The knight hits b7 and eyes e6. [b6] is safe: after Nb3, Black continues ...e6 and ...Nf6, though the c6 pawn has lost a defender and can be hit later by Ne5, Qf3 or a bishop on g2. [e5]! is the active answer, hitting the centre at once: if the knight takes on b7, ...Qb6 attacks it, and Black wins the time back. After ...e5, Black develops fast and the knight on c5 may become a target. [%csl Yb7,Yc5][%cal Gb7b6,Ge7e5] }
5... b6 ( 5... e5 ) ) ( 5. Ng5
{ The knight aims at e6 and f7, and White hopes for a sacrifice there later. [e6] is the most accurate: Black develops with ...Nd7, ...Ngf6 and ...Bd6 or ...Be7, and kicks the knight with ...h6 once e6 and f7 are safe. [Bg6] also works, but after N1f3 and ...h6 White has the Martian gambit tricks and the quieter Nh3 retreat. Don't kick the knight before your pieces cover e6 and f7. [%csl Ye6,Yf7][%cal Ge7e6,Gf5g6] }
5... e6 ( 5... Bg6 6. N1f3
{ [h6] kicks the knight at once. White's best is Nh3, heading for f4 to hit the bishop; the sacrifices on e6 or f7 don't work here, and Black should know how to accept them. [Nd7] is also good, keeping ...h6 for later. After Nh3, Black plays ...e6, ...Nd7 and ...Bd6 to trade the knight when it lands on f4. [%csl Yg5,Yf4][%cal Gh7h6,Gb8d7] }
6... h6
{ White should retreat, [Nh3], heading for f4 to hit the bishop on g6; Black answers ...e6 and ...Bd6, and the bishop drops back to h7 when the knight lands on f4. The Martian gambit sacrifices on e6 and f7 are popular online, but they don't work: Black takes and keeps the extra piece with careful play, as long as the king and the bishop stay out of knight forks. [%csl Yf4,Yg6][%cal Gg5h3] }
( 6... Nd7 ) 7. Nh3 ( 7. Ne6 $2
{ The Martian gambit. The knight sacrifice looks scary, but after [fxe6] White has some pressure on e6 and Black's king, not enough for a piece. Black should take, then shore up e6 with ...Bf7 and develop. Declining with ...Qd6, a common reply, lets Nxf8 get the material back. White should retreat the knight to h3 instead. [%csl Re6][%cal Gf7e6] }
7... fxe6 ) ( 7. Nxf7 $2
{ Two pawns for a knight. Take with the bishop, [Bxf7]: after ...Kxf7? Ne5+, the knight forks the king and the bishop on g6, and White is fine. With the bishop recapture, Black is simply a piece up for a pawn. White should retreat with Nh3. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gg6f7] }
7... Bxf7 ) ) ) 5... Bg6 ) ( 3. Bd3
{ White defends e4 with the bishop. [dxe4] is the clearest answer: after Bxe4 Nf6, the bishop has to move again and Black develops with tempo, often with ...e5 or ...Bg4 next. [e5] hits back in the centre at once, a good alternative. What Black must avoid is ...Nf6, which invites e5 and a bad Advance in which the knight has no good square. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd5e4,Ge7e5] }
3... dxe4 ( 3... e5 ) ( 3... Nf6 $2
{ Developing with an attack on e4 looks natural, but [e5] kicks the knight, and it has no good square: ...Ne4 is trapped by f3, and ...Nfd7 runs into e6!. White's natural Nc3 or exd5 let Black off, but the push gives a bad Advance for Black. Take on e4 first. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge4e5] }
4. e5
{ The knight is kicked, and every retreat leaves Black worse. [Nfd7] is the most natural, but it walks into e6!, a pawn sacrifice that breaks Black's position; [Ng8] and [Ng4] are the engine's choices, though both lose time. The tempting ...Ne4 is the big mistake: it looks active, but f3 traps it in the middle of the board. Remember the lesson for next time: against 3.Bd3, take on e4 first. [%csl Ye6][%cal Gf6d7,Gf6g8] }
4... Nfd7
{ [e6]! is the strong move: White gives a pawn to break Black's structure. After ...fxe6, Qh5+ and Bxg6+ lead to mate, so Black has to answer with ...Nf6, and after exf7+ Kxf7 Black's king has lost its castling and stands in the open. White develops with Nf3, O-O and Re1. Calmer moves like Nf3 or c3 keep an edge, but a smaller one: they let Black play ...e6 and ...c5 into a French-like game. [%csl Yf7,Yh5][%cal Ge5e6] }
( 4... Ng8 ) ( 4... Ng4 ) ( 4... Ne4 $4
{ The knight jumps forward and looks well placed, but it has no way back. [f3] hits it, and every square it could go to is covered by a pawn or a piece; propping it up with ...f5 only loses it for a pawn after fxe4 fxe4. It is the most common reply here and the worst. Retreat with ...Nfd7 or ...Ng8. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf2f3] }
5. f3 ) 5. e6
{ The pawn on e6 attacks f7 and d7, and it must not be taken with the f-pawn: Qh5+ g6 Bxg6+ hxg6 Qxg6 is mate. [Nf6] is the best answer, bringing the knight back to guard h7 and h5; after exf7+ Kxf7, Black's king is uncomfortable but the position holds. Retreating to b6 instead leaves the kingside bare. Black must play precisely for several moves. [%csl Yf7,Yh5][%cal Gd7f6] }
( 5. c4 $2
{ Hitting d5 looks natural, but it opens the centre while White is ahead. After [dxc4] Bxc4 Nb6, Black gains time on the bishop and follows with ...c5 or ...Bf5, and most of White's edge is gone. The pawn push e6 was much stronger. [%csl Rc4][%cal Gd5c4] }
5... dxc4 ) 5... Nf6 ( 5... fxe6 $4
