6. exf7+ ) ) 4. Bxe4
{ [Nf6] hits the bishop, which has to move again. Black follows with ...e5 or ...Bg4, hitting White's centre with active pieces, and White's bishop has used three moves to reach a square it could have had in one. Black has a free, easy game and no weaknesses. White usually keeps the bishop on f3 or d3 and develops with Ne2 and O-O, but Black is already level. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gg8f6,Ge7e5] }
4... Nf6 ) 3... Bf5 4. Nf3
{ The quiet main line. White develops, guards d4 and plans [Be2] and castling. Black goes on with [e6], which is safe now that the bishop is already on f5. Then there are two ways to play. ...c5 at once hits the centre; with the pawn on e6, the f8 bishop can take back if White plays dxc5. Or ...Nd7 and ...Ne7 first, keeping ...c5 for later and watching e5. Keep an eye on Nh4: White's knight wants to hunt the bishop, so Black often answers castling with ...h6, giving it a home on h7. [%csl Yd4,Yf5][%cal Ge7e6] }
( 4. Nc3
{ White takes e4 away from the bishop and prepares g4, Nge2 and h4: the Van der Wiel, or Shirov, attack. Black plays [e6], and after g4 retreats the bishop to g6. The key idea comes next: ...c5! at once. In this sharp line every tempo counts, and Black's counterplay is in the centre, where White's pawns on d4 and e5 are left with little support once the knights head for the kingside. Black often gives up the h-pawn, or even the bishop, to open lines against White's king. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ge7e6,Gg2g4] }
4... e6
{ [g4] is the critical move, starting the hunt for the bishop: Black answers ...Bg6, and Nge2 with h4 follows. It is the most testing line, and both sides need to know it. [Nf3] or Be3 are quieter and lead to Short-like positions, with the knight on c3 instead of a pawn, so ...c5 and ...Nc6 hit d4 even harder. In the quiet lines Black's plan is the usual ...c5, ...Nc6, ...Qb6 and a knight to e7. Against g4, Black must play ...c5 quickly rather than worry about the bishop. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg2g4,Gg1f3] }
( 4... c5 $6
{ Rushing [c5] before playing [b:e6] tries to save a tempo, yet with Black's f8-bishop still hemmed in by e7 Black cannot recapture c5 smoothly! [dxc5 e6 Bb5+ Nd7 b4] clings to the extra c5-pawn with tempo, leaving Black's queenside bound up and a pawn down. Solidify the chain with [b:e6] first. [%csl Yc5][%cal Rd4c5] }
5. dxc5 e6 6. Bb5+ Nd7 7. b4 ) 5. g4
{ Retreat with [Bg6]. The bishop is safe for now, but White's next ideas are Nge2-f4 to hit it and h4-h5 to trap it, so Black has to be quick. After Nge2, the right answer is ...c5!, opening the centre before White's kingside play gets going. The bishop on g6 can even be given up: if White wins it, Black gets pawns, open lines and a lead in development in return. The tempting ...Be4 is the natural mistake: with a knight on c3, the bishop has no support there. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gf5g6,Gc6c5] }
( 5. Nf3 ) 5... Bg6
{ White's main move is [Nge2], heading for f4 to hit the bishop, with h4 and h5 to come. [h4] at once is also played, threatening h5; Black answers ...h5 or ...h6 and the bishop stays on its diagonal. [Nf3] is quieter. Black's plan is the same in every line: ...c5!, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6, hitting d4 before White's kingside pawns can do damage. The bishop is worth less than time here: if White traps it for two pawns, Black's development and central play usually compensate. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg1e2,Gh2h4,Gc6c5] }
( 5... Be4 $2
{ The bishop looks active on e4, but with White's knight on c3 it has no support there. After [Nxe4 dxe4], Black has given the bishop for a knight, and the pawn on e4 is weak: Bg2 and Qe2 will collect it. Retreat to g6 instead and hit back with ...c5. [%csl Re4][%cal Gc3e4] }
6. Nxe4 dxe4 ) 6. Nge2
{ Strike back with [c5]! White's knights are heading for the kingside, so the centre is where Black's play is. If White plays h4, Black answers ...h5, ready to give the h-pawn: after Nf4 Bh7 Nxh5, Black takes on d4 and develops with ...Nc6, ...Qb6 and ...Nge7, and White's king has no safe home. Keep the bishop away from a knight on f4: being forced to take back with the f-pawn on g6 leaves Black's king too open. Slow setups with ...h6 or ...Ne7 first let White push h4 and h5 with tempo. [%csl Yd4,Yg6][%cal Gc6c5] }
( 6. h4 ) ( 6. Nf3 ) 6... c5
{ [h4] is the main line, threatening h5 to trap the bishop; Black answers ...h5, and White usually takes the pawn with Nf4 and Nxh5 at the cost of time. [Be3] is the solid alternative: it guards d4 and prepares Qd2 and long castling, with a race on both wings. Black's moves are easy to find: ...Nc6, ...cxd4 and ...Qb6, with every piece aimed at d4 and b2. White's pawns on g4 and e5 can turn into targets as soon as the centre opens. [%csl Yd4,Yb2][%cal Gh2h4,Gc1e3] }
7. h4 ( 7. Be3 ) ( 7. Nf4 $2
{ Hitting the bishop at once looks logical, but before h4 the knight jump is too slow. Black develops with [Nc6], hits d4 with ...cxd4 and ...Qb6, and the centre opens while White's pawns on g4 and e5 become targets. Play h4 first, threatening h5, so that Black has to spend a move on ...h5. [%csl Rd4,Rg4][%cal Gb8c6] }
7... Nc6 ) 7... h5
{ [Nf4] hits the bishop and the h5 pawn. After ...Bh7 Nxh5, White has won a pawn, but Black has ...Nc6 and ...cxd4 with fast play in the centre, and the knight on h5 is offside. Black should not let the knight take the bishop on g6, and should not waste time with ...h6-style moves: the counterattack in the centre is everything. Black's queen on b6 and knight on c6 soon put d4 and b2 under fire. [%csl Yd4,Yh5][%cal Ge2f4,Gb8c6] }
8. Nf4 ) 4... e6
{ Black's pawns now form a triangle on c6, d5 and e6, as in the Slav, with the f5 bishop outside it. White's main move is [Be2], followed by castling: Short's system, in which White simply develops and keeps the space the pawn on e5 gives. [Nbd2] heads for b3, from where the knight watches c5, the square of Black's main break. [Bd3] offers to trade bishops; it's playable, but trading White's good bishop for Black's problem piece is a gift. White shouldn't hurry with g4 or h4 without support: the e5 pawn and d4 become targets for ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6. [%csl Yc5,Yd4][%cal Gf1e2,Gb1d2] }
( 4... c5 $2
{ The usual break, but one move too early with the knight already on f3. After [dxc5], the pawn is hard to win back: ...e6 and ...Bxc5 run into b4, and ...Nc6 can be met by Bb5, while the d5 pawn has lost its support. Play ...e6 first, so the f8 bishop is ready to take back on c5. [%csl Rc5,Rd5][%cal Gd4c5] }
5. dxc5 ) 5. Be2
{ Black chooses how to start the queenside play. [c5] hits d4 at once: if White takes on c5, the f8 bishop recaptures with tempo, and if White supports d4 with Be3, ...Qb6 or ...cxd4 follow. [Nd7] keeps more options: it watches e5, prepares ...Ne7, and after White castles, ...h6 gives the bishop a retreat on h7 so that Nh4 doesn't win the bishop pair cheaply. [Ne7] heads for f5, g6 or c6; it is playable, but it blocks the f8 bishop for a while. In all lines Black aims for ...c5 and pressure on d4, while White uses the extra space on the kingside. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc6c5,Gb8d7,Gg8e7] }
( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. Nbd2 ) 5... c5
{ Black has hit the centre. White's main move is [Be3], adding a defender to d4 so that ...cxd4 can be met by Nxd4. [O-O] is also common: it lets Black take on d4, and after ...Nc6 and ...cxd4 Black develops easily, so White often answers with c4 to open the game. Taking at once with dxc5 lets the bishop recapture on c5 with a good game for Black. Remember the key squares: d4 for Black's pressure, b2 for the queen's raids once the c1 bishop has left, and e5, which Black can later undermine with ...f6. [%csl Yd4,Yb2][%cal Gc1e3,Ge1g1] }
( 5... Nd7
{ Black keeps ...c5 in reserve and first watches e5. White castles with [O-O]. Jumping at the bishop with [Nh4] at once is premature: after ...Bg6, a trade on g6 gives Black's rook the h-file, and White has spent two moves on a knight on the rim. Black's plan from here: ...Ne7, ...h6 to give the bishop h7, then ...c5. A more aggressive plan, used by top players, is ...h6 with ...g5, gaining space on the kingside where White's pieces will gather. [%csl Ye5][%cal Ge1g1,Gh7h6] }
6. O-O
{ [h6] is the main move: it gives the bishop a home on h7, so Nh4 only chases it to a square where it still watches c2 and b1. [Ne7] at once is also fine; then Nh4 can be met by ...Bg6, accepting Nxg6 hxg6, when the h-file gives Black's rook a job. After ...h6 Black often plays ...Ne7 and ...c5, or, more ambitiously, ...Ne7 and ...g5, gaining space on the kingside and preparing ...Bg7 to hit e5 from the side. White's typical plan is Nbd2-b3 to control c5, and c4 to open lines. [%cal Gh7h6,Gg7g5] }
6... h6 ( 6... Ne7
{ White has two plans. [Nh4] goes after the bishop at once: after ...Bg6 Nxg6 hxg6, White has the bishop pair, but the h-file is half-open for Black's rook, and Black's structure stays solid; ...Nf5, ...c5 and ...Qb6 follow. [Nbd2] with Nb3 is slower and aims at c5, the square of Black's main break, so that ...c5 can be met by a capture that leaves White in charge of the dark squares. Against Nbd2, ...h6 is a useful move: it makes h7 available to the bishop and rules out Ng5 ideas. [%csl Yc5,Yf5][%cal Gf3h4,Gb1d2] }
7. Nh4 ( 7. Nbd2 ) ) ) ( 5... Ne7 ) 6. Be3
{ The bishop on e3 defends d4 but leaves b2 without a guard. [Qb6] hits it at once and adds a defender to c5. [cxd4] first is the other main line: after [cxd4 Nxd4], Black plays ...Ne7, so that Nxf5 can be met by ...Nxf5, then ...Nbc6 and pressure on e5. [Nd7] keeps the tension and prepares ...Ne7. Grabbing on b2 is often possible in these lines, but it is never free: White gets open lines and quick development against the queen, so check that it has a way back before you take. [%csl Yb2,Yd4][%cal Gd8b6,Gc5d4] }
( 6. O-O ) 6... Qb6
{ White usually develops with [Nc3], ignoring b2 for one more move. After Nc3, taking on b2 at once is risky: the queen can be hunted with Na4 or Nb5 and Rb1 while Black is behind in development. Black does better to play ...Nc6 first, adding a third attacker on d4, and only then think about the b-pawn. White also has dxc5 at some point, giving the pawn back to gain time on the queen and open lines while Black's king is still in the centre. [%csl Yb2,Yd4][%cal Gb1c3,Gb8c6] }
( 6... cxd4
{ White takes back with the knight, [Nxd4], the main recapture: the knight lands in the centre and attacks the bishop on f5 at once, so Black's next move must deal with Nxf5. The usual answer is ...Ne7, which develops and covers f5; then the knight from b8 goes to c6, and both knights hit e5, which no longer has a pawn on d4 to support it. [%csl Yf5,Ye5][%cal Gf3d4] }
7. Nxd4
{ The knight on d4 attacks the f5 bishop. [Ne7] defends it: if White takes on f5, ...Nxf5 recaptures with a knight that stands very well there. Then Black plays ...Nbc6 and ...Ng6 and puts pressure on e5, which no pawn supports any more. Black often castles short after ...Be7. Retreating the bishop to g6 at once is slower: White castles and plays c4, opening the centre while Black's king is still on e8. White's best chances are to keep the knight on d4 and push c4 to fight for d5. [%csl Yf5,Ye5][%cal Gg8e7,Gb8c6] }
7... Ne7
{ Both sides are close to finishing development. White castles with [O-O] or opens the centre with [c4], hitting d5 while Black's king is still in the middle. Black's pieces have natural squares: ...Nbc6, ...Ng6 to hit e5, ...Be7 and short castling, or ...a6 to keep a white knight out of b5. Now that d4 is no longer held by a pawn, e5 is as much a target as a strength, and ...Qc7 adds to the pressure. If White plays Nxf5, take back with the knight, which stands well on f5. [%csl Ye5,Yf5][%cal Ge1g1,Gc2c4] }
8. O-O ( 8. c4 ) ) ( 6... Nd7 ) 7. Nc3
{ [Nc6] develops and adds a third attacker on d4: now ...cxd4 threatens to win the pawn, and ...Qxb2 is a real option too. White's usual answer is O-O, offering the b-pawn: after ...Qxb2, Na4 and Rb1 chase the queen, and White gets open lines and a lead in development for the pawn. Na4 at once is also played, hitting the queen and c5. If you take on b2, have an exit ready for the queen: a3 and a5 are the usual squares. [%csl Yd4,Yb2][%cal Gb8c6] }
7... Nc6 *

[Event "Scandinavian Defence (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 ( 1. Nf3 d5 2. e4 dxe4 3. Ng5 f5
{ Trying to cling to the extra e4 pawn with [f5] in the Tennison Gambit severely weakens the a2-g8 diagonal and blocks Black's own light-squared bishop! White punishes that immediately with [Bc4], aiming straight at f7 and threatening [w:Bf7+] [b:Kd7] [w:Ne6] trapping Black's queen in broad daylight! Developing [b:Nf6]?? still loses the queen to [w:Bf7+], so Black is reduced to [Bc4 e6 Nxe6 Bxe6 Bxe6 Qf6] or the awkward [Bc4 Nh6], surrendering the light squares and king safety just to survive. [%csl Rf7,Yf5][%cal Gf1c4] }
( 3... Qd5
{ Bringing the queen out to [Qd5] hits the unguarded g5 knight and defends e4 at the same time, sidestepping [w:Bc4] tricks on f7. White must not play [w:Nc3]?? because g5 is completely undefended ([b:Qxg5] wins a full piece!). Instead, [d3] is the sharp gambit reply, defending g5 with the c1 bishop while challenging e4 ([d3 exd3 Nc3 Qe5+ Be3] gives White rapid development for the pawn, or [d3 e5 Nc3 Qd8 dxe4] equalises cleanly), while [h4] secures the knight quietly. [%cal Gd2d3,Rd5g5,Bc1g5] }
4. d3 ( 4. h4 ) ) 4. Bc4 ) 1... d5
{ The Scandinavian, or Centre Counter. Black hits e4 on move one and forces an early decision. It is the oldest opening on record: the Valencia game of 1475 began this way, and Scandinavian masters built its theory in the 19th century. [exd5] is the main test, met by ...Qxd5 or ...Nf6. [e5] gains space but gives Black an improved French with the light bishop free. Sidelines like Nc3 invite ...d4, Nf3 is the trappy Tennison gambit, and d4 transposes to the Blackmar-Diemer. [%csl Yd5,Ye5][%cal Ge4d5] }
2. exd5 ( 2. e5
{ White gains space, but this is a French Advance where Black has not yet played ...e6, so the light-squared bishop can come out first. [Bf5] does exactly that, and ...e6 follows. [c5] hits the base of the chain at once, so that d4 is hard to support. [Nc6] also eyes e5 and d4. The pawn on e5 has lost its partner on e4, and Black can attack it with ...Nc6, ...Qb6 and ...Ne7-f5. Black is at least equal. [%csl Ye5,Yf5,Yg4][%cal Gc8f5] }
2... Bf5
{ The bishop is out before the pawn chain closes. White usually plays [d4] to support e5 with a pawn; then ...e6 gives a French in which Black's bad bishop has become a good one. [Nf3] and [c3] are quieter. If Black follows with ...c6 instead of ...e6, the position becomes a Caro-Kann Advance, a different opening. In this line Black aims for ...e6, ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6, hitting d4 while the bishop on f5 eyes the queenside. [%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3,Be7e6] }
( 2... c5
{ Black hits the base of the chain before White can build it. [Nf3] develops and keeps d4 under watch; ...Nc6 and ...Bg4 then pin the knight that holds e5. [d4] gets the pawn chain up at once, and after ...cxd4 or ...Nc6 Black fights for the dark squares. [c3] prepares d4 with a pawn. [f4] props up e5 from the other side, at the cost of the dark squares near White's king. Black develops the light bishop before ...e6 and is fine. [%cal Gg1f3,Gd2d4,Gc2c3] }
3. Nf3 ( 3. d4 ) ( 3. c3 ) ( 3. f4 ) ) ( 2... Nc6 ) 3. d4
{ White props up the e5 pawn, and now [e6] completes Black's set-up: the pawns on d5 and e6 hold the light squares, the bishop already sits outside the chain on f5, and ...c5 comes next. This is a French Advance in which Black has solved the opening's main problem, the bishop on c8. White will play c3, Nf3, Be2 and castle; Black plays ...c5, ...Nc6, ...Qb6 and ...Ne7, putting pressure on d4. [%csl Yf5][%cal Ge7e6,Bc7c5] }
( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. c3 ) 3... e6
{ A French Advance with Black's light bishop already outside the chain. White develops with [Nf3], [c3], Be2 and short castling to hold d4. [Bd3] offers a swap of light bishops, which suits Black because the dark bishop is the good one. [Ne2] keeps the f-pawn free. Black's plan is ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 against d4 and b2, with ...Ne7-f5 adding a fourth attacker. One trap to remember: if White plays Bd3 before c3, ...cxd4 and ...Nxd4 still must watch out for Bb5+ uncovering White's queen against d4. [%cal Gg1f3,Gc2c3,Bc7c5] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. c3 ) ( 4. Bd3 ) ( 4. Ne2 ) ) ( 2. f3 $2
{ Guarding e4 with the f-pawn weakens the e1-h4 diagonal and robs White's knight of its best square on f3. [e5] seizes the centre at once (and ...dxe4 is strong too), leaving White cramped with no good way to develop the kingside. Capture with exd5 instead. [%csl Ye4,Ye1,Yh4][%cal Re7e5] }
2... e5 ) ( 2. Bb5+ $6
{ Checking on b5 only helps Black build an ideal pawn centre with gain of time. [c6] attacks the bishop at once, and after dxc6 bxc6 or Be2 dxe4 Black wins time and central control. Capture on d5 first instead. [%csl Yb5,Yc6,Yd5][%cal Rc7c6] }
2... c6 ) ( 2. Nc3
{ White defends e4 with a knight and keeps the centre fluid. [d4] gains space with tempo and is the engine's choice: the knight must go back to e2 or b1, or wander to e4 or d5 where ...e5 and ...c6 harass it. [dxe4] takes the pawn; after Nxe4 Black plays ...Bf5, ...Nd7 or ...e5, positions close to the Caro-Kann or the French but with pawns on other squares. Quieter moves like ...Nf6 let White play e5 and gain space. [%csl Yd4,Yd5,Yc6][%cal Gd5d4] }
2... d4
{ The pawn on d4 cramps White and has kicked the knight. [Nce2] is the usual retreat: the knight goes on to g3, and White plays d3, Nf3, Bd2 or g3 with a King's Indian set-up. [Nb1] looks silly but is playable: c3 follows, hitting d4. Advancing to d5 or e4 only runs into ...c6 or ...e5 and ...f5, and the knight soon runs out of squares. Black is comfortable: ...e5, ...c5 and ...Nc6 hold the centre. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc3e2,Bc3b1] }
( 2... dxe4
{ Black has taken on e4, and White must take back. [Nxe4] restores the balance with a well placed knight. The position looks like a Caro-Kann or French Rubinstein, but White has not played d4 and Black has not committed the c- or e-pawn, so both sides have more choice. Black usually goes on with ...e5, ...Bf5, ...Nd7 or ...Nf6, and White with d4 or Nf3, Bc4 and castling. The game is balanced. [%cal Gc3e4,Bc8f5] }
3. Nxe4
{ White recaptures in the centre, reaching a Caro-Kann or French Rubinstein shape in which Black has saved the ...c6 or ...e6 tempo. [Bf5] challenges the centralised knight at once and develops the light-squared bishop outside the future pawn chain. [Nf6] invites a knight swap directly, [Nd7] prepares ...Ngf6 without doubling pawns, and [e5] stakes out space immediately. Black equalises comfortably in every branch. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gc3e4] }
3... Bf5
{ Black develops the light-squared bishop with tempo, mirroring the Classical Caro-Kann without having spent a move on ...c6. [Ng3] is White's universal reply, questioning the bishop at once so ...Bg6 leaves White free to follow with Nf3, d4, Bc4 and h4-h5. Black answers solidly with ...e6, ...Nf6, ...Nbd7 and ...Bd6, keeping a compact pawn structure and an active diagonal toward b1. [%csl Yc6,Yd4,Yh4][%cal Ge4g3] }
( 3... Nf6
{ Black challenges the centralised knight directly rather than committing the light-squared bishop on move three. [Nxf6+] is the most common continuation: ...exf6 opens lines for ...Bd6, ...O-O and ...Re8 in a brisk Tartakower-style game, whereas ...gxf6 keeps the central pawns intact and opens the g-file for counterplay. [Ng3] avoids the swap and prepares Nf3, Bc4 and d4 in an even middlegame. [%cal Ge4f6,Ge4g3] }
4. Nxf6+ ( 4. Ng3 ) ) ( 3... Nd7 ) ( 3... e5
{ In this reversed-Vienna line (often called the Grunfeld Defense to the 2.[Nc3] Scandinavian), Black stakes out d4 and f4 with [e5] to gain free diagonals for both bishops. [Bc4] is White's sharpest reply, targeting the softened f7 pawn at once so [b:Be7] or [b:Nc6] must tread carefully against [w:Qh5] and [w:Nf3]. [Nf3] develops solidly ([Nf3 Nc6 Bc4] or [Nf3 Nf6]), keeping balanced central play. [%cal Gf1c4] }
4. Bc4 ( 4. Nf3 ) ) 4. Ng3 ) 3. Nce2
{ White's knight will come back via g3, and Black has a pawn wedge on d4 to support. [e5] makes it a pawn duo and gives the dark bishop the d6 or c5 squares. [c5] props up d4 from the queenside and keeps ...e5 for later. Black then plays ...Nc6, ...Nf6 and ...Bd6. White's plan is c3 or f4 to undermine the centre, with Ng3, Nf3, Bc4 and castling. The position resembles an Old Indian reversed, with extra space for Black. [%csl Ye5,Yc5,Yd4][%cal Ge7e5] }
( 3. Nb1 ) 3... e5 ( 3... c5 ) ) ( 2. Nf3
{ White offers the e4 pawn: the Tennison Gambit, a reversed Budapest where Ng5 next targets e4 and f7. [dxe4] is the critical reply and clearly best: Black keeps the extra pawn safely with ...Bf5, ...e5 or ...Nf6 and stands ahead. Declining gets Black in trouble: ...Nf6 runs into e5 with a free tempo, and ...d4? hits nothing on c3 and walks straight into c3 undermining the overextended pawn; ...c6 is passive but playable. [%csl Ye4,Yf7][%cal Gd5e4] }
2... dxe4
{ White has given a pawn and now goes after it. [Ng5] is the main move: the knight hits e4 and eyes f7, so Black must guard e4 carefully. [Ne5] also attacks f7 and prepares Bc4 and Qh5. Black is a pawn up and wants to keep it with ...Bf5, ...Nf6 or ...e5 while developing. The d-file is now open for Black's queen, so White's knight must not land on d4. Greedy moves like ...h6 early can backfire, as f7 is the target. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gf3g5,Bf3e5] }
( 2... Nf6 $2
{ Black declines the gambit with a natural move, but it hands White the space instead. [e5] kicks the knight to d7 or e4, and after d4 and c4 White has a French Advance in which Black's light bishop is shut in and White's pawns stand on c4, d4 and e5. The engine gives White a clear edge. Take the pawn: ...dxe4 leaves White chasing it with Ng5 while Black develops. [%csl Ye5,Yd7,Ye4][%cal Re4e5] }
3. e5 ) ( 2... d4 $2
{ Pushing past as against 2.Nc3 backfires here because no knight sits on c3 to gain time on, and White's c-pawn is free to strike immediately. [c3] undermines the overextended d4 pawn at once, handing White central control and rapid development. Capture on e4 instead. [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc2c3] }
3. c3 ) 3. Ng5
{ White's knight hits e4. [Bf5] is the engine's choice: the bishop guards the pawn and stays out of reach. [e5] is the most active reply: it keeps the pawn, gains space and opens the bishop towards c5. [Nf6], the most common move, defends e4 and develops, though White then gets Bc4 or Nc3 with pressure on f7. Black should be careful about f7: a knight on g5 and a bishop on c4 together can make ...h6 or a careless queen move dangerous. [%csl Ye5][%cal Ge7e5] }
( 3. Ne5
{ Instead of attacking e4 from g5, White centralises the knight to eye f7 and prepare Bc4, d3 or Qh5. [Nf6] is solid and natural, guarding d5 and h5 while preparing ...Bf5 or ...Nbd7 to contest the knight. [Nd7] challenges e5 immediately, forcing Nxd7 Bxd7 or Nc4 while Black stays a clean pawn ahead. Black only needs to avoid careless ...f6 pushes that expose h5. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gg8f6,Gb8d7] }
3... Nf6 ( 3... Nd7 ) ) ( 3. Nd4 $4
{ The knight heads for the queenside, but it walks onto a square the queen covers down the open d-file. [Qxd4] wins it for nothing. With the d-pawn gone, the d-file belongs to Black's queen, so White's pieces must keep off it. The knight goes to g5, hitting e4 and eyeing f7. [%cal Rd8d4] }
3... Qxd4 ) ( 3. Ng1 $2
{ Retreating the knight all the way home surrenders both the e4 pawn and two full developing moves without a fight. [Nc6] develops smoothly and prepares ...e5 and ...Bf5, leaving Black a clean pawn and several tempi ahead. Jump forward with Ng5 or Ne5 to attack e4 and f7 instead. [%csl Ye4][%cal Rb8c6] }
3... Nc6 ) 3... Bf5
{ Black defends the extra pawn on e4 with a developing move that keeps the bishop safe on an open diagonal. [Nc3] is White's main try, adding a second attacker to e4, which Black meets calmly with ...Nf6 so ...e6 and ...Be7 can follow. Aiming at f7 immediately with Bc4 is easily blunted by ...e6, leaving Black a solid pawn up. [%csl Ye4,Ye6,Yf7][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 3... e5
{ Black returns the e4 pawn on Black's own terms to seize central space, open the diagonal for the bishop on f8, and uncover the queen on d8 against g5. [Nxe4] is practically forced since the knight on g5 was en prise: Black then follows up actively with ...Nc6 or ...f5 and ...Nf6, enjoying free development and central control at zero tactical risk. [%csl Ye4,Yf8,Yd8][%cal Gg5e4] }
4. Nxe4 ) ( 3... Nf6
{ Black guards e4 with the knight and develops at the same time. [Bc4] is White's sharpest continuation, aiming straight at f7 before Black can play ...e6 and consolidate. [Nc3] adds a second piece against e4, usually answered by ...Bf5, while [d3] is the most popular club try because ...exd3 Bxd3 sets up deadly sacrifices on f7 if Black carelessly pushes ...h6. Objectively Black keeps a healthy extra pawn by meeting d3 with ...Bg4 or with ...exd3 Bxd3 Nc6. [%cal Gf1c4,Gd2d3,Bb1c3] }
4. Bc4
{ White aims a second minor piece straight at f7 before attacking e4 a second time. [e6] is Black's only sound reply, shutting the a2-g8 diagonal at once and freeing the bishop on f8 so Black keeps a healthy extra pawn after Nc3 Be7 or ...Nc6. Chasing the knight prematurely with ...h6?? drops f7, while ...Bg4?? walks into Bxf7+ Kd7 Qxg4+. [%cal Ge7e6,Yc4f7,Yg5f7] }
( 4. Qe2 $2
{ Lining the queen up on e4 blocks the bishop on f1 while Black develops with tempo. [Nc6] guards key central squares and threatens ...Nd4 hitting both the queen and c2, leaving White's pieces tangled a pawn down. Develop the bishop actively with Bc4 instead. [%cal Rb8c6,Rc6d4] }
4... Nc6 ) ( 4. d3
{ White attacks e4 a second time. [exd3] is the normal answer: after Bxd3 White's bishop is active, but Black is still a pawn up and needs one careful move, ...Nc6, to finish the danger. [Bg4] is the engine's choice: it hits the queen and leaves the pawn on e4, and Black stays clearly better. The one move to remember is the one Black must not play later: ...h6 while Bd3 and Ng5 point at f7 and g6. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gc8g4,Ge4d3] }
4... Bg4 ( 4... exd3
{ Take back with the bishop. [Bxd3] develops with an eye on h7 and g6, and together with the knight on g5 it gives White some threats against f7. White is a pawn down for this activity. Taking with the queen would also work but exposes it on the d-file to ...Qxd3 trades or ...Nc6-b4 ideas. With the bishop on d3, White wants O-O, Re1 and Nc3, and quick play against Black's kingside. [%csl Yf7][%cal Gf1d3,Bd3h7] }
5. Bxd3
{ Black is a pawn up and needs one calm developing move. [Nc6] develops a piece, covers d4 and e5, and keeps the d-file shielded so ...e5, ...Bg4 or ...g6 can follow safely. Several tempting moves walk straight into traps while Ng5 and Bd3 line up on f7: ...h6 opens g6 and loses Black's queen to Nxf7, ...e5 runs into Bb5+ blocking the d-file before Bc4 piles onto f7, and ...g6 allows the fork Nxf7. [%csl Ye5,Yg6,Yh6][%cal Gb8c6] }
5... Nc6 ( 5... h6 $4
{ Chasing the knight is the exact blunder White's gambit hopes for. [Nxf7] forks the queen on d8 and the rook on h8: taking with [Nxf7 Kxf7 Bg6+] checks from the square ...h6 just left unguarded and uncovers White's queen on d1 against d8, winning Black's queen, while declining drops the queen or the corner rook. Develop with ...Nc6 first. [%csl Yh6,Yg6,Yd1][%cal Rg5f7] }
6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. Bg6+ ) ( 5... e5 $2
{ Grabbing central space looks natural, but f7 is not ready for it. [Bb5+] forces ...Bd7, blocking Black's queen on the d-file (since ...c6 allows Qxd8+ Kxd8 Nxf7+ winning the rook), and then Bc4 piles onto f7 with no queen trade available: after ...Qe7 Nxf7 White wins f7 and traps the rook in the corner. Play ...Nc6 or ...Bg4 first. [%csl Yb5,Yc6,Yh8][%cal Rd3b5] }
6. Bb5+ ) ( 5... g6 $2
{ Blunting the bishop's diagonal to h7 leaves f7 fatally short of defenders. [Nxf7] forks the queen on d8 and the rook on h8: taking with ...Kxf7 loses the queen to Bxg6+, while ...Qd5 Nxh8 Qxg2 leaves White the exchange up even after Black grabs g2 and traps the corner knight. Develop calmly with ...Nc6 instead. [%csl Yh7,Yf7,Yd8][%cal Rg5f7] }
6. Nxf7 ) ) ) ( 4. Nc3
{ White brings out the queen's knight to attack e4 twice instead of offering another pawn with d3. [Bf5] is Black's main continuation, defending e4 firmly while staying off vulnerable squares so Bc4 or Qe2 can be met calmly. [Bg4] counterattacks White's queen on d1, forcing Be2 Bxe2 Qxe2 Nc6 when White regains e4 only at the cost of the bishop pair. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gc8f5,Gc8g4] }
4... Bf5
{ Black reinforces e4 a second time, reaching the main tabiya of the Nc3 Tennison Gambit. [Bc4] is White's best continuation, targeting f7 immediately to force ...e6 before White follows up with Qe2 or f3 to undermine the pawn wedge. Playing Qe2 right away is objectively dubious against ...Nc6!, yet sets a notorious club trap if Black carelessly centralises with ...Qd4??. [%cal Gf1c4,Bd1e2,Yc4f7] }
( 4... Bg4 ) ( 4... e5 $2
{ Pushing ...e5 opens the dark-squared bishop, yet leaves f7 dangerously exposed before ...e6 can shield it. [Bc4] piles a second piece onto f7 immediately, forcing ...Be6 Nxe6 fxe6 and leaving Black with shattered pawns and a weak king. Defend e4 cleanly with ...Bf5 instead. [%csl Rf7][%cal Rf1c4] }
5. Bc4 ) 5. Bc4
{ With both the bishop on c4 and the knight on g5 converging on f7, Black must secure that square before touching anything else. [e6] shuts the diagonal cleanly and opens e7 for the dark-squared bishop: after Qe2 Black continues with ...Nc6 or ...Bb4 and castles, nursing a clean extra pawn. Kicking the knight prematurely with ...h6?? hands White a crushing sacrifice on f7. [%csl Ye6,Yf7,Yh6][%cal Ge7e6] }
( 5. h3 $4
{ Pushing [h3] hopes to open h2 for the g5-knight, yet it seals the knight's doom by blocking h3 itself! [h6] traps it cold: e4 is guarded twice, f3 is covered by e4, h7 is watched by f6, and h3 holds White's own pawn, dropping a piece ([h6 Bb5+ c6 Nxf7 Kxf7]). Play [w:Bc4] instead. [%csl Yh3][%cal Rh7h6] }
5... h6 6. Bb5+ c6 7. Nxf7 Kxf7 ) ( 5. Qe2
{ White lines up a third piece on e4 and sets a trap that catches over a quarter of club players. [Nc6] is the right refutation: after Ngxe4 Nd4! White must step back to d1 or d3 to prevent ...Nxc2+, while Qb5 is met cleanly by ...Qd7 defending both f5 and b7. Centralising with ...Qd4?? blunders straight into the fork Qb5+, while passive moves like ...c6 or ...e6 surrender the extra pawn. [%cal Gb8c6,Bc6d4] }
5... Nc6 ( 5... Qd4 $4
{ Centralising the queen to guard e4 walks straight into a triple fork that catches over a quarter of players here. [Qb5+] checks the king while simultaneously attacking the loose bishop on f5 and the pawn on b7, winning a piece or wrecking Black's queenside completely. Play ...Nc6 instead. [%cal Re2b5,Rb5e8,Rb5f5] }
6. Qb5+ ) ) 5... e6 ) 4... e6 ) ( 3... f5 ) ( 3... Qd5 ) 4. Nc3 ) ( 2. d3
{ White supports e4 with a pawn, which is solid but passive. [dxe4] trades in the centre: after dxe4 Qxd1+ Kxd1 White's king has lost the right to castle, and Black has an easy endgame. White usually avoids that by playing Nd2 or Nf3 instead. [e5] takes the centre and turns the game into a reversed Philidor where Black has an extra tempo. [Nf6] keeps all options. Black is fine in every line. [%csl Yd3][%cal Gd5e4] }
2... dxe4 ( 2... e5 ) ( 2... Nf6 ) ) ( 2. b3
{ White prepares Bb2 to support e4 down the long diagonal instead of trading in the centre. [dxe4] is the direct refutation: after Bb2 Black keeps a healthy extra pawn with ...Nf6, ...Bf5 and ...e6, while Nc3 gambit play falls short of full compensation. Declining with ...e5 is also playable in club games, meeting Bb2 with ...Bd6 or ...Nc6 in a reversed Owen structure. [%cal Gd5e4,Yc1b2] }
2... dxe4 ) 2... Qxd5 ( 2... Nf6 3. d4
{ White takes the centre. [Nxd5] wins the pawn back with a well placed knight, though c4 will kick it soon. [Bg4] is the Portuguese gambit: Black develops with a pin and offers the d5 pawn for quick play. [Qxd5] takes with the queen, reaching a 2...Qxd5 line where the knight is already on f6. [c6] is the Kadas gambit. Moves that don't win the pawn back, like ...g6 or ...Bf5, let White keep it with c4 or Bb5+. [%cal Gf6d5,Gc8g4,Bd8d5] }
3... Nxd5
{ The knight is centralised, but White can kick it with gain of time. [Nf3] develops first and keeps c4 for later; Black then plays ...Bg4, ...g6 or ...Bf5. [c4] kicks the knight at once: it goes to b6, f6 or the sharp b4. [Bc4] hits the knight from the other side and prepares Nf3 and castling. White has more space in all lines, and Black relies on piece play against the pawn centre. [%cal Gg1f3,Gc2c4,Bf1c4] }
( 3... Bg4
{ The Portuguese gambit. Black develops with a pin instead of taking back the pawn. [f3] kicks the bishop and holds on to d5: after ...Bf5, c4 and ...e6 give a famous gambit where Black's pieces fly out. [Be2] trades bishops and lets Black win the pawn back. [Nf3] offers the pawn back with a quiet game. [Bb5+] checks first. The gambit is objectively dubious but very dangerous, and has been tried by strong players in fast games. [%csl Yd5,Yf3,Yc4][%cal Gf2f3] }
4. f3
{ White kicks the bishop and keeps the pawn. [Bf5] is the main move: it stays on a good diagonal and prepares the gambit with ...e6 after c4. [Bd7] is the engine's choice: quieter, but the bishop is safe and ...Nxd5 or ...Bb5 follow, and White's f3 is a weakness. Retreating to h5 keeps the pin idea but the bishop is easily hit by g4 or c4 and Nc3. After ...Bf5 White chooses between g4 and c4, and Black sacrifices the d-pawn for quick development. [%cal Gg4f5,Gg4d7] }
( 4. Be2
{ The Wuss Variation of the Portuguese: White declines complications, unpins, and returns the extra pawn for smooth development. [Bxe2] is practically forced: after Qxe2 Qxd5 Nf3, Black recovers d5 with an active queen and develops comfortably with ...Nc6, ...e6 and ...O-O-O in an even game. Avoiding the swap with ...Bf5 leaves Black unable to win back d5 once White plays c4. [%csl Yg2][%cal Gg4e2] }
