{ The bishop guards e5, but it is a target there. After [dxe5] it is hit, and taking back loses a piece: after ...Bxe5, Bb5+ cuts the fifth rank between the queen and e5, and Nxe5 follows. So Black must play ...Bb4 Bd2, a clean pawn down with less development. Play ...Bb4 or ...exd4. [%cal Rd4e5,Rf1b5] }
6. dxe5 ) ( 5... f6 $2
{ The pawn props up e5, but it weakens the king's diagonals and takes f6 from the knight. After [dxe5], ...fxe5 runs into Bb5+, which cuts the queen off from e5, and Nxe5 wins the pawn with a huge attack on the open king. After ...Nc6 Bb5 instead, White is a pawn up with a big lead. Play ...Bb4 or ...exd4. [%cal Rd4e5,Rf1b5] }
6. dxe5 ) ( 5... e4 $2
{ Pushing forward to kick the knight hands White the e5 outpost and leaves the e4 pawn overextended. [Ne5] plants the knight centrally with threats of Bc4 against f7 and Bd2 discoveries against a5, while ...Be6 runs into Bd2. Pin with ...Bb4 or trade with ...exd4 instead. [%csl Re4][%cal Rf3e5] }
6. Ne5 ) ( 5... Bg4
{ Black pins the knight that guards d4. [Bc4] is the engine's choice: White develops and eyes f7, and after ...Nc6 the sacrifice Bxf7+ followed by Ng5+ wins the bishop on g4 with interest. [Be2] breaks the pin calmly, and [h3] asks the bishop first: after ...exd4 hxg4 dxc3, White's rook gets the open h-file. Taking on e5 at once is less precise. White is clearly better if it keeps developing. [%cal Gf1c4,Gh2h3,Yc4f7] }
6. Bc4 ( 6. h3 ) ) ) ( 4... Nc6 $2
{ The knight attacks d4, but it has no good square once the pawn advances. After [d5] it must retreat to b4, e5 or b8: a3 chases b4, f4 or Bf4 hits e5, and b8 surrenders two full tempi. Once on d5, White's pawn cramps Black's camp while Bd2, Nf3 and Bb5+ threats build rapidly against the queen. Play ...Nf6 or ...c6 first. [%csl Yd5][%cal Rd4d5] }
5. d5 ) ( 4... Bg4 $4
{ A plain blunder that happens often: with no knight on f3 yet, the bishop simply hangs. [Qxg4] wins a piece, and the queen even eyes c8 and g7. The pin on the knight only works once White has played Nf3. Develop with ...Nf6 or ...c6 first and keep ...Bg4 for when it hits something. [%csl Yf3,Yc8,Yg7][%cal Rd1g4] }
5. Qxg4 ) *

[Event "Scandinavian Defence (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 d5 2. exd5
{ Black must win the pawn back, and there are two ways. [Qxd5] is the classical one: the queen comes out early and will lose a tempo to Nc3, but Black gets a Caro-Kann-like position with the light bishop free. [Nf6], the Modern, recaptures with the knight next move, or invites White to hang on to d5 with c4, which costs White time. The gambits ...c6 and ...e5 are rarer: Black gives the pawn for a quick lead in development, and White does best to take it and develop calmly. [%cal Gd8d5,Gg8f6,Bc7c6] }
2... Qxd5
{ Black's queen sits in the centre, and White can gain time on her. [Nc3] is the main move: the queen must move again to a5, d6, d8 or e5, though the knight on c3 blocks White's c-pawn. [Nf3] and [d4] keep the c-pawn free for a later c4. Pushing c4 immediately is premature because ...Qe4+ checks and regains time or the pawn, while careless moves like Be2 leave g2 hanging to the queen. [%csl Yd4,Yc3,Yc4][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 2... Nf6
{ The Modern Scandinavian, or Marshall line. Black takes back on d5 with the knight next move instead of the queen, so there are no queen moves to lose time on. [d4] is the main move and lets Black choose between ...Nxd5 and the Portuguese gambit ...Bg4. [c4] tries to keep the pawn, which costs time: Black answers with the gambits ...c6 or ...e6. [Nc3] and [Nf3] develop and allow ...Nxd5. [Bb5+] is a clever check: the piece Black blocks with ends up on an awkward square. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd2d4] }
3. d4 ( 3. c4
{ White tries to keep the extra pawn. That costs time, and Black gets a choice of gambits. [c6] offers a pawn: after dxc6 Nxc6 Black has quick development and control of d4, and after d4 cxd5 the game becomes a Caro-Kann Panov. [e6] is the Icelandic gambit, even sharper. Quiet moves let White keep the pawn with d4 and Nc3. [%csl Yc6,Yd4][%cal Gc7c6] }
3... c6
{ Black offers the c-pawn. [dxc6] takes it: after ...Nxc6, Black has a lead in development, the open d-file, and a hole on d4 for a knight, since White's d-pawn is still at home. [d4] goes back to the Caro-Kann Panov after ...cxd5. [Nc3] develops: then ...cxd5 and cxd5 Nxd5 leads to an open position where Black is fine. Taking the pawn is more ambitious, but practical results are good for Black. [%cal Gd5c6,Gd2d4,Bb1c3] }
( 3... e6
{ The Icelandic gambit. Black offers a second pawn for development. [dxe6] accepts and is the engine's choice: after ...Bxe6 Black has a lead and pressure on c4 and the d-file, but White should be better with careful play. [d4] declines: after ...exd5 the game is a kind of French where White may end up with an isolated pawn on d4. Taking is the most testing, and White must then develop very carefully. [%cal Gd5e6,Gd2d4] }
4. dxe6
{ White accepts the Icelandic Gambit pawn. [Bxe6] is the only real recapture, developing the light-squared bishop with an eye on c4 while keeping the d- and e-files clear for ...Bb4+, ...Qe7, ...Nc6 and long castling. Recapturing with ...fxe6 instead opens Black's king to Qh5+ ideas and blocks the bishop on c8 for nothing, leaving White a safe pawn up after Nf3 and d4. [%cal Gc8e6,Bf8b4,Ye6c4] }
( 4. d4 ) 4... Bxe6
{ Black has a lead in development for the pawn. [Nf3] is the safest: White develops, castles and plays d4 later, and the extra pawn usually holds. [d4] takes the centre at once but walks into ...Bb4+ and ...Ne4 tricks. [Nc3] also develops. The queen must stay at home for now: on e2 she pins the bishop but blocks White's own bishop. White's light bishop is the problem piece: Be2 allows ...Bxc4. [%cal Gg1f3,Gd2d4,Bb1c3] }
5. Nf3
{ White develops calmly. [Qe7] is the engine's choice: the queen pins nothing yet but eyes the e-file, and ...Nc6 and ...O-O-O follow; White's best answer is Qe2, offering a trade. [Nc6] brings the knight out first, with ...Bc5 or ...Qd7 and long castling. White plays Be2, O-O and d3 or d4, and tries to keep the extra pawn with a sound position. Putting the bishop on c5 at once lets d4 come with tempo. [%csl Bd7][%cal Gd8e7,Gb8c6] }
( 5. d4
{ White takes the centre. [Bb4+] gains time: White must block on c3 or d2, and both have problems. Black's pieces fly out, and the d-file and the e-file both work against White's king. Developing the knight to c6 at once is a mistake: d5 hits it and the bishop together. White should consider Nf3 first next time; here precise moves are needed. [%csl Ye1][%cal Gf8b4] }
5... Bb4+
{ Black develops with check to seize the initiative before White can untangle the kingside. [Nc3] is the most common block, walking into the sharp pin line ...Ne4 where White must find Ne2 to return c4 safely and equalise. [Bd2] avoids the pin and offers a bishop swap, met by ...Qe7! threatening discovered checks along the e-file and rapid pressure after Bxb4 Qxb4+ Qd2 Nc6. [%cal Gb1c3,Gc1d2,Bf6e4] }
( 5... Nc6 $2
{ The knight develops and hits d4, but the pawn walks past it. [d5] hits the knight and the bishop together: after ...Bxd5 cxd5 or ...Bb4+ Nc3 and dxc6 White wins a piece, and Black's only tries are tricks against f2. Check first with ...Bb4+: it gains time and keeps ...Nc6 for later. [%csl Yd4,Yd5,Yf2][%cal Rd4d5] }
6. d5 ) 6. Nc3
{ With the knight on c3 pinned to the king, Black piles on immediately. [Ne4] attacks c3 a second time while opening lines for ...Qf6 or ...Qxd4 against f2, setting the Icelandic Gambit's sharpest traps. White's clean defence is Ne2, reinforcing c3 and returning c4 to castle; Qd3 runs into ...Bf5 with strong pressure, while Bd2 and Nf3 lose material or get mated. [%csl Yf2][%cal Gf6e4,Ye4c3] }
( 6. Bd2 ) 6... Ne4
{ Black piles a second piece onto the pinned knight while eyeing d4 and f2. [Ne2] is White's clean defence: the knight reinforces c3, gives back the c4 pawn after ...Bxc4 Qa4+ Nc6, and keeps equality. Defending with Qd3 looks tempting, yet ...Bf5 Qf3 Nc6! gives Black a fierce initiative, while both natural moves fail outright: Bd2 drops d4 with mate threats on f2, and Nf3 loses the exchange to ...Nxc3. [%cal Gd1d3] }
7. Ne2 ( 7. Bd2 $2
{ The bishop unpins the knight, but it leaves d4 to the queen. [Qxd4] takes it, and the queen and the knight on e4 both hit f2: White must find Nxe4 or Qe2 at once, and Black has regained the pawn with a lead. The natural Nf3, hitting the queen, is a famous blunder there. The defence is Ne2 or Qd3, which keep d4 covered. [%cal Rd8d4] }
7... Qxd4
{ The queen and the knight both hit f2. [Nxe4] removes the knight and ends the threat; after ...Qxe4+ Black has won the pawn back with a good game and the safer king. Guarding f2 with Qe2 also stops the mate but loses more material to ...Nxd2 or ...Bxc3. Developing the knight to f3 with tempo on the queen is the famous blunder: the queen takes on f2, and it is mate. [%cal Gc3e4,Rd4f2] }
8. Nxe4 ( 8. Nf3 $4
{ The knight hits the queen with tempo, but f2 is defended only by the king. [Qxf2#] is mate: the knight on e4 guards f2, White's king has no square and nothing can take the queen. This trap has caught thousands of players. Take the knight on e4 or defend f2 with Qe2. [%cal Rd4f2] }
8... Qxf2# ) ) ( 7. Nf3 $2
{ The knight develops and covers d4, but the pinned knight on c3 falls. [Nxc3] and after bxc3 Bxc3+ Black wins the exchange on a1, with a sound extra rook against White's attack. Keep the knight on c3 defended: Ne2 is the engine's choice, and Qd3 also holds. [%csl Yd4,Yc3,Ya1][%cal Re4c3] }
7... Nxc3 ) ) ( 5. Nc3 ) ( 5. Qe2 $2
{ The queen pins the bishop on e6, but she blocks White's own bishop on f1. [Bb4] develops with an eye on d2, and after ...O-O and ...Re8 Black's rook comes to the e-file against her. The engine gives Black the better game. White's best set-up is Nf3, d4 or Be2 later, and castling before the queen comes out. [%csl Ye6,Yf1,Yd2][%cal Rf8b4] }
5... Bb4 ) 5... Qe7 ( 5... Nc6 ) ) ( 3... Bg4 $2
{ The bishop comes out with a threat on d1, but White just trades it and keeps the pawn. [Be2] offers the swap: after ...Bxe2 Nxe2 or Qxe2, the pawns on c4 and d5 stand firm, and Black has neither the pawn nor a lead. Against 3.c4 Black has to act at once with ...c6 or ...e6, which break the chain before White consolidates. [%csl Yc6][%cal Rf1e2] }
4. Be2 ) ( 3... b5 $2
{ The pawn hits c4 to undermine d5, but White just develops. [Nc3] supports d5, and after ...bxc4 Bxc4 White is a pawn up with a lead in development, while Black's queenside is loose. The right way to undermine d5 is ...c6 or ...e6, which open lines for Black's pieces. [%csl Yc4,Yd5,Yc6][%cal Rb1c3] }
4. Nc3 ) ( 3... Bf5 $6
{ Developing the bishop does nothing to challenge the pawn on d5. [d4] claims the full centre and prepares Nc3, leaving White a pawn up unless Black belatedly gambits with ...e6 under worse terms. Against 3.c4 Black must strike at the pawn chain right away with ...c6 or ...e6. [%csl Yd5,Yd4,Ye6][%cal Rd2d4] }
4. d4 ) 4. dxc6
{ White accepts the gambit pawn on c6, giving up central control for material. [Nxc6] is the whole point: Black has both knights developed, an open d-file, and a firm grip on the d4 hole left behind White's c-pawn, with ...e5 and ...Bc5 or ...Bf5 coming fast. Taking with ...bxc6 wastes the gambit's lead. Careful defence keeps White slightly better, but Black's practical attack is easy to play. [%cal Gb8c6,Yc6d4,Be7e5] }
( 4. d4 ) ( 4. Nc3 ) 4... Nxc6
{ White is a pawn up, while Black has two active knights, an open d-file and a lasting grip on the d4 hole. [Nc3] is safest, fighting for d5 and e4 before Nf3 and d3; Black answers with ...e5 and ...Bc5 or ...Bf5. [d3] frees the dark bishop but leaves d3 backward on the open file. [Nf3] invites ...e5!, threatening ...e4 to chase the knight while Black's pieces flood the centre. [%cal Gb1c3,Gd2d3,Bg1f3] }
5. Nc3 ( 5. d3 ) ( 5. Nf3 ) ) ( 3. Nc3
{ White develops a piece and returns the d5 pawn cleanly. [Nxd5] is Black's natural reply: if White trades with Nxd5 Qxd5, Black's queen sits safely in the centre with no knight left on c3 to chase her, so White usually prefers Bc4 or Nf3. Pawn gambits like ...c6 and especially ...e6 are dubious here because White has wasted no time on c4. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gf6d5] }
3... Nxd5
{ The knight is centralised. [Bc4] hits it at once; after ...Nb6 or ...Nxc3 White develops with tempo. [Nf3] and [d4] develop. [Nxd5] trades knights: after ...Qxd5 Black's queen is centralised and cannot be hit by a knight, so the trade suits Black. One trap to know: after Bc4, Nf3 and a pin with ...Bg4, Bxf7+ and Ng5+ can win the bishop. [%cal Gf1c4,Gg1f3,Bd2d4] }
( 3... e6 $2
