{ Take back with the bishop. [Bxe6] develops a piece, points at a2 and c4, and keeps the pawns on the kingside intact, so Black has a little lead in development for the pawn. The bishop on e6 also stops White from settling a piece on c4 or d5 easily. Taking with the f-pawn would open Black's own king and leave a weak pawn on e6 for no gain. [%cal Gc8e6,Ge6a2] }
( 3. c4 ) ( 3. Nc3 ) 3... Bxe6
{ Black has a piece out and the open e- and d-files for a pawn. White's simplest course is to develop: [Nf3], [d4], Be2 and castling, and maybe c4 to take away d5. [Bb5+] is a useful check: it forces ...c6 or ...Nc6 and gains time. Black plays ...Nf6, ...Nc6, ...Bc5 or ...Bd6 and castles, often queenside, to use the d-file. If White stays calm the extra pawn counts. [%cal Gg1f3,Gd2d4,Bf1b5] }
( 3... fxe6 $2
{ Black takes back with the pawn to keep the centre, but it weakens the king and gives nothing for the pawn. [Nf3] develops, and d4, Bd3 and O-O follow; the pawn on e6 blocks Black's light bishop and the diagonal h5-e8 is open for a queen check. White is a sound pawn up. Take back with the bishop, which develops. [%csl Re6][%cal Rg1f3,Yd1h5] }
