( 4. d4 ) ( 4. Bb5+ ) 4... Nf6 ( 4... Bd6 ) ) ( 2... e6
{ Black offers a pawn to open the dark-squared bishop and invite a harmless French Exchange. [Bb5+] is the sharpest reply, forcing ...Bd7 Be2 when Black's bishop stands clumsily on d7, while [dxe6] accepts the gambit and transposes after ...Bxe6 directly into the Boehnke Gambit a sound pawn ahead with Nf3 and d4. Playing d4 quietly lets ...exd5 reach a symmetrical French Exchange. [%csl Yd7,Yd4][%cal Gf1b5] }
3. Bb5+ ( 3. dxe6 ) ) 3. Nc3
{ The queen must move again. Each retreat gives a different game. [Qa5] is the main line: the queen pins the knight once White plays d4, and Black aims for ...c6, ...Nf6 and ...Bf5. [Qd8] is the safest square, where the queen will not be chased again, at the cost of passivity. [Qd6], the Pytel or Gubinsky-Melts line, avoids the Bd2 tricks against a5 but walks into Nb5 and Bf4. [Qe5+] is a check that blocks Black's own e-pawn and loses more time. [%csl Ya5,Yd4,Yd6][%cal Gd5a5] }
( 3. Nf3
{ White develops and keeps Nc3 for later, and also the option of c4. [Nf6] is the simplest and usually transposes to the main lines after d4 and Nc3. [Bg4] pins the knight at once; after Be2 Black plays ...Nc6 and ...O-O-O, a sharp plan. [Bf5] develops the bishop to the other good square, as in the Caro-Kann. Black should be wary of an early c4 that kicks the queen with no knight on c3 to block it. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc8g4] }
3... Nf6
{ A quiet move order. [d4] takes the centre; after ...Bg4 or ...Bf5 and Nc3 the game is back in the main lines with the queen on a5, d6 or d8. [Nc3] gains the tempo on the queen at once. [Be2] keeps the c-pawn free: with c4 later, White can gain space and time without a knight in front of the pawn. Black develops with ...Bg4 or ...Bf5, ...c6 and ...e6. [%cal Gd2d4,Gb1c3,Bf1e2] }
( 3... Bg4
{ Black pins the knight before White plays d4. [Be2] breaks the pin and prepares castling; Black then has ...Nc6 and ...O-O-O in mind. [Nc3] hits the queen first. White's other idea is h3 to ask the bishop a question. Black wants the queen on a5 or d7 and a quick long castle, with the rook on d8 facing White's queen and d-pawn. White does best to avoid d4 until the pin is gone, or the pawn becomes a target. [%cal Gf1e2,Gb1c3,Bb8c6] }
