{ Chasing the queen with the c-pawn instead of Nc3 is played in millions of club games, yet it weakens d4 and d3 permanently. [Qe4+] is the sharpest reply, eyeing both c4 and g2 so White must either sacrifice c4 for tempi with Ne2 or trade queens with Qe2. [Qa5] keeps queens on the board to press the backward d-pawn and d4 hole with ...Nc6 and ...e5. [%csl Yd4,Yd3,Yc4][%cal Gd5e4] }
3... Qe4+
{ Black checks on the open e-file while eyeing both c4 and g2 simultaneously. [Ne2] is White's sharpest defence, gambiting c4 so that after ...Qxc4 Nbc3 and d4 White regains rapid tempi against the exposed queen. [Qe2] is the safe alternative: after ...Qxe2+ Bxe2 Black brings out ...Nc6 and ...Bf5 before castling long to press the d4 hole, while Be2 drops g2 to ...Qxg2. [%csl Yc4,Yg2,Yd4][%cal Gg1e2] }
( 3... Qa5 ) 4. Ne2 ( 4. Qe2 ) ) ( 3. Qf3
{ White brings out the queen early to offer an immediate trade and sidestep main-line theory. [Qxf3] is the simplest equaliser: after Nxf3 Black develops smoothly with ...Nf6, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4, ...c6 and ...e6 in a balanced queenless game. [Nf6], [Qa5] and [Qe5+] keep queens on the board while exploiting the fact that White's queen temporarily occupies f3, the natural post of the king's knight. [%cal Gd5a5] }
