{ White plays a quiet King's Indian Attack setup, keeping the d-pawn back so Black gets no target on d4. With no pressure on the queen, Black seizes central space freely: [e5] claims the middle at once ahead of ...Nc6 and ...Bf5, while [Nc6] and [Nf6] develop rapidly toward ...Bg4 and ...O-O-O. White usually continues with Nc3 or Nf3, g3 and Bg2 in an even game. [%cal Ge7e5,Gb8c6,Bg8f6] }
3... e5 ( 3... Nc6 ) ( 3... Nf6 ) ) 3... Qa5
{ The main line. On a5 the queen is out of the way of White's minor pieces and, once White plays d4, pins the knight on c3 against the king. Black plans ...Nf6, ...c6 and a bishop to f5 or g4 before ...e6. [d4] is the main move, [Nf3] keeps d4 or d3 open, [Bc4] eyes f7, and [g3] starts a fianchetto system with Bg2, Nge2 and b4. White's trumps are space and quicker development; Black's is a sound pawn structure. Bd2 with a knight jump can uncover an attack on the queen. [%csl Ya5,Yd4][%cal Gd2d4] }
