( 4... c6 ) 5. d4 ( 5. Bc4 ) ) ( 4. Bc4
{ White develops the bishop first and keeps the knights flexible. [Nf6] and [c6] are the main moves. ...e6 is too passive, as it shuts the light bishop in. White's plan here is often d3 instead of d4: then Bd2, Qe2 and long castling, a set-up in which Black's ...Bg4 can be met by f3 and g4, and ...Bf5 by Nge2-g3 or Nd4. One mistake to avoid: with no knight on f3, a bishop on g4 simply hangs to the queen. [%cal Gg8f6,Gc7c6,Yc4f7] }
4... Nf6
{ A flexible position. [d4] is the engine's choice and transposes to the main line. [Nf3] keeps d4 or d3 open. [d3] is the anti-Scandinavian plan: White avoids d4, which would give Black's pieces a target, and plays Bd2, Qe2 and castles long. Against d3, Black should develop calmly with ...c6, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 and ...e6, and watch out for Nb5 and Nd5 tricks once the bishop reaches d2. [%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3,Bd2d3] }
