{ The Leonhardt gambit. White gives the b-pawn to open the b-file and gain time on the queen. [Qxb4] accepts, and then White plays Rb1 or Nb5. [Qb6] declines and keeps the queen safe, but White gets space with Nf3, Rb1 and Na4. [Qe5+] also declines. The gambit is playable for White and has scored well in fast games, because Black's queen can be trapped or hit with tempo many times. [%csl Yb4][%cal Ga5b4] }
4... Qxb4
{ Black has taken the pawn. [Rb1] is the main move: the rook hits b7 and the queen must move again, and the engine calls the game equal. [Nf3] develops and keeps Rb1 for later. Jumping at c7 with Nb5 looks strong but costs White the balance after ...Qa5; it is still a dangerous try, because the natural replies to it lose the queen to a sacrifice on f7. Hitting the queen with Ba3 would simply hang the bishop. [%cal Ga1b1,Gg1f3,Yc3b5] }
( 4... Qb6 ) 5. Rb1
{ The rook drives the queen back. [Qd6] is the engine's choice: the queen is centralised and covers b8 and c7. [Qc5] eyes c3 and f2, but Nb5 and d4 can chase it again. Going back to a5 keeps the pin on c3 but invites Nb5 or Rxb7 tricks later. White's compensation is the open b-file, the bishop on b2 or a3, and quick development with Nf3, Bc4 and castling. Black should develop quickly with ...Nf6, ...c6 and ...e6 rather than grab more. [%csl Yd4,Yg3][%cal Gb4d6] }
( 5. Nf3 ) ( 5. Nb5 $6
{ Jumping at c7 looks fierce, since c6 no longer stops Nxc7+, but the queen simply drops back. [Qa5] guards c7 and eyes b5 along the fifth rank, and after Bc4 Nf6 Black stays a sound pawn ahead. The move is still a tricky practical try because careless pawn pushes by Black run into Bxf7+. Play Rb1 for objective equality. [%csl Yf7,Yc7][%cal Rb4a5] }
5... Qa5
