{ The basic position of the Qa5 Scandinavian. [Nf3] is the main move, developing and keeping e5 for the knight. [Bd2] unpins the knight on c3 at once and sets up tricks with Nd5 or Nb5, which would uncover an attack on the queen; Black answers with ...c6 so the queen can go to c7. [Bc4] aims at f7 and leaves White free to choose between Nf3 and Nge2. In all lines Black aims for ...c6, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4, ...e6 and ...Nbd7. [%csl Yc6,Yf3][%cal Gf1c4] }
5. Nf3
{ Black chooses how to arrange the light-squared bishop and pawn structure. [c6] first is the most flexible: the queen gets c7, b5 is covered, and Black can still pick f5 or g4. [Bf5] develops at once and usually transposes. The Lasker pin ...Bg4 is also common, though h3 forces Black either to give up the bishop pair on f3 or to allow g4 and Ne5 with a clear White edge. Always bring the light bishop out before ...e6. [%csl Yh3,Yg4,Yh4][%cal Gc7c6] }
( 5. Bd2
{ White unpins the knight at once. The bishop now x-rays the queen through the knight, so Nd5 or Nb5 would uncover an attack on it. [Bg4] is the engine's choice: it develops with a threat to d1, and after f3 the bishop drops back to d7. [c6] gives the queen c7 and covers d5 and b5. [Qb6] steps out of the bishop's line and hits d4 and b2. Moving the bishop to f5 here is less accurate, and ...e5 is a real mistake. [%csl Yd2,Yc6,Yc7][%cal Gc7c6] }
5... Bg4
