{ Black has covered f7. [g4] chases the bishop and is the critical try: after ...Bg6, h4 threatens h5, and Black must react with ...Bb4 or ...Nbd7. [O-O] and [Bd2] are calmer and lead to normal main-line positions. The engine likes White's space but the lines are sharp: Black's best answer to the pawn storm is often ...Bb4 and ...Nd5, hitting the knight on c3 and pinning it. [%cal Gg2g4,Ge1g1,Bc1d2] }
( 7... Bxc2 $4
{ The bishop grabs a pawn, but leaves f7 to its fate. [Bxf7+] checks; after ...Kd8, Qxc2 takes the bishop back, and Black's king is stuck in the centre with no castling. White is a pawn up with a winning attack. Play ...e6 to cover f7. [%csl Yf7,Ye6][%cal Rc4f7] }
8. Bxf7+ ) 8. g4
{ The bishop goes to g6, its only good square. White plans h4 to threaten h5, trapping it. Black's best reply is ...Bb4 or ...Nbd7, aiming to swap the knight on e5 or to get play against the knight on c3. If White takes on g6, Black takes back with the h-pawn and gets a half-open h-file. Trying to defend the bishop with ...h6 or ...h5 runs into h4-h5 and the bishop is lost. [%cal Gf5g6,Bf8b4] }
( 8. O-O ) ( 8. Bd2 ) 8... Bg6
{ White threatens h4-h5, trapping the bishop on g6. [h4] is the most direct, and Black should answer with ...Bb4 or ...Nbd7, accepting Nxg6 hxg6 rather than ...h5. [Bd2] keeps things calmer and prepares Qe2 and O-O-O. Black's counterplay comes from the pin on c3, the outpost on d5 and White's loose kingside pawns. White is better, but the position is hard to play for both. [%cal Gh2h4,Gc1d2,Bf8b4] }
( 8... Bxc2 $4
{ The bishop runs from the pawn by grabbing c2, but White's queen on d1 still defends that square. [Qxc2] wins a full piece for a pawn. When g4 attacks the bishop, its sound retreat is g6, accepting Nxg6 hxg6 with the half-open h-file in return. [%csl Yc2,Yd1,Yg4][%cal Rd1c2] }
9. Qxc2 ) 9. h4 ( 9. Bd2 ) ) 7... e6
{ The main tabiya of the 3...Qa5 Scandinavian. [Nd5] is the modern main line: the knight uncovers the bishop on d2 against Black's queen, forcing ...Qd8, and Nxf6+ trades off Black's best defender. [Qe2] is the older plan, preparing O-O-O, Ne5 and g4; Black answers actively with ...Bb4 to trade off the c3 knight and claim the d5 outpost. White can also play Ne4 and Ng3 to gain time on f5 while keeping pieces on the board. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gc3d5] }
8. Nd5
{ The knight has uncovered the bishop on d2 against the queen. [Qd8] is the only good square: it hits the knight on d5 and keeps an eye on the d-file. After Nxf6+ Black chooses how to take back. Every other move loses material: taking the knight drops the queen to the bishop, and the squares b6 and a4 are covered by the knight or by Bb3. [%cal Ga5d8] }
( 8. Qe2
{ The classical Qe2 plan: White prepares long castling, Ne5 and a kingside pawn storm while clearing d1 for the rook. [Bb4] is Black's most accurate reply, pinning and trading off the c3 knight after O-O-O Nbd7 a3 Bxc3 Bxc3 Qc7 so a black knight can claim the d5 outpost before ...O-O-O. Passive play with ...Be7 lets Ne5 and g4 roll forward unchecked. [%csl Yc3,Yd5][%cal Gf8b4] }
8... Bb4 ) 8... Qd8
{ Forced off a5 by White's discovered attack, Black's queen drops home and counterattacks the knight on d5. [Nxf6+] is the main line, trading off Black's best defensive piece and forcing a structural choice between ...gxf6 and ...Qxf6. [Ne3] preserves pieces and hits the bishop on f5, leading after ...Bg6 or ...Be4 to manoeuvring play where White keeps a slight edge. [%csl Ya5,Yd5,Yf5][%cal Gd5f6] }
( 8... cxd5 $4
{ The knight on d5 looks free, but taking it removes the shield between White's bishop on d2 and Black's queen on a5. [Bxa5] wins the queen for a knight — the exact discovered attack Bd2 and Nd5 set up. Drop the queen back to d8 instead. [%cal Rd2a5] }
9. Bxa5 ) ( 8... Qa4 $4
{ The queen steps off the bishop's diagonal, but onto a square where it can be trapped. [Bb3] hits it, and with the knight on d5 covering b4, c3 and b6, the queen has to take on b3 and Black gives it for a bishop. Go back to d8. [%csl Yd5,Yb4,Yc3][%cal Rc4b3] }
9. Bb3 ) ( 8... Qb6 $4
{ The queen moves to b6, but the knight on d5 attacks that square. [Nxb6] wins the queen for nothing more than a pawn. Go back to d8, the only safe square, which also hits the knight. [%csl Yb6,Yd5,Yd8][%cal Rd5b6] }
9. Nxb6 ) 9. Nxf6+
{ White trades the knight on f6, Black's best defender. [Qxf6] is the engine's choice: it keeps the structure, but the queen can be hit by Bg5 or g4 later. [gxf6] is the main line in practice: the pawns on f6, e6 and f7 look ugly but control e5 and g5, and the half-open g-file can be useful. White's plan is Nh4 to hunt the bishop, and castling long; Black castles long too and aims for ...c5. White keeps a small edge in both. [%csl Ye5,Yg5][%cal Gg7f6] }
( 9. Ne3 ) 9... Qxf6
{ Recapturing with the queen keeps Black's kingside pawn structure intact, at the cost of exposing the queen to minor-piece harassment. [Qe2] prepares long castling and Ne5 or Bg5 while eyeing the e-file; [O-O] castles short safely before Re1 and c3, and [Bg5] gains immediate time on the queen, forcing ...Qg6. Black regroups with ...Nd7, ...Bd6 or ...Bg4 and ...Nb6 to contest d5. [%csl Yg4][%cal Gd1e2] }
( 9... gxf6
{ The signature structure of the modern Qa5 main line. Black's doubled f-pawns look ragged, yet they firmly control e5 and g5 while opening the g-file for counterplay. [Bb3] tucks the bishop away ahead of Qe2, O-O-O and Nh4 to hunt the bishop on f5; [Qe2] and [O-O] are solid alternatives. Black answers with ...Nd7, ...Qc7 and ...O-O-O, using ...Bg6 and a timely ...f5 or ...c5. [%csl Ye5,Yg5,Yf5][%cal Gc4b3] }
10. Bb3 ( 10. Qe2 ) ( 10. O-O ) ) 10. Qe2 ( 10. O-O ) ( 10. Bg5 ) *

[Event "Pirc and Modern Defence (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 d6
{ The Pirc Defence, named after the Yugoslav grandmaster Vasja Pirc. Instead of meeting [e4] head-on in the centre, Black holds back the central pawns, invites [d4], and plans [b:Nf6] and [b:g6] so the dark-squared bishop can fianchetto on g7 and fire down the long diagonal at White's centre. Once White's pawns step forward, Black chips away at their base with [b:c5] or [b:e5]. [d4] is White's sharpest test, taking the full centre at once, while [Nf3], [Nc3] and [f4] steer into more flexible setups. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gd2d4] }
( 1... g6
{ The Modern (or Robatsch) Defence, pioneered by Karl Robatsch and Duncan Suttles. Like the Pirc, Black fianchettoes on g7 and invites White to build a broad pawn centre, but by holding back [b:Nf6] Black keeps the g7 bishop's diagonal wide open, avoids [w:e5] with tempo, and reserves flexible setups from [b:c6]-[b:d5] and [b:c5] (Pterodactyl) to [b:a6]-[b:b5] (Tiger's Modern) and the Hippopotamus. [d4] is White's principled answer; [Nf3], [Nc3] and [Bc4] are common alternatives. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gd2d4] }
2. d4
{ White claims the full pawn centre. [Bg7] is the standard Modern move, putting immediate hypermodern pressure on d4 before deciding where the knights and pawns go; [d6] transposes to the Pirc/Modern complex, while [Nf6] is the tricky Norwegian Defence (played by Magnus Carlsen), provoking [w:e5] [b:Nh5]. Holding back [b:Nf6] is the Modern's defining feature: Black keeps the h8-a1 diagonal unblocked and denies White any free tempo with [w:e5]. [%csl Yd4,Yh8,Ya1][%cal Gf8g7] }
( 2. Nf3
{ Developing [Nf3] first keeps [c3]-[d4] or [c4]-[d4] available while ruling out early [b:e5] ideas. Black finishes the fianchetto with [Bg7] (or [c5] transposing to the Hyperaccelerated Dragon, covered in the Sicilian chapter). Keeping c2 unobstructed means White can build an Alapin-like c3-d4 centre that denies Black's g7 bishop open targets down the long diagonal and prepares smooth [w:Bc4] and [w:O-O] development. [%csl Yc2,Yc3,Yd4][%cal Gf8g7] }
2... Bg7
{ Black's bishop is on its long diagonal without committing a central pawn yet. [d4] is White's main choice, reaching the Two Knights Modern after [d6 Nc3], while [Bc4] eyes f7 ([d6] or [e6] blunts it cleanly) and [c3] prepares a rock-solid [d4] centre. White's soundest strategy against the delayed-pawn Modern is to occupy d4, protect e4 cleanly, and castle before Black's flank counterplay gets rolling. [%cal Gd2d4,Gf1c4,Gc2c3] }
3. d4 ( 3. Bc4
{ Aiming the bishop at f7 against a fianchettoed bishop hits solid granite as soon as Black shields the diagonal, and the exposed bishop on c4 often becomes a tactical target for Black's central breaks. [c5] strikes straight into Sicilian Dragon territory with [Nc6] and [d6] to follow; [d6] prepares [Nf6] and [c5] (watching out for center-fork tricks if White gets careless), while [c6] readies [d5] with tempo on c4. [%cal Gc7c5,Gd7d6,Bc7c6] }
3... c5 ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... c6 ) ) ( 3. Nc3 ) ( 3. c3 ) 3... c5 4. Bc4 cxd4 5.
Nxd4
{ The Siroccopteryx Variation of the Western Pterodactyl looks like an Accelerated Dragon where White already put the bishop on c4 — and with Black's queen's knight still on b8, [Qa5+] ([w:Nc3] [b:Qc5]) is a venomous resource alongside [Nc6] ([w:Be3] [b:Nf6] [w:Nc3]) and [d6]! Black must avoid the careless Scheveningen reflex [b:e6], which weakens d6 terribly when g6 is already on the board. [%cal Gb8c6,Gd7d6,Bd8a5] }
5... Nc6 ( 5... d6 ) ( 5... Qa5+ ) ( 5... e6 $2
{ Combining [g6] with [e6] leaves a crater on d6 while b8 is still undeveloped: [Nb5] threatens [w:Nd6+] stripping Black of castling rights and forces desperate concessions in the centre. Develop [b:Nc6] or play [b:d6] instead! [%csl Rd6][%cal Rd4b5] }
6. Nb5 d5 7. exd5 exd5 8. Qxd5 ) ) ( 2. Nc3
{ Bringing out [Nc3] keeps [f4] (the Austrian/Grand Prix setup), [Be3]-[Qd2] and [Bc4] in reserve alongside [d4]. Black plays [Bg7] (or [c5] into the Closed Sicilian), waiting to see White's pawn structure before committing [b:d6] or [b:c5]. If Black plays [d6], [w:d4] reaches the main Modern tabiya, whereas [w:f4] or [w:Bc4] keeps the game in Closed-Sicilian or Grand-Prix channels where White attacks on the kingside. [%cal Gf8g7] }
2... Bg7
{ White can transpose to the main Modern with [d4], play [Nf3], or choose [f4] to build a Three-Pawns/Grand Prix wedge. Against [f4], Black should strike at the dark squares with [c5] or [d6] rather than trading off the prized fianchetto bishop or walking into [w:e5] with [b:Nf6]. Because Black has not yet played [b:Nf6], White's [f4] or [w:Be3] setups face fast dark-square counterplay via [b:c5], [b:Nc6] and [b:b5] rather than kingside targets. [%cal Gd2d4,Gf2f4,Gg1f3] }
( 2... d6 ) 3. d4 ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. f4
{ White builds an aggressive kingside front before touching the d-pawn. Black's cleanest replies are [c5] clamping down on d4 in Closed Sicilian style and [d6] keeping the centre flexible; giving up the g7 bishop or putting the knight into [w:e5] hands White an easy advantage. Against [c5], White typically develops [w:Nf3] and [w:Bc4] (or [w:Bb5] check) aiming for [w:O-O], [w:d3] and a kingside attack with [w:Qe1]-[w:Qh4] and [w:f5]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc7c5,Gd7d6] }
3... c5
{ Black strikes back at the light squares immediately, turning White's aggressive [f4] setup into a Grand Prix / Closed Sicilian battle where the g7 bishop rakes the long diagonal unopposed. [Nf3] is White's main continuation, heading for [w:Bc4] (or [w:Bb5+]), [w:d3], [w:O-O] and the classic [w:Qe1]-[w:Qh4] kingside transfer; [Bc4] eyes f7 straight away before Black plays [b:e6]; and [d3] keeps everything solid. White should avoid premature [w:e5] before minor pieces are out, as [b:d6] undermines the chain at once. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1c4] }
( 3... d6 ) ( 3... Bxc3 $2
{ Trading off the fianchettoed dark-squared bishop on move three leaves gaping holes around Black's kingside on f6 and h6 while handing White the bishop pair and [Qd4] threats for free. Keep the bishop and play [b:c5] or [b:d6]. [%csl Rf6][%cal Rd2c3,Gc7c5] }
4. dxc3 d6 5. Be3 Nf6 6. Bd3 ) ( 3... Nf6 $2
{ Without [b:d6] on the board, [Nf6] walks straight into [e5], chasing the knight back home to g8 (since h5 gets trapped by [w:Be2] and [w:g4]) while White claims the entire centre with [d4]. Strike at the dark squares with [b:c5] or play [b:d6] first. [%cal Re4e5,Rf6g8,Gc7c5] }
4. e5 Ng8 5. d4 d6 6. Nf3 ) 4. Nf3
{ The Grand Prix Pterodactyl reaches a Closed-Sicilian battleground where White's early [f4] aims for [w:Bc4] (or [w:Bb5+]), [w:O-O], [w:d3] and a direct [w:Qe1]-[w:Qh4] plus [w:f5] kingside assault. Black's cleanest counterplay is [Nc6], clamping down on d4 before choosing between [b:d6] plus [b:Nf6] and [b:e6] plus [b:Nge7] with [b:Rb8] and [b:b5] on the queenside; playing [b:e6] before [b:Nc6] leaves d6 horribly weak! [%csl Yd6][%cal Gb8c6,Gd7d6] }
( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. d3 ) 4... Nc6 ( 4... d6 ) ( 4... e6 $2
{ Playing [e6] before [b:Nc6] or [b:d6] leaves the d6 dark square gaping wide open: [e5 d5 d4] locks out Black's g7 bishop completely and threatens [w:Nb5]-[w:Nd6+], giving White a commanding dark-square bind! Develop [b:Nc6] or play [b:d6] first. [%csl Rd6][%cal Re4e5] }
5. e5 d5 6. d4 Bf8 7. Be3 ) ) ( 3. Bc4 ) 3... b6
{ The Mongredien Defence with [Nc3]: Black delays central pawn commitments altogether to fianchetto both bishops on g7 and b7, aiming long-range crossfire at d4 and e4 before tucked knights reach e7 and d7. White's sharpest answer is [Be3] ([b:Bb7] [w:Qd2] [b:d6] [w:f3] or [w:h4]), building a fast attacking battery while Black's centre stays passive; [Nf3] and [f4] are strong too. [%csl Yg7,Yb7,Yd4][%cal Gc1e3] }
4. Be3 ( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. f4 ) ) ( 2. Bc4
{ Pointing the bishop at f7 introduces the club-favourite Monkey's Bum ([Bg7 Qf3]), trying to punish careless fianchetto players on move four. Black plays [Bg7] confidently — as long as [w:Qf3] is met calmly with [b:e6] — or blunts the diagonal right away with [e6]. Once Black's pawn lands on e6 (with [b:Ne7] and [b:d5] to follow), White's c4 bishop often has to drop back to b3 or e2, so White should develop [w:Nf3] and [w:d4] promptly. [%csl Yf7,Ye6,Yc4][%cal Gf8g7] }
2... Bg7
{ Here [Qf3] is the Monkey's Bum, threatening mate in one on f7 and tempting careless opponents into fatal blunders; [d4] and [Nf3] are sounder developing moves, since Black has a clean equalizer against the early queen sortie. Sound development with [d4] or [Nf3] keeps a lasting space pull in the centre, whereas [Qf3] banks everything on Black missing the calm [b:e6] blocker. [%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3,Yd1f3] }
( 2... e6 ) 3. d4 ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. Qf3
{ The Monkey's Bum threatens [w:Qxf7#] immediately on move three. Despite its crude appearance, countless club games end right here when Black plays a reflex Pirc move on autopilot! [e6] is the calm, complete antidote: it shuts the c4 bishop's diagonal cold and prepares [b:Ne7] and [b:d5] with tempo against White's exposed queen and bishop, leaving White having to untangle awkwardly on the kingside. [%csl Yc4][%cal Ge7e6,Rf3f7] }
3... e6
{ With [e6] blunting the diagonal to f7, White's queen on f3 stands in front of the g1 knight's natural square. White does best to regroup modestly with [Ne2] or [Nc3] followed by [d3]. Pushing [w:d4] sacrifices a central pawn to [b:Bxd4] — where White must continue [w:Ne2] or [w:c3], never careless [w:Be3], which blunders b2 and the a1 rook to [b:Bxb2] — while [w:Nh3] strands the knight on the rim. [%cal Gg1e2,Gb1c3,Gd2d3] }
( 3... Nf6 $4
{ Blocking the mate with the knight walks straight into [e5]: if the knight moves, [w:Qxf7#] is mate, and after [e5 O-O exf6 Bxf6] Black has lost a clean minor piece on move four! Blunt the a2-g8 diagonal calmly with [b:e6] instead. [%cal Re4e5,Rf3f7,Ge7e6] }
4. e5 O-O 5. exf6 Bxf6 6. Nc3 ) ( 3... d6 $4
