4. Qxf7+ Kd7 5. Qxg7 Nf6 6. d3 ) ( 3... Nh6 $6
{ Guarding f7 with [Nh6] avoids mate in one, yet it parks the knight awkwardly on the rim where [w:Bxh6] can shatter Black's kingside or [d4] claims the whole centre with tempo threats! After [d4 O-O Nc3 Nc6 Nge2], White gets a free central grip while h6 stays misplaced. Shut the diagonal cleanly with [b:e6] instead. [%csl Yh6][%cal Rd2d4] }
4. d4 O-O 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nge2 ) ( 3... f6 $2
{ Blocking the f7 mate threat with [f6] is a classic beginner reflex that entombs Black's own g7-bishop behind granite and steals the f6 square from the g8-knight! After [d4 Nc6 Ne2], White claims the entire centre while Black's kingside pieces trip over each other. Shut the c4-bishop's diagonal cleanly with [b:e6] instead. [%csl Yf6][%cal Rd2d4] }
4. d4 Nc6 5. Ne2 Na5 6. Bd3 ) 4. Ne2 ( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. c3 ) ( 4. d3 ) ( 4. Nh3 $2
{ Developing the knight to the rim leaves d4 and e5 unguarded: [Nc6 c3 Ne5 Qe2 Nxc4 Qxc4 d5] lets Black snag the bishop pair with tempo and seize full command of the centre. Tuck the knight smoothly onto [w:Ne2] or develop [w:Nc3] instead. [%cal Rb8c6,Rc6e5,Gg1e2] }
4... Nc6 5. c3 Ne5 6. Qe2 Nxc4 7. Qxc4 d5 ) ( 4. d4
