3... Kxf7 4. d4 d5 5. e5 c5 ) ) ( 2. d3
{ A quiet closed response to 1...g6: White plans [w:Nf3], [w:g3], [w:Bg2] and [w:O-O] in King's Indian Attack style, aiming to build up slowly with [w:Nbd2] and [w:e5] later on. Because White puts no immediate pressure on d5 or e5, Black finishes [Bg7] and claims easy central space with [c5], [b:Nc6], [b:d6] and [b:e6]-[b:Nge7], preparing queenside expansion via [b:Rb8] and [b:b5]. [%csl Yd5,Ye5][%cal Gf8g7] }
2... Bg7 ( 2... c5 ) ) ( 2. f4
{ Grabbing f4 on move two aims for a Three-Pawns wall after [w:d4] and [w:Nf3]. Black replies [Bg7] (or immediate [d5] striking at e4 while White's diagonal to h4 is slightly softened) and counters d4 promptly with [b:c5]. If Black strikes immediately with [d5], [w:e5] ([b:c5] [w:Nf3] [b:Nc6]) leads to a reversed French structure where Black's light-squared bishop is still free to reach g4 or f5. [%csl Yf4,Ye4,Yh4][%cal Gf8g7] }
2... Bg7 ( 2... d5 ) ( 2... c5 ) ) ( 2. c4
{ Grabbing c4 and e4 on move two stakes out a Maróczy- or English-style clamp before Black's bishop even reaches g7, ruling out [b:d5] breaks and preparing [w:d4] and [w:Nc3] into a towering Averbakh three-pawn front. Black finishes [Bg7] and strikes back at the dark squares with [c5] ([w:Nc3] [b:Nc6] [w:g3] [b:d6] [w:Bg2]) or [b:d6] followed by [b:e5] or [b:Nc6] to contest d4. [%csl Yc4,Ye4,Yg7][%cal Gf8g7] }
2... Bg7 ( 2... c5 ) ) 2... Bg7
{ The foundational tabiya of the Modern Defence. Black's g7 bishop X-rays d4 and e5 while every central pawn and both knights remain uncommitted, ready to adapt to White's setup. [Nc3] is the sharpest test, [Nf3] develops flexibly, [c4] builds the broad Averbakh three-pawn front, [f4] is the Three Pawns Attack, and [c3] sets up a granite chain preventing [b:c5]/[b:Nd4] counterplay. Because Black has revealed neither [b:d6] nor [b:c5], White gets best results by developing minor pieces efficiently and keeping d4 and e4 overprotected against dark-square tactics. [%csl Yg7,Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 2... d6 3. Nc3
{ Whether reached via [g6] or [d6] on move one, delaying [b:Nf6] is the hallmark of the Modern move order: Black keeps the g7 diagonal wide open and denies White any [w:e5] tempo against a knight on f6. [Bg7] enters the Standard Modern, where Black can meet [Bg7 Be3] with [b:a6] and [b:b5] (Tiger's Modern); [Nf6] transposes directly into the main-line Pirc; and [c6] sets up Suttles's flexible dark-square shell, taking d5 and b5 away from White's knight while preparing [b:b5] or [b:d5]. [%cal Gf8g7,Gg8f6,Gc7c6] }
3... Bg7 ( 3... Nf6 ) ( 3... c6 ) ) ( 2... Nf6
{ The Norwegian Defence (or "Norwegian Rat") invites [e5] before [b:d6] is played: after [e5 Nh5], Black plans [b:d6] (gambiting or undermining e5) while keeping g7 for the knight ([b:Ng7]) rather than the bishop! White must accept the challenge with [e5] (or [Nc3] transposing to the Pirc), never passive [Nf3]. In the main Norwegian line [e5] [b:Nh5] [w:Be2] [b:d6], Black gambits or trades off e5 to untangle the rim knight via g7 while White enjoys easy development. [%csl Ye5,Yg7][%cal Ge4e5] }
3. e5
