{ The main tabiya of the Classical Pirc. [Be2] is the traditional master main line (played by Karpov in his decisive Game 32 win over Korchnoi in 1978), [h3] stops [b:Bg4] before developing the bishop to e3, while [Bc4], [Bd3], [Be3] and [Bg5] are the most popular club continuations. Early [e5] is harmless after [b:dxe5] [w:Nxe5] [O-O]. White's central duo on d4 and e4 gives freer piece play, so Black's top priority after castling is to force through [b:c5], [b:e5] or [b:Bg4] before White consolidates. [%csl Ye3,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gf1e2] }
5. Be2
{ The Quiet (or Classical) System: White unpins f3 in advance, prepares [O-O], and builds a rock-solid central foundation for [Be3], [Qd2] and [Re1] without creating pawn weaknesses. Black castles with [O-O] and gets ready to strike at the centre with [b:c5] or [b:Nc6] and [b:e5]. Once both kings castle short, White centralises with [w:Re1] and [w:Bf4] or [w:Be3], daring Black to clarify the central structure with [b:c5], [b:Bg4] or [b:Nc6]. [%csl Yf3][%cal Ge8g8] }
( 5. h3
