{ Both kings are tucked away and Black must choose how to challenge White's central duo before getting suffocated by [Re1], [Bf4]/[Be3] and [Qd2]. [c5] hits d4 directly (after [d5 Na6], Karpov's [Bf4] [b:Nc7] [a4] clamps down on [b:b5]), [Bg4] trades off f3 to weaken d4 before [b:Nc6], and [Nc6] prepares [b:e5] (provided White does not stop it with [d5]). Each of Black's breaks changes the pawn structure completely — Benoni after [c5] [w:d5], open Sicilian-style files after [c5] [w:dxc5], or King's Indian tension after [Nc6] and [b:e5]. [%csl Yd4,Yf3][%cal Gc7c5] }
