[Event "Pirc and Modern Defence (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 d6 ( 1... g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3
{ The main crossroads of the Modern Defence after 3.Nc3. [d6] is the Standard Line, keeping [b:a6]-[b:b5] (Tiger's Modern), [b:c6] (Suttles) and [b:Nf6] (Pirc transposition) in reserve; [c6] prepares [b:d5] (Gurgenidze) to blunt the fianchetto challenges; and [c5] is the razor-sharp Pterodactyl ("The Sniper"), hitting d4 immediately with pawn and bishop. Whichever third move Black chooses, the underlying theme is dark-square pressure: Black delays kingside castling and attacks d4 or the queenside while White prepares a central or h-file assault. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd7d6] }
( 3. Nf3
{ Developing [Nf3] keeps White's setup flexible between [Nc3], [c3] (Geller), [Bc4] and [Be3] while stopping early [b:e5] breaks. Black usually plays [d6] (or [c6] preparing [b:d5] in Gurgenidze style); [e6] and [b6] lead into the Hippopotamus double-fianchetto. Because c2 is still unblocked, White can meet [c6] or [d6] with either [w:Nc3] aggression or Geller's solid [w:c3] wall depending on taste. [%csl Yc2][%cal Gd7d6] }
3... d6
{ From here [Nc3] reaches the Two Knights Modern (where Black can transpose to the Classical Pirc with [b:Nf6], or stay independent via [b:a6] + [b:b5] Tiger's Modern, [b:Bg4], or [b:c6]); [c3] is Geller's solid anti-counterplay system; and [Bc4] or [Be3] develops actively without blocking the c-pawn. Delaying [b:Nf6] lets Black answer [Bc4] with [b:e6]-[b:Ne7] or [Nc3] with [b:a6]-[b:b5], sidestepping White's prepared Classical Pirc lines. [%cal Gb1c3] }
( 3... c6
{ Preparing [b:d5] (Gurgenidze) or [b:d6] while keeping b5 and d5 covered. White usually plays [Nc3] ([d5] or [d6]), [Bc4] ([d5 exd5 cxd5 Bb5+ Bd7]) or [c3] ([d5 e5 Bg4]). Against [c3] [b:d5] [w:e5], Black pins with [b:Bg4] and routes [b:Nh6]-[b:Nf5], getting active light-square play against White's fixed pawn chain while keeping the g7 bishop ready to join a timely [b:f6] break. [%csl Yb5,Yd5,Yg7][%cal Gb1c3] }
4. Nc3 ( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. c3 ) ( 4. Be2 ) ) ( 3... e6
{ Playing [e6] introduces the Hippopotamus System, pioneered by Slovak master Maximilián Ujtelky and brought onto the World Championship stage by Boris Spassky against Petrosian in 1966. Black tucks pawns onto g6, e6, d6 and b6 with bishops on g7 and b7 and knights on e7 and d7, offering White's pieces no immediate targets while waiting to see which wing White castles on before striking back with [b:c5], [b:e5] or [b:f5]. [%csl Yg6,Ye6,Yd6][%cal Gb1c3] }
4. Nc3 ( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. Bd3 ) ( 4. Be3 ) ( 4. c3 ) ) ( 3... b6
{ The double-fianchetto route into the Hippopotamus (or Mongredien Defence): [b:Bb7] will join [Bg7] so both bishops rake the centre from the flanks, backed by [b:e6], [b:Ne7], [b:d6] and [b:Nd7]. White's best antidote is rapid central development ([Nc3], [Bd3], [Be3], [Qd2]) while watching out for [b:c5] or [b:f5] counter-strikes if White's pawns overreach; Black's main risk in the Hippo is opening files prematurely while uncastled or letting [w:h4]-[w:h5] pry open g6 after castling short. [%csl Yg6][%cal Gb1c3] }
4. Nc3 ( 4. Bd3 ) ( 4. Bc4 ) ( 4. c4 ) ) 4. Nc3 ( 4. Bc4
{ Pointing the bishop at f7 discourages immediate [b:e5] breaks and prepares [O-O], [Qe2] and [h3]. Black answers with [Nf6] (transposing into the Classical Pirc 5.Bc4, where [Qe2] or [Nc3] [O-O] follows) or [e6] + [b:Ne7] in Hippopotamus style, blunting the c4 bishop completely. When Black chooses Hippo-style [e6] and [b:Ne7], White typically tucks the bishop back to b3 after [w:c3] and builds central pressure with [w:O-O] and [w:Re1]. [%csl Yf7,Yc4,Yb3][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... e6 ) ( 4... c6 ) ) ( 4. Be3
{ Developing [Be3] keeps White's queenside knight flexible (allowing [Qd2], [Nc3] or [c3]/[c4]) while discouraging [b:c5]. Black can play [Nf6] [Nc3] into the Pirc, [a6] + [b:b5] in Tiger's Modern style, or [Nd7] + [b:e5]. If Black expands on the queenside with [a6] and [b:b5], White can answer [w:Qd2], [w:Bd3] and [w:O-O] for a controlled central squeeze rather than castling into the storm. [%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... a6 ) ( 4... c6 ) ( 4... Nd7 ) ) ( 4. c3
{ Geller's System builds a solid c3-d4-e4 wall that blunts the g7 bishop's long diagonal and prepares [w:Bd3] (or [w:Be3]), [w:O-O], [w:Re1] and a Ruy-Lopez-style [w:Nbd2]-[w:Nf1]-[w:Ng3] maneuver. Black can transpose to a quiet Pirc with [Nf6], pin with [Bg4] to trade off f3 before striking at the centre via [b:Nd7] and [b:e5], or play [Nd7] immediately to challenge d4 and e4 without blocking the f-pawn. [%csl Yc3,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gg8f6] }
4... Nf6 ( 4... Nd7 ) ( 4... Bg4 ) ) ) ( 3. c4
{ The Averbakh System builds an imposing three-pawn front (c4-d4-e4) that claims maximum space. Notice that if Black plays [d6 Nc3] and later [b:Nf6], the game transposes directly into the King's Indian Defence! Pure Modern specialists keep their options independent with [d6] followed by [b:Nc6], [b:e5] or [b:Nd7], [c5] (Benoni/Sniper), or [e6] + [b:Ne7] (Hippopotamus). White's broad three-pawn front cramps Black severely if left unchallenged, so Black has to strike at d4 or e4 quickly before [w:Nc3], [w:Be3] and [w:Nf3] cement the bind. [%csl Yc4,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gd7d6] }
3... d6
{ Holding back [b:Nf6] lets Black attack d4 immediately without blocking the g7 bishop! After [Nc3], if Black plays [b:Nf6] we enter the King's Indian, whereas [b:Nc6] (Kotov's Variation, hitting d4 twice to force [w:Be3] or [w:d5]), [b:e5] ([w:d5] [b:f5]/[b:Nd7]) and [b:Nd7] keep pure Modern flexibility. Keeping g8 uncommitted lets Black hit d4 with [b:Nc6] and [b:e5] (or [b:Bg4] once [w:Nf3] appears) without ever blocking the g7 bishop's sightline. [%csl Yd4,Yg7,Yg8][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 3... e6 ) ( 3... c5
{ Black challenges d4 immediately before White's knights come out. [d5] is the critical response, shutting the g7 bishop's diagonal and claiming a Benoni wedge ([d6 Nc3 e6] or [d6 Nc3 a6]); [Nf3] allows [cxd4] [w:Nxd4] [Nc6] (Maróczy Accelerated Dragon), whereas trying to hold d4 with a piece fails tactically. After [d5] [b:d6] [w:Nc3], Black can aim for [b:e6] (Modern Benoni) or [b:a6] and [b:b5] (Benko-style queenside play) while the g8 knight stays flexible for e7 or f6. [%csl Yd4,Yg7,Yg8][%cal Gd4d5] }
4. d5 ( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. Be3 $6
{ Defending d4 with [Be3] walks into [Nc6 Ne2 Qb6], piling three attackers onto d4 while eyeing b2 before White's kingside pieces have moved. Push [w:d5] to shut down the g7 diagonal and claim space instead. [%csl Rd4,Rb2][%cal Gb8c6] }
4... Nc6 5. Ne2 Qb6 6. Nbc3 cxd4 7. Nd5 Qa5+ 8. Bd2 Qd8 ) 4... d6 5. Nc3
{ A Modern Benoni structure where Black has held back [b:Nf6] and [b:e6], keeping the g7 bishop's long diagonal completely unobstructed! Black can transpose into the main-line Modern Benoni with [e6] and [Nf6] ([w:Nf3] [b:exd5] [w:cxd5] [b:O-O]), or exploit the extra move-order flexibility via [a6] preparing [b:b5] on the queenside before committing the king's knight to f6 or e7. [%csl Yg7,Yf6,Ye7][%cal Gg8f6] }
5... Nf6 ( 5... e6 ) ( 5... a6 ) ) 4. Nc3
{ White reinforces e4 and d5 with the knight. Leaving aside [b:Nf6], which transposes into the King's Indian Defence, Black's sharpest pure Modern weapon is Kotov's [Nc6], double-attacking d4 with knight and g7 bishop so White must answer [w:Be3] (met by [b:e5] [w:d5] [b:Nce7] and a timely [b:f5] break) or [w:d5] [b:Nd4]. [e5] and [Nd7] keep a compact structure where Black contests d4 and e5 without committing the g8 knight early. [%csl Ye4,Yd5,Yd4][%cal Gb8c6] }
( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. Be3 ) 4... Nc6
{ Kotov's Variation puts immediate double pressure on d4 with the c6 knight and g7 bishop, forcing White to react on move five! [Be3] holds d4 and meets [e5] with [d5] [Nce7] (followed by [g4] or [f3], while Black prepares [b:f5]), whereas [d5] [b:Nd4] [Be3] [c5] [Nge2] [b:Qb6] triggers a sharp fight for the d4 outpost. Because Black committed c6 ahead of the c-pawn, White keeps a lasting space edge with [Be3], [w:f3]/[w:Nge2] and [w:Qd2], waiting to meet [b:f5] by opening central lines. [%csl Yd4,Yc6,Yg7][%cal Gc1e3] }
( 4... Nd7 ) ( 4... e5 ) 5. Be3 ( 5. d5 ) ( 5. Nf3 ) ) 3... d6
{ With [d6] played while g8 stays home, Black threatens queenside counterplay before committing the king. Against [Be3] (the 150 setup), Tiger Hillarp Persson's [b:a6] and [b:b5] — Tiger's Modern — launches an immediate queenside wave via [b:Bb7] and [b:Nd7] while the g7 bishop stays unblocked! Against [f4] (Pseudo-Austrian), Black can strike with [b:c5] or [b:a6] without fearing [w:e5] with tempo; [Nf3] reaches the Two Knights Modern. [%csl Yg8,Yg7][%cal Gc1e3] }
( 3... c6
