[Event "Alekhine's Defence"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 Nf6
{ Alekhine's Defence, introduced to elite chess by Alexander Alekhine at Budapest in 1921 and famously used twice by Bobby Fischer against Boris Spassky in their 1972 World Championship match. By attacking e4 with the knight on move one, Black tempts White's pawns to march forward and chase the knight so that Black can later chip away at an overextended pawn chain with [b:d6], [b:c5], [b:Bg4] and [b:g6]. [e5] is the principled reply, seizing central space with tempo; [Nc3] and [d3] keep the game quieter. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4e5] }
2. e5
{ White accepts the gauntlet, seizing central space and forcing Black's knight to move a second time on move two. [Nd5] is the only fully sound retreat, centralising the knight so it eyes f4 and b6 while provoking [d4] and [c4]. [Ng8] (the Brooklyn Variation) undevelops the knight to dodge [c4] tempos, while [Ne4] (Mokele Mbembe) is a reckless lunge into White's half of the board. [%csl Yf4,Yb6][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 2. Nc3
{ Developing [Nc3] defends e4 calmly and sidesteps main-line Alekhine pawn chases. If Black plays [e5], the game transposes into the Vienna Game; dedicated Alekhine players prefer [d5] (the Scandinavian Variation) to keep an asymmetric fight, while [d6] leads toward the Pirc after [d4]. Choosing [d5] keeps true Alekhine asymmetry on the board, forcing White to decide whether to close the centre with [w:e5] or open files via [w:exd5]. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ The Scandinavian Variation of the Alekhine strikes at e4 before White can build a broad centre. White has two main paths: [e5] pushes past to gain space ([Nfd7] or [d4]), while [exd5] enters an open game where [b:Nxd5] transposes to the Scandinavian Defence and [b:c6] is the sharp Geschev Gambit; [d3] is a quiet sideline. Whichever branch White selects, Black's queen's knight and light-squared bishop come out far more freely than in the cramped four-pawn main lines. [%csl Ye4][%cal Ge4e5] }
( 2... e5 ) ( 2... d6
{ Playing [d6] invites [d4], transposing directly into the main-line Pirc Defence where Black prepares [b:g6] or [b:c6]; if White prefers to stay in Open-style waters, [Nf3] ([g6] or [e5]) and [f4] ([g6 Nf3 Bg7]) are both sound. If White sidesteps Pirc main lines with [Nf3], [b:g6] [w:Bc4] [b:Bg7] [w:d3] leads to a quiet positional game where both sides castle short and maneuver in the centre. [%cal Gd2d4] }
3. d4 ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. f4 ) ) ( 2... g6 $2
