{ White completes the Three-Pawn centre via the 3.c4 move order. Because White's pawns stifle every piece on Black's back rank, Black has only one good move: [d6], attacking the e5 spearhead to force a decision before developing. Developing [Nc6] here is a fatal blunder! Once [d6] challenges e5, every White move must weigh central space against piece development before Black's bishops and knights gang up on d4. [%csl Ye5,Yd4][%cal Gd7d6] }
( 4. c5
{ The Lasker (or Chase) Variation: White kicks the knight yet again to cramp Black's camp and launch a kingside attack, accepting the strategic trade-off that d5 can never again be controlled by a White pawn! Black's knight hops right back to its dream outpost with [Nd5] (since a4 and c4 drop the piece). From here White tries to whip up a fast piece attack against Black's king before Black's [b:b6] and [b:d6] pawn breaks dismantle the c5-e5 wedge. [%csl Yd5,Ya4,Yc4][%cal Gb6d5] }
4... Nd5
{ Black's knight has moved four times in four turns, yet it sits unassailably on d5 while White has zero pieces developed. White's most accurate continuation is [Nc3], challenging the blockade knight right away ([e6] [Nxd5] [b:exd5] [d4] [b6] or [b:Nxc3] [w:dxc3] [e6]/[d6]); [Bc4] is very popular too ([e6 Nc3] Lasker's Simul Gambit, or [e6 d4 b6] chipping away at c5), and [d4] bolsters the chain. [%csl Yd5,Yc5][%cal Gb1c3] }
5. Nc3
{ Challenging the d5 knight directly is White's cleanest plan in the Chase Variation: if Black trades [Nxc3], White recaptures [w:dxc3] (opening the d-file and c1 diagonal while eyeing Matsukevich's sharp [d6] [Bg5] trap) or [w:bxc3] (building a big [d4] centre); if Black bolsters the knight with [e6], [Nxd5] [b:exd5] [d4] followed by [b:b6] undermining c5 leads to a rich strategic fight. Trading off or dislodging that d5 blockader is White's central priority, since otherwise Black sits safely behind it while chipping away at c5 and e5. [%csl Yd5,Yc1,Yc5][%cal Ge7e6] }
( 5. Bc4
{ Aiming at d5 and f7 invites sharp play, though Black's centralized knight is easy to support. Black should play [e6] (or [c6]), cementing d5 while preparing [b:b6] and [b:d6] to dissolve White's overextended c5-e5 chain from the base up. Once [e6] cements the d5 knight, White must follow up energetically with [w:Nc3] or [w:d4] before [b:b6] and [b:d6] leave White's advanced pawns stranded. [%csl Yd5,Yf7,Yc5][%cal Ge7e6] }
