{ Developing [Nf3] before [d4] keeps [c4] and [Bc4] flexible while preventing any [b:Nf4] ideas. Black challenges the spearhead with [d6] (usually transposing to the Modern Variation after [d4], or [Bc4 Nb6 Bb3]) or solidifies d5 quietly with [e6] ([d4 d6]/[c5]). Delaying [w:d4] also lets White answer [d6] with [w:Bc4] [b:Nb6] [w:Bb3], eyeing f7 while keeping the d-pawn flexible between d3 and d4. [%csl Yd5,Yf7,Yd3][%cal Gd7d6] }
3... d6 ( 3... e6 ) ) 3... d6
{ Black's [d6] is the fulcrum of the entire Alekhine Defence: it attacks the e5 spearhead and forces White to decide what kind of game to play. [Nf3] is the Modern Variation, supporting e5 with a piece for a controlled positional edge; [c4] kicks the knight to b6 before choosing between the Exchange Variation (5.exd6) and the explosive Four Pawns Attack (5.f4); and [Bc4] is the tricky Balogh Variation. [%csl Ye5,Yb6][%cal Gc2c4] }
