{ White expands with [c4], grabbing central territory and driving Black's knight away from d5 before resolving the tension on e5. Black has only one safe square: [Nb6]! Going to b4 is a classic blunder that loses a piece on the spot. Once the knight retreats to [Nb6], White stands at the main junction between trading on d6 for positional control and launching [w:f4] for an all-out brawl. [%csl Yd5,Ye5,Yb4][%cal Gd5b6] }
( 4. Nf3
{ The Modern Variation is White's highest-scoring and most solid weapon against the Alekhine: instead of overpushing pawns, White develops a knight to bolster e5 and d4. Black's three main answers all fight for e5: [Bg4] pins the f3 defender (Main Line), [g6] prepares [b:Bg7] pressure along the long diagonal (Alburt Variation, Fischer's Game 13 choice), and [dxe5] trades immediately (Larsen Variation). Because White's c-pawn stays back for now, d4 remains far less vulnerable than in the Four Pawns Attack, forcing Black to work patiently for equality. [%csl Ye5,Yd4,Yf3][%cal Gc8g4] }
4... Bg4
{ The Classical Main Line of the Modern Variation: Black pins the f3 knight so it can no longer recapture on e5 if Black plays [b:dxe5]. [Be2] is the main move, calmly breaking the pin while preparing [O-O] and [c4]; [h3] is Panov's direct challenge ([Bxf3 Qxf3 dxe5 dxe5 e6]), and [c4 Nb6 exd6] is Alekhine's own line. That pin on f3 is Black's key tactical equalizer in the Modern Variation, indirectly pressuring both e5 and d4 while clearing the queenside for [b:Nc6]. [%csl Yf3,Ye5,Yd4][%cal Gf1e2] }
( 4... g6
{ Lev Alburt's Variation — immortalised by Bobby Fischer's victory over Boris Spassky in Game 13 of the 1972 World Championship — delays trading on e5 and fianchettoes [b:Bg7] to besiege White's centre from afar. White's sharpest test is [Bc4 Nb6 Bb3 Bg7] (setting up [w:Ng5] or [w:Qe2] pressure; Nigel Short famously walked his king to g4 in this line against Timman!), while [c4 Nb6] often transposes to the Exchange Variation. [%csl Ye5,Yg4][%cal Gf1c4] }
