{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. e4 Nc6
{ The Nimzowitsch Defence, brought into tournament chess by Aron Nimzowitsch and championed in modern times by Tony Miles. By developing the queen's knight on move one without touching the d- or e-pawn, Black keeps the central strike hidden: if White claims the full centre with [d4], Black hits back immediately with [b:d5] or [b:e5]. [Nf3] is the calmest reply, inviting Black back into an Open Game with [b:e5] while questioning Black's misplaced knight on c6 if Black chooses [b:d6] or [b:d5]; [Nc3] and [Bb5] are playable sidelines. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 1... b6
{ Owen's Defence, named after English vicar John Owen — who defeated Paul Morphy with it in London in 1858 — and later favoured by Tony Miles and Christian Bauer. Black cedes the centre on move one in order to fianchetto [b:Bb7], sniping at e4 and g2 down the long light-squared diagonal before striking at White's centre with [b:e6], [b:c5] or [b:f5]. [d4] is White's principled reply, claiming the full centre; [Nf3] and [Nc3] are solid alternatives. [%csl Ye4,Yg2][%cal Gd2d4] }
2. d4
{ White occupies the centre with both pawns. [Bb7] is the point of Owen's Defence, attacking e4 immediately down the long diagonal; [e6] prepares [b:Bb7] while keeping [b:Bb4+] available if White plays [c4] (the English Defence). Once [Bb7] lands on the long diagonal, White's guiding rule is to protect e4 solidly ([w:Bd3] or [w:Nc3]) rather than pushing [w:e5] prematurely into thin air. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gc8b7] }
( 2. Nf3
{ Developing [Nf3] keeps White's options open between [d4] (watching out that e4 will need a defender once [Bb7] lands!), [Nc3] and [Bc4]. Black plays [Bb7] attacking e4 with tempo, forcing [Nc3] or [d3]. After [Bb7] [w:Nc3] [b:e6] [w:d4] [b:Bb4] [w:Bd3], White neutralises the pin on c3 and castles short with a clean, unshakeable central duo and smooth kingside piece play. [%csl Ye4,Yc3][%cal Gc8b7] }
