{ The Barnes Defence (used by Thomas Wilson Barnes to beat Morphy in an 1858 offhand game) violates every opening rule at once: it opens zero diagonals, steals f6 from the g8 knight, and exposes the e8-h5 diagonal. White punishes it simply by taking the full centre with [d4] (though cheeky [Qh5+ g6 Be2] sets the hilarious [b:gxh5]?? [w:Bxh5#] trap!). After [d4], White follows up with [w:Nc3], [w:Bc4] or [w:Bd3], and [w:Nf3], ready to blast open the e- and d-files the moment Black touches a central pawn. [%csl Yf6,Yg8,Ye8][%cal Gd2d4] }
2. d4
{ With [d4] on the board, White controls the centre and tactically prevents [b:e5]! Black's only semi-solid try is [e6] (followed by [b:Ne7] and [b:d5]) or [g6] + [b:Nh6]-[b:Nf7]; [Kf7]?! is the tongue-in-cheek Fried Fox ("Hammerschlag"), while [g5]?? and [e5]? walk into immediate disasters! Even against Black's most stubborn [e6] and [b:Ne7] shell, White's uncontested d4-e4 duo, [w:Nc3], [w:Nf3] and [w:Bd3] deliver a lasting, effortless edge. [%csl Yd4,Ye4][%cal Ge7e6] }
