{ The Lemming Defence puts a knight on the rim where it controls zero central squares, though cunning players use it to transpose into the De Bruycker Caro-Kann ([d4 c6], routing [b:Nc7]) or to defend [b:c5] from a6. White takes the centre with [d4] (avoiding premature [Bxa6]? [b:bxa6], which hands Black the bishop pair and open b-file for free!). After [d4], White develops [w:Nf3], [w:Nc3] and [w:Bd3] naturally, forcing Black to spend another move rerouting the rim knight via c7 or b4. [%csl Ya6,Yc7,Yb4][%cal Gd2d4] }
2. d4 ( 2. Nf3 ) ) 2. Nf3
{ The Nimzowitsch Declined: White develops the king's knight first and dares Black to play 2...e5 into the Ruy Lopez, Italian or Scotch (covered in their own chapters). Hardcore Nimzowitsch players stay on independent ground with [d6] (the solid Williams Variation), [e6] (Franco-Nimzowitsch), [d5] (a delayed Scandinavian where c6 is blocked), or [Nf6]; [f5] is the wild Colorado Countergambit. Because White's d2 and c2 pawns remain untouched, White can adapt smoothly — claiming d4 against [d6] or [e6], or opening files immediately against [d5] and [f5]. [%csl Yc6,Yd2,Yc2][%cal Gd7d6] }
( 2. d4
{ White accepts Nimzowitsch's invitation and plants two pawns in the centre, threatening [w:d5] with tempo! Black has two classic counter-strikes: [d5] (Nimzowitsch's own favourite, hitting e4 in delayed-Scandinavian style) and [e5] (the Kennedy Variation, favoured by Tony Miles, hitting d4 directly); [e6] and [d6] (Mikėnas) are quieter sidelines. Whichever central strike Black chooses, White's unblocked c-pawn remains a key asset compared with Black's knight standing in front of c7. [%csl Ye4,Yd4,Yc7][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5
{ Nimzowitsch's main line attacks e4 immediately while d4 is already on the board — a key difference from the regular Scandinavian! White has three major paths: [e5] (Advance Variation, locking the centre so Black develops [b:Bf5] outside the chain), [exd5 Qxd5] (Exchange Variation, where d4 is already under attack so 4.Nc3 sacrifices it!), and [Nc3] (Bogoljubow Variation, inviting 3...dxe4 4.d5). Knowing that d4 is already under fire once Black's queen recaptures on d5 saves White from blundering a central pawn by Scandinavian habit. [%csl Ye4,Yd4,Yd5][%cal Ge4e5] }
( 2... e5
{ The Kennedy Variation — Tony Miles's dynamic weapon — attacks d4 twice on move two! Note that 3.Nf3 transposes directly into the Scotch Game (covered in the Scotch chapter). Independent Nimzowitsch battles arise from [d5] (the Linksspringer Variation, kicking the knight and locking the centre) and [dxe5] (opening the board for [Nxe5] or the tricky de Smet Gambit [d6]). Avoiding 3.Nf3 keeps the game in pure Nimzowitsch territory, where [d5] clamps down on space and [dxe5] opens the d-file for piece play. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd4d5] }
3. d5
{ The Linksspringer ("left knight") Variation pushes past to gain space and attack Black's knight — and nearly a quarter of club players blunder the game away on this exact move! Black MUST retreat the knight to [Nce7] (heading for g6, followed by [b:Bc5] before [b:d6], [b:Nf6] and [b:O-O] in a King's Indian-style kingside attack) or [Nb8] ([d6], [Be7]-[Bg5]); jumping forward to d4 traps the knight in broad daylight! [%csl Yg6,Yd4][%cal Gc6e7] }
( 3. dxe5
{ Capturing e5 opens the d-file and prevents Black from locking the centre. [Nxe5] is the sound main line, centralising the knight and challenging White to prove an edge with [Nf3] (Paulsen), [Nc3] (Keres) or [f4]; [d6] is the tricky de Smet Gambit ([w:exd6] [b:Bxd6], sacrificing a pawn for [b:Qe7], [b:Bg4] and [b:O-O-O] activity). In the [d6] de Smet Gambit, White keeps a clear edge after [w:exd6] [b:Bxd6] simply by developing [w:Nc3], [w:Be3], [w:Nf3] and [w:Be2] solidly. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gc6e5] }
