4. c3 Nf5 5. exf5 d6 6. Bd3 ) 4. Nf3 ( 4. c4 ) ( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. h4 ) ) ( 2... d6
{ The Mikėnas Variation of Nimzowitsch's Defence holds back the d-pawn to keep the game closed and flexible, often aiming for [Nf6] and [g6] or [b:e5]. White's most testing reply is [d5], kicking the knight back to b8 or e5 and claiming a big central wedge at once. Developing [Nf3] (where [Bg4] pins the knight in Pirc-Mikėnas style) or [Nc3] keeps both central pawns abreast with an easy spatial edge. [%cal Gd4d5] }
