{ The Woodchuck Variation combines [Nc6] with an early [a6], preparing [b:e5] or [b:e6] while ruling out [w:Bb5] pins and teasing a queenside [b:b5] advance. Because Black has spent two moves on the flank without contesting the middle, [d5] is White's most direct punishment, driving the knight back to [b:Nb8] or [b:Ne5] ([w:f4]) with a big space lead. Developing calmly with [Nf3] or [Nc3] is equally strong, keeping the central duo intact until Black commits a central pawn. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gd4d5,Gg1f3] }
3. d5 ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. Nc3 ) ) ( 2... Nf6 $2
{ Developing [Nf6] without touching a central pawn walks straight into [d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5], chasing both Black knights from pillar to post while White builds a crushing four-pawn roller! Strike with [b:d5] or [b:e5]. [%cal Gd4d5] }
3. d5 Ne5 4. f4 Ng6 5. e5 ) 3. e5
{ The Advance Variation closes the centre and gives White kingside space. Black's best reply is [Bf5], placing the light-squared bishop on an active diagonal ahead of [b:e6]! The strategic trade-off is clear: Black has solved the 'French bishop', yet the knight on c6 blocks the usual [b:c5] break, so Black attacks White's chain via [b:Qd7], [b:O-O-O] and [b:f6] (or retreats [b:Nb8] to push [b:c5]); immediate [f6] is sharper and riskier. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gc8f5] }
( 3. exd5
{ Trading on d5 opens the centre, and Black recaptures with [Qxd5], putting immediate double pressure on White's d4 pawn with queen and c6 knight! (Aachen's gambit [Nb4]? fails to [c4] holding the extra pawn while threatening [Qa4].) After [Qxd5] [w:Nf3], Black fights for dynamic piece play via [b:Bg4] and [b:O-O-O] down the open d-file to compensate for the blocked c-pawn. [%csl Yd5,Yd4,Yc6][%cal Gd8d5] }
3... Qxd5
{ Unlike the 1.e4 d5 Scandinavian where White plays 3.Nc3 with free tempo, here White's d4 pawn is ALREADY attacked twice! [Nf3] is White's accurate move, defending d4 first while saving [Nc3] for the next turn ([Bg4 Be2 O-O-O], where White lacked time for [h3], or [e5]); [Nc3] is Marshall's dubious gambit of the d4 pawn, and [Be3]/[c3] are solid alternatives. Once [Nf3] guards d4, White threatens [w:Nc3] with real tempo, forcing Black to pin f3 with [b:Bg4] or strike immediately with [b:e5]. [%csl Yd4,Yf3][%cal Gg1f3] }
4. Nf3
