[Event "Queen's Gambit (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 d5 2. c4
{ The Queen's Gambit. White offers the c-pawn to lure Black's d-pawn away from the center, so that [e4] can follow. It is not a real gambit: if Black takes, White wins the pawn back in most lines. Black's main answers are [e6], the Declined, which holds d5 firmly but shuts in the bishop on c8; [c6], the Slav, which holds d5 and keeps that bishop free; and [dxc4], the Accepted, which gives up the center for a moment to develop freely and hit back with [c5] or [e5]. [Nf6] looks natural but lets [cxd5], and after [b:Nxd5] [w:e4] White gets the big center for free. [%csl Rd5,Rc8][%cal Ge7e6] }
2... e6 ( 2... dxc4
{ The Queen's Gambit Accepted. Black gives up the center for a moment, planning to hit back with [c5] or [e5] once White has spent a move winning the pawn back. White should not hurry: [Nf3] first rules out [e5], [e3] prepares [Bxc4], and [e4] takes the whole center at once. On Lichess the most played move is [Nc3], but it lets Black strike with [e5] at once. Trying to keep the pawn with [b5] is a famous mistake: [a4] breaks up the queenside. [%cal Gg1f3] }
3. Nf3
{ White develops and stops [e5], Black's most direct way to fight for the center. Black's main move is [Nf6], usually followed by [e6] and [c5]: Black lets the pawn go back and strikes at d4 instead. [a6] prepares [b5] to keep the pawn a little longer, at the cost of time. Holding on to c4 with [b5] at once is met by [a4]: after [b:c6] [w:axb5] [b:cxb5] [w:b3], the pawns are given back and White's pieces come out first. [%csl Rd4,Rc4][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 3. e3
{ A solid move: the bishop on f1 is ready to take back on c4, and d4 is covered. The drawback is that e5 is no longer watched by a knight, so Black's best is [e5], striking at once: after [w:Bxc4] [b:exd4] [w:exd4], White gets an isolated pawn and Black free piece play. [Nf6] and [e6] lead back to the main lines. [b5]?!, holding on to the pawn, is played in a fifth of the games and fails: [w:a4] undermines the chain, and [b:c6]?! still drops a pawn to [w:axb5], since [b:cxb5]?? walks into [w:Qf3] winning a piece. [%csl Rf1,Rc4,Rd4][%cal Ge7e5] }
3... e5
{ The most active answer to [e3]: Black hits d4 before White's knight reaches f3. White takes back the pawn first with [Bxc4], and after [exd4 exd4] the position has an isolated pawn on d4. Black puts the bishop on d6, castles and blockades d5; White's pieces are active and the d-pawn may later push forward to free the game. The engine sees full equality. [dxe5] instead trades queens with [b:Qxd1+] [w:Kxd1], and White's king is left in the center. [%csl Rd4,Rf3,Rd6][%cal Gf1c4] }
( 3... Nf6
{ Black develops instead of clinging to the pawn, which is the healthy way to play the Accepted. [Bxc4] takes it back at once, and after [b:e6] [w:Nf3] [b:c5] [w:O-O] [b:a6], the game is the classical main line: White's pieces are active and [w:e4] or [w:d5] may come, while Black hits d4 with [b:c5] and gains space with [b:b5]. [Nf3] first is fine too. If Black fianchettoes with [b:g6] and [b:Bg7], the bishop on g7 presses on d4, while White's bishop on c4 eyes f7 and a queen check on a4 can test Black's queenside. [%csl Rd4,Rg7][%cal Gf1c4,Gg1f3] }
4. Bxc4
{ White has the pawn back and the bishop stands well on c4. Black's plan is the same as in the main line: [e6], then [c5] to hit d4, [a6] and [b5] to gain space with tempo on the bishop, and the other bishop to b7. [Bg4], pinning a knight that isn't on f3 yet, looks active but hits nothing: [Qb3] attacks b7 and f7 at once, and Black is in trouble. Develop the kingside first and only then think about the light-squared bishop. [%csl Rc4,Rd4,Rb7][%cal Ge7e6] }
( 4. Nf3 ) 4... e6
{ The classical set-up: Black opens the bishop on f8 and gets ready for [c5]. White plays [Nf3] and castles, reaching the main line of the Accepted, or [Nc3], keeping [Nge2] and [e4] in mind. Against both, Black's plan is the same: [c5] to hit d4, then [a6] and [b5], gaining space with tempo on the bishop, and the other bishop to b7. The check [Bb4+], the most played move on Lichess after [Nf3], does little: White blocks with [w:Bd2] or [w:Nc3], castles, and the bishop has no target, while the fight for d4 still needs [c5]. [%csl Rf8,Rd4][%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3] }
( 4... c5 ) ( 4... Bg4 $6
{ The bishop comes out before [e6] can shut it in, but with no knight on f3 it pins nothing. [Qb3] hits b7 and f7 at once, and Black has no good way to cover both. Develop the kingside first with [b:e6] and [b:c5], and bring the light-squared bishop to b7 after [b:a6] and [b:b5]. [%csl Rf3,Rb7,Rf7][%cal Gd1b3] }
5. Qb3 ) ( 4... Be6 $2
{ Opposing White's active bishop on c4 directly on e6 without pawn support is a classic structural blunder. White trades [Bxe6 fxe6] immediately, saddling Black with isolated doubled pawns on e6 and e7, a ruined kingside shelter, and a permanent blockade square on e5 after [Nf3]. Close the diagonal safely with [b:e6] or strike at d4 via [b:c5] instead. [%cal Rc4e6] }
5. Bxe6 fxe6 6. Nf3 c5 7. Nc3 ) ( 4... Ne4 $6
{ Jumping into e4 with an unsupported knight while the rest of Black's army sits on the back rank violates basic opening development. White develops [Nf3] and [Nc3] while eyeing [w:Qb3] against f7, forcing the knight to retreat via [Nd6 Bb3] after wasting two tempi. Play [b:e6] and [b:c5] to challenge the center soundly instead. [%cal Rg1f3] }
5. Nf3 Nd6 6. Bb3 g6 7. Nc3 ) 5. Nf3 ( 5. Nc3 ) ) ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... b5 $6
{ Holding on to the extra pawn feels natural, and a fifth of Lichess players try it. But the chain on b5 and c4 can be hit from the side: [a4] attacks it at once, and Black can't keep both pawns. Supporting it with [b:c6]?! still drops a pawn to [w:axb5], since [b:cxb5]?? walks into [w:Qf3] winning a piece. Let the pawn go and develop: [b:e5], [b:Nf6] or [b:e6]. [%csl Rb5,Rc4,Ra8][%cal Ga2a4] }
