{ The Marshall Defense, an attempt to develop the knight before defending d5. It is considered dubious, because White can take on d5 at once: after [cxd5 Nxd5 e4], the pawn chases the knight and White gets the ideal center with gain of time. Black usually has to retreat the knight to f6 or b6 and fight a big pawn center. [Nc3] and [Nf3] are fine too, often leading back to normal lines. [%csl Rd5,Rf6,Rb6][%cal Gc4d5] }
3. cxd5
{ White takes on d5, and Black must decide how to recapture. [Nxd5] is the usual move, though after [w:Nf3] and [w:e4] White gets a big center with tempo on the knight. [b:Qxd5] runs into [w:Nc3], gaining time again. [c6], the Tan Gambit, offers a pawn for rapid development after [w:dxc6] [b:Nxc6]. Without a pawn left on d5, Black cannot prevent White's [w:e4]. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 3. Nc3
