[Event "Queen's Gambit Declined (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6
{ The Queen's Gambit Declined is among the oldest and sturdiest defenses in chess. The pawn on e6 holds d5 firmly and opens a diagonal for the bishop on f8, which usually goes to e7 or b4. The price is the bishop on c8, shut in behind its own pawns: much of Black's play is about freeing it with [b:b6] and [b:Bb7], with [b:dxc4] and [b:c5], or with [b:e5]. White's main moves are [Nc3], [Nf3] and the Exchange [cxd5]. [%csl Re6,Rd5,Rf8][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 2... Nc6 3. Nf3 ( 3. Nc3 e6 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. a3
{ White takes a quiet prophylactic moment with [a3] against the Chigorin-Knight's hybrid, ruling out Black's annoying [b:Bb4] pin once and for all while preparing [w:b4] if Black ever captures on c4. Because Black's knight on c6 blocks the c-pawn, Black's position is slightly cramped: [Be7] prepares castling and [b:Ne4], [Bd6] eyes e5 more ambitiously, while [dxc4] opens the game so Black can follow up with [b:Bd6] and [b:e5] before White locks down the queenside. [%csl Rb4][%cal Gf8e7,Gf8d6] }
5... Be7 ( 5... Bd6 ) ( 5... dxc4 ) ) 3... e5
{ The Lazard Gambit in the Chigorin Defense, an Albin-style central strike with knights already out. Black sacrifices a pawn to rip open lines and disrupt White's smooth anti-Chigorin setup before [w:Nc3] lands. White's cleanest response is [dxe5], forcing Black to choose between pushing [b:d4] (an Albin where White's knight is already nicely placed on f3 for [w:g3] or [w:Nbd2]) or trading [b:Bb4+] and [b:dxc4]. Capturing with the piece via [Nxe5] [b:Nxe5] [w:dxe5] [b:d4] is also solid for White. [%csl Re5][%cal Gd4e5,Gf3e5] }
4. dxe5 ( 4. Nxe5 ) ) ( 2... Nf6 3. cxd5 c6
{ The Tan Gambit of the Marshall Defense. Realizing that recapturing [b:Nxd5] hands White a dream center after [w:e4], Black offers the c-pawn so [b:Nxc6] can develop with tempo and fight actively against d4. Accepting with [dxc6] [b:Nxc6] [w:Nf3] [b:e5] gives Black dangerous open-file initiative in blitz, though White keeps an extra pawn with accurate defense ([w:dxe5] [b:Qxd1+] [w:Kxd1] [b:Ng4] [w:Ke1]). Declining via [Nc3] or [Nf3] lets [b:cxd5] transpose cleanly into a completely harmless Exchange Slav! [%csl Rc6][%cal Gd5c6,Gb1c3] }
4. dxc6 ( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. Nf3 ) ) 3. Nc3
{ White adds pressure on d5 and prepares [Bg5]. Black's main move is [Nf6], after which the bishop on g5 can pin the knight. [Be7], the Alatortsev, avoids that pin and waits for White's knight to come to f3 first. [c5], the Tarrasch, strikes at once and accepts an isolated pawn for free piece play. [c6] heads for the Semi-Slav. All are sound; they lead to very different middlegames. [%csl Rd5,Rg5,Rf3][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 3. Nf3
{ By developing the king's knight first, White keeps the Catalan [w:g3] open and rules out the Schara Gambit against [c5] as well as the Nimzo-Indian pin against [w:Nc3]. Black's main reply is [Nf6], leading to the main Declined, Ragozin, Vienna or Catalan lines; [c5] enters the Tarrasch, [c6] the Semi-Slav, and [dxc4] the Queen's Gambit Accepted. [%csl Rf3][%cal Gg8f6] }
3... Nf6
{ The most flexible position of the Declined, often reached after [d4] [b:Nf6] [c4] [b:e6] [Nf3] [b:d5] when White sidesteps the Nimzo-Indian. White chooses between [Nc3] for the main lines, [Bg5] to pin before c3 is occupied, [e3] for a quiet game, and [w:g3] for the Catalan. Black picks between [b:Be7], [b:c5], [b:Bb4], [b:c6], [b:Nbd7] and [b:dxc4], developing and castling before freeing c8. [%csl Rc3][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 3... c6 ) ( 3... dxc4 ) ( 3... c5 4. cxd5
{ White resolves the tension on d5 immediately, aiming to saddle Black with an isolated queen's pawn while White's knight is still flexible on b1. Black's clean and standard recapture is [exd5], opening the c8-h3 diagonal for the light-squared bishop and preparing active piece play with [b:Nc6] and [b:Nf6]. Recapturing with [Qxd5] wastes queen tempos once [w:Nc3] develops with gain of time, and the Hennig-Schara gambit [cxd4] is far less effective here because [w:Nxd4] keeps d5 firmly under fire. [%csl Rc8,Rd5][%cal Ge6d5] }
4... exd5
{ The Tarrasch structure arises before White has played [Nc3]. Black accepts an isolated pawn on d5 in return for open lines, easy piece development and control of the e4 outpost. White's sharpest try is [g3], heading straight for the Rubinstein fianchetto system where [w:Bg2] eyeballs d5 down the long diagonal. [Nc3] transposes back to the main Tarrasch, [Bg5] hits the queen before [b:Nf6] is in, while [dxc5] lets [b:Bxc5] develop smoothly with active piece play. [%csl Rd5,Yd4][%cal Gg2g3,Gb1c3] }
5. Nc3 ( 5. g3 ) ( 5. dxc5 ) ( 5. Bg5
{ By playing [Nf3] before [w:Nc3], White sidesteps the Schara Gambit and hits Black's queen on d8 right away. Black should break the pin calmly with [Be7] or [Nf6], followed by [b:Nc6] and castling into the standard Tarrasch battle. Raiding b2 early with [Qb6]? backfires: [w:Nc3] or [w:Qb3] defends while piling up on the isolated d5 pawn before Black's kingside has moved. [%csl Rd8,Rb2,Rb6][%cal Gf8e7] }
5... Be7 ( 5... Nf6 ) ( 5... Qb6 $2
{ The queen goes after b2 before Black has developed. [Nc3] defends calmly, and after [b:cxd4] [w:Qxd4], the queen trade leaves Black with a weak isolated pawn and nothing to show for it. [b:Be7] or [b:Nf6] was the move. [%csl Rb2][%cal Gb1c3] }
6. Nc3 ) ) ) 4. Nc3 ( 4. Bg5
{ White pins the knight before playing [Nc3]. Black breaks the pin with [Be7], and after [w:Nc3] the game reaches the Orthodox. [h6] asks the bishop at once: after [w:Bxf6] [b:Qxf6], Black has the bishop pair. [Bb4+] uses the fact that White's knight is still on b1, and [dxc4] grabs the pawn: with no knight on c3, White can't build the center with [e4] at once. Black's plan is the same as always in the Declined: develop, castle, then free the bishop on c8. [%csl Yb1,Yc3,Yc8][%cal Gf8e7] }
4... Be7
{ Black breaks the pin cleanly and prepares kingside castling while White's queenside knight still sits on b1. Bringing out [Nc3] transposes straight into the main Orthodox Queen's Gambit Declined, while [e3] keeps the option of recapturing on c4 without blocking the c-file if Black grabs [e3 dxc4]. Clarifying the center first with [cxd5 exd5 e3] enters a sound Exchange Variation where White's knight has not yet committed to c3. [%csl Rb1][%cal Gb1c3,Ge2e3,Bc4d5] }
( 4... h6 ) ( 4... Bb4+ ) 5. Nc3 ( 5. e3 ) ( 5. cxd5 ) ) ( 4. e3
{ White plays the quiet set-up with [e3], keeping the bishop on c1 at home for now. Black is fully comfortable here and chooses a structure: [c5], hitting d4 at once; [Be7] and castling; [c6], a Semi-Slav; or [Bd6] and [Nbd7], preparing [e5]. The check [Bb4+] is played a lot but only helps White develop with [w:Bd2] or [w:Nbd2]. The key rule: take on c4 after White's bishop has moved from f1, so that [dxc4] wins a tempo, and then free the game with [c5] or [e5]. [%csl Rc1][%cal Gf8e7,Gc7c5,Bc7c6] }
4... Be7
{ Black prepares castling. White's main move is [Nc3], and after [b:O-O] the game reaches the classical position with [e3]. [Bd3] first lets Black take at once, [b:dxc4] [w:Bxc4], gaining the tempo White's bishop spent. [Nbd2] keeps c4 covered by the knight, so that [b:dxc4] can be met by [w:Nxc4]. In all these lines the bishop on c1 stays behind the e-pawn, so White often plays [b3] and [Bb2] later. [%csl Rc4][%cal Gb1c3,Gf1d3,Bb1d2] }
( 4... c5
{ Black strikes at d4 at once. The center may open in several ways. [Nc3] keeps the tension, and after [b:Nc6] the position is a symmetrical Semi-Tarrasch, where the first side to release the tension often gives the other an easy game. [cxd5] [b:exd5] gives Black an isolated pawn on d5 after the trade on c5 or d4, with free pieces in return. [dxc5] [b:Bxc5] is the simplest, with a level, symmetrical position. Black's pieces belong on c6, e7 or d6, and the rooks on the c- and d-files. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gb1c3,Gc4d5,Bd4c5] }
5. Nc3 ( 5. cxd5 ) ( 5. dxc5 ) ) ( 4... c6 ) ( 4... Bd6 ) ( 4... b6 5. Nc3
{ Black has chosen a Queen's Indian set-up with the pawn on d5. The bishop goes to b7 or the other bishop to e7. [Bb4]?? is a blunder: [w:Qa4+] checks the king and hits the bishop on b4, and Black cannot save both b4 and c6. Before putting an unguarded bishop on b4, always check for queen checks on a4 and b3. [%csl Rd5,Rb7,Re7][%cal Gc8b7] }
5... Bb7
{ Tartakower and Makogonov's fianchetto setup with an early e3 from White: Black stations the c8 bishop on b7 to watch e4 instead of locking it behind c6. Trading [cxd5 exd5] before developing [w:Bd3] or [w:Bb5+] stops Black from ever recapturing on d5 with a piece and blunts the bishop on b7 for a while, whereas [Bd3] and [Be2] keep full central tension ahead of [w:O-O]. [%cal Gc4d5,Gf1d3] }
( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... Bb4 $4
{ The bishop pins the knight, but on b4 it has no defender: [Qa4+] checks the king and hits the bishop. After [b:Nc6] [w:Ne5], the pinned knight falls or the bishop does. [b:Bb7] or [b:Be7] was the move. [%csl Rb4,Ra5][%cal Gd1a4] }
6. Qa4+ ) 6. cxd5 ( 6. Bd3 ) ( 6. Be2 ) ) 5. Nc3
{ A classical Declined with White's bishop shut in on c1. Black castles first with [O-O]. Then come the freeing ideas: [dxc4] once White's bishop has moved to d3, then [c5], or the slower [b6] and [Bb7], or [Nbd7] and [c5]. [c6] leads to Semi-Slav positions. Black should not rush [dxc4] before White's bishop has moved: then [w:Bxc4] wins it back in one move, and Black has given up the center for nothing. [%csl Rc1][%cal Ge8g8,Gc7c6,Bb8d7] }
( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. Nbd2 ) 5... O-O
