( 5... c6 ) ( 5... Nbd7 ) 6. Bd3 ( 6. Be2 ) ( 6. b3 ) ) ( 4. g3
{ The Catalan via the Queen's Gambit Declined move order: White fianchettoes on g2 to exert long-term pressure down the h1-a8 diagonal against d5, c6 and b7. Black has four major setups: [Be7] for the Closed Catalan ([Be7 Bg2 O-O O-O]), [Bb4+] to coax [Bb4+ Bd2 Be7] before tucking the bishop back, [dxc4] for the Open Catalan where Black holds or returns the pawn actively, and [c6] building a Semi-Slav Catalan triangle. [%csl Rg2][%cal Gf8e7,Gf8b4,Bd5c4] }
4... Be7 ( 4... Bb4+ ) ( 4... dxc4 ) ( 4... c6 ) ) 4... b6 ( 4... Nc6
{ Black mixes the Chigorin knight with the Declined pawn chain, adding piece pressure on d4 at the price of blocking the c-pawn. Because Black can no longer play [b:c5] or [b:c6], White gets a pleasant edge with [Bg5] or [cxd5]. After [Bg5] [b:Be7] [w:e3] [b:O-O], White builds with [w:Rc1] and [w:Bd3], while Black relies on [b:h6], [b:Ne4] or [b:dxc4] and [b:e5]. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gc1g5] }
5. Bg5
{ White pins the knight on f6. Black's best answers are [Be7], unpinning and castling next, [dxc4], taking the pawn while White's bishop is away from f1, and [Bb4], pinning White's knight in turn, as in the Ragozin. [h6] is less exact: [w:Bxf6] and [w:cxd5] follow, and Black's pawns are split. The knight on c6 blocks the c-pawn, so Black can't free the game with [b:c5] or [b:c6]: Black's play comes from the pieces, with [b:Ne4] or [b:e5] later, while White plans [w:e3], [w:Rc1] and pressure on the c-file. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gf8e7,Gd5c4,Bf8b4] }
( 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 Be7 7. e3
{ In this Exchange QGD against the Knight's Defense, Black's knight on c6 blocks the c-pawn, creating a double-edged Carlsbad variation. On the downside, Black cannot blunt White's queenside with [b:c6], so d5 relies on piece protection; on the upside, Black saves a tempo and prepares [Bf5] or [Bg4] comfortably. Pushing [h6] [w:Bh4] [b:O-O] or [b:Bf5] gets Black's light-squared bishop into the game before castling, while [a6] stops [w:Bb5] from undermining the c6 defender of d5. [%csl Rc6,Rd5][%cal Gh7h6,Gc8g4] }
7... h6 ( 7... Bg4 ) ( 7... a6 ) ) ( 5. a3 ) 5... Be7
{ In the Knight's Defense (or Guimard-Chigorin hybrid), Black breaks the pin on f6 cleanly, having placed the queen's knight in front of the c-pawn. Because c7 is blocked, Black cannot easily play [b:c5], so Black's counterplay relies on [b:O-O], [b:Ne4] or [b:dxc4] followed by [b:Nd5] or [b:e5]. White keeps a comfortable space advantage with [e3], preparing [w:Rc1] and [w:Bd3], or fixes the immobile c7 pawn immediately with [cxd5] [b:exd5], leaving Black zero queenside pawn breaks. [%csl Rc6,Rc7][%cal Ge2e3,Gc4d5] }
