{ The Prague Variation of the Tarrasch Defense, Black's mainstream response to Rubinstein's fianchetto system ([g3]). Rather than pushing [b:c4] early (the Swedish Variation), Black develops the king's knight naturally to f6, fortifying the isolated d5 pawn and preparing [b:Be7] and [b:O-O]. White completes the fianchetto with [Bg2], bringing maximum pressure to bear along the h1-a8 diagonal against d5 and getting ready to castle short before asking Black's center the big question with [w:Bg5] or [w:dxc5]. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gf1g2,Bf8e7] }
