{ Both sides have brought out their knights in the Orthodox Defense while Black has delayed castling by a move. Castling [O-O] transposes directly into the main Orthodox tabiya, where White fights for a tempo with [w:Rc1] or [w:Qc2]. Inserting [h6] [w:Bh4] first gives Black useful kingside luft and tests whether White truly wants the pin, while [c6] fortifies d5 immediately and [b6] prepares to fianchetto the problem bishop on b7 in Tartakower fashion. [%cal Ge8g8,Gc7c6,Gh7h6] }
6... O-O ( 6... c6 ) ( 6... h6 ) ( 6... b6 ) ) 6. Nf3
{ The main position of the Orthodox. Black's most common move is [h6], asking the bishop to choose between h4 and f6 before Black decides on a plan. [Nbd7], the classical move, prepares [c6] and the freeing ideas with [dxc4] and [e5]. In both cases Black's strategic problem is the bishop on c8, and White's plans are pressure on the c-file, a minority attack after [cxd5], or a central push with [e4]. [%csl Rh4,Rf6,Rc8][%cal Gh7h6] }
( 6. Rc1 ) ( 6. Qc2 ) 6... h6
{ Black asks the bishop to choose. [Bh4] keeps the pin and leads to the Lasker with [b:Ne4] or the Tartakower with [b:b6]. [Bxf6] gives up the bishop pair to gain time and to remove a defender of d5 and e4, the Anti-Tartakower. The small move [h6] matters: Black's king gets an escape square for later, and once the bishop has gone to h4, [b:g5] is always there to break the pin, or [b:Ne4] to trade it. [%csl Rd5,Re4][%cal Gg5h4] }
( 6... Nbd7
{ The classical Orthodox Defense. Black supports the freeing breaks [b:c5] and [b:e5] and waits to take on c4 until White's bishop has left f1, so that [b:dxc4] wins a tempo. White's main moves are [Rc1], taking the c-file before the fight starts, and [Qc2], the Rubinstein Attack, covering e4. After [w:Bd3], Black takes on c4 at once and gains a move; [cxd5] fixes the Carlsbad structure. [%csl Rc4,Rf1,Re4][%cal Ga1c1] }
7. Rc1
{ The main line of the Orthodox. [c6] is Black's main move, preparing Capablanca's freeing maneuver: after [Bd3 dxc4 Bxc4], [Nd5] trades a pair of minor pieces, and [e5] follows. [a6], the Henneberger, prepares [dxc4] and [b5]. [b6] aims for [Bb7], but after [cxd5 exd5], the c-file and c6 become weak, and Black must take back on d5 with the pawn. [%csl Rc6,Rd5][%cal Gc7c6] }
( 7. Qc2
{ The Rubinstein Variation of the Orthodox Defense. White develops the queen to c2 to fight for e4, prepare [w:Rd1] or [w:O-O-O], and stay in the tempo battle so [w:Bd3] only moves once after [b:dxc4]. Black's standard reply is [c6], cementing d5 before taking on c4 or striking with [b:e5]/[b:c5]; [h6] forces the g5 bishop to choose between [w:Bh4], [w:Bf4] and [w:Bxf6]; and [b6] fianchettoes the light-squared bishop on b7. [%csl Be4,Rd5][%cal Gc7c6,Gh7h6] }
