{ Having placed the rook on c1, White finally develops [Bd3], ending the famous Orthodox tempo struggle. Black now captures [dxc4] [w:Bxc4] to execute Capablanca's maneuver [b:Nd5], trading dark-squared bishops and preparing [b:Nxc3] followed by [b:e5] or [b:c5]. Black can also insert [h6] [w:Bh4] first before playing [b:dxc4], keeping the option of [b:b5] and [b:a6], or play [Re8] to guard e7 and hint at [b:dxc4] [w:Bxc4] [b:b5] or [b:Nf8]. [%csl Bd5][%cal Gd5c4,Gh7h6,Gf8e8] }
( 8. Qc2 ) ( 8. a3 ) 8... dxc4 ( 8... h6 9. Bh4
{ By inserting [h6] [Bh4] before releasing the center, Black secures kingside luft and pushes White's bishop off the c1-h6 diagonal. Now [dxc4] [w:Bxc4] is prime, letting Black choose between Capablanca's simplifying [b:Nd5] and the ambitious [b:b5] [w:Bd3] [b:a6] [w:e4] [b:c5], striking at White's center in Anti-Meran style. [Re8] [w:O-O] [b:dxc4] [w:Bxc4] [b:Nd5] is an equally classic order that guards e7 so [w:Bxe7] can be met by [b:Rxe7] with [b:e5] to follow, while [b6] heads for Tartakower play. [%cal Gd5c4,Gf8e8,Gb7b6] }
9... dxc4 ( 9... Re8 ) ( 9... b6 ) ) ( 8... Re8 ) 9. Bxc4
{ Capablanca's freeing maneuver. [Nd5] offers to trade the bishop on e7 for White's bishop on g5, the piece that cramps Black most. After [w:Bxe7] [b:Qxe7] [w:O-O] [b:Nxc3] [w:Rxc3], Black plays [e5], the central break that finally frees the bishop on c8. White keeps a small edge in space but no more. The idea is worth remembering: in cramped positions, every trade helps the side with less room. [%csl Re7,Rg5,Rc8][%cal Gf6d5] }
9... Nd5 ) 7. Bh4
{ The bishop keeps the pin. Black now chooses a plan to free the position. [b6], the Tartakower, puts the bishop on b7, where it watches e4 and d5, and prepares [c5]. [Ne4], the Lasker, trades two pairs of minor pieces to relieve the cramp. [Nbd7] heads back to the Orthodox, and [dxc4] with [c5] leads to an isolated pawn for White. [%csl Rb7,Re4,Rd5][%cal Gb7b6] }
( 7. Bxf6 ) 7... b6
{ The Tartakower Defense, combining the solid Declined center with a Queen's Indian fianchetto. The bishop goes to b7, where it fights for e4, and Black aims for [b:c5], often accepting hanging pawns on c5 and d5: two pawns side by side that control central squares and can advance, though they need piece support on open files. White's plans are [cxd5], fixing the structure, or [Be2] and [Bd3] with [w:Rc1]. Black's bishop on b7 is finally active. [%csl Rb7,Re4,Rc5][%cal Gf1e2] }
( 7... Ne4
{ The Lasker Defense, a simplifying idea when Black lacks space. The knight forces trades: after [Bxe7 Qxe7], the queen covers b4 and e5, and Black follows with [b:Nxc3] or [b:c6], reaching a slightly passive but very solid position. Every trade eases Black's cramp. White's pieces stay a little more active, and the fight revolves around Black's [b:c5] or [b:e5] break to free the bishop on c8. [%csl Rb4,Re5,Rc8][%cal Gh4e7] }
8. Bxe7 Qxe7
{ The standard Lasker tabiya: Black has traded off one pair of minor pieces to ease the cramp and placed the queen where it eyes b4 and e5. White keeps a nagging pull with [Rc1] or [Qc2], waiting for Black to trade on c3, or [cxd5] [b:Nxc3] [w:bxc3] [b:exd5]. Club players often grab [w:Nxe4], which is harmless: [b:dxe4] kicks White's other knight to d2 and gives Black easy [b:f5] or [b:e5] play. [%csl Yb4,Ye5,Yc3][%cal Ga1c1] }
9. Rc1 ( 9. cxd5
{ White takes on d5 before Black trades on c3. The right order is [Nxc3], an in-between move: after [w:bxc3] [b:exd5], Black keeps a solid pawn center and White's queenside pawns are split, though [w:Qb3] and [w:c4] will test d5 and b7. [exd5]?, recapturing at once, is a mistake: [w:Nxd5] wins a pawn with tempo on the queen, since e7 no longer guards d5. [%csl Rd5,Rc3,Re7][%cal Ge4c3] }
9... Nxc3 ( 9... exd5 $2
{ Taking back at once looks automatic, but the queen on e7 no longer guards d5: [Nxd5] takes the pawn, and Black's knight on e4 can't win it back. The in-between [b:Nxc3] first, then [b:exd5], kept the material level. [%csl Re7,Rd5,Re4][%cal Gc3d5] }
10. Nxd5 ) ) ( 9. Qc2 ) ) 8. Be2 ( 8. cxd5
{ White fixes the center before Black's bishop reaches b7. Recapturing with the knight, [Nxd5], is the Tartakower main line: after [w:Bxe7] [b:Qxe7] [w:Nxd5] [b:exd5], two pairs of minor pieces leave the board and Black follows with [b:Be6] and [b:c5], accepting hanging pawns on c5 and d5 with free piece play. [exd5] keeps more pieces on the board at the risk of blocking the bishop headed for b7. [%csl Yb7,Yc5,Yd5][%cal Gf6d5] }
8... Nxd5 ( 8... exd5 ) ) ( 8. Bd3 ) *

[Event "Queen's Gambit Declined (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6
{ The main position of the Declined. White's classical plan is [Bg5], pinning the knight and adding pressure on d5, then [e3], [Nf3], [Rc1] or [Qc2] and [Bd3]. The Exchange Variation, [cxd5 exd5], fixes the pawn structure and leads to the minority attack. [Nf3] keeps options open, and [Bf4] puts the bishop where it can't be hit by [h6]. Black's choice of set-up decides the character of the game: the Orthodox, the Lasker, the Tartakower, the Cambridge Springs, the Ragozin or the Semi-Tarrasch. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gc1g5] }
( 3... c5
{ The Tarrasch Defense, championed by Siegbert Tarrasch. Black hits d4 at once and accepts an isolated pawn on d5 after [cxd5 exd5]. In return the bishop on c8 is free, the knight gets c6, and Black's pieces enjoy open lines. Black plays for piece activity and avoids trades; White aims to blockade d5, swap pieces and target the pawn. [cxd5] is critical, [Nf3] and [e3] calmer. [Bf4]?? blunders d4 to [b:cxd4]. [%csl Rd4,Rd5,Rc8][%cal Gc4d5] }
4. cxd5
{ Black must decide how to take back. [exd5] is the Tarrasch proper: Black accepts the isolated pawn and gets free development. [cxd4], the Schara Gambit, gives a pawn for a lead in development: after [w:Qxd4] [b:Nc6] [w:Qd1] [b:exd5] [w:Qxd5] [b:Bd7], Black's pieces fly out while White's queen is chased around. It is sharp, and White must know a few accurate moves. [%cal Ge6d5] }
( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. e3
{ White meets the Tarrasch with a solid [e3], refusing to give Black the isolated-pawn game Black wanted. Black develops with [Nf6] and [Nc6], and the game usually becomes a symmetrical Semi-Tarrasch after [w:Nf3]. [cxd4] [w:exd4] gives White the isolated pawn instead, and Black blockades on d5 with a knight. White's bishop on c1 is shut in, so Black has no problems and should play actively: [Nc6], [Bd6] or [Be7], castling, and pressure on d4. [%csl Rd5,Rc1][%cal Gg8f6,Gb8c6] }
4... Nf6
{ Black develops the knight. [Nf3] is the usual move and leads to the quiet Semi-Tarrasch with [e3], where both sides keep the tension. [cxd5] [b:exd5] gives Black an isolated pawn after the trade on c5 or d4, and [dxc5] [b:Bxc5] leads to a symmetrical, level game. Black should answer White's bishop moves by timing [dxc4]: taking once the bishop has left f1 gains a tempo. [%csl Rc5][%cal Gg1f3,Gc4d5,Bd4c5] }
( 4... Nc6 ) 5. Nf3 ( 5. cxd5 ) ( 5. dxc5 ) ) ( 4. Bf4 $4
{ The bishop develops, but d4 has lost a defender. [cxd4] hits the knight on c3, and the trick [w:Nb5] doesn't help: after [b:e5] or [b:a6], the pawn on d4 stays Black's. [w:cxd5], [w:Nf3] or [w:e3] kept the center. [%csl Rd4,Rc3][%cal Gc5d4] }
4... cxd4 ) 4... exd5 ( 4... cxd4
{ The Schara Gambit: Black gives up a pawn for quick development. White should take back with the queen, [Qxd4], and after [b:Nc6] [w:Qd1] [b:exd5] [w:Qxd5] [b:Bd7], Black's lead in development gives full play. [Qa4+] first is a good alternative. [dxe6]?, grabbing more, is a mistake: [b:Bxe6] and the pawn on d4 hits the knight on c3, and Black is ahead in development with the material level. [%csl Rd4,Rc3][%cal Gd1d4] }
5. Qxd4
{ White recaptures on d4 with the queen and threatens to keep the extra pawn on d5. Black must develop with tempo via [Nc6]: after [Qd1 exd5 Qxd5 Be6] or [b:Bd7], Black's rapid mobilization and open files compensate for the pawn. Taking [exd5]? before [Nc6], chosen in nearly a quarter of club games, lets [w:Qxd5] hold the extra pawn with no loss of time. [%cal Gb8c6] }
( 5. Qa4+ ) ( 5. dxe6 $2
{ Grabbing a second pawn opens lines for Black: [Bxe6] develops, the pawn on d4 hits the knight on c3, and Black is ahead in development with the material level. [w:Qxd4] was the move. [%csl Rd4,Rc3][%cal Gc8e6] }
5... Bxe6 ) 5... Nc6 ( 5... exd5 $2
{ Taking back the pawn first is too slow: [Qxd5] keeps the extra pawn, since Black gains no time on the queen. [b:Nc6], hitting the queen, then [b:exd5], was the gambit's order. [%cal Gd4d5] }
6. Qxd5 ) ( 5... Nf6 $6
{ Developing [Nf6] without hitting the queen first lets White seize the center with [e4]: after [e4 exd5 exd5] (or [w:Bb5+]), White keeps a powerful central d-pawn and active pieces with no compensation for Black. Play [b:Nc6] with tempo on the queen first. [%cal Re2e4] }
6. e4 exd5 7. exd5 ) 6. Qd1
{ White's queen retreats home to safety while eyeing d8. Black now recovers the pawn on c5's file with [exd5], and after [w:Qxd5] [b:Be6] [w:Qxd8+] [b:Rxd8], Black's active rook and minor pieces press against White's queenside in the endgame. Jumping early with [Nb4]?? to threaten c2 fails completely: [w:e4] defends d5, [w:a3] kicks the knight, and White stays a clean pawn ahead. [%csl Rc2,Rd5][%cal Ge6d5] }
6... exd5 ( 6... Nb4 $4
{ The knight jumps toward c2, but White just keeps the extra pawn: [e4] supports d5, and [w:a3] kicks the knight next. [b:exd5] first won the pawn back. [%csl Rc2,Rd5][%cal Ge2e4] }
7. e4 ) 7. Qxd5 Be6
{ Black offers the queen trade to win back time. White's best is [Qxd8+], and the endgame after [b:Rxd8] [w:e3] is slightly better for White, who holds an extra pawn while watching the knight jumps to b4 and d4 toward c2. Keeping the queens on with [w:Qb5] gives Black full compensation after [b:Nf6] and [b:a6], so trading queens is the clean path. [%csl Rd4,Rb4][%cal Gd5d8] }
8. Qxd8+ Rxd8
{ The queens are off, and White is a pawn up. Yet Black's knight on c6 threatens to jump to b4 or d4, from where [b:Nc2+] forks the king and the rook on a1. [e3] is the one move that stops [b:Nd4] and clears e2 for the king; White then develops with [w:Bb5] or [w:Bd2]. Natural moves like [Nf3]?? and [a3]?? walk straight into [b:Nb4] and [b:Nd4], losing the rook on a1. [%csl Rc6,Rb4,Rd4][%cal Ge2e3] }
9. e3 ( 9. Nf3 $4
{ Developing the knight is the most natural move, and it blunders the rook on a1: [Nb4] threatens [b:Nc2+], forking the king and the rook, and even after [w:Bd2] [b:Nc2+] [w:Kd1] [b:Nxa1], the rook is gone. [w:e3] first takes d4 from the knight and lets the king step to e2. [%csl Ra1,Rd4,Re2][%cal Gc6b4] }
9... Nb4 ) ( 9. a3 $4
{ Taking b4 from the knight isn't enough: [Nd4] goes the other way, with the same [b:Nc2+] fork, and after [w:f4] [b:Nc2+], the rook on a1 falls. [w:e3] covers d4 and frees e2 for the king. [%csl Rb4,Ra1,Rd4][%cal Gc6d4] }
9... Nd4 ) ) ) 4. Bg5 ( 4. cxd5
{ The Exchange Variation. White settles the structure at once. Black takes back with [exd5], getting the Carlsbad structure; [Nxd5] gives White the center after [e4]. It seems odd to free Black's bishop on c8, but White's plan is to give it no good square: with [Bd3] and [Qc2], the bishop has no future on f5 or g4. White also gets a clear long-term plan, the minority attack on the queenside. [%csl Rc8,Rf5,Rg4][%cal Ge6d5] }
4... exd5 ( 4... Nxd5
{ Black takes back with the knight, avoiding the Carlsbad structure and keeping the game fluid. White should grab the center at once with [e4]: after [Nxc3 bxc3], White has a classical pawn center and Black must attack it immediately with [b:c5], [b:Bb4+] and [b:Nc6], as in the Semi-Tarrasch. [Nf3] with [w:e4] next is just as good. Black trades central space for active, weakness-free piece play. [%cal Ge2e4] }
5. e4
{ White grabs the center with gain of time. [Nxc3] is the move: after [w:bxc3], Black hits the big center at once with [b:c5], then [b:cxd4], [b:Bb4+] and [b:Nc6], as in the Semi-Tarrasch. Retreating with [Nf6]?! or [Nb6]? gives White the center and time: [w:Nf3], [w:Bd3] and [w:e5] follow, and Black's pieces are pushed back. When White builds d4 and e4, Black must attack the center with pawns at once. [%csl Rd4,Re4][%cal Gd5c3] }
( 5. Nf3 ) 5... Nxc3
{ In the Semi-Tarrasch style Exchange, Black surrenders d5 to give White a broad center that Black can immediately chip away at. Trading on c3 with [Nxc3] is essential before retreating: White's only sensible recapture is [bxc3], bolstering d4 with a thick pawn mass, after which Black strikes straight at the base with [b:c5] and follows with [b:Nc6], [b:cxd4] and [b:Bb4+] or [b:Be7]. White gets attacking chances on the kingside, while Black targets the d4-c3 hanging center. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gb2c3,Bc7c5] }
( 5... Nf6 $6
{ Retreating the knight leaves White's classical center untouched: [Nf3], [w:Bd3] and [w:O-O] follow with easy space and attacking chances. [b:Nxc3] first, preparing [b:c5] and [b:Bb4+], was the right reaction. [%csl Rf6][%cal Gg1f3] }
6. Nf3 ) ( 5... Nb6 $2
{ Stepping to the queenside gives White the whole center for free: [Nf3] and [w:Bd3] develop rapidly while the knight on b6 sits away from the fight. [b:Nxc3] first, then [b:c5], challenged the center. [%csl Rb6][%cal Gg1f3] }
6. Nf3 ) 6. bxc3
{ White has established the classical pawn duo on d4 and e4, supported by the pawn on c3. Black must counterattack the center immediately with [c5], followed by [b:cxd4], [b:Bb4+] and castling, just as in the Semi-Tarrasch. Developing with [b:Nc6]? blocks the c-pawn and runs into [w:d5], while a Grünfeld-style [g6]? leaves Black's dark squares weak against [w:Nf3], [w:Bd3] and [w:h4]. [%csl Re6][%cal Gc7c5] }
6... c5 ( 6... g6 $2
{ A Grünfeld-style fianchetto doesn't fit here: with the pawn on e6, the dark squares around Black's king are weak, and [h4] starts an attack at once. [b:c5], hitting the center, was the move. [%csl Re6][%cal Gh2h4] }
7. h4 ) ( 6... Nc6 $6
{ Developing the knight in front of the c-pawn blocks Black's essential [b:c5] counter against White's center: [Nf3] and [w:Bd3] build an unchallenged central roller. Strike first with [b:c5]. [%csl Rc6][%cal Gg1f3] }
7. Nf3 ) ) ) ( 4. Nf3
{ The Three Knights position, reached from many move orders. Black chooses the system: [Be7] for the Orthodox and Harrwitz lines, [c5] for the Semi-Tarrasch, [Bb4] for the Ragozin, [dxc4] for the Vienna, and [c6] for the Semi-Slav. With the knight on f3, White can no longer use [w:Nge2] and [f3] plans, so the Exchange Variation is less dangerous here, and [Bg5] or [Bf4] are White's main tries. [%csl Rf3][%cal Gf8e7] }
4... Be7 ( 4... c5 ) ( 4... Bb4
{ The Ragozin Defense, blending the Declined pawn center with the Nimzo-Indian pin on c3. The bishop fights for e4 right away, and Black plans [b:c5], [b:Qa5] or [b:Nbd7]. White's main answers are [Bg5], pinning in return, [cxd5], fixing the center so [w:Bg5] keeps its pin after [b:h6] [w:Bh4], and [Qa4+], the Alekhine line, unpinning with a check against b4. [%csl Rc3,Re4][%cal Gc1g5] }
5. Bg5
{ Both sides pin a knight. Black's best is [h6], forcing the bishop to decide: [Bxf6 Qxf6] gives Black the bishop pair, and [Bh4] allows [dxc4], after which the pawn is hard to win back. [dxc4] at once is the Vienna-like line. Black often gives up the bishop on c3 later to develop the bishop on c8 with [Bd7] and [Bc6]. [%csl Rc3,Rc8][%cal Gh7h6] }
( 5. cxd5
{ White exchanges on d5 first so that [w:Bg5] can maintain its pin after [b:h6] [w:Bh4] without fearing [b:dxc4]. After [exd5 Bg5 h6 Bh4], Black develops with [b:O-O], [b:Bf5] or [b:c5], using the freed light-squared bishop. White builds with [w:e3] and [w:Bd3]; Black often trades on c3 to double White's pawns and play against them, pitting structure against White's bishop pair. [%csl Rc4][%cal Ge6d5] }
5... exd5 ) ( 5. Qa4+
{ The Alekhine Variation of the Ragozin: White unpins c3 with a double attack on the king and the loose bishop on b4. Black has only one move: [Nc6], blocking the check while protecting b4, since [Bd7]?? and [c6]?? both drop the bishop on b4 for nothing. After [Nc6], White continues with [w:e3], [w:Bg5] or [w:a3], and Black castles and fights for e4 and [b:e5] or [b:dxc4]. [%csl Yc3,Yb4,Ye4][%cal Gb8c6] }
5... Nc6 ( 5... Bd7 $4
{ Blocking the check with the bishop leaves b4 unguarded: [Qxb4] takes the dark bishop for free. Only [b:Nc6], defending b4 while blocking the check, held everything. [%csl Yb4][%cal Ga4b4] }
6. Qxb4 ) ( 5... c6 $4
{ Interposing the pawn forgets why White checked on a4: the bishop on b4 has no defender, and [Qxb4] wins a full piece. [b:Nc6] was forced. [%csl Ya4,Yb4][%cal Ga4b4] }
6. Qxb4 ) ) 5... h6 ( 5... dxc4 ) ( 5... Nbd7 ) ) ( 4... dxc4
{ The Vienna Variation: Black gives up the center to hit d4 later with [b:c5] and to pin with [b:Bb4]. [e4] is White's sharpest test, met by [b:Bb4] [w:Bg5] [b:c5]. [w:Bg5] at once pins f6 before committing the e-pawn, [e3] recovers c4 quietly, and [Qa4+] wins it back with check. Against [e4], slow moves like [b:c6]? or [b:Nc6]? let [w:Bxc4] and [w:e5] overrun the center. [%csl Rd4,Rf6][%cal Ge2e4] }
5. e4
{ White builds the center and threatens [w:Bxc4]. Black's best is [Bb4], pinning the knight and hitting e4: after [Bb4] [w:Bg5] [b:c5] or [Bb4] [w:Bxc4] [b:Nxe4], Black gets active counterplay. Speed is the whole point of the Vienna: Black hits the center with pieces and [b:c5] before White castles and pushes [w:e5]. [b5] is a sharper try; [Nc6]? and [c6]? are too slow against [w:e5] and [w:Bxc4]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf8b4] }
( 5. e3
{ The Quiet Vienna: White declines the razor-sharp [w:e4] lines and recovers c4 calmly, aiming for a classical Queen's Gambit Accepted with an extra knight out. Black equalizes with [c5], challenging d4 at once, or [a6] to prepare [b:b5] and [b:c5]; [Be7] is solid too. Two club mistakes stand out: [b5]? without [a6] drops the pawn to [w:Nxb5], and [Nc6]?! blocks the c-pawn's freeing break. [%csl Yc4,Yd4][%cal Ga7a6] }
5... a6 ( 5... c5 ) ( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... b5 $2
{ Holding c4 with the b-pawn forgets that White's knight is already on c3 and unpinned: [Nxb5] wins a clean pawn, and [b:Bb4+] [w:Bd2] rescues nothing. [b:a6] first or [b:c5] was required. [%csl Yc4,Yc3][%cal Gc3b5] }
6. Nxb5 ) ( 5... Nc6 $6
{ Putting the knight in front of the c-pawn blocks Black's essential [b:c5] break: [Bxc4] recovers c4 and prepares [w:O-O] and [w:e4] while Black's bishop on c8 stays cramped. Strike with [b:c5] or play [b:a6] instead. [%csl Yc4,Yc8][%cal Gf1c4] }
6. Bxc4 ) ) ( 5. Qa4+ ) ( 5. Bg5
{ White pins the knight on f6 before recovering c4, hoping to push [w:e4] without allowing an easy counterstrike. Black has three good ways to fight back: [a6], preparing [b:b5] to hold c4 in Botvinnik style; [Bb4], pinning c3 and transposing to the main Vienna after [w:e4] [b:c5]; and [h6], forcing [w:Bxf6] [b:Qxf6] so Black gets the bishop pair before White builds the center. [%csl Yf6,Yc4,Yc3][%cal Ga7a6] }
5... a6 ( 5... Bb4 ) ( 5... h6 ) ( 5... Be7
{ Rather than fighting to hold c4 with [b:a6] or pinning via [b:Bb4], Black calmly unpins f6 and gets ready to castle. Because f6 is no longer free to strike at the center, [e4] is White's most incisive reply, claiming a broad central duo and answering [e4 c5] with [w:Bxc4] or [w:dxc5]. Quiet [e3] recovers the pawn safely and transposes back into Queen's Gambit Accepted lines with an extra bishop move. [%cal Ge2e4,Ge2e3] }
6. e4 ( 6. e3 ) ) ) 5... Bb4 ( 5... b5 ) ( 5... Nc6 $2
{ The knight develops, but it doesn't hit White's center: [Bxc4] wins the pawn back, and White's pawns on d4 and e4 dominate. [b:Bb4], pinning and hitting e4, was the Vienna's point. [%csl Rd4,Re4][%cal Gf1c4] }
6. Bxc4 ) ( 5... c6 $2
{ Setting up pawns on both c6 and e6 after [b:dxc4] and [w:e4] is too slow and hands c4 back for nothing: [e5] drives the knight to d5, and [w:Bxc4] recovers the pawn with a commanding center. Black needed active play first: [b:Bb4] with e6 already played, or [b:b5] to hold c4 in the Geller Gambit. [%cal Gf1c4] }
6. e5 ) ) ( 4... Nbd7
{ The Barmen Variation: Black backs up f6 and c5 with the queen's knight while keeping f8 flexible for e7 or b4. White has three strong plans: [Bf4], claiming the h2-b8 diagonal where [b:Bd6] or [b:dxc4] must be timed carefully; [cxd5] [b:exd5] [w:Bg5], entering a favorable Exchange structure; and [Bg5], inviting [b:Be7] for the Orthodox or [b:c6] and [b:Qa5] for the Cambridge Springs. [%csl Yf6,Yc5,Yf8][%cal Gc1f4] }
5. Bf4 ( 5. cxd5 ) ( 5. Bg5 ) ) ) ( 4. e3
{ White shuts in the bishop on c1 but gets ready for [w:Bd3] and [w:Nf3]: a calm system, very popular at club level. Black has four good set-ups: [Be7] and castling, the classical way; [c5], hitting the center at once; [c6], heading for the Semi-Slav; and [Bb4], pinning the knight as in the Nimzo-Indian. With White's bishop locked behind e3, Black's freeing ideas come easily: take on c4 only once White's bishop has left f1, so that [b:dxc4] wins a tempo, then [b:c5] or [b:e5]. [%csl Rc1][%cal Gf8e7,Gc7c5,Bf8b4] }
4... Be7
{ Black breaks any pin in advance and gets ready to castle. White develops with [Nf3] and [Bd3]; [cxd5] [b:exd5] gives a Carlsbad structure in which White's bishop on c1 is shut in, so it is less dangerous than the usual Exchange. After [Nf3] [b:O-O] [w:Bd3], Black takes on c4 to gain a tempo, [b:dxc4] [w:Bxc4], and plays [b:c5], reaching the classical Queen's Gambit Accepted structures with an extra move for nobody. Black's other plan is [b:b6] and [b:Bb7]. [%csl Rc1][%cal Gg1f3,Gf1d3,Bc4d5] }
( 4... c5 ) ( 4... Bb4
{ With White's dark-squared bishop already hemmed in behind e3, Black's pin on c3 reaches a classical Nimzo-Ragozin tabiya. Questioning the bishop at once with [a3 Bxc3+ bxc3] hands White the bishop pair and a rock-solid central mass ready for [w:Bd3] and [w:Ne2], while [Nf3] and [Bd3] develop smoothly toward kingside castling. Checking with [Qa4+ Nc6 Bd2] is another clean way to break the pin. [%csl Re3][%cal Ga2a3,Gg1f3,Bf1d3] }
5. a3 ( 5. Nf3 ) ( 5. Bd3 ) ) ( 4... c6 ) 5. Nf3 ( 5. Bd3 ) ( 5. cxd5 exd5 6.
Bd3 O-O 7. Nf3 c6
{ The Exchange QGD with White's dark-squared bishop locked inside the pawn chain on c1. Because White gave up the classic g5 pin, Black's light-squared bishop is completely free to step out to g4 or f5—the dream setup for QGD players! White's sharpest move is [Qc2], controlling f5 so [b:Bf5]? drops a pawn and preparing [w:O-O] followed by the minority attack ([w:Rab1], [w:b4]). [h3] stops [b:Bg4], while [Ne5] grabs the central outpost immediately to support [w:f4]. [%csl Rf5,Rc8][%cal Gd1c2,Gf3e5] }
8. Qc2 ( 8. h3 ) ( 8. Ne5 ) ) ) ( 4. Bf4
{ The bishop goes to f4 before the knight comes to f3, so it can't be chased by [h6]. Black has several good answers. [c5] strikes at once: after [w:e3] [b:Nc6] [w:Nf3], the game is a Harrwitz with an early [c5]. [Be7] and [O-O] keep it classical, and [Bd6] offers to trade the strong bishop, though after [w:Bxd6] [b:Qxd6] White has a small space edge. [Bb4] pins the knight and is also fine. Black should not let the bishop on f4 and a knight on b5 gang up on c7: watch the c7 square. [%csl Yf4,Yf3,Yb5][%cal Gf8e7] }
