[Event "Slav and Semi-Slav"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 d5 ( 1... c6
{ The Caro-Slav move order, played in over thirty-six million online games as a universal bridge for Caro-Kann and Slav players alike. By playing [c6] on move one, Black prepares [b:d5] supported by a pawn while keeping the c8 bishop unobstructed. White can steer straight into the Caro-Kann with [e4], enter the Slav with [c4] or [Nf3], or set up the London System with [Bf4]. [%csl Rc8][%cal Gc2c4,Gg1f3,Bc1f4] }
2. c4
{ With [c4], White claims queenside space and invites the Slav Defence. [d5] is Black's overwhelming main choice, contesting the center at once while keeping the c8-h3 diagonal open for the light-squared bishop. [Nf6] is a flexible Anglo-Slav finesse that keeps [d5] and [d6] (heading toward Old Indian or King's Indian structures after [Nc3]) on the table, whereas passive [d6] at once concedes central space. [%csl Rc8,Rh3][%cal Gd7d5,Gg8f6] }
( 2. Nf3
{ Developing [Nf3] prevents an immediate [e5] and keeps White's options open between the Slav ([c4]), the London ([Bf4]), and quiet Colle or Torre setups ([e3] or [Bg5]). Black almost always answers [d5] to stake out the center and reach standard Slav or London tabiyas; [Nf6] is equally sound, waiting to see whether White commits to [c4] or [Bf4] before placing the d-pawn in the center. [%cal Gd7d5,Gg8f6] }
2... d5 ( 2... Nf6 ) ) ( 2. Bf4 ) ( 2. e4 ) ( 2. e3
{ A quiet Colle-style reply to 1...c6 that shores up d4 and opens the f1-a6 diagonal while keeping the c-pawn ready for either [c4] (transposing into a Slow Slav after [d5]) or [c3] in a reversed Caro-Kann Stonewall. Because White's dark-squared bishop is now shut inside e3, Black equalizes effortlessly with [d5]—freeing the c8 bishop to f5 or g4 without any trouble—or [Nf6]. [%csl Rd4,Rf1][%cal Gd7d5,Gg8f6] }
2... d5 ( 2... Nf6 ) ) ( 2. Nc3
{ Bringing the queen's knight to c3 ahead of the c-pawn prepares an immediate [e4] push into the Caro-Kann Classical or Fantasy, or a Veresov/Jobava setup if Black answers in Slav style. Black should seize the center right away with [d5], preventing uncontested [e4]—after which [d5 e4] transposes directly into the Classical Caro-Kann, while [d5 Bf4] enters the Jobava London against a solid [c6]–[d5] triangle. [%csl Rc3][%cal Gd7d5] }
2... d5 ) 2... d5 ( 2... Nf6 ) ( 2... d6
{ Holding the d-pawn at d6 turns the Anglo-Slav into an Old Indian or Czech Pirc shell where Black plans [Nd7], [Qc7] and [e5]. Because Black has left e4 completely uncontested, [e4] claims an ideal three-pawn Maróczy/King's Indian center at once; [Nc3] and [Nf3] are equally strong, developing smoothly before expanding with [e4] while Black's dark-squared bishop remains cramped behind d6. [%csl Rd6][%cal Gb1c3,Ge2e4,Bg1f3] }
3. Nc3
{ White develops the queenside knight to bear down on e4 and d5 against Black's Caro-Indian setup. Black can push [d5] anyway—conceding a clear tempo to reach a Slav after spending two moves with the d-pawn—or stay in an Old Indian / Czech Pirc structure via [Nf6] and [g6], letting White claim a spacious center with [w:e4] while Black prepares [b:Nbd7] and [b:e5]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gg8f6,Gg7g6,Bd6d5] }
( 3. e4 ) ( 3. Nf3 ) 3... Nf6 ( 3... g6 ) ( 3... d5 ) ) ) 2. c4 c6
{ The Slav Defense. Black supports d5 with the c-pawn, so the bishop on c8 is not shut in, as it is after [e6]. The downside is that the knight on b8 loses its best square, and that Black has done nothing yet for the kingside. White's main moves are [Nf3] and [Nc3]; [cxd5] gives the symmetrical Exchange Slav. Black's plan in many lines is [dxc4] and then the bishop to f5 or g4 before [e6]: the order matters, as we'll see. [%csl Rd5,Rc8,Rb8][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 2... e6 3. Nc3 c6
{ Black plays the Semi-Slav move order with [e6] first, to avoid the Exchange Slav. White's sharpest answer is [e4], the Marshall Gambit; [Nf3] allows the Noteboom with [dxc4]; [e3] keeps things quiet. After [Nf3 Nf6], we are back in the Semi-Slav. The triangle on c6, d5 and e6 is solid, but the bishop on c8 stays shut in until Black takes on c4 or plays [e5]. [%csl Rc6,Rd5,Re6][%cal Gg1f3] }
4. Nf3
{ White develops and keeps [w:e4] in reserve. Black's best moves are [Nf6], the Semi-Slav, and [dxc4], the Noteboom, keeping the pawn with [b:b5] and [b:a5]. [Bd6]?! puts the bishop on its active square before the knight is out, giving White a target: [w:e4] gains space against d5, and the push [w:e5] comes with tempo on the bishop. [%cal Gg8f6] }
( 4. e4
{ The Marshall Gambit. White offers the d-pawn for a quick attack on the dark squares. Black's best is [dxe4], and after [w:Nxe4] [b:Bb4+] [w:Bd2] [b:Qxd4] [w:Bxb4] [b:Qxe4+] [w:Be2], Black has a pawn while White's dark-squared bishop stops short castling. [Bb4], pinning first, is playable too. [Ne7]? is too passive, letting [w:Nf3] and [w:Bd3] build a big center; [dxc4]? cedes the middle to [w:Bxc4]. [%cal Gd5e4] }
4... dxe4 ( 4... Bb4 ) ( 4... Ne7 $2
{ A passive knight move: [Nf3] and White keeps a big center, with [w:Bd3] and [w:h4] ideas. [b:dxe4], accepting the Marshall Gambit, or [b:Bb4], pinning first, was the move. [%cal Gg1f3] }
5. Nf3 ) ( 4... f5 $2
{ Setting up a Stonewall barrier against the Marshall Gambit backfires because it leaves e6 backward, weakens the light squares and entombs the bishop on c8. [e5 dxc4 Bxc4] plants a powerful space wedge on e5, clamps down on d6 and gives White effortless piece play against e6. Accept the gambit with [b:dxe4] or pin with [b:Bb4] instead. [%csl Re6,Bd6][%cal Ge4e5] }
5. e5 dxc4 6. Bxc4 ) 5. f3
{ White offers a second pawn in Gambits style to open the f-file for rapid development. Black's best response is [Bb4] or [Nf6], keeping the pawn on e4 and getting ready to castle, since that pawn hampers White's knight and is hard to win back. [exf3]?! helps White bring the knight to f3 with an open f-file, and [e3]? hands the pawn back so [w:Bxe3] develops with tempo. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf8b4] }
( 5. Nxe4
{ White's knight recaptures on e4, eyeing d6 and f6 while offering d4. Black's critical test is [Bb4+]: White either sacrifices the pawn with [w:Bd2] [b:Qxd4] [w:Bxb4] [b:Qxe4+] [w:Be2] for fierce dark-square pressure, or retreats [w:Nc3] for a positional bind after [b:c5]. [Nf6] is the solid alternative: after [w:Nxf6+] [b:Qxf6] [w:Nf3], Black avoids fireworks at the cost of White's space edge. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf8b4] }
5... Bb4+ ( 5... Nf6 ) 6. Bd2 Qxd4 7. Bxb4 Qxe4+ 8. Be2
{ The main line of the Marshall Gambit. White is a pawn down but has the bishop pair and attacking chances on the dark squares, especially d6 and e7. Black's best moves are [Na6], bringing the knight into play with tempo ideas on the bishop, and [c5], hitting it at once. [Ne7] is less exact: the bishop on b4 eyes e7 and Black's king stays in the center. [%csl Rd6,Re7,Rb4][%cal Gb8a6] }
8... Na6 ( 8... c5
{ Black challenges the powerful bishop on b4 at once to fight for castling rights. White's best move is [Bxc5], keeping the bishop on the f8-a3 diagonal so Black's king still cannot castle short. Retreating to [Ba5]? sidelines the bishop and lets [b:Nc6] and [b:Nge7] develop with tempo, while [w:Bc3] drops g2 without keeping Black's king trapped. [%csl Rb4][%cal Gb4c5] }
9. Bxc5
{ White's bishop commands d6 and f8, cutting Black's king off from the kingside. Black's critical move is [Qxg2], grabbing a second pawn to hit the rook on h1: White answers [w:Bf3] or [w:Qd6] with fierce open-file pressure. [Nd7] develops more cautiously, aiming to trade the bishop. [Na6]? fails to [w:Ba3], keeping the clamp on f8 while Black's knight sits offside. [%csl Rg2][%cal Ge4g2] }
( 9. Ba5 $2
{ The bishop steps out of play: [Nc6] and [b:Nge7] develop with tempo, and Black keeps the extra pawn. [w:Bxc5] kept the bishop on the dark squares, where it stops Black from castling. [%cal Gb8c6] }
9... Nc6 ) 9... Qxg2 ( 9... Nd7 ) ( 9... Na6 $2
{ The knight hits the bishop, but after [Ba3], the bishop still stops Black from castling short, and White develops fast. [b:Qxg2] or [b:Nd7] was the move. [%cal Gc5a3] }
10. Ba3 ) ) ( 8... Ne7 $6
