{ The Noteboom Variation, introduced by Dutch master Daniël Noteboom: Black takes on c4 and plans [b:b5] and [b:a5] to create connected queenside passed pawns, ceding the center in return. White's main moves are [a4] and [e3]. After [a4 Bb4 e3 b5 Bd2 a5], Black's pawns on a5, b5 and c4 roll fast unless White breaks them accurately with [w:axb5] first and only then [w:b3]. [%csl Rc4][%cal Ga2a4] }
5. a4 ( 5. e3
{ White prepares [w:Bxc4] to recover the gambit pawn in one move. Black's consistent Noteboom answer is [b5], holding c4: after [w:a4] [b:b4], the knight on c3 is driven to e4 or a2 while Black's queenside pawns advance. Developing first with [Bb4]?! hands the pawn back to [w:Bxc4] for nothing, leaving White with an unchallenged center and easy play. [%cal Gb7b5] }
