{ Against White's quiet [e3] Slav, Black adopts the Schlechter setup with [g6], fianchettoing the dark-squared bishop rather than playing [b:e6]. With the c1 bishop shut inside White's chain, Black need not fear a quick kingside h-pawn storm. Completing the fianchetto with [Bg7] is overwhelmingly natural, preparing [b:O-O] and later [b:Bg4], [b:Nbd7] and [b:e5] or [b:c5]. Bringing the light-squared bishop out first via [Bg4] is also playable to trade off White's active defender of d4. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gf8g7,Bc8g4] }
