{ The Classical Meran Variation, one of the richest battlegrounds in chess. Black secures b5 with [a6] so that the freeing break [b:c5] can follow at once, bringing the c8 bishop to b7 with tremendous energy down the long diagonal. White's main strike is [e4], seizing the center and meeting [b:c5] with the famous thrust [w:e5] [b:cxd4] [w:Nxb5], or playing [w:d5] (Reynolds' variation). [a4] challenges Black's queenside pawn chain immediately ([b:b4] [w:Ne4]), while [a3] prepares [w:O-O] or [w:b4]. [%csl Rb5][%cal Ge3e4,Ga2a4,Bc6c5] }
( 8... Bb7
{ The Wade (or Larsen/Modern) Variation of the Meran. Black develops [Bb7] straight onto the long diagonal before spending a tempo on [b:a6], keeping [b:b4] followed by [b:c5] in reserve! Because b5 isn't fortified by a6 yet, [b:c5] right away is impossible, which alters White's timing: castling [O-O] is the modern main line, waiting for [b:a6] before pushing [w:e4]. [e4] immediately runs into the annoying [b:b4] [w:Na4] [b:c5], while [a3] stops [b:b4] and [Ne4] forces [b:Nxe4] [w:Bxe4]. [%cal Ge1g1,Ge3e4,Bb5b4,Bc6c5] }
