{ Black breaks the pin cleanly, sidestepping the razor-sharp Botvinnik and Moscow lines. The resulting position is an Orthodox Declined with the c-pawn on c6: rock-solid, though the bishop on c8 stays shut in behind the triangle. White plays [e3] or [Qc2], and after [b:O-O] or [b:Nbd7], builds with [w:Rd1] and [w:Bd3]. Black's freeing plan is [b:dxc4] once White's bishop moves, followed by Capablanca's [b:Nd5] trade or [b:e5]. [%csl Rc6,Rc8][%cal Ge2e3,Gd1c2] }
6. e3
{ Black castles with [O-O], or plays [Nbd7] first, keeping [b:dxc4] and [b:Nd5] in reserve: the Capablanca idea of trading the dark-squared bishops. [h6] asks the bishop first, and [Ne4] is a Lasker-type trade. In all lines White develops with [w:Bd3] or [w:Qc2] and castles, while Black waits for White's bishop to leave f1 before taking [b:dxc4] and freeing with [b:Nd5] or [b:e5]. [%csl Rf1][%cal Ge8g8,Gb8d7,Bh7h6] }
( 6. Qc2 ) 6... O-O
{ The Anti-Moscow [Be7] Semi-Slav reaches classical Orthodox QGD waters where Black has already played [c6] before [b:Nbd7]. [Qc2] is White's most accurate continuation, fighting for e4 while waiting for Black to commit to [b:Nbd7] or [b:h6] before moving the bishop on f1 (avoiding [b:dxc4] with tempo). [Bd3] invites [Bd3 dxc4 Bxc4 Nd5], Capablanca's simplifying maneuver, while [cxd5] enters a clean Carlsbad Exchange. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd1c2,Gf1d3,Bc4d5] }
