{ The tabiya of the Anti-Moscow Gambit. Black has held the extra pawn on c4 and broken the pin, yet both wings are stretched thin and Black's king has no safe castling side. White's best plans are [Be2], preparing [w:O-O], [w:Ne5] and [w:f4] to rip open the center, and [h4], chipping directly at Black's weakened g5-h6 chain. Prematurely pushing [w:e5] hands the glorious d5 outpost to Black's knight. [%csl Yc4,Yg5,Yh6][%cal Gf1e2] }
9. Be2 ( 9. h4 ) ) 6... Qxf6
{ The Moscow Variation: White hands over the bishop pair to win development time and saddle Black with an exposed queen on f6. White's main move is [e3], followed by [b:Nd7] [w:Bd3] [b:dxc4] [w:Bxc4], when White enjoys more central space and faster castling while Black tucks the queen away, fianchettoes with [b:g6] and [b:Bg7], and prepares [b:e5] or [b:c5] to unleash the two bishops. [Qb3] and [cxd5] are solid alternatives. [%csl Yf6][%cal Ge2e3] }
7. e3 ( 7. Qb3 ) ( 7. cxd5 ) ) ( 5. cxd5 ) 5... Nbd7
{ The main crossroads of the Semi-Slav. If White plays the natural [Bd3], Black captures [b:dxc4] with tempo and expands with [b:b5], entering the Meran. [Qc2], the Anti-Meran or Stoltz Variation, sidesteps that tempo loss: because White's bishop is still on f1, Black usually answers [b:Bd6] and waits before taking on c4. Both sides then race for the central breaks [w:e4] and [b:e5]. [%csl Rc4,Rf1][%cal Gf1d3] }
( 5... a6
{ The Accelerated Meran: Black prepares [b:b5] without waiting for [b:Nbd7]. White can keep the tension with [b3] or [Bd3], or close the queenside with [c5], which takes away the [b:b5] and [b:c5] plans while giving Black [b:Nbd7] and [b:e5] later. After [Bd3], Black takes on c4 and expands with [b:b5], [b:Bb7] and [b:c5]; after [b3], Black can pin with [b:Bb4]. [%cal Gf1d3] }
6. Bd3 ( 6. b3 ) ( 6. c5 ) ) ( 5... Bd6
