[Event "London System"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 d5 ( 1... Nf6 2. Bf4
{ White enters the London straight away, placing the bishop outside the pawn chain before [e3]. Because Black has not committed a pawn to d5, four distinct structures branch from here: [d5] reaches the classical London tabiyas, [e6] keeps a choice between [d5] and a Queen's Indian [b6] fianchetto, [c5] strikes at d4 and clears b6 for the queen to hit b2, and [g6] sets up a King's Indian or Grünfeld fianchetto where White's light-squared bishop belongs on e2 rather than d3. [%csl Yc5,Yg6][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 2. Nf3 g6 ( 2... e6 3. Bf4
{ Through the [Nf3] move order, White brings out [Bf4] while Black keeps the d-pawn flexible behind e6. Black has four healthy setups from here: [d5] transposing into the classical London, [c5] challenging d4 right away while clearing b6 for the queen, [b6] fianchettoing on b7 in Queen's Indian style to control e4 and e5, and [Be7] preparing [b:Nh5] before White plays [w:h3]. [%cal Gd7d5,Gc7c5,Gb7b6] }
