[Event "Queen's Pawn systems (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 d5 ( 1... Nf6 2. Nc3
{ A flexible transpositional move order favored by Jobava, Rapport, and Veresov players: White prepares [e4] next move if Black fianchettoes lazily, while side-stepping [b:c5] anti-London lines after [w:Bf4]. Black's main move is [d5], stopping [e4] and inviting [d5 Bf4] (Rapport-Jobava), [d5 Bg5] (Richter-Veresov), or [d5 e4] (Blackmar-Diemer). [g6] allows [g6 e4 d6] (Pirc) or [g6 Bf4] (Barry), and [e6] prepares [d5] or [Bb4]. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gd7d5,Gg7g6] }
