[Event "Gambits and sidelines after 1.d4"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 e5
{ The Englund Gambit, named after Fritz Englund's 1932 Stockholm tournament and shaped earlier by Henry Charlick and Kārlis Bētiņš. Black sacrifices a central pawn on move one to drag [d4] players into open blitz traps against b2 and c1. Objectively the gambit is unsound, and [dxe5] is the only move that punishes it: declining with [d5], [Nf3], [c4], or [e3] throws away White's entire opening advantage, while pre-moving [Bf4] blunders a piece! [%csl Yb2][%cal Gd4e5] }
( 1... d5 2. e4
{ The Blackmar-Diemer Gambit, pioneered by Armand Blackmar (1882), refined with [Nc3] by Ignacy von Popiel (1889), and championed by Emil Josef Diemer in the 1930s–50s. White offers a central pawn on move two to blast open the d- and f-files for a direct kingside attack. [dxe4] is Black's principled test (while [e6] and [c6] transpose into the French and Caro-Kann, and [Nc6] lets [Nc6 e5] cramp Black's c-pawn). [%cal Gd5e4,Ge7e6,Gc7c6] }
( 2. g4
{ The Zürich Gambit: White hurls the g-pawn forward on move two hoping to pry open the g-file and long diagonal after [Bxg4 Bg2]. Because g4 weakens White's own kingside shelter and sacrifices a pawn with zero lead in development, Black simply captures [Bxg4]—followed calmly by [Nf6], [c6], and [e6], keeping the extra pawn safely—rather than ignoring it with passive moves. [%csl Yg4][%cal Gc8g4,Gg8f6] }
2... Bxg4 ) 2... dxe4
{ Black accepts the central pawn and challenges White to justify the gambit. Popiel's [Nc3] is essential, attacking e4 with a piece first so White forces Black to defend it ([Nc3 Nf6 f3]) before offering the second pawn. Pushing [f3] immediately—Blackmar's original 1882 move order—lets [f3 e5 dxe5 Qxd1+ Kxd1 Nc6] trade queens and regain e5 with White's king stranded in the center. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gb1c3,Gf1c4] }
( 2... Nf6 $2
{ Developing the knight before resolving e4 lets [e5] chase it away from its best post to d7 with gain of space and tempo. Capture the pawn with [b:dxe4] instead. [%csl Yf6][%cal Re4e5] }
3. e5 ) 3. Nc3
{ White attacks e4 with the knight, often reached when White meets [b:Nf6] and [d5] with [Nc3] and [e4]. Black's main move is [Nf6], defending e4 cleanly and inviting [Nf6 f3] (the Blackmar-Diemer proper) or [Nf6 Bg5] (the von Popiel Gambit). [e5] is the Lemberger Countergambit, returning the pawn at once to avoid White's attack, and [Bf5] holds e4 actively (while [c5] and [f5] are sharp sidelines). [%cal Gg8f6,Ge7e5,Gc8f5] }
( 3. Bc4
