{ The Diemer-Rosenberg Attack: White develops the dark-squared bishop to e3 on move three, holding back c3 so that [w:Nd2] and [w:c3] or [w:f3] can follow without allowing pins on b4. Because e4 is not attacked yet, Black has an easy path to an advantage: [Nf6] defends the extra pawn cleanly while preventing [w:Qh5], [e6] and [c6] build a rock-solid barrier against White's bishops, and [Bf5] overprotects e4 while eyeing c2. Black keeps the extra central pawn with minimal risk. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gg8f6,Ge7e6] }
