[Event "King's Indian Defence (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 Nf6 ( 1... d6 2. c4
{ Meeting [d4] with [d6] invites White to take the full centre with [e4] (heading into a Pirc or Philidor), so when White instead plays [c4], Black enters Old Indian, King's Indian or Rat territory while dodging the Trompowsky and London sidelines. Black's main continuations are [Nf6] (transposing to the Old or King's Indian), [e5] (the English Rat, challenging d4 before [Nf6]), and [g6] (Modern / King's Indian). [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg8f6] }
2... Nf6 ( 2... e5 ) ( 2... g6 ) ) 2. Nf3 ( 2. c4 d6 ( 2... g6
{ The King's Indian and Grünfeld crossroads. Black prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop to g7, yielding central pawn territory early in order to strike back at White's centre with pieces and timely pawn breaks. [Nc3] is the main move, claiming e4 and inviting Black to choose between [Nc3 Bg7] (the King's Indian proper) and [Nc3 d5] (the Grünfeld). [Nf3] delays e4 and prevents an immediate [e5], while [g3] steers directly into the Fianchetto System and [f3] prepares e4 without allowing a Grünfeld knight exchange on c3. [%csl Yg7,Ye4,Yc3][%cal Gb1c3] }
3. Nc3 ( 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O ( 4... d6
{ By playing [d6] before castling, Black declares a pure King's Indian Fianchetto setup right away, ruling out Neo-Grünfeld [b:d5] lines and reserving the option of an early [b:c6] and [b:Qa5] or [b:Bg4]. White's natural replies both transpose smoothly into main Fianchetto channels: [Bg2] completes the kingside fianchetto ready for [Bg2 O-O O-O], while [Nc3] develops the queenside knight first and discourages any immediate [b:c5] or [b:e5] break. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gf1g2,Gb1c3] }
5. Bg2 ( 5. Nc3 ) ) 5. Bg2
{ White completes the kingside fianchetto, opposing Black's g7 bishop directly along the long diagonal and shielding the white king before committing the b1-knight. Black now chooses the pawn structure: [d6] enters the King's Indian Fianchetto Variation (preparing [b:Nbd7] and [b:e5], or [b:Nc6] and [b:a6] in the Panno), whereas [d5] strikes the center immediately in the Neo-Grünfeld, and [c6] sets up a solid Schlechter/Grünfeld wall before pushing [b:d5]. [%csl Yg2,Yg7][%cal Gd7d6,Gd7d5] }
5... d6 ( 5... d5 ) ( 5... c6 6. O-O d5 7. cxd5 cxd5 8. Ne5 Nc6 9. Nc3
{ In this Symmetrical Neo-Grünfeld Exchange structure, White plants an outpost knight on e5 and follows with [Nc3] to pile pressure onto d5 while inviting [Nxc6 bxc6] to saddle Black with a backward c6-pawn on the open c-file. Because the pawn structure is completely symmetrical, Black must develop actively rather than drift passively: [e6] solidly bolsters d5 ready to reroute the f6-knight via [b:Nd7] to challenge e5, while [Bf5] and [Be6] get the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before playing [b:Rc8]. [%csl Ye5,Yd5][%cal Ge7e6,Gc8f5] }
9... e6 ( 9... Bf5 ) ( 9... Be6 ) ) ) ( 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nc3
{ Bringing out [Nc3] before [w:Nf3] keeps White's e-pawn unblocked (preserving [w:e4] and [w:Nge2] options) while increasing pressure on d5 so Black can no longer play [d5] comfortably without losing a pawn after [d5 cxd5 Nxd5 Nxd5]. Black therefore plays [d6] into the King's Indian Fianchetto System, [c5] to challenge d4 asymmetrically (inviting [c5 d5] or [c5 Nf3]), or [c6] to prepare [b:d5] with pawn support. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd7d6,Gc7c5] }
5... d6 ( 5... c6 ) ( 5... c5 ) 6. Nf3 c5
{ The Yugoslav Variation ([c5]) challenges White's d4-pawn directly instead of building up for [b:e5], inviting an asymmetrical fight along the open h8-a1 diagonal. White has three clear plans: [d5] grabs central space and enters the Fianchetto Benoni (where Black undermines the wedge with [d5 e6] or plays [d5 Na6] and [b:Nc7] for [b:b5]), [O-O] or [dxc5 dxc5 O-O] steers into a Maroczy-style symmetrical endgame where White presses on d5 with [w:Bg5] or [w:Bf4], and [e3] holds d4 quietly. [%csl Yg7,Yd4][%cal Gd4d5,Gd4c5] }
7. d5 ( 7. dxc5 ) ( 7. e3 ) ) ( 3. f3 Bg7
{ When Black answers [f3] calmly with [Bg7] rather than challenging the centre with a Grünfeld [b:d5] or Benoni [b:c5], White claims the full fourth rank immediately with [e4]. After [e4 d6 Nc3 O-O] (or [e4 O-O Nc3 d6]), play joins the main Sämisch King's Indian without White ever having allowed a Grünfeld knight trade on c3, ready for [Be3] and [Qd2]. [%csl Yc3][%cal Ge2e4] }
4. e4 ( 4. Nc3 ) ) ( 3. Bg5
{ Pinning f6 on move three (a Seirawan-style anti-Indian stab) tries to disrupt Black's smooth fianchetto before [Nc3] or [e4] is played. Because White's c1-bishop has abandoned b2 and no knight guards e4 yet, Black has an easy choice: [Bg7] develops calmly into King's Indian or Grünfeld channels ([Bg7 Nc3 d6] or [Bg7 Nc3 O-O]), while [Ne4] immediately questions the bishop with tempo ([Ne4 Bf4 Bg7 f3 Nf6 e4 d6]) before White can consolidate. [%csl Yf6,Yb2,Ye4][%cal Gf8g7] }
3... Bg7 ( 3... Ne4 ) ( 3... d6 ) ) ( 3. Bf4
{ Bringing the dark-squared bishop to f4 right after [c4] blends London piece placement with queenside space, aiming to meet [Bg7] with [Bg7 Nc3] and [e3] (or a sharp [Qd2] and [w:Bh6] battery) while keeping h2 ready as a retreat pocket if Black hunts the bishop with [Nh5]. Black develops [Bg7] and castles short, preparing either [d6] and [Nbd7] to fight for e5 or [c5] and [b:Qa5] to probe the unguarded queenside. [%csl Yf4,Yh2,Ye5][%cal Gf8g7] }
3... Bg7 ( 3... d6 ) ) ( 3. e3
{ Keeping the e-pawn modestly on e3 blunts any tactical ambitions on d4 and aims for a quiet queenside expansion with [Nc3], [Nf3], [Be2] (or [Bd3]) and [b4] once White castles short. Black develops [Bg7] and prepares [O-O], followed by either [d6] and [e5] (King's Indian style, claiming dark squares in the centre) or [c5] and [d5] to challenge White's pawn chain early. [%csl Ye3,Yd4][%cal Gf8g7] }
3... Bg7 ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... c5 ) ) 3... Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3
{ The Classical Variation. White develops naturally, defending d4 a second time with the king's knight and preparing [Be2] and [w:O-O] before choosing how to meet Black's central strike. [O-O] is by far Black's main move, tucking the king away before hitting the centre. [Bg4] is an active sideline aiming to trade off the f3-knight and weaken White's grip on d4, while [Nbd7] prepares [e5] right away. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ge8g8] }
( 5. f3
{ The Sämisch Variation, introduced by Friedrich Sämisch in the 1920s and championed by Mikhail Botvinnik, Boris Spassky and Garry Kasparov. By bolstering e4 with a pawn, White rules out [Ng4] and [Bg4], prepares a dark-square battery with [Be3] and [Qd2], and keeps the option of [w:O-O-O] with a [g4]-[h4] kingside pawn storm. The price is time and the loss of f3 for the g1-knight. Black almost always castles [O-O] first, though [c6] (Byrne-style, delaying castling) and [e5] are playable. [%csl Ye4,Yf3][%cal Ge8g8] }
5... O-O
{ Black castles and waits to see how White deploys the queenside bishop and g1-knight. [Be3] is the traditional main move, eyeing h6 and preparing [Qd2] for a kingside storm; [Nge2] (the Bobotsov-Korchnoi-Petrosian move order, perfected by Alexey Dreev) develops the knight first to sidestep Black's [b:c5] gambit; and [Bg5] is the Steiner Attack, pinning f6 before Black can play [b:e5]. [%csl Yh6,Yf6][%cal Gc1e3] }
( 5... c6 ) ( 5... e5 ) ( 5... Nbd7 ) 6. Be3 ( 6. Nge2
{ The flexible [Nge2] move order, favoured by Anatoly Karpov and Alexey Dreev. By developing the kingside knight before putting the bishop on e3, White sidesteps the [Be3 c5 dxc5 dxc5] gambit and discourages [e5] because [e5 Bg5] pins f6 comfortably. Black's main replies are [c5] (steering into a Benoni where [c5 d5 e6 Ng3] gives White a rock-solid chain), [Nc6] (heading for the Panno), and Dariusz Swiercz's modern [a6] preparing a Benko-style [b5] sacrifice. [%csl Ye3,Yf6][%cal Gc7c5] }
6... c5 ( 6... Nc6 ) ( 6... a6
{ Dariusz Swiercz's modern gambit against [Nge2]: Black prepares an immediate Benko-style [b5] pawn sacrifice without spending a move on [Nc6] or [b:c5]. If White plays [Be3] (or [Bg5]), Black sacrifices [Be3 b5 cxb5 axb5 Nxb5 c6 Nbc3 Nbd7] and strikes in the centre with [b:e5] and [b:d5]; if White prevents [b5] with [Ng3], Black challenges the knight aggressively with [Ng3 h5]. [%cal Gc1e3] }
7. Be3 ( 7. Ng3 ) ( 7. Bg5 ) ) ( 6... e5 ) 7. d5
{ White locks the queenside-leaning pawn wedge and prepares [Ng3], clearing the f1-bishop while eyeing h5 and fortifying e4. Black must strike immediately at the d5-spearhead with [e6] ([e6 Ng3 exd5 cxd5 a6 a4 Nbd7 Be2]), fighting for the e-file and [b5] counterplay; [a6] preparing [b5] or [e6] is also playable, whereas locking the centre with [e5] leaves Black horribly passive. [%csl Yh5,Ye4][%cal Ge7e6] }
7... e6 ( 7... a6 ) ( 7... e5 $2
{ Closing the entire centre with [e5] after [c5] leaves Black with zero queenside breaks and a backward d6-pawn, while White hasn't castled short and can launch a free kingside clamp with [h4], [Bg5] and [g4]. Undermine d5 immediately with [b:e6]. [%csl Yd6][%cal Rh2h4,Rc1g5] }
8. h4 h5 9. Bg5 ) ) ( 6. Bg5
{ The Steiner Attack. White pins f6 to prevent [e5] and prepares [Qd2] with [w:Bh6] to trade off Black's fianchetto bishop and soften the dark squares around g8. Because [e5] loses tactically to the g5 pin, Black strikes at the queenside and centre instead: [c5] is the main reply ([c5 d5 e6 Qd2 exd5 cxd5]), while [a6] prepares a Benko-style [b5] counter-sacrifice. [%csl Yf6,Yg8,Yg5][%cal Gc7c5] }
6... c5 ( 6... a6 ) ( 6... Nbd7 ) ( 6... Nc6 ) ( 6... e5 $2
