[Event "King's Indian Defence (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 ( 2. c4 d6 ( 2... g6 3. Nc3
{ White develops the queenside knight to its best square, fighting for e4 and d5. Black reaches the defining fork of Indian setups: [Bg7] commits to the King's Indian Defence, allowing White to build a broad pawn centre with [Bg7 e4], whereas [d5] is the Grünfeld Defence, challenging the centre with pawns before e4 lands. [d6] leads back to the King's Indian after [d6 e4 Bg7], though it gives up the Grünfeld option early. [%csl Ye4,Yd5][%cal Gf8g7] }
3... Bg7
{ The King's Indian Defence. Black places the dark-squared bishop on g7 to eye e5 and d4 from afar while preparing [d6] and [O-O]. [e4] is the principled reply, seizing the full central trio on c4, d4 and e4. [Nf3] keeps the centre flexible and avoids early tactics if White prefers a Fianchetto, Smyslov or [e3] setup, while [Bg5] and [g3] steer into independent anti-King's Indian lines. [%csl Yg7,Ye5,Yd4][%cal Ge2e4] }
( 3... d5 ) ( 3... d6
{ Playing [d6] on move three before [Bg7] commits Black to the King's Indian right away and forfeits the Grünfeld [b:d5], a move order many King's Indian specialists use to signal they welcome [e4] while sidestepping Anti-Grünfeld sidelines. White seizes the full centre with [e4] ([e4 Bg7]), or develops [Nf3] ([Nf3 Bg7]) to keep Fianchetto, Smyslov and Semi-Classical options open. [%cal Ge2e4] }
4. e4 ( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. g3 ) ) ( 3... c5
{ Striking with [c5] on move three before [Bg7] invites a Modern Benoni ([d5 d6 e4 Bg7]) or Benko Gambit ([d5 b5]), while testing whether White is willing to enter asymmetrical territory. White's main move is [d5], claiming central space ([d5 d6 e4 Bg7]), while [Nf3] ([Nf3 cxd4 Nxd4 Bg7 e4]) steers into a Maroczy Accelerated Dragon where White holds a steady bind. [%cal Gd4d5] }
4. d5 ( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. e3 ) ) 4. e4
{ White erects the classical three-pawn phalanx on c4, d4 and e4, claiming central space and threatening [w:e5] to kick Black's knight from f6. [d6] is overwhelmingly Black's best move, stopping [w:e5] in its tracks and preparing [O-O] followed by [e5] or [c5]. [O-O] is playable too, daring White to overextend with [O-O e5 Ne8], though White can simply answer [O-O Be2 d6] or [O-O Nf3 d6] and reach the same positions safely. [%csl Yc4,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gd7d6] }
( 4. Nf3 O-O
{ Black castles before committing the d-pawn, keeping [b:d5] in reserve if White plays passively without contesting d5. [e4] forces Black's hand with [e4 d6], transposing directly into the Classical King's Indian. [Bg5] and [g3] are strong alternatives where [b:d5] stays playable for Black, while [e3] prepares [Be2] and [w:O-O] for a calm positional squeeze on the queenside. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge2e4] }
( 4... d6
{ By playing [Nf3] before [e4], White invites [d6] first and can now choose among virtually every system against the King's Indian. [e4] returns to the main Orthodox, Gligoric and Petrosian tabiyas; [g3] enters the Fianchetto Variation now that Black can no longer play a Grünfeld [b:d5]; [Bg5] is the Smyslov System, pinning f6 and keeping the e-pawn back on e3; and [e3] sets up the solid Semi-Classical line. [%csl Yf6,Ye3][%cal Ge2e4] }
