{ The Delayed Fianchetto. By waiting for [d6] before playing [g3], White enters the Fianchetto King's Indian only after Black has given up the Grünfeld [d5] option, reaching main-line Fianchetto territory with [Nc3] already developed. Black castles [O-O], after which [O-O Bg2] invites Black's four main setups — [Nbd7], [Nc6], [c5] and [c6] — while White enjoys a safe kingside shelter and steady central space. [%cal Ge8g8] }
5... O-O ( 5... Nbd7 ) ( 5... Nc6 ) ) ( 5. Bg5 O-O
{ The Smyslov System. White pins f6 and holds the e-pawn back on e3 rather than building a broad target with [e4]: without a pawn on e4, Black's usual [f5] kingside storm loses its main hook. [e3] is the hallmark move, keeping d4 rock-solid and preparing [Be2] and [w:O-O], while [e4] switches into the Zinnowitz Variation. Black answers [e3] by challenging d4 with [b:c5] or chasing the bishop with [h6] and [b:g5] to claim the bishop pair. [%csl Yf6,Ye3,Ye4][%cal Ge2e3] }
6. e3
{ White bolsters d4 with a compact triangle and denies Black any central target on e4. Black's sharpest counterplay is [c5], striking at d4 while preparing [h6], [b:g5] and [Nh5] to hunt down the bishop on g3, as Garry Kasparov played; [Nbd7] prepares [e5] cleanly once [Be2] is in, and [h6] asks the bishop to declare itself between h4 and f4 right away. [%csl Yd4,Ye4,Yg3][%cal Gc7c5] }
