{ Playing [c6] on move four instead of [d6] leaves the e5-square unguarded, allowing White's incisive [e5]: after [e5 Ng8 Bf4 d6 Nf3], Black's knight is driven all the way back home while White enjoys a commanding lead in space and development! Developing quietly with [Nf3] or [Be3] lets Black get [d6] in unpunished and forfeits most of White's advantage. [%cal Ge4e5] }
5. e5 ) 5. Nf3 ( 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3
{ The classical Sämisch tabiya. White develops the dark-squared bishop to e3, supporting d4 and preparing [Qd2]. Black's sharpest theoretical answer is [c5], sacrificing a pawn for dark-square control if White accepts ([c5 dxc5 dxc5]) or steering into a Benoni after [c5 d5] or [c5 Nge2]. [Nc6] (followed by [a6] and [Rb8], the Sämisch Panno) and [e5] (the Orthodox Sämisch) are major fighting alternatives, alongside [a6] and [c6] (Byrne). [%csl Ye3,Yd4][%cal Gc7c5] }
6... c5
{ The Sämisch Gambit. Black strikes at d4 even though c5 is not defended by a pawn, banking on dark-square piece activity and the open g7 diagonal if White grabs it. White has three sound approaches: [Nge2] holds the tension ([Nge2 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Ng3 e6]), [d5] closes the centre into a Kapengut Benoni ([d5 e6 Qd2 exd5 cxd5]), and [dxc5] accepts the gambit. Putting the bishop on d3 drops material to a tactic. [%csl Yd4,Yc5,Yg7][%cal Gg1e2] }
( 6... Nc6
{ The Sämisch Panno / Yates setup. Black develops [Nc6] to pressure d4 and prepare Oscar Panno's queenside counterattack with [a6], [Rb8] and [b5] — especially potent if White castles queenside into the line of fire. White's main replies are [Nge2] (keeping castling flexible and preparing [Qd2] and [Nc1] or [Rc1]) and [Qd2] ([Qd2 a6 O-O-O Rb8]). Pushing [d5] unprovoked lets Black's knight leap to e5 hitting c4. [%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yc4][%cal Gg1e2] }
