4. e4 ( 4. Nf3 ) ) 4. f3
{ The main line against the Janowski Old Indian. [f3] prepares [e4] with tempo on the f5-bishop while taking g4 and e4 away from Black's f6-knight, essentially reaching a Sämisch structure where Black has committed [Bf5] instead of [g6] and [Bg7]. Black must strike at the centre immediately with [e5] ([e5 d5 Be7 e4 Bd7]) rather than playing [Nc6], which gets pushed back by [w:d5] and [e4]. [%csl Yg4,Ye4][%cal Ge7e5] }
( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. g3 ) ( 4. Qb3 Nc6
{ When White hits b7 early with [Qb3] against the Janowski Old Indian, Black's sharpest reply is [Nc6], offering the b7-pawn as a poisoned gambit while counter-attacking d4 with tempo! White must guard d4 first with [Nf3] ([Nf3 Qc8] or [Nf3 e5 d5 Nd4 Nxd4 exd4]), because grabbing b7 lets Black's knight crash into d4 and c2 against White's uncastled king. [%csl Yb7,Yd4,Yc2][%cal Gg1f3] }
5. Nf3 ( 5. e3 ) ( 5. Qxb7 $4
{ Snatching b7 falls into the Janowski trap: [Nxd4] threatens [Nc2+] forking king and a1-rook, and after [Nxd4 Kd1 Bc2+ Kd2 Rb8 Qa6 e5], White's king is stranded in the middle of the board under a devastating crossfire. Defend d4 with [Nf3] first. [%csl Rb7,Rd4][%cal Gc6d4] }
5... Nxd4 6. Kd1 Bc2+ 7. Kd2 Rb8 ) ) 4... e5 ( 4... c6 ) ) 2... g6 3. c4 Bg7 4.
g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6
{ With [d6] on the board, we stand in the pure King's Indian Fianchetto: Black concedes White a comfortable central foothold on c4 and d4 in exchange for solid dark-square piece coordination and an unopposed g7-bishop if the centre opens. White castles [O-O] (or develops [Nc3] first, transposing a move later) to shelter the king behind g2 and ask which Fianchetto defence Black will adopt. [%csl Yc4,Yd4,Yg2][%cal Ge1g1] }
6. O-O
{ The main crossroads of the Fianchetto Variation. Black chooses among four rich systems: [Nbd7] is the Classical (Debrecen) line, preparing [e5] to hold a firm dark-square foothold; [Nc6] is the Carlsbad setup, branching into the Panno ([a6] and [Rb8]), Simagin ([Bg4]) and Uhlmann-Szabo ([e5]); [c5] is the dynamic Yugoslav System; and [c6] prepares [b:Qa5] (Kavalek) or [Bf5] (Larsen) to blunt White's g2-bishop. [%cal Gb8d7] }
( 6. Nc3 ) 6... Nbd7
{ The Classical (Debrecen) Fianchetto: Black prepares [e5] with the queenside knight on d7 so c6 stays open for a pawn to guard d5 and no knight sits exposed to a tempo-gaining [d5] push. [Nc3] is White's principled reply, claiming central control and inviting [Nc3 e5 e4]; [Qc2] (eyeing Reti's [Qc2 e5 Rd1] squeeze) and [b3] are solid, slower alternatives. [%csl Yd7,Yc6,Yd5][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 6... Nc6 ) ( 6... c5
{ The Yugoslav System. Black challenges d4 with the c-pawn before developing the queenside knight, steering for either an asymmetrical Benoni / Panno structure if White pushes [d5] or a lively symmetrical Maroczy battle if White holds the tension with [Nc3]. [Nc3] is the main move ([Nc3 Nc6 d5 Na5] or [Nc3 Nc6 dxc5 dxc5]), while immediate [d5] invites a Benko-style [d5 b5] or [d5 Na6]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb1c3] }
7. Nc3
{ White holds the central tension in the Yugoslav Fianchetto, developing [Nc3] to reinforce d4 and e4 rather than releasing the pressure early. Black has two main continuations: [Nc6] piles a third attacker onto d4 to provoke [Nc6 d5 Na5] (or [Nc6 dxc5 dxc5]), while [cxd4] ([cxd4 Nxd4 Nc6 Nc2]) trades pawns immediately to open the c-file and test White's centralized d4-knight along the g7-a1 diagonal. [%csl Yd4,Ye4,Yg7][%cal Gb8c6] }
( 7. d5 ) ( 7. dxc5 ) 7... Nc6
{ With both c-pawns and knights facing off, White must resolve the double attack on d4. [d5] is the main line, driving the knight to a5 ([d5 Na5 Nd2 a6 Qc2 Rb8], reaching the Panno-Yugoslav tabiya where Black often sacrifices the exchange with [b:Rxb2] for dark-square domination), while [dxc5] ([dxc5 dxc5 Be3]) leads to an open symmetrical fight where Black gambits c5 with [Be6] or [Qa5] and [b:Nd4]. [%csl Yd4,Ya5,Yc5][%cal Gd4d5] }
( 7... cxd4 ) 8. d5
{ The Advance Line of the Yugoslav Fianchetto. White claims space with [d5] and kicks Black's knight off c6. Black's main move is jumping to the rim with [Na5], attacking White's unguarded c4-pawn with tempo ([Na5 Nd2 a6 Qc2 Rb8 b3 b5], reaching the sharp Panno-Yugoslav tabiya where Black fights for the queenside dark squares); [Ne5] ([Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 b3 Bd7], heading for a [Ne8] and [b:Nd6] blockade) is playable too. [%csl Yc6][%cal Gc6a5] }
( 8. dxc5 dxc5
{ The Exchange Line of the Yugoslav Fianchetto. With the d-file open and symmetrical c-pawns on c4 and c5, White aims to exploit the slight lead in development and the unopposed g2-bishop with [Be3] (hitting c5 immediately so Black must respond dynamically with [Be3 Qa5 Qa4] or [Be3 Nd7] / [Be3 Be6]) or [Bf4]. Slow pawn moves like [a3] let Black equalize effortlessly with [Be6]. [%csl Yc4,Yc5][%cal Gc1e3] }
9. Be3 ( 9. Bf4 ) ( 9. Qa4 ) ) 8... Na5 ( 8... Ne5 ) ) ( 6... c6
{ A solid, flexible system favoured by Lubomir Kavalek and Teimour Radjabov. [c6] blunts White's g2-bishop along the long diagonal, takes d5 away from White's pieces, and opens the d8-a5 diagonal for Black's queen. After [Nc3], Black chooses between [Nc3 Qa5] (Kavalek), [Nc3 Nbd7] (heading for [e5]), [Nc3 Bf5] (Larsen) and [Nc3 Qc7] without fearing any tempo-gaining [d5] push. [%csl Yd5,Yd8,Ya5][%cal Gb1c3] }
7. Nc3
{ Once White develops [Nc3] against Black's solid [c6] setup, Black chooses how to deploy the queenside pieces while d5 is safely covered: [Qa5] is Lubomir Kavalek's active line (eyeing c3 and preparing [e5] or [Be6]), [Nbd7] heads calmly for [e5] and [Re8], [Bf5] is Bent Larsen's light-square development before [e5], and [Bg4] pins f3 to fight for d4 and e5. [%csl Yd5,Yc3,Yf3][%cal Gd8a5] }
( 7. Qc2 ) ( 7. b3 ) 7... Qa5
{ The Kavalek Defence. Black's queen steps to a5 to clear d8 for a rook, eye c3, and prepare either [e5] in the centre or a bold swing to h5 to trade off White's fianchetto bishop. White's strongest answer is [e4] ([e4 e5 h3]), grabbing the centre while watching out for [Bg4]. Note a famous trap after [h3]: if Black rushes [h3 Qh5] prematurely, [h3 Qh5 Ng5] threatens [Bf3] trapping Black's queen! [%csl Ya5,Yd8,Yc3][%cal Ge2e4] }
( 7... Nbd7 ) ( 7... Bf5
{ Bent Larsen's Defence to the Fianchetto Variation. Developing the light-squared bishop to f5 before pushing [e5] solves Black's problem piece early and eyes e4 so White cannot claim the centre casually ([Nh4 Bc8] or [Nh4 Be6 d5 cxd5 cxd5 Bd7]). White's strongest replies are [h3] (preventing [b:Ne4] and [b:Bh3] ideas while preparing [Be3] or [g4]), [Nh4], and [b3] with [Bb2]. [%csl Yf5,Ye4][%cal Gh2h3] }
8. h3 ( 8. Nh4 ) ( 8. b3 ) ( 8. Re1 ) ) ( 7... Bg4
{ Pinning f3 after [c6] combines solid d5-control with Simagin-style pressure on White's knight: Black intends [Bxf3] followed by [Nbd7] and [e5], leaving White without the knight that normally eyes d4 and e5. White questions the bishop immediately with [h3] ([h3 Bxf3 Bxf3 Nbd7 e4 e5 d5] or [h3 Bxf3 exf3]), enjoying the bishop pair, safe king, and steady central space. [%csl Yf3,Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gh2h3] }
8. h3 ( 8. e4 ) ( 8. Qb3 ) ) 8. e4 ( 8. h3
{ Against Kavalek's [Qa5], [h3] secures e3 for White's dark-squared bishop and prepares [e4] without allowing [Bg4] to trade off White's f3-knight and soften d4. Black's main continuations are [e5] ([e5 e4 Nbd7 Be3], holding a sturdy dark-square centre), [Be6] (hitting c4 to provoke [Be6 Nd2]), and [Nbd7]; swinging the queen to [Qh5] prematurely walks into a queen-trapping net! [%csl Ye3,Yd4,Yc4][%cal Ge7e5] }
8... e5 ( 8... Be6 ) ( 8... Nbd7 ) ( 8... Qh5 $4
{ Bringing the queen to h5 to threaten [Bxh3] leaves her stranded out of squares! White plays [Ng5], threatening [Bf3] (or [g4] and [Bf3]) to trap the queen completely, forcing ugly contortions like [Ng5 Bh8 b4] that cost Black decisive material or time. Play [e5] or [Be6] instead. [%csl Rh5][%cal Gf3g5] }
9. Ng5 Bh8 10. b4 ) ) ( 8. Bd2 ) ) 7. Nc3
{ White brings the queenside knight into the fight for e4 and d5, daring Black to step into the centre. [e5] is by far Black's main move, contesting d4 right away to claim dark-square space and open the c8-bishop; [c6] and [c5] are playable transpositions, while [a6] (the Hungarian Variation) prepares [Rb8] and [b5] on the queenside before committing the e-pawn. [%csl Ye4,Yd5,Yd4][%cal Ge7e5] }
( 7. Qc2 ) ( 7. b3 ) 7... e5
{ Black plants a sturdy pawn on e5, challenging d4 while keeping c6 open for a pawn to guard d5. [e4] is the Classical main line, establishing a powerful Maroczy-like grip on c4 and e4 with the g2-bishop reinforcing e4 from behind; [h3] (preventing [Ng4] and [Bg4] before playing [e4] and [Be3]), [Qc2], and [b3] (the Double Fianchetto) are reliable companions. [%csl Ye5,Yd4,Yc6][%cal Ge2e4] }
( 7... c6 ) ( 7... c5 ) ( 7... a6 ) 8. e4
{ The Classical Fianchetto tabiya. White occupies c4, d4 and e4 with the king safely castled behind g2. Black has two main paths: [c6] holds the central tension (inviting [c6 h3 Qb6] or [c6 h3 exd4 Nxd4 Re8]), while [exd4] (followed by [exd4 Nxd4 Re8 h3 Nc5], the Long / Gallagher Variation) surrenders the centre at once to target White's e4-pawn with pieces. [%csl Yc4,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gc7c6] }
( 8. h3 ) ( 8. b3
{ The Double Fianchetto Attack: White prepares [Bb2] and [Qc2] (or [w:e4] and [w:Rad1]) to put long-range x-ray pressure on e5 and d6 from both flanks while keeping the d-file half-closed and avoiding early simplifications. Black maintains the central anchor with [Re8] followed by [c6] (or trades [exd4 Nxd4 Re8]), never pushing [e4] prematurely before [Re8] backs the pawn up. [%csl Ye5,Yd6][%cal Gf8e8] }
8... Re8 ( 8... c6 ) ( 8... exd4 ) ( 8... e4 $2
{ Pushing [e4] without [Re8] behind it drops the e4-pawn for nothing! After [Ng5], Black has no way to hold e4 ([Ng5 Re8 Ngxe4 Nxe4 Nxe4] or [Ng5 c5 Ngxe4]), leaving White a clean central pawn ahead with two raking fianchetto bishops. Play [Re8] first. [%csl Re4][%cal Rf3g5] }
9. Ng5 c5 10. Ngxe4 ) ) ( 8. Qc2 ) 8... c6
{ Black shores up d5 and keeps d4 under observation, ready to answer careless moves with [Qb6] or [exd4]. [h3] is the main line — ruling out [Ng4] and [Bg4] forever so White's bishop can stand safely on e3 ([h3 Qb6 Re1 exd4 Nxd4 Re8 Be3]) — while [Re1] is also good. Developing [Bg5] here runs into [h6] and [Ng4]. [%csl Yd5,Yd4,Ye3][%cal Gh2h3] }
( 8... exd4 9. Nxd4 Re8 10. h3 Nc5
{ The Long Variation, popularized by Grandmaster Joe Gallagher. Black's rook on e8 and knight on c5 attack White's e4-pawn twice while the g7-bishop x-rays the d4-knight along the long diagonal, setting a famous tactical trap if White moves the queen off the d-file! White must defend e4 accurately with [Re1] ([Re1 a5]), keeping d4 guarded so [Qc2] or [Ndb5] can follow safely. [%csl Ye8,Yc5,Ye4][%cal Gf1e1] }
11. Re1 ( 11. Qc2 $2
{ Stepping off the d-file with [Qc2] leaves the d4-knight guarded only by the c1-bishop! Black snatches the pawn with [Ncxe4 Nxe4 Nxe4]: White cannot recapture on e4 with the bishop because d4 hangs, and [Ncxe4 Nxe4 Nxe4 Qxe4 Rxe4 Bxe4 Bxh3] wins for Black. Defend e4 with [Re1]. [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc5e4] }
11... Ncxe4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 ) 11... a5
{ Black secures the c5-outpost with [a5] and keeps latent threats of [Nfxe4] or [b:a4] alive. Because the e1-rook is x-rayed and d4 still relies on the queen's protection, White's sharpest continuations are [Qc2] (only once [Re1] is in, meeting [b:a4] with [Be3]), [Ndb5] (attacking d6 to disrupt Black's coordination), or [Bf4]. Developing [Be3] prematurely blocks the e-file and drops e4! [%csl Yd4,Yd6,Ye4][%cal Gd4b5] }
12. Ndb5 ( 12. Qc2 ) ( 12. Bf4 ) ( 12. Rb1 ) ( 12. Be3 $6
