[Event "Nimzo-Indian and Queen's Indian (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 Nf6 ( 1... e6 2. c4
{ White takes queenside space and threatens [w:Nc3] followed by [w:e4], turning Black's flexible first move into a major crossroads. [Nf6] enters the Indian complexes where Black waits to see which knight White brings out first, while [d5] is the direct path into the Queen's Gambit Declined. [Bb4+], the Kangaroo or Keres Defense, checks immediately before White can stop it, and [f5] steers into the Dutch Defense. [%cal Gg8f6] }
2... Nf6 ( 2... d5 ) ( 2... Bb4+
{ The Kangaroo Defense, also known as the Keres Defense after Estonian grandmaster Paul Keres. Black checks on b4 before developing the g8-knight, keeping the option of advancing the f-pawn or trading dark-squared bishops early. [Nc3] is the most common reply and usually transposes into the Nimzo-Indian after [Nc3 Nf6]. [Bd2] challenges the bishop right away, steering toward Bogo-Indian structures where White keeps the pawn structure intact. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gb1c3] }
3. Nc3
{ Blocking with the knight invites [Nf6], transposing directly into the main starting position of the Nimzo-Indian Defense. Because the dark-squared bishop has already committed to b4 while the g8-knight stays at home, Black can also strike at d4 immediately with [c5] or trade [Bxc3+] to double White's c-pawns while keeping [b:f5] in reserve for a Dutch-style clamp on e4. Early queen sorties hand White the center with gain of time. [%csl Yb4,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gg8f6] }
( 3. Bd2
{ White blocks the check with the bishop and attacks b4 directly, forcing Black to defend, trade, or retreat the bishop at once. [Qe7] is the most flexible defense, keeping the bishop on b4 while leaving [b:Nf6], [b:d6] and [b:e5] open; taking [Qe7 Bxb4 Qxb4+] only forks c4 and b2 in Black's favor. [Bxd2+] trades off the dark-squared bishops cleanly, [a5] defends b4 while claiming queenside space, and [Be7] drops back quietly. [%csl Yb4,Yc4,Yb2][%cal Gd8e7] }
3... Qe7 ( 3... Bxd2+ ) ( 3... a5 ) ( 3... Be7 ) ) ( 3. Nd2 ) 3... Nf6 ( 3...
c5 ) ( 3... Bxc3+ ) ( 3... Qf6 $2
{ Bringing the queen to f6 takes the best square from the g8-knight and fails to hold back White's center. [e4] seizes space at once, and after [e4 Bxc3+ bxc3] White enjoys the bishop pair, a broad pawn center, and smooth development with [w:Nf3] and [w:Bd3] while Black's queen stands exposed. Play [b:Nf6] to enter the Nimzo-Indian instead. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge2e4] }
4. e4 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 ) ) ( 2... f5 ) ) 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 ( 3. Nf3 b6 ( 3... d5 4.
Nc3 ( 4. Bg5 h6
{ When White rushes [Bg5] before developing [w:Nc3], Black's [h6] (the Capablanca / Anti-Neo-Orthodox finesse) immediately highlights the missing queenside knight. If White tries to maintain the pin with [Bh4], Black has the disruptive [Bh4 Bb4+] check followed by [b:dxc4] to grab and hold the pawn! White's cleanest equalizer-plus is therefore [Bxf6 Qxf6 Nc3], giving up the bishop pair temporarily to target d5 before Black has consolidated, while [Bf4] switches cleanly into a Harrwitz-style setup. [%csl Yb4,Yd5][%cal Gg5f6,Gg5h4] }
( 4... Bb4+
{ Punishing White's early [Bg5] (played before [w:Nc3]), Black throws in [Bb4+] to disrupt White's coordination and pull a piece onto d2 or c3. White's sharpest reply is [Nc3], entering the Ragozin-Vienna complex where [Nc3 dxc4] or [Nc3 h6 Bxf6 Qxf6] leads to concrete tactical play. Blocking with [Nbd2] keeps the c-file open and prepares [w:a3] after [Nbd2 dxc4], whereas [Bd2 Be7] eases Black's cramp by trading off the dark-squared bishops. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gb1c3,Gg5d2,Gb1d2] }
5. Nc3 ( 5. Nbd2 ) ( 5. Bd2 ) ) 5. Bh4 ( 5. Bxf6 ) ( 5. Bf4 ) ) 4... Nbd7 (
4... Bb4 5. Bg5 dxc4
{ The Vienna Variation ([dxc4]) is one of modern chess's richest battlegrounds: with White's dark-squared bishop committed outside the pawn chain on g5 and the c3-knight pinned, Black snatches the c4-pawn and threatens [b:b5] or [b:c5] to shatter the center. White's mainline principled push is [e4], seizing the center and daring [e4 c5 Bxc4 cxd4 Nxd4 Qa5] (or [e4 h6 Bxf6 Qxf6 Bxc4]) in razor-sharp tactical lines; [Qa4+ Nc6] recovers the pawn more quietly, and [a3 Bxc3+ bxc3] builds a broad pawn center. [%csl Rc4,Yb5][%cal Ge2e4,Gd1a4] }
6. e4 ( 6. Qa4+ ) ( 6. a3 ) ) ( 4... c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4
{ This is the main line of the Semi-Tarrasch Defense: by recapturing on d5 with the knight instead of the e-pawn, Black avoids an isolated queen's pawn and invites [e4] on purpose! Black now trades [Nxc3 bxc3 cxd4 cxd4 Bb4+ Bd2 Bxd2+ Qxd2 O-O], swapping off minor pieces to reach a classic Grünfeld-style endgame/middlegame: White boasts an imposing central duo on d4 and e4, while Black enjoys a clean 2-on-1 queenside majority (a- and b-pawns against White's lone a-pawn) and pressure against d4. [%csl Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gd5c3,Gc5d4] }
6... Nxc3 ( 6... cxd4 ) ) ( 4... Be7 5. Bf4 ( 5. Bg5 O-O 6. e3 h6 7. Bxf6
{ The Anti-Tartakower trade ([Bxf6]) sidesteps both Lasker's [b:Ne4] freeing jump and Tartakower's [b:b6]/[b:Bb7] counterplay before Black can set them up. Black must recapture [Bxf6] (taking with the pawn via [gxf6] ruins the kingside shelter for nothing). Play then revolves around a subtle battle for central tempos: White aims for [Bxf6 Rc1 c6 Bd3 Nd7 O-O] to fight for the [w:e4] break, while Black uses the bishop pair and solid f6 post to prepare [b:dxc4] followed by [b:e5] or [b:c5]. [%csl Ye4,Yc5][%cal Ge7f6] }
7... Bxf6 ) 5... O-O 6. e3 Nbd7
{ In the Harrwitz Attack ([Bf4]), White places the bishop on an active diagonal eyeing c7 where it cannot be harassed by [b:Nh5] without giving White control of e5. With [Nbd7], Black prepares both [b:c5] and the Lasker-style liberating trade [b:dxc4] followed by [b:Nb6] and [b:Nfd5] or [b:c5]. White's sharpest try is the space-gaining [c5] clamp (stopping [b:c5] cold and launching a [w:b4]-[w:b5] queenside storm, met by [c5 Nh5] or [c5 c6]), while [cxd5] and [Bd3] keep healthy central tension. [%csl Yc7,Yd5][%cal Gc4c5,Gf1d3] }
7. Bd3 ( 7. c5 ) ( 7. cxd5 ) ) 5. Bg5
{ With Black's knight already on d7, [Bg5] sets the famous Queen's Gambit Declined trap: [dxc4] is no blunder here because d7 blocks the queen, yet careless moves still let [w:cxd5] clarify the center on White's terms. Black usually unpins calmly with [Be7] into the Orthodox Defense, bolsters d5 with [c6] (ready to meet [c6 e3] with the sharp Cambridge Springs [c6 e3 Qa5]!), or plays the active Westphalia [Bb4] to counter-pin c3 and prepare [b:c5]. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gf8e7,Gc7c6,Gf8b4] }
( 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 c6 7. e3 Be7 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Qc2
{ This is the pure classical Carlsbad tabiya with [Nf3]: because f3 is occupied, White's main strategic plan is the queenside minority attack — castling short, placing rooks on b1 and c1, and advancing [w:b4]-[w:b5] to saddle Black with a backward c6-pawn or an isolated d5-pawn. Meanwhile [Qc2] prevents Black's freeing [b:Ne4] and eyes h7. Black answers with [Re8], freeing f8 so the d7-knight can maneuver via [Re8 O-O Nf8] to e6 or g6 while preparing kingside counterplay or [b:a5] to slow [w:b4]. [%csl Yb4,Yc6,Yf8][%cal Gf8e8] }
9... Re8 ( 9... h6 ) ( 9... g6 ) ) 5... Be7 ( 5... c6 ) ( 5... Bb4 ) ) 4. g3
{ The Fianchetto Variation, White's classical and most popular answer to the Queen's Indian. Since Black is putting a bishop on the a8-h1 diagonal to clamp down on e4 and d5, White fianchettoes on g2 to oppose it directly — with the practical plus that after castling, White's g2-bishop is guarded by the king while Black's b7-bishop sits undefended. Black chooses between modern [Ba6], traditional [Bb7], and [Bb4+]. [%csl Ye4,Yd5,Yg2][%cal Gc8b7] }
( 4. a3
{ The Petrosian Variation, elaborated by Tigran Petrosian in the 1950s and turned into a sharp attacking weapon by Garry Kasparov in the 1980s. White invests a move on [a3] to rule out [b:Bb4] permanently, planning [w:Nc3], [w:Qc2] and [w:e4] to claim the whole center. Black's main answer is [Bb7], preparing to meet [w:Nc3] with [b:d5]; [Ba6] hits c4 at once, and [c5 d5] enters a Benoni structure. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gc8b7] }
4... Bb7
{ Black develops the light-squared bishop to b7 to clamp down on e4, ignoring White's prophylactic [a3] move. White's main continuation is [Nc3], stepping into the key square now that [b:Bb4] is impossible and forcing Black to stop [w:e4] and [w:d5] with [Nc3 d5]. Quieter alternatives such as [e3], [Bg5] or [g3] are playable but let Black equalize more comfortably since a3 is less urgent there. [%csl Yb7,Ye4,Ya3][%cal Gb1c3] }
