7... dxc4 ( 7... c5 ) ( 7... b6 ) ) ( 6. cxd5 ) ( 6. Ne2 ) 6... c5
{ The Gligorić System, the classical crossroads of the [e3] Nimzo-Indian. All four central pawns stand in tension, and White's best move is simply [O-O], tucking the king away rather than resolving the center prematurely (though [cxd5] and [a3] are playable). Once White castles, Black chooses between trading on c4 and d4 to saddle White with an isolated d-pawn and developing [b:Nc6] to keep maximum tension. [%csl Yc4,Yd4][%cal Ge1g1] }
( 6... b6 ) ( 6... dxc4 ) ( 6... Nc6 ) ( 6... Ne4 $6
{ Hopping into e4 before challenging White's center with [b:c5] costs time and leaves Black's queenside asleep: after [O-O f5 Qc2] (or [Qc2] right away), White threatens to trade on e4 or kick the b4-bishop while Black's c8-bishop is stuck behind the pawns. Challenge d4 with [b:c5] instead. [%csl Re4,Rd4][%cal Ge1g1] }
7. O-O f5 8. Qc2 ) 7. O-O
{ The main tabiya of the Rubinstein Nimzo-Indian: White is castled and ready for central action, so Black must decide how to handle the pawn tension. [dxc4] followed by [dxc4 Bxc4] leads to the Karpov ([b:cxd4]) and Parma ([b:Nbd7]) systems, [Nc6] keeps all the tension in the Bernstein/Main Variation, and [cxd4] followed by [cxd4 exd4 dxc4] reaches the same isolated queen's pawn battle by another order. [%cal Gd5c4] }
( 7. cxd5 ) ( 7. a3 ) 7... dxc4 ( 7... Nc6
{ Black increases the pressure on d4 without releasing the pawn tension yet. White's classic continuation is [a3], forcing [a3 Bxc3 bxc3] (whereas [cxd5] enters an early IQP trade), after which [b:dxc4] and [b:Qc7] reach the historic Main Variation of the Nimzo-Indian: Black prepares the [b:e5] break, threatening [b:e4] to fork f3 and d3, while White maneuvers the light-squared bishop and prepares [w:e4]. [%csl Yd4,Yf3,Yd3][%cal Ga2a3] }
8. a3 ( 8. cxd5 ) 8... Bxc3 9. bxc3
{ White has traded a knight for the dark-squared bishop, gaining a sturdy pawn center and the bishop pair at the cost of doubled c-pawns. Black's two main paths are [dxc4] followed by [dxc4 Bxc4 Qc7], clearing the d-file to push [b:e5] with counterplay in the center, and [Qc7] immediately (the Khasin Variation), waiting for [cxd5 exd5] to restrain White's pawns on the light squares while mobilizing the queenside majority. [%cal Gd5c4] }
9... dxc4 ( 9... Qc7 ) ( 9... b6 ) ) ( 7... cxd4 ) ( 7... Nbd7 ) 8. Bxc4
{ Black gives up d5 to draw White's bishop out to c4 and open the d-file. From here Black has three respected setups: [cxd4] enters the Karpov Variation, giving White an isolated queen's pawn; [Nbd7] is the Parma Variation, keeping tension while preparing [b:b6] and [b:Bb7]; and [Nc6] is the Larsen Variation, where [w:a3] is met safely by [b:Ba5] now that c4 is gone and White can no longer isolate d5 with a c-pawn capture. [%csl Yd5,Yc4][%cal Gc5d4] }
8... cxd4 ( 8... Nbd7
{ The Parma (or Bronstein) Variation: Black develops the queen's knight to d7 so it backs up c5 without blocking the b7-bishop once [b:b6] is played. White usually plays [Qe2], clearing d1 for [w:Rd1] to support a well-timed [w:d5] break or [w:e4] push, while [a3] forces the b4-bishop to trade or retreat to a5. Pushing [d5] immediately without rook support drops a central pawn. [%csl Yd7,Yc5,Yd1][%cal Gd1e2] }
9. Qe2 ( 9. a3 ) ( 9. Bd3 ) ( 9. d5 $2
{ Advancing [w:d5] before placing the rook on d1 or queen on e2 leaves both the c4-bishop and the d5-pawn loose: [Nb6] hits the bishop with tempo and adds pressure to d5, and after [Nb6 Bd3 Bxc3 bxc3 exd5] Black wins a clean central pawn. Prepare the break with [w:Qe2] first. [%csl Rd1,Re2][%cal Gd7b6] }
9... Nb6 10. Bd3 Bxc3 ) ) ( 8... Nc6 ) ( 8... b6 ) 9. exd4
{ The Karpov Variation, long one of Black's most reliable equalizers at the top level. White carries an isolated d4-pawn, which provides central space, open lines for the c1-bishop and rooks, and kingside attacking chances with [w:Bg5] or [w:Ne5], yet turns into a fixed target if minor pieces come off. Black plays [b6] or [Nc6], placing the bishop on b7, a knight on d7 or c6, and rooks on c8 and d8 to blockade d5. [%csl Yb7,Yd7,Yc6][%cal Gb7b6] }
9... b6 ( 9... Nc6 ) *

[Event "Nimzo-Indian and Queen's Indian (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 ( 2. Nf3 e6
{ A flexible Indian move order: Black opens the f8-bishop's diagonal while keeping [b:d5], [b:b6] and [b:c5] ready. White's main choice is [c4], entering the Anti-Nimzo-Indian crossroads where Black picks between the Queen's Indian, Bogo-Indian and Queen's Gambit Declined. [Bf4] is the London System, [Bg5] is the Torre Attack, [g3] heads for a Catalan or Reti setup, and [e3] builds a quiet Colle/Zukertort frame. [%cal Gc2c4] }
( 2... b6
{ The Pseudo-Queen's Indian Defense: because White already committed the g1-knight to f3, White cannot blunt the b7-bishop with a quick [w:f3] and [w:e4] pawn roller, making [b6] completely sound on move two. [c4] transposes into the Queen's Indian after [b:e6], [g3] fianchettoes to oppose b7 along the long diagonal (leading to Nimzowitsch's Marienbad System if Black plays [g3 Bb7 Bg2 c5]), and [Bf4] or [Bg5] develops the queen's bishop outside the chain. [%csl Yf3,Yb7][%cal Gc2c4] }
3. c4 ( 3. g3 Bb7 4. Bg2
{ Both sides fianchetto to contest the long light-squared diagonal before committing their central pawns. Black's classic hypermodern try is [c5], the Marienbad System, attacking d4 from the flank to trade off a wing pawn for White's central pawn; [e6] is the solid alternative, usually transposing into the main-line Queen's Indian once White plays [w:c4] and [w:O-O], while [g6] sets up a symmetrical double fianchetto. [%csl Yd4][%cal Ge7e6] }
4... e6 ( 4... c5 ) ( 4... g6 ) ) ( 3. Bf4 ) ( 3. e3 Bb7 4. Bd3 e6
{ A popular Colle-Zukertort tabiya reached via the Pseudo-Queen's Indian move order. Both bishops eye the opposing kingsides across the board, and White chooses between [O-O] (keeping [w:c4] and [w:Nc3] open to transpose into the Spassky Queen's Indian), [Nbd2] supporting a direct [w:e4] central push or a Pillsbury-style [w:Ne5] outpost, and [b3] for a double fianchetto while Black counters with [b:c5] or [b:d5]. [%cal Gb1d2] }
5. Nbd2 ( 5. O-O
{ A Colle-Zukertort setup against the Queen's Indian: White has developed the kingside and castled without committing the c-pawn yet, often planning [w:Nbd2], [w:b3], [w:Bb2] and [w:c4] (or [w:Qe2] and [w:e4]). Black should strike at the center with [c5] or [d5] (or develop [Be7] calmly so g5 is covered before castling), keeping the queen's knight ready for d7 rather than blocking the b7-bishop on c6. [%csl Yg5,Yd7,Yc6][%cal Gc7c5] }
5... c5 ( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... d5 ) ) ( 5. b3 ) ) ( 3. Bg5 ) ) 3. c4 ( 3. Bf4 ) (
3. g3 ) ( 3. Bg5 ) ( 3. e3 b6 4. Bd3 Bb7 5. O-O Be7 6. c4 O-O 7. Nc3
{ By delaying [c4] and [Nc3] until both kings are castled, White reaches the [e3] Queen's Indian without ever allowing a Nimzo-Indian pin on c3, and now threatens [w:e4] to claim the full center. Black has to contest e4 and d5 right away with [d5] (or [c5] to undermine d4 into hanging pawns); slow flank moves let White roll [w:e4] forward without opposition. [%csl Yc3,Ye4,Yd5][%cal Gd7d5] }
7... d5 ( 7... c5 ) ( 7... h6 $2
{ Preventing [w:Bg5] with [h6] wastes a vital tempo when White is poised to seize the center: [e4] establishes a strong pawn trio on c4, d4 and e4, cramping Black on three ranks after [e4 d6 Bc2]. Contest e4 immediately with [b:d5] instead. [%csl Rc4,Rd4][%cal Ge3e4] }
8. e4 d6 9. Bc2 ) ) ) 2... e6
{ Black opens the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop without committing a pawn to d5 yet, keeping maximum flexibility on move three. If White plays [Nc3] to prepare [w:e4], Black can pin the knight with [b:Bb4] in the Nimzo-Indian rather than settle for a standard Queen's Gambit Declined. To sidestep that pin, White often chooses [Nf3], inviting the Queen's Indian ([b:b6]) or Bogo-Indian ([b:Bb4+]), or [g3], steering the game into the Catalan. Quiet [e3] is playable too and usually meets [b:b6] or [b:d5]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 2... b6
{ The Accelerated Queen's Indian Defense: Black prepares to fianchetto on b7 on move two without playing [b:e6] first. The catch is that with no pawn on e6, Black has no pin on b4 to stop White from seizing the center. White's critical test is [Nc3], preparing [w:Qc2] or [w:f3] followed by [w:e4]; [Nf3] is calmer and transposes into the normal Queen's Indian after [Nf3 e6], while [f3] prepares [w:e4] directly. [%csl Yb7,Ye6,Yb4][%cal Gb1c3] }
3. Nc3 ( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. f3 ) 3... Bb7
{ Black places the bishop on b7 to watch e4 immediately, before White can push the e-pawn for free. White needs to prepare that advance first: [Qc2] supports e4 with the queen so [b:e6] is met by [w:e4], [f3] bolsters e4 with a pawn while blunting the b7-bishop, and [d5] shuts the long diagonal at once ([Nf3] transposes after [b:e6]). Pushing [w:e4] without support drops a central pawn. [%csl Yb7,Ye4][%cal Gd1c2] }
4. Qc2 ( 4. f3 ) ( 4. d5 ) ( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. e4 $2
{ Rushing with [e4] overlooks that Black has two pieces — both the f6-knight and the b7-bishop — aimed at e4, while White has only the c3-knight defending it. [Nxe4] wins a clean central pawn after [Nxe4 Nxe4 Bxe4]. Support e4 first with [w:Qc2] or [w:f3]. [%csl Re4][%cal Gf6e4] }
4... Nxe4 5. Nxe4 Bxe4 ) ) 3. Nc3
{ White brings the queen's knight to its natural post, adding control over d5 and threatening [w:e4] next turn to claim the entire center. Black has two classic ways to prevent [w:e4]: [Bb4], the Nimzo-Indian Defense, pins the knight to the king so it can no longer support the advance, while [d5] holds e4 with a pawn and transposes to the Queen's Gambit Declined. [c5 d5] steers the game into the Modern Benoni. [%csl Yd5,Ye4][%cal Gf8b4] }
( 3. Nf3 b6
{ The Queen's Indian Defense, introduced by Aron Nimzowitsch in 1914 and long the sister opening of the Nimzo-Indian in the repertoires of Karpov, Anand and Kramnik. Because White played [Nf3] to sidestep the pin on c3, e4 is not yet supported: Black uses that moment to fianchetto the queen's bishop on b7 or a6 and control the light squares e4 and d5 with pieces. White's main replies are [g3], [a3] and [Nc3]. [%csl Yc3,Ye4,Yb7][%cal Gg2g3] }
( 3... Bb4+
{ The Bogo-Indian Defense, introduced by Efim Bogoljubow in the 1920s. When [Nf3] sidesteps the Nimzo-Indian pin, Black checks on b4 anyway. The strategic point is to trade off (or activate) Black's dark-squared bishop before placing central pawns on dark squares (d6 and e5), leaving Black's remaining light-squared bishop unobstructed. White's three good replies are [Bd2], [Nbd2], and [Nc3]; blocking with the queen hangs her on the spot. [%csl Yb4,Yd6,Ye5][%cal Gc1d2] }
4. Bd2
{ Main line of the Bogo-Indian: White blocks the check and asks the b4-bishop its intentions. Black has a rich menu of five replies: [Qe7] (Nimzowitsch's flexible choice, keeping the tension), [Bxd2+] (Ulf Andersson's solid Exchange Variation), [a5] (the Wade-Smyslov, defending b4 while grabbing queenside space), [c5] (Vītoliņš's strike, accepting doubled b-pawns after [c5 Bxb4 cxb4] to take c3 away from White's knight), and [Be7] (the Retreat). [%csl Yb4,Yc3][%cal Gd8e7] }
( 4. Nbd2
{ The Grünfeld Variation of the Bogo-Indian: White blocks with the knight so that [w:a3] will win the bishop pair without allowing doubled pawns, at the cost of temporarily shutting in the c1-bishop and placing the knight on modest d2 rather than c3. Black answers comfortably with [O-O], [b6] (planning [b6 a3 Bxd2+ Qxd2 Bb7]), or [d5] (a favorable QGD where White's knight sits passively on d2). [%csl Yd2,Yc3][%cal Ge8g8] }
4... O-O ( 4... d5 ) ( 4... b6 ) ( 4... c5 ) ) ( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. Qd2 $4
{ Blocking the check with [Qd2] is a costly oversight: [Bxd2+ Bxd2 Ne4] wins White's queen for a bishop right away. Block with [w:Bd2], [w:Nbd2] or [w:Nc3] instead. [%cal Gb4d2] }
4... Bxd2+ 5. Bxd2 Ne4 ) 4... Qe7
{ Nimzowitsch's [Qe7], today's main line of the Bogo-Indian. Black defends the b4-bishop without trading yet (note that [Bxb4] achieves nothing for White, as [Bxb4 Qxb4+] forks b2 and c4 and forces [Qd2]). White's best setup is [g3], fianchettoing the light-squared bishop on g2, which Black meets with [b:Nc6]—ready to trade on d2 or c3 and build a sturdy dark-square center with [b:d6] and [b:e5]. [%csl Yb2,Yc4,Yg2][%cal Gg2g3] }
( 4... Bxd2+
{ The Exchange Variation, favored by endgame virtuoso Ulf Andersson. Black trades dark-squared bishops immediately to simplify the game and avoid space headaches. How should White recapture? [Qxd2] is the master choice: although developing the knight with [Nbxd2] is very common at club level, recapturing with the queen keeps the prime c3 square open for White's b1-knight to fight for e4 and d5. [%csl Yc3,Ye4,Yd5][%cal Gd1d2] }
5. Qxd2
{ By recapturing with the queen, White keeps c3 open for the queen's knight and prepares [w:Nc3] followed by [w:e4] or [w:g3] and [w:Bg2]. Black chooses between [d5] (a solid Queen's Gambit Declined without dark-squared bishops), [d6] followed by [b:Nbd7] and [b:e5] (placing pawns on dark squares to restrict White's f1-bishop), [O-O], and [b6] fianchettoing on b7 (where [b:Ne4] and [b:Nxc3] can lead to the Monticelli Trap [w:Ng5], though Black gets active compensation). [%csl Yc3,Yb7][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 5. Nbxd2 ) 5... d5 ( 5... b6 ) ( 5... O-O ) ( 5... d6 ) ) ( 4... a5
{ The Wade-Smyslov Variation, also favored by David Bronstein. Black defends the b4-bishop with the a-pawn and claims queenside space: trading on b4 would open the a-file for Black's rook and let the b4-pawn take c3 away from White's knight. Instead White plays [g3] followed by [w:Bg2] and [w:O-O] (or [Nc3] or [e3]), while Black builds a dark-square center with [b:d6], [b:Nbd7] and [b:e5]. [%csl Yb4,Yc3][%cal Gg2g3] }
5. g3 ( 5. Nc3 ) ( 5. e3 ) ) ( 4... c5
{ The Vītoliņš Variation: Black strikes at d4 immediately and invites [Bxb4 cxb4]. Although Black's b-pawns are doubled, the advanced pawn on b4 is an annoying thorn that denies c3 to White's knight while Black sets up a dark-square grip with [b:d6], [b:Nc6] or [b:Na6], and [b:e5] (or plays [dxc5 Bxc5] for open piece play). White usually takes [Bxb4] and undermines b4 later with [w:a3], or fianchettoes with [g3]. [%csl Yd4,Yb4,Yc3][%cal Gd2b4] }
5. Bxb4 ( 5. g3 ) ( 5. dxc5 ) ) ( 4... Be7
{ The Retreat Variation: Black drops the bishop back to e7, arguing that White's bishop stands clumsily on d2 (blocking the d-file and taking d2 away from the knight, whereas against a normal QGD it prefers g5 or f4). White answers with [g3], transposing into the trendy Bogo-Catalan after [g3 d5 Bg2 O-O O-O], or [Nc3] inviting [Nc3 d5 Bf4] now that d2 can redeploy. [%csl Ye7,Yd2,Yg5][%cal Gg2g3] }
5. g3 ( 5. Nc3 ) ) 5. g3 ( 5. Nc3 ) ( 5. e3 ) ) ( 3... c5
{ With White sidestepping the Nimzo-Indian via [Nf3], Black challenges the center immediately with [c5]. White faces a fundamental structural choice: [d5] grabs central space and steers the game into the Modern Benoni (or the Blumenfeld Gambit after [d5 b5]), where White's central wedge battles Black's queenside pawn play. Alternatively, [Nc3] invites an Open Sicilian in reverse or a Semi-Tarrasch after [Nc3 cxd4 Nxd4 d5], while [e3] keeps a solid symmetrical frame ready for [e3 d5]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd4d5,Gb1c3] }
4. d5
{ White pushes past c5 to seize space on d5 and cramp Black's pieces. Because the white knight is already on f3 (ruling out the sharpest [w:f4] Four Pawns / Taimanov setups), Black gets very comfortable Benoni counterplay: [exd5 cxd5 d6] enters the Modern Benoni with an asymmetrical pawn structure, while [d6] delays the capture to keep options open. The enterprising [b5] is the Blumenfeld Countergambit, sacrificing a wing pawn to build a massive mobile center after [b5 cxb5 exd5] or [b5 dxe6 fxe6]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Ge6d5,Gb7b5] }
( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. e3 ) 4... exd5
{ Black trades on d5 to clear the e-file for a future rook on e8 and establish the classic Modern Benoni imbalance: White gets a central pawn majority (e-pawn and d5-pawn), while Black gets an active queenside majority (a-, b-, and c-pawns) backed by a fianchettoed dark-squared bishop on g7. White recaptures with [cxd5] to keep the space wedge intact and fight for the e4-d5 pawn duo; gambiting the pawn with [Bg5] or [Bf4] lets [Bg5 dxc4] keep a clean extra pawn. [%csl Ye8,Yg7][%cal Gc4d5] }
( 4... d6 ) ( 4... b5 ) 5. cxd5
{ Recapturing [cxd5] sets up the trademark Modern Benoni structure while preventing Black's light-squared bishop from ever developing smoothly along the c8-h3 diagonal without preparation. Black almost always plays [d6] now to halt [w:d6] forever and prepare [d6 Nc3 g6] for the dark-square fianchetto. Starting with [g6] immediately runs into the annoying [g6 d6], burying Black's queenside, while the tricky Snake Benoni [Bd6] aims to reroute the bishop to c7 and a5 before pushing d6. [%csl Yd6,Yg7][%cal Gd7d6] }
5... d6
{ With [d6] stopping White's d-pawn and anchoring c5 and e5, the Modern Benoni battleground is set: White plays for [w:e4] and a central breakthrough with [w:e5], while Black eyes the half-open e-file, the long h8-a1 diagonal, and the [b:b5] queenside expansion. White usually develops [Nc3] (leading to the Classical [Nc3 g6 e4] or Knight's Tour [Nc3 g6 Nd2]), pushes [e4] right away after [h3] or directly, or chooses the positional Fianchetto Variation with [g3]. [%csl Yb5,Yg7][%cal Gb1c3,Ge2e4] }
( 5... Bd6 ) ( 5... g6 ) 6. Nc3
{ Both White knights are out, defending d5 and preparing [w:e4] to claim full central control. Black's signature move is [g6], putting the dark-squared bishop on g7 where it rakes across e5, d4 and b2 to fuel [b:b5] counterplay. Developing [a6] first hurries [a6 a4 Bg4] or [b:b5] before fianchettoing (though [a6 a4] clamps b5), while the Old Benoni-style [Be7] aims for a positional dark-square trade via [b:Bg4] and [b:Bxf3] without weakening the kingside. [%csl Yg7,Yb5][%cal Gg7g6,Ge2e4] }
( 6. e4 ) ( 6. g3 ) 6... g6 ( 6... Be7 ) ( 6... a6 ) 7. e4 Bg7 8. Bg5
{ The Averbakh-Grivas Attack ([Bg5]) pins the f6-knight before Black has castled, directly fighting against Black's half-open e-file play and threatening [w:Nd2] followed by [w:Nc4] against the backward d6-pawn. Black should respond energetically with [h6]: after [h6 Bh4 g5 Bg3 Nh5], Black chases the bishop and wins the two bishops with dynamic dark-square counterplay (even [h6 Bf4 g5] works well!). [a6] preparing [b:b5] and [b:Bg4] is the main alternative, whereas a routine [O-O] lets [O-O Nd2] clamp the position. [%csl Rd6,Yf6][%cal Gh7h6,Ga7a6] }
8... h6 ( 8... Bg4 ) ( 8... a6 ) ) ( 3... d5 ) 4. g3 ( 4. Nc3
{ The Kasparov Variation: after sidestepping the Nimzo-Indian on move three, White develops [Nc3] anyway once Black has spent a tempo on b6. Black has two sound replies: [Bb4] transposes into the Three Knights Nimzo-Indian where b6 prepares [b:Bb7], while [Bb7] stays in the Queen's Indian, ready to answer [Bb7 a3] with [b:d5] or [Bb7 Bg5] with [b:Be7] or [b:h6]. Putting the bishop on a6 fails to control e4. [%csl Yb6,Ya6,Ye4][%cal Gc8b7] }
4... Bb7
{ Black declines the Nimzo-Indian transposition and places the bishop directly on b7 to control e4. White now sets the direction of the game: [a3] reaches the Petrosian Variation while having avoided [b:Ba6] and [b:c5] on move four, [Bg5] pins the f6-knight to contest e4 in the Classical E13 system, and [e3], [g3] or [Qc2] steer into quieter channels. Pushing [w:e4] right away drops a pawn. [%csl Yb7,Ye4][%cal Ga2a3] }
( 4... Bb4 ) ( 4... Ba6 $2
{ Unlike against [w:g3], playing [Ba6] against [Nc3] leaves e4 under-defended. White pushes [e4] at once, sacrificing nothing because capturing on c4 is met by [w:Bxc4] while White dominates the center and the a6-bishop sits offside. Play [b:Bb7] or [b:Bb4] instead. [%csl Re4,Rc4][%cal Ge2e4] }
5. e4 d6 6. Bd3 ) 5. a3 ( 5. Bg5
{ The Classical E13 Queen's Indian: White pins the f6-knight to weaken Black's grip on e4 and threaten [w:e4] next turn. Black prevents [w:e4] solidly with [Be7], breaking the pin so [Be7 e3 O-O Bd3 d5] reaches a Tartakower-style middlegame, or inserts [h6] first ([h6 Bh4 Be7], or Botvinnik's [h6 Bh4 g5 Bg3 Nh5], winning the bishop pair at the cost of kingside space). [Bb4] transposes to the Nimzo-Indian hybrid. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf8e7] }
5... Be7 ( 5... h6
{ Black puts the question to the g5-bishop before deciding how to defend e4 in the Kasparov-Classical Queen's Indian. Retreating [Bh4] is White's principled test, maintaining the pin on f6 so Black has to choose between solid [Bh4 Be7] and Botvinnik's sharp [Bh4 g5]; trading [Bxf6] hands Black the bishop pair without compensation after [Bxf6 Qxf6], when Black controls e4 and the long diagonal effortlessly. [%csl Ye4,Yf6][%cal Gg5h4] }
