{ White tucks the bishop back to h4 while keeping the f6-knight pinned. Black chooses between three distinct systems: [Be7] breaks the pin solidly, ready to meet [Be7 Qc2] with [b:c5] and [b:bxc5] for half-open b-file play or [Be7 e3] with [b:Ne4] (note White cannot force [w:e4] after [Be7], as [b:Nxe4] wins a pawn); [g5] launches the sharp Botvinnik Attack [g5 Bg3 Nh5]; and [Bb4] transposes to the Nimzo-Indian. [%csl Yh4][%cal Gf8e7] }
( 6. Bxf6 ) 6... Be7 ( 6... g5 7. Bg3
{ The Botvinnik Attack: Black has expanded boldly on the kingside to break the pin on f6, accepting dark-square weaknesses in exchange for hunting down White's g3-bishop. Black's essential follow-up is [Nh5], trading off the bishop after [Nh5 e3 Nxg3 hxg3 Bg7] (where Black often castles queenside or keeps the king flexible) or pinning on b4 with [Bb4]. Routine [Nc6] walks straight into [w:d5]. [%csl Yf6,Yb4][%cal Gf6h5] }
7... Nh5 ( 7... Bb4 ) ( 7... Nc6 $2
{ Developing [Nc6] after weakening the kingside with [b:h6] and [b:g5] walks straight into [d5], chasing the knight off c6, shutting in the b7-bishop, and opening central lines while Black's king has no safe home on either wing. Play [b:Nh5] or [b:Bb4] instead. [%csl Rc6][%cal Gd4d5] }
8. d5 Nb4 9. e3 ) ) ( 6... Bb4 ) ) ( 5... Bb4 ) ) ( 5. e3 ) ( 5. g3 ) ( 5. Qc2
) ( 5. e4 $2
