{ Developing [Nf3] before [w:Bd3] keeps the f1-bishop from losing a tempo if Black ever waits for it to move before taking on c4, while guarding d4 and e5 right away. Black's main reply is [d5], steering into a cross between the Rubinstein Nimzo-Indian and the Ragozin where White picks between [w:Bd3], [w:Bd2] and [w:Qc2]. [c5] challenges d4 directly, [b6] fianchettoes on b7 to watch e4, and [Bxc3+] doubles White's c-pawns early. [%csl Yc4,Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gd7d5] }
5... d5
{ This position bridges the Rubinstein Nimzo-Indian and the Ragozin Defense. With both knights developed, White's main continuation is [Bd3], heading into the Gligorić tabiya where [b:c5] creates full central pawn tension. [Bd2] breaks the pin on c3 so [w:a3] or [w:Rc1] will never allow doubled pawns, [Qc2] guards c3 with the queen while eyeing h7, and [a3] puts the question to the b4-bishop immediately. [%csl Yc3,Yh7][%cal Gf1d3] }
( 5... c5 ) ( 5... b6 ) ( 5... Bxc3+ ) 6. Bd3 ( 6. Bd2
{ White unpins the c3-knight with the bishop, preparing [w:Rc1] and threatening to trade on d5 or chase the b4-bishop with [w:a3] without allowing doubled pawns. Black's best replies are [b6], developing the c8-bishop to b7 to watch e4, [Be7], dropping back before [w:a3] forces a trade, or [c5] to contest the center. Jumping into e4 forgets that c3 is no longer pinned and drops a piece. [%csl Yd5,Yb7,Ye4][%cal Gb7b6] }
