[Event "Catalan Opening"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 Nf6 ( 1... d5 2. c4 e6 3. g3
{ The Queen's Gambit Declined move order into the Catalan: White prepares to fianchetto on g2 before bringing out the g1-knight, offering c4 as a temporary gambit pawn. Black almost always develops [Nf6] to reach the main Catalan crossroads. Taking [dxc4] right away enters an Open Catalan where White either regains the pawn with [w:Qa4+] or develops [w:Bg2] and [w:Nf3] for rapid pressure down the long diagonal, while [c6] builds a Semi-Slav pawn wall. [%csl Yg2,Yc4][%cal Gg8f6] }
3... Nf6 ( 3... dxc4 ) ( 3... c6 ) ) 2. c4 e6 3. g3
{ The Catalan Opening, christened at the 1929 Barcelona tournament when Savielly Tartakower combined the Queen's Gambit center on d4 and c4 with a kingside fianchetto on g2, and later a World Championship staple of Kramnik, Anand and Carlsen. From g2 the bishop slices down the long light-squared diagonal toward b7 and a8 while White sidesteps both the Nimzo-Indian and Queen's Indian. Black's main answer is [d5]; [Bb4+] is the Bogo-Catalan, and [c5 d5] steers into a Fianchetto Benoni. [%csl Yd4,Yc4,Yg2][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 3. Nf3 d5 ( 3... b6 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 Be7 6. Nc3
{ The Classical Fianchetto Queen's Indian with an early [Nc3], bridging Catalan and Queen's Indian territory. Bringing the knight to c3 before castling fights directly for e4 and d5, threatening [w:Qc2] or [w:Re1] followed by [w:e4], though it exposes the knight to an immediate jump. Black's sharpest equalizer is [Ne4], trading knights or forcing [Ne4 Bd2 Bf6] to challenge d4, while [O-O] (allowing [O-O Qc2] or [O-O O-O]) and [d5] (transposing toward Catalan/QGD structures) are rock-solid. [%cal Gf6e4,Gg2b7,Gd7d5,Ge2e4] }
6... Ne4 ( 6... O-O ) ( 6... d5 ) ) 4. g3 Be7
{ Reached over a million times via the standard [Nf3] move order, Black develops the bishop to e7 and prepares short castling before deciding whether to capture on c4 or build a Closed Catalan wall with [b:c6]. Because White's knight is already on f3, early [w:Qa4+] lines are off the table if Black later takes c4. White completes the fianchetto with [Bg2], heading into the main Catalan tabiya after [Bg2 O-O O-O]. [%csl Ye7,Yc4,Yf3][%cal Gf1g2] }
( 4... Bb4+
{ With d5 and f3 already on the board, Black checks on b4 to disrupt White's smooth Catalan coordination before White can fianchetto and castle. White's main reply is [Bd2], challenging the bishop at the small cost of occupying the b1-knight's favorite d2 square. [Nbd2] preserves the bishop pair if Black trades on d2, while [Nc3] invites Ragozin-style play after [Nc3 O-O Bg2 dxc4]. Blocking with the queen is a catastrophic mouseslip. [%csl Yd5,Yf3,Yb4][%cal Gc1d2] }
5. Bd2 ( 5. Nbd2 ) ( 5. Nc3 ) ( 5. Qd2 $4
{ Interposing the queen on d2 hangs her outright to [Bxd2+], leaving White a queen for a bishop down on move five. Block the check with [w:Bd2], [w:Nbd2] or [w:Nc3] instead. [%csl Rd2][%cal Gb4d2] }
5... Bxd2+ 6. Nbxd2 O-O ) 5... Be7
{ The Bogo-Catalan Retreat: having lured White's queen's bishop onto d2 — where it blocks the d-file and takes d2 away from the b1-knight — Black calmly drops the bishop back to e7. Although Black spent two moves with the bishop, White will usually spend a move later redeploying the d2-bishop to f4 or c3. White continues with [Bg2] (or [Qc2] first), heading for [Bg2 O-O O-O c6]. [%csl Yd2,Ye7,Yf4][%cal Gf1g2] }
6. Bg2 ( 6. Qc2 ) 6... O-O
{ Black castles into safety in the Bogo-Catalan Retreat, ready to meet White's castling with [b:c6] or [b:Nbd7] (or switch plans with [b:dxc4] while White's bishop on d2 blocks the queen from recapturing). White almost always castles short with [O-O], though [Qc2] first is a common move-order finesse that protects c4 before castling so Black never gets a free Open Catalan capture on c4. [%csl Yd2,Yc4][%cal Ge1g1] }
7. O-O ( 7. Qc2 ) 7... c6
{ Black completes the Semi-Slav pawn triangle in the Bogo-Catalan Retreat, blunting White's g2-bishop along the long diagonal and threatening [b:dxc4] now that b5 is supported. White's main move is [Qc2], protecting c4 ahead of [Qc2 Nbd7 Bf4 b6] when the d2-bishop finds an active home on f4 (or c3) and clears d2 for the b1-knight to support [w:e4]. [Bf4] and [b3] are solid positional alternatives. [%csl Yb5,Yc4,Yf4][%cal Gd1c2] }
8. Qc2 ( 8. Bf4 ) ( 8. b3 ) ) ( 4... h6
{ A subtle modern sideline tried at super-grandmaster level: Black plays [h6] early to rule out [w:Bg5] pins before developing the dark-squared bishop actively to d6 instead of passive e7. After [Bg2 Bd6 O-O O-O], Black aims either for [b:Nc6], [b:dxc4] and an active [b:e5] central break (impossible when White could pin on g5) or a solid fianchetto with [b:b6], [b:Bb7] and [b:Nbd7]. [%csl Yd6,Ye7,Yg5][%cal Gf1g2] }
5. Bg2 ) 5. Bg2 ) 3... d5
{ Black plants a pawn firmly on d5, reaching the pure Catalan. Notice why White keeps the queen's knight on b1 instead of developing it to c3: in the Catalan that knight usually belongs on d2 or a3, where it can recapture on c4 and support the [w:e4] break without blocking the c-file or the queen on c2. White continues with [Bg2] or [Nf3], which transpose to the same main tabiya on the next move. [%csl Yd5,Yb1,Yc3][%cal Gf1g2] }
( 3... Bb4+
{ The Bogo-Catalan check, a practical grandmaster favorite for sidestepping main-line Catalan preparation before committing a pawn to d5. By checking on b4, Black lures White's dark-squared bishop onto d2, where it blocks the queen's view of d5 and takes the natural d2 square away from the b1-knight. White usually blocks with [Bd2]; [Nd2] fights to win the bishop pair after [w:a3], while [Nc3] transposes into the Romanishin Nimzo-Indian. [%csl Yd5,Yb4,Yd2][%cal Gc1d2] }
4. Bd2
{ White blocks the check and forces the b4-bishop to declare its plan at once. Black's most popular modern choice is [Be7]: retreating looks like a lost tempo, yet White's bishop sits clumsily on d2 once play continues [Be7 Bg2 d5 Nf3 O-O] into a Closed Catalan where the b1-knight lacks d2. [Qe7] defends the bishop in Bogo-Indian fashion, [Bxd2+] trades off the dark-squared bishops cleanly, [a5] grabs queenside space, and [c5] strikes at d4. [%csl Yd2,Yd4][%cal Gb4e7] }
( 4. Nd2 ) ( 4. Nc3 ) 4... Be7 ( 4... Qe7 ) ( 4... Bxd2+ ) ( 4... a5 ) ( 4...
c5 ) 5. Bg2 d5 6. Nf3
{ The Bogo-Catalan Retreat main line, frequent in top-level tournament play. Compared with the normal Closed Catalan, Black has invested two bishop moves to leave White's dark-squared bishop on d2, where it blocks pressure down the d-file and keeps the b1-knight off d2. Black castles short with [O-O] and follows up with [O-O O-O c6 Qc2 Nbd7], while White usually spends a move redeploying the bishop to [w:Bf4] or [w:Bc3] before preparing [w:e4]. [%csl Yd2][%cal Ge8g8] }
6... O-O ( 6... c6 ) ( 6... dxc4 ) ( 6... Nbd7 7. O-O O-O 8. Qc2
{ White places the queen on c2 to protect c4, clear d1 for a rook, and support a later [w:e4] break once the d2-bishop moves to f4 or c3. Here Black needs to shore up d5 first with [c6], completing the sturdy Semi-Slav pawn triangle before fianchettoing on the queenside with [b:b6] and [b:Bb7]. Preparing the fianchetto immediately leaves d5 without pawn support and lets White rip open the center and the long diagonal. [%csl Yc2,Yc4,Yd1][%cal Gc7c6] }
8... c6 ( 8... b6 $6
{ Playing [b6] before [b:c6] leaves d5 without pawn support: [cxd5] opens the c-file and the long diagonal at once, forcing Black into a cramped defense of c7 after [cxd5 exd5 Bf4] or ceding the center to [w:e4] after [b:Nxd5]. Solidify d5 with [b:c6] first. [%csl Rd5,Rc7][%cal Gc4d5] }
9. cxd5 exd5 10. Bf4 ) ) ) ( 3... c5
{ Black strikes at d4 before White's bishop reaches g2, steering away from standard Catalan structures. White's most ambitious reply is [d5], gaining central space and transposing after [d5 exd5 cxd5 d6] into the Fianchetto Modern Benoni, a solid positional system where White restrains Black's queenside pawn majority from g2. [Nf3] is the calmer path, reaching a Symmetrical English or Tarrasch structure after [Nf3 cxd4 Nxd4] with open lines in the center. [%csl Yd4,Yg2][%cal Gd4d5] }
4. d5 ( 4. Nf3 ) 4... exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 7. Bg2 Bg7 8. Nf3 O-O
{ The Fianchetto Variation of the Modern Benoni, often reached from a Catalan move order when Black strikes early with c5. Unlike sharp e4-based Benoni lines, White keeps the long h1-a8 diagonal open for the g2-bishop to neutralize Black's counterpart on g7 and discourage an unassisted [b:b5] break. White usually castles [O-O]—heading into the main tabiya where [O-O a6 a4] clamps down on Black's queenside majority before [w:Nd2] and [w:Nc4] eye the backward d6-pawn—or plays [Bf4] and [Nd2] directly. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gg2b7,Ga7a6,Gb7b5] }
9. O-O ( 9. Bf4 ) ( 9. Nd2 ) ) 4. Bg2
{ White places the Catalan bishop on g2, aiming through d5 straight at b7 and the rook on a8. Black now faces the central strategic fork of the opening: [dxc4] enters the Open Catalan, winning a pawn at the price of unleashing White's g2-bishop down the open diagonal; [Be7] enters the Closed Catalan, keeping d5 defended; [Bb4+] checks to misplace White's queen's bishop on d2; [c6] builds a Semi-Slav wall; and [c5] counterattacks in Tarrasch style. [%csl Yg2,Yd5,Yb7][%cal Gf8e7] }
( 4. Nf3 Bb4+ ( 4... c6
{ Black builds the Semi-Slav pawn triangle on move four, blunting White's future g2-bishop along the h1-a8 diagonal before committing the dark-squared bishop. That move order keeps two distinct plans alive: Black can either capture [Bg2 dxc4] and hold the extra pawn with [b:b5], or play a solid Closed Catalan with [b:Be7] and [b:Nbd7]. White usually plays [Bg2], while [Qc2] guards c4 first to rule out the Open capture altogether. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gf1g2] }
5. Bg2
{ With both c6 and g2 in place, Black picks between two well-tested structures. [Be7], [Nbd7] and [Bd6] keep the center closed, fortifying d5 while accepting a passive light-squared bishop on c8 that will need [b:b6] and [b:Bb7] later. [dxc4] enters the Semi-Slav Open Catalan instead, grabbing the pawn while c6 already shields b7 and a8 so [b:b5] can follow after White castles. [%csl Yc6,Yg2,Yd5][%cal Gf8e7] }
( 5. Qc2 ) 5... Be7 ( 5... dxc4 ) ( 5... Nbd7 ) ( 5... Bd6 ) ) 5. Bd2
{ When Black checks on b4 with d5 already on the board, blocking with [Bd2] reaches a major Bogo-Catalan crossroads. [Be7] drops back now that White's bishop occupies d2, heading for a Closed setup after [Be7 Bg2 O-O O-O c6]. [Bxd2+] trades dark-squared bishops at once, where [Bxd2+ Nbxd2] eyes a future c4-pawn and [Bxd2+ Qxd2] keeps c3 free for the knight, while [a5] and [Qe7] maintain the tension. [%csl Yb4,Yd5,Yd2][%cal Gb4e7] }
5... Be7 ( 5... Bxd2+ ) ( 5... a5 ) ( 5... Qe7 ) 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O
{ The main tabiya of the Bogo-Catalan Retreat after both sides castle. White's bishop on d2 temporarily blocks the d-file and keeps the b1-knight off d2, justifying Black's bishop retreat. Black usually builds the Closed triangle with [c6] (or plays [Nbd7] first ahead of [b:c6] and [b:b6], while [dxc4] enters an Open line), and White answers with [w:Qc2] before shifting the d2-bishop to f4 or c3. [%csl Yd2,Yf4,Yc3][%cal Gc7c6] }
7... c6 ( 7... Nbd7 ) ( 7... dxc4 ) ) 4... Be7
{ Black develops the dark-squared bishop calmly to e7, keeping two major Catalan systems open: Black can castle and build a Closed Catalan wall with [b:c6] and [b:b6], or castle first and capture [b:dxc4] into the Classical Open Catalan, a move order that neatly rules out White's early [w:Qa4+] check. White develops [Nf3] to protect d4, control e5, and prepare short castling on the next move. [%csl Ye7,Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gg1f3] }
( 4... dxc4
{ The Open Catalan. Black takes on c4 while White's light-squared bishop is already on g2 and cannot recapture in one move. In return, Black gives up the central stronghold on d5 and opens the long h1-a8 diagonal for White's Catalan bishop, which presses straight down on b7 and a8 and makes Black's queenside awkward to untangle. White's main move is [Nf3]; [Qa4+] regains the pawn safely at once, whereas [w:Qc2] drops d4. [%csl Yc4,Yg2,Yd5][%cal Gg1f3] }
5. Nf3
{ The main crossroads of the Open Catalan. White develops the knight to guard d4 and eye [w:Ne5], unleashing the g2-bishop against c6, b7 and a8, while daring Black either to cling to the extra pawn or return it for development. [a6] prepares [b:b5] safely, [Be7] prioritizes kingside castling in the Classical line, [c5] counterattacks d4, [Nc6] and [Nbd7] develop pieces, [Bb4+] checks to misplace White's bishop, [c6] shields the diagonal, and [b5] defends c4 directly. [%csl Yd4,Yc6,Yb7][%cal Gf8e7] }
( 5. Qa4+
{ The [Qa4+] Open Catalan, a clean practical choice when White wants to regain the c4-pawn right away without entering deep gambit lines. Black has three solid blocks: [Nbd7] is the most flexible, planning [Nbd7 Qxc4 a6 Qc2 c5] to challenge d4 and fianchetto on b7; [Bd7] invites [Bd7 Qxc4 Bc6], trading off White's strong g2-bishop along the long diagonal; and [c6] prepares [b:b5] with queenside counterplay. [%csl Yd4,Yb7][%cal Gb8d7] }
5... Nbd7 ( 5... Bd7 ) ( 5... c6 ) 6. Qxc4
{ White has regained the gambit pawn, trading the c-pawn for Black's d-pawn to keep a two-to-one central majority on d4 and e2 against e6. The drawback is that the queen stands exposed on c4, giving Black targets for queenside development: [a6] prepares [b:b5] with tempo followed by [b:Bb7] and the freeing [b:c5] break (which White often sidesteps in advance with [w:Qc2]), while [c5] challenges d4 immediately and [Be7] prepares castling. [%csl Yd4,Ye2,Ye6][%cal Ga7a6] }
6... a6 ( 6... c5 ) ( 6... Be7 ) ) ( 5. Qc2 $6
{ Moving the queen to c2 to regain c4 before developing [w:Nf3] leaves d4 undefended: [Qxd4] snatches a second central pawn, and after [Qxd4 Nf3 Qc5] Black keeps the extra material while White has to fight uphill for compensation. Develop [w:Nf3] first, or check with [w:Qa4+]. [%csl Rc2,Rc4][%cal Gd8d4] }
5... Qxd4 6. Nf3 Qc5 ) 5... Be7
{ The Classical Line of the Open Catalan (ECO E05). Instead of weakening the queenside early to hold onto c4, Black develops the kingside bishop to e7 and gets ready to castle short. Black's plan is to return the c4-pawn on favorable terms, oppose White's g2-bishop on b7 or c6, and equalize with a timely [b:c5] break against d4. White castles with [O-O] (usually followed by [O-O O-O Qc2]) or regains c4 with [Qa4+]. [%csl Yc4,Ye7,Yb7][%cal Ge1g1] }
( 5... a6
{ The modern main line of the Open Catalan: Black plays [a6] before pushing [b:b5] so the a8-rook is defended and never pinned along the a-file when White challenges the chain with [w:a4]. White usually castles with [O-O], keeping every central and queenside plan flexible. Immediate [a4] prevents [b:b5] outright at the price of ceding b4 after [a4 c5], while [Ne5] uncovers the g2-bishop early and is met by [b:Bb4+] or [b:c5]. [%csl Yb4][%cal Ge1g1] }
6. O-O
{ With White castled, Black chooses between three major setups. [Nc6] develops a piece first to pressure d4 and prepare [b:Bd7] or [b:Rb8] before expanding with [b:b5]. [b5] defends the extra c4-pawn right away, inviting the forcing theoretical line [b5 Ne5 Nd5 a4 Bb7] where White chips at the pawn chain before Black's kingside comes out. [Be7] prioritizes safe castling and transposes into Classical Open lines. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gb8c6] }
( 6. Ne5 ) ( 6. a4 ) 6... Nc6
{ The main tabiya of the [a6] Open Catalan: Black develops the queen's knight to hit d4 before committing to [b:b5], keeping [b:Rb8] and [b:Bd7] ready to neutralize the long diagonal. White's most popular plan is [e3], bolstering d4 ahead of [w:Qe2], [w:Rd1] and a permanent pawn sacrifice with [w:b3] for open queenside files. [Nc3] is the sharp tactical alternative, meeting [Nc3 Rb8] with [w:e4] and [w:d5], while [Nbd2] recovers c4 directly. [%csl Yd4,Yc4][%cal Ge2e3] }
( 6... b5
{ Black holds onto the extra pawn immediately, daring White to break down the light-square phalanx on a6, b5 and c4 before Black catches up in development. White's critical continuation is [Ne5], unleashing the g2-bishop against a8 to force [Ne5 Nd5] before chipping at the chain with [w:a4] (whereas playing [w:a4] or [w:b3] before [Ne5] lets [b:Bb7] defend a8 comfortably along the diagonal). [%csl Ya6,Yb5,Yc4][%cal Gf3e5] }
7. Ne5 ) ( 6... Be7 ) 7. e3
