{ White attacks c4 with the queen while preparing [w:Rd1] and [w:e4]. [a6] is the classical main line, threatening [b:b5] so White chooses between [a6 Qxc4 b5 Qc2 Bb7] and preventing the expansion with [a6 a4]. [b5] is the modern gambit popularized by Ian Nepomniachtchi in the 2021 World Championship, meeting [b5 a4] with [b:Bb7] (or [b:a6]) to sacrifice a queenside pawn for rapid piece activity; [c5] and [Nbd7] are solid alternatives. [%csl Yc4][%cal Ga7a6] }
( 7. Ne5 ) ( 7. Na3 ) 7... a6
{ The classical crossroads after [a6]: Black threatens [b:b5] to hold the extra pawn. [Qxc4] recovers the pawn at once, leading after [Qxc4 b5 Qc2 Bb7] to a rich strategic battle over the c5 square: if Black achieves [b:c5] cleanly Black equalizes, so White fights to clamp down on c5 with [w:Bd2], [w:Rd1], [w:Bg5] and knights on e5 or c5. [a4] has become the modern favorite, stopping [b:b5] outright and forcing [a4 Bd7 Qxc4 Bc6 Bg5]. [%csl Yc5,Ye5][%cal Gc2c4] }
( 7... b5 8. a4
