[Event "Grünfeld Defence"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 ( 2. Nf3 g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. O-O
c5 8. dxc5
{ Black has struck at d4 with [c5] before retreating the d5-knight, sacrificing a pawn temporarily to open both the long diagonal and the queenside files. [Na6] is the right follow-up, eyeing c5 while keeping the d-file open on White's queen, and [Nb4] jumps in with tempo toward d3 or a6. Blocking the c8-bishop with [e6] is a bad mistake that leaves the c5-pawn alive and Black's dark squares full of holes. [%csl Yd4,Yc5,Yd3][%cal Gb8a6,Bd5b4] }
8... Na6 ( 8... Nb4 ) ( 8... e6 $2
{ Playing [e6] shuts in the c8-bishop, weakens d6 and does nothing to win back the c5-pawn. White seizes the center with [e4], kicking the knight, and after [e4 Nb4 Qb3] White keeps the extra pawn with full control. Recover c5 actively with [b:Na6] instead. [%csl Yd6,Yc5][%cal Ge2e4,Yd1b3] }
9. e4 Nb4 10. Qb3 ) ) 2... g6 3. f3
{ The Alekhine Anti-Grünfeld. By propping up e4 with a pawn before touching the queenside knight, White rules out the knight exchange on c3 that powers the main Grünfeld lines. If Black still strikes with [d5], White trades [d5 cxd5 Nxd5] and claims the center with [w:e4], forcing the knight back to b6. Black can also pivot into a Benoni with [c5] or develop [Bg7], steering into the Sämisch King's Indian once White pushes [Bg7 e4] and Black answers with [b:d6]. [%csl Ye4,Yc3,Yb6][%cal Gd7d5,Bf8g7] }
( 3. h4
{ The Basman-Williams Attack, essayed by Michael Basman in 1980, revived by Simon Williams and brought into elite fashion after AlphaZero's h-pawn storms. Delaying [Nc3] takes the sting out of [d5] (since no knight stands on c3 to trade) while threatening [w:h5] to pry open the h-file or provoke [b:Nxh5]. Black's cleanest answers are [Bg7], ready to meet [Bg7 Nc3] with [b:d6] or [b:c5], [d6] for a King's Indian, or [c5] inviting a Benko after [c5 d5 b5]. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gf8g7,Bd7d6,Yc7c5] }
3... Bg7 ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... c5 ) ) ( 3. d5
{ The Advance Anti-Grünfeld shuts down [b:d5] on move three by occupying the square first. The drawback is that White commits the d-pawn before Black has even spent a move on [e6] or [c5], leaving e5 and c5 inviting for Black's pieces. Black develops [Bg7] and follows with [d6], [O-O] and either [c6] to chip away at d5 or [e6] to open the e-file and challenge White's overextended spearhead. [%csl Ye5,Yc5,Yd5][%cal Gf8g7,Bd7d6] }
3... Bg7 ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... b5 ) ) ( 3. g3
{ The Fianchetto System sidesteps both the classical King's Indian race and the main Exchange Grünfeld by putting the light-squared bishop on g2 first, where it defends d4 and e4 from safety and eyes Black's queenside. Black normally plays [Bg7], keeping both [b:d6] (Fianchetto King's Indian) and [b:d5] (Neo-Grünfeld) open, or strikes straight away with [d5] or [c6] to contest d4 before White's bishop reaches the long diagonal. [%csl Yg2,Yd4,Ye4][%cal Gf8g7,Bd7d5] }
3... Bg7
{ Black answers White's kingside fianchetto in kind, preparing to castle short before revealing whether the center will be contested in King's Indian style (with [b:d6] and [b:e5]) or Neo-Grünfeld style (with [b:d5]). White almost always completes the fianchetto with [Bg2], keeping both knights flexible on the back rank until Black commits a central pawn, though [Nc3] and [Nf3] are common transpositions. [%cal Gf1g2,Bb1c3,Yg1f3] }
( 3... d5
{ The Kemeri or Early Neo-Grünfeld: Black hits d4 and c4 before fianchettoing, asking White to clarify the center right away. White usually plays [Bg2] (or trades [cxd5 Nxd5] first and then [w:Bg2]), keeping tension along the long diagonal where the fianchetto bishops will cross swords. Because no knight stands on c3 yet, once White trades on d5 Black's recapturing knight cannot exchange on c3 and has to retreat to b6 or e7 against [w:e4]. [%csl Yd4,Yc4,Yc3][%cal Gf1g2,Bc4d5] }
4. Bg2 ( 4. cxd5 ) ( 4. Nf3 ) 4... Bg7 5. Nf3
{ The Symmetrical Fianchetto main tabiya of the Neo-Grünfeld. Both sides have completed their kingside fianchettoes while keeping full pawn tension between c4 and d5. Because White's knight stands on f3 rather than c3, Black cannot shatter White's queenside with an exchange on c3, so the game turns positional: Black usually castles [O-O] (inviting [O-O O-O cxd5 Nxd5]), bolsters d5 Slav-style with [c6], or grabs the pawn via [dxc4] to test whether White recovers it with [dxc4 Qa4+] or [dxc4 O-O]. [%cal Gg2d5,Gg7d4,Gc7c6,Gd5c4] }
5... O-O ( 5... c6 ) ( 5... dxc4 ) ) ( 3... c6 ) 4. Bg2 ( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. Nf3 )
4... d5
{ The Neo-Grünfeld Defence. With [Nc3] held back, White denies Black the tempo-gaining exchange on c3 and meets Black's central strike with a fianchetto of White's own. White has two main paths: [Nf3], keeping tension and heading for the symmetrical or pawn-grab tabiyas after both sides castle, and [cxd5], which forces [cxd5 Nxd5] and lets White choose between [w:Nf3] and an immediate [w:e4] chasing the knight to b6. [%csl Yc3,Yb6][%cal Gg1f3,Bc4d5] }
