{ White castles and dares Black to clarify the center. [c6] is Karpov's sturdy choice, supporting d5 with a pawn so Black keeps a foothold in the middle if White trades on d5, though it takes c6 away from the b8-knight. [dxc4] is the dynamic modern main line: Black surrenders the center for a moment to disrupt White's queenside and strike back with [b:c5], while [Nc6] hits d4 and c4 with pieces. [%csl Yd5,Yc6,Yd4][%cal Gc7c6,Bd5c4] }
( 6. cxd5 ) 6... c6 ( 6... dxc4
{ Black takes on c4 while White's b1-knight still blocks the c1-bishop and a1-rook. Since c4 is awkward to win back with the bishop, [Na3] is the principled move, aiming for [Nxc4] with a clean Catalan-like pull; [Qc2] and [Ne5] are sharp alternatives. Against [Na3], Black's key resource is [Na3 c3], returning the extra pawn after [Na3 c3 bxc3 c5] to leave White with a backward c3-pawn on an open file under the g7-bishop's gaze. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gb1a3,Bd1c2,Yf3e5] }
7. Na3 ( 7. Qc2 ) ( 7. Ne5 ) ) ( 6... Nc6 ) 7. cxd5 cxd5
{ The Symmetrical Neo-Grünfeld. With identical pawn chains and an open c-file, Black's camp looks rock-solid, yet White's small lead in time still counts along the c-file and on the e5 outpost. [Nc3] develops naturally toward [Bf4] and [Ne5], usually met by [Nc3 Nc6] and [b:Ne4] or [b:Bf5] to contest the dark squares. [Ne5] jumps straight into the outpost before Black's knight reaches c6, keeping the g2-bishop's diagonal wide open. [%csl Ye5,Yc6][%cal Gb1c3,Bf3e5] }
8. Nc3
{ The Ultra-Delayed Exchange tabiya of the Symmetrical Neo-Grünfeld, contested in several Karpov-Kasparov World Championship games. Although the symmetrical d4/d5 pawns look deceptively quiet, White holds a nagging first-move pull via [Bf4], [Ne5] and queenside pressure down the open c-file, so Black cannot just copy moves forever. [Nc6] is the classical main line (often met by [Nc6 Ne5]), while [Ne4] trades knights right away to break symmetry, and [Bf5] fights actively for e4. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 8. Ne5 Bf5 9. Nc3
{ Black has developed the light-squared bishop actively to f5, outside the pawn chain, before touching the e-pawn, while White pressures d5 with knight and bishop. Black's right continuation is [Nc6] or [Ne4], challenging White's central knights directly. Playing the automatic [e6] here is a notorious trap: with the bishop already outside on f5 and the knight on e5, White cuts off the bishop's retreat. [%csl Yf5,Yd5,Ye5][%cal Gb8c6,Bf6e4] }
