Bg7 ( 3... d5
{ The Grünfeld Defence, introduced to master play by Ernst Grünfeld in 1922 (defeating Alekhine at Vienna that year) and championed by Smyslov, Fischer, Kasparov and Svidler. Instead of allowing White a free hand in the center as in the King's Indian, Black strikes at d4 and c4 on move three. If White builds a broad pawn center with [cxd5], Black attacks it with [b:Bg7], [b:c5], [b:Nc6], [b:Qa5] and [b:Bg4]. White's main alternatives keep pieces on the board: [Nf3] (Three Knights and Russian), [Bf4], [Bg5] and quiet [e3]. [%csl Yd4,Yc4][%cal Gc4d5,Bg1f3,Ye2e3] }
4. cxd5
{ White accepts the Grünfeld challenge by trading on d5, clearing the way for [w:e4] to build a full two-pawn center. Black recaptures with the piece, [Nxd5], putting the knight right in the middle where White's [w:e4] advance can be met by [b:Nxc3]. That exchange pulls White's b-pawn to c3, opening the long diagonal for [b:Bg7] and giving Black a queenside pawn majority for the endgame. [%csl Yd5,Yc3][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 4. Nf3
{ The Three Knights Variation. White develops the kingside knight first, keeping tension on d5 and reserving the choice between the Russian System ([Bg7 Qb3]), the Petrosian ([Bg7 Bg5]), the Brinckmann/Hungarian ([Bg7 Bf4]), the Quiet [Bg7 e3], or a delayed Exchange ([Bg7 cxd5]). Black plays [Bg7], completing the fianchetto; note that [c6] crosses into the Schlechter Slav, while premature [Bg4] leaves b7 and d5 vulnerable to [Qb3]. [%csl Yd5,Yb7][%cal Gf8g7,Bc7c6] }
4... Bg7
{ The main crossroads of the Three Knights Grünfeld. Five distinct plans branch out for White: [Qb3] (the sharp Russian System, forcing [Qb3 dxc4 Qxc4] to build a big center without trading knights), [Bg5] (Petrosian's pin, testing d5 immediately), [Bf4] (the Hungarian or Delayed Brinckmann setup), [e3] (the solid Burille system), and [cxd5], which steers directly into the Modern Exchange Variation after [cxd5 Nxd5 e4 Nxc3 bxc3]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd1b3,Bc1g5,Ye2e3] }
( 4... c6 ) ( 4... Bg4 $2
{ Bringing the light-squared bishop out before fianchettoing leaves both b7 and d5 undefended. [Qb3 dxc4 Qxb7 Nbd7] hits both pawns at once and gives White a dangerous extra pawn and center. Play [b:Bg7] as planned. [%csl Yb7,Yd5][%cal Gd1b3] }
5. Qb3 dxc4 6. Qxb7 ) 5. Qb3
{ The Russian System, forged by Soviet theoreticians Ragozin, Botvinnik and Boleslavsky in the 1930s and 1940s. White puts a third attacker on d5, forcing Black to release the tension with [dxc4] (since [c6] leaves Black passive after [c6 cxd5 cxd5 Bf4]). In return for winning the full d4-e4 pawn center without doubled pawns, White's queen stands exposed on c4, giving Black targets for rapid piece counterplay. [%csl Yd5,Yc4][%cal Gd5c4,Bc7c6] }
( 5. Bg5
{ The Petrosian (or Taimanov) Variation. By pinning (or attacking) the f6-knight that guards d5, White threatens to win a central pawn without allowing doubled c-pawns. Just as in the Stockholm line, passive defense with [c6] lets White trade [c6 cxd5 cxd5] into a comfortable Carlsbad structure where Black's g7-bishop bites on granite, so [Ne4] is Black's active solution, hitting g5 and c3 simultaneously. [%csl Yd5,Yg5,Yc3][%cal Gf6e4,Bc7c6] }
5... Ne4
{ Black's knight leaps into e4, unleashing the g7-bishop down the long diagonal while attacking both g5 and c3. White has two sound ways on: [cxd5], when [cxd5 Nxg5 Nxg5 e6] regains the pawn via a discovered attack on g5 and leads to rich strategic play after [w:Nf3] ([b:exd5]), and [Bh4], keeping tension as Black trades [Bh4 Nxc3 bxc3 dxc4] followed by [w:e3] and [b:Be6] or [b:c5]. Taking d5 with the knight is a famous blunder. [%csl Ye4,Yg5,Yc3][%cal Gc4d5,Bg5h4] }
( 5... c6 ) 6. cxd5 ( 6. Bh4 ) ( 6. Nxd5 $4
{ Taking d5 with the knight walks into a classic double attack! After [Nxg5 Nxg5 e6], Black's e-pawn attacks the d5-knight while uncovering the queen's attack on the unprotected g5-knight, winning a full minor piece. Trade [w:cxd5] or retreat [w:Bh4] instead. [%csl Rd5,Rg5][%cal Ge4g5] }
6... Nxg5 7. Nxg5 e6 ) ) ( 5. e3
{ The Three Knights Quiet (Burille) Variation. With d4 firmly defended by both e3 and f3, White plans [w:Bd3] (or [w:Be2]), [w:O-O] and [w:Qb3] or [w:b3] for a controlled central squeeze. Black castles [O-O] and prepares [b:c5] to open the long diagonal and steer into an Isolated Queen's Pawn battle where the g7-bishop shines, while [c6] builds a sturdy Slav wall for slower maneuvering. [%csl Yd4,Ye3,Yf3][%cal Ge8g8,Bc7c6] }
5... O-O ( 5... c6 ) 6. Bd3
{ The Paris Variation of the Quiet Grünfeld. Posting the light-squared bishop on d3 eyes Black's kingside and supports a future [w:e4] push once the center opens, while clearing f1 for short castling. Black's thematic response is [c5], striking at d4 while the g7-bishop x-rays the center ([c5 O-O cxd4 exd4 Nc6] creates a classic Isolated Queen's Pawn fight); [dxc4 Bxc4 c5] and [c6] are sound alternatives. [%csl Yd3,Yf1,Yd4][%cal Gc7c5,Bd5c4] }
6... c5 ( 6... dxc4 ) ( 6... c6 ) ) ( 5. cxd5
{ Trading on d5 after [Nf3] and [Bg7] steers the Three Knights toward the Modern Exchange Variation while sidestepping Black's early fourth-move sidelines. Black recaptures with the knight, [Nxd5], standing ready to answer [Nxd5 e4] with [b:Nxc3] and an immediate [b:c5] before castling, or to meet Carlsen's quieter [Nxd5 Bg5] by retreating [b:Nb6] or striking at d4 with [b:c5] while knights remain on the board. [%csl Yd5,Yd4][%cal Gf6d5] }
