{ With the knight on f3 already developed, White chooses between three clean paths: [e4] forces [e4 Nxc3 bxc3] directly into the Modern Exchange tabiya (where Black must strike with [b:c5] before castling), while [Bg5] delays [e4] to keep knights on the board and discourage an immediate [b:c5] while e7 is unguarded. [Bd2] is Kramnik's solid third option, preparing [w:e4] without allowing doubled c-pawns. [%csl Yf3,Ye7][%cal Ge2e4,Bc1g5,Yc1d2] }
6. e4 ( 6. Bg5
{ A subtle modern move-order twist favoured by Carlsen and Aronian: White trades on d5 first and develops [Bg5] instead of pushing [e4], avoiding the c3-knight trade while discouraging [b:c5] while e7 is unguarded. Black strikes with [Nb6] (clearing the long diagonal and preparing [b:Nc6] or [b:Bg4]) or [c5] anyway, using [c5 Rc1 Nxc3 bxc3 O-O] to hit White's center before [e4] lands. [%csl Yd5,Ye7][%cal Gd5b6,Bc7c5,Yh7h6] }
6... Nb6 ( 6... c5 ) ( 6... h6 ) ) ( 6. Bd2 ) ) ( 5. Bf4
{ Developing [Bf4] inside the Three Knights gets the dark-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before [w:e3] while avoiding early [b:c5] and [b:Qa5] pins until Black has castled. Black castles [O-O], preparing [O-O e3 c5] (as in Donald Byrne vs Bobby Fischer, New York 1956) or [O-O Rc1 dxc4] to challenge White's center before White finishes kingside development, whereas [c6] builds a quieter Slav-style wall. [%cal Ge8g8,Bc7c6] }
5... O-O ( 5... c6 ) 6. e3
{ The Hungarian or Delayed Brinckmann Variation, famous from Donald Byrne vs Bobby Fischer (New York 1956), where the 13-year-old Fischer uncorked [c5 dxc5 Na6] and a legendary queen sacrifice. [c5] remains Black's sharpest and cleanest equalizer, striking at d4 while White's f1-bishop is still at home; [c6] is the solid alternative, turning the position into a Schlechter-Slav wall with [b:Be6] or [b:Bg4]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc7c5,Bc7c6] }
6... c5 ( 6... c6 ) ( 6... a6 $6
{ Preparing [b:b5] or [b:dxc4] with a slow flank move gives White a free tempo to clarify the center on favourable terms. [cxd5 Nxd5 Nxd5 Qxd5 Bxc7] snatches the c7-pawn a move ahead of normal gambit lines. Challenge the center at once with [b:c5] or bolster d5 with [b:c6]. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gc4d5,Bc3d5] }
