{ The Modern Exchange Variation, revitalized by Kramnik, Kasparov and Gelfand from the 1980s onward. Putting the knight on its natural f3 square controls e5 and d4 actively without exposing the f1-bishop to [b:Na5] or c-file x-rays. Note Black's accurate move order: strike immediately with [c5] before castling, since castling first lets [O-O Be2 c5 O-O] tuck White's king away a tempo faster so [b:Bg4] can be met by [w:d5] without check on c3. [%csl Yf3,Ye5,Yd4][%cal Gc7c5,Be8g8] }
