{ Black fires the essential [c5] break against d4, opening the long diagonal for the g7-bishop. White's whole setup is built around [Ne2], developing the knight where it cannot be pinned by [b:Bg4] and keeping f3 free for the f-pawn (if White mixes setups with [Nf3], Black castles first rather than rushing [b:Bg4], which would blunder to [w:Bxf7+] and [w:Ng5+]). Grabbing c5 loses straight away on the long diagonal. [%csl Yd4,Yf3,Yc5][%cal Gg1e2] }
( 7... O-O ) 8. Ne2
{ Spassky's Classical Exchange setup reaches its critical juncture: by placing the knight on e2 rather than f3, White defends both d4 and c3 without ever allowing an awkward [Bg4] pin, leaving [Be3], [w:O-O] and a later [f4]-[w:f5] bayonet attack ready to roll. Black responds with [O-O] or [Nc6] (the two usually transpose after [O-O O-O Nc6 Be3]), combining [cxd4], [Bg4] and [Na5] or [Qc7] to siege White's d4-center. [%csl Yc3,Yd4,Ye2][%cal Gg1e2] }
( 8. dxc5 $2
