{ In the Classical Exchange, castling before developing [b:Nc6] keeps Black's king tucked safely away while leaving the d8-a5 diagonal open for the queen. White castles [O-O] (or develops [Be3] first, inviting [Be3 Nc6 O-O] back into the main tabiya or Kramnik's [Be3 Qa5] and [Be3 cxd4 cxd4 Qa5+] queen checks). After [O-O Nc6 Be3], both sides reach the primary Classical battleground around White's d4-e4 center. [%cal Ge1g1,Bc1e3] }
( 8... Nc6 9. Be3 O-O 10. O-O
{ The main Classical Exchange tabiya, contested in World Championship matches from Botvinnik-Smyslov to Kasparov-Karpov and Kasparov-Kramnik. Black chooses between three grand plans: [Bg4] (provoking [Bg4 f3 Na5 Bd3 cxd4 cxd4 Be6] to soften White's dark squares while keeping the c8-diagonal open), Smyslov's [Qc7] (preparing [b:Rd8] and x-raying c4 along the c-file so White plays [w:Rc1]), and [b6] followed by [b:Bb7] and [b:Na5]. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gc8g4,Bd8c7,Yb7b6] }
10... Bg4 ( 10... Qc7 ) ( 10... b6 ) ( 10... cxd4 11. cxd4
{ The Spassky Exchange structure: Black liquidates c5 into d4 to activate the 2-vs-1 queenside pawn majority and open the c-file plus the c4 square for Black's pieces, while handing White a mobile central pawn duo. Black's sharpest continuation is [Bg4] ([Bg4 f3 Na5 Bd3 Be6], blockading the dark squares and inviting White's [w:d5] exchange sacrifice), while [b6] prepares [b:Bb7] and [b:Na5]. [%csl Yc5,Yd4,Yc4][%cal Gc8g4,Bb7b6] }
11... Bg4 ( 11... b6 ) ) 11. f3 Na5
{ Black's [Bg4] provoked [f3] (loosening White's dark squares slightly and blunting the g1-a7 diagonal), and [Na5] now hits the c4-bishop to clear the c-file. White has two famous replies: retreating [Bd3] to preserve the bishop and center, or Karpov's 1987 Seville Variation [Bxf7+ Rxf7 fxg4 Rxf1+ Kxf1], trading into an unbalanced fight where Black's queenside majority and c4 outpost battle White's extra kingside pawn. [%csl Yc4][%cal Gc4d3,Bc4f7] }
