{ The [Bb5+] Exchange Variation, a favorite of Carlsen and Aronian. Checking on b5 disrupts Black's smooth [b:c5] and [b:Nc6] rhythm by forcing a concession on c6 or d7: after the main reply [c6 Ba4 O-O Ne2], Black has spent a move on c6 and still has to prepare [b:c5] (or Svidler's [b:e5] and [b:b5]), while White's knight lands safely on e2 out of reach of [b:Bg4]. Blocking with [Bd7] trades light-squared bishops at the cost of queenside pressure. [%csl Yb5,Yc6,Yd7][%cal Gc7c6,Bc8d7] }
7... c6 ( 7... Bd7 ) ) 7... c5
{ With [c5] on the board before either side has castled, every tempo counts along the h8-a1 diagonal. White has four major weapons: [Rb1] (the sharpest modern main line, stepping off a1 and eyeing b7 so Black's c8-bishop cannot easily leave home), [Be3] (bolstering d4, met by [Be3 Qa5 Qd2]), [Bb5+] (disrupting Black's coordination), and Kramnik's [h3] (ruling out [b:Bg4]). Taking on c5 surrenders the center, while pushing [d5] hangs a1. [%csl Ya1,Yb7,Yd4][%cal Ga1b1,Bc1e3] }
( 7... O-O ) 8. Rb1
{ The [Rb1] Modern Exchange, the main line of top-level Grünfeld play. Moving the rook off a1 sidesteps every [b:Bxc3+] check along the long diagonal while pressing straight down the half-open b-file so Black's c8-bishop cannot leave b7 unguarded. Black castles [O-O] (preparing [O-O Be2 cxd4 cxd4 Qa5+ Bd2 Qxa2] or [O-O Be2 b6]), or develops [Nc6] to challenge d4 immediately. [%csl Ya1,Yb7,Yd4][%cal Ge8g8,Bb8c6] }
( 8. Be3 ) ( 8. Bb5+ ) ( 8. h3 ) ( 8. Be2 ) ( 8. d5 $2
{ Pushing [d5] prematurely abandons c3 while the a1-rook is still on the long diagonal. After [Bxc3+ Bd2 Bxa1 Qxa1 f6], White's dark-square compensation falls short of the lost exchange because Black's king is unweakened. Play [w:Rb1] first if you want [w:d5] ideas. [%csl Yc3][%cal Gg7c3] }
