[Event "Benoni and Benko"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 c5
{ The Old Benoni, named after Aaron Reinganum's 1825 Frankfurt treatise Ben-Oni ('Son of my sorrow', written to combat melancholy) and played by Saint-Amant against Staunton in 1843. Striking at d4 on move one sidesteps London and Trompowsky setups and asks an immediate question of White's center. [d5] is by far the most testing reply, seizing space and taking c6 away from Black's knight; [e3], [c3], [Nf3] and [dxc5] are quieter choices, while [e4] crosses into an Open Sicilian. [%csl Yd4,Yc6][%cal Gd4d5,Be2e4,Ye2e3] }
( 1... Nf6 2. c4 c5
{ The Benoni Defence. Black attacks d4 with the c-pawn to lure [d5] and create an asymmetric, fighting pawn structure from move three. [d5] is White's only ambitious try for an opening advantage, claiming central space and shutting out [b:Nc6]. Quiet replies like [Nf3] (Symmetrical English) and [e3] give Black easy equality, while [dxc5] hands away White's central d-pawn for nothing after [dxc5 e6] or [dxc5 Na6]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd4d5,Bg1f3,Ye2e3] }
( 2... e6 3. g3 ( 3. Nf3 c5 4. d5 b5
{ The Blumenfeld Countergambit, made famous when Alekhine crushed Tarrasch at Bad Pistyan in 1922. Unlike the Benko Gambit (where Black's e-pawn stays on e7 and Black plays for long-term queenside file pressure), here [e6] is already on the board, so Black sacrifices the b-pawn to build a mobile central pawn duo with [b:exd5] and [b:d5]. Consequently, White usually declines with [Bg5] (Duz-Khotimirsky's antidote) or [e3], while [dxe6] accepts the challenge. [%csl Ye7][%cal Gc1g5,Bd5e6,Ye2e3] }
5. Bg5
{ The Duz-Khotimirsky Variation, White's most respected antidote to the Blumenfeld Countergambit. By declining the b5-pawn and pinning f6, White threatens to shatter Black's center or recapture on d5 with pieces rather than pawns. After [exd5 cxd5], Black must question the bishop at once with [b:h6] ([exd5 cxd5 h6 Bxf6 Qxf6]), whereas developing [b:Bb7] passively lets White roll [w:e4] and [w:e5] straight through the center. [%csl Yf6,Yd5][%cal Ge6d5,Bb5b4] }
( 5. dxe6 fxe6 6. cxb5 d5
{ The Blumenfeld Countergambit Accepted. In return for the sacrificed b-pawn, Black owns a rolling c5-d5-e6 pawn center, a half-open f-file once Black castles, and sweeping diagonals for [b:Bd6] and [b:Bb7] as soon as [b:e5] advances. White answers with [e3] or [g3] and [w:Bg2] (or [Bg5] pinning f6), aiming to blockade the central pawns on dark squares so the extra queenside b5-pawn tells in the endgame. [%csl Yf6][%cal Ge2e3,Bg2g3,Yc1g5] }
7. e3 ( 7. g3 ) ( 7. Bg5 ) ) ( 5. e3 ) 5... exd5 ( 5... b4 ) 6. cxd5
{ With f6 pinned to Black's queen, White is threatening [e4] followed by [e5] to overload and win the pinned knight. Therefore Black must break or clarify the pin right now with [h6 Bxf6 Qxf6 Qc2 d6] (giving up the f6-knight for the bishop pair and dark-square play) or [d6] guarding e5. Fianchettoing [Bb7] to eye d5 overlooks the pin on f6 completely. [%csl Yf6,Ye5,Yd5][%cal Gh7h6,Bd7d6] }
6... h6 ( 6... d6 ) ( 6... Bb7 $2
{ Fianchettoing [Bb7] forgets that [w:e4]-[w:e5] is coming with lethal force against the pinned f6-knight! After [e4 h6 Bxf6 Qxf6 Bxb5] (or [e4 Qa5+ Bd2]), White wins a pawn or dominates the whole center. Question the bishop first with [b:h6]. [%cal Ge2e4,Yg5f6] }
7. e4 h6 8. Bxf6 ) ) 3... c5 4. d5
{ When White fianchettoes early in Catalan fashion and meets Black's central c-pawn challenge by pushing d5, Black should trade [exd5 cxd5 d6] into the solid Fianchetto Modern Benoni. Trying to play a Blumenfeld or Benko-style [b5] here when White has not yet spent a move on [w:Nf3] and already has g3 ready for [w:Bg2] backfires tactically along the h1-a8 diagonal. [%cal Ge6d5,Gf1g2,Gg2a8] }
4... exd5 ( 4... b5 $2
{ Playing [b5] with [g3] already on the board walks straight into a long-diagonal trap! After [dxe6 fxe6 Bg2], White's bishop attacks the cornered a8-rook directly and forces ugly concessions like [b:d5] or [b:Nc6] under heavy pressure. Trade [b:exd5] instead. [%csl Ra8][%cal Gd5e6,Gf1g2] }
5. dxe6 fxe6 6. Bg2 ) ) 3. d5
{ The main crossroads of the Benoni complex. White's wedge on d5 cramps Black's center and denies c6 to the b8-knight, so Black must choose how to fight back: [e6] is the sharp Modern Benoni (trading pawns to open the e-file and create a queenside majority), [b5] is the positional Benko/Volga Gambit, [e5] locks the center in the solid Czech Benoni, and [d6] or [g6] keep move-order flexibility while Bücker's [Ne4] is the offbeat Vulture. [%csl Yd5,Yc6][%cal Ge7e6,Bb7b5,Yd7d6] }
( 3. Nf3
{ Declining to push [w:d5] steers the game into a Symmetrical English / Anti-Benoni, a quiet positional favorite of players who want to avoid Benko and Modern Benoni sharpness. Black equalizes cleanly by trading [cxd4 Nxd4] (followed by [b:e6], [b:Nc6] or a [b:b6] Hedgehog setup), fianchettoing [g6], or playing [e6], which keeps both [e6 Nc3] and a delayed [e6 d5] Benoni or Blumenfeld open. [%cal Gc5d4,Be7e6,Yg7g6] }
3... cxd4
{ By capturing on d4 after White declined to push d5, Black steers the game out of the Benoni and straight into an Open Sicilian / Symmetrical English hybrid with colors reversed. White almost always recaptures [Nxd4], taking a central outpost and inviting either a Maroczy-style clamp ([Nxd4 g6 Nc3] or [Nxd4 e6 Nc3 Bb4]) or the sharp gambit strike [Nxd4 e5 Nb5 d5]; recapturing [Qxd4] lets [Qxd4 Nc6] chase the queen with a free developing tempo. [%cal Gf3d4,Gd1d4,Ge7e5,Gd7d5] }
( 3... e6 ) ( 3... g6 ) ( 3... b5 $6
{ Playing a Benko-style [b5] wing gambit when White has not pushed [w:d5] throws away a clean pawn for zero positional compensation: without a locked center, White's pieces are completely free to defend and trade. After [cxb5 e6 a3 cxd4 Nxd4], White pockets the pawn while keeping effortless central control. Trade [b:cxd4] or play [b:e6] / [b:g6] instead. [%cal Rc4b5] }
4. cxb5 e6 5. a3 cxd4 6. Nxd4 ) 4. Nxd4 ( 4. Qxd4 ) ) ( 3. e3
{ Supporting d4 calmly with [e3] sidesteps all Modern Benoni and Benko Gambit complications, keeping the position closed and solid at the modest cost of hemming in the c1-bishop for the moment. Black equalizes comfortably in several ways: [cxd4 exd4 d5] creates a balanced Panov/Tarrasch isolated-queen's-pawn battle, [e6] heads into Symmetrical QGD or Nimzo-Indian territory after [e6 Nf3 d5], and [g6] prepares a flexible kingside fianchetto. [%cal Gc5d4,Ge7e6,Gd7d5,Gf1d3] }
3... cxd4 ( 3... g6 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2
{ White has declined the Benoni challenge with a quiet [e3] setup, reaching a symmetrical Panov/Maróczy-like structure where White hopes to squeeze slowly without risk. Black equalizes cleanly by striking in the center with [cxd4 exd4 d5] (creating an Isolated Queen's Pawn battle where Black's g7-bishop shines against d4), [d5] directly, or [b6] for a double fianchetto. Developing [Nc6] without trading or pushing [d5] walks right into [w:d5] with White a tempo up. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gc5d4,Bd7d5,Yb7b6] }
6... cxd4 ( 6... d5 ) ( 6... b6 ) ( 6... Nc6 $2
{ Putting the knight on c6 while d4 can still advance lets White play [d5] on far better terms than a normal Benoni: the c6-knight is kicked to the rim ([d5 Na5 e4]) and Black has no open e-file or queenside majority to compensate. Play [b:cxd4] or [b:d5] first. [%csl Yc6,Yd4][%cal Gd4d5,Ye3e4] }
7. d5 Na5 8. e4 ) 7. exd4
{ Once Black trades on d4 in the quiet Anti-Benoni, White recaptures with the e-pawn to free the c1-bishop and avoid awkward queen or knight retreats. Black must strike at the center right now with [d5], creating a textbook Isolated Queen's Pawn battle where Black's g7-bishop bears straight down on d4. Developing [Nc6] without pushing [d5] first walks headlong into [w:d5] with gain of time. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gd7d5] }
7... d5 ( 7... Nc6 $2
{ Developing [Nc6] instead of pushing [d5] lets White advance [d5] with tempo ([d5 Nb4 Bf4 d6]), driving Black's knight out of the center and locking Black's g7-bishop behind White's pawn chain! Play [b:d5] immediately. [%cal Gd4d5,Yc1f4] }
8. d5 Nb4 9. Bf4 ) ) ( 3... e6 ) ) ( 3. dxc5 e6
{ The Weenink Variation. Once White surrenders the d4-pawn for c5, Black opens the f8-bishop's diagonal to recover the pawn effortlessly while keeping a central majority. White should develop smoothly with [Nc3 Bxc5] or [Nf3 Bxc5], accepting equality rather than trying to cling to c5 with [b4], which runs into [b4 a5] undermining White's queenside and eyeing the undefended rook on a1. [%csl Yc5,Ya1][%cal Gb1c3,Bg1f3,Ye2e3] }
4. Nc3 ( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. e3 ) ) 3... e6
{ The Modern Benoni, introduced by Frank Marshall at New York 1927 and turned into a fearsome winning weapon in the 1950s–1980s by Mikhail Tal, Bobby Fischer and Garry Kasparov. Black strikes at d5 before White plays [e4], forcing White to recapture on d5 with the c-pawn (waiting until after [e4] would let White recapture [w:exd5] and kill Black's counterplay). White develops [Nc3], [Nf3] or [g3], avoiding [dxe6 fxe6] which surrenders White's space edge and opens Black's f-file and d5 square. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb1c3,Bg1f3,Yg2g3] }
( 3... b5
{ The Benko (or Volga) Gambit: analysed by Boris Argunov in Kuibyshev on the Volga in 1946 and forged into a world-class weapon by Pal Benko in the late 1960s and 1970s. Unlike tactical mate gambits, Black offers a pure positional pawn sacrifice ([cxb5 a6 bxa6]): in return for one pawn, Black gets half-open a- and b-files for heavy pieces, a monster unopposed bishop on g7, and enduring queenside pressure that persists even after queens are traded! White accepts with [cxb5] or declines with [Nf3], [a4], [Nd2] or [Qc2]. [%csl Yg7][%cal Gc4b5,Bg1f3,Ya2a4] }
4. cxb5
{ White takes the first gambit pawn, and Black follows through with the signature Benko lever [a6], offering a second pawn to prise open the a- and b-files for Black's rooks and light-squared bishop. Playing [g6] first usually transposes once Black plays [b:a6] on the next move, whereas [Qa5+ Bd2 Qxb5 Nc3] only helps White develop with tempo against the exposed queen. [%cal Ga7a6] }
( 4. Nf3
{ The Main Benko Declined. White develops calmly and refuses to open the a-file on Black's terms. Black has three good ways on: [g6] (keeping the Benko invitation alive if White later takes b5, or meeting [g6 Qc2] with [b:Na6]/[b:Bg7]), [Bb7] (attacking d5 directly, when [Bb7 a4] or [Bb7 Qc2 bxc4 e4 e6] leads to sharp play), and [bxc4 Nc3] (or [e6] crossing into the Blumenfeld). [%csl Yb5,Yd5][%cal Gg7g6,Bc8b7,Yb5c4] }
4... g6 ( 4... Bb7 ) ( 4... bxc4 ) ( 4... e6 ) ) ( 4. a4
{ Sosonko's Declined Variation: White challenges b5 with the a-pawn so that b5 cannot be backed up by [a6] without opening the a-file for White's rook. Black either trades [bxc4 Nc3 d6 e4] (allowing White to win back c4 smoothly while Black plays down the half-open b-file) or locks the queenside with [b4] (or [Qa5+ Bd2 b4]), surrendering c4 to a White knight via [w:Nd2]-[w:Nc4] while eyeing e4 and [b:e6]. [%csl Yb5,Yc4,Ye4][%cal Gb5c4,Bb5b4] }
4... bxc4 ( 4... b4 ) ( 4... Qa5+ ) ) ( 4. Nd2
{ The Quiet Soviet Sideline, a practical favorite of Daniel Naroditsky. Putting the knight on d2 prepares [w:e4] while standing ready to recapture on c4 with a piece if Black trades lazily, keeping the a- and b-files closed. Black's sharpest equalizer is [bxc4 e4 c3 bxc3 g6], throwing the c-pawn onto c3 to fracture White's queenside pawns before White's knight can recapture cleanly; [b4] and [g6] are playable too. [%csl Yd2,Yc4,Yc3][%cal Gb5c4,Bb5b4] }
4... bxc4 ( 4... b4 ) ( 4... g6 ) ) ( 4. Qc2 ) ( 4. e4
