[Event "Dutch Defence"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 f5
{ The Dutch Defence, recommended in 1789 by Elias Stein in The Hague (Nouvel essai sur le jeu des échecs) and played by both Botvinnik and Bronstein in their 1951 World Championship match. Black stakes an asymmetric claim on e4 and prepares a kingside attack down the f-file without blocking the f-pawn behind a knight, at the price of loosening the e8-h5 and a2-g8 diagonals and spending a non-developing move. White's classical antidote is the kingside fianchetto ([g3], [c4], [Nf3]), while [e4] (Staunton Gambit), [Bg5] (Hopton), [Nc3] (Raphael) and [g4] test Black's king immediately. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gc2c4,Bg2g3,Yg1f3] }
2. c4
{ White grabs queenside space and prepares [g3] and [Nc3]. Black almost always develops [Nf6] first, guarding e4 and h5 before choosing between the three great Dutch systems: the dynamic Leningrad ([Nf6 g3 g6]), the rock-solid Stonewall ([Nf6 g3 e6] with [b:d5] and [b:c6]), and the flexible Classical / Ilyin-Zhenevsky ([Nf6 g3 e6] with [b:Be7] and [b:d6]). Playing [e6] directly invites [e6 Nc3 Bb4] in Nimzo-Dutch fashion. [%csl Ye4,Yh5][%cal Gg8f6,Be7e6,Yg7g6] }
( 2. g3
{ Fianchettoing on move two is White's most accurate and flexible anti-Dutch move order: the bishop on g2 will slice across the long diagonal, bolster an eventual [w:e4] or [w:d5] break, and discourage Black from fianchettoing with [b:b6] and [b:Bb7], while White keeps the g1-knight flexible (for f3, h3-f4 or e2). Black answers [Nf6], keeping Leningrad ([b:g6]), Stonewall ([b:e6] and [b:d5]) and Classical ([b:e6] and [b:Be7]) all open. [%csl Yg2,Yf3,Yh3][%cal Gg8f6,Be7e6] }
2... Nf6
{ Black develops the kingside knight first to clamp down on e4 before choosing a pawn setup. White naturally completes the fianchetto with [Bg2] (or grabs queenside space with [c4] / [Nf3]), waiting to see whether Black steers into the dynamic Leningrad Dutch ([Bg2 g6]), the solid Classical and Stonewall family ([Bg2 e6]), or the flexible Hort-Antoshin system ([Bg2 d6] and [b:c6]). [%cal Gf1g2,Gc2c4,Ge7e6,Gg7g6] }
( 2... e6 ) ( 2... g6 ) 3. Bg2
{ With White's light-squared bishop already posted on its finest diagonal at g2 (eyeing d5, supporting a future [w:e4] break and discouraging [b:b6] plus [b:Bb7]), Black chooses a Dutch system: [g6] enters the dynamic Leningrad Dutch (aiming for [b:Bg7], [b:O-O], [b:d6] and [b:e5]), while [e6] prepares [b:Be7] (Classical) or [b:d5] and [b:c6] (Stonewall); [d6] is Rubinstein's flexible waiting sideline keeping c8 open. [%csl Yg2,Yd5,Yc8][%cal Ge7e6,Bg7g6] }
( 3. c4 ) ( 3. Nf3 ) 3... e6 ( 3... g6
{ The Leningrad Variation, forged in 1930s Leningrad by Alexander Tolush, Grigory Levenfish and Vladimir Zak and modernized by Viktor Malaniuk, Vladimir Kramnik and Hikaru Nakamura. Black combines [f5] with a King's Indian fianchetto ([Bg7], [O-O], [d6]), aiming to prepare the central pawn break [b:e5] rather than locking the center on light squares. White can develop smoothly with [Nf3] and [c4], or play [Nh3] aiming for f4 or g5. [%csl Yf4,Yg5][%cal Gg1f3,Bc2c4,Yg1h3] }
4. Nf3 ( 4. c4 ) ( 4. Nh3 ) 4... Bg7 5. O-O O-O 6. c4 d6 7. Nc3
{ The main Leningrad tabiya at move seven. Both sides have castled and developed naturally, and Black's overarching strategic goal is to push [b:e5] without falling victim to White's [w:d5] space wedge or [w:e4] counter-break (which would rip open the e-file against Black's backward e7-pawn). Black chooses between three major lines: [Qe8] (the flexible main line), [c6] (the Warsaw Variation), and Matulović's [Nc6]. [%cal Gd8e8,Bc7c6,Yb8c6] }
7... Qe8
{ Malaniuk's [Qe8]: the flagship move of the Leningrad Dutch. Stepping the queen to e8 supports the [b:e5] break, prepares [b:Qf7] eyeing c4 and e4 (especially effective against [Re1 Qf7], when a careless [w:d5] runs into [b:Ne4] hitting c3 and f2), and keeps [b:h6], [b:g5] and [b:Qh5] kingside play in reserve. White's main tests are [d5] (seizing space at once), [b3], [Re1] and Karpov's [Qb3] (met by [b:Na6] or [b:Kh8]). [%csl Ye8,Yc4,Ye4][%cal Gd4d5,Bb2b3] }
( 7... c6
{ The Warsaw Variation, championed by Kamsky and Nakamura. Black blunts White's g2-bishop with c6, takes d5 away from the c3-knight, and prepares [b:Qe8] or [b:Na6]-[b:Nc7] plus [b:e5]. White's main test is [d5], forcing [d5 e5 dxe6 Bxe6] where Black's bishop hits c4 with tempo (after which White defends with [w:b3] or [w:Qd3], and Black answers with [b:Na6] or Nakamura's [b:Ne4] pawn sacrifice); [b3] and [Qb3] are solid alternatives. [%csl Yc6,Yd5,Yc4][%cal Gd4d5,Bb2b3] }
8. d5 ( 8. b3 ) ( 8. Qb3 ) 8... e5 9. dxe6 Bxe6
{ The main Warsaw Leningrad tabiya. By trading en passant on e6, White saddled Black with a backward d6-pawn on the half-open d-file, while Black's light-squared bishop bounded into play with tempo against c4. Top grandmasters favour [b3] (guarding c4 and eyeing a3, met by [b3 Na6] or Nakamura's [b3 Ne4] pawn sacrifice — never [b:Bxa1] after [w:Nxe4], which loses to [w:Nxd6]), while [Qd3] attacks d6 directly. [%csl Ye6,Yc4,Ya3][%cal Gb2b3,Bd1d3] }
10. b3 ( 10. Qd3 ) ) ( 7... Nc6
{ The Matulović Variation. Black develops the knight directly to c6 to fight for e5 without spending a move on [Qe8] or [c6]. White almost always pushes [d5], forcing Black to choose between [d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5] (followed by [b:e6] to undouble the pawns and claim kingside space) and [d5 Na5 Nd2 c5], turning the game into a sharp queenside Benoni-like struggle with [b:a6] and [b:b5]. [%csl Yc6,Ye5][%cal Gd4d5] }
8. d5 ) 8. d5
