[Event "Other Indian defences"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. d4 Nf6 ( 1... Nc6
{ The Queen's Knight (or Mikenas) Defence, championed by Lithuanian master Vladas Mikėnas. Like a mirror image of Alekhine's Defence, Black develops the c6-knight in front of the c-pawn to prepare [b:e5] (or [b:d5]) and lure White's central pawns forward as targets. White can cross into King's Pawn territory with [e4] (Nimzowitsch Defence), develop [Nf3], grab space with [c4] (inviting the Lithuanian [c4 e5]), or kick the knight with [d5]. [%cal Gc2c4,Bg1f3,Yd4d5] }
2. c4
{ White grabs central and queenside space while Black's c-pawn stays blocked behind the c6-knight. To justify that provocative first move, Black must fight for the dark squares immediately with [e5] (the Lithuanian Variation, asking White to push [e5 d5 Nce7]) or [e6] (preparing [b:Bb4+] or [b:Nf6] into the Black Knights' Tango). Passive moves let White build an unchallenged d4-c4-e4 steamroller. [%cal Ge7e5,Be7e6,Yg8f6] }
( 2. Nf3 ) ( 2. d5
{ White kicks the c6-knight on move two to claim central space immediately. Black chooses between [Ne5], jumping into the middle to provoke [Ne5 f4 Ng6 e4 e5] (setting sharp queen-check traps on h4 if White gets greedy), and the paradoxical Montevideo retreat [Nb8], undeveloping the knight after luring d5 forward so Black can play [b:d6], [b:e5] or [b:g6] with the c-pawn unblocked. [%csl Yh4,Yd5][%cal Gc6e5,Bc6b8] }
2... Ne5 ( 2... Nb8
{ The Montevideo Defence: having provoked White's d-pawn forward to d5, Black retreats the knight all the way home to b8! Strange as undeveloping on move two looks, Black argues that the advanced d5-pawn blocks d5 for White's pieces, leaves c5 and e5 weaker, and can now be undermined with [c6], [e6] or [e5] without the knight standing in the c-pawn's way. White should still punish the lost two tempi energetically with [e4], [Nc3] or [c4] to dominate central space. [%csl Yb8,Yc5,Yd5][%cal Gc6b8] }
3. e4
{ After the Montevideo retreat to b8, White occupies a full e4-d5 center while Black still has every piece on its starting square. Black should play [e6] (challenging d5 immediately), [d6] or [e5] to anchor the center before bringing out the kingside knight. Developing [Nf6] without pawn support lets [Nf6 e5 Ng8] chase Black's second knight right back home on move four in the comical Zaire Defence. [%csl Gd5,Ge4][%cal Ge7e6,Gd7d6] }
( 3. c4 ) ( 3. Nc3 ) 3... e6 ( 3... d6 ) ( 3... e5 ) ( 3... Nf6 $2
{ Bringing out [Nf6] without pawn support lets [e5 Ng8 Nf3] kick Black's second knight all the way back to g8 too — the Zaire Defence, with every Black piece back on its starting square on move four while White owns d5 and e5. Play [b:d6] or [b:e5] first. [%csl Rf6][%cal Ge4e5,Gf6g8] }
4. e5
{ White chases Black's second knight across the board. Having already undeveloped the queenside knight on move two and brought out [Nf6] without pawn support on move three, Black has only one humbling reply left: [Ng8] — the comical Zaire Defence, returning every single Black piece to its starting square on move four while White commands d5 and e5. Jumping forward to e4 instead loses the knight. [%csl Gd5,Ge5][%cal Gf6g8] }
4... Ng8 ( 4... Ne4 $4
{ Hopping forward to e4 instead of retreating to g8 strands the knight deep inside White's camp with zero escape squares: [Qd4 f5 f3] surrounds and wins the trapped knight cleanly. Retreat [b:Ng8] instead. [%csl Re4][%cal Gd1d4,Gf2f3] }
