3... dxe5 ( 3... Nc6
{ The Lisbon Gambit in the English Rat: instead of trading queens after [b:dxe5], Black offers a central pawn so [exd6 Bxd6] brings the dark-squared bishop out in one stroke and prepares [b:Bf5] (or [b:Bg4]), [b:Qe7] and queenside castling! Accepting with [exd6 Bxd6 Nc3 Nf6 Nf3] is White's critical test—weathering Black's active development with solid play for a real edge—while declining via [Nf3 Nxe5] or [Nc3 Nxe5] gives Black easy equality without risk. [%cal Ge5d6,Gf8d6,Gc8f5,Gd8e7] }
