{ The Wade Defence, championed by New Zealand IM Bob Wade, Julian Hodgson and Michael Adams. Pinning f3 on move two before playing [b:Nf6] prepares to trade [b:Bxf3] if provoked and establish a dark-square clamp with [b:c6], [b:Nd7], [b:e5] and [b:Be7] (or [b:g6] and [b:Bg7]). White answers ambitiously with [c4] (accepting doubled f-pawns after [c4 Bxf3 gxf3] for the bishop pair) or [e4] ([e4 Nf6 Nc3]); [Nbd2] is solid. [%cal Gg4f3,Gc2c4,Ge2e4] }
( 2... g6
{ The Rossolimo Variation of the Queen's Pawn Game: Black combines d6 and g6 before developing [Nf6], keeping Pirc, King's Indian and Modern Robatsch plans open—including an early [Bg4] pin against f3 and flexible setups with [Nd7] and [e5]. White has rich choices: [c4] grabs queenside space toward King's Indian or Modern lines, [e4] crosses into the Pirc/Modern family, [Nc3] develops actively, and [g3] or [Bf4] keeps the position strategic and controlled. [%csl Yd6,Yf3,Yg6][%cal Gg7g6] }
