{ The Normal Indian Crossroads: one of the great branching points of chess, named in 1884 after the fianchetto games of Kolkata masters Moheschunder Bannerjee and Saumchurn Guttack against John Cochrane. White seizes queenside space and clears c3 for the knight. Black splits between hypermodern bishop setups — [e6] (Nimzo-, Queen's and Bogo-Indian or Catalan), [g6] (King's Indian and Grünfeld) and [d6] (Old Indian) — and direct strikes: [c5] (Benoni/Benko), [e5] (Budapest), [Nc6] (Tango) and [c6] (Slav-Indian). [%csl Yc3][%cal Ge7e6,Bg7g6,Yc7c5] }
