{ The Gligorić / Kornl Richter Line: the one major variation of the Budapest where White keeps the gambit pawn by force. White accepts isolated doubled c-pawns and an early queen move in return for a full extra pawn and the bishop pair. Because White threatens [h3] driving the g4-knight to passive h6, Black must act immediately with [f6] ([f6 exf6 Nxf6 Qd3 d6], met by Seirawan's [w:g3] and [w:Bg2]) or Vidmar's [Qa3] ([Qa3 Rc1 f6]). [%csl Yh6][%cal Gf7f6,Be7a3] }
8... f6 ( 8... Qa3 ) ) 6... Qe7
{ With three attackers piled on e5 and d5 blocked by the d2-knight, Black is guaranteed to recover the gambit pawn. White chooses between [a3] (questioning the b4-bishop immediately and inviting the famous Kieninger mate trap) and Karpov's [e3], which saves a3 until Black's bishop commits: after [e3 Ngxe5 Nxe5 Nxe5 Be2 O-O O-O d6], Karpov's [w:Nb3] hunts down the dark-squared bishop ([b:b6] [w:a3] [b:Bc5] [w:Nxc5] [b:bxc5], never [b:dxc5] allowing [w:Qd5]). [%csl Ye5,Yd5,Ya3][%cal Ga2a3,Be2e3] }
