{ Watch out for the famous Kieninger Trap (Georg Kieninger vs Godai, Vienna 1925). Black ignores the attack on the b4-bishop and captures e5 anyway because the e2-pawn is pinned to White's king by the e7-queen. White must trade knights first with [Nxe5 Nxe5] and then unpin with [w:e3], since snatching b4 either now or on the next move walks straight into smothered checkmate on d3. [%csl Ye5,Yb4,Yd3][%cal Gf3e5] }
8. Nxe5 ( 8. axb4 $4
{ The Kieninger Trap! Snatching the 'hanging' bishop on b4 walks straight into [Nd3#] smothered checkmate on move eight: the e2-pawn cannot take the knight because Black's queen on e7 pins it to White's king! Trade [w:Nxe5] and play [w:e3] first! [%csl Yb4,Ye7][%cal Ge5d3] }
