{ With knights on f6 and c3 and Black's pawn on d6, Black reaches the crossroads between two classic Indian philosophies: [g6] enters the King's Indian (fianchettoing on g7 for a sharp kingside attack), while [e5] or [Nbd7] enters the Old Indian (keeping the kingside pawns unmoved without a g6 hook and placing the bishop modestly on e7). [Bf5] is Janowski's Indian, and [c6] prepares [b:Qa5] and [b:e5]. [%cal Gg7g6,Ge7e5,Gb8d7] }
( 3. Nf3 ) 3... g6 ( 3... Nbd7 ) ( 3... e5
{ The pure Old Indian center. Because Black's f8-bishop is heading for e7 rather than g7, White has two clear choices: [Nf3] keeps tension in the center ([Nf3 Nbd7 e4 Be7 Be2 O-O O-O c6], where White enjoys lasting space while Black maneuvers via [b:Re8], [b:Bf8] and [b:a6]+[b:b5]), and [dxe5 dxe5 Qxd8+ Kxd8], steering straight into a queenless endgame where Black's king hides safely on c7 behind [b:c6]. [%cal Gg1f3,Gd4e5,Gf8e7] }
