{ Developing [Nc3] threatens [w:e4] right away to clamp down on the center, which takes away most of Black's hypermodern side-steps in the Slav-Indian. Black's only sound equalizer is [d5], transposing directly into the main-line Three Knights Slav and Semi-Slav complex; trying to stay in a King's Indian or Old Indian with [d6] or [g6] after spending a tempo on c6 hands White an uncontested [w:e4] center. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 3. Nf3 ) ( 3. Bf4 ) 3... d5 ) ( 2... Nc6
{ The Black Knights' Tango (also known as the Mexican or Kevitz-Trajković Defence), pioneered by Carlos Torre at Baden-Baden in 1925 and revitalized by Georgi Orlov. Black develops both knights before touching a single pawn, inviting White to push [d5] so the knight can dance to e5 and g6 while Black strikes back at the center. White's main replies are [Nf3] (preventing [b:e5]), [Nc3] (allowing [b:e5]) and the direct [d5]. [%csl Ye5,Yg6][%cal Gg1f3,Bb1c3,Yd4d5] }
