{ The Anti-Nimzo-Indian: one of modern chess's busiest crossroads. By developing [Nf3] before [w:Nc3], White sidesteps the Nimzo-Indian pin on b4 and invites four major defenses: [d5] (Queen's Gambit Declined), [b6] (Queen's Indian), [Bb4+] (Bogo-Indian), and [c5] — the move-order bridge into the Modern Benoni ([c5 d5 exd5]) and the Blumenfeld Countergambit ([c5 d5 b5]), dodging White's Taimanov and Four Pawns attacks. [%csl Yb4][%cal Gb7b6,Bd7d5,Yf8b4] }
