[Event "English Opening (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. c4
{ The English Opening, championed by Howard Staunton in his 1843 match against Saint-Amant and at the 1851 London tournament. Instead of staking a central pawn on move one, White fights for d5 with a flank pawn: whenever the c-pawn trades for a black central pawn, White keeps two center pawns against one. Play often stays flexible for several moves before either side commits d2 or e2, and White frequently fianchettoes on g2 to press all the way down to b7 and a8. Black's main replies are [e5] for a Reversed Sicilian, [c5] for the Symmetrical Variation, and [Nf6], [e6] or [c6] to contest d4 and d5. [%csl Yd5,Yd2,Ye2,Yg2][%cal Ge7e5] }
1... e5 ( 1... Nf6 2. Nc3
{ White fights for d5 and e4 right away, inviting Black to declare a pawn structure. [e5] enters the main Reversed Sicilian lines; [e6] prepares [b:Bb4] or [b:d5] while inviting the sharp Mikenas-Carls Attack [w:e4]; [g6] heads toward King's Indian or Maroczy structures; [c5] reaches the Symmetrical; and [d5] opens the Anglo-Grünfeld after [d5 cxd5 Nxd5]. Every reply leads to a distinct pawn structure, making this position one of the main transpositional crossroads between flank play and classical central battles. [%csl Yd5,Ye4][%cal Ge7e5,Be7e6,Yg7g6] }
2... e5 ( 2... e6
{ Black prepares [b:Bb4] or [b:d5] without blocking the light-squared bishop on c8 yet. If White plays [d4], Black reaches the Nimzo-Indian ([d4 Bb4]) or Queen's Gambit Declined ([d4 d5]). Staying in the English gives White two strong independent weapons: [e4], the aggressive Mikenas-Carls Variation threatening [w:e5] at once, and [Nf3], the Three Knights system where [Nf3 Bb4], [Nf3 d5] and [Nf3 b6] all lead to rich play. [%csl Yc8][%cal Gg1f3,Be2e4,Yg2g3] }
3. Nf3
{ The English Three Knights sidesteps the Nimzo-Indian while keeping [w:e4], [w:g3] and [w:d4] in reserve. Black has four good replies: [Bb4] pins c3 ([Bb4 Qc2] or [Bb4 g3]), [d5] enters Queen's Gambit or Neo-Catalan territory, [c5] reaches the Symmetrical Four Knights, and [b6] aims for a Queen's Indian fianchetto, where White has Garry Kasparov's famous central clamp [b6 e4 Bb7 Bd3] to shut down the b7 bishop. [%csl Yc3,Yb7][%cal Gf8b4,Bd7d5,Yb7b6] }
( 3. e4
{ The Mikenas-Carls Variation, a favorite surprise weapon for aggressive English players. White seizes central space immediately and threatens [w:e5] chasing the knight on f6. Black must strike back in the center at once with [d5] (the main line) or [c5] ([c5 e5 Ng8 Nf3 Nc6 d4]); passive developing moves get overrun by [e5]! Play immediately takes on a sharp, tactical character quite unlike quiet English fianchetto lines, rewarding the side that calculates central pawn pushes and pin motifs more accurately. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gd7d5,Bc7c5] }
3... d5
{ Black meets White's central thrust with an immediate counterblow. White's main try is [e5], pushing past to disrupt Black's kingside knight: Black's critical answer is the counterattack [e5 d4], leading after [e5 d4 exf6 dxc3 bxc3 Qxf6] to a sharp, unbalanced fight where White's center and queenside lines face Black's active pieces. [cxd5] is the calmer path ([cxd5 exd5 e5 Ne4]), keeping pawns intact. [%cal Ge4e5,Bc4d5] }
( 3... c5
{ The Sicilian Variation of the Mikenas-Carls Attack. Instead of contesting e4 on d5, Black clamps down on d4 with [c5], accepting a serious backward-knight problem if White pushes forward. White's critical test is [e5], driving the knight all the way back to its home square ([e5 Ng8 Nf3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4]), where White sacrifices or opens the d-pawn for tremendous piece activity against Black's cramped ranks. [Nf3] develops flexibly while keeping [e5] and [d4] ready, and [f4] reinforces the space wedge. [%csl Yd4,Rf6][%cal Ge4e5,Bg1f3] }
4. e5 ( 4. Nf3 ) ( 4. f4 ) ) ( 3... Bb4 $2
{ Pinning c3 does not stop [e5]! Because c3 is guarded, [e5] drives Black's knight all the way home to g8 (since e4 is covered and h5 gets trapped by [Qg4]), and [Qg4] hits g7 and b4 at the same time. Challenge the center immediately with [d5] or [b:c5]. [%csl Rc3,Rg8,Re4,Rh5][%cal Ge4e5] }
