{ The King's Indian Formation of the Anglo-Indian: Black prepares [b:Bg7] while keeping both [b:e5] and [b:d5] on the table. Pushing [d4] transposes straight into the King's Indian or Grünfeld, so English players stay independent with [e4], the Botvinnik and Maroczy clamp that threatens [w:e5] right away and usually prompts [e4 d6 d4]. [g3] and [Nf3] keep a calmer fianchetto duel where White delays committing the d-pawn until Black shows a central break. [%cal Ge2e4,Bg2g3,Yg1f3] }
3. e4 ( 3. g3
{ Instead of grabbing space with an early e-pawn advance, White opts for the positional fianchetto setup on g2, keeping the game in subtle English territory where neither side rushes to fix the center. Black usually completes the fianchetto with [Bg7], waiting to meet [Bg7 Bg2 O-O] with [d6] or [e5]; [c5] challenges White's grip on d4 at once to steer into the Symmetrical Fianchetto, while [c6] prepares a direct Grünfeld-style [d5] thrust against c4 before White's bishop even lands on g2. [%csl Yd5,Yd4][%cal Gf8g7,Bc7c5] }
3... Bg7 ( 3... c5 ) ( 3... c6 ) ( 3... e5 ) ) ( 3. Nf3
{ Developing both knights early invites Black to choose between two major systems. [Bg7] stays in King's Indian and Symmetrical English channels, where White can seize space right away with [Bg7 e4] (the Three Knights System, threatening [w:e5] unless Black stops it with [Bg7 e4 d6]) or fianchetto calmly with [Bg7 g3]. Meanwhile [d5] launches the sharp Anglo-Grünfeld, challenging c4 before White's center pawns ever step forward. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf8g7,Bd7d5] }
