( 3... d5 ) ( 3... d6 ) 4. g3 ( 4. e4
{ The Anti-Anti-Grünfeld of the Anglo-Indian. By holding back the d-pawn on move three and planting [e4] on move four, White sidesteps Black's Grünfeld repertoire completely while clamping down on d5 in Maróczy fashion. White can still push [d4] into the Classical King's Indian, or keep the structure closed with [d3], [Be2] and [h3] so Black's g7 bishop bites on granite. Black normally plays [d6] or [O-O] to prepare [e5], though striking with [e5] immediately to claim d4 before [d4] lands is sharp and playable. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd7d6,Be8g8,Be7e5] }
