d5
{ The Anglo-Grünfeld challenge: Black strikes in the center on move two before White fianchettoes. White must capture [cxd5], after which [cxd5 Nxd5] reaches the Anglo-Grünfeld proper (where White fianchettoes with [g3] and g2), whereas [cxd5 Nxd5 e4 Nxc3 bxc3] builds a broad pawn center much like the Exchange Grünfeld. After the trade on d5, White's extra central pawn and open c-file give lasting structural trumps if Black fails to generate active piece play against White's center. [%csl Yg2,Yd5][%cal Gc4d5] }
3. cxd5
{ White captures on d5 to clear the c-file and preserve both central pawns for the middlegame. Because White's knight already stands on c3 guarding d5, Black MUST recapture with the knight ([Nxd5]), centralizing it to enter the Anglo-Grünfeld ([Nxd5 g3 g6 Bg2]) or inviting [Nxd5 Nf3] and [Nxd5 e4]! Once the knight lands on d5, White chooses between [g3] and [Bg2] to challenge it down the long diagonal and [e4] to seize a broad central pawn duo. [%csl Yd5,Yc3][%cal Gf6d5] }
( 3. g3 $2
{ Leaving d5 untouched lets Black push [d4] with gain of time, banishing White's knight to the edge on a4 while Black claims the center with [e5]. Always capture [cxd5] first. [%csl Rd5,Ra4][%cal Gd5d4] }
