[Event "English Opening (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. c4 e5
{ The King's English, or Reversed Sicilian: Black stakes a claim in the center, controls d4 and f4, and opens diagonals for the queen and dark-squared bishop. Because White is a full tempo ahead of the Sicilian Defence, lines that are slightly risky for Black become much more dangerous for White: and vice versa. [Nc3] is the classical main move, adding a second grip on d5; [g3] heads straight for the signature g2 fianchetto while keeping d2 and b1 flexible; [Nf3] attacks e5 at once, and [e3] prepares [d4] in Taimanov or Kan style. [%csl Yd4,Yf4,Yd5,Yg2][%cal Gb1c3] }
( 1... Nf6
{ The Anglo-Indian Defence: Black develops the king's knight without committing a single pawn, keeping every major structure on the table, Reversed Sicilian ([e5]), Symmetrical ([b:c5]), Nimzo/Agincourt ([e6]), King's Indian/Grünfeld ([g6]) or Anglo-Slav ([c6]). White stays in English territory with [Nc3], [Nf3] or [g3], whereas [d4] transposes directly into the Indian defences. By waiting with the d-pawn, White keeps the option of fianchettoing on g2 or b2 first, often reaching Catalan, Hedgehog or Reversed Sicilian structures while dodging Black's pet anti-[w:d4] gambits. [%csl Yg2,Yb2][%cal Gb1c3,Bg1f3,Yg2g3] }
2. Nc3 ( 2. Nf3
{ The most flexible knight pairing: White prevents [e5] altogether while waiting to see Black's pawn setup before deciding between [Nc3], [g3], [b3] and [d4]. Black chooses between [e6] (heading toward Neo-Catalan, Queen's Indian or Hedgehog lines), [g6] (King's Indian or Grünfeld), [c5] (Symmetrical), and [c6] with [d5] (Anglo-Slav). Many positional grandmasters favor this move order to steer into Neo-Catalan and Réti double-fianchetto systems without ever allowing sharp reversed-Sicilian complications. [%cal Ge7e6,Bg7g6,Yc7c5] }
2... e6
{ A subtle move-order duel where both sides keep their queenside knights and d-pawns uncommitted. White chooses [g3] for the Catalan and Neo-Catalan family, [Nc3] for the English Three Knights (inviting [Nc3 Bb4], [Nc3 d5] or Kasparov's [Nc3 b6 e4]), [b3] for a Classical Réti double fianchetto, or [d4] into the Queen's Indian and Bogo-Indian. Black waits to match White's pawn commitment with [b:d5], [b:b6] or [b:c5]. [%cal Gg2g3,Bb1c3,Yd2d4] }
( 2... g6
{ Black prepares [b:Bg7] while keeping both [b:d5] (Grünfeld) and [b:d6] with [b:e5] (King's Indian) in reserve. English and Réti players sidestep early Grünfeld theory by fianchettoing on either wing: [g3] sets up a symmetrical kingside fianchetto where White castles before touching d2, and [b3] controls e5 and d4 from b2 with [w:Bb2], whereas [Nc3] and [d4] steer back toward mainstream Indian lines. [%csl Yd2,Ye5,Yd4,Yb2][%cal Gb1c3] }
3. Nc3 ( 3. g3
{ By pairing [w:Nf3] with [w:g3] before developing b1, White holds back the target that Black's Grünfeld knights love to trade on c3. Black almost universally completes the fianchetto with [Bg7], though [b:c6] preparing [b:d5] and [b:c5] aiming for a Symmetrical English often transpose a move later. White will follow with [w:Bg2] and [w:O-O], retaining the freedom to play [w:Nc3], [w:d4], or a queenside expansion with [w:b3] and [w:Bb2]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gf8g7,Bf1g2] }
3... Bg7
{ Both kingside fianchettoes are nearly complete and neither central pawn has budged from its starting rank. [Bg2] keeps maximum Mystery-English flexibility, waiting for Black to castle before choosing between [w:Nc3], [w:d4], or [w:b3]. [Nc3] invites [Nc3 d5] (Anglo-Grünfeld) or [Nc3 c5] (Symmetrical), while [d4] steers directly into the Fianchetto King's Indian or Grünfeld Defense, one of the soundest positional systems in modern grandmaster chess. [%cal Gf1g2,Bb1c3,Bd2d4] }
4. Bg2
{ With both fianchettoes completed and neither queen's knight committed, the position retains peak hypermodern tension. [O-O] is the standard continuation, tucking the king away while waiting to see whether White develops b1 or pushes [w:d4]. Black can also choose an immediate structure: [c6] prepares a Slav-Grünfeld [b:d5] strike, [c5] enters a Symmetrical English, and [d6] signals a King's Indian setup aiming for [b:e5]. [%cal Ge8g8,Bc7c6,Gg2d5] }
( 4. Nc3 ) ( 4. d4 ) 4... O-O
