{ Black pushes past to chase the knight rather than defending e5. White's strongest reply is [Ng5], piling right onto the overextended e4 pawn with both knights. Black's trappiest try is the Bellón Gambit [b5], sacrificing a wing pawn so the light-squared bishop can take the long diagonal on b7, while [c6] and [Qe7] fight directly to hold or trade e4. [Nd4] is the calmer positional retreat. Play quickly becomes asymmetrical and concrete: Black tries to use the e4 spearhead to launch a kingside attack or gambit on the queenside, while White chips away at the overextended pawn before Black can consolidate. [%csl Ye5,Ye4,Yb7][%cal Gf3g5,Bf3d4] }
4. Ng5
{ Both white knights gang up on e4, and Black cannot defend it with a routine pawn move. [b5], the Bellón Gambit, is Black's sharpest bid for counterplay: after [b5 cxb5 d5], Black gives a pawn to blunt c3 and rush pieces into play. Quiet players prefer [Qe7] to guard e4 for a moment or [c6] to answer [Ngxe4] with [d5]. Once the dust settles around e4, White aims to fianchetto on g2 and castle quickly so Black's lead in piece activity evaporates and any structural weaknesses left by Black's pawn pushes tell. [%csl Ye4,Yc3,Yg2][%cal Gb7b5,Bc7c6] }
( 4. Nd4 ) 4... b5 ( 4... c6 ) ( 4... Qe7 ) ( 4... h6 $2
