[Event "Réti and flank openings (1)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. Nf3 ( 1. g3 ) ( 1. b3 ) ( 1. f4
{ Bird's Opening, recorded as early as Greco (1620) and championed across more than a hundred games by 19th-century English master Henry Edward Bird. White stakes an immediate flank claim on e5 and clears the f-file for the castled rook later, playing a Dutch Defence with colors reversed and an extra move in hand. [d5] and [Nf6] are Black's soundest replies, while [e5], From's Gambit, is a speculative pawn sacrifice aiming at the e1-h4 diagonal that White refutes with accurate play. [%csl Ye5,Ye1,Yh4][%cal Gd7d5,Bg8f6] }
1... d5
{ The Dutch Variation of the Bird: a Reversed Dutch Defence where White's extra tempo turns defense into attack. [Nf3] is White's essential developing move, guarding the h4 check square while adding a second grip on e5. From there White chooses between the Classical Bird ([e3], [b3], [Bb2] and [w:Bb5] or [Be2], immortalized by Emanuel Lasker's double-bishop sacrifice against Bauer at Amsterdam 1889) and the Leningrad / Polar Bear fianchetto ([g3], [Bg2], [d3]). [%csl Yh4,Ye5][%cal Gg1f3,Be2e3,Yb2b3] }
( 1... Nf6
{ A flexible answer to the Bird: Black controls e4 and keeps [d5], [g6], [c5] and [d6] in reserve. [Nf3] is White's cleanest move, covering h4 right away so Black cannot stir up Michael Brooks's delayed From's Gambit ([b3 d6 Bb2 e5], a tricky pawn sacrifice that White refutes only with accurate defense after [b3 d6 Bb2 e5 fxe5 dxe5 e3]). Once [Nf3] secures h4 and e5, White sets up [e3] and [b3] or [g3] and [w:Bg2]. [%csl Ye4,Yh4,Ye5][%cal Gg1f3,Be2e3] }
2. Nf3
{ White develops [Nf3] to secure the e1-h4 diagonal and reinforce the signature Bird outpost on e5. Black now picks a defensive blueprint: [g6] prepares [Bg7] to contest the long diagonal against a future [b3] and [Bb2] (where aggressive Bird players sometimes launch the Batavo-Polish [g6 b4]), [d5] enters the Classical Bird, [c5] strikes at d4 from the flank, and [e6] or [d6] keeps the center flexible for a well-timed [e5] or [d5] break. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gg7g6,Bd7d5,Yc7c5] }
( 2. e3 ) ( 2. g3 ) 2... g6
{ Black's kingside fianchetto [g6] is one of the most reliable setups against the Bird: the g7 bishop will neutralize White's future queen's fianchetto on b2 along the long diagonal while supporting a timely [b:d6] and [b:e5] (or [b:d5] and [b:c5]) counter-strike. White chooses between classical Bird play with [e3] and [w:b3], Leningrad-style [g3] and [w:Bg2] clamping down on e4, or [d4] entering a Stonewall. [%cal Gf8g7,Ge2e3,Bg2g3,Yd2d4] }
( 2... d5 ) ( 2... c5 ) ( 2... e6 ) ( 2... d6 ) 3. e3 ( 3. g3 ) ( 3. d4 ) ) (
1... c5
{ Black challenges the dark central squares from the flank without blocking any pieces, inviting White into Closed Sicilian territory. Pushing [e4] accepts that invitation directly, transposing into the Grand Prix Attack where White plans [Nc3], [Nf3] and [Bc4] or [Bb5] for a kingside offensive. Staying in pure Bird channels with [Nf3] and [d3] (or [b3] and [Bb2]) is equally sound, building a clamp on e5 while Black develops [Nc6], [g6] and [Bg7]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gg1f3,Be2e4,Yd2d3] }
2. Nf3 ( 2. e4 ) ( 2. d3 ) ) ( 1... g6 ) ( 1... f5
{ The Symmetrical Bird: Black copies White's flank claim on e4 and invites a maneuvering game if White mirrors back. Positional players develop [Nf3] and [g3] or enter the symmetrical Stonewall with [d4 d5], whereas tacticians strike immediately at Black's loosened kingside with Alexander Wagner's Swiss Gambit [e4], playing a From's Gambit in reverse where White's f4 pawn supports rapid kingside attacks. [%csl Ye4,Yf4][%cal Gg1f3,Bd2d4,Ye2e4] }
2. Nf3 ( 2. d4 d5
{ The Double Duck Formation: with [f4], [d4] and Black's [f5], [d5], both sides plant pawns on the d- and f-files, locking into a symmetrical Stonewall mirror where e5 is White's natural knight outpost and e4 is Black's! Because the move in hand gives White first crack at occupying e5, White plays [Nf3] and [e3] followed by [w:Bd3], [w:O-O] and [w:Ne5] (or [w:c4] to chip at d5), while Black mirrors carefully with [b:Nf6] and [b:e6]. [%csl Ye5,Ye4][%cal Ge2e3,Bg1f3] }
