{ The Swiss (or Wagner-Zwitersch) Gambit, introduced by Polish analyst Alexander Wagner in a 1910–11 correspondence tournament: White offers a pawn to rip open Black's weakened kingside diagonals! Black must accept with [fxe4] and meet [fxe4 d3] calmly with [fxe4 d3 exd3 Bxd3 Nf6] (watching out for [w:Qh5+] [b:g6] tricks); declining lets White overrun the center. If Black accepts and plays accurately by returning the pawn in the center, the game reaches dynamic equality; if Black clings greedily to e4, White's bishops and queen slice open the kingside. [%csl Ye4][%cal Gf5e4] }
