{ The signature Polish crossroads: b4 and e5 attack each other across the board. Black's best and sharpest choice is [Bxb4], trading White's flank pawn for Black's central pawn after [Bxb4 Bxe5 Nf6] to seize a lead in development! [f6] defends e5 solidly while blunting the b2 diagonal (inviting [f6 b5 d5 e3] or the Schiffler-Sokolsky Gambit [f6 e4 Bxb4 Bc4]), and [d6] holds e5 passively, letting White keep the b4 space clamp with [e3] and [c4]. [%csl Yb4,Ye5,Yb2][%cal Gf8b4,Bf7f6] }
