[Event "Réti and flank openings (2)"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. Nf3
{ The Zukertort Opening, played in the 1880s by World Championship challenger Johannes Zukertort and transformed into a hypermodern weapon in 1923 by Richard Réti. Developing the knight on move one controls d4 and e5, preventing Black from playing [e5] safely, while keeping every central pawn flexible until Black shows a hand. Depending on Black's reply, White can steer into the Réti ([d5 c4]), the King's Indian Attack ([g3] and [Bg2]), the Classical Nimzo-Larsen ([b3]), or transpose into mainstream Queen's Gambit, Catalan or Sicilian lines. [%csl Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gd7d5,Bg8f6,Yc7c5] }
( 1. g3
{ The Hungarian (or King's Fianchetto / Benko's) Opening, used by Pal Benko in the 1962 Candidates tournament to defeat both Bobby Fischer and Mikhail Tal. White prepares [Bg2] to control e4 and d5 from afar while keeping f3 open for [Ne2] or [f4] if needed; the trade-off compared to opening with the king's knight is that Black is free to occupy the center at once with [e5] as well as [d5]. [%csl Ye4,Yd5,Yf3][%cal Gd7d5,Be7e5] }
1... d5
{ Black stakes a central claim and asks how White will accompany the fianchetto. [Bg2] keeps White's knights uncommitted, inviting [Bg2 Nf6], [Bg2 e5] (a reversed Modern battle where White strikes from the flank with [c4]), [Bg2 c6] (blunting the long diagonal in Slav style), or [Bg2 c5], while [Nf3] transposes directly into the King's Indian Attack. Because White has not yet committed the king's knight to f3, White retains the option of placing pawns on d3 and e4 with the knight on e2 so the f-pawn stays free to advance. [%csl Yf3,Yd3,Ye4,Ye2][%cal Gf1g2] }
( 1... e5
{ Black takes advantage of White's omission of [Nf3] on move one to plant a pawn on e5 right away. [Bg2] is the main Hungarian continuation, letting Black build [d5] so White can undermine it with [d3], [c4] or [Nf3]; [c4] transposes into the Fianchetto English; and [Nf3] enters the Reversed Alekhine ([Nf3 e4 Nd4]), played in the famous Réti–Alekhine brilliancy at Baden-Baden 1925. White's goal in every line is to provoke Black's central pawns forward onto squares where they become targets for flank levers like [c4], [d3] and [f4] backed by the g2 bishop. [%csl Ye5,Yg2][%cal Gf1g2,Bc2c4,Yg1f3] }
2. Bg2
{ The main Hungarian / Reversed Modern tabiya: White places the bishop on g2 before touching a single central pawn or knight, inviting Black to build a broad classical center that White can chip at from the wings. [d5] accepts the dare, staking full central space so White answers with [d5 d3] (Reversed Pirc/Modern) or [d5 c4] hitting d5 immediately; [Nf6] and [Bc5] develop pieces smoothly while keeping [d5] or [d6] and [c6] in reserve. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gd7d5,Bg8f6,Yb8c6] }
( 2. c4 ) ( 2. Nf3
{ The Reversed Alekhine: White lures Black's e-pawn forward to e4 with an extra move already invested in [g3] for the g2 fianchetto. Black's principled continuation is [e4 Nd4 d5 d3] (as Alexander Alekhine played against Réti at Baden-Baden 1925), or simple protection with [Nc6] or [d6]. Careless developing moves drop e5! Having [g3] on the board already gives White a natural home for the light-squared bishop on g2, where it pressures e4 and d5 as soon as the center opens. [%csl Ye4,Yg2,Ye5,Yd5][%cal Ge5e4] }
2... e4
{ Black chases the knight and claims space, just as White does in Alekhine's Defence. The knight must hop to the center with [Nd4] (where White follows up with [Bg2] and [d3] or [c4] to chip away at e4 and d5), or to the flank with [Nh4] aiming for [d3]. Forgetting the knight is under fire is a common online blunder! [%csl Ye4,Yd5][%cal Gf3d4,Bf3h4] }
( 2... Nc6 ) ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... Bc5 $2
{ With g3 already played, f2 tricks have zero bite because White's king tucks calmly behind g2, so [Bc5] simply hangs a clean central pawn to [Nxe5] followed by retreating to f3! Advance [b:e4] or defend e5 with [Nc6]. [%csl Rg3,Rf2,Rg2,Rf3][%cal Gf3e5] }
3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 ) ( 2... Nf6 $2
{ Unlike the Petrov Defence, White has no pawn on e4 for Black to capture in return, so [Nf6] drops the e-pawn for nothing to [Nxe5] before the knight tucks safely back to f3. Push [b:e4] or play [Nc6]. [%csl Re4,Rf3][%cal Gf3e5] }
