Nf6
{ Black develops the king's knight flexibly and waits to see how White accompanies the fianchetto before committing central pawns. White plays [Bg2], inviting [Bg2 d5], [Bg2 g6] or [Bg2 e5] (where Black builds a center only after shielding f6 with the knight), while [c4] and [Nf3] transpose smoothly into Anglo-Indian and Symmetrical King's Indian Attack setups with zero early central risk. [%csl Yf6][%cal Gf1g2,Bc2c4,Yg1f3] }
2. Bg2
{ With White's bishop settled on g2 and Black's knight guarding h5 and d5, Black chooses the central landscape of the game. [d5] claims space toward King's Indian Attack and Catalan structures, [g6] keeps hypermodern symmetry until White commits a central pawn, [e5] seizes Reversed English space safely now that f6 is already developed, and [e6] prepares either [d5] or [c5] while blunting the long diagonal. [%csl Yg2][%cal Gd7d5,Bg7g6,Ye7e5] }
( 2. c4 ) ( 2. Nf3 ) 2... d5 ( 2... g6 ) ( 2... e5 ) ( 2... e6 ) ) ( 1... g6
{ Symmetrical Hungarian: Black mirrors White's fianchetto and waits for White to declare a central structure before committing d7 or e7. After [Bg2 Bg7], White can steer into the Symmetrical or Closed English ([c4]), the King's Indian Attack ([Nf3] and [d3]), or a direct central grab with [d4] and [e4]. Because symmetrical fianchetto games stay closed early on, accurate timing of the first central pawn break, [d4] or [c4] for White, [d5] or [c5] for Black, decides who claims the initiative. [%csl Yd7,Ye7][%cal Gf1g2,Bc2c4,Yg1f3] }
2. Bg2
{ Both sides have prepared kingside fianchettoes without touching a central pawn, turning the opening into a subtle battle of move orders. Black nearly always completes the mirror with [Bg7], leaving White to choose between [c4] (Symmetrical/Anglo-Indian English), [Nf3] and [d3] (Symmetrical KIA), or [d4] (Modern/King's Indian). Breaking symmetry immediately with [d5] or [c5] before [Bg7] is also completely sound and asks White to react to a fixed central pawn right away. [%csl Yg2][%cal Gf8g7,Bd7d5,Yc7c5] }
( 2. c4 ) ( 2. Nf3 ) ( 2. d4 ) 2... Bg7 ( 2... d5 ) ( 2... c5 ) ) ( 1... c5
{ The Sicilian Invitation against the Hungarian Opening: Black fights for d4 from move one and keeps [b:Nc6], [b:g6] and [b:d5] ready. White normally finishes the fianchetto with [Bg2], after which [w:c4] enters the Symmetrical English, [w:Nf3] builds a Closed Sicilian and KIA hybrid, and [e4] transposes directly into the Closed Sicilian where White attacks on the kingside with [w:d3] and [w:f4]. [%csl Yd4][%cal Gf1g2,Bc2c4,Yg1f3] }
2. Bg2
{ White completes the Hungarian fianchetto on g2, aiming along the long diagonal at d5 and b7 while waiting to see how Black accompanies the c-pawn. Black has four good setups: [Nc6] tightens the grip on d4 ahead of [g6] and [Bg7], [g6] mirrors the fianchetto immediately, [Nf6] prepares a central [d5] break in Rubinstein-English style, and [d5] right away stakes out central space before White plays [c4] or [Nf3]. [%csl Yd5][%cal Gb8c6,Bg7g6,Yd7d5] }
( 2. c4 ) ( 2. Nf3 ) ( 2. e4 ) 2... Nc6 ( 2... g6 ) ( 2... Nf6 ) ( 2... d5 ) )
( 1... Nc6
{ Developing [Nc6] on move one fights for e5 and d4 without committing a central pawn, keeping both Open-style [b:e5] setups and Chigorin-style [b:d5] formations ready once White's bishop lands on g2. White completes the fianchetto immediately with [Bg2]—inviting [Bg2 e5 c4] into a main-line English or [Bg2 d5 d4] into a Catalan—or develops [Nf3] first to stop an uncontested [b:e5] push. [%csl Ye5,Yd4][%cal Gf1g2,Bg1f3] }
2. Bg2 ( 2. Nf3 ) ) ( 1... b6 2. Bg2
