3. Bxb7 Nc6 4. Bxa8 ) ) ( 1... h5
{ The Lasker Simul Special: Black flings the h-pawn at White's fianchetto on move one, hoping to pry open the h-file with [b:h4] if White castles short blindly. White meets it calmly by developing toward the center with [Nf3] and [d4] (if Black insists on [Nf3 h4], [Nf3 h4 Nxh4] wins a clean pawn because sacrificing the exchange on h4 is unsound) or [Bg2] followed by [c4] and [d4]. [%csl Yh4][%cal Gf1g2,Bg1f3,Yd2d4] }
2. Bg2 ( 2. Nf3 ) ( 2. d4 ) ) ( 1... f5
{ The Dutch Defence against the Hungarian Opening: Black stakes an immediate claim to e4 before White has revealed a central pawn. White's main positional answer is [Bg2] followed by [c4], [Nf3] and [d4] into a Leningrad or Classical Dutch with the fianchetto already complete, while aggressive gambiteers strike immediately at the weakened kingside with Pachman's [e4]. Against Black's loosened kingside, once White puts the bishop on g2 it eyes the long diagonal all the way to a8, making any careless queenside push by Black tactically risky. [%csl Ye4,Yg2,Ya8][%cal Gf1g2,Bc2c4] }
2. Bg2 ( 2. c4 ) ( 2. Nf3 ) ( 2. d4 ) ( 2. e4
{ Pachman's Gambit in the Hungarian Opening: because [g3] already prepares [Bg2] and [Qh5+] ideas while cushioning White's kingside shelter, White strikes directly at f5 on move two to rip open diagonals! Black must accept the challenge with [fxe4] ([fxe4 d3 exd3 Bxd3 Nf6]), since declining passively lets White overrun the center. Once Black captures on e4 and White follows with [d3], White's rapid piece development and open diagonals toward f7 and g6 give dangerous practical attacking chances for the gambit pawn. [%csl Yf5,Ye4,Yf7,Yg6][%cal Gf5e4] }
2... fxe4 ( 2... Nf6 $2
