{ The Réti Opening, introduced to master practice by Richard Réti in 1923 and immortalized when he ended José Raúl Capablanca's eight-year undefeated streak at New York 1924; Gukesh Dommaraju also used it to win Game 11 of the 2024 World Championship. White attacks d5 from the flank while holding d2 back so the g2 and b2 diagonals stay open. Black has four distinct replies: [d4] gains space in Reversed Benoni style, [dxc4] accepts the pawn temporarily, and [c6] or [e6] defends d5 in Anglo-Slav or Neo-Catalan fashion. [%csl Yd5,Yd2,Yg2,Yb2][%cal Ge7e6] }
2... e6 ( 2... c6 ) ( 2... dxc4
{ The Réti Accepted: Black surrenders the center to grab White's flank pawn, much like the Queen's Gambit Accepted without [d4] on the board. Because White hasn't committed d4, White has three distinct ways to recover c4 with a pleasant game: [e3] opens f1 right away, [Na3] recaptures with the knight to eye e5 and b5 while keeping e2 and d2 flexible, and [Qa4+] regains c4 immediately with check. [%csl Yd4,Yc4,Yf1,Ye5][%cal Ge2e3] }
