[Event "Unusual first moves"]

{ Lichess Neo's coach explains these positions in its Game Review. In a comment, [Nf3] is a move, [Qb3 Qxb3 axb3] moves one after the other, [w:Re1] a later move of White, and a square is written as it is, e5. Each position's first moves are those theory goes on with. }
1. g4
{ The Grob Opening, studied across hundreds of correspondence games by Swiss IM Henri Grob and championed over the board in the 1980s by IM Michael Basman. Objectively dubious because it rips open White's kingside shelter and leaves permanent holes on f4 and h4, the Grob thrives on ambush: White rushes [Bg2], [c4] and [Qb3] to pile sudden pressure onto d5 and b7 while punishing careless pawn grabs. Black's cleanest antidotes are [d5] (attacking g4 with the bishop) and [e5]. [%csl Yf4,Yh4,Yd5,Yb7][%cal Gd7d5] }
( 1. Nc3
{ The Dunst (or Van Geet / Heinrichsen / Sleipnir) Opening, analyzed by New York master Ted Dunst and played extensively by Dutch IM Dick van Geet. Developing the queen's knight to c3 fights for d5 and e4 without weakening a single square, but has two drawbacks compared to developing the king's knight: it blocks White's own c-pawn, and it cannot stop [d5] because Black's queen already guards d5. Black's best answers are [d5], [c5], [Nf6] and [e5]. [%csl Yc3,Yd5,Ye4][%cal Gd7d5,Be7e5] }
1... d5
{ Black occupies the center immediately and threatens [b:d4] to gain space with tempo against the c3 knight. White's signature Van Geet continuation is [e4], transposing into the Queen's Knight Scandinavian where [e4 d4 Nce2] reroutes the knight to g3 and [e4 dxe4 Nxe4] plays an open Caro-Kann-like game; [d4] enters the Jobava London / Veresov family, and [e3] keeps things quiet. [%csl Yc3,Yg3][%cal Ge2e4,Bd2d4,Yg1f3] }
( 1... e5
{ The Reversed Nimzowitsch: Black stakes a claim in the center and invites [e4] into the Vienna Game. Staying in independent Dunst territory, White's sharpest try is [Nf3]: after [Nf3 Nc6 d4] (the Napoleon Attack), White puts immediate pressure on e5 and c6. [d4] right away ([d4 exd4 Qxd4 Nc6 Qa4]) plays a Scandinavian with colors reversed and an extra tempo! [%csl Ye5,Yc6][%cal Ge2e4,Bg1f3,Yd2d4] }
2. e4 ( 2. Nf3
{ White attacks e5 right away in the Reversed Nimzowitsch, forcing Black to look after the central pawn. [Nc6] is the main reply, where instead of [e4] (Four Knights), Dunst specialists strike in the center with [Nc6 d4] (the Napoleon Attack) to open lines before Black's bishop develops, while [d6] holds e5 solidly in Philidor fashion. Unlike [e4], holding the e-pawn back for a turn invites Black into open central play if Black answers [Nc6], giving Dunst players an independent tactical path out of Four Knights symmetry. [%csl Ye5][%cal Gb8c6,Bd7d6] }
2... Nc6
