{ The Barnes Opening (named after Thomas Wilson Barnes, who beat Paul Morphy in casual games in 1858), widely considered White's worst legal first move: it develops nothing, blocks the g1 knight's natural f3 post, and fatally weakens the e1-h4 and g1-a7 diagonals around White's king. Black claims an immediate advantage with [e5] or [d5]! Because f3 strips the king of its natural pawn shield and takes away the knight's best square, White ends up defending from move two if Black plays energetically in the center. [%csl Yg1,Yf3,Ye1,Yh4][%cal Ge7e5] }
