{ The Sodium Attack (or Durkin Opening, named after New Jersey player Robert Durkin and dubbed 'Sodium' from its algebraic symbol 'Na'). Putting the queen's knight on the rim controls neither d4 nor e5; White's only constructive idea is hopping to c4 after [e5 Nc4 Nc6] or tucking [c3] and [Nc2]. Black claims an easy edge simply by occupying the center with [e5] or [d5]. [%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yc4][%cal Ge7e5,Bd7d5] }
1... e5
{ Black occupies the center and uncovers the f8 bishop's gaze toward a3, standing ready to double White's a-pawns if White carelessly moves the d-pawn or b-pawn! White's best try to justify the rim knight is [Nc4], attacking e5 at once ([Nc4 Nc6 e4 Nf6]), or [c3] preparing [Nc2] and [d4]. Even so, the knight on c4 can be chased with [d5] whenever Black pleases, leaving Black with easy central space and smooth development. [%csl Yf8,Ya3,Ye5,Yc4][%cal Ga3c4] }
