{ The Dutch Defense to the Polish Opening: meeting [b4] with [f5] fights flank with flank, clamping down on e4 while leaving the long h1-a8 diagonal completely unguarded for a moment! White immediately exploits that diagonal with [Bb2], forcing Black to shield g7 via [Bb2 Nf6] before building either a Leningrad ([b:g6]) or Classical ([b:e6]) setup, whereas [b5] grabs early queenside space to keep Black's knight off c6. [%csl Yg7,Ye5][%cal Gc1b2,Bb4b5] }
2. Bb2 ( 2. b5 ) ) ( 1... b5
{ The Symmetrical Polish: answering [b4] with [b5] mirrors the Orangutan push to stop [w:b5] in its tracks and contest the long light-squared diagonal with [b:Bb7]. White develops [Bb2] to hit g7 first (forcing [Bb2 Nf6] or [Bb2 f6]), plays [e3] opening the f1 bishop's attack on the unguarded b5 pawn ([e3 Bb7 Bxb5 Bxg2] leads to wild complications, so Black normally plays [e3 a6] or [e3 e6]), or undermines the wing right away with [a4]. [%csl Yb5][%cal Gc1b2,Be2e3,Ya2a4] }
