{ Black stakes a central claim and eyes the weakened f4 square, where a knight routed via e7 and g6 to f4 is often devastating. White usually continues [Bg2] (inviting [Bg2 d5 c4 c6], the Hurst Attack, or [Bg2 Nc6 d3 Nge7]) or defends g4 with [h3]. Above all, White must NEVER touch the f-pawn here! Because f4 and h4 can never be guarded by white pawns again, Black enjoys an easy strategic game simply by controlling the center and eyeing those weak light and dark squares. [%csl Yf4,Ye7,Yg6,Yg4][%cal Gf1g2] }
