{ The Grob Gambit: White offers g4 to lure Black's bishop away from b7 and open tactical lines against the center. Black has three strong ways to answer. [c6], the Grob Gambit Declined, is the grandmaster choice, blunting g2 with a granite Slav wall so White's weakened kingside remains a long-term liability without giving White any tricks. [Bxg4] accepts the pawn and stays strong when followed by [Bxg4 c4 c6], while [e5] grabs the center (avoiding [b:Nf6], which invites [w:g5] with tempo). [%csl Yg4,Yb7,Yg2][%cal Gc8g4,Bc7c6] }
( 2. h3
{ Michael Basman's solid Grob setup (also reached via Clemenz's [h3] followed by [g4]): White protects g4 before fianchettoing, avoiding gambit complications at the cost of two flank moves. Black seizes the entire center effortlessly with [e5]: preparing Raymond Keene's [e5 Bg2 c6] system, followed by [Bd6] and [Ne7] eyeing the weakened f4 square (much stronger than playing [c6] before [e5])! Because White has weakened short castling, White usually keeps the king in the center or castles long after [Bg2], [d3] and [Nc3], hoping to launch a kingside pawn storm if Black castles short. [%csl Yg4,Yf4][%cal Ge7e5,Bc7c5] }
2... e5 ( 2... c5 ) 3. Bg2
{ Raymond Keene's antidote to Basman's Grob: Black has seized an unchallenged d5-e5 center while White spent three moves on the kingside flank. Now Black's key move is [c6], cementing d5 against any [w:c4] strike so Black can develop [Bd6], [Ne7] and [b:O-O] (or [Be6] and [Nd7]) with a clear positional advantage. From there Black's plan is straightforward: keep the center well supported, route a knight to the weak f4 outpost, and castle queenside if White tries to pry open kingside files. [%csl Yd5,Yf4][%cal Gc7c6,Bb8c6] }
